se Master manages assignment of Regions over a cluster of RegionServers."},"233":{"id":"/docs/amv2-1","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-background","content":"Background"},"234":{"id":"/docs/amv2-2","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"New System"},"235":{"id":"/docs/amv2-3","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#procedures-detail","content":"Procedures Detail"},"236":{"id":"/docs/amv2-4","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Assign/Unassign"},"237":{"id":"/docs/amv2-5","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-ui","content":"UI"},"238":{"id":"/docs/amv2-6","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#logging","content":"Logging"},"239":{"id":"/docs/amv2-7","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#implementation-notes","content":"Implementation Notes"},"240":{"id":"/docs/amv2-8","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#region-transition-rpc-and-rs-heartbeat-can-arrive-at-same-time-on-master","content":"Region Transition RPC and RS Heartbeat can arrive at ~same time on Master"},"241":{"id":"/docs/amv2-9","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#master-as-regionserver-or-as-regionserver-that-just-does-system-tables","content":"Master as RegionServer or as RegionServer that just does system tables"},"242":{"id":"/docs/amv2-10","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#new-configs","content":"New Configs"},"243":{"id":"/docs/amv2-11","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatcherthreadpoolsize-toc","content":"hbase.procedure.remote.dispatcher.threadpool.size [!toc]"},"244":{"id":"/docs/amv2-12","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatcherdelaymsec-toc","content":"hbase.procedure.remote.dispatcher.delay.msec [!toc]"},"245":{"id":"/docs/amv2-13","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatchermaxqueuesize-toc","content":"hbase.procedure.remote.dispatcher.max.queue.size [!toc]"},"246":{"id":"/docs/amv2-14","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseregionserverrpcstartupwaittime-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.rpc.startup.waittime [!toc]"},"247":{"id":"/docs/amv2-15","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-tools","content":"Tools"},"248":{"id":"/docs/amv2-16","page_id":"/docs/amv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/amv2#masterprocedureschedulerperformanceevaluation","content":"MasterProcedureSchedulerPerformanceEvaluation"},"249":{"id":"/docs/amv2-17","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2","content":"The AMv2 project is a redo of Assignment in an attempt at addressing the root cause of many of our operational issues in production, namely slow assignment and problematic accounting such that Regions are misplaced stuck offline in the notorious &#x2A;Regions-In-Transition (RIT)* limbo state."},"250":{"id":"/docs/amv2-18","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2","content":"Below are notes for devs on key aspects of AMv2 in no particular order."},"251":{"id":"/docs/amv2-19","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-background","content":"Assignment in HBase 1.x has been problematic in operation. It is not hard to see why. Region state is kept at the other end of an RPC in ZooKeeper (Terminal states — i.e. OPEN or CLOSED — are published to the *hbase:meta* table). In HBase-1.x.x, state has multiple writers with Master and RegionServers all able to make state edits concurrently (in *hbase:meta* table and out on ZooKeeper). If clocks are awry or watchers missed, state changes can be skipped or overwritten. Locking of HBase Entities — tables, regions — is not comprehensive so a table operation — disable/enable — could clash with a region-level operation; a split or merge. Region state is distributed and hard to reason about and test. Assignment is slow in operation because each assign involves moving remote znodes through transitions. Cluster size tends to top out at a couple of hundred thousand regions; beyond this, cluster start/stop takes hours and is prone to corruption."},"252":{"id":"/docs/amv2-20","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-background","content":"AMv2 (AssignmentManager Version 2) is a refactor (HBASE-14350) of the hbase-1.x AssignmentManager putting it up on a ProcedureV2 (HBASE-12439) basis. ProcedureV2 (Pv2), is an awkwardly named system that allows describing and running multi-step state machines. It is performant and persists all state to a Store which is recoverable post crash. See the companion chapter on Procedure Framework (Pv2), to learn more about the ProcedureV2 system."},"253":{"id":"/docs/amv2-21","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-background","content":"In AMv2, all assignment, crash handling, splits and merges are recast as Procedures(v2). ZooKeeper is purged from the mix. As before, the final assignment state gets published to *hbase:meta* for non-Master participants to read (all-clients) with intermediate state kept in the local Pv2 WAL-based 'store' but only the active Master, a single-writer, evolves state. The Master's in-memory cluster image is the authority and if disagreement, RegionServers are forced to comply. Pv2 adds shared/exclusive locking of all core HBase Entities — namespace, tables, and regions — to ensure one actor at a time access and to prevent operations contending over resources (move/split, disable/assign, etc.)."},"254":{"id":"/docs/amv2-22","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-background","content":"This redo of AM atop of a purposed, performant state machine with all operations taking on the common Procedure form with a single state writer only moves our AM to a new level of resilience and scale."},"255":{"id":"/docs/amv2-23","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"Each Region Assign or Unassign of a Region is now a Procedure. A Move (Region) Procedure is a compound of Procedures; it is the running of an Unassign Procedure followed by an Assign Procedure. The Move Procedure spawns the Assign and Unassign in series and then waits on their completions."},"256":{"id":"/docs/amv2-24","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"And so on. ServerCrashProcedure spawns the WAL splitting tasks and then the reassign of all regions that were hosted on the crashed server as subprocedures."},"257":{"id":"/docs/amv2-25","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"AMv2 Procedures are run by the Master in a ProcedureExecutor instance. All Procedures make use of utility provided by the Pv2 framework."},"258":{"id":"/docs/amv2-26","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"For example, Procedures persist each state transition to the frameworks' Procedure Store. The default implementation is done as a WAL kept on HDFS. On crash, we reopen the Store and rerun all WALs of Procedure transitions to put the Assignment State Machine back into the attitude it had just before crash. We then continue Procedure execution."},"259":{"id":"/docs/amv2-27","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"In the new system, the Master is the Authority on all things Assign. Previous we were ambiguous; e.g. the RegionServer was in charge of Split operations. Master keeps an in-memory image of Region states and servers. If disagreement, the Master always prevails; at an extreme it will kill the RegionServer that is in disagreement."},"260":{"id":"/docs/amv2-28","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"A new RegionStateStore class takes care of publishing the terminal Region state, whether OPEN or CLOSED, out to the *hbase:meta* table."},"261":{"id":"/docs/amv2-29","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-system","content":"RegionServers now report their run version on Connection. This version is available inside the AM for use running migrating rolling restarts."},"262":{"id":"/docs/amv2-30","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Assign and Unassign subclass a common RegionTransitionProcedure. There can only be one RegionTransitionProcedure per region running at a time since the RTP instance takes a lock on the region. The RTP base Procedure has three steps; a store the procedure step (REGION\\_TRANSITION\\_QUEUE); a dispatch of the procedure open or close followed by a suspend waiting on the remote regionserver to report successful open or fail (REGION\\_TRANSITION\\_DISPATCH) or notification that the server fielding the request crashed; and finally registration of the successful open/close in hbase:meta (REGION\\_TRANSITION\\_FINISH)."},"263":{"id":"/docs/amv2-31","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Here is how the assign of a region 56f985a727afe80a184dac75fbf6860c looks in the logs. The assign was provoked by a Server Crash (Process ID 1176 or pid=1176 which when it is the parent of a procedure, it is identified as ppid=1176). The assign is pid=1179, the second region of the two being assigned by this Server Crash."},"264":{"id":"/docs/amv2-32","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Next we start the assign by queuing ('registering') the Procedure with the framework."},"265":{"id":"/docs/amv2-33","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Track the running of Procedures in logs by tracing their process id — here pid=1179."},"266":{"id":"/docs/amv2-34","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Next we move to the dispatch phase where we update hbase:meta table setting the region state as OPENING on server ve540. We then dispatch an rpc to ve540 asking it to open the region. Thereafter we suspend the Assign until we get a message back from ve540 on whether it has opened the region successfully (or not)."},"267":{"id":"/docs/amv2-35","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Below we log the incoming report that the region opened successfully on ve540. The Procedure is woken up (you can tell it the procedure is running by the name of the thread, its a ProcedureExecutor thread, ProcExecWrkr-9). The woken up Procedure updates state in hbase:meta to denote the region as open on ve0540. It then reports finished and exits."},"268":{"id":"/docs/amv2-36","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Unassign looks similar given it is based on the base RegionTransitionProcedure. It has the same state transitions and does basically the same steps but with different state name (CLOSING, CLOSED)."},"269":{"id":"/docs/amv2-37","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#assignunassign","content":"Most other procedures are subclasses of a Pv2 StateMachine implementation. We have both Table and Region focused StateMachines types."},"270":{"id":"/docs/amv2-38","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-ui","content":"Along the top-bar on the Master, you can now find a 'Procedures\\&Locks' tab which takes you to a page that is ugly but useful. It dumps currently running procedures and framework locks. Look at this when you can't figure what stuff is stuck; it will at least identify problematic procedures (take the pid and grep the logs...). Look for ROLLEDBACK or pids that have been RUNNING for a long time."},"271":{"id":"/docs/amv2-39","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#logging","content":"Procedures log their process ids as pid= and their parent ids (ppid=) everywhere. Work has been done so you can grep the pid and see history of a procedure operation."},"272":{"id":"/docs/amv2-40","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#implementation-notes","content":"In this section we note some idiosyncrasies of operation as an attempt at saving you some head-scratching."},"273":{"id":"/docs/amv2-41","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#region-transition-rpc-and-rs-heartbeat-can-arrive-at-same-time-on-master","content":"Reporting Region Transition on a RegionServer is now a RPC distinct from RS heartbeating ('RegionServerServices' Service). An heartbeat and a status update can arrive at the Master at about the same time. The Master will update its internal state for a Region but this same state is checked when heartbeat processing. We may find the unexpected; i.e. a Region just reported as CLOSED so heartbeat is surprised to find region OPEN on the back of the RS report. In the new system, all slaves must cow to the Masters' understanding of cluster state; the Master will kill/close any misaligned entities."},"274":{"id":"/docs/amv2-42","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#region-transition-rpc-and-rs-heartbeat-can-arrive-at-same-time-on-master","content":"To address the above, we added a lastUpdate for in-memory Master state. Let a region state have some vintage before we act on it (one second currently)."},"275":{"id":"/docs/amv2-43","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#master-as-regionserver-or-as-regionserver-that-just-does-system-tables","content":"AMv2 enforces current master branch default of HMaster carrying system tables only; i.e. the Master in an HBase cluster acts also as a RegionServer only it is the exclusive host for tables such as *hbase:meta*, *hbase:namespace*, etc., the core system tables. This is causing a couple of test failures as AMv1, though it is not supposed to, allows moving hbase:meta off Master while AMv2 does not."},"276":{"id":"/docs/amv2-44","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#new-configs","content":"These configs all need doc on when you'd change them."},"277":{"id":"/docs/amv2-45","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatcherthreadpoolsize-toc","content":"Defaults 128"},"278":{"id":"/docs/amv2-46","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatcherdelaymsec-toc","content":"Default 150ms"},"279":{"id":"/docs/amv2-47","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseprocedureremotedispatchermaxqueuesize-toc","content":"Default 32"},"280":{"id":"/docs/amv2-48","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#hbaseregionserverrpcstartupwaittime-toc","content":"Default 60 seconds."},"281":{"id":"/docs/amv2-49","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-tools","content":"HBASE-15592 Print Procedure WAL Content"},"282":{"id":"/docs/amv2-50","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#amv2-tools","content":"Patch in HBASE-18152 \\[AMv2] Corrupt Procedure WAL file; procedure data stored out of order https\\://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/attachment/12871066/reading\\_bad\\_wal.patch"},"283":{"id":"/docs/amv2-51","page_id":"/docs/amv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/amv2#masterprocedureschedulerperformanceevaluation","content":"Tool to test performance of locks and queues in procedure scheduler independently from other framework components. Run this after any substantial changes in proc system. Prints nice output:"},"284":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"page","content":"Bulk Loading","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading"},"285":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","content":"Efficient methods for loading large datasets into HBase using MapReduce to generate HFiles and directly load them into the cluster."},"286":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-overview","content":"Overview"},"287":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-load-architecture","content":"Bulk Load Architecture"},"288":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"Preparing data via a MapReduce job"},"289":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#completing-the-data-load","content":"Completing the data load"},"290":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-see-also","content":"See Also"},"291":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#advanced-usage","content":"Advanced Usage"},"292":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"'Adopting' Stray Data"},"293":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"Bulk Loading Replication"},"294":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-overview","content":"HBase includes several methods of loading data into tables. The most straightforward method is to either use the `TableOutputFormat` class from a MapReduce job, or use the normal client APIs; however, these are not always the most efficient methods."},"295":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-overview","content":"The bulk load feature uses a MapReduce job to output table data in HBase's internal data format, and then directly load the generated StoreFiles into a running cluster. Using bulk load will use less CPU and network resources than loading via the HBase API."},"296":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-load-architecture","content":"The HBase bulk load process consists of two main steps."},"297":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"The first step of a bulk load is to generate HBase data files (StoreFiles) from a MapReduce job using `HFileOutputFormat2`. This output format writes out data in HBase's internal storage format so that they can be later loaded efficiently into the cluster."},"298":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"In order to function efficiently, `HFileOutputFormat2` must be configured such that each output HFile fits within a single region. In order to do this, jobs whose output will be bulk loaded into HBase use Hadoop's `TotalOrderPartitioner` class to partition the map output into disjoint ranges of the key space, corresponding to the key ranges of the regions in the table."},"299":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"`HFileOutputFormat2` includes a convenience function, `configureIncrementalLoad()`, which automatically sets up a `TotalOrderPartitioner` based on the current region boundaries of a table."},"300":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#completing-the-data-load","content":"After a data import has been prepared, either by using the `importtsv` tool with the “importtsv.bulk.output” option or by some other MapReduce job using the `HFileOutputFormat`, the `completebulkload` tool is used to import the data into the running cluster. This command line tool iterates through the prepared data files, and for each one determines the region the file belongs to. It then contacts the appropriate RegionServer which adopts the HFile, moving it into its storage directory and making the data available to clients."},"301":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#completing-the-data-load","content":"If the region boundaries have changed during the course of bulk load preparation, or between the preparation and completion steps, the `completebulkload` utility will automatically split the data files into pieces corresponding to the new boundaries. This process is not optimally efficient, so users should take care to minimize the delay between preparing a bulk load and importing it into the cluster, especially if other clients are simultaneously loading data through other means."},"302":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#completing-the-data-load","content":"The `-c config-file` option can be used to specify a file containing the appropriate hbase parameters (e.g., hbase-site.xml) if not supplied already on the CLASSPATH (In addition, the CLASSPATH must contain the directory that has the zookeeper configuration file if zookeeper is NOT managed by HBase)."},"303":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-see-also","content":"For more information about the referenced utilities, see ImportTsv and CompleteBulkLoad."},"304":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-see-also","content":"See How-to: Use HBase Bulk Loading, and Why for an old blog post on loading."},"305":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#advanced-usage","content":"Although the `importtsv` tool is useful in many cases, advanced users may want to generate data programmatically, or import data from other formats. To get started doing so, dig into `ImportTsv.java` and check the JavaDoc for HFileOutputFormat."},"306":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#advanced-usage","content":"The import step of the bulk load can also be done programmatically. See the `LoadIncrementalHFiles` class for more information."},"307":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"Should an HBase cluster lose account of regions or files during an outage or error, you can use the `completebulkload` tool to add back the dropped data. HBase operator tooling such as HBCK2 or the reporting added to the Master's UI under the `HBCK Report` (Since HBase 2.0.6/2.1.6/2.2.1) can identify such 'orphan' directories."},"308":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"Before you begin the 'adoption', ensure the `hbase:meta` table is in a healthy state. Run the `CatalogJanitor` by executing the `catalogjanitor_run` command on the HBase shell. When finished, check the `HBCK Report` page on the Master UI. Work on fixing any inconsistencies, holes, or overlaps found before proceeding. The `hbase:meta` table is the authority on where all data is to be found and must be consistent for the `completebulkload` tool to work properly."},"309":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"The `completebulkload` tool takes a directory and a `tablename`. The directory has subdirectories named for column families of the targeted `tablename`. In these subdirectories are `hfiles` to load. Given this structure, you can pass errant region directories (and the table name to which the region directory belongs) and the tool will bring the data files back into the fold by moving them under the approprate serving directory. If stray files, then you will need to mock up this structure before invoking the `completebulkload` tool; you may have to look at the file content using the HFile Tool to see what the column family to use is. When the tool completes its run, you will notice that the source errant directory has had its storefiles moved/removed. It is now desiccated since its data has been drained, and the pointed-to directory can be safely removed. It may still have `.regioninfo` files and other subdirectories but they are of no relevance now (There may be content still under the *recovered\\_edits* directory; a TODO is tooling to replay the content of *recovered\\_edits* if needed; see Add RecoveredEditsPlayer). If you pass `completebulkload` a directory without store files, it will run and note the directory is storefile-free. Just remove such 'empty' directories."},"310":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"For example, presuming a directory at the top level in HDFS named `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` has data we need to re-add to the HBase `TestTable`:"},"311":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#adopting-stray-data","content":"After it successfully completes, any files that were in `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` have been moved under hbase and the `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` directory can be deleted (Check content before and after by running `ls -r` on the HDFS directory)."},"312":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"HBASE-13153 adds replication support for bulk loaded HFiles, available since HBase 1.3/2.0. This feature is enabled by setting `hbase.replication.bulkload.enabled` to `true` (default is `false`). You also need to copy the source cluster configuration files to the destination cluster."},"313":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"Additional configurations are required too:"},"314":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.source.fs.conf.provider`\\\nThis defines the class which loads the source cluster file system client configuration in the destination cluster. This should be configured for all the RS in the destination cluster. Default is `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.replication.regionserver.DefaultSourceFSConfigurationProvider`."},"315":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.conf.dir`\\\nThis represents the base directory where the file system client configurations of the source cluster are copied to the destination cluster. This should be configured for all the RS in the destination cluster. Default is `$HBASE_CONF_DIR`."},"316":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.cluster.id`\\\nThis configuration is required in the cluster where replication for bulk loaded data is enabled. A source cluster is uniquely identified by the destination cluster using this id. This should be configured for all the RS in the source cluster configuration file for all the RS."},"317":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"For example: If source cluster FS client configurations are copied to the destination cluster under directory `/home/user/dc1/`, then `hbase.replication.cluster.id` should be configured as `dc1` and `hbase.replication.conf.dir` as `/home/user`."},"318":{"id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/bulk-loading#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`DefaultSourceFSConfigurationProvider` supports only `xml` type files. It loads source cluster FS\nclient configuration only once, so if source cluster FS client configuration files are updated,\nevery peer(s) cluster RS must be restarted to reload the configuration."},"319":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","type":"page","content":"Catalog Tables","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables"},"320":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","content":"Understanding hbase:meta catalog table structure, location tracking, and how HBase maintains region metadata."},"321":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"hbase:meta"},"322":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#startup-sequencing","content":"Startup Sequencing"},"323":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","content":"The catalog table `hbase:meta` exists as an HBase table and is filtered out of the HBase shell's `list` command, but is in fact a table just like any other."},"324":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"The `hbase:meta` table (previously called `.META.`) keeps a list of all regions in the system, and the location of `hbase:meta` is stored in ZooKeeper."},"325":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"The `hbase:meta` table structure is as follows:"},"326":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"**Key:**"},"327":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"Region key of the format (`[table],[region start key],[region id]`)"},"328":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"**Values:**"},"329":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"`info:regioninfo` (serialized RegionInfo instance for this region)"},"330":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"`info:server` (server:port of the RegionServer containing this region)"},"331":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"`info:serverstartcode` (start-time of the RegionServer process containing this region)"},"332":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"When a table is in the process of splitting, two other columns will be created, called `info:splitA` and `info:splitB`. These columns represent the two daughter regions. The values for these columns are also serialized HRegionInfo instances. After the region has been split, eventually this row will be deleted."},"333":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"The empty key is used to denote table start and table end. A region with an empty start key is the\nfirst region in a table. If a region has both an empty start and an empty end key, it is the only\nregion in the table"},"334":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#hbasemeta","content":"In the (hopefully unlikely) event that programmatic processing of catalog metadata is required, see the RegionInfo.parseFrom utility."},"335":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#startup-sequencing","content":"First, the location of `hbase:meta` is looked up in ZooKeeper. Next, `hbase:meta` is updated with server and startcode values."},"336":{"id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/catalog-tables#startup-sequencing","content":"For information on region-RegionServer assignment, see Region-RegionServer Assignment."},"337":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"page","content":"Client Request Filters","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters"},"338":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","content":"Using filters with Get and Scan operations to efficiently query HBase data, including comparison, column, row, and utility filters."},"339":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#structural","content":"Structural"},"340":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#filterlist","content":"FilterList"},"341":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#column-value","content":"Column Value"},"342":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#singlecolumnvaluefilter","content":"SingleColumnValueFilter"},"343":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnvaluefilter","content":"ColumnValueFilter"},"344":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#column-value-comparators","content":"Column Value Comparators"},"345":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#regexstringcomparator","content":"RegexStringComparator"},"346":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#substringcomparator","content":"SubstringComparator"},"347":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binaryprefixcomparator","content":"BinaryPrefixComparator"},"348":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binarycomparator","content":"BinaryComparator"},"349":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binarycomponentcomparator","content":"BinaryComponentComparator"},"350":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#keyvalue-metadata","content":"KeyValue Metadata"},"351":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#familyfilter","content":"FamilyFilter"},"352":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#qualifierfilter","content":"QualifierFilter"},"353":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnprefixfilter","content":"ColumnPrefixFilter"},"354":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"MultipleColumnPrefixFilter"},"355":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"ColumnRangeFilter"},"356":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#rowkey","content":"RowKey"},"357":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#rowfilter","content":"RowFilter"},"358":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#utility","content":"Utility"},"359":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#firstkeyonlyfilter","content":"FirstKeyOnlyFilter"},"360":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","content":"Get and Scan instances can be optionally configured with filters which are applied on the RegionServer."},"361":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","content":"Filters can be confusing because there are many different types, and it is best to approach them by understanding the groups of Filter functionality."},"362":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#structural","content":"Structural Filters contain other Filters."},"363":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#filterlist","content":"FilterList represents a list of Filters with a relationship of `FilterList.Operator.MUST_PASS_ALL` or `FilterList.Operator.MUST_PASS_ONE` between the Filters. The following example shows an 'or' between two Filters (checking for either 'my value' or 'my other value' on the same attribute)."},"364":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#singlecolumnvaluefilter","content":"A SingleColumnValueFilter (see: https\\://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/filter/SingleColumnValueFilter.html) can be used to test column values for equivalence (`CompareOperaor.EQUAL`), inequality (`CompareOperaor.NOT_EQUAL`), or ranges (e.g., `CompareOperaor.GREATER`). The following is an example of testing equivalence of a column to a String value \"my value\"..."},"365":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnvaluefilter","content":"Introduced in HBase-2.0.0 version as a complementation of SingleColumnValueFilter, ColumnValueFilter gets matched cell only, while SingleColumnValueFilter gets the entire row (has other columns and values) to which the matched cell belongs. Parameters of constructor of ColumnValueFilter are the same as SingleColumnValueFilter."},"366":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnvaluefilter","content":"Note. For simple query like \"equals to a family:qualifier:value\", we highly recommend to use the following way instead of using SingleColumnValueFilter or ColumnValueFilter:"},"367":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnvaluefilter","content":"This scan will restrict to the specified column 'family:qualifier', avoiding scan of unrelated families and columns, which has better performance, and `ValueFilter` is the condition used to do the value filtering."},"368":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnvaluefilter","content":"But if query is much more complicated beyond this book, then please make your good choice case by case."},"369":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#column-value-comparators","content":"There are several Comparator classes in the Filter package that deserve special mention. These Comparators are used in concert with other Filters, such as SingleColumnValueFilter."},"370":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#regexstringcomparator","content":"RegexStringComparator supports regular expressions for value comparisons."},"371":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#regexstringcomparator","content":"See the Oracle JavaDoc for supported RegEx patterns in Java."},"372":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-34","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#substringcomparator","content":"SubstringComparator can be used to determine if a given substring exists in a value. The comparison is case-insensitive."},"373":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-35","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binaryprefixcomparator","content":"See BinaryPrefixComparator."},"374":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-36","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binarycomparator","content":"See BinaryComparator."},"375":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-37","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binarycomponentcomparator","content":"BinaryComponentComparator can be used to compare specific value at specific location with in the cell value. The comparison can be done for both ascii and binary data."},"376":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-38","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#binarycomponentcomparator","content":"See HBASE-22969 for other use cases and details."},"377":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#keyvalue-metadata","content":"As HBase stores data internally as KeyValue pairs, KeyValue Metadata Filters evaluate the existence of keys (i.e., ColumnFamily:Column qualifiers) for a row, as opposed to values the previous section."},"378":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-40","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#familyfilter","content":"FamilyFilter can be used to filter on the ColumnFamily. It is generally a better idea to select ColumnFamilies in the Scan than to do it with a Filter."},"379":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-41","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#qualifierfilter","content":"QualifierFilter can be used to filter based on Column (aka Qualifier) name."},"380":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-42","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnprefixfilter","content":"ColumnPrefixFilter can be used to filter based on the lead portion of Column (aka Qualifier) names."},"381":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnprefixfilter","content":"A ColumnPrefixFilter seeks ahead to the first column matching the prefix in each row and for each involved column family. It can be used to efficiently get a subset of the columns in very wide rows."},"382":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnprefixfilter","content":"Note: The same column qualifier can be used in different column families. This filter returns all matching columns."},"383":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnprefixfilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family that start with \"abc\""},"384":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"MultipleColumnPrefixFilter behaves like ColumnPrefixFilter but allows specifying multiple prefixes."},"385":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"Like ColumnPrefixFilter, MultipleColumnPrefixFilter efficiently seeks ahead to the first column matching the lowest prefix and also seeks past ranges of columns between prefixes. It can be used to efficiently get discontinuous sets of columns from very wide rows."},"386":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family that start with \"abc\" or \"xyz\""},"387":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"A ColumnRangeFilter allows efficient intra row scanning."},"388":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"A ColumnRangeFilter can seek ahead to the first matching column for each involved column family. It can be used to efficiently get a 'slice' of the columns of a very wide row. i.e. you have a million columns in a row but you only want to look at columns bbbb-bbdd."},"389":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"Note: The same column qualifier can be used in different column families. This filter returns all matching columns."},"390":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family between \"bbbb\" (inclusive) and \"bbdd\" (inclusive)"},"391":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#columnrangefilter","content":"Note: Introduced in HBase 0.92"},"392":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#rowfilter","content":"It is generally a better idea to use the startRow/stopRow methods on Scan for row selection, however RowFilter can also be used."},"393":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#rowfilter","content":"You can supplement a scan (both bounded and unbounded) with RowFilter constructed from BinaryComponentComparator for further filtering out or filtering in rows. See HBASE-22969 for use cases and other details."},"394":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client-request-filters#firstkeyonlyfilter","content":"This is primarily used for rowcount jobs. See FirstKeyOnlyFilter."},"395":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"page","content":"Client","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client"},"396":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client","content":"HBase client architecture, connection management, metadata caching, and client-side configuration for optimal performance."},"397":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#cluster-connections","content":"Cluster Connections"},"398":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-as-of-hbase-100","content":"API as of HBase 1.0.0"},"399":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-before-hbase-100","content":"API before HBase 1.0.0"},"400":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-pooling","content":"Connection Pooling"},"401":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"WriteBuffer and Batch Methods"},"402":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"Asynchronous Client"},"403":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"Asynchronous Admin"},"404":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#external-clients","content":"External Clients"},"405":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Master Registry (new as of 2.3.0)"},"406":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#masterregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"MasterRegistry RPC hedging"},"407":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"Additional Notes"},"408":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"Rpc Connection Registry (new as of 2.5.0)"},"409":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"RpcConnectionRegistry RPC hedging"},"410":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"RpcConnectionRegistry bootstrap nodes refreshing"},"411":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-uri","content":"Connection URI"},"412":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"Supported Schemes"},"413":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#specify-configuration-through-uri-queries","content":"Specify Configuration through URI Queries"},"414":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/client#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"Implement Your Own Connection Registry"},"415":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client","content":"The HBase client finds the RegionServers that are serving the particular row range of interest. It does this by querying the `hbase:meta` table. See hbase:meta for details. After locating the required region(s), the client contacts the RegionServer serving that region, rather than going through the master, and issues the read or write request. This information is cached in the client so that subsequent requests need not go through the lookup process. Should a region be reassigned either by the master load balancer or because a RegionServer has died, the client will requery the catalog tables to determine the new location of the user region."},"416":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client","content":"See Runtime Impact for more information about the impact of the Master on HBase Client communication."},"417":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client","content":"Administrative functions are done via an instance of Admin"},"418":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#cluster-connections","content":"The API changed in HBase 1.0. For connection configuration information, see Client configuration and dependencies connecting to an HBase cluster."},"419":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-as-of-hbase-100","content":"It's been cleaned up and users are returned Interfaces to work against rather than particular types. In HBase 1.0, obtain a `Connection` object from `ConnectionFactory` and thereafter, get from it instances of `Table`, `Admin`, and `RegionLocator` on an as-need basis. When done, close the obtained instances. Finally, be sure to cleanup your `Connection` instance before exiting. `Connections` are heavyweight objects but thread-safe so you can create one for your application and keep the instance around. `Table`, `Admin` and `RegionLocator` instances are lightweight. Create as you go and then let go as soon as you are done by closing them. See the Client Package Javadoc Description for example usage of the new HBase 1.0 API."},"420":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-before-hbase-100","content":"Instances of `HTable` are the way to interact with an HBase cluster earlier than 1.0.0. *Table instances are not thread-safe*. Only one thread can use an instance of Table at any given time. When creating Table instances, it is advisable to use the same HBaseConfiguration instance. This will ensure sharing of ZooKeeper and socket instances to the RegionServers which is usually what you want. For example, this is preferred:"},"421":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-before-hbase-100","content":"as opposed to this:"},"422":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#api-before-hbase-100","content":"For more information about how connections are handled in the HBase client, see ConnectionFactory."},"423":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-pooling","content":"For applications which require high-end multithreaded access (e.g., web-servers or application servers that may serve many application threads in a single JVM), you can pre-create a `Connection`, as shown in the following example:"},"424":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-pooling","content":"**Example 24. Pre-Creating a `Connection`**"},"425":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-pooling","content":"Previous versions of this guide discussed `HTablePool`, which was deprecated in HBase 0.94, 0.95,\nand 0.96, and removed in 0.98.1, by\nHBASE-6580, or `HConnection`, which is\ndeprecated in HBase 1.0 by `Connection`. Please use\nConnection\ninstead."},"426":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"In HBase 1.0 and later, HTable is deprecated in favor of Table. `Table` does not use autoflush. To do buffered writes, use the BufferedMutator class."},"427":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"In HBase 2.0 and later, HTable does not use BufferedMutator to execute the `Put` operation. Refer to HBASE-18500 for more information."},"428":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"For additional information on write durability, review the ACID semantics page."},"429":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"For fine-grained control of batching of `Put`s or `Delete`s, see the batch methods on Table."},"430":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-34","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"It is a new API introduced in HBase 2.0 which aims to provide the ability to access HBase asynchronously."},"431":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-35","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"You can obtain an `AsyncConnection` from `ConnectionFactory`, and then get a asynchronous table instance from it to access HBase. When done, close the `AsyncConnection` instance(usually when your program exits)."},"432":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-36","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"For the asynchronous table, most methods have the same meaning with the old `Table` interface, expect that the return value is wrapped with a CompletableFuture usually. We do not have any buffer here so there is no close method for asynchronous table, you do not need to close it. And it is thread safe."},"433":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-37","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"There are several differences for scan:"},"434":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-38","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"There is still a `getScanner` method which returns a `ResultScanner`. You can use it in the old way and it works like the old `ClientAsyncPrefetchScanner`."},"435":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"There is a `scanAll` method which will return all the results at once. It aims to provide a simpler way for small scans which you want to get the whole results at once usually."},"436":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-40","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"The Observer Pattern. There is a scan method which accepts a `ScanResultConsumer` as a parameter. It will pass the results to the consumer."},"437":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-41","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"Notice that `AsyncTable` interface is templatized. The template parameter specifies the type of `ScanResultConsumerBase` used by scans, which means the observer style scan APIs are different. The two types of scan consumers are - `ScanResultConsumer` and `AdvancedScanResultConsumer`."},"438":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-42","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"`ScanResultConsumer` needs a separate thread pool which is used to execute the callbacks registered to the returned CompletableFuture. Because the use of separate thread pool frees up RPC threads, callbacks are free to do anything. Use this if the callbacks are not quick, or when in doubt."},"439":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-client","content":"`AdvancedScanResultConsumer` executes callbacks inside the framework thread. It is not allowed to do time consuming work in the callbacks else it will likely block the framework threads and cause very bad performance impact. As its name, it is designed for advanced users who want to write high performance code. See `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.client.example.HttpProxyExample` for how to write fully asynchronous code with it."},"440":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"You can obtain an `AsyncConnection` from `ConnectionFactory`, and then get a `AsyncAdmin` instance from it to access HBase. Notice that there are two `getAdmin` methods to get a `AsyncAdmin` instance. One method has one extra thread pool parameter which is used to execute callbacks. It is designed for normal users. Another method doesn't need a thread pool and all the callbacks are executed inside the framework thread so it is not allowed to do time consuming works in the callbacks. It is designed for advanced users."},"441":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"The default `getAdmin` methods will return a `AsyncAdmin` instance which use default configs. If you want to customize some configs, you can use `getAdminBuilder` methods to get a `AsyncAdminBuilder` for creating `AsyncAdmin` instance. Users are free to only set the configs they care about to create a new `AsyncAdmin` instance."},"442":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"For the `AsyncAdmin` interface, most methods have the same meaning with the old `Admin` interface, expect that the return value is wrapped with a CompletableFuture usually."},"443":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"For most admin operations, when the returned CompletableFuture is done, it means the admin operation has also been done. But for compact operation, it only means the compact request was sent to HBase and may need some time to finish the compact operation. For `rollWALWriter` method, it only means the rollWALWriter request was sent to the region server and may need some time to finish the `rollWALWriter` operation."},"444":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#asynchronous-admin","content":"For region name, we only accept `byte[]` as the parameter type and it may be a full region name or a encoded region name. For server name, we only accept `ServerName` as the parameter type. For table name, we only accept `TableName` as the parameter type. For `list*` operations, we only accept `Pattern` as the parameter type if you want to do regex matching."},"445":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#external-clients","content":"Information on non-Java clients and custom protocols is covered in Apache HBase External APIs"},"446":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Starting from 2.5.0, MasterRegistry is deprecated. It's functionality is completely superseded by the RpcConnectionRegistry. Please see Rpc Connection Registry (new as of 2.5.0) for more details."},"447":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Client internally works with a *connection registry* to fetch the metadata needed by connections. This connection registry implementation is responsible for fetching the following metadata."},"448":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Active master address"},"449":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Current meta region(s) locations"},"450":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Cluster ID (unique to this cluster)"},"451":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"This information is needed as a part of various client operations like connection set up, scans, gets, etc. Traditionally, the connection registry implementation has been based on ZooKeeper as the source of truth and clients fetched the metadata directly from the ZooKeeper quorum. HBase 2.3.0 introduces a new connection registry implementation based on direct communication with the Masters. With this implementation, clients now fetch required metadata via master RPC end points instead of maintaining connections to ZooKeeper. This change was done for the following reasons."},"452":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Reduce load on ZooKeeper since that is critical for cluster operation."},"453":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Holistic client timeout and retry configurations since the new registry brings all the client operations under HBase rpc framework."},"454":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Remove the ZooKeeper client dependency on HBase client library."},"455":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"This means:"},"456":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"At least a single active or stand by master is needed for cluster connection setup. Refer to Runtime Impact for more details."},"457":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Master can be in a critical path of read/write operations, especially if the client metadata cache is empty or stale."},"458":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"There is higher connection load on the masters that before since the clients talk directly to HMasters instead of ZooKeeper ensemble\\`"},"459":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-63","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"To reduce hot-spotting on a single master, all the masters (active & stand-by) expose the needed service to fetch the connection metadata. This lets the client connect to any master (not just active). Both ZooKeeper-based and Master-based connection registry implementations are available in 2.3+. For 2.x and earlier, the ZooKeeper-based implementation remains the default configuration. For 3.0.0, RpcConnectionRegistry becomes the default configuration, as the alternate to MasterRegistry."},"460":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-64","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Change the connection registry implementation by updating the value configured for `hbase.client.registry.impl`. To explicitly enable the ZooKeeper-based registry, use"},"461":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-65","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"To explicitly enable the Master-based registry, use"},"462":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-66","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#masterregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"MasterRegistry implements hedging of connection registry RPCs across active and stand-by masters. This lets the client make the same request to multiple servers and which ever responds first is returned back to the client immediately. This improves performance, especially when a subset of servers are under load. The hedging fan out size is configurable, meaning the number of requests that are hedged in a single attempt, using the configuration key *hbase.client.master\\_registry.hedged.fanout* in the client configuration. It defaults to 2. With this default, the RPCs are tried in batches of 2. The hedging policy is still primitive and does not adapt to any sort of live rpc performance metrics."},"463":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-67","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"Clients hedge the requests in a randomized order to avoid hot-spotting a single master."},"464":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-68","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"Cluster internal connections (masters ↔ regionservers) still use ZooKeeper based connection registry."},"465":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-69","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"Cluster internal state is still tracked in Zookeeper, hence ZK availability requirements are same as before."},"466":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-70","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"Inter cluster replication still uses ZooKeeper based connection registry to simplify configuration management."},"467":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-71","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#additional-notes","content":"For more implementation details, please refer to the design doc and HBASE-18095."},"468":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-72","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"As said in the Master Registry (new as of 2.3.0) section, there are some disadvantages and limitations for MasterRegistry, especially that it puts master in the critical path of read/write operations. In order to address these problems, we introduced a more generic RpcConnectionRegistry."},"469":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-73","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"It is also rpc based, like MasterRegistry, with several differences"},"470":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-74","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"Region server also implements the necessary rpc service, so you can config any nodes in the cluster as bootstrap nodes, not only masters"},"471":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-75","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"Support refreshing bootstrap nodes, for spreading loads across the nodes in the cluster, and also remove the dead nodes in bootstrap nodes."},"472":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-76","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"To explicitly enable the rpc-based registry, use"},"473":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-77","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"To configure the bootstrap nodes, use"},"474":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-78","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"If not configured, we will fallback to use master addresses as the bootstrap nodes."},"475":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-79","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"RpcConnectionRegistry is available in 2.5+, and becomes the default client registry implementation in 3.0.0."},"476":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-80","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"Hedged read is still supported, the configuration key is now *hbase.client.bootstrap.hedged.fanout*, and its default value is still 2."},"477":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-81","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"There are basically two reasons for us to refresh the bootstrap nodes"},"478":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-82","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"Periodically. This is for spreading loads across the nodes in the cluster. There are two configurations"},"479":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-83","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"*hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*: the refresh interval in seconds, default 300. A value less than or equal to zero means disable refreshing."},"480":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-84","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"*hbase.client.bootstrap.initial\\_refresh\\_delay\\_secs*: the initial refresh interval in seconds, the default value is 1/10 of *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*. The reason why we want to introduce a separated configuration for the delay for first refreshing is that, as end users could configure any nodes in a cluster as the initial bootstrap nodes, it is possible that different end users will configure the same machine which makes the machine over load. So we should have a shorter delay for the initial refresh, to let users quickly switch to the bootstrap nodes we want them to connect to."},"481":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-85","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"When there is a connection error while requesting the nodes, we will refresh immediately, to remove the dead nodes. To avoid putting too much pressure to the cluster, there is a configuration *hbase.client.bootstrap.min\\_secs\\_between\\_refreshes*, to control the minimum interval between two refreshings. The default value is 60, but notice that, if you change *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs* to a small value, you need to make sure to also change *hbase.client.bootstrap.min\\_secs\\_between\\_refreshes* to a value smaller than *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*, otherwise an IllegalArgumentException will be thrown."},"482":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-86","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"(Advanced) In case of any issues with the rpc/master based registry, use the following\nconfiguration to fallback to the ZooKeeper based connection registry implementation."},"483":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-87","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#connection-uri","content":"Starting from 2.7.0, we add the support for specifying the connection information for a HBase cluster through an URI, which we call a \"connection URI\". And we've added several methods in *ConnectionFactory* to let you get a connection to the cluster specified by the URI. It looks like:"},"484":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-88","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"Currently there are two schemes supported, *hbase+rpc* for *RpcConnectionRegistry* and *hbase+zk* for *ZKConnectionRegistry*. *MasterRegistry* is deprecated so we do not expose it through connection URI."},"485":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-89","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"For *hbase+rpc*, it looks like"},"486":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-90","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"The authority part *server1:16020,server2:16020,server3:16020* specifies the bootstrap nodes and their rpc ports, i.e, the configuration value for *hbase.client.bootstrap.servers* in the past."},"487":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-91","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"For *hbase+zk*, it looks like"},"488":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-92","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#supported-schemes","content":"The authority part *zk1:2181,zk2:2181,zk3:2181* is the zk quorum, i.e, the configuration value for *hbase.zookeeper.quorum&#x2A; in the past. The path part */hbase* is the znode parent, i.e, the configuration value for *zookeeper.znode.parent* in the past."},"489":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-93","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#specify-configuration-through-uri-queries","content":"To let users fully specify the connection information through a connection URI, we support specifying configuration values through URI Queries. It looks like:"},"490":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-94","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#specify-configuration-through-uri-queries","content":"In this way you can set the operation timeout to 10 seconds. Notice that, the configuration values specified in the connection URI will override the ones in the configuration file."},"491":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-95","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"We use *ServiceLoader* to load different connection registry implementations, the entry point is *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.client.ConnectionRegistryURIFactory*. So if you implement your own *ConnectionRegistryURIFactory* which has a different scheme, and register it in the services file, we can load it at runtime."},"492":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-96","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"Connection URI is still a very new feature which has not been used extensively in production, so we do not want to expose the ability to customize *ConnectionRegistryURIFactory* yet as the API may be changed frequently in the beginning."},"493":{"id":"/docs/architecture/client-97","page_id":"/docs/architecture/client","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/client#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"If you really want to implement your own connection registry, you can use the above way but take your own risk."},"494":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"page","content":"Storing Medium-sized Objects (MOB)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob"},"495":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","content":"Optimized storage and handling of medium-sized objects (100KB-10MB) in HBase using the MOB feature for improved performance."},"496":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configuring-columns-for-mob","content":"Configuring Columns for MOB"},"497":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configure-a-column-for-mob-using-hbase-shell","content":"Configure a Column for MOB Using HBase Shell"},"498":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configure-a-column-for-mob-using-the-java-api","content":"Configure a Column for MOB Using the Java API"},"499":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#testing-mob","content":"Testing MOB"},"500":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-architecture","content":"MOB architecture"},"501":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"Overview"},"502":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-compaction","content":"MOB compaction"},"503":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"MOB file archiving"},"504":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-optimization-tasks","content":"MOB Optimization Tasks"},"505":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"Further limiting write amplification"},"506":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configuring-the-mob-cache","content":"Configuring the MOB Cache"},"507":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#example-mob-cache-configuration","content":"Example MOB Cache Configuration"},"508":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#manually-compacting-mob-files","content":"Manually Compacting MOB Files"},"509":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-troubleshooting","content":"MOB Troubleshooting"},"510":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"Adjusting the MOB cleaner's tolerance for new hfiles"},"511":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"Retrieving MOB metadata through the HBase Shell"},"512":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#moving-a-column-family-out-of-mob","content":"Moving a column family out of MOB"},"513":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#procedure-stop-mob-maintenance-change-mob-threshold-rewrite-data-via-compaction","content":"Procedure: Stop MOB maintenance, change MOB threshold, rewrite data via compaction"},"514":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#change-the-mob-size-threshold","content":"Change the MOB size threshold"},"515":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#perform-a-major-compaction-on-the-table","content":"Perform a major compaction on the table"},"516":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"Monitor for the end of the major compaction"},"517":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"Disable the MOB feature for the column family"},"518":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#clean-up-residual-mob-data","content":"Clean up residual MOB data"},"519":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#data-values-over-than-the-mob-threshold-show-up-stored-in-non-mob-hfiles","content":"Data values over than the MOB threshold show up stored in non-MOB hfiles"},"520":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-upgrade-considerations","content":"MOB Upgrade Considerations"},"521":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Upgrading to a version with the \"distributed MOB compaction\" feature"},"522":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","content":"Data comes in many sizes, and saving all of your data in HBase, including binary\ndata such as images and documents, is ideal. While HBase can technically handle\nbinary objects with cells that are larger than 100 KB in size, HBase's normal\nread and write paths are optimized for values smaller than 100KB in size. When\nHBase deals with large numbers of objects over this threshold, referred to here\nas medium objects, or MOBs, performance is degraded due to write amplification\ncaused by splits and compactions. When using MOBs, ideally your objects will be between\n100KB and 10MB (see the faq). HBase 2 added special internal handling of MOBs\nto maintain performance, consistency, and low operational overhead. MOB support is\nprovided by the work done in HBASE-11339.\nTo take advantage of MOB, you need to use HFile version 3. Optionally,\nconfigure the MOB file reader's cache settings for each RegionServer (see\nConfigure the MOB Cache), then configure specific columns to hold MOB data.\nClient code does not need to change to take advantage of HBase MOB support. The\nfeature is transparent to the client."},"523":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configuring-columns-for-mob","content":"You can configure columns to support MOB during table creation or alteration,\neither in HBase Shell or via the Java API. The two relevant properties are the\nboolean `IS_MOB` and the `MOB_THRESHOLD`, which is the number of bytes at which\nan object is considered to be a MOB. Only `IS_MOB` is required. If you do not\nspecify the `MOB_THRESHOLD`, the default threshold value of 100 KB is used."},"524":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#testing-mob","content":"The utility `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.IntegrationTestIngestWithMOB` is provided to assist with testing\nthe MOB feature. The utility is run as follows:"},"525":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#testing-mob","content":"**`threshold`** is the threshold at which cells are considered to be MOBs.\nThe default is 1 kB, expressed in bytes."},"526":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#testing-mob","content":"**`minMobDataSize`** is the minimum value for the size of MOB data.\nThe default is 512 B, expressed in bytes."},"527":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#testing-mob","content":"**`maxMobDataSize`** is the maximum value for the size of MOB data.\nThe default is 5 kB, expressed in bytes."},"528":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-architecture","content":"This section is derived from information found in\nHBASE-11339, which covered the initial GA\nimplementation of MOB in HBase and\nHBASE-22749, which improved things by\nparallelizing MOB maintenance across the RegionServers. For more information see\nthe last version of the design doc created during the initial work,\n\"HBASE-11339 MOB GA design.pdf\",\nand the design doc for the distributed mob compaction feature,\n\"HBASE-22749 MOB distributed compaction.pdf\"."},"529":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-34","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"The MOB feature reduces the overall IO load for configured column families by storing values that\nare larger than the configured threshold outside of the normal regions to avoid splits, merges, and\nmost importantly normal compactions."},"530":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-35","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"When a cell is first written to a region it is stored in the WAL and memstore regardless of value\nsize. When memstores from a column family configured to use MOB are eventually flushed two hfiles\nare written simultaneously. Cells with a value smaller than the threshold size are written to a\nnormal region hfile. Cells with a value larger than the threshold are written into a special MOB\nhfile and also have a MOB reference cell written into the normal region HFile. As the Region Server\nflushes a MOB enabled memstore and closes a given normal region HFile it appends metadata that lists\neach of the special MOB hfiles referenced by the cells within."},"531":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-36","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"MOB reference cells have the same key as the cell they are based on. The value of the reference cell\nis made up of two pieces of metadata: the size of the actual value and the MOB hfile that contains\nthe original cell. In addition to any tags originally written to HBase, the reference cell prepends\ntwo additional tags. The first is a marker tag that says the cell is a MOB reference. This can be\nused later to scan specifically just for reference cells. The second stores the namespace and table\nat the time the MOB hfile is written out. This tag is used to optimize how the MOB system finds\nthe underlying value in MOB hfiles after a series of HBase snapshot operations (ref HBASE-12332).\nNote that tags are only available within HBase servers and by default are not sent over RPCs."},"532":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-37","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"All MOB hfiles for a given table are managed within a logical region that does not directly serve\nrequests. When these MOB hfiles are created from a flush or MOB compaction they are placed in a\ndedicated mob data area under the hbase root directory specific to the namespace, table, mob\nlogical region, and column family. In general that means a path structured like:"},"533":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-38","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"With default configs, an example table named 'some\\_table' in the\ndefault namespace with a MOB enabled column family named 'foo' this HDFS directory would be"},"534":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"These MOB hfiles are maintained by special chores in the HBase Master and across the individual\nRegion Servers. Specifically those chores take care of enforcing TTLs and compacting them. Note that\nthis compaction is primarily a matter of controlling the total number of files in HDFS because our\noperational assumptions for MOB data is that it will seldom update or delete."},"535":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-40","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"When a given MOB hfile is no longer needed as a result of our compaction process then a chore in\nthe Master will take care of moving it to the archive just\nlike any normal hfile. Because the table's mob region is independent of all the normal regions it\ncan coexist with them in the regular archive storage area:"},"536":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-41","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#hbase-mob-overview","content":"The same hfile cleaning chores that take care of eventually deleting unneeded archived files from\nnormal regions thus also will take care of these MOB hfiles. As such, if there is a snapshot of a\nMOB enabled table then the cleaning system will make sure those MOB files stick around in the\narchive area as long as they are needed by a snapshot or a clone of a snapshot."},"537":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-42","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-compaction","content":"Each time the memstore for a MOB enabled column family performs a flush HBase will write values over\nthe MOB threshold into MOB specific hfiles. When normal region compaction occurs the Region Server\nrewrites the normal data files while maintaining references to these MOB files without rewriting\nthem. Normal client lookups for MOB values transparently will receive the original values because\nthe Region Server internals take care of using the reference data to then pull the value out of a\nspecific MOB file. This indirection means that building up a large number of MOB hfiles doesn't\nimpact the overall time to retrieve any specific MOB cell. Thus, we need not perform compactions of\nthe MOB hfiles nearly as often as normal hfiles. As a result, HBase saves IO by not rewriting MOB\nhfiles as a part of the periodic compactions a Region Server does on its own."},"538":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-compaction","content":"However, if deletes and updates of MOB cells are frequent then this indirection will begin to waste\nspace. The only way to stop using the space of a particular MOB hfile is to ensure no cells still\nhold references to it. To do that we need to ensure we have written the current values into a new\nMOB hfile. If our backing filesystem has a limitation on the number of files that can be present, as\nHDFS does, then even if we do not have deletes or updates of MOB cells eventually there will be a\nsufficient number of MOB hfiles that we will need to coalesce them."},"539":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-compaction","content":"Periodically a chore in the master coordinates having the region servers\nperform a special major compaction that also handles rewriting new MOB files. Like all compactions\nthe Region Server will create updated hfiles that hold both the cells that are smaller than the MOB\nthreshold and cells that hold references to the newly rewritten MOB file. Because this rewriting has\nthe advantage of looking across all active cells for the region our several small MOB files should\nend up as a single MOB file per region. The chore defaults to running weekly and can be\nconfigured by setting `hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` to the desired period in seconds."},"540":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-compaction","content":"By default, the periodic MOB compaction coordination chore will attempt to keep every region\nbusy doing compactions in parallel in order to maximize the amount of work done on the cluster.\nIf you need to tune the amount of IO this compaction generates on the underlying filesystem, you\ncan control how many concurrent region-level compaction requests are allowed by setting\n`hbase.mob.major.compaction.region.batch.size` to an integer number greater than zero. If you set\nthe configuration to 0 then you will get the default behavior of attempting to do all regions in\nparallel."},"541":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"Eventually we will have MOB hfiles that are no longer needed. Either clients will overwrite the\nvalue or a MOB-rewriting compaction will store a reference to a newer larger MOB hfile. Because any\ngiven MOB cell could have originally been written either in the current region or in a parent region\nthat existed at some prior point in time, individual Region Servers do not decide when it is time\nto archive MOB hfiles. Instead a periodic chore in the Master evaluates MOB hfiles for archiving."},"542":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"A MOB HFile will be subject to archiving under any of the following conditions:"},"543":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"Any MOB HFile older than the column family's TTL"},"544":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"Any MOB HFile older than a \"too recent\" threshold with no references to it from the regular hfiles\nfor all regions in a column family"},"545":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"To determine if a MOB HFile meets the second criteria the chore extracts metadata from the regular\nHFiles for each MOB enabled column family for a given table. That metadata enumerates the complete\nset of MOB HFiles needed to satisfy the references stored in the normal HFile area."},"546":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-file-archiving","content":"The period of the cleaner chore can be configured by setting `hbase.master.mob.cleaner.period` to a\npositive integer number of seconds. It defaults to running daily. You should not need to tune it\nunless you have a very aggressive TTL or a very high rate of MOB updates with a correspondingly\nhigh rate of non-MOB compactions."},"547":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"If your MOB workload has few to no updates or deletes then you can opt-in to MOB compactions that\noptimize for limiting the amount of write amplification. It achieves this by setting a\nsize threshold to ignore MOB files during the compaction process. When a given region goes\nthrough MOB compaction it will evaluate the size of the MOB file that currently holds the actual\nvalue and skip rewriting the value if that file is over threshold."},"548":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"The bound of write amplification in this mode can be approximated as\n\"Write Amplification\" = $\\log_{K}\\!\\left(\\frac{M}{S}\\right)$ where **K** is the number of files in compaction\nselection, **M** is the configurable threshold for MOB files size, and **S** is the minmum size of\nmemstore flushes that create MOB files in the first place. For example given 5 files picked up per\ncompaction, a threshold of 1 GB, and a flush size of 10MB the write amplification will be\n$\\log\\\\_{5}\\!\\left(\\frac{1\\,\\text{GB}}{10\\,\\text{MB}}\\right) = \\log\\\\_{5}(100) \\approx 2.86$."},"549":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"If we are using an underlying filesystem with a limitation on the number of files, such as HDFS,\nand we know our expected data set size we can choose our maximum file size in order to approach\nthis limit but stay within it in order to minimize write amplification. For example, if we expect to\nstore a petabyte and we have a conservative limitation of a million files in our HDFS instance, then\n$\\frac{1\\,\\text{PB}}{1\\,\\text{M}} = 1\\,\\text{GB}$ gives us a target limitation of a gigabyte per MOB file."},"550":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"To opt-in to this compaction mode you must set `hbase.mob.compaction.type` to `optimized`. The\ndefault MOB size threshold in this mode is set to 1GB. It can be changed by setting\n`hbase.mob.compactions.max.file.size` to a positive integer number of bytes."},"551":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"Additionally, when operating in this mode the compaction process will seek to avoid writing MOB\nfiles that are over the max file threshold. As it is writing out a additional MOB values into a MOB\nhfile it will check to see if the additional data causes the hfile to be over the max file size.\nWhen the hfile of MOB values reaches limit, the MOB hfile is committed to the MOB storage area and\na new one is created. The hfile with reference cells will track the complete set of MOB hfiles it\nneeds in its metadata."},"552":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"When using the write amplification optimized compaction mode you need to watch for the maximum\ntime to compact a single region. If it nears an hour you should read through the troubleshooting\nsection below Adjusting the MOB cleaner's tolerance for new\nhfiles. Failure\nto make the adjustments discussed there could lead to dataloss."},"553":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#configuring-the-mob-cache","content":"Because there can be a large number of MOB files at any time, as compared to the number of HFiles,\nMOB files are not always kept open. The MOB file reader cache is a LRU cache which keeps the most\nrecently used MOB files open. To configure the MOB file reader's cache on each RegionServer, add\nthe following properties to the RegionServer's `hbase-site.xml`, customize the configuration to\nsuit your environment, and restart or rolling restart the RegionServer."},"554":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#manually-compacting-mob-files","content":"To manually compact MOB files, rather than waiting for the\nperiodic chore to trigger compaction, use the\n`major_compact` HBase shell commands. These commands\nrequire the first argument to be the table name, and take a column\nfamily as the second argument. If used with a column family that includes MOB data, then\nthese operator requests will result in the MOB data being compacted."},"555":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#manually-compacting-mob-files","content":"This same request can be made via the `Admin.majorCompact` Java API."},"556":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"The MOB cleaner chore ignores all MOB hfiles that were created more recently than an hour prior to\nthe start of the chore to ensure we don't miss the reference metadata from the corresponding regular\nhfile. Without this safety check it would be possible for the cleaner chore to see a MOB hfile for\nan in progress flush or compaction and prematurely archive the MOB data. This default buffer should\nbe sufficient for normal use."},"557":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"You will need to adjust the tolerance if you use write amplification optimized MOB compaction and\nthe combination of your underlying filesystem performance and data shape is such that it could take\nmore than an hour to complete major compaction of a single region. For example, if your MOB data is\ndistributed such that your largest region adds 80GB of MOB data between compactions that include\nrewriting MOB data and your HDFS cluster is only capable of writing 20MB/s for a single file then\nwhen performing the optimized compaction the Region Server will take about a minute to write the\nfirst 1GB MOB hfile and then another hour and seven minutes to write the remaining seventy-nine 1GB\nMOB hfiles before finally committing the new reference hfile at the end of the compaction. Given\nthis example, you would need a larger tolerance window."},"558":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-63","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"You will also need to adjust the tolerance if Region Server flush operations take longer than an\nhour for the two HDFS move operations needed to commit both the MOB hfile and the normal hfile that\nreferences it. Such a delay should not happen with a normally configured and healthy HDFS and HBase."},"559":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-64","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"The cleaner's window for \"too recent\" is controlled by setting `hbase.mob.min.age.archive` to a\npositive integer number of milliseconds."},"560":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-65","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"While working on troubleshooting failures in the MOB system you can retrieve some of the internal\ninformation through the HBase shell by specifying special attributes on a scan."},"561":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-66","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"The MOB internal information is stored as four bytes for the size of the underlying cell value and\nthen a UTF8 string with the name of the MOB HFile that contains the underlying cell value. Note that\nby default the entirety of this serialized structure will be passed through the HBase shell's binary\nstring converter. That means the bytes that make up the value size will most likely be written as\nescaped non-printable byte values, e.g. '\\x03', unless they happen to correspond to ASCII\ncharacters."},"562":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-67","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"Let's look at a specific example:"},"563":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-68","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"In this case the first four bytes are `\\x00\\x02|\\x94` which corresponds to the bytes\n`[0x00, 0x02, 0x7C, 0x94]`. (Note that the third byte was printed as the ASCII character '|'.)\nDecoded as an integer this gives us an underlying value size of 162,964 bytes."},"564":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-69","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"The remaining bytes give us an HFile name,\n'd41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e19700118ffd9c244fe69488bbc9f2c77d24a3e6a'. This HFile will most\nlikely be stored in the designated MOB storage area for this specific table. However, the file could\nalso be in the archive area if this table is from a restored snapshot. Furthermore, if the table is\nfrom a cloned snapshot of a different table then the file could be in either the active or archive\narea of that source table. As mentioned in the explanation of MOB reference cells above, the Region\nServer will use a server side tag to optimize looking at the mob and archive area of the correct\noriginal table when finding the MOB HFile. Since your scan is client side it can't retrieve that tag\nand you'll either need to already know the lineage of your table or you'll need to search across all\ntables."},"565":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-70","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"Assuming you are authenticated as a user with HBase superuser rights, you can search for it:"},"566":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-71","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#moving-a-column-family-out-of-mob","content":"If you want to disable MOB on a column family you must ensure you instruct HBase to migrate the data\nout of the MOB system prior to turning the feature off. If you fail to do this HBase will return the\ninternal MOB metadata to applications because it will not know that it needs to resolve the actual\nvalues."},"567":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-72","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#moving-a-column-family-out-of-mob","content":"The following procedure will safely migrate the underlying data without requiring a cluster outage.\nClients will see a number of retries when configuration settings are applied and regions are\nreloaded."},"568":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-73","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#procedure-stop-mob-maintenance-change-mob-threshold-rewrite-data-via-compaction","content":"Ensure the MOB compaction chore in the Master is off by setting\n`hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` to `0`. Applying this configuration change will require a\nrolling restart of HBase Masters. That will require at least one fail-over of the active master,\nwhich may cause retries for clients doing HBase administrative operations."},"569":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-74","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#procedure-stop-mob-maintenance-change-mob-threshold-rewrite-data-via-compaction","content":"Ensure no MOB compactions are issued for the table via the HBase shell for the duration of this\nmigration."},"570":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-75","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#change-the-mob-size-threshold","content":"Use the HBase shell to change the MOB size threshold for the column family you are migrating to a\nvalue that is larger than the largest cell present in the column family. E.g. given a table named\n'some\\_table' and a column family named 'foo' we can pick one gigabyte as an arbitrary \"bigger than\nwhat we store\" value:"},"571":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-76","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#change-the-mob-size-threshold","content":"Note that if you are still ingesting data you must ensure this threshold is larger than any cell value you might write; MAX\\_INT would be a safe choice."},"572":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-77","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#perform-a-major-compaction-on-the-table","content":"Specifically you are performing a \"normal\" compaction and not a MOB compaction."},"573":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-78","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"Since compaction is handled asynchronously you'll need to use the shell to first see the compaction start and then see it end."},"574":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-79","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"HBase should first say that a \"MAJOR\" compaction is happening."},"575":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-80","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"When the compaction has finished the result should print out \"NONE\"."},"576":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-81","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"Run the *mobrefs* utility to ensure there are no MOB cells. Specifically, the tool will launch a\nHadoop MapReduce job that will show a job counter of 0 input records when we've successfully\nrewritten all of the data."},"577":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-82","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"If the data has not successfully been migrated out, this report will show both a non-zero number\nof input records and a count of mob cells."},"578":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-83","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"If this happens you should verify that MOB compactions are disabled, verify that you have picked a sufficiently large MOB threshold, and redo the major compaction step."},"579":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-84","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"When the *mobrefs* report shows that no more data is stored in the MOB system then you can safely alter the column family configuration so that the MOB feature is disabled."},"580":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-85","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"The MOB feature will be disabled on a column family only after altering the column family and performing a major compaction. Before performing the major compaction after altering the column family, the MOB cells will still be present in the MOB storage."},"581":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-86","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"After the column family no longer shows the MOB feature enabled, it is safe to start MOB maintenance chores again. You can allow the default to be used for `hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` by removing it from your configuration files or restore it to whatever custom value you had prior to starting this process."},"582":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-87","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#clean-up-residual-mob-data","content":"Once the MOB feature is disabled for the column family there will be no internal HBase process\nlooking for data in the MOB storage area specific to this column family. There will still be data\npresent there from prior to the compaction process that rewrote the values into HBase's data area.\nYou can check for this residual data directly in HDFS as an HBase superuser."},"583":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-88","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#clean-up-residual-mob-data","content":"This data is spurious and may be reclaimed. You should sideline it, verify your application's view of the table, and then delete it."},"584":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-89","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#data-values-over-than-the-mob-threshold-show-up-stored-in-non-mob-hfiles","content":"Bulk load and WAL split-to-HFile don't consider MOB threshold and write data into normal hfile (under /hbase/data directory)."},"585":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-90","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#data-values-over-than-the-mob-threshold-show-up-stored-in-non-mob-hfiles","content":"This won't cause any functional problem, during next compaction such data will be written out to\nthe MOB hfiles."},"586":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-91","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#mob-upgrade-considerations","content":"Generally, data stored using the MOB feature should transparently continue to work correctly across\nHBase upgrades."},"587":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-92","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Prior to the work in HBASE-22749, \"Distributed MOB compactions\", HBase had the Master coordinate all\ncompaction maintenance of the MOB hfiles. Centralizing management of the MOB data allowed for space\noptimizations but safely coordinating that management with Region Servers resulted in edge cases that\ncaused data loss (ref HBASE-22075)."},"588":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-93","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Users of the MOB feature upgrading to a version of HBase that includes HBASE-22749 should be aware\nof the following changes:"},"589":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-94","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer allows setting \"MOB Compaction Policies\""},"590":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-95","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer attempts to group MOB values by the date of the original cell's timestamp\naccording to said compaction policies, daily or otherwise"},"591":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-96","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer needs to track individual cell deletes through the use of special\nfiles in the MOB storage area with the suffix `_del`. After upgrading you should sideline these\nfiles."},"592":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-97","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Under default configuration the MOB system should take much less time to perform a compaction of\nMOB stored values. This is a direct consequence of the fact that HBase will place a much larger\nload on the underlying filesystem when doing compactions of MOB stored values; the additional load\nshould be a multiple on the order of magnitude of number of region servers. I.e. for a cluster\nwith three region servers and two masters the default configuration should have HBase put three\ntimes the load on HDFS during major compactions that rewrite MOB data when compared to Master\nhandled MOB compaction; it should also be approximately three times as fast."},"593":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-98","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"When the MOB system detects that a table has hfiles with references to MOB data but the reference\nhfiles do not yet have the needed file level metadata (i.e. from use of the MOB feature prior to\nHBASE-22749) then it will refuse to archive *any* MOB hfiles from that table. The normal course of\nperiodic compactions done by Region Servers will update existing hfiles with MOB references, but\nuntil a given table has been through the needed compactions operators should expect to see an\nincreased amount of storage used by the MOB feature."},"594":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-99","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Performing a compaction with type \"MOB\" no longer has special handling to compact specifically the\nMOB hfiles. Instead it will issue a warning and do a compaction of the table. For example using\nthe HBase shell as follows will result in a warning in the Master logs followed by a major\ncompaction of the 'example' table in its entirety or for the 'big' column respectively."},"595":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-100","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The same is true for directly using the Java API for `admin.majorCompact(TableName.valueOf(\"example\"), CompactType.MOB)`."},"596":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-101","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Similarly, manually performing a major compaction on a table or region will also handle compacting\nthe MOB stored values for that table or region respectively."},"597":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-102","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The following configuration setting has been deprecated and replaced:"},"598":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-103","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.master.mob.ttl.cleaner.period` has been replaced with `hbase.master.mob.cleaner.period`"},"599":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-104","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The following configuration settings are no longer used:"},"600":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-105","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.mergeable.threshold`"},"601":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-106","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.delfile.max.count`"},"602":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-107","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.batch.size`"},"603":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-108","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compactor.class`"},"604":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob-109","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hbase-mob#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.threads.max`"},"605":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","type":"page","content":"HDFS","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs"},"606":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","content":"How HBase leverages HDFS for distributed storage, including NameNode and DataNode architecture and file replication."},"607":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs#hdfs-namenode","content":"NameNode"},"608":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs#hdfs-datanode","content":"DataNode"},"609":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","content":"As HBase runs on HDFS (and each StoreFile is written as a file on HDFS), it is important to have an understanding of the HDFS Architecture especially in terms of how it stores files, handles failovers, and replicates blocks."},"610":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","content":"See the Hadoop documentation on HDFS Architecture for more information."},"611":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs#hdfs-namenode","content":"The NameNode is responsible for maintaining the filesystem metadata. See the above HDFS Architecture link for more information."},"612":{"id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/hdfs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/hdfs#hdfs-datanode","content":"The DataNodes are responsible for storing HDFS blocks. See the above HDFS Architecture link for more information."},"613":{"id":"/docs/architecture","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"page","content":"Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture"},"614":{"id":"/docs/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture","content":"Comprehensive guide to HBase architecture including client-server model, regions, WAL, compaction, and advanced features."},"615":{"id":"/docs/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture#architecture-resources","content":"Resources"},"616":{"id":"/docs/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#architecture-resources","content":"More information about the design and implementation can be found at the jira issue: HBASE-10070"},"617":{"id":"/docs/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture#architecture-resources","content":"HBaseCon 2014 talk: HBase Read High Availability Using Timeline-Consistent Region Replicas also contains some details and slides."},"618":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"page","content":"Master","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master"},"619":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master","content":"HBase Master server responsibilities including RegionServer monitoring, metadata operations, load balancing, and failover behavior."},"620":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#startup-behavior","content":"Startup Behavior"},"621":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#runtime-impact","content":"Runtime Impact"},"622":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#up-until-releases-2xy","content":"Up until releases 2.x.y"},"623":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"Staring release 3.0.0"},"624":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-interface","content":"Interface"},"625":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-processes","content":"Processes"},"626":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#loadbalancer","content":"LoadBalancer"},"627":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#catalogjanitor","content":"CatalogJanitor"},"628":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#masterprocwal","content":"MasterProcWAL"},"629":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"Configurations for MasterProcWAL"},"630":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master","content":"`HMaster` is the implementation of the Master Server. The Master server is responsible for monitoring all RegionServer instances in the cluster, and is the interface for all metadata changes. In a distributed cluster, the Master typically runs on the NameNode. J Mohamed Zahoor goes into some more detail on the Master Architecture in this blog posting, HBase HMaster Architecture."},"631":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#startup-behavior","content":"If run in a multi-Master environment, all Masters compete to run the cluster. If the active Master loses its lease in ZooKeeper (or the Master shuts down), then the remaining Masters jostle to take over the Master role."},"632":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#runtime-impact","content":"A common dist-list question involves what happens to an HBase cluster when the Master goes down. This information has changed starting 3.0.0."},"633":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#up-until-releases-2xy","content":"Because the HBase client talks directly to the RegionServers, the cluster can still function in a \"steady state\". Additionally, per Catalog Tables, `hbase:meta` exists as an HBase table and is not resident in the Master. However, the Master controls critical functions such as RegionServer failover and completing region splits. So while the cluster can still run for a short time without the Master, the Master should be restarted as soon as possible."},"634":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"As mentioned in section Master Registry (new as of 2.3.0), the default connection registry for clients is now based on master rpc end points. Hence the requirements for masters' uptime are even tighter starting this release."},"635":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"At least one active or stand by master is needed for a connection set up, unlike before when all the clients needed was a ZooKeeper ensemble."},"636":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"Master is now in critical path for read/write operations. For example, if the meta region bounces off to a different region server, clients need master to fetch the new locations. Earlier this was done by fetching this information directly from ZooKeeper."},"637":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"Masters will now have higher connection load than before. So, the server side configuration might need adjustment depending on the load."},"638":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#staring-release-300","content":"Overall, the master uptime requirements, when this feature is enabled, are even higher for the client operations to go through."},"639":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-interface","content":"The methods exposed by `HMasterInterface` are primarily metadata-oriented methods:"},"640":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-interface","content":"Table (createTable, modifyTable, removeTable, enable, disable)"},"641":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-interface","content":"ColumnFamily (addColumn, modifyColumn, removeColumn)"},"642":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-interface","content":"Region (move, assign, unassign) For example, when the `Admin` method `disableTable` is invoked, it is serviced by the Master server."},"643":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#architecture-master-processes","content":"The Master runs several background threads:"},"644":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#loadbalancer","content":"Periodically, and when there are no regions in transition, a load balancer will run and move regions around to balance the cluster's load. See Balancer for configuring this property."},"645":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#loadbalancer","content":"See Region-RegionServer Assignment for more information on region assignment."},"646":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#catalogjanitor","content":"Periodically checks and cleans up the `hbase:meta` table. See hbase:meta for more information on the meta table."},"647":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#masterprocwal","content":"*MasterProcWAL is replaced in hbase-2.3.0 by an alternate Procedure Store implementation; see in-master-procedure-store-region. This section pertains to hbase-2.0.0 through hbase-2.2.x*"},"648":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#masterprocwal","content":"HMaster records administrative operations and their running states, such as the handling of a crashed server, table creation, and other DDLs, into a Procedure Store. The Procedure Store WALs are stored under the MasterProcWALs directory. The Master WALs are not like RegionServer WALs. Keeping up the Master WAL allows us to run a state machine that is resilient across Master failures. For example, if a HMaster was in the middle of creating a table encounters an issue and fails, the next active HMaster can take up where the previous left off and carry the operation to completion. Since hbase-2.0.0, a new AssignmentManager (A.K.A AMv2) was introduced and the HMaster handles region assignment operations, server crash processing, balancing, etc., all via AMv2 persisting all state and transitions into MasterProcWALs rather than up into ZooKeeper, as we do in hbase-1.x."},"649":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#masterprocwal","content":"See AMv2 Description for Devs (and Procedure Framework (Pv2): HBASE-12439 for its basis) if you would like to learn more about the new AssignmentManager."},"650":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"Here are the list of configurations that effect MasterProcWAL operation. You should not have to change your defaults."},"651":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.periodic.roll.msec`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Frequency of generating a new WAL\\\n&#x2A;*Default:** `1h (3600000 in msec)`"},"652":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.roll.threshold`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Threshold in size before the WAL rolls. Every time the WAL reaches this size or the above period, 1 hour, passes since last log roll, the HMaster will generate a new WAL.\\\n&#x2A;*Default:** `32MB (33554432 in byte)`"},"653":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-34","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.warn.threshold`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** If the number of WALs goes beyond this threshold, the following message should appear in the HMaster log with WARN level when rolling."},"654":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-35","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `64`"},"655":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-36","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.max.retries.before.roll`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Max number of retry when syncing slots (records) to its underlying storage, such as HDFS. Every attempt, the following message should appear in the HMaster log."},"656":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-37","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `3`"},"657":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-38","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.sync.failure.roll.max`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** After the above 3 retrials, the log is rolled and the retry count is reset to 0, thereon a new set of retrial starts. This configuration controls the max number of attempts of log rolling upon sync failure. That is, HMaster is allowed to fail to sync 9 times in total. Once it exceeds, the following log should appear in the HMaster log."},"658":{"id":"/docs/architecture/master-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/master","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/master#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `3`"},"659":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/overview"},"660":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview","content":"Introduction to HBase as a NoSQL distributed database, key features, scalability characteristics, and when to use HBase."},"661":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"NoSQL?"},"662":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"When Should I Use HBase?"},"663":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/overview#what-is-the-difference-between-hbase-and-hadoophdfs","content":"What Is The Difference Between HBase and Hadoop/HDFS?"},"664":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"HBase is a type of \"NoSQL\" database. \"NoSQL\" is a general term meaning that the database isn't an RDBMS which supports SQL as its primary access language, but there are many types of NoSQL databases: BerkeleyDB is an example of a local NoSQL database, whereas HBase is very much a distributed database. Technically speaking, HBase is really more a \"Data Store\" than \"Data Base\" because it lacks many of the features you find in an RDBMS, such as typed columns, secondary indexes, triggers, and advanced query languages, etc."},"665":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"However, HBase has many features which supports both linear and modular scaling. HBase clusters expand by adding RegionServers that are hosted on commodity class servers. If a cluster expands from 10 to 20 RegionServers, for example, it doubles both in terms of storage and as well as processing capacity. An RDBMS can scale well, but only up to a point - specifically, the size of a single database server - and for the best performance requires specialized hardware and storage devices. HBase features of note are:"},"666":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Strongly consistent reads/writes: HBase is not an \"eventually consistent\" DataStore. This makes it very suitable for tasks such as high-speed counter aggregation."},"667":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Automatic sharding: HBase tables are distributed on the cluster via regions, and regions are automatically split and re-distributed as your data grows."},"668":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Automatic RegionServer failover"},"669":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Hadoop/HDFS Integration: HBase supports HDFS out of the box as its distributed file system."},"670":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"MapReduce: HBase supports massively parallelized processing via MapReduce for using HBase as both source and sink."},"671":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Java Client API: HBase supports an easy to use Java API for programmatic access."},"672":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Thrift/REST API: HBase also supports Thrift and REST for non-Java front-ends."},"673":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Block Cache and Bloom Filters: HBase supports a Block Cache and Bloom Filters for high volume query optimization."},"674":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#nosql","content":"Operational Management: HBase provides build-in web-pages for operational insight as well as JMX metrics."},"675":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"HBase isn't suitable for every problem."},"676":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"First, make sure you have enough data. If you have hundreds of millions or billions of rows, then HBase is a good candidate. If you only have a few thousand/million rows, then using a traditional RDBMS might be a better choice due to the fact that all of your data might wind up on a single node (or two) and the rest of the cluster may be sitting idle."},"677":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"Second, make sure you can live without all the extra features that an RDBMS provides (e.g., typed columns, secondary indexes, transactions, advanced query languages, etc.) An application built against an RDBMS cannot be \"ported\" to HBase by simply changing a JDBC driver, for example. Consider moving from an RDBMS to HBase as a complete redesign as opposed to a port."},"678":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"Third, make sure you have enough hardware. Even HDFS doesn't do well with anything less than 5 DataNodes (due to things such as HDFS block replication which has a default of 3), plus a NameNode."},"679":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"HBase can run quite well stand-alone on a laptop - but this should be considered a development configuration only."},"680":{"id":"/docs/architecture/overview-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/overview#what-is-the-difference-between-hbase-and-hadoophdfs","content":"HDFS is a distributed file system that is well suited for the storage of large files. Its documentation states that it is not, however, a general purpose file system, and does not provide fast individual record lookups in files. HBase, on the other hand, is built on top of HDFS and provides fast record lookups (and updates) for large tables. This can sometimes be a point of conceptual confusion. HBase internally puts your data in indexed \"StoreFiles\" that exist on HDFS for high-speed lookups. See the Data Model and the rest of this chapter for more information on how HBase achieves its goals."},"681":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"page","content":"Regions","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions"},"682":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions","content":"Understanding HBase regions, stores, memstore, write-ahead log (WAL), compaction, splits, and region management strategies."},"683":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#considerations-for-number-of-regions","content":"Considerations for Number of Regions"},"684":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"Why should I keep my Region count low?"},"685":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-assignment","content":"Region-RegionServer Assignment"},"686":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"Startup"},"687":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"Failover"},"688":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-load-balancing","content":"Region Load Balancing"},"689":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Region State Transition"},"690":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Region-RegionServer Locality"},"691":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-splits","content":"Region Splits"},"692":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#custom-split-policies","content":"Custom Split Policies"},"693":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-the-split-policy-globally-in-hbase-sitexml","content":"Configuring the Split Policy Globally in *hbase-site.xml*"},"694":{"id":"/docs/architecture/re