cs/architecture/regions-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"Minor Compaction File Selection - Example #2 (Not Enough Files ToCompact)"},"722":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-40","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"Minor Compaction File Selection - Example #3 (Limiting Files To Compact)"},"723":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-41","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#date-tiered-compaction","content":"Date Tiered Compaction"},"724":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-42","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"When To Use Date Tiered Compactions"},"725":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Enabling Date Tiered Compaction"},"726":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Configuring Date Tiered Compaction"},"727":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Experimental: Stripe Compactions"},"728":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"When To Use Stripe Compactions"},"729":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Enabling Stripe Compaction"},"730":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Configuring Stripe Compaction"},"731":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#fifo-compaction","content":"FIFO Compaction"},"732":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"When To Use FIFO Compaction"},"733":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"Enabling FIFO Compaction"},"734":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions","content":"Regions are the basic element of availability and distribution for tables, and are comprised of a Store per Column Family. The hierarchy of objects is as follows:"},"735":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions","content":"For a description of what HBase files look like when written to HDFS, see Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects."},"736":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#considerations-for-number-of-regions","content":"In general, HBase is designed to run with a small (20-200) number of relatively large (5-20Gb) regions per server. The considerations for this are as follows:"},"737":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"Typically you want to keep your region count low on HBase for numerous reasons. Usually right around 100 regions per RegionServer has yielded the best results. Here are some of the reasons below for keeping region count low:"},"738":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"MSLAB (MemStore-local allocation buffer) requires 2MB per MemStore (that's 2MB per family per region). 1000 regions that have 2 families each is 3.9GB of heap used, and it's not even storing data yet. NB: the 2MB value is configurable."},"739":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"If you fill all the regions at somewhat the same rate, the global memory usage makes it that it forces tiny flushes when you have too many regions which in turn generates compactions. Rewriting the same data tens of times is the last thing you want. An example is filling 1000 regions (with one family) equally and let's consider a lower bound for global MemStore usage of 5GB (the region server would have a big heap). Once it reaches 5GB it will force flush the biggest region, at that point they should almost all have about 5MB of data so it would flush that amount. 5MB inserted later, it would flush another region that will now have a bit over 5MB of data, and so on. This is currently the main limiting factor for the number of regions; see Number of regions per RS - upper bound for detailed formula."},"740":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"The master as is is allergic to tons of regions, and will take a lot of time assigning them and moving them around in batches. The reason is that it's heavy on ZK usage, and it's not very async at the moment (could really be improved — and has been improved a bunch in 0.96 HBase)."},"741":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"In older versions of HBase (pre-HFile v2, 0.90 and previous), tons of regions on a few RS can cause the store file index to rise, increasing heap usage and potentially creating memory pressure or OOME on the RSs"},"742":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"Another issue is the effect of the number of regions on MapReduce jobs; it is typical to have one mapper per HBase region. Thus, hosting only 5 regions per RS may not be enough to get sufficient number of tasks for a MapReduce job, while 1000 regions will generate far too many tasks."},"743":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"See Determining region count and size for configuration guidelines."},"744":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-assignment","content":"This section describes how Regions are assigned to RegionServers."},"745":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-63","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"When HBase starts regions are assigned as follows (short version):"},"746":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-64","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"The Master invokes the `AssignmentManager` upon startup."},"747":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-65","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"The `AssignmentManager` looks at the existing region assignments in `hbase:meta`."},"748":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-66","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"If the region assignment is still valid (i.e., if the RegionServer is still online) then the assignment is kept."},"749":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-67","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"If the assignment is invalid, then the `LoadBalancerFactory` is invoked to assign the region. The load balancer (`StochasticLoadBalancer` by default in HBase 1.0) assign the region to a RegionServer."},"750":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-68","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#startup","content":"`hbase:meta` is updated with the RegionServer assignment (if needed) and the RegionServer start codes (start time of the RegionServer process) upon region opening by the RegionServer."},"751":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-69","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"When a RegionServer fails:"},"752":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-70","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"The regions immediately become unavailable because the RegionServer is down."},"753":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-71","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"The Master will detect that the RegionServer has failed."},"754":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-72","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"The region assignments will be considered invalid and will be re-assigned just like the startup sequence."},"755":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-73","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"In-flight queries are re-tried, and not lost."},"756":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-74","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#failover","content":"Operations are switched to a new RegionServer within the following amount of time:"},"757":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-75","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-load-balancing","content":"Regions can be periodically moved by the LoadBalancer."},"758":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-76","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"HBase maintains a state for each region and persists the state in `hbase:meta`. The state of the `hbase:meta` region itself is persisted in ZooKeeper. You can see the states of regions in transition in the Master web UI. Following is the list of possible region states."},"759":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-77","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"**Possible Region States:**"},"760":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-78","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`OFFLINE`: the region is offline and not opening"},"761":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-79","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`OPENING`: the region is in the process of being opened"},"762":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-80","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`OPEN`: the region is open and the RegionServer has notified the master"},"763":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-81","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`FAILED_OPEN`: the RegionServer failed to open the region"},"764":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-82","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`CLOSING`: the region is in the process of being closed"},"765":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-83","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`CLOSED`: the RegionServer has closed the region and notified the master"},"766":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-84","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`FAILED_CLOSE`: the RegionServer failed to close the region"},"767":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-85","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLITTING`: the RegionServer notified the master that the region is splitting"},"768":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-86","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLIT`: the RegionServer notified the master that the region has finished splitting"},"769":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-87","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLITTING_NEW`: this region is being created by a split which is in progress"},"770":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-88","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGING`: the RegionServer notified the master that this region is being merged with another region"},"771":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-89","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGED`: the RegionServer notified the master that this region has been merged"},"772":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-90","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGING_NEW`: this region is being created by a merge of two regions"},"773":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-91","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"**Graph Legend:**"},"774":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-92","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Brown: Offline state, a special state that can be transient (after closed before opening), terminal (regions of disabled tables), or initial (regions of newly created tables)"},"775":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-93","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Palegreen: Online state that regions can serve requests"},"776":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-94","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Lightblue: Transient states"},"777":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-95","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Red: Failure states that need OPS attention"},"778":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-96","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Gold: Terminal states of regions split/merged"},"779":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-97","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Grey: Initial states of regions created through split/merge"},"780":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-98","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"**Transition State Descriptions:**"},"781":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-99","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"The master moves a region from `OFFLINE` to `OPENING` state and tries to assign the region to a RegionServer. The RegionServer may or may not have received the open region request. The master retries sending the open region request to the RegionServer until the RPC goes through or the master runs out of retries. After the RegionServer receives the open region request, the RegionServer begins opening the region."},"782":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-100","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the master is running out of retries, the master prevents the RegionServer from opening the region by moving the region to `CLOSING` state and trying to close it, even if the RegionServer is starting to open the region."},"783":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-101","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"After the RegionServer opens the region, it continues to try to notify the master until the master moves the region to `OPEN` state and notifies the RegionServer. The region is now open."},"784":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-102","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer cannot open the region, it notifies the master. The master moves the region to `CLOSED` state and tries to open the region on a different RegionServer."},"785":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-103","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the master cannot open the region on any of a certain number of regions, it moves the region to `FAILED_OPEN` state, and takes no further action until an operator intervenes from the HBase shell, or the server is dead."},"786":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-104","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"The master moves a region from `OPEN` to `CLOSING` state. The RegionServer holding the region may or may not have received the close region request. The master retries sending the close request to the server until the RPC goes through or the master runs out of retries."},"787":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-105","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer is not online, or throws `NotServingRegionException`, the master moves the region to `OFFLINE` state and re-assigns it to a different RegionServer."},"788":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-106","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer is online, but not reachable after the master runs out of retries, the master moves the region to `FAILED_CLOSE` state and takes no further action until an operator intervenes from the HBase shell, or the server is dead."},"789":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-107","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer gets the close region request, it closes the region and notifies the master. The master moves the region to `CLOSED` state and re-assigns it to a different RegionServer."},"790":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-108","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"Before assigning a region, the master moves the region to `OFFLINE` state automatically if it is in `CLOSED` state."},"791":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-109","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"When a RegionServer is about to split a region, it notifies the master. The master moves the region to be split from `OPEN` to `SPLITTING` state and add the two new regions to be created to the RegionServer. These two regions are in `SPLITTING_NEW` state initially."},"792":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-110","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"After notifying the master, the RegionServer starts to split the region. Once past the point of no return, the RegionServer notifies the master again so the master can update the `hbase:meta` table. However, the master does not update the region states until it is notified by the server that the split is done. If the split is successful, the splitting region is moved from `SPLITTING` to `SPLIT` state and the two new regions are moved from `SPLITTING_NEW` to `OPEN` state."},"793":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-111","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the split fails, the splitting region is moved from `SPLITTING` back to `OPEN` state, and the two new regions which were created are moved from `SPLITTING_NEW` to `OFFLINE` state."},"794":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-112","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"When a RegionServer is about to merge two regions, it notifies the master first. The master moves the two regions to be merged from `OPEN` to `MERGING` state, and adds the new region which will hold the contents of the merged regions region to the RegionServer. The new region is in `MERGING_NEW` state initially."},"795":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-113","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"After notifying the master, the RegionServer starts to merge the two regions. Once past the point of no return, the RegionServer notifies the master again so the master can update the META. However, the master does not update the region states until it is notified by the RegionServer that the merge has completed. If the merge is successful, the two merging regions are moved from `MERGING` to `MERGED` state and the new region is moved from `MERGING_NEW` to `OPEN` state."},"796":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-114","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"If the merge fails, the two merging regions are moved from `MERGING` back to `OPEN` state, and the new region which was created to hold the contents of the merged regions is moved from `MERGING_NEW` to `OFFLINE` state."},"797":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-115","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-state-transition","content":"For regions in `FAILED_OPEN` or `FAILED_CLOSE` states, the master tries to close them again when they are reassigned by an operator via HBase Shell."},"798":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-116","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Over time, Region-RegionServer locality is achieved via HDFS block replication. The HDFS client does the following by default when choosing locations to write replicas:"},"799":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-117","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"First replica is written to local node"},"800":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-118","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Second replica is written to a random node on another rack"},"801":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-119","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Third replica is written on the same rack as the second, but on a different node chosen randomly"},"802":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-120","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Subsequent replicas are written on random nodes on the cluster. See *Replica Placement: The First Baby Steps* on this page: HDFS Architecture"},"803":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-121","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Thus, HBase eventually achieves locality for a region after a flush or a compaction. In a RegionServer failover situation a RegionServer may be assigned regions with non-local StoreFiles (because none of the replicas are local), however as new data is written in the region, or the table is compacted and StoreFiles are re-written, they will become \"local\" to the RegionServer."},"804":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-122","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-regionserver-locality","content":"For more information, see *Replica Placement: The First Baby Steps* on this page: HDFS Architecture and also Lars George's blog on HBase and HDFS locality."},"805":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-123","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-splits","content":"Regions split when they reach a configured threshold. Below we treat the topic in short. For a longer exposition, see Apache HBase Region Splitting and Merging by our Enis Soztutar."},"806":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-124","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#region-splits","content":"Splits run unaided on the RegionServer; i.e. the Master does not participate. The RegionServer splits a region, offlines the split region and then adds the daughter regions to `hbase:meta`, opens daughters on the parent's hosting RegionServer and then reports the split to the Master. See Managed Splitting for how to manually manage splits (and for why you might do this)."},"807":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-125","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#custom-split-policies","content":"You can override the default split policy using a custom RegionSplitPolicy (HBase 0.94+). Typically a custom split policy should extend HBase's default split policy: IncreasingToUpperBoundRegionSplitPolicy."},"808":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-126","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#custom-split-policies","content":"The policy can set globally through the HBase configuration or on a per-table basis."},"809":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-127","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-the-split-policy-on-a-table-using-hbase-shell","content":"The policy can be set globally through the HBaseConfiguration used or on a per table basis:"},"810":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-128","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-the-split-policy-on-a-table-using-hbase-shell","content":"The `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` policy blocks manual region splitting."},"811":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-129","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"It is possible to manually split your table, either at table creation (pre-splitting), or at a later time as an administrative action. You might choose to split your region for one or more of the following reasons. There may be other valid reasons, but the need to manually split your table might also point to problems with your schema design."},"812":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-130","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"**Reasons to Manually Split Your Table:**"},"813":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-131","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"Your data is sorted by timeseries or another similar algorithm that sorts new data at the end of the table. This means that the Region Server holding the last region is always under load, and the other Region Servers are idle, or mostly idle. See also Monotonically Increasing Row Keys/Timeseries Data."},"814":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-132","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"You have developed an unexpected hotspot in one region of your table. For instance, an application which tracks web searches might be inundated by a lot of searches for a celebrity in the event of news about that celebrity. See perf.one.region for more discussion about this particular scenario."},"815":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-133","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"After a big increase in the number of RegionServers in your cluster, to get the load spread out quickly."},"816":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-134","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"Before a bulk-load which is likely to cause unusual and uneven load across regions."},"817":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-135","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"See Managed Splitting for a discussion about the dangers and possible benefits of managing splitting completely manually."},"818":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-136","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#manual-region-splitting","content":"The `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` policy blocks manual region splitting."},"819":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-137","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#determining-split-points","content":"The goal of splitting your table manually is to improve the chances of balancing the load across the cluster in situations where good rowkey design alone won't get you there. Keeping that in mind, the way you split your regions is very dependent upon the characteristics of your data. It may be that you already know the best way to split your table. If not, the way you split your table depends on what your keys are like."},"820":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-138","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#determining-split-points","content":"**Alphanumeric Rowkeys**\\\nIf your rowkeys start with a letter or number, you can split your table at letter or number boundaries. For instance, the following command creates a table with regions that split at each vowel, so the first region has A-D, the second region has E-H, the third region has I-N, the fourth region has O-V, and the fifth region has U-Z."},"821":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-139","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#determining-split-points","content":"**Using a Custom Algorithm**\\\nThe RegionSplitter tool is provided with HBase, and uses a *SplitAlgorithm* to determine split points for you. As parameters, you give it the algorithm, desired number of regions, and column families. It includes three split algorithms. The first is the `HexStringSplit` algorithm, which assumes the row keys are hexadecimal strings. The second is the `DecimalStringSplit` algorithm, which assumes the row keys are decimal strings in the range 00000000 to 99999999. The third, `UniformSplit`, assumes the row keys are random byte arrays. You will probably need to develop your own `SplitAlgorithm`, using the provided ones as models."},"822":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-140","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#online-region-merges","content":"Both Master and RegionServer participate in the event of online region merges. Client sends merge RPC to the master, then the master moves the regions together to the RegionServer where the more heavily loaded region resided. Finally the master sends the merge request to this RegionServer which then runs the merge. Similar to process of region splitting, region merges run as a local transaction on the RegionServer. It offlines the regions and then merges two regions on the file system, atomically delete merging regions from `hbase:meta` and adds the merged region to `hbase:meta`, opens the merged region on the RegionServer and reports the merge to the Master."},"823":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-141","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#online-region-merges","content":"An example of region merges in the HBase shell"},"824":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-142","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#online-region-merges","content":"It's an asynchronous operation and call returns immediately without waiting merge completed. Passing `true` as the optional third parameter will force a merge. Normally only adjacent regions can be merged. The `force` parameter overrides this behaviour and is for expert use only."},"825":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-143","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#store","content":"A Store hosts a MemStore and 0 or more StoreFiles (HFiles). A Store corresponds to a column family for a table for a given region."},"826":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-144","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#memstore","content":"The MemStore holds in-memory modifications to the Store. Modifications are Cells/KeyValues. When a flush is requested, the current MemStore is moved to a snapshot and is cleared. HBase continues to serve edits from the new MemStore and backing snapshot until the flusher reports that the flush succeeded. At this point, the snapshot is discarded. Note that when the flush happens, MemStores that belong to the same region will all be flushed."},"827":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-145","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#memstore-flush","content":"A MemStore flush can be triggered under any of the conditions listed below. The minimum flush unit is per region, not at individual MemStore level."},"828":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-146","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#memstore-flush","content":"When a MemStore reaches the size specified by `hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size`, all MemStores that belong to its region will be flushed out to disk."},"829":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-147","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#memstore-flush","content":"When the overall MemStore usage reaches the value specified by `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.upperLimit`, MemStores from various regions will be flushed out to disk to reduce overall MemStore usage in a RegionServer.\\\nThe flush order is based on the descending order of a region's MemStore usage. Regions will have their MemStores flushed until the overall MemStore usage drops to or slightly below `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.lowerLimit`."},"830":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-148","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#memstore-flush","content":"When the number of WAL log entries in a given region server's WAL reaches the value specified in `hbase.regionserver.max.logs`, MemStores from various regions will be flushed out to disk to reduce the number of logs in the WAL.\\\nThe flush order is based on time. Regions with the oldest MemStores are flushed first until WAL count drops below `hbase.regionserver.max.logs`."},"831":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-149","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"When a client issues a scan against a table, HBase generates `RegionScanner` objects, one per region, to serve the scan request."},"832":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-150","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"The `RegionScanner` object contains a list of `StoreScanner` objects, one per column family."},"833":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-151","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"Each `StoreScanner` object further contains a list of `StoreFileScanner` objects, corresponding to each StoreFile and HFile of the corresponding column family, and a list of `KeyValueScanner` objects for the MemStore."},"834":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-152","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"The two lists are merged into one, which is sorted in ascending order with the scan object for the MemStore at the end of the list."},"835":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-153","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"When a `StoreFileScanner` object is constructed, it is associated with a `MultiVersionConcurrencyControl` read point, which is the current `memstoreTS`, filtering out any new updates beyond the read point."},"836":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-154","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#storefile-hfile","content":"StoreFiles are where your data lives."},"837":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-155","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-hfile-format","content":"The *HFile* file format is based on the SSTable file described in the BigTable \\[2006] paper and on Hadoop's TFile (The unit test suite and the compression harness were taken directly from TFile). Schubert Zhang's blog post on HFile: A Block-Indexed File Format to Store Sorted Key-Value Pairs makes for a thorough introduction to HBase's HFile. Matteo Bertozzi has also put up a helpful description, HBase I/O: HFile."},"838":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-156","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-hfile-format","content":"For more information, see the HFile source code. Also see HBase file format with inline blocks (version 2) for information about the HFile v2 format that was included in 0.92."},"839":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-157","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"To view a textualized version of HFile content, you can use the `hbase hfile` tool. Type the following to see usage:"},"840":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-158","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"For example, to view the content of the file *hdfs\\://10.81.47.41:9000/hbase/default/TEST/1418428042/DSMP/4759508618286845475*, type the following:"},"841":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-159","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"If you leave off the option -v to see just a summary on the HFile. See usage for other things to do with the `hfile` tool."},"842":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-160","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"In the output of this tool, you might see 'seqid=0' for certain keys in places such as\n'Mid-key'/'firstKey'/'lastKey'. These are 'KeyOnlyKeyValue' type instances - meaning their seqid\nis irrelevant & we just need the keys of these Key-Value instances."},"843":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-161","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#storefile-directory-structure-on-hdfs","content":"For more information of what StoreFiles look like on HDFS with respect to the directory structure, see Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects."},"844":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-162","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#blocks","content":"StoreFiles are composed of blocks. The blocksize is configured on a per-ColumnFamily basis."},"845":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-163","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#blocks","content":"Compression happens at the block level within StoreFiles. For more information on compression, see Compression and Data Block Encoding In HBase."},"846":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-164","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#blocks","content":"For more information on blocks, see the HFileBlock source code."},"847":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-165","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"The KeyValue class is the heart of data storage in HBase. KeyValue wraps a byte array and takes offsets and lengths into the passed array which specify where to start interpreting the content as KeyValue."},"848":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-166","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"The KeyValue format inside a byte array is:"},"849":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-167","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"keylength"},"850":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-168","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"valuelength"},"851":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-169","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"key"},"852":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-170","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"value"},"853":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-171","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"The Key is further decomposed as:"},"854":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-172","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"rowlength"},"855":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-173","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"row (i.e., the rowkey)"},"856":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-174","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"columnfamilylength"},"857":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-175","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"columnfamily"},"858":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-176","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"columnqualifier"},"859":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-177","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"timestamp"},"860":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-178","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"keytype (e.g., Put, Delete, DeleteColumn, DeleteFamily)"},"861":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-179","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#keyvalue","content":"KeyValue instances are *not* split across blocks. For example, if there is an 8 MB KeyValue, even if the block-size is 64kb this KeyValue will be read in as a coherent block. For more information, see the KeyValue source code."},"862":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-180","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"To emphasize the points above, examine what happens with two Puts for two different columns for the same row:"},"863":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-181","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Put #1: `rowkey=row1, cf:attr1=value1`"},"864":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-182","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Put #2: `rowkey=row1, cf:attr2=value2`"},"865":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-183","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Even though these are for the same row, a KeyValue is created for each column:"},"866":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-184","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Key portion for Put #1:"},"867":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-185","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`rowlength -----------→ 4`"},"868":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-186","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`row -----------------→ row1`"},"869":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-187","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamilylength --→ 2`"},"870":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-188","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamily --------→ cf`"},"871":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-189","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnqualifier -----→ attr1`"},"872":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-190","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`timestamp -----------→ server time of Put`"},"873":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-191","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`keytype -------------→ Put`"},"874":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-192","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Key portion for Put #2:"},"875":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-193","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`rowlength -----------→ 4`"},"876":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-194","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`row -----------------→ row1`"},"877":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-195","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamilylength --→ 2`"},"878":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-196","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamily --------→ cf`"},"879":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-197","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnqualifier -----→ attr2`"},"880":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-198","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`timestamp -----------→ server time of Put`"},"881":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-199","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`keytype -------------→ Put`"},"882":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-200","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"It is critical to understand that the rowkey, ColumnFamily, and column (aka columnqualifier) are embedded within the KeyValue instance. The longer these identifiers are, the bigger the KeyValue is."},"883":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-201","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"**Ambiguous Terminology:**"},"884":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-202","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"A *StoreFile* is a facade of HFile. In terms of compaction, use of StoreFile seems to have prevailed in the past."},"885":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-203","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"A *Store* is the same thing as a ColumnFamily. StoreFiles are related to a Store, or ColumnFamily."},"886":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-204","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"If you want to read more about StoreFiles versus HFiles and Stores versus ColumnFamilies, see HBASE-11316."},"887":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-205","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"When the MemStore reaches a given size (`hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size`), it flushes its contents to a StoreFile. The number of StoreFiles in a Store increases over time. *Compaction* is an operation which reduces the number of StoreFiles in a Store, by merging them together, in order to increase performance on read operations. Compactions can be resource-intensive to perform, and can either help or hinder performance depending on many factors."},"888":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-206","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"Compactions fall into two categories: minor and major. Minor and major compactions differ in the following ways."},"889":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-207","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"*Minor compactions* usually select a small number of small, adjacent StoreFiles and rewrite them as a single StoreFile. Minor compactions do not drop (filter out) deletes or expired versions, because of potential side effects. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions. The end result of a minor compaction is fewer, larger StoreFiles for a given Store."},"890":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-208","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction","content":"The end result of a *major compaction* is a single StoreFile per Store. Major compactions also process delete markers and max versions. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions."},"891":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-209","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-and-deletions","content":"When an explicit deletion occurs in HBase, the data is not actually deleted. Instead, a *tombstone* marker is written. The tombstone marker prevents the data from being returned with queries. During a major compaction, the data is actually deleted, and the tombstone marker is removed from the StoreFile. If the deletion happens because of an expired TTL, no tombstone is created. Instead, the expired data is filtered out and is not written back to the compacted StoreFile."},"892":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-210","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-and-versions","content":"When you create a Column Family, you can specify the maximum number of versions to keep, by specifying `ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder.setMaxVersions(int versions)`. The default value is `1`. If more versions than the specified maximum exist, the excess versions are filtered out and not written back to the compacted StoreFile."},"893":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-211","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-and-versions","content":"In some situations, older versions can be inadvertently resurrected if a newer version is\nexplicitly deleted. See Major compactions change query\nresults for a more in-depth explanation.\nThis situation is only possible before the compaction finishes."},"894":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-212","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-and-versions","content":"In theory, major compactions improve performance. However, on a highly loaded system, major compactions can require an inappropriate number of resources and adversely affect performance. In a default configuration, major compactions are scheduled automatically to run once in a 7-day period. This is sometimes inappropriate for systems in production. You can manage major compactions manually. See Managed Compactions."},"895":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-213","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-and-versions","content":"Compactions do not perform region merges. See Merge for more information on region merging."},"896":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-214","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-switch","content":"We can switch on and off the compactions at region servers. Switching off compactions will also interrupt any currently ongoing compactions. It can be done dynamically using the \"compaction\\_switch\" command from hbase shell. If done from the command line, this setting will be lost on restart of the server. To persist the changes across region servers modify the configuration hbase.regionserver .compaction.enabled in hbase-site.xml and restart HBase."},"897":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-215","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Compacting large StoreFiles, or too many StoreFiles at once, can cause more IO load than your cluster is able to handle without causing performance problems. The method by which HBase selects which StoreFiles to include in a compaction (and whether the compaction is a minor or major compaction) is called the *compaction policy*."},"898":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-216","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Prior to HBase 0.96.x, there was only one compaction policy. That original compaction policy is still available as `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy`. The new compaction default policy, called `ExploringCompactionPolicy`, was subsequently backported to HBase 0.94 and HBase 0.95, and is the default in HBase 0.96 and newer. It was implemented in HBASE-7842. In short, `ExploringCompactionPolicy` attempts to select the best possible set of StoreFiles to compact with the least amount of work, while the `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` selects the first set that meets the criteria."},"899":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-217","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Regardless of the compaction policy used, file selection is controlled by several configurable parameters and happens in a multi-step approach. These parameters will be explained in context, and then will be given in a table which shows their descriptions, defaults, and implications of changing them."},"900":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-218","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#being-stuck","content":"When the MemStore gets too large, it needs to flush its contents to a StoreFile. However, Stores are configured with a bound on the number StoreFiles, `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles`, and if in excess, the MemStore flush must wait until the StoreFile count is reduced by one or more compactions. If the MemStore is too large and the number of StoreFiles is also too high, the algorithm is said to be \"stuck\". By default we'll wait on compactions up to `hbase.hstore.blockingWaitTime` milliseconds. If this period expires, we'll flush anyways even though we are in excess of the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` count."},"901":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-219","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#being-stuck","content":"Upping the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` count will allow flushes to happen but a Store with many StoreFiles in will likely have higher read latencies. Try to figure why Compactions are not keeping up. Is it a write spurt that is bringing about this situation or is a regular occurance and the cluster is under-provisioned for the volume of writes?"},"902":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-220","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The ExploringCompactionPolicy algorithm considers each possible set of adjacent StoreFiles before choosing the set where compaction will have the most benefit."},"903":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-221","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"One situation where the ExploringCompactionPolicy works especially well is when you are bulk-loading data and the bulk loads create larger StoreFiles than the StoreFiles which are holding data older than the bulk-loaded data. This can \"trick\" HBase into choosing to perform a major compaction each time a compaction is needed, and cause a lot of extra overhead. With the ExploringCompactionPolicy, major compactions happen much less frequently because minor compactions are more efficient."},"904":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-222","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"In general, ExploringCompactionPolicy is the right choice for most situations, and thus is the default compaction policy. You can also use ExploringCompactionPolicy along with Experimental: Stripe Compactions."},"905":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-223","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The logic of this policy can be examined in hbase-server/src/main/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/regionserver/compactions/ExploringCompactionPolicy.java. The following is a walk-through of the logic of the ExploringCompactionPolicy."},"906":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-224","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Make a list of all existing StoreFiles in the Store. The rest of the algorithm filters this list to come up with the subset of HFiles which will be chosen for compaction."},"907":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-225","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If this was a user-requested compaction, attempt to perform the requested compaction type, regardless of what would normally be chosen. Note that even if the user requests a major compaction, it may not be possible to perform a major compaction. This may be because not all StoreFiles in the Column Family are available to compact or because there are too many Stores in the Column Family."},"908":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-226","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Some StoreFiles are automatically excluded from consideration. These include:"},"909":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-227","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that are larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`"},"910":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-228","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that were created by a bulk-load operation which explicitly excluded compaction. You may decide to exclude StoreFiles resulting from bulk loads, from compaction. To do this, specify the `hbase.mapreduce.hfileoutputformat.compaction.exclude` parameter during the bulk load operation."},"911":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-229","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Iterate through the list from step 1, and make a list of all potential sets of StoreFiles to compact together. A potential set is a grouping of `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` contiguous StoreFiles in the list. For each set, perform some sanity-checking and figure out whether this is the best compaction that could be done:"},"912":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-230","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the number of StoreFiles in this set (not the size of the StoreFiles) is fewer than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` or more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`, take it out of consideration."},"913":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-231","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Compare the size of this set of StoreFiles with the size of the smallest possible compaction that has been found in the list so far. If the size of this set of StoreFiles represents the smallest compaction that could be done, store it to be used as a fall-back if the algorithm is \"stuck\" and no StoreFiles would otherwise be chosen. See Being Stuck."},"914":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-232","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Do size-based sanity checks against each StoreFile in this set of StoreFiles."},"915":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-233","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the size of this StoreFile is larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`, take it out of consideration."},"916":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-234","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the size is greater than or equal to `hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size`, sanity-check it against the file-based ratio to see whether it is too large to be considered."},"917":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-235","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The sanity-checking is successful if:"},"918":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-236","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"There is only one StoreFile in this set, or"},"919":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-237","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"For each StoreFile, its size multiplied by `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` (or `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak` if off-peak hours are configured and it is during off-peak hours) is less than the sum of the sizes of the other HFiles in the set."},"920":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-238","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If this set of StoreFiles is still in consideration, compare it to the previously-selected best compaction. If it is better, replace the previously-selected best compaction with this one."},"921":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-239","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"When the entire list of potential compactions has been processed, perform the best compaction that was found. If no StoreFiles were selected for compaction, but there are multiple StoreFiles, assume the algorithm is stuck (see Being Stuck) and if so, perform the smallest compaction that was found in step 3."},"922":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-240","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The RatioBasedCompactionPolicy was the only compaction policy prior to HBase 0.96, though ExploringCompactionPolicy has now been backported to HBase 0.94 and 0.95. To use the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy rather than the ExploringCompactionPolicy, set `hbase.hstore.defaultengine.compactionpolicy.class` to `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` in the *hbase-site.xml* file. To switch back to the ExploringCompactionPolicy, remove the setting from the *hbase-site.xml*."},"923":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-241","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The following section walks you through the algorithm used to select StoreFiles for compaction in the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy."},"924":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-242","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The first phase is to create a list of all candidates for compaction. A list is created of all StoreFiles not already in the compaction queue, and all StoreFiles newer than the newest file that is currently being compacted. This list of StoreFiles is ordered by the sequence ID. The sequence ID is generated when a Put is appended to the write-ahead log (WAL), and is stored in the metadata of the HFile."},"925":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-243","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Check to see if the algorithm is stuck (see Being Stuck, and if so, a major compaction is forced. This is a key area where The ExploringCompactionPolicy Algorithm is often a better choice than the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy."},"926":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-244","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the compaction was user-requested, try to perform the type of compaction that was requested. Note that a major compaction may not be possible if all HFiles are not available for compaction or if too many StoreFiles exist (more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`)."},"927":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-245","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Some StoreFiles are automatically excluded from consideration. These include:"},"928":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-246","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that are larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`"},"929":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-247","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that were created by a bulk-load operation which explicitly excluded compaction. You may decide to exclude StoreFiles resulting from bulk loads, from compaction. To do this, specify the `hbase.mapreduce.hfileoutputformat.compaction.exclude` parameter during the bulk load operation."},"930":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-248","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The maximum number of StoreFiles allowed in a major compaction is controlled by the `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` parameter. If the list contains more than this number of StoreFiles, a minor compaction is performed even if a major compaction would otherwise have been done. However, a user-requested major compaction still occurs even if there are more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` StoreFiles to compact."},"931":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-249","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the list contains fewer than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` StoreFiles to compact, a minor compaction is aborted. Note that a major compaction can be performed on a single HFile. Its function is to remove deletes and expired versions, and reset locality on the StoreFile."},"932":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-250","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The value of the `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` parameter is multiplied by the sum of StoreFiles smaller than a given file, to determine whether that StoreFile is selected for compaction during a minor compaction. For instance, if hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio is 1.2, FileX is 5MB, FileY is 2MB, and FileZ is 3MB:"},"933":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-251","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"In this scenario, FileX is eligible for minor compaction. If FileX were 7MB, it would not be eligible for minor compaction. This ratio favors smaller StoreFile. You can configure a different ratio for use in off-peak hours, using the parameter `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak`, if you also configure `hbase.offpeak.start.hour` and `hbase.offpeak.end.hour`."},"934":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-252","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the last major compaction was too long ago and there is more than one StoreFile to be compacted, a major compaction is run, even if it would otherwise have been minor. By default, the maximum time between major compactions is 7 days, plus or minus a 4.8 hour period, and determined randomly within those parameters. Prior to HBase 0.96, the major compaction period was 24 hours. See `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction` in the table below to tune or disable time-based major compactions."},"935":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-253","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"This table contains the main configuration parameters for compaction. This list is not exhaustive. To tune these parameters from the defaults, edit the *hbase-default.xml* file. For a full list of all configuration parameters available, see config.files"},"936":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-254","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min`\\\nThe minimum number of StoreFiles which must be eligible for compaction before compaction can run. The goal of tuning `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` is to avoid ending up with too many tiny StoreFiles to compact. Setting this value to 2 would cause a minor compaction each time you have two StoreFiles in a Store, and this is probably not appropriate. If you set this value too high, all the other values will need to be adjusted accordingly. For most cases, the default value is appropriate. In previous versions of HBase, the parameter `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` was called `hbase.hstore.compactionThreshold`.\\\n**Default**: 3"},"937":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-255","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max`\nThe maximum number of StoreFiles which will be selected for a single minor compaction, regardless of the number of eligible StoreFiles. Effectively, the value of `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` controls the length of time it takes a single compaction to complete. Setting it larger means that more StoreFiles are included in a compaction. For most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: 10"},"938":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-256","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size`\\\nA StoreFile smaller than this size will always be eligible for minor compaction. StoreFiles this size or larger are evaluated by `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` to determine if they are eligible. Because this limit represents the \"automatic include\" limit for all StoreFiles smaller than this value, this value may need to be reduced in write-heavy environments where many files in the 1-2 MB range are being flushed, because every StoreFile will be targeted for compaction and the resulting StoreFiles may still be under the minimum size and require further compaction. If this parameter is lowered, the ratio check is triggered more quickly. This addressed some issues seen in earlier versions of HBase but changing this parameter is no longer necessary in most situations.\\\n**Default**:128 MB"},"939":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-257","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`\\\nA StoreFile larger than this size will be excluded from compaction. The effect of raising `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` is fewer, larger StoreFiles that do not get compacted often. If you feel that compaction is happening too often without much benefit, you can try raising this value.\\\n**Default**: `Long.MAX_VALUE`"},"940":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-258","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`\\\nFor minor compaction, this ratio is used to determine whether a given StoreFile which is larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` is eligible for compaction. Its effect is to limit compaction of large StoreFile. The value of `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` is expressed as a floating-point decimal."},"941":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-259","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"A large ratio, such as 10, will produce a single giant StoreFile. Conversely, a value of .25, will produce behavior similar to the BigTable compaction algorithm, producing four StoreFiles."},"942":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-260","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"A moderate value of between 1.0 and 1.4 is recommended. When tuning this value, you are balancing write costs with read costs. Raising the value (to something like 1.4) will have more write costs, because you will compact larger StoreFiles. However, during reads, HBase will need to seek through fewer StoreFiles to accomplish the read. Consider this approach if you cannot take advantage of Bloom Filters."},"943":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-261","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"Alternatively, you can lower this value to something like 1.0 to reduce the background cost of writes, and use to limit the number of StoreFiles touched during reads. For most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: `1.2F`"},"944":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-262","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak`\\\nThe compaction ratio used during off-peak compactions, if off-peak hours are also configured (see below). Expressed as a floating-point decimal. This allows for more aggressive (or less aggressive, if you set it lower than `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`) compaction during a set time period. Ignored if off-peak is disabled (default). This works the same as `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`.\\\n**Default**: `5.0F`"},"945":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-263","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.offpeak.start.hour`\\\nThe start of off-peak hours, expressed as an integer between 0 and 23, inclusive. Set to -1 to disable off-peak.\\\n**Default**: `-1` (disabled)"},"946":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-264","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.offpeak.end.hour`\\\nThe end of off-peak hours, expressed as an integer between 0 and 23, inclusive. Set to -1 to disable off-peak.\\\n**Default**: `-1` (disabled)"},"947":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-265","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.regionserver.thread.compaction.throttle`\\\nThere are two different thread pools for compactions, one for large compactions and the other for small compactions. This helps to keep compaction of lean tables (such as `hbase:meta`) fast. If a compaction is larger than this threshold, it goes into the large compaction pool. In most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: `2 x hbase.hstore.compaction.max x hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size` (which defaults to `128`)"},"948":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-266","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hregion.majorcompaction`\\\nTime between major compactions, expressed in milliseconds. Set to 0 to disable time-based automatic major compactions. User-requested and size-based major compactions will still run. This value is multiplied by `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction.jitter` to cause compaction to start at a somewhat-random time during a given window of time.\\\n**Default**: 7 days (`604800000` milliseconds)"},"949":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-267","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hregion.majorcompaction.jitter`\\\nA multiplier applied to hbase.hregion.majorcompaction to cause compaction to occur a given amount of time either side of `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction`. The smaller the number, the closer the compactions will happen to the `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction` interval. Expressed as a floating-point decimal.\\\n**Default**: `.50F`"},"950":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-268","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"This section has been preserved for historical reasons and refers to the way compaction worked\nprior to HBase 0.96.x. You can still use this behavior if you enable RatioBasedCompactionPolicy\nAlgorithm. For information on\nthe way that compactions work in HBase 0.96.x and later, see\nCompaction."},"951":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-269","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"To understand the core algorithm for StoreFile selection, there is some ASCII-art in the Store source code that will serve as useful reference."},"952":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-270","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"It has been copied below:"},"953":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-271","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"**Important knobs:**"},"954":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-272","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` Ratio used in compaction file selection algorithm (default 1.2f)."},"955":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-273","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` (in HBase v 0.90 this is called `hbase.hstore.compactionThreshold`) (files) Minimum number of StoreFiles per Store to be selected for a compaction to occur (default 2)."},"956":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-274","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` (files) Maximum number of StoreFiles to compact per minor compaction (default 10)."},"957":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-275","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` (bytes) Any StoreFile smaller than this setting with automatically be a candidate for compaction. Defaults to `hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size` (128 mb)."},"958":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-276","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` (.92) (bytes) Any StoreFile larger than this setting with automatically be excluded from compaction (default Long.MAX\\_VALUE)."},"959":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-277","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#compaction-file-selection","content":"The minor compaction StoreFile selection logic is size based, and selects a file for compaction when the `file ⇐ sum(smaller_files) * hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`."},"960":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-278","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"961":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-279","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"962":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-280","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"963":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-281","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"964":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-282","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"965":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-283","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"966":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-284","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 100, 50, 23, 12, and 12 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, the files that would be selected for minor compaction are 23, 12, and 12."},"967":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-285","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"Why?"},"968":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-286","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"100 → No, because sum(50, 23, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 97."},"969":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-287","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"50 → No, because sum(23, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 47."},"970":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-288","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"23 → Yes, because sum(12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 24."},"971":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-289","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"12 → Yes, because the previous file has been included, and because this does not exceed the max-file limit of 5"},"972":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-290","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"12 → Yes, because the previous file had been included, and because this does not exceed the max-file limit of 5."},"973":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-291","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"974":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-292","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"975":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-293","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"976":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-294","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"977":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-295","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"978":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-296","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"979":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-297","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 100, 25, 12, and 12 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, no compaction will be started."},"980":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-298","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"Why?"},"981":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-299","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"100 → No, because sum(25, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 47"},"982":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-300","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"25 → No, because sum(12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 24"},"983":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-301","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"12 → No. Candidate because sum(12) \\* 1.0 = 12, there are only 2 files to compact and that is less than the threshold of 3"},"984":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-302","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"12 → No. Candidate because the previous StoreFile was, but there are not enough files to compact"},"985":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-303","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"986":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-304","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"987":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-305","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"988":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-306","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"989":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-307","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"990":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-308","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"991":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-309","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, and 1 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, the files that would be selected for minor compaction are 7, 6, 5, 4, 3."},"992":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-310","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"Why?"},"993":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-311","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"7 → Yes, because sum(6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 21. Also, 7 is less than the min-size"},"994":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-312","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"6 → Yes, because sum(5, 4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 15. Also, 6 is less than the min-size."},"995":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-313","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"5 → Yes, because sum(4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 10. Also, 5 is less than the min-size."},"996":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-314","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"4 → Yes, because sum(3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 6. Also, 4 is less than the min-size."},"997":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-315","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"3 → Yes, because sum(2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 3. Also, 3 is less than the min-size."},"998":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-316","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"2 → No. Candidate because previous file was selected and 2 is less than the min-size, but the max-number of files to compact has been reached."},"999":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-317","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"1 → No. Candidate because previous file was selected and 1 is less than the min-size, but max-number of files to compact has been reached."},"1000":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-318","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"This information is now included in the configuration parameter table in Parameters Used by\nCompaction Algorithm."},"1001":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-319","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#date-tiered-compaction","content":"Date tiered compaction is a date-aware store file compaction strategy that is beneficial for time-range scans for time-series data."},"1002":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-320","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Consider using Date Tiered Compaction for reads for limited time ranges, especially scans of recent data"},"1003":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-321","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Don't use it for"},"1004":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-322","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"random gets without a limited time range"},"1005":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-323","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"frequent deletes and updates"},"1006":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-324","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Frequent out of order data writes creating long tails, especially writes with future timestamps"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-325","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"frequent bulk loads with heavily overlapping time ranges"},"1008":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-326","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"**Performance Improvements**\\\nPerformance testing has shown that the performance of time-range scans improve greatly for limited time ranges, especially scans of recent data."},"1009":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-327","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You can enable Date Tiered compaction for a table or a column family, by setting its `hbase.hstore.engine.class` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DateTieredStoreEngine`."},"1010":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-328","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You also need to set `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to a high number, such as 60, if using all default settings, rather than the default value of 12). Use 1.5\\~2 x projected file count if changing the parameters, Projected file count = windows per tier x tier count + incoming window min + files older than max age"},"1011":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-329","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You also need to set `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` to the same value as `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to unblock major compaction."},"1012":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-330","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Enable Date Tiered Compaction**"},"1013":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-331","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Run one of following commands in the HBase shell. Replace the table name `orders_table` with the name of your table."},"1014":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-332","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Configure other options if needed. See Configuring Date Tiered Compaction for more information."},"1015":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-333","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Disable Date Tiered Compaction**"},"1016":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-334","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Set the `hbase.hstore.engine.class` option to either nil or `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DefaultStoreEngine`. Either option has the same effect. Make sure you set the other options you changed to the original settings too."},"1017":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-335","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"When you change the store engine either way, a major compaction will likely be performed on most regions. This is not necessary on new tables."},"1018":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-336","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Each of the settings for date tiered compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-337","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Data Tier Parameters**\\\nYou can configure your date tiers by changing the settings for the following parameters:"},"1020":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-338","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Setting"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-339","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Notes"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-340","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.max.storefile.age.millis`"},"1023":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-341","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Files with max-timestamp smaller than this will no longer be compacted.Default at Long.MAX\\_VALUE."},"1024":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-342","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.base.window.millis`"},"1025":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-343","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Base window size in milliseconds. Default at 6 hours."},"1026":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-344","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.windows.per.tier`"},"1027":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-345","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Number of windows per tier. Default at 4."},"1028":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-346","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.incoming.window.min`"},"1029":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-347","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Minimal number of files to compact in the incoming window. Set it to expected number of files in the window to avoid wasteful compaction. Default at 6."},"1030":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-348","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.window.policy.class`"},"1031":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-349","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"The policy to select store files within the same time window. It doesn't apply to the incoming window. Default at exploring compaction. This is to avoid wasteful compaction."},"1032":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-350","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Compaction Throttler**\\\nWith tiered compaction all servers in the cluster will promote windows to higher tier at the same time, so using a compaction throttle is recommended: Set `hbase.regionserver.throughput.controller` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.compactions.PressureAwareCompactionThroughputController`."},"1033":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-351","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"For more information about date tiered compaction, please refer to the design specification at\nhttps\\://docs.google.com/document/d/1\\_AmlNb2N8Us1xICsTeGDLKIqL6T-oHoRLZ323MG\\_uy8"},"1034":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-352","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compactions is an experimental feature added in HBase 0.98 which aims to improve compactions for large regions or non-uniformly distributed row keys. In order to achieve smaller and/or more granular compactions, the StoreFiles within a region are maintained separately for several row-key sub-ranges, or \"stripes\", of the region. The stripes are transparent to the rest of HBase, so other operations on the HFiles or data work without modification."},"1035":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-353","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compactions change the HFile layout, creating sub-regions within regions. These sub-regions are easier to compact, and should result in fewer major compactions. This approach alleviates some of the challenges of larger regions."},"1036":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-354","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compaction is fully compatible with Compaction and works in conjunction with either the ExploringCompactionPolicy or RatioBasedCompactionPolicy. It can be enabled for existing tables, and the table will continue to operate normally if it is disabled later."},"1037":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-355","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Consider using stripe compaction if you have either of the following:"},"1038":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-356","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Large regions. You can get the positive effects of smaller regions without additional overhead for MemStore and region management overhead."},"1039":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-357","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Non-uniform keys, such as time dimension in a key. Only the stripes receiving the new keys will need to compact. Old data will not compact as often, if at all"},"1040":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-358","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"**Performance Improvements**\\\nPerformance testing has shown that the performance of reads improves somewhat, and variability of performance of reads and writes is greatly reduced. An overall long-term performance improvement is seen on large non-uniform-row key regions, such as a hash-prefixed timestamp key. These performance gains are the most dramatic on a table which is already large. It is possible that the performance improvement might extend to region splits."},"1041":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-359","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"You can enable stripe compaction for a table or a column family, by setting its `hbase.hstore.engine.class` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.StripeStoreEngine`. You also need to set the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to a high number, such as 100 (rather than the default value of 10)."},"1042":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-360","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Enable Stripe Compaction**"},"1043":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-361","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Run one of following commands in the HBase shell. Replace the table name `orders_table` with the name of your table."},"1044":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-362","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Configure other options if needed. See Configuring Stripe Compaction for more information."},"1045":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-363","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Enable the table."},"1046":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-364","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Disable Stripe Compaction**"},"1047":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-365","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Set the `hbase.hstore.engine.class` option to either nil or `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DefaultStoreEngine`. Either option has the same effect."},"1048":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-366","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Enable the table."},"1049":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-367","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"When you enable a large table after changing the store engine either way, a major compaction will likely be performed on most regions. This is not necessary on new tables."},"1050":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-368","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Each of the settings for stripe compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"},"1051":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-369","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Region and stripe sizing**\\\nYou can configure your stripe sizing based upon your region sizing. By default, your new regions will start with one stripe. On the next compaction after the stripe has grown too large (16 x MemStore flushes size), it is split into two stripes. Stripe splitting continues as the region grows, until the region is large enough to split."},"1052":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-370","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"You can improve this pattern for your own data. A good rule is to aim for a stripe size of at least 1 GB, and about 8-12 stripes for uniform row keys. For example, if your regions are 30 GB, 12 x 2.5 GB stripes might be a good starting point."},"1053":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-371","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Stripe Sizing Settings**"},"1054":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-372","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Setting"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-373","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Notes"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-374","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.initialStripeCount`"},"1057":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-375","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The number of stripes to create when stripe compaction is enabled. You can use it as follows:- For relatively uniform row keys, if you know the approximate target number of stripes from the above, you can avoid some splitting overhead by starting with several stripes (2, 5, 10...). If the early data is not representative of overall row key distribution, this will not be as efficient.- For existing tables with a large amount of data, this setting will effectively pre-split your stripes.- For keys such as hash-prefixed sequential keys, with more than one hash prefix per region, pre-splitting may make sense."},"1058":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-376","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.sizeToSplit`"},"1059":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-377","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The maximum size a stripe grows before splitting. Use this in conjunction with `hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount` to control the target stripe size (`sizeToSplit = splitPartsCount * target stripe size`), according to the above sizing considerations."},"1060":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-378","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount`"},"1061":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-379","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The number of new stripes to create when splitting a stripe. The default is 2, which is appropriate for most cases. For non-uniform row keys, you can experiment with increasing the number to 3 or 4, to isolate the arriving updates into narrower slice of the region without additional splits being required."},"1062":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-380","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**MemStore Size Settings**\\\nBy default, the flush creates several files from one MemStore, according to existing stripe boundaries and row keys to flush. This approach minimizes write amplification, but can be undesirable if the MemStore is small and there are many stripes, because the files will be too small.\\\nIn this type of situation, you can set `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.flushToL0` to `true`. This will cause a MemStore flush to create a single file instead. When at least `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFilesL0` such files (by default, 4) accumulate, they will be compacted into striped files."},"1063":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-381","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Normal Compaction Configuration and Stripe Compaction**\\\nAll the settings that apply to normal compactions (see Parameters Used by Compaction Algorithm) apply to stripe compactions. The exceptions are the minimum and maximum number of files, which are set to higher values by default because the files in stripes are smaller. To control these for stripe compactions, use `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFiles` and `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.maxFiles`, rather than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` and `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`."},"1064":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-382","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#fifo-compaction","content":"FIFO compaction policy selects only files which have all cells expired. The column family **MUST** have non-default TTL. Essentially, FIFO compactor only collects expired store files."},"1065":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-383","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#fifo-compaction","content":"Because we don't do any real compaction, we do not use CPU and IO (disk and network) and evict hot data from a block cache. As a result, both RW throughput and latency can be improved."},"1066":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-384","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Consider using FIFO Compaction when your use case is"},"1067":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-385","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Very high volume raw data which has low TTL and which is the source of another data (after additional processing)."},"1068":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-386","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Data which can be kept entirely in a a block cache (RAM/SSD). No need for compaction of a raw data at all."},"1069":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-387","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Do not use FIFO compaction when"},"1070":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-388","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Table/ColumnFamily has MIN\\_VERSION > 0"},"1071":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-389","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Table/ColumnFamily has TTL = FOREVER (HColumnDescriptor.DEFAULT\\_TTL)"},"1072":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-390","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"For Table:"},"1073":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-391","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"For Column Family:"},"1074":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-392","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"From HBase Shell:"},"1075":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regions-393","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regions","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regions#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"Although region splitting is still supported, for optimal performance it should be disabled, either by setting explicitly `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` or by setting `ConstantSizeRegionSplitPolicy` and very large max region size. You will have to increase to a very large number store's blocking file (`hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles`) as well. There is a sanity check on table/column family configuration in case of FIFO compaction and minimum value for number of blocking file is 1000."},"1076":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"page","content":"RegionServer","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver"},"1077":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","content":"HBase RegionServer implementation, interfaces, read/write paths, block cache, memstore management, and performance tuning."},"1078":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"Interface"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-processes","content":"Processes"},"1080":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#compactsplitthread","content":"CompactSplitThread"},"1081":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#majorcompactionchecker","content":"MajorCompactionChecker"},"1082":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#memstoreflusher","content":"MemStoreFlusher"},"1083":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architectur