ng","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-wal-splitting-based-on-procedurev2-performance","content":"Performance [!toc]"},"1124":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Enable this feature [!toc]"},"1125":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-compression","content":"WAL Compression"},"1126":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"Durability"},"1127":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"Custom WAL Directory"},"1128":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#disabling-the-wal","content":"Disabling the WAL"},"1129":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","content":"`HRegionServer` is the RegionServer implementation. It is responsible for serving and managing regions. In a distributed cluster, a RegionServer runs on a DataNode."},"1130":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"The methods exposed by `HRegionRegionInterface` contain both data-oriented and region-maintenance methods:"},"1131":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"Data (get, put, delete, next, etc.)"},"1132":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"Region (splitRegion, compactRegion, etc.) For example, when the `Admin` method `majorCompact` is invoked on a table, the client is actually iterating through all regions for the specified table and requesting a major compaction directly to each region."},"1133":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-processes","content":"The RegionServer runs a variety of background threads:"},"1134":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#compactsplitthread","content":"Checks for splits and handle minor compactions."},"1135":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#majorcompactionchecker","content":"Checks for major compactions."},"1136":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#memstoreflusher","content":"Periodically flushes in-memory writes in the MemStore to StoreFiles."},"1137":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#logroller","content":"Periodically checks the RegionServer's WAL."},"1138":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#coprocessors","content":"Coprocessors were added in 0.92. There is a thorough Blog Overview of CoProcessors posted. Documentation will eventually move to this reference guide, but the blog is the most current information available at this time."},"1139":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-block-cache","content":"HBase provides two different BlockCache implementations to cache data read from HDFS: the default on-heap `LruBlockCache` and the `BucketCache`, which is (usually) off-heap. This section discusses benefits and drawbacks of each implementation, how to choose the appropriate option, and configuration options for each."},"1140":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-63","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-block-cache","content":"See the RegionServer UI for detail on caching deploy. See configurations, sizings, current usage,\ntime-in-the-cache, and even detail on block counts and types."},"1141":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-64","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-choices","content":"`LruBlockCache` is the original implementation, and is entirely within the Java heap. `BucketCache` is optional and mainly intended for keeping block cache data off-heap, although `BucketCache` can also be a file-backed cache. In file-backed we can either use it in the file mode or the mmaped mode. We also have pmem mode where the bucket cache resides on the persistent memory device."},"1142":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-65","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-choices","content":"When you enable BucketCache, you are enabling a two tier caching system. We used to describe the tiers as \"L1\" and \"L2\" but have deprecated this terminology as of hbase-2.0.0. The \"L1\" cache referred to an instance of LruBlockCache and \"L2\" to an off-heap BucketCache. Instead, when BucketCache is enabled, all DATA blocks are kept in the BucketCache tier and meta blocks — INDEX and BLOOM blocks — are on-heap in the `LruBlockCache`. Management of these two tiers and the policy that dictates how blocks move between them is done by `CombinedBlockCache`."},"1143":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-66","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#general-cache-configurations","content":"Apart from the cache implementation itself, you can set some general configuration options to control how the cache performs. See CacheConfig. After setting any of these options, restart or rolling restart your cluster for the configuration to take effect. Check logs for errors or unexpected behavior."},"1144":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-67","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#general-cache-configurations","content":"See also Prefetch Option for Blockcache, which discusses a new option introduced in HBASE-9857."},"1145":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-68","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"The LruBlockCache is an LRU cache that contains three levels of block priority to allow for scan-resistance and in-memory ColumnFamilies:"},"1146":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-69","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"Single access priority: The first time a block is loaded from HDFS it normally has this priority and it will be part of the first group to be considered during evictions. The advantage is that scanned blocks are more likely to get evicted than blocks that are getting more usage."},"1147":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-70","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"Multi access priority: If a block in the previous priority group is accessed again, it upgrades to this priority. It is thus part of the second group considered during evictions."},"1148":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-71","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"In-memory access priority: If the block's family was configured to be \"in-memory\", it will be part of this priority disregarding the number of times it was accessed. Catalog tables are configured like this. This group is the last one considered during evictions.\\\nTo mark a column family as in-memory, call"},"1149":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-72","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"if creating a table from java, or set `IN_MEMORY ⇒ true` when creating or altering a table in the shell: e.g."},"1150":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-73","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-design","content":"For more information, see the LruBlockCache source"},"1151":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-74","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Block caching is enabled by default for all the user tables which means that any read operation will load the LRU cache. This might be good for a large number of use cases, but further tunings are usually required in order to achieve better performance. An important concept is the working set size, or WSS, which is: \"the amount of memory needed to compute the answer to a problem\". For a website, this would be the data that's needed to answer the queries over a short amount of time."},"1152":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-75","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The way to calculate how much memory is available in HBase for caching is:"},"1153":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-76","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The default value for the block cache is 0.4 which represents 40% of the available heap. The last value (99%) is the default acceptable loading factor in the LRU cache after which eviction is started. The reason it is included in this equation is that it would be unrealistic to say that it is possible to use 100% of the available memory since this would make the process blocking from the point where it loads new blocks. Here are some examples:"},"1154":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-77","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"One region server with the heap size set to 1 GB and the default block cache size will have 405 MB of block cache available."},"1155":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-78","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"20 region servers with the heap size set to 8 GB and a default block cache size will have 63.3 GB of block cache."},"1156":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-79","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"100 region servers with the heap size set to 24 GB and a block cache size of 0.5 will have about 1.16 TB of block cache."},"1157":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-80","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Your data is not the only resident of the block cache. Here are others that you may have to take into account:"},"1158":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-81","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Catalog Tables**\\\nThe `hbase:meta` table is forced into the block cache and have the in-memory priority which means that they are harder to evict."},"1159":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-82","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The hbase:meta tables can occupy a few MBs depending on the number of regions."},"1160":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-83","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**HFiles Indexes**\\\nAn *HFile* is the file format that HBase uses to store data in HDFS. It contains a multi-layered index which allows HBase to seek the data without having to read the whole file. The size of those indexes is a factor of the block size (64KB by default), the size of your keys and the amount of data you are storing. For big data sets it's not unusual to see numbers around 1GB per region server, although not all of it will be in cache because the LRU will evict indexes that aren't used."},"1161":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-84","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Keys**\\\nThe values that are stored are only half the picture, since each value is stored along with its keys (row key, family qualifier, and timestamp). See Try to minimize row and column sizes."},"1162":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-85","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Bloom Filters**\\\nJust like the HFile indexes, those data structures (when enabled) are stored in the LRU."},"1163":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-86","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Currently the recommended way to measure HFile indexes and bloom filters sizes is to look at the region server web UI and checkout the relevant metrics. For keys, sampling can be done by using the HFile command line tool and look for the average key size metric. Since HBase 0.98.3, you can view details on BlockCache stats and metrics in a special Block Cache section in the UI. As of HBase 2.4.14, you can estimate HFile indexes and bloom filters vs other DATA blocks using blockCacheCount and blockCacheDataBlockCount in JMX. The formula `(blockCacheCount - blockCacheDataBlockCount) * blockSize` will give you an estimate which can be useful when trying to enable the BucketCache. You should make sure the post-BucketCache config gives enough memory to the on-heap LRU cache to hold at least the same number of non-DATA blocks from pre-BucketCache. Once BucketCache is enabled, the L1 metrics like l1CacheSize, l1CacheCount, and l1CacheEvictionCount can help you further tune the size."},"1164":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-87","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"It's generally bad to use block caching when the WSS doesn't fit in memory. This is the case when you have for example 40GB available across all your region servers' block caches but you need to process 1TB of data. One of the reasons is that the churn generated by the evictions will trigger more garbage collections unnecessarily. Here are two use cases:"},"1165":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-88","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Fully random reading pattern: This is a case where you almost never access the same row twice within a short amount of time such that the chance of hitting a cached block is close to 0. Setting block caching on such a table is a waste of memory and CPU cycles, more so that it will generate more garbage to pick up by the JVM. For more information on monitoring GC, see JVM Garbage Collection Logs."},"1166":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-89","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Mapping a table: In a typical MapReduce job that takes a table in input, every row will be read only once so there's no need to put them into the block cache. The Scan object has the option of turning this off via the setCacheBlocks method (set it to false). You can still keep block caching turned on on this table if you need fast random read access. An example would be counting the number of rows in a table that serves live traffic, caching every block of that table would create massive churn and would surely evict data that's currently in use."},"1167":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-90","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#caching-meta-blocks-only-data-blocks-in-fscache","content":"An interesting setup is one where we cache META blocks only and we read DATA blocks in on each access. If the DATA blocks fit inside fscache, this alternative may make sense when access is completely random across a very large dataset. To enable this setup, alter your table and for each column family set `BLOCKCACHE ⇒ 'false'`. You are 'disabling' the BlockCache for this column family only. You can never disable the caching of META blocks. Since HBASE-4683 Always cache index and bloom blocks, we will cache META blocks even if the BlockCache is disabled."},"1168":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-91","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"The usual deployment of BucketCache is via a managing class that sets up two caching tiers: an on-heap cache implemented by LruBlockCache and a second cache implemented with BucketCache. The managing class is CombinedBlockCache by default. The previous link describes the caching 'policy' implemented by CombinedBlockCache. In short, it works by keeping meta blocks — INDEX and BLOOM in the on-heap LruBlockCache tier — and DATA blocks are kept in the BucketCache tier."},"1169":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-92","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"**Pre-hbase-2.0.0 versions**"},"1170":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-93","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"Fetching will always be slower when fetching from BucketCache in pre-hbase-2.0.0, as compared to the native on-heap LruBlockCache. However, latencies tend to be less erratic across time, because there is less garbage collection when you use BucketCache since it is managing BlockCache allocations, not the GC. If the BucketCache is deployed in off-heap mode, this memory is not managed by the GC at all. This is why you'd use BucketCache in pre-2.0.0, so your latencies are less erratic, to mitigate GCs and heap fragmentation, and so you can safely use more memory. See Nick Dimiduk's BlockCache 101 for comparisons running on-heap vs off-heap tests. Also see Comparing BlockCache Deploys which finds that if your dataset fits inside your LruBlockCache deploy, use it otherwise if you are experiencing cache churn (or you want your cache to exist beyond the vagaries of java GC), use BucketCache."},"1171":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-94","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"In pre-2.0.0, one can configure the BucketCache so it receives the `victim` of an LruBlockCache eviction. All Data and index blocks are cached in L1 first. When eviction happens from L1, the blocks (or `victims`) will get moved to L2. Set `cacheDataInL1` via `(HColumnDescriptor.setCacheDataInL1(true)` or in the shell, creating or amending column families setting `CACHE_DATA_IN_L1` to true: e.g."},"1172":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-95","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"**hbase-2.0.0+ versions**"},"1173":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-96","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"HBASE-11425 changed the HBase read path so it could hold the read-data off-heap avoiding copying of cached data on to the java heap. See Offheap read-path. In hbase-2.0.0, off-heap latencies approach those of on-heap cache latencies with the added benefit of NOT provoking GC."},"1174":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-97","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"From HBase 2.0.0 onwards, the notions of L1 and L2 have been deprecated. When BucketCache is turned on, the DATA blocks will always go to BucketCache and INDEX/BLOOM blocks go to on heap LRUBlockCache. `cacheDataInL1` support has been removed."},"1175":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-98","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"The BucketCache Block Cache can be deployed *offheap*, *file* or *mmaped* file mode."},"1176":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-99","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"You set which via the `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` setting. Setting it to `offheap` will have BucketCache make its allocations off-heap, and an ioengine setting of `file:PATH_TO_FILE` will direct BucketCache to use file caching (Useful in particular if you have some fast I/O attached to the box such as SSDs). From 2.0.0, it is possible to have more than one file backing the BucketCache. This is very useful especially when the Cache size requirement is high. For multiple backing files, configure ioengine as `files:PATH_TO_FILE1,PATH_TO_FILE2,PATH_TO_FILE3`. BucketCache can be configured to use an mmapped file also. Configure ioengine as `mmap:PATH_TO_FILE` for this."},"1177":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-100","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"It is possible to deploy a tiered setup where we bypass the CombinedBlockCache policy and have BucketCache working as a strict L2 cache to the L1 LruBlockCache. For such a setup, set `hbase.bucketcache.combinedcache.enabled` to `false`. In this mode, on eviction from L1, blocks go to L2. When a block is cached, it is cached first in L1. When we go to look for a cached block, we look first in L1 and if none found, then search L2. Let us call this deploy format, *Raw L1+L2*. NOTE: This L1+L2 mode is removed from 2.0.0. When BucketCache is used, it will be strictly the DATA cache and the LruBlockCache will cache INDEX/META blocks."},"1178":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-101","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"Other BucketCache configs include: specifying a location to persist cache to across restarts, how many threads to use writing the cache, etc. See the CacheConfig.html class for configuration options and descriptions."},"1179":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-102","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"To check it enabled, look for the log line describing cache setup; it will detail how BucketCache has been deployed. Also see the UI. It will detail the cache tiering and their configuration."},"1180":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-103","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"This sample provides a configuration for a 4 GB off-heap BucketCache with a 1 GB on-heap cache."},"1181":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-104","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Configuration is performed on the RegionServer."},"1182":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-105","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Setting `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` and `hbase.bucketcache.size` > 0 enables `CombinedBlockCache`. Let us presume that the RegionServer has been set to run with a 5G heap: i.e. `HBASE_HEAPSIZE=5g`."},"1183":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-106","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"First, edit the RegionServer's *hbase-env.sh* and set `HBASE_OFFHEAPSIZE` to a value greater than the off-heap size wanted, in this case, 4 GB (expressed as 4G). Let's set it to 5G. That'll be 4G for our off-heap cache and 1G for any other uses of off-heap memory (there are other users of off-heap memory other than BlockCache; e.g. DFSClient in RegionServer can make use of off-heap memory). See Direct Memory Usage In HBase below."},"1184":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-107","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Next, add the following configuration to the RegionServer's *hbase-site.xml*."},"1185":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-108","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Restart or rolling restart your cluster, and check the logs for any issues."},"1186":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-109","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"In the above, we set the BucketCache to be 4G. We configured the on-heap LruBlockCache have 20% (0.2) of the RegionServer's heap size (0.2 \\* 5G = 1G). In other words, you configure the L1 LruBlockCache as you would normally (as if there were no L2 cache present)."},"1187":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-110","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"HBASE-10641 introduced the ability to configure multiple sizes for the buckets of the BucketCache, in HBase 0.98 and newer. To configurable multiple bucket sizes, configure the new property `hbase.bucketcache.bucket.sizes` to a comma-separated list of block sizes, ordered from smallest to largest, with no spaces. The goal is to optimize the bucket sizes based on your data access patterns. The following example configures buckets of size 4096 and 8192."},"1188":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-111","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"The default maximum direct memory varies by JVM. Traditionally it is 64M or some relation to allocated heap size (-Xmx) or no limit at all (JDK7 apparently). HBase servers use direct memory, in particular short-circuit reading (See Leveraging local data), the hosted DFSClient will allocate direct memory buffers. How much the DFSClient uses is not easy to quantify; it is the number of open HFiles \\* `hbase.dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size` where `hbase.dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size` is set to 128k in HBase — see *hbase-default.xml* default configurations. If you do off-heap block caching, you'll be making use of direct memory. The RPCServer uses a ByteBuffer pool. From 2.0.0, these buffers are off-heap ByteBuffers. Starting your JVM, make sure the `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize` setting in *conf/hbase-env.sh* considers off-heap BlockCache (`hbase.bucketcache.size`), DFSClient usage, RPC side ByteBufferPool max size. This has to be bit higher than sum of off heap BlockCache size and max ByteBufferPool size. Allocating an extra of 1-2 GB for the max direct memory size has worked in tests. Direct memory, which is part of the Java process heap, is separate from the object heap allocated by -Xmx. The value allocated by `MaxDirectMemorySize` must not exceed physical RAM, and is likely to be less than the total available RAM due to other memory requirements and system constraints."},"1189":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-112","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"You can see how much memory — on-heap and off-heap/direct — a RegionServer is configured to use and how much it is using at any one time by looking at the *Server Metrics: Memory* tab in the UI. It can also be gotten via JMX. In particular the direct memory currently used by the server can be found on the `java.nio.type=BufferPool,name=direct` bean. Terracotta has a good write up on using off-heap memory in Java. It is for their product BigMemory but a lot of the issues noted apply in general to any attempt at going off-heap. Check it out."},"1190":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-113","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"This is a pre-HBase 1.0 configuration removed because it was confusing. It was a float that you would set to some value between 0.0 and 1.0. Its default was 0.9. If the deploy was using CombinedBlockCache, then the LruBlockCache L1 size was calculated to be `(1 - hbase.bucketcache.percentage.in.combinedcache) * size-of-bucketcache` and the BucketCache size was `hbase.bucketcache.percentage.in.combinedcache * size-of-bucket-cache`. where size-of-bucket-cache itself is EITHER the value of the configuration `hbase.bucketcache.size` IF it was specified as Megabytes OR `hbase.bucketcache.size` \\* `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize` if `hbase.bucketcache.size` is between 0 and 1.0."},"1191":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-114","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"In 1.0, it should be more straight-forward. Onheap LruBlockCache size is set as a fraction of java heap using `hfile.block.cache.size setting` (not the best name) and BucketCache is set as above in absolute Megabytes."},"1192":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-115","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"HBASE-28463 introduced time based priority for blocks in BucketCache. It allows for defining an age threshold at individual column families' configuration, whereby blocks older than this configured threshold would be targeted first for eviction."},"1193":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-116","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Blocks from column families that don't define the age threshold wouldn't be evaluated by the time based priority, and would only be evicted following the LRU eviction logic."},"1194":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-117","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"This feature is mostly useful for use cases where most recent data is more frequently accessed, and therefore should get higher priority in the cache. Configuring Time Based Priority with the \"age\" of most accessed data would then give a finer control over blocks allocation in the BucketCache than the built-in LRU eviction logic."},"1195":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-118","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Time Based Priority for BucketCache provides three different strategies for defining data age:"},"1196":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-119","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Cell timestamps: Uses the timestamp portion of HBase cells for comparing the data age."},"1197":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-120","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Custom cell qualifiers: Uses a custom-defined date qualifier for comparing the data age. It uses that value to tier the entire row containing the given qualifier value. This requires that the custom qualifier be a valid Java long timestamp."},"1198":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-121","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Custom value provider: Allows for defining a pluggable implementation that contains the logic for identifying the date value to be used for comparison. This also provides additional flexibility for different use cases that might have the date stored in other formats or embedded with other data in various portions of a given row."},"1199":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-122","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"For use cases where priority is determined by the order of record ingestion in HBase (with the most recent being the most relevant), the built-in cell timestamp offers the most convenient and efficient method for configuring age-based priority. See Using Cell timestamps for Time Based Priority."},"1200":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-123","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Some applications may utilize a custom date column to define the priority of table records. In such instances, a custom cell qualifier-based priority is advisable. See Using Custom Cell Qualifiers for Time Based Priority."},"1201":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-124","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Finally, more intricate schemas may incorporate domain-specific logic for defining the age of each record. The custom value provider facilitates the integration of custom code to implement the appropriate parsing of the date value that should be used for the priority comparison. See Using a Custom value provider for Time Based Priority."},"1202":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-125","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"With Time Based Priority for BucketCache, blocks age is evaluated when deciding if a block should be cached (i.e. during reads, writes, compaction and prefetch), as well as during the cache freeSpace run (mass eviction), prior to executing the LRU logic."},"1203":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-126","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Because blocks don't hold any specific meta information other than type, it's necessary to group blocks of the same \"age group\" on separate files, using specialized compaction implementations (see more details in the configuration section below). The time range of all blocks in each file is then appended at the file meta info section, and is used for evaluating the age of blocks that should be considered in the Time Based Priority logic."},"1204":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-127","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Finding the age of each block involves an extra overhead, therefore the feature is disabled by default at a global configuration level."},"1205":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-128","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"To enable it, the following configuration should be set on RegionServers' *hbase-site.xml*:"},"1206":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-129","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Once enabled globally, it's necessary to define the desired strategy-specific settings at the individual column family level."},"1207":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-130","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"This strategy is the most efficient to run, as it uses the timestamp portion of each cell containing the data for comparing the age of blocks. It requires DateTieredCompaction for splitting the blocks into separate files according to blocks' ages."},"1208":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-131","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"The example below sets the hot age threshold to one week (in milliseconds) for the column family 'cf1' in table 'orders':"},"1209":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-132","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"In the example above, the properties governing the number of windows and period of each window in the date tiered compaction were not set. With the default settings, the compaction will create initially four windows of six hours, then four windows of one day each, then another four windows of four days each and so on until the minimum timestamp among the selected files is covered. This can create a large number of files, therefore, additional changes to the 'hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles', 'hbase.hstore.compaction.min' and 'hbase.hstore.compaction.max' are recommended."},"1210":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-133","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"Alternatively, consider adjusting the initial window size to the same as the hot age threshold, and two windows only per tier:"},"1211":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-134","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"This strategy uses a new compaction implementation designed for Time Based Priority. It extends date tiered compaction, but instead of producing multiple tiers of various time windows, it simply splits files into two groups: the \"cold\" group, where all blocks are older than the defined threshold age, and the \"hot\" group, where all blocks are newer than the threshold age."},"1212":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-135","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"The example below defines a cell qualifier 'event\\_date' to be used for comparing the age of blocks within the custom cell qualifier strategy:"},"1213":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-136","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"Note that there are two different configurations for defining the hot age threshold. This is\nbecause the Time Based Priority enforcer operates independently of the compaction implementation."},"1214":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-137","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"It's also possible to hook in domain-specific logic for defining the data age of each row to be used for comparing blocks priorities. The Custom Time Based Priority framework defines the `CustomTieredCompactor.TieringValueProvider` interface, which can be implemented to provide the specific date value to be used by compaction for grouping the blocks according to the threshold age."},"1215":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-138","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"In the following example, the `RowKeyPortionTieringValueProvider` implements the `getTieringValue` method. This method parses the date from a segment of the row key value, specifically between positions 14 and 29, using the \"yyyyMMddHHmmss\" format. The parsed date is then returned as a long timestamp, which is then used by custom tiered compaction to group the blocks based on the defined hot age threshold:"},"1216":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-139","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"The Tiering Value Provider above can then be configured for Time Based Priority as follows:"},"1217":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-140","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"Upon enabling Custom Time Based Priority (either the custom qualifier or custom value provider) in\nthe column family configuration, it is imperative that major compaction be executed twice on the\nspecified tables to ensure the effective application of the newly configured priorities within the\nbucket cache."},"1218":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-141","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"Time Based Priority was originally implemented with the cell timestamp strategy only. The original design covering cell timestamp based strategy is available here."},"1219":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-142","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"The second phase including the two custom strategies mentioned above is detailed in this separate design doc."},"1220":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-143","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#compressed-blockcache","content":"HBASE-11331 introduced lazy BlockCache decompression, more simply referred to as compressed BlockCache. When compressed BlockCache is enabled data and encoded data blocks are cached in the BlockCache in their on-disk format, rather than being decompressed and decrypted before caching."},"1221":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-144","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#compressed-blockcache","content":"For a RegionServer hosting more data than can fit into cache, enabling this feature with SNAPPY compression has been shown to result in 50% increase in throughput and 30% improvement in mean latency while, increasing garbage collection by 80% and increasing overall CPU load by 2%. See HBASE-11331 for more details about how performance was measured and achieved. For a RegionServer hosting data that can comfortably fit into cache, or if your workload is sensitive to extra CPU or garbage-collection load, you may receive less benefit."},"1222":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-145","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#compressed-blockcache","content":"The compressed BlockCache is disabled by default. To enable it, set `hbase.block.data.cachecompressed` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml* on all RegionServers."},"1223":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-146","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Depending on the data size and the configured cache size, the cache warm up can take anywhere from a few minutes to a few hours. This becomes even more critical for HBase deployments over cloud storage, where compute is separated from storage. Doing this everytime the region server starts can be a very expensive process. To eliminate this, HBASE-27313 implemented the cache persistence feature where the region servers periodically persist the blocks cached in the bucket cache. This persisted information is then used to resurrect the cache in the event of a region server restart because of normal restart or crash."},"1224":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-147","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"HBASE-27999 implements the cache aware load balancer, which adds to the load balancer the ability to consider the cache allocation of each region on region servers when calculating a new assignment plan, using the region/region server cache allocation information reported by region servers to calculate the percentage of HFiles cached for each region on the hosting server. This information is then used by the balancer as a factor when deciding on an optimal, new assignment plan."},"1225":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-148","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The master node captures the caching information from all the region servers and uses this information to decide on new region assignments while ensuring a minimal impact on the current cache allocation. A region is assigned to the region server where it has a better cache ratio as compared to the region server where it is currently hosted."},"1226":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-149","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The CacheAwareLoadBalancer uses two cost elements for deciding the region allocation. These are described below:"},"1227":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-150","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"**Cache Cost**\\\nThe cache cost is calculated as the percentage of data for a region cached on the region server where it is either currently hosted or was previously hosted. A region may have multiple HFiles, each of different sizes. A HFile is considered to be fully prefetched when all the data blocks in this file are in the cache. The region server hosting this region calculates the ratio of number of HFiles fully cached in the cache to the total number of HFiles in the region. This ratio will vary from 0 (region hosted on this server, but none of its HFiles are cached into the cache) to 1 (region hosted on this server and all the HFiles for this region are cached into the cache).\\\nEvery region server maintains this information for all the regions currently hosted there. In addition to that, this cache ratio is also maintained for the regions which were previously hosted on this region server giving historical information about the regions."},"1228":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-151","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Skewness Cost"},"1229":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-152","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The cache aware balancer will consider cache cost with the skewness cost to decide on the region assignment plan under following conditions:"},"1230":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-153","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"There is an idle server in the cluster. This can happen when an existing server is restarted or a new server is added to the cluster."},"1231":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-154","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"When the cost of maintaining the balance in the cluster is greater than the minimum threshold defined by the configuration *hbase.master.balancer.stochastic.minCostNeedBalance*."},"1232":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-155","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The CacheAwareLoadBalancer can be enabled in the cluster by setting the following configuration properties in the master master configuration:"},"1233":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-156","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Within HBASE-29168, the CacheAwareLoadBalancer implements region move throttling. This mitigates the impact of \"losing\" cache factor when balancing mainly due to region skewness, i.e. when new region servers are added to the cluster, a large bulk of cached regions may move to the new servers at once, which can cause noticeable read performance impacts for cache sensitive use cases. The throttling sleep time is determined by the **hbase.master.balancer.move.throttlingMillis** property, and it defaults to 60000 millis. If a region planned to be moved has a cache ratio on the target server above the thershold configurable by the **hbase.master.balancer.stochastic.throttling.cacheRatio** property (80% by default), no throttling will be applied in this region move."},"1234":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-157","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"As write requests are handled by the region server, they accumulate in an in-memory storage system called the *memstore*. Once the memstore fills, its content are written to disk as additional store files. This event is called a *memstore flush*. As store files accumulate, the RegionServer will compact them into fewer, larger files. After each flush or compaction finishes, the amount of data stored in the region has changed. The RegionServer consults the region split policy to determine if the region has grown too large or should be split for another policy-specific reason. A region split request is enqueued if the policy recommends it."},"1235":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-158","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"Logically, the process of splitting a region is simple. We find a suitable point in the keyspace of the region where we should divide the region in half, then split the region's data into two new regions at that point. The details of the process however are not simple. When a split happens, the newly created *daughter regions* do not rewrite all the data into new files immediately. Instead, they create small files similar to symbolic link files, named Reference files, which point to either the top or bottom part of the parent store file according to the split point. The reference file is used just like a regular data file, but only half of the records are considered. The region can only be split if there are no more references to the immutable data files of the parent region. Those reference files are cleaned gradually by compactions, so that the region will stop referring to its parents files, and can be split further."},"1236":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-159","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"Although splitting the region is a local decision made by the RegionServer, the split process itself must coordinate with many actors. The RegionServer notifies the Master before and after the split, updates the `.META.` table so that clients can discover the new daughter regions, and rearranges the directory structure and data files in HDFS. Splitting is a multi-task process. To enable rollback in case of an error, the RegionServer keeps an in-memory journal about the execution state. The steps taken by the RegionServer to execute the split are illustrated in the \"RegionServer Split Process\" schema below. Each step is labeled with its step number. Actions from RegionServers or Master are shown in red, while actions from the clients are shown in green."},"1237":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-160","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer decides locally to split the region, and prepares the split. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS STARTED.** As a first step, the RegionServer acquires a shared read lock on the table to prevent schema modifications during the splitting process. Then it creates a znode in zookeeper under `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name`, and sets the znode's state to `SPLITTING`."},"1238":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-161","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The Master learns about this znode, since it has a watcher for the parent `region-in-transition` znode."},"1239":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-162","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates a sub-directory named `.splits` under the parent's `region` directory in HDFS."},"1240":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-163","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer closes the parent region and marks the region as offline in its local data structures. &#x2A;*THE SPLITTING REGION IS NOW OFFLINE.** At this point, client requests coming to the parent region will throw `NotServingRegionException`. The client will retry with some backoff. The closing region is flushed."},"1241":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-164","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates region directories under the `.splits` directory, for daughter regions A and B, and creates necessary data structures. Then it splits the store files, in the sense that it creates two Reference files per store file in the parent region. Those reference files will point to the parent region's files."},"1242":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-165","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates the actual region directory in HDFS, and moves the reference files for each daughter."},"1243":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-166","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer sends a `Put` request to the `.META.` table, to set the parent as offline in the `.META.` table and add information about daughter regions. At this point, there won't be individual entries in `.META.` for the daughters. Clients will see that the parent region is split if they scan `.META.`, but won't know about the daughters until they appear in `.META.`. Also, if this `Put` to `.META`. succeeds, the parent will be effectively split. If the RegionServer fails before this RPC succeeds, Master and the next Region Server opening the region will clean dirty state about the region split. After the `.META.` update, though, the region split will be rolled-forward by Master."},"1244":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-167","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer opens daughters A and B in parallel."},"1245":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-168","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer adds the daughters A and B to `.META.`, together with information that it hosts the regions. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT REGIONS (DAUGHTERS WITH REFERENCES TO PARENT) ARE NOW ONLINE.** After this point, clients can discover the new regions and issue requests to them. Clients cache the `.META.` entries locally, but when they make requests to the RegionServer or `.META.`, their caches will be invalidated, and they will learn about the new regions from `.META.`."},"1246":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-169","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer updates znode `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name` in ZooKeeper to state `SPLIT`, so that the master can learn about it. The balancer can freely re-assign the daughter regions to other region servers if necessary. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS NOW FINISHED.**"},"1247":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-170","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"After the split, `.META.` and HDFS will still contain references to the parent region. Those references will be removed when compactions in daughter regions rewrite the data files. Garbage collection tasks in the master periodically check whether the daughter regions still refer to the parent region's files. If not, the parent region will be removed."},"1248":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-171","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#note-on-split-directory-behavior-hbase-26187","content":"The numbered split procedure above documents the original, pre-HBASE-26187 implementation, in\nwhich daughter regions are first created under a `.splits` sub-directory of the parent region\ndirectory. In newer HBase implementations that include\nHBASE-26187, this on-disk layout has changed:\ndaughter region directories are created directly under the table directory, rather than under a\nparent region's `.splits` directory. When reading the numbered procedure for such newer versions,\ninterpret any steps that mention creating or using a `.splits` directory as instead preparing and\npromoting daughter region files directly under the table directory. This update does not change\nthe high-level split semantics (references, meta updates, and online transition of daughters); it\nonly affects where temporary/initial daughter files are prepared in HDFS."},"1249":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-172","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#purpose","content":"The &#x2A;Write Ahead Log (WAL)* records all changes to data in HBase, to file-based storage. Under normal operations, the WAL is not needed because data changes move from the MemStore to StoreFiles. However, if a RegionServer crashes or becomes unavailable before the MemStore is flushed, the WAL ensures that the changes to the data can be replayed. If writing to the WAL fails, the entire operation to modify the data fails."},"1250":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-173","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#purpose","content":"HBase uses an implementation of the WAL interface. Usually, there is only one instance of a WAL per RegionServer. An exception is the RegionServer that is carrying *hbase:meta*; the *meta* table gets its own dedicated WAL. The RegionServer records Puts and Deletes to its WAL, before recording them these Mutations MemStore for the affected Store."},"1251":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-174","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#purpose","content":"Prior to 2.0, the interface for WALs in HBase was named `HLog`. In 0.94, HLog was the name of the\nimplementation of the WAL. You will likely find references to the HLog in documentation tailored\nto these older versions."},"1252":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-175","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#purpose","content":"The WAL resides in HDFS in the &#x2A;/hbase/WALs/* directory, with subdirectories per RegionServer."},"1253":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-176","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#purpose","content":"For more general information about the concept of write ahead logs, see the Wikipedia Write-Ahead Log article."},"1254":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-177","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"In HBase, there are a number of WAL implementations (or 'Providers'). Each is known by a short name label (that unfortunately is not always descriptive). You set the provider in *hbase-site.xml* passing the WAL provider short-name as the value on the *hbase.wal.provider* property (Set the provider for *hbase:meta* using the *hbase.wal.meta\\_provider* property, otherwise it uses the same provider configured by *hbase.wal.provider*)."},"1255":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-178","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"*asyncfs*: The **default**. New since hbase-2.0.0 (HBASE-15536, HBASE-14790). This *AsyncFSWAL* provider, as it identifies itself in RegionServer logs, is built on a new non-blocking dfsclient implementation. It is currently resident in the hbase codebase but intent is to move it back up into HDFS itself. WALs edits are written concurrently (\"fan-out\") style to each of the WAL-block replicas on each DataNode rather than in a chained pipeline as the default client does. Latencies should be better. See Apache HBase Improvements and Practices at Xiaomi at slide 14 onward for more detail on implementation."},"1256":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-179","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"*filesystem*: This was the default in hbase-1.x releases. It is built on the blocking *DFSClient* and writes to replicas in classic *DFSCLient* pipeline mode. In logs it identifies as *FSHLog* or *FSHLogProvider*."},"1257":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-180","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"*multiwal*: This provider is made of multiple instances of *asyncfs* or *filesystem*. See the next section for more on *multiwal*."},"1258":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-181","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"Look for the lines like the below in the RegionServer log to see which provider is in place (The below shows the default AsyncFSWALProvider):"},"1259":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-182","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"As the *AsyncFSWAL* hacks into the internal of DFSClient implementation, it will be easily broken\nby upgrading the hadoop dependencies, even for a simple patch release. So if you do not specify\nthe wal provider explicitly, we will first try to use the *asyncfs*, if failed, we will fall back\nto use *filesystem*. And notice that this may not always work, so if you still have problem\nstarting HBase due to the problem of starting *AsyncFSWAL*, please specify *filesystem* explicitly\nin the config file."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-183","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-providers","content":"EC support has been added to hadoop-3.x, and it is incompatible with WAL as the EC output stream\ndoes not support hflush/hsync. In order to create a non-EC file in an EC directory, we need to use\nthe new builder-based create API for *FileSystem*, but it is only introduced in hadoop-2.9+ and\nfor HBase we still need to support hadoop-2.7.x. So please do not enable EC for the WAL directory\nuntil we find a way to deal with it."},"1261":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-184","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#multiwal","content":"With a single WAL per RegionServer, the RegionServer must write to the WAL serially, because HDFS files must be sequential. This causes the WAL to be a performance bottleneck."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-185","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#multiwal","content":"HBase 1.0 introduces support MultiWal in HBASE-5699. MultiWAL allows a RegionServer to write multiple WAL streams in parallel, by using multiple pipelines in the underlying HDFS instance, which increases total throughput during writes. This parallelization is done by partitioning incoming edits by their Region. Thus, the current implementation will not help with increasing the throughput to a single Region."},"1263":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-186","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#multiwal","content":"RegionServers using the original WAL implementation and those using the MultiWAL implementation can each handle recovery of either set of WALs, so a zero-downtime configuration update is possible through a rolling restart."},"1264":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-187","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configure-multiwal","content":"To configure MultiWAL for a RegionServer, set the value of the property `hbase.wal.provider` to `multiwal` by pasting in the following XML:"},"1265":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-188","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configure-multiwal","content":"Restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."},"1266":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-189","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#configure-multiwal","content":"To disable MultiWAL for a RegionServer, unset the property and restart the RegionServer."},"1267":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-190","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-flushing","content":"TODO (describe)."},"1268":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-191","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-splitting","content":"A RegionServer serves many regions. All of the regions in a region server share the same active WAL file. Each edit in the WAL file includes information about which region it belongs to. When a region is opened, the edits in the WAL file which belong to that region need to be replayed. Therefore, edits in the WAL file must be grouped by region so that particular sets can be replayed to regenerate the data in a particular region. The process of grouping the WAL edits by region is called *log splitting*. It is a critical process for recovering data if a region server fails."},"1269":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-192","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-splitting","content":"Log splitting is done by the HMaster during cluster start-up or by the ServerShutdownHandler as a region server shuts down. So that consistency is guaranteed, affected regions are unavailable until data is restored. All WAL edits need to be recovered and replayed before a given region can become available again. As a result, regions affected by log splitting are unavailable until the process completes."},"1270":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-193","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"Renaming the directory is important because a RegionServer may still be up and accepting requests even if the HMaster thinks it is down. If the RegionServer does not respond immediately and does not heartbeat its ZooKeeper session, the HMaster may interpret this as a RegionServer failure. Renaming the logs directory ensures that existing, valid WAL files which are still in use by an active but busy RegionServer are not written to by accident."},"1271":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-194","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"The new directory is named according to the following pattern:"},"1272":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-195","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"An example of such a renamed directory might look like the following:"},"1273":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-196","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"The log splitter reads the log file one edit entry at a time and puts each edit entry into the buffer corresponding to the edit's region. At the same time, the splitter starts several writer threads. Writer threads pick up a corresponding buffer and write the edit entries in the buffer to a temporary recovered edit file. The temporary edit file is stored to disk with the following naming pattern:"},"1274":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-197","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"This file is used to store all the edits in the WAL log for this region. After log splitting completes, the *.temp* file is renamed to the sequence ID of the first log written to the file."},"1275":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-198","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"To determine whether all edits have been written, the sequence ID is compared to the sequence of the last edit that was written to the HFile. If the sequence of the last edit is greater than or equal to the sequence ID included in the file name, it is clear that all writes from the edit file have been completed."},"1276":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-199","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#after-log-splitting-is-complete-each-affected-region-is-assigned-to-a-regionserver","content":"When the region is opened, the *recovered.edits* folder is checked for recovered edits files. If any such files are present, they are replayed by reading the edits and saving them to the MemStore. After all edit files are replayed, the contents of the MemStore are written to disk (HFile) and the edit files are deleted."},"1277":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-200","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"If you set the `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` option to `true`, errors are treated as follows:"},"1278":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-201","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"Any error encountered during splitting will be logged."},"1279":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-202","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"The problematic WAL log will be moved into the *.corrupt* directory under the hbase `rootdir`,"},"1280":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-203","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"Processing of the WAL will continue"},"1281":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-204","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"If the `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` option is set to `false`, the default, the exception will be propagated and the split will be logged as failed. See HBASE-2958 When hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors is set to false, we fail the split but that's it. We need to do more than just fail split if this flag is set."},"1282":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-205","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#how-eofexceptions-are-treated-when-splitting-a-crashed-regionservers-wals","content":"If an EOFException occurs while splitting logs, the split proceeds even when `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` is set to `false`. An EOFException while reading the last log in the set of files to split is likely, because the RegionServer was likely in the process of writing a record at the time of a crash. For background, see HBASE-2643 Figure how to deal with eof splitting logs"},"1283":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-206","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#performance-improvements-during-log-splitting","content":"WAL log splitting and recovery can be resource intensive and take a long time, depending on the number of RegionServers involved in the crash and the size of the regions. Distributed log splitting was developed to improve performance during log splitting."},"1284":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-207","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enabling-or-disabling-distributed-log-splitting","content":"Distributed log processing is enabled by default since HBase 0.92. The setting is controlled by the `hbase.master.distributed.log.splitting` property, which can be set to `true` or `false`, but defaults to `true`."},"1285":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-208","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-splitting-based-on-procedurev2","content":"After HBASE-20610, we introduce a new way to do WAL splitting coordination by procedureV2 framework. This can simplify the process of WAL splitting and no need to connect zookeeper any more."},"1286":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-209","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#background-toc","content":"Currently, splitting WAL processes are coordinated by zookeeper. Each region server are trying to grab tasks from zookeeper. And the burden becomes heavier when the number of region server increase."},"1287":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-210","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#implementation-on-master-side-toc","content":"During ServerCrashProcedure, SplitWALManager will create one SplitWALProcedure for each WAL file which should be split. Then each SplitWALProcedure will spawn a SplitWalRemoteProcedure to send the request to region server. SplitWALProcedure is a StateMachineProcedure and here is the state transfer diagram."},"1288":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-211","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#implementation-on-region-server-side-toc","content":"Region Server will receive a SplitWALCallable and execute it, which is much more straightforward than before. It will return null if success and return exception if there is any error."},"1289":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-212","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#architecture-regionserver-wal-splitting-based-on-procedurev2-performance","content":"According to tests on a cluster which has 5 regionserver and 1 master. procedureV2 coordinated WAL splitting has a better performance than ZK coordinated WAL splitting no master when restarting the whole cluster or one region server crashing."},"1290":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-213","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"To enable this feature, first we should ensure our package of HBase already contains these code. If not, please upgrade the package of HBase cluster without any configuration change first. Then change configuration 'hbase.split.wal.zk.coordinated' to false. Rolling upgrade the master with new configuration. Now WAL splitting are handled by our new implementation. But region server are still trying to grab tasks from zookeeper, we can rolling upgrade the region servers with the new configuration to stop that."},"1291":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-214","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Steps as follows:"},"1292":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-215","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade whole cluster to get the new Implementation."},"1293":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-216","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade Master with new configuration 'hbase.split.wal.zk.coordinated'=false."},"1294":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-217","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade region server to stop grab tasks from zookeeper."},"1295":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-218","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-compression","content":"The content of the WAL can be compressed using LRU Dictionary compression. This can be used to speed up WAL replication to different datanodes. The dictionary can store up to 215 elements; eviction starts after this number is exceeded."},"1296":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-219","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-compression","content":"To enable WAL compression, set the `hbase.regionserver.wal.enablecompression` property to `true`. The default value for this property is `false`. By default, WAL tag compression is turned on when WAL compression is enabled. You can turn off WAL tag compression by setting the `hbase.regionserver.wal.tags.enablecompression` property to 'false'."},"1297":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-220","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#wal-compression","content":"A possible downside to WAL compression is that we lose more data from the last block in the WAL if it is ill-terminated mid-write. If entries in this last block were added with new dictionary entries but we failed persist the amended dictionary because of an abrupt termination, a read of this last block may not be able to resolve last-written entries."},"1298":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-221","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"It is possible to set *durability* on each Mutation or on a Table basis. Options include:"},"1299":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-222","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"*SKIP\\_WAL*: Do not write Mutations to the WAL (See the next section, Disabling the WAL)."},"1300":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-223","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"*ASYNC\\_WAL*: Write the WAL asynchronously; do not hold-up clients waiting on the sync of their write to the filesystem but return immediately. The edit becomes visible. Meanwhile, in the background, the Mutation will be flushed to the WAL at some time later. This option currently may lose data. See HBASE-16689."},"1301":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-224","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"*SYNC\\_WAL*: The **default**. Each edit is sync'd to HDFS before we return success to the client."},"1302":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-225","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"*FSYNC\\_WAL*: Each edit is fsync'd to HDFS and the filesystem before we return success to the client."},"1303":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-226","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#durability","content":"Do not confuse the *ASYNC\\_WAL* option on a Mutation or Table with the *AsyncFSWAL* writer; they are distinct options unfortunately closely named"},"1304":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-227","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"HBASE-17437 added support for specifying a WAL directory outside the HBase root directory or even in a different FileSystem since 1.3.3/2.0+. Some FileSystems (such as Amazon S3) don't support append or consistent writes, in such scenario WAL directory needs to be configured in a different FileSystem to avoid loss of writes."},"1305":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-228","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"Following configurations are added to accomplish this:"},"1306":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-229","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.wal.dir`\\\nThis defines where the root WAL directory is located, could be on a different FileSystem than the root directory. WAL directory can not be set to a subdirectory of the root directory. The default value of this is the root directory if unset."},"1307":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-230","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.rootdir.perms`\\\nConfigures FileSystem permissions to set on the root directory. This is '700' by default."},"1308":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-231","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.wal.dir.perms`\\\nConfigures FileSystem permissions to set on the WAL directory FileSystem. This is '700' by default."},"1309":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-232","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#custom-wal-directory","content":"While migrating to custom WAL dir (outside the HBase root directory or a different FileSystem)\nexisting WAL files must be copied manually to new WAL dir, otherwise it may lead to data\nloss/inconsistency as HMaster has no information about previous WAL directory."},"1310":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-233","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#disabling-the-wal","content":"It is possible to disable the WAL, to improve performance in certain specific situations. However, disabling the WAL puts your data at risk. The only situation where this is recommended is during a bulk load. This is because, in the event of a problem, the bulk load can be re-run with no risk of data loss."},"1311":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-234","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#disabling-the-wal","content":"The WAL is disabled by calling the HBase client field `Mutation.writeToWAL(false)`. Use the `Mutation.setDurability(Durability.SKIP_WAL)` and Mutation.getDurability() methods to set and get the field's value. There is no way to disable the WAL for only a specific table."},"1312":{"id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver-235","page_id":"/docs/architecture/regionserver","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/regionserver#disabling-the-wal","content":"If you disable the WAL for anything other than bulk loads, your data is at risk."},"1313":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"page","content":"Scan Over Snapshot","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner"},"1314":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","content":"Using TableSnapshotScanner to scan HBase snapshots directly from HDFS, bypassing RegionServers for better performance."},"1315":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#tablesnapshotscanner","content":"TableSnapshotScanner"},"1316":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#use-tablesnapshotscanner","content":"Use TableSnapshotScanner"},"1317":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#tablesnapshotinputformat","content":"TableSnapshotInputFormat"},"1318":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#use-tablesnapshotinputformat","content":"Use TableSnapshotInputFormat"},"1319":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#permission-to-access-snapshot-and-data-files","content":"Permission to access snapshot and data files"},"1320":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#hdfs-acls","content":"HDFS ACLs"},"1321":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"Basic idea"},"1322":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Configuration to use this feature"},"1323":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"Limitation"},"1324":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","content":"In HBase, a scan of a table costs server-side HBase resources reading, formating, and returning data back to the client.\nLuckily, HBase provides a TableSnapshotScanner and TableSnapshotInputFormat (introduced by HBASE-8369),\nwhich can scan HBase-written HFiles directly in the HDFS filesystem completely by-passing hbase. This access mode\nperforms better than going via HBase and can be used with an offline HBase with in-place or exported\nsnapshot HFiles."},"1325":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","content":"To read HFiles directly, the user must have sufficient permissions to access snapshots or in-place hbase HFiles."},"1326":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#tablesnapshotscanner","content":"TableSnapshotScanner provides a means for running a single client-side scan over snapshot files.\nWhen using TableSnapshotScanner, we must specify a temporary directory to copy the snapshot files into.\nThe client user should have write permissions to this directory, and the dir should not be a subdirectory of\nthe hbase.rootdir. The scanner deletes the contents of the directory once the scanner is closed."},"1327":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#tablesnapshotinputformat","content":"TableSnapshotInputFormat provides a way to scan over snapshot HFiles in a MapReduce job."},"1328":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#permission-to-access-snapshot-and-data-files","content":"Generally, only the HBase owner or the HDFS admin have the permission to access HFiles."},"1329":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#permission-to-access-snapshot-and-data-files","content":"HBASE-18659 uses HDFS ACLs to make HBase granted user have permission to access snapshot files."},"1330":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#hdfs-acls","content":"HDFS ACLs supports an \"access ACL\", which defines the rules to enforce during permission checks, and a \"default ACL\", which defines the ACL entries that new child files or sub-directories receive automatically during creation. Via HDFS ACLs, HBase syncs granted users with read permission to HFiles."},"1331":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"The HBase files are organized in the following ways:"},"1332":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/.tmp/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"1333":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"1334":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/archive/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"1335":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/.hbase-snapshot/{snapshotName}`"},"1336":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"So the basic idea is to add or remove HDFS ACLs to files of the global/namespace/table directory\nwhen grant or revoke permission to global/namespace/table."},"1337":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#basic-idea","content":"See the design doc in HBASE-18659 for more details."},"1338":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Firstly, make sure that HDFS ACLs are enabled and umask is set to 027"},"1339":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Add master coprocessor, please make sure the SnapshotScannerHDFSAclController is configured after AccessController"},"1340":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Enable this feature"},"1341":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Modify table scheme to enable this feature for a specified table, this config is\nfalse by default for every table, this means the HBase granted ACLs will not be synced to HDFS"},"1342":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"There are some limitations for this feature:"},"1343":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"If we enable this feature, some master operations such as grant, revoke, snapshot... (See the design doc for more details) will be slower as we need to sync HDFS ACLs to related hfiles."},"1344":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"HDFS has a config which limits the max ACL entries num for one directory or file:"},"1345":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"The 32 entries include four fixed users for each directory or file: owner, group, other, and mask. For a directory, the four users contain 8 ACL entries(access and default) and for a file, the four users contain 4 ACL entries(access). This means there are 24 ACL entries left for named users or groups.\\\nBased on this limitation, we can only sync up to 12 HBase granted users' ACLs. This means, if a table enables this feature, then the total users with table, namespace of this table, global READ permission should not be greater than 12."},"1346":{"id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/snapshot-scanner#limitation","content":"There are some cases that this coprocessor has not handled or could not handle, so the user HDFS ACLs are not synced normally. It will not make a reference link to another hfile of other tables."},"1347":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"page","content":"Timeline-consistent High Available Reads","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts","Architecture"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads"},"1348":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-0","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","content":"Using region replicas to achieve high availability for reads with timeline consistency, reducing read unavailability during failures."},"1349":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-1","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"Introduction"},"1350":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-2","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"Timeline Consistency"},"1351":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-3","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Tradeoffs"},"1352":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-4","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#where-is-the-code","content":"Where is the code"},"1353":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-5","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#propagating-writes-to-region-replicas","content":"Propagating writes to region replicas"},"1354":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-6","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#storefile-refresher","content":"StoreFile Refresher"},"1355":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-7","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"Async WAL replication"},"1356":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-8","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#store-file-ttl","content":"Store File TTL"},"1357":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-9","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#region-replication-for-meta-tables-region","content":"Region replication for META table's region"},"1358":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-10","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-240","content":"Async WAL Replication for META table as of hbase-2.4.0+"},"1359":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-11","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-300","content":"Async WAL Replication for META table as of hbase-3.0.0+"},"1360":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-12","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"Load Balancing META table load"},"1361":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-13","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#memory-accounting","content":"Memory accounting"},"1362":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-14","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#secondary-replica-failover","content":"Secondary replica failover"},"1363":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-15","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#configuration-properties","content":"Configuration properties"},"1364":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-16","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#server-side-properties","content":"Server side properties"},"1365":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-17","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#client-side-properties","content":"Client side properties"},"1366":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-18","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#user-interface","content":"User Interface"},"1367":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-19","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#creating-a-table-with-region-replication","content":"Creating a table with region replication"},"1368":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-20","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-create","content":"Shell"},"1369":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-21","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#architecture-timeline-consistent-reads-creating-table-with-region-replication-java","content":"Java"},"1370":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-22","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#read-api-and-usage","content":"Read API and Usage"},"1371":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-23","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"Shell"},"1372":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-24","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#java","content":"Java"},"1373":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-25","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"HBase, architecturally, always had the strong consistency guarantee from the start. All reads and writes are routed through a single region server, which guarantees that all writes happen in an order, and all reads are seeing the most recent committed data."},"1374":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-26","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"However, because of this single homing of the reads to a single location, if the server becomes unavailable, the regions of the table that were hosted in the region server become unavailable for some time. There are three phases in the region recovery process - detection, assignment, and recovery. Of these, the detection is usually the longest and is presently in the order of 20-30 seconds depending on the ZooKeeper session timeout. During this time and before the recovery is complete, the clients will not be able to read the region data."},"1375":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-27","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"However, for some use cases, either the data may be read-only, or doing reads against some stale data is acceptable. With timeline-consistent high available reads, HBase can be used for these kind of latency-sensitive use cases where the application can expect to have a time bound on the read completion."},"1376":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-28","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"For achieving high availability for reads, HBase provides a feature called *region replication*. In this model, for each region of a table, there will be multiple replicas that are opened in different RegionServers. By default, the region replication is set to 1, so only a single region replica is deployed and there will not be any changes from the original model. If region replication is set to 2 or more, then the master will assign replicas of the regions of the table. The Load Balancer ensures that the region replicas are not co-hosted in the same region servers and also in the same rack (if possible)."},"1377":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-29","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#introduction","content":"All of the replicas for a single region will have a unique replica*id, starting from 0. The region replica having replica\\_id==0 is called the primary region, and the others \\_secondary regions* or secondaries. Only the primary can accept writes from the client, and the primary will always contain the latest changes. Since all writes still have to go through the primary region, the writes are not highly-available (meaning they might block for some time if the region becomes unavailable)."},"1378":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-30","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"With this feature, HBase introduces a Consistency definition, which can be provided per read operation (get or scan)."},"1379":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-31","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"`Consistency.STRONG` is the default consistency model provided by HBase. In case the table has region replication = 1, or in a table with region replicas but the reads are done with this consistency, the read is always performed by the primary regions, so that there will not be any change from the previous behaviour, and the client always observes the latest data."},"1380":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-32","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"In case a read is performed with `Consistency.TIMELINE`, then the read RPC will be sent to the primary region server first. After a short interval (`hbase.client.primaryCallTimeout.get`, 10ms by default), parallel RPC for secondary region replicas will also be sent if the primary does not respond back. After this, the result is returned from whichever RPC is finished first. If the response came back from the primary region replica, we can always know that the data is latest. For this Result.isStale() API has been added to inspect the staleness. If the result is from a secondary region, then Result.isStale() will be set to true. The user can then inspect this field to possibly reason about the data."},"1381":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-33","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"In terms of semantics, TIMELINE consistency as implemented by HBase differs from pure eventual consistency in these respects:"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-34","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"Single homed and ordered updates: Region replication or not, on the write side, there is still only 1 defined replica (primary) which can accept writes. This replica is responsible for ordering the edits and preventing conflicts. This guarantees that two different writes are not committed at the same time by different replicas and the data diverges. With this, there is no need to do read-repair or last-timestamp-wins kind of conflict resolution."},"1383":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-35","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"The secondaries also apply the edits in the order that the primary committed them. This way the secondaries will contain a snapshot of the primaries data at any point in time. This is similar to RDBMS replications and even HBase's own multi-datacenter replication, however in a single cluster."},"1384":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-36","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"On the read side, the client can detect whether the read is coming from up-to-date data or is stale data. Also, the client can issue reads with different consistency requirements on a per-operation basis to ensure its own semantic guarantees."},"1385":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-37","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"The client can still observe edits out-of-order, and can go back in time, if it observes reads from one secondary replica first, then another secondary replica. There is no stickiness to region replicas or a transaction-id based guarantee. If required, this can be implemented later though."},"1386":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-38","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"**Timeline Consistency**"},"1387":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-39","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"To better understand the TIMELINE semantics, let's look at the above diagram. Let's say that there are two clients, and the first one writes x=1 at first, then x=2 and x=3 later. As above, all writes are handled by the primary region replica. The writes are saved in the write ahead log (WAL), and replicated to the other replicas asynchronously. In the above diagram, notice that replica\\_id=1 received 2 updates, and its data shows that x=2, while the replica\\_id=2 only received a single update, and its data shows that x=1."},"1388":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-40","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistency","content":"If client1 reads with STRONG consistency, it will only talk with the replica\\_id=0, and thus is guaranteed to observe the latest value of x=3. In case of a client issuing TIMELINE consistency reads, the RPC will go to all replicas (after primary timeout) and the result from the first response will be returned back. Thus the client can see either 1, 2 or 3 as the value of x. Let's say that the primary region has failed and log replication cannot continue for some time. If the client does multiple reads with TIMELINE consistency, she can observe x=2 first, then x=1, and so on."},"1389":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-41","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Having secondary regions hosted for read availability comes with some tradeoffs which should be carefully evaluated per use case. Following are advantages and disadvantages."},"1390":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-42","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"**Advantages:**"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-43","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"High availability for read-only tables"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-44","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"High availability for stale reads"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-45","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Ability to do very low latency reads with very high percentile (99.9%+) latencies for stale reads"},"1394":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-46","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"**Disadvantages:**"},"1395":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-47","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Double / Triple MemStore usage (depending on region replication count) for tables with region replication > 1"},"1396":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-48","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Increased block cache usage"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-49","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Extra network traffic for log replication"},"1398":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-50","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"Extra backup RPCs for replicas"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-51","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#tradeoffs","content":"To serve the region data from multiple replicas, HBase opens the regions in secondary mode in the region servers. The regions opened in secondary mode will share the same data files with the primary region replica, however each secondary region replica will have its own MemStore to keep the unflushed data (only primary region can do flushes). Also to serve reads from secondary regions, the blocks of data files may be also cached in the block caches for the secondary regions."},"1400":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-52","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#where-is-the-code","content":"This feature is delivered in two phases, Phase 1 and 2. The first phase is done in time for HBase-1.0.0 release. Meaning that using HBase-1.0.x, you can use all the features that are marked for Phase 1. Phase 2 is committed in HBase-1.1.0, meaning all HBase versions after 1.1.0 should contain Phase 2 items."},"1401":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-53","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#propagating-writes-to-region-replicas","content":"As discussed above writes only go to the primary region replica. For propagating the writes from the primary region replica to the secondaries, there are two different mechanisms. For read-only tables, you do not need to use any of the following methods. Disabling and enabling the table should make the data available in all region replicas. For mutable tables, you have to use **only** one of the following mechanisms: storefile refresher, or async wal replication. The latter is recommended."},"1402":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-54","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#storefile-refresher","content":"The first mechanism is store file refresher which is introduced in HBase-1.0+. Store file refresher is a thread per region server, which runs periodically, and does a refresh operation for the store files of the primary region for the secondary region replicas. If enabled, the refresher will ensure that the secondary region replicas see the new flushed, compacted or bulk loaded files from the primary region in a timely manner. However, this means that only flushed data can be read back from the secondary region replicas, and after the refresher is run, making the secondaries lag behind the primary for an a longer time."},"1403":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-55","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#storefile-refresher","content":"For turning this feature on, you should configure `hbase.regionserver.storefile.refresh.period` to a non-zero value. See Configuration section below."},"1404":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-56","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"The second mechanism for propagation of writes to secondaries is done via the “Async WAL Replication” feature. It is only available in HBase-1.1+. This works similarly to HBase's multi-datacenter replication, but instead the data from a region is replicated to the secondary regions. Each secondary replica always receives and observes the writes in the same order that the primary region committed them. In some sense, this design can be thought of as “in-cluster replication”, where instead of replicating to a different datacenter, the data goes to secondary regions to keep secondary region's in-memory state up to date. The data files are shared between the primary region and the other replicas, so that there is no extra storage overhead. However, the secondary regions will have recent non-flushed data in their memstores, which increases the memory overhead. The primary region writes flush, compaction, and bulk load events to its WAL as well, which are also replicated through wal replication to secondaries. When they observe the flush/compaction or bulk load event, the secondary regions replay the event to pick up the new files and drop the old ones."},"1405":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-57","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"Committing writes in the same order as in primary ensures that the secondaries won't diverge from the primary regions data, but since the log replication is asynchronous, the data might still be stale in secondary regions."},"1406":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-58","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"Async WAL Replication is **disabled** by default. You can enable this feature by setting `hbase.region.replica.replication.enabled` to `true`."},"1407":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-59","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"Before 3.0.0, this feature works as a replication endpoint, the performance and latency characteristics is expected to be similar to inter-cluster replication. And once enabled, it will create a replication peer named `region_replica_replication` as a replication peer when you create a table with region replication > 1 for the first time."},"1408":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-60","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"if you want to disable this feature, you need to do two actions in the following order: \\* Set configuration property `hbase.region.replica.replication.enabled` to false in `hbase-site.xml` (see Configuration section below) \\* Disable the replication peer named `region_replica_replication` in the cluster using hbase shell or `Admin` class:"},"1409":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-61","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"In 3.0.0, this feature is re-implemented to decouple with the general replication framework. Now we do not need to create a special replication peer. And during rolling upgrading, we will remove this replication peer automatically if it is present. See HBASE-26233 and the design doc in our git repo for more details."},"1410":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-62","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication","content":"Async WAL Replication and the `hbase:meta` table is a little more involved and gets its own section below; see Region replication for META table's region"},"1411":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-63","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#store-file-ttl","content":"In both of the write propagation approaches mentioned above, store files of the primary will be opened in secondaries independent of the primary region. So for files that the primary compacted away, the secondaries might still be referring to these files for reading. Both features are using HFileLinks to refer to files, but there is no protection (yet) for guaranteeing that the file will not be deleted prematurely. Thus, as a guard, you should set the configuration property `hbase.master.hfilecleaner.ttl` to a larger value, such as 1 hour to guarantee that you will not receive IOExceptions for requests going to replicas."},"1412":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-64","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#region-replication-for-meta-tables-region","content":"The general Async WAL Replication does not work for the META table's WAL. The meta table's secondary replicas refresh themselves from the persistent store files every `hbase.regionserver.meta.storefile.refresh.period`, (a non-zero value). Note how the META replication period is distinct from the user-space `hbase.regionserver.storefile.refresh.period` value."},"1413":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-65","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-240","content":"Async WAL replication for META is added as a new feature in 2.4.0. Set `hbase.region.replica.replication.catalog.enabled` to enable async WAL Replication for META region replicas. It is off by default."},"1414":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-66","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-240","content":"Regarding META replicas count, up to hbase-2.4.0, you would set the special property 'hbase.meta.replica.count'. Now you can alter the META table as you would a user-space table (if `hbase.meta.replica.count` is set, it will take precedent over what is set for replica count in the META table updating META replica count to match)."},"1415":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-67","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-300","content":"In HBASE-26233 we re-implemented the region replication framework to not rely on the general replication framework, so it can work together with META table as well. The code described in the above section have been removed mostly, but the config `hbase.region.replica.replication.catalog.enabled` is still kept, you could still use it to control whether to enable async wal replication for META table. And the ability to alter META table is also kept."},"1416":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-68","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"hbase-2.4.0 also adds a new client-side `LoadBalance` mode. When enabled client-side, clients will try to read META replicas first before falling back on the primary. Before this, the replica lookup mode — now named `HedgedRead` in hbase-2.4.0 — had clients read the primary and if no response after a configurable amount of time had elapsed, it would start up reads against the replicas. Starting from hbase-2.4.12(and all higher minor versions), with client-side `LoadBalance` mode, clients load balance META scan requests across all META replica regions, including the primary META region. In case of exceptions such as NotServingRegionException, it will fall back on the primary META region."},"1417":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-69","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"The new 'LoadBalance' mode helps alleviate hotspotting on the META table distributing META read load."},"1418":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-70","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"To enable the meta replica locator's load balance mode, please set the following configuration at on the **client-side** (only): set 'hbase.locator.meta.replicas.mode' to \"LoadBalance\". Valid options for this configuration are `None`, `HedgedRead`, and `LoadBalance`. Option parse is case insensitive. The default mode is `None` (which falls through to `HedgedRead`, the current default). Do NOT put this configuration in any hbase server-side's configuration, Master or RegionServer (Master could make decisions based off stale state — to be avoided)."},"1419":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-71","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#memory-accounting","content":"The secondary region replicas refer to the data files of the primary region replica, but they have their own memstores (in HBase-1.1+) and uses block cache as well. However, one distinction is that the secondary region replicas cannot flush the data when there is memory pressure for their memstores. They can only free up memstore memory when the primary region does a flush and this flush is replicated to the secondary. Since in a region server hosting primary replicas for some regions and secondaries for some others, the secondaries might cause extra flushes to the primary regions in the same host. In extreme situations, there can be no memory left for adding new writes coming from the primary via wal replication. For unblocking this situation (and since secondary cannot flush by itself), the secondary is allowed to do a “store file refresh” by doing a file system list operation to pick up new files from primary, and possibly dropping its memstore. This refresh will only be performed if the memstore size of the biggest secondary region replica is at least `hbase.region.replica.storefile.refresh.memstore.multiplier` (default 4) times bigger than the biggest memstore of a primary replica. One caveat is that if this is performed, the secondary can observe partial row updates across column families (since column families are flushed independently). The default should be good to not do this operation frequently. You can set this value to a large number to disable this feature if desired, but be warned that it might cause the replication to block forever."},"1420":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-72","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#secondary-replica-failover","content":"When a secondary region replica first comes online, or fails over, it may have served some edits from its memstore. Since the recovery is handled differently for secondary replicas, the secondary has to ensure that it does not go back in time before it starts serving requests after assignment. For doing that, the secondary waits until it observes a full flush cycle (start flush, commit flush) or a “region open event” replicated from the primary. Until this happens, the secondary region replica will reject all read requests by throwing an IOException with message “The region's reads are disabled”. However, the other replicas will probably still be available to read, thus not causing any impact for the rpc with TIMELINE consistency. To facilitate faster recovery, the secondary region will trigger a flush request from the primary when it is opened. The configuration property `hbase.region.replica.wait.for.primary.flush` (enabled by default) can be used to disable this feature if needed."},"1421":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-73","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#configuration-properties","content":"To use highly available reads, you should set the following properties in `hbase-site.xml` file. There is no specific configuration to enable or disable region replicas. Instead you can change the number of region replicas per table to increase or decrease at the table creation or with alter table. The following configuration is for using async wal replication and using meta replicas of 3."},"1422":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-74","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#server-side-properties","content":"One thing to keep in mind also is that, region replica placement policy is only enforced by the `StochasticLoadBalancer` which is the default balancer. If you are using a custom load balancer property in hbase-site.xml (`hbase.master.loadbalancer.class`) replicas of regions might end up being hosted in the same server."},"1423":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-75","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#client-side-properties","content":"Ensure to set the following for all clients (and servers) that will use region replicas."},"1424":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-76","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#client-side-properties","content":"Note HBase-1.0.x users should use `hbase.ipc.client.allowsInterrupt` rather than `hbase.ipc.client.specificThreadForWriting`."},"1425":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-77","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#user-interface","content":"In the masters user interface, the region replicas of a table are also shown together with the primary regions. You can notice that the replicas of a region will share the same start and end keys and the same region name prefix. The only difference would be the appended replica\\_id (which is encoded as hex), and the region encoded name will be different. You can also see the replica ids shown explicitly in the UI."},"1426":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-78","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#creating-a-table-with-region-replication","content":"Region replication is a per-table property. All tables have `REGION_REPLICATION = 1` by default, which means that there is only one replica per region. You can set and change the number of replicas per region of a table by supplying the `REGION_REPLICATION` property in the table descriptor."},"1427":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-79","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#creating-a-table-with-region-replication","content":"There is another per-table property `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION`.All tables have `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION = true` by default, which means the new data written to the primary region should be replicated. If you set this to `false`, replicas do not receive memstore updates from the primary RegionServer,they will only receive updates for events like flushes and bulkloads, and will not have access to data which the primary has not yet flushed. Please note that if you set `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION` to false,`hbase.region.replica.wait.for.primary.flush` will be ignored."},"1428":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-80","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#architecture-timeline-consistent-reads-creating-table-with-region-replication-java","content":"You can also use `setRegionReplication()` and alter table to increase, decrease the region replication for a table."},"1429":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-81","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"You can do reads in shell using a the Consistency.TIMELINE semantics as follows"},"1430":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-82","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"You can simulate a region server pausing or becoming unavailable and do a read from the secondary replica:"},"1431":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-83","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"Using scans is also similar"},"1432":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-84","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#java","content":"You can set the consistency for Gets and Scans and do requests as follows."},"1433":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-85","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#java","content":"You can also pass multiple gets:"},"1434":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-86","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#java","content":"And Scans:"},"1435":{"id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads-87","page_id":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/architecture/timeline-consistent-reads#java","content":"You can inspect whether the results are coming from primary region or not by calling the `Result.isStale()` method:"},"1436":{"id":"/docs/asf","page_id":"/docs/asf","type":"page","content":"HBase and the Apache Software Foundation","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Appendices"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/asf"},"1437":{"id":"/docs/asf-0","page_id":"/docs/asf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/asf","content":"HBase is a project in the Apache Software Foundation and as such there are responsibilities to the ASF to ensure a healthy project."},"1438":{"id":"/docs/asf-1","page_id":"/docs/asf","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/asf#asf-development-process","content":"ASF Development Process"},"1439":{"id":"/docs/asf-2","page_id":"/docs/asf","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/asf#asf-board-reporting","content":"ASF Board Reporting"},"1440":{"id":"/docs/asf-3","page_id":"/docs/asf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/asf#asf-development-process","content":"See the Apache Development Process page for all sorts of information on how the ASF is structured (e.g., PMC, committers, contributors), to tips on contributing and getting involved, and how open-source works at ASF."},"1441":{"id":"/docs/asf-4","page_id":"/docs/asf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/asf#asf-board-reporting","content":"Once a quarter, each project in the ASF portfolio submits a report to the ASF board. This is done by the HBase project lead and the committers. See ASF board reporting for more information."},"1442":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"page","content":"Additional Topics","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features","Backup and Restore"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics"},"1443":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-0","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","content":"Configuration keys, security considerations, and best practices for HBase backup and restore operations."},"1444":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-1","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#configuration-keys","content":"Configuration keys"},"1445":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-2","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"Required properties"},"1446":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-3","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#optional-properties","content":"Optional properties"},"1447":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-4","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#best-practices","content":"Best Practices"},"1448":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-5","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#formulate-a-restore-strategy-and-test-it","content":"Formulate a restore strategy and test it."},"1449":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-6","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#secure-a-full-backup-image-first","content":"Secure a full backup image first."},"1450":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-7","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#define-and-use-backup-sets-for-groups-of-tables-that-are-logical-subsets-of-the-entire-dataset","content":"Define and use backup sets for groups of tables that are logical subsets of the entire dataset."},"1451":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-8","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#document-the-backup-and-restore-strategy-and-ideally-log-information-about-each-backup","content":"Document the backup and restore strategy, and ideally log information about each backup."},"1452":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-9","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"Scenario: Safeguarding Application Datasets on Amazon S3"},"1453":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-10","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#security-of-backup-data","content":"Security of Backup Data"},"1454":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-11","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"Technical Details of Incremental Backup and Restore"},"1455":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-12","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"A Warning on File System Growth"},"1456":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-13","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#capacity-planning","content":"Capacity Planning"},"1457":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-14","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"Full Backups"},"1458":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-15","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"Incremental Backup"},"1459":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-16","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"Limitations of the Backup and Restore Utility"},"1460":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-17","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#configuration-keys","content":"The backup and restore feature includes both required and optional configuration keys."},"1461":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-18","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.enable***: Controls whether or not the feature is enabled (Default: `false`). Set this value to `true`."},"1462":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-19","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.master.logcleaner.plugins***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked when cleaning logs in the HBase Master. Set\nthis value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.master.BackupLogCleaner` or append it to the current value."},"1463":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-20","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.procedure.master.classes***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked with the Procedure framework in the Master. Set\nthis value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.master.LogRollMasterProcedureManager` or append it to the current value."},"1464":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-21","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.procedure.regionserver.classes***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked with the Procedure framework in the RegionServer.\nSet this value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.regionserver.LogRollRegionServerProcedureManager` or append it to the current value."},"1465":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-22","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.coprocessor.region.classes***: A comma-separated list of RegionObservers deployed on tables. Set this value to\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupObserver` or append it to the current value."},"1466":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-23","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.coprocessor.master.classes***: A comma-separated list of MasterObservers deployed on tables. Set this value to\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupMasterObserver` or append it to the current value."},"1467":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-24","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#required-properties","content":"***hbase.master.hfilecleaner.plugins***: A comma-separated list of HFileCleaners deployed on the Master. Set this value\nto `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupHFileCleaner` or append it to the current value."},"1468":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-25","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.system.ttl***: The time-to-live in seconds of data in the `hbase:backup` tables (default: forever). This property\nis only relevant prior to the creation of the `hbase:backup` table. Use the `alter` command in the HBase shell to modify the TTL\nwhen this table already exists. See the below section for more details on the impact of this\nconfiguration property."},"1469":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-26","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.attempts.max***: The number of attempts to perform when taking hbase table snapshots (default: 10)."},"1470":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-27","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.attempts.pause.ms***: The amount of time to wait between failed snapshot attempts in milliseconds (default: 10000)."},"1471":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-28","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.logroll.timeout.millis***: The amount of time (in milliseconds) to wait for RegionServers to execute a WAL rolling\nin the Master's procedure framework (default: 30000)."},"1472":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-29","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#formulate-a-restore-strategy-and-test-it","content":"Before you rely on a backup and restore strategy for your production environment, identify how backups must be performed,\nand more importantly, how restores must be performed. Test the plan to ensure that it is workable.\nAt a minimum, store backup data from a production cluster on a different cluster or server. To further safeguard the data,\nuse a backup location that is at a different physical location."},"1473":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-30","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#formulate-a-restore-strategy-and-test-it","content":"If you have a unrecoverable loss of data on your primary production cluster as a result of computer system issues, you may\nbe able to restore the data from a different cluster or server at the same site. However, a disaster that destroys the whole\nsite renders locally stored backups useless. Consider storing the backup data and necessary resources (both computing capacity\nand operator expertise) to restore the data at a site sufficiently remote from the production site. In the case of a catastrophe\nat the whole primary site (fire, earthquake, etc.), the remote backup site can be very valuable."},"1474":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-31","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#secure-a-full-backup-image-first","content":"As a baseline, you must complete a full backup of HBase data at least once before you can rely on incremental backups. The full\nbackup should be stored outside of the source cluster. To ensure complete dataset recovery, you must run the restore utility\nwith the option to restore baseline full backup. The full backup is the foundation of your dataset. Incremental backup data\nis applied on top of the full backup during the restore operation to return you to the point in time when backup was last taken."},"1475":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-32","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#define-and-use-backup-sets-for-groups-of-tables-that-are-logical-subsets-of-the-entire-dataset","content":"You can group tables into an object called a backup set. A backup set can save time when you have a particular group of tables\nthat you expect to repeatedly back up or restore."},"1476":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-33","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#define-and-use-backup-sets-for-groups-of-tables-that-are-logical-subsets-of-the-entire-dataset","content":"When you create a backup set, you type table names to include in the group. The backup set includes not only groups of related\ntables, but also retains the HBase backup metadata. Afterwards, you can invoke the backup set name to indicate what tables apply\nto the command execution instead of entering all the table names individually."},"1477":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-34","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#document-the-backup-and-restore-strategy-and-ideally-log-information-about-each-backup","content":"Document the whole process so that the knowledge base can transfer to new administrators after employee turnover. As an extra\nsafety precaution, also log the calendar date, time, and other relevant details about the data of each backup. This metadata\ncan potentially help locate a particular dataset in case of source cluster failure or primary site disaster. Maintain duplicate\ncopies of all documentation: one copy at the production cluster site and another at the backup location or wherever it can be\naccessed by an administrator remotely from the production cluster."},"1478":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-35","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"This scenario describes how a hypothetical retail business uses backups to safeguard application data and then restore the dataset\nafter failure."},"1479":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-36","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The HBase administration team uses backup sets to store data from a group of tables that have interrelated information for an\napplication called green. In this example, one table contains transaction records and the other contains customer details. The\ntwo tables need to be backed up and be recoverable as a group."},"1480":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-37","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin team also wants to ensure daily backups occur automatically."},"1481":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-38","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The following is an outline of the steps and examples of commands that are used to backup the data for the *green* application and\nto recover the data later. All commands are run when logged in as HBase superuser."},"1482":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-39","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"A backup set called *green\\_set* is created as an alias for both the transactions table and the customer table. The backup set can\nbe used for all operations to avoid typing each table name. The backup set name is case-sensitive and should be formed with only\nprintable characters and without spaces."},"1483":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-40","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The first backup of green\\_set data must be a full backup. The following command example shows how credentials are passed to Amazon\nS3 and specifies the file system with the s3a: prefix."},"1484":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-41","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"Incremental backups should be run according to a schedule that ensures essential data recovery in the event of a catastrophe. At\nthis retail company, the HBase admin team decides that automated daily backups secures the data sufficiently. The team decides that\nthey can implement this by modifying an existing Cron job that is defined in `/etc/crontab`. Consequently, IT modifies the Cron job\nby adding the following line:"},"1485":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-42","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"A catastrophic IT incident disables the production cluster that the green application uses. An HBase system administrator of the\nbackup cluster must restore the *green\\_set* dataset to the point in time closest to the recovery objective."},"1486":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-43","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"If the administrator of the backup HBase cluster has the backup ID with relevant details in accessible records, the following\nsearch with the `hdfs dfs -ls` command and manually scanning the backup ID list can be bypassed. Consider continuously maintaining\nand protecting a detailed log of backup IDs outside the production cluster in your environment."},"1487":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-44","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The HBase administrator runs the following command on the directory where backups are stored to print the list of successful backup\nIDs on the console:"},"1488":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-45","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin scans the list to see which backup was created at a date and time closest to the recovery objective. To do this, the\nadmin converts the calendar timestamp of the recovery point in time to Unix time because backup IDs are uniquely identified with\nUnix time. The backup IDs are listed in reverse chronological order, meaning the most recent successful backup appears first."},"1489":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-46","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin notices that the following line in the command output corresponds with the *green\\_set* backup that needs to be restored:"},"1490":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-47","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin restores green\\_set invoking the backup ID and the -overwrite option. The -overwrite option truncates all existing data\nin the destination and populates the tables with data from the backup dataset. Without this flag, the backup data is appended to the\nexisting data in the destination. In this case, the admin decides to overwrite the data because it is corrupted."},"1491":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-48","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#security-of-backup-data","content":"With this feature which makes copying data to remote locations, it's important to take a moment to clearly state the procedural\nconcerns that exist around data security. Like the HBase replication feature, backup and restore provides the constructs to automatically\ncopy data from within a corporate boundary to some system outside of that boundary. It is imperative when storing sensitive data that with backup and restore, much\nless any feature which extracts data from HBase, the locations to which data is being sent has undergone a security audit to ensure\nthat only authenticated users are allowed to access that data."},"1492":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-49","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#security-of-backup-data","content":"For example, with the above example of backing up data to S3, it is of the utmost importance that the proper permissions are assigned\nto the S3 bucket to ensure that only a minimum set of authorized users are allowed to access this data. Because the data is no longer\nbeing accessed via HBase, and its authentication and authorization controls, we must ensure that the filesystem storing that data is\nproviding a comparable level of security. This is a manual step which users **must** implement on their own."},"1493":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-50","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"HBase incremental backups enable more efficient capture of HBase table images than previous attempts\nat serial backup and restore solutions, such as those that only used HBase Export and Import APIs.\nIncremental backups use Write Ahead Logs (WALs) to capture the data changes since the\nprevious backup was created. A WAL roll (create new WALs) is executed across all RegionServers\nto track the WALs that need to be in the backup.\nIn addition to WALs, incremental backups also track bulk-loaded HFiles for tables under backup."},"1494":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-51","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"Incremental backup gathers all WAL files generated since the last backup from the source cluster,\nconverts them to HFiles in a `.tmp` directory under the `BACKUP_ROOT`, and then moves these\nHFiles to their final location under the backup root directory to form the backup image.\nIt also reads bulk load records from the backup system table, forms the paths for the corresponding\nbulk-loaded HFiles, and copies those files to the backup destination.\nBulk-loaded files are preserved (not deleted by cleaner chores) until they've been included in a\nbackup (for each backup root).\nA process similar to the DistCp (distributed copy) tool is used to move the backup files to the\ntarget file system."},"1495":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-52","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"When a table restore operation starts, a two-step process is initiated.\nFirst, the full backup is restored from the full backup image.\nSecond, all HFiles from incremental backups between the last full backup and the incremental backup\nbeing restored (including bulk-loaded HFiles) are bulk loaded into the table using the\nHBase Bulk Load utility."},"1496":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-53","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"You can only restore on a live HBase cluster because the data must be redistributed to complete the restore operation successfully."},"1497":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-54","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"As a reminder, incremental backups are implemented via retaining the write-ahead logs which HBase primarily uses for data durability.\nThus, to ensure that all data needing to be included in a backup is still available in the system, the HBase backup and restore feature\nretains all write-ahead logs since the last backup until the next incremental backup is executed."},"1498":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-55","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"Like HBase Snapshots, this can have an expectedly large impact on the HDFS usage of HBase for high volume tables. Take care in enabling\nand using the backup and restore feature, specifically with a mind to removing backup sessions when they are not actively being used."},"1499":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-56","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"The only automated, upper-bound on retained write-ahead logs for backup and restore is based on the TTL of the `hbase:backup` system table which,\nas of the time this document is written, is infinite (backup table entries are never automatically deleted). This requires that administrators\nperform backups on a schedule whose frequency is relative to the amount of available space on HDFS (e.g. less available HDFS space requires\nmore aggressive backup merges and deletions). As a reminder, the TTL can be altered on the `hbase:backup` table using the `alter` command\nin the HBase shell. Modifying the configuration property `hbase.backup.system.ttl` in hbase-site.xml after the system table exists has no effect."},"1500":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-57","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#capacity-planning","content":"When designing a distributed system deployment, it is critical that some basic mathmatical rigor is executed to ensure sufficient computational\ncapacity is available given the data and software requirements of the system. For this feature, the availability of network capacity is the largest\nbottleneck when estimating the performance of some implementation of backup and restore. The second most costly function is the speed at which\ndata can be read/written."},"1501":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-58","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"To estimate the duration of a full backup, we have to understand the general actions which are invoked:"},"1502":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-59","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"Write-ahead log roll on each RegionServer: ones to tens of seconds per RegionServer in parallel. Relative to the load on each RegionServer."},"1503":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-60","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"Take an HBase snapshot of the table(s): tens of seconds. Relative to the number of regions and files that comprise the table."},"1504":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-61","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"Export the snapshot to the destination: see below. Relative to the size of the data and the network bandwidth to the destination."},"1505":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-62","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"To approximate how long the final step will take, we have to make some assumptions on hardware. Be aware that these will *not* be accurate for your\nsystem — these are numbers that your or your administrator know for your system. Let's say the speed of reading data from HDFS on a single node is\ncapped at 80MB/s (across all Mappers that run on that host), a modern network interface controller (NIC) supports 10Gb/s, the top-of-rack switch can\nhandle 40Gb/s, and the WAN between your clusters is 10Gb/s. This means that you can only ship data to your remote at a speed of 1.25GB/s — meaning\nthat 16 nodes (`1.25 * 1024 / 80 = 16`) participating in the ExportSnapshot should be able to fully saturate the link between clusters. With more\nnodes in the cluster, we can still saturate the network but at a lesser impact on any one node which helps ensure local SLAs are made. If the size\nof the snapshot is 10TB, this would full backup would take in the ballpark of 2.5 hours (`10 * 1024 / 1.25 / (60 * 60) = 2.23hrs`)"},"1506":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-63","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"As a general statement, it is very likely that the WAN bandwidth between your local cluster and the remote storage is the largest\nbottleneck to the speed of a full backup."},"1507":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-64","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#full-backups","content":"When the concern is restricting the computational impact of backups to a \"production system\", the above formulas can be reused with the optional\ncommand-line arguments to `hbase backup create`: `-b`, `-w`, `-q`. The `-b` option defines the bandwidth at which each worker (Mapper) would\nwrite data. The `-w` argument limits the number of workers that would be spawned in the DistCp job. The `-q` allows the user to specify a YARN\nqueue which can limit the specific nodes where the workers will be spawned — this can quarantine the backup workers performing the copy to\na set of non-critical nodes. Relating the `-b` and `-w` options to our earlier equations: `-b` would be used to restrict each node from reading\ndata at the full 80MB/s and `-w` is used to limit the job from spawning 16 worker tasks."},"1508":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-65","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"Like we did for full backups, we have to understand the incremental backup process to approximate its runtime and cost."},"1509":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-66","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"Identify new write-ahead logs since the last full or incremental backup: negligible. Apriori knowledge from the backup system table(s)."},"1510":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-67","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"Read, filter, and write \"minimized\" HFiles equivalent to the WALs: dominated by the speed of writing data. Relative to write speed of HDFS."},"1511":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-68","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"Read bulk load records from the backup system table, form the paths for bulk-loaded HFiles, and copy them to the backup destination."},"1512":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-69","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"DistCp the HFiles to the destination: see above."},"1513":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-70","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#incremental-backup","content":"For the second step, the dominating cost of this operation would be the re-writing the data (under the assumption that a majority of the\ndata in the WAL is preserved). In this case, we can assume an aggregate write speed of 30MB/s per node. Continuing our 16-node cluster example,\nthis would require approximately 15 minutes to perform this step for 50GB of data (50 \\* 1024 / 60 / 60 = 14.2). The amount of time to start the\nDistCp MapReduce job would likely dominate the actual time taken to copy the data (50 / 1.25 = 40 seconds) and can be ignored."},"1514":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-71","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Serial backup operations**\\\nBackup operations cannot be run concurrently. An operation includes actions like create, delete, restore, and merge. Only one active backup session is supported. HBASE-16391\nwill introduce multiple-backup sessions support."},"1515":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-72","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**No means to cancel backups**\\\nBoth backup and restore operations cannot be canceled. (HBASE-15997, HBASE-15998).\nThe workaround to cancel a backup would be to kill the client-side backup command (`control-C`), ensure all relevant MapReduce jobs have exited, and then\nrun the `hbase backup repair` command to ensure the system backup metadata is consistent."},"1516":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-73","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Backups can only be saved to a single location**\\\nCopying backup information to multiple locations is an exercise left to the user. HBASE-15476 will\nintroduce the ability to specify multiple-backup destinations intrinsically."},"1517":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-74","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**HBase superuser access is required**\\\nOnly an HBase superuser (e.g. hbase) is allowed to perform backup/restore, can pose a problem for shared HBase installations. Current mitigations would require\ncoordination with system administrators to build and deploy a backup and restore strategy (HBASE-14138)."},"1518":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-75","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Backup restoration is an online operation**\\\nTo perform a restore from a backup, it requires that the HBase cluster is online as a caveat of the current implementation (HBASE-16573)."},"1519":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-76","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Some operations may fail and require re-run**\\\nThe HBase backup feature is primarily client driven. While there is the standard HBase retry logic built into the HBase Connection, persistent errors in executing operations\nmay propagate back to the client (e.g. snapshot failure due to region splits). The backup implementation should be moved from client-side into the ProcedureV2 framework\nin the future which would provide additional robustness around transient/retryable failures. The `hbase backup repair` command is meant to correct states which the system\ncannot automatically detect and recover from."},"1520":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-77","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Avoidance of declaration of public API**\\\nWhile the Java API to interact with this feature exists and its implementation is separated from an interface, insufficient rigor has been applied to determine if\nit is exactly what we intend to ship to users. As such, it is marked as for a `Private` audience with the expectation that, as users begin to try the feature, there\nwill be modifications that would necessitate breaking compatibility (HBASE-17517)."},"1521":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics-78","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/additional-topics#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Lack of global metrics for backup and restore**\\\nIndividual backup and restore operations contain metrics about the amount of work the operation included, but there is no centralized location (e.g. the Master UI)\nwhich present information for consumption (HBASE-16565)."},"1522":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"page","content":"Administration of Backup Images","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features","Backup and Restore"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration"},"1523":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-0","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","content":"Managing HBase backup images including listing, describing, deleting, and merging backup sets for efficient storage."},"1524":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-1","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#managing-backup-progress","content":"Managing Backup Progress"},"1525":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-2","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-progress-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1526":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-3","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-manging-backup-progress-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1527":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-4","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-manging-backup-progress-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1528":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-5","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#managing-backup-history","content":"Managing Backup History"},"1529":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-6","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1530":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-7","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1531":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-8","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1532":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-9","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#describing-a-backup-image","content":"Describing a Backup Image"},"1533":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-10","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1534":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-11","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1535":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-12","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1536":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-13","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#deleting-backup-images","content":"Deleting Backup Images"},"1537":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-14","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#syntax","content":"Syntax"},"1538":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-15","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1539":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-16","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Example Usage"},"1540":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-17","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-repair-command","content":"Backup Repair Command"},"1541":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-18","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1542":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-19","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1543":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-20","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1544":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-21","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","content":"The `hbase backup` command has several subcommands that help with administering backup images as they accumulate. Most production\nenvironments require recurring backups, so it is necessary to have utilities to help manage the data of the backup repository.\nSome subcommands enable you to find information that can help identify backups that are relevant in a search for particular data.\nYou can also delete backup images."},"1545":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-22","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","content":"The following list details each `hbase backup subcommand` that can help administer backups. Run the full command-subcommand line as\nthe HBase superuser."},"1546":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-23","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#managing-backup-progress","content":"You can monitor a running backup in another terminal session by running the *hbase backup progress* command and specifying the backup ID as an argument."},"1547":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-24","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#managing-backup-progress","content":"For example, run the following command as hbase superuser to view the progress of a backup"},"1548":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-25","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-progress-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nSpecifies the backup that you want to monitor by seeing the progress information. The backupId is case-sensitive."},"1549":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-26","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-manging-backup-progress-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"1550":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-27","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#managing-backup-history","content":"This command displays a log of backup sessions. The information for each session includes backup ID, type (full or incremental), the tables\nin the backup, status, and start and end time. Specify the number of backup sessions to display with the optional -n argument."},"1551":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-28","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nSpecifies the backup that you want to monitor by seeing the progress information. The backupId is case-sensitive."},"1552":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-29","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-n \\<num\\_records>***\\\n(Optional) The maximum number of backup records (Default: 10)."},"1553":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-30","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-p \\<backup\\_root\\_path>***\\\nThe full filesystem URI of where backup images are stored."},"1554":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-31","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\&#xA;The name of the backup set to obtain history for. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"1555":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-32","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name>***\\&#xA;The name of table to obtain history for. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option."},"1556":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-33","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#describing-a-backup-image","content":"This command can be used to obtain information about a specific backup image."},"1557":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-34","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\nThe ID of the backup image to describe."},"1558":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-35","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"1559":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-36","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#deleting-backup-images","content":"The `hbase backup delete` command deletes backup images that are no longer needed."},"1560":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-37","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-l \\<backup\\_id1,backup\\_id2,...>***\\\nComma-separated list of backup IDs to delete."},"1561":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-38","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-k \\<days>***\\\nDeletes all backup images completed more than the specified number of days ago."},"1562":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-39","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"These options are **mutually exclusive**. Only one of `-l` or `-k` may be used at a time."},"1563":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-40","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Delete specific backup images by ID:"},"1564":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-41","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Delete all backup images older than 30 days:"},"1565":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-42","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Deleting a backup may affect all following incremental backups (in the same backup root) up to\nthe next full backup. For example, if you take a full backup every 2 weeks and\ndaily incremental backups, running `hbase backup delete -k 7` when the full backup is older than\n7 days will effectively remove the data for all subsequent incremental backups.\nThe backup IDs may still be listed, but their data will be gone."},"1566":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-43","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"If the most recent backup is an incremental backup and you delete it,\nyou should run a **full backup** next.\nRunning another incremental backup immediately after may result in missing data in the\nbackup image. (See HBASE-28084)"},"1567":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-44","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-repair-command","content":"This command attempts to correct any inconsistencies in persisted backup metadata which exists as\nthe result of software errors or unhandled failure scenarios. While the backup implementation tries\nto correct all errors on its own, this tool may be necessary in the cases where the system cannot\nautomatically recover on its own."},"1568":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-45","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"1569":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration-46","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/administration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/administration#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"1570":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"page","content":"Backup and Restore commands","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features","Backup and Restore"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands"},"1571":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-0","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","content":"Command-line utilities for creating, restoring, and merging HBase backups including full and incremental backup operations."},"1572":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-1","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"Creating a Backup Image"},"1573":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-2","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1574":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-3","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1575":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-4","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1576":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-5","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#restoring-a-backup-image","content":"Restoring a Backup Image"},"1577":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-6","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1578":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-7","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1579":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-8","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage","content":"Example of Usage"},"1580":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-9","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#merging-incremental-backup-images","content":"Merging Incremental Backup Images"},"1581":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-10","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1582":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-11","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"Named Command-Line Arguments"},"1583":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-12","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Example usage"},"1584":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-13","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"Using Backup Sets"},"1585":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-14","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"Backup Set Subcommands"},"1586":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-15","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Positional Command-Line Arguments"},"1587":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-16","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage-1","content":"Example of Usage"},"1588":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-17","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","content":"This covers the command-line utilities that administrators would run to create, restore, and merge backups. Tools to\ninspect details on specific backup sessions is covered in the next section, Administration of Backup Images."},"1589":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-18","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","content":"Run the command `hbase backup help <command>` to access the online help that provides basic information about a command\nand its options. The below information is captured in this help message for each command."},"1590":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-19","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"For HBase clusters also using Apache Phoenix: include the SQL system catalog tables in the backup.\nIn the event that you need to restore the HBase backup, access to the system catalog tables enable\nyou to resume Phoenix interoperability with the restored data."},"1591":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-20","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"The first step in running the backup and restore utilities is to perform a full backup and to store the data in a separate image\nfrom the source. At a minimum, you must do this to get a baseline before you can rely on incremental backups."},"1592":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-21","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"Run the following command as HBase superuser:"},"1593":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-22","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"After the command finishes running, the console prints a SUCCESS or FAILURE status message. The SUCCESS message includes a *backup* ID.\nThe backup ID is the Unix time (also known as Epoch time) that the HBase master received the backup request from the client."},"1594":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-23","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#creating-a-backup-image","content":"Record the backup ID that appears at the end of a successful backup. In case the source cluster\nfails and you need to recover the dataset with a restore operation, having the backup ID readily\navailable can save time."},"1595":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-24","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***type***\\\nThe type of backup to execute: *full* or *incremental*. As a reminder, an *incremental* backup requires a *full* backup to\nalready exist."},"1596":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-25","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_path***\\\nThe *backup\\_path* argument specifies the full filesystem URI of where to store the backup image. Valid prefixes are\n&#x2A;hdfs:*, &#x2A;webhdfs:*, &#x2A;s3a:* or other compatible Hadoop File System implementations."},"1597":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-26","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name\\[,table\\_name]>***\\\nA comma-separated list of tables to back up. If no tables are specified, all tables are backed up. No regular-expression or\nwildcard support is present; all table names must be explicitly listed. See Backup Set&#x73; for more\ninformation about peforming operations on collections of tables. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option; one of these\nnamed options are required."},"1598":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-27","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\\nIdentify tables to backup based on a backup set. See Using Backup Set&#x73; for the purpose and usage\nof backup sets. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"1599":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-28","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-w \\<number\\_workers>***\\\n(Optional) Specifies the number of parallel workers to copy data to backup destination. Backups are currently executed by MapReduce jobs\nso this value corresponds to the number of Mappers that will be spawned by the job."},"1600":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-29","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-b \\<bandwidth\\_per\\_worker>***\\\n(Optional) Specifies the bandwidth of each worker in MB per second."},"1601":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-30","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-d***\\\n(Optional) Enables \"DEBUG\" mode which prints additional logging about the backup creation."},"1602":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-31","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-i***\\\n(Optional) Ignore checksum verify between source snapshot and exported snapshot. Especially when the source and target file system types\nare different, we should use -i option to skip checksum-checks."},"1603":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-32","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-q \\<name>***\\\n(Optional) Allows specification of the name of a YARN queue which the MapReduce job to create the backup should be executed in. This option\nis useful to prevent backup tasks from stealing resources away from other MapReduce jobs of high importance."},"1604":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-33","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-example-usage","content":"This command creates a full backup image of two tables, SALES2 and SALES3, in the HDFS instance who NameNode is host5:9000\nin the path */data/backup&#x2A;. The *-w* option specifies that no more than three parallel works complete the operation."},"1605":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-34","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#restoring-a-backup-image","content":"Run the following command as an HBase superuser. You can only restore a backup on a running HBase cluster because the data must be\nredistributed the RegionServers for the operation to complete successfully."},"1606":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-35","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_path***\\\nThe *backup\\_path* argument specifies the full filesystem URI of where to store the backup image. Valid prefixes are\n&#x2A;hdfs:*, &#x2A;webhdfs:*, &#x2A;s3a:* or other compatible Hadoop File System implementations."},"1607":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-36","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nThe backup ID that uniquely identifies the backup image to be restored."},"1608":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-37","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name\\[,table\\_name]>***\\\nA comma-separated list of tables to restore. See Backup Set&#x73; for more\ninformation about peforming operations on collections of tables. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option; one of these\nnamed options are required."},"1609":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-38","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\\nIdentify tables to backup based on a backup set. See Using Backup Set&#x73; for the purpose and usage\nof backup sets. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"1610":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-39","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-q \\<name>***\\\n(Optional) Allows specification of the name of a YARN queue which the MapReduce job to create the backup should be executed in. This option\nis useful to prevent backup tasks from stealing resources away from other MapReduce jobs of high importance."},"1611":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-40","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-c***\\\n(Optional) Perform a dry-run of the restore. The actions are checked, but not executed."},"1612":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-41","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-m \\<target\\_tables>***\\\n(Optional) A comma-separated list of tables to restore into. If this option is not provided, the original table name is used. When\nthis option is provided, there must be an equal number of entries provided in the `-t` option."},"1613":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-42","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-o***\\\n(Optional) Overwrites the target table for the restore if the table already exists."},"1614":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-43","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage","content":"This command restores two tables of an incremental backup image. In this example:\n• `/tmp/backup_incremental` is the path to the directory containing the backup image.\n• `backupId_1467823988425` is the backup ID.\n• `mytable1` and `mytable2` are the names of tables in the backup image to be restored."},"1615":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-44","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage","content":"If the namespace of a table being restored does not exist in the target environment, it will be\nautomatically created during the restore operation.\nHBASE-25707"},"1616":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-45","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#merging-incremental-backup-images","content":"This command can be used to merge two or more incremental backup images into a single incremental\nbackup image. This can be used to consolidate multiple, small incremental backup images into a single\nlarger incremental backup image. This command could be used to merge hourly incremental backups\ninto a daily incremental backup image, or daily incremental backups into a weekly incremental backup."},"1617":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-46","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_ids***\\\nA comma-separated list of incremental backup image IDs that are to be combined into a single image."},"1618":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-47","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"1619":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-48","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"Backup sets can ease the administration of HBase data backups and restores by reducing the amount of repetitive input\nof table names. You can group tables into a named backup set with the `hbase backup set add` command. You can then use\nthe `-set` option to invoke the name of a backup set in the `hbase backup create` or `hbase restore` rather than list\nindividually every table in the group. You can have multiple backup sets."},"1620":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-49","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"Note the differentiation between the `hbase backup set add&#x60; command and the *-set* option. The\n`hbase backup set add` command must be run before using the `-set` option in a different command\nbecause backup sets must be named and defined before using backup sets as a shortcut."},"1621":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-50","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"If you run the `hbase backup set add` command and specify a backup set name that does not yet exist on your system, a new set\nis created. If you run the command with the name of an existing backup set name, then the tables that you specify are added\nto the set."},"1622":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-51","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"In this command, the backup set name is case-sensitive."},"1623":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-52","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"The metadata of backup sets are stored within HBase. If you do not have access to the original\nHBase cluster with the backup set metadata, then you must specify individual table names to\nrestore the data."},"1624":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-53","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#using-backup-sets","content":"To create a backup set, run the following command as the HBase superuser:"},"1625":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-54","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"The following list details subcommands of the hbase backup set command."},"1626":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-55","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"You must enter one (and no more than one) of the following subcommands after hbase backup set to\ncomplete an operation. Also, the backup set name is case-sensitive in the command-line utility."},"1627":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-56","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***add***\\\nAdds table\\[s] to a backup set. Specify a *backup\\_set\\_name* value after this argument to create a backup set."},"1628":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-57","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***remove***\\\nRemoves tables from the set. Specify the tables to remove in the tables argument."},"1629":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-58","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***list***\\\nLists all backup sets."},"1630":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-59","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***describe***\\\nDisplays a description of a backup set. The information includes whether the set has full\nor incremental backups, start and end times of the backups, and a list of the tables in the set. This subcommand must precede\na valid value for the *backup\\_set\\_name* value."},"1631":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-60","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***delete***\\\nDeletes a backup set. Enter the value for the *backup\\_set\\_name* option directly after the `hbase backup set delete` command."},"1632":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-61","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_set\\_name***\\\nUse to assign or invoke a backup set name. The backup set name must contain only printable characters and cannot have any spaces."},"1633":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-62","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***tables***\\\nList of tables (or a single table) to include in the backup set. Enter the table names as a comma-separated list. If no tables\nare specified, all tables are included in the set."},"1634":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-63","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Maintain a log or other record of the case-sensitive backup set names and the corresponding tables\nin each set on a separate or remote cluster, backup strategy. This information can help you in\ncase of failure on the primary cluster."},"1635":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-64","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage-1","content":"Depending on the environment, this command results in *one* of the following actions:"},"1636":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-65","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage-1","content":"If the `Q1Data` backup set does not exist, a backup set containing tables `TEAM_3` and `TEAM_4` is created."},"1637":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands-66","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/commands","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/commands#example-of-usage-1","content":"If the `Q1Data` backup set exists already, the tables `TEAM_3` and `TEAM_4` are added to the `Q1Data` backup set."},"1638":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore","type":"page","content":"Backup and Restore","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features","Backup and Restore"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore"},"1639":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore-0","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore","content":"Comprehensive guide to HBase backup and restore capabilities for data protection, disaster recovery, and point-in-time recovery."},"1640":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features","Backup and Restore"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview"},"1641":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-0","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"Introduction to HBase backup and restore feature, including full and incremental backups for disaster recovery and point-in-time recovery."},"1642":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-1","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"Terminology"},"1643":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-2","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#planning","content":"Planning"},"1644":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-3","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-within-a-cluster","content":"Backup within a cluster"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-4","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-using-a-dedicated-cluster","content":"Backup using a dedicated cluster"},"1646":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-5","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-to-the-cloud-or-a-storage-vendor-appliance","content":"Backup to the Cloud or a storage vendor appliance"},"1647":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-6","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#first-time-configuration-steps","content":"First-time configuration steps"},"1648":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-7","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"Allow the \"hbase\" system user in YARN"},"1649":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-8","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#hbase-specific-changes","content":"HBase specific changes"},"1650":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-9","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"Backup and restore is a standard operation provided by many databases. An effective backup and restore\nstrategy helps ensure that users can recover data in case of unexpected failures. The HBase backup and restore\nfeature helps ensure that enterprises using HBase as a canonical data repository can recover from catastrophic\nfailures. Another important feature is the ability to restore the database to a particular\npoint-in-time, commonly referred to as a snapshot."},"1651":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-10","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"The HBase backup and restore feature provides the ability to create full backups and incremental backups on\ntables in an HBase cluster. The full backup is the foundation on which incremental backups are applied\nto build iterative snapshots. Incremental backups can be run on a schedule to capture changes over time,\nfor example by using a Cron task. Incremental backups are more cost-effective than full backups because they only capture\nthe changes since the last backup and they also enable administrators to restore the database to any prior incremental backup. Furthermore, the\nutilities also enable table-level data backup-and-recovery if you do not want to restore the entire dataset\nof the backup."},"1652":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-11","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"The backup and restore feature supplements the HBase Replication feature. While HBase replication is ideal for\ncreating \"hot\" copies of the data (where the replicated data is immediately available for query), the backup and\nrestore feature is ideal for creating \"cold\" copies of data (where a manual step must be taken to restore the system).\nPreviously, users only had the ability to create full backups via the ExportSnapshot functionality. The incremental\nbackup implementation is the novel improvement over the previous \"art\" provided by ExportSnapshot."},"1653":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-12","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"The backup and restore feature uses DistCp to transfer files between clusters .\nHADOOP-15850 fixes a bug where CopyCommitter#concatFileChunks\nunconditionally tried to concatenate the files being DistCp'ed to target cluster (though the files are\nindependent) . Without the fix from\nHADOOP-15850 , the transfer would fail.\nSo the backup and restore feature need hadoop version as below"},"1654":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-13","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"2.7.x"},"1655":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-14","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"2.8.x"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-15","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"2.9.2+"},"1657":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-16","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"2.10.0+"},"1658":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-17","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"3.0.4+"},"1659":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-18","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"3.1.2+"},"1660":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-19","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"3.2.0+"},"1661":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-20","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","content":"3.3.0+"},"1662":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-21","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"The backup and restore feature introduces new terminology which can be used to understand how control flows through the\nsystem."},"1663":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-22","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"*A backup*: A logical unit of data and metadata which can restore a table to its state at a specific point in time."},"1664":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-23","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"*Full backup*: a type of backup which wholly encapsulates the contents of the table at a point in time."},"1665":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-24","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"*Incremental backup*: a type of backup which contains the changes in a table since a full backup."},"1666":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-25","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"*Backup set*: A user-defined name which references one or more tables over which a backup can be executed."},"1667":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-26","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#terminology","content":"*Backup ID*: A unique names which identifies one backup from the rest, e.g. `backupId_1467823988425`"},"1668":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-27","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#planning","content":"There are some common strategies which can be used to implement backup and restore in your environment. The following section\nshows how these strategies are implemented and identifies potential tradeoffs with each."},"1669":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-28","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#planning","content":"This backup and restore tools has not been tested on Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) enabled\nHDFS clusters. This is related to the open issue\nHBASE-16178."},"1670":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-29","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-within-a-cluster","content":"This strategy stores the backups on the same cluster as where the backup was taken. This approach is only appropriate for testing\nas it does not provide any additional safety on top of what the software itself already provides."},"1671":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-30","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-using-a-dedicated-cluster","content":"This strategy provides greater fault tolerance and provides a path towards disaster recovery. In this setting, you will\nstore the backup on a separate HDFS cluster by supplying the backup destination cluster's HDFS URL to the backup utility.\nYou should consider backing up to a different physical location, such as a different data center."},"1672":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-31","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-using-a-dedicated-cluster","content":"Typically, a backup-dedicated HDFS cluster uses a more economical hardware profile to save money."},"1673":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-32","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-to-the-cloud-or-a-storage-vendor-appliance","content":"Another approach to safeguarding HBase incremental backups is to store the data on provisioned, secure servers that belong\nto third-party vendors and that are located off-site. The vendor can be a public cloud provider or a storage vendor who uses\na Hadoop-compatible file system, such as S3 and other HDFS-compatible destinations."},"1674":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-33","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#backup-to-the-cloud-or-a-storage-vendor-appliance","content":"The HBase backup utility does not support backup to multiple destinations. A workaround is to\nmanually create copies of the backup files from HDFS or S3."},"1675":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-34","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#first-time-configuration-steps","content":"This section contains the necessary configuration changes that must be made in order to use the backup and restore feature.\nAs this feature makes significant use of YARN's MapReduce framework to parallelize these I/O heavy operations, configuration\nchanges extend outside of just `hbase-site.xml`."},"1676":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-35","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"The YARN *container-executor.cfg* configuration file must have the following property setting: *allowed.system.users=hbase*. No spaces\nare allowed in entries of this configuration file."},"1677":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-36","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"Skipping this step will result in runtime errors when executing the first backup tasks."},"1678":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-37","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"**Example of a valid container-executor.cfg file for backup and restore:**"},"1679":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-38","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#hbase-specific-changes","content":"Add the following properties to hbase-site.xml and restart HBase if it is already running."},"1680":{"id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview-39","page_id":"/docs/backup-restore/overview","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/backup-restore/overview#hbase-specific-changes","content":"The \",...\" is an ellipsis meant to imply that this is a comma-separated list of values, not\nliteral text which should be added to hbase-site.xml."},"1681":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","type":"page","content":"Announcing Releases","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Building and Developing Apache HBase"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing"},"1682":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing-0","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","content":"Email template and guidelines for announcing new HBase releases to the community and mailing lists."},"1683":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing-1","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","content":"Once an RC has passed successfully and the needed artifacts have been staged for disribution, you'll need to let everyone know about our shiny new release. It's not a requirement, but to make things easier for release managers we have a template you can start with. Be sure you replace \\_version\\_ and other markers with the relevant version numbers. You should manually verify all links before sending."},"1684":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing-2","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/announcing","content":"You should sent this message to the following lists: dev\\@hbase.apache.org, user\\@hbase.apache.org, announce\\@apache.org. If you'd like a spot check before sending, feel free to ask via jira or the dev list."},"1685":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"page","content":"Building Apache HBase","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Building and Developing Apache HBase"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building"},"1686":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-0","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","content":"Maven build commands, JDK requirements, Hadoop version selection, and building HBase from source including protobuf and thrift generation."},"1687":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-1","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#basic-compile","content":"Basic Compile"},"1688":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-2","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-basic-compile-jdk-version-requirements","content":"JDK Version Requirements"},"1689":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-3","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#maven-build-commands","content":"Maven Build Commands"},"1690":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-4","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#package","content":"Package"},"1691":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-5","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#compile","content":"Compile"},"1692":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-6","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#install","content":"Install"},"1693":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-7","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-hbase-2x-on-apple-silicon","content":"Building HBase 2.x on Apple Silicon"},"1694":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-8","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#running-all-or-individual-unit-tests","content":"Running all or individual Unit Tests"},"1695":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-9","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-against-various-hadoop-versions","content":"Building against various Hadoop versions"},"1696":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-10","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"Hadoop Profile Activation by HBase Release"},"1697":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-11","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-1-building-hbase-17-against-hadoop-2100","content":"Example 1: Building HBase 1.7 against Hadoop 2.10.0"},"1698":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-12","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-2-building-hbase-23-or-24-against-hadoop-340-snapshot","content":"Example 2: Building HBase 2.3 or 2.4 against Hadoop 3.4.0-SNAPSHOT"},"1699":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-13","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-3-building-hbase-30-against-hadoop-340-snapshot","content":"Example 3: Building HBase 3.0 against Hadoop 3.4.0-SNAPSHOT"},"1700":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-14","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-with-jdk11-and-hadoop3","content":"Building with JDK11 and Hadoop3"},"1701":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-15","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-1-building-hbase-23-or-24-with-jdk11","content":"Example 1: Building HBase 2.3 or 2.4 with JDK11"},"1702":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-16","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-2-building-hbase-30-with-jdk11","content":"Example 2: Building HBase 3.0 with JDK11"},"1703":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-17","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-and-testing-in-an-ide-with-jdk11-and-hadoop3","content":"Building and testing in an IDE with JDK11 and Hadoop3"},"1704":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-18","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"Build Protobuf"},"1705":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-19","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-thrift","content":"Build Thrift"},"1706":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-20","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-a-tarball","content":"Build a Tarball"},"1707":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-21","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-gotchas","content":"Build Gotchas"},"1708":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-22","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#maven-site-failure","content":"Maven Site failure"},"1709":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-23","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-on-linux-aarch64","content":"Build On Linux Aarch64"},"1710":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-24","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#set-environment-variables","content":"Set Environment Variables"},"1711":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-25","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#use-protobuf-supported-on-aarch64","content":"Use Protobuf Supported On Aarch64"},"1712":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-26","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#basic-compile","content":"HBase is compiled using Maven. You must use at least Maven 3.0.4. To check your Maven version, run\nthe command `mvn -version`."},"1713":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-27","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-basic-compile-jdk-version-requirements","content":"HBase has Java version compiler requirements that vary by release branch. At compilation time,\nHBase has the same version requirements as it does for runtime. See Java for a complete\nsupport matrix of Java version by HBase version."},"1714":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-28","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#maven-build-commands","content":"All commands are executed from the local HBase project directory."},"1715":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-29","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#package","content":"The simplest command to compile HBase from its java source code is to use the `package` target, which builds JARs with the compiled files."},"1716":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-30","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#package","content":"Or, to clean up before compiling:"},"1717":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-31","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#package","content":"With Eclipse set up as explained above in Eclipse, you can also use the **Build** command in Eclipse.\nTo create the full installable HBase package takes a little bit more work, so read on."},"1718":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-32","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#compile","content":"The `compile` target does not create the JARs with the compiled files."},"1719":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-33","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#install","content":"To install the JARs in your &#x2A;\\~/.m2/* directory, use the `install` target."},"1720":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-34","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-hbase-2x-on-apple-silicon","content":"Building a non-master branch requires protoc 2.5.0 binary which is not available for Apple Silicon.\nHBASE-27741 added a workaround to the build to fall back to osx-x86\\_64 version of protoc automatically by `apple-silicon-workaround` Maven profile.\nThe intention is that this change will permit the build to proceed with the x86 version of `protoc`, making use of the Rosetta instruction translation service built into the OS.\nIf you'd like to provide and make use of your own aarch\\_64 `protoc`, you can disable this profile on the command line by adding `-P'!apple-silicon-workaround'`, or through configuration in your `settings.xml`."},"1721":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-35","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-hbase-2x-on-apple-silicon","content":"You can use the following commands to build protoc on your Apple Silicon machine."},"1722":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-36","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#running-all-or-individual-unit-tests","content":"See the Running tests section in Unit Tests"},"1723":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-37","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-against-various-hadoop-versions","content":"HBase supports building against Apache Hadoop versions: 2.y and 3.y (early release artifacts).\nExactly which version of Hadoop is used by default varies by release branch. See the section\nHadoop for the complete breakdown of supported Hadoop version by HBase release."},"1724":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-38","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-against-various-hadoop-versions","content":"The mechanism for selecting a Hadoop version at build time is identical across all releases. Which\nversion of Hadoop is default varies. We manage Hadoop major version selection by way of Maven\nprofiles. Due to the peculiarities of Maven profile mutual exclusion, the profile that builds\nagainst a particular Hadoop version is activated by setting a property, *not* the usual profile\nactivation. Hadoop version profile activation is summarized by the following table."},"1725":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-39","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"Hadoop2 Activation"},"1726":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-40","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"Hadoop3 Activation"},"1727":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-41","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"HBase 1.3+"},"1728":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-42","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"*active by default*"},"1729":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-43","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"`-Dhadoop.profile=3.0`"},"1730":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-44","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"HBase 3.0+"},"1731":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-45","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"*not supported*"},"1732":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-46","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"*active by default*"},"1733":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-47","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"Please note that where a profile is active by default, `hadoop.profile` must NOT be provided."},"1734":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-48","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#hadoop-profile-activation-by-hbase-release","content":"Once the Hadoop major version profile is activated, the exact Hadoop version can be\nspecified by overriding the appropriate property value. For Hadoop2 versions, the property name\nis `hadoop-two.version`. With Hadoop3 versions, the property name is `hadoop-three.version`."},"1735":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-49","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-1-building-hbase-17-against-hadoop-2100","content":"For example, to build HBase 1.7 against Hadoop 2.10.0, the profile is set for Hadoop2 by default,\nso only `hadoop-two.version` must be specified:"},"1736":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-50","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-2-building-hbase-23-or-24-against-hadoop-340-snapshot","content":"This is how a developer might check the compatibility of HBase 2.3 or 2.4 against an unreleased\nHadoop version (currently 3.4). Both the Hadoop3 profile and version must be specified:"},"1737":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-51","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-3-building-hbase-30-against-hadoop-340-snapshot","content":"The same developer might want also to check the development version of HBase (currently 3.0)\nagainst the development version of Hadoop (currently 3.4). In this case, the Hadoop3 profile is\nactive by default, so only `hadoop-three.version` must be specified:"},"1738":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-52","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-with-jdk11-and-hadoop3","content":"HBase manages JDK-specific build settings using Maven profiles. The profile appropriate to the JDK\nin use is automatically activated. Building and running on JDK8 supports both Hadoop2 and Hadoop3.\nFor JDK11, only Hadoop3 is supported. Thus, the Hadoop3 profile must be active when building on\nJDK11, and the artifacts used when running HBase on JDK11 must be compiled against Hadoop3.\nFurthermore, the JDK11 profile requires a minimum Hadoop version of 3.2.0. This value is specified\nby the JDK11 profile, but it can be overridden using the `hadoop-three.version` property as normal.\nFor details on Hadoop profile activation by HBase branch, see\nBuilding against various Hadoop versions. See Java for a complete\nsupport matrix of Java version by HBase version."},"1739":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-53","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-1-building-hbase-23-or-24-with-jdk11","content":"To build HBase 2.3 or 2.4 with JDK11, the Hadoop3 profile must be activated explicitly."},"1740":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-54","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#example-2-building-hbase-30-with-jdk11","content":"For HBase 3.0, the Hadoop3 profile is active by default, no additional properties need be\nspecified."},"1741":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-55","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#building-and-testing-in-an-ide-with-jdk11-and-hadoop3","content":"Continuing the discussion from the earlier section, building and\ntesting with JDK11 and Hadoop3 within an IDE may require additional configuration. Specifically,\nmake sure the JVM version used by the IDE is a JDK11, the active JDK Maven profile is for JDK11,\nand the Maven profile for JDK8 is NOT active. Likewise, ensure the Hadoop3 Maven profile is active\nand the Hadoop2 Maven profile is NOT active."},"1742":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-56","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"You may need to change the protobuf definitions that reside in the *hbase-protocol* module or other modules."},"1743":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-57","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"Previous to hbase-2.0.0, protobuf definition files were sprinkled across all hbase modules but now all\nto do with protobuf must reside in the hbase-protocol module; we are trying to contain our protobuf\nuse so we can freely change versions without upsetting any downstream project use of protobuf."},"1744":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-58","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"The protobuf files are located in *hbase-protocol/src/main/protobuf*.\nFor the change to be effective, you will need to regenerate the classes."},"1745":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-59","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"Similarly, protobuf definitions for internal use are located in the *hbase-protocol-shaded* module."},"1746":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-60","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"Typically, protobuf code generation is done using the native `protoc` binary. In our build we use a maven plugin for\nconvenience; however, the plugin may not be able to retrieve appropriate binaries for all platforms. If you find yourself\non a platform where protoc fails, you will have to compile protoc from source, and run it independent of our maven build.\nYou can disable the inline code generation by specifying `-Dprotoc.skip` in your maven arguments, allowing your build to proceed further."},"1747":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-61","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"If you need to manually generate your protobuf files, you should not use `clean` in subsequent\nmaven calls, as that will delete the newly generated files."},"1748":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-62","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-protobuf","content":"Read the *hbase-protocol/README.txt* for more details"},"1749":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-63","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-thrift","content":"You may need to change the thrift definitions that reside in the *hbase-thrift* module or other modules."},"1750":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-64","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-thrift","content":"The thrift files are located in *hbase-thrift/src/main/resources*.\nFor the change to be effective, you will need to regenerate the classes.\nYou can use maven profile `compile-thrift` to do this."},"1751":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-65","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-thrift","content":"You may also want to define `thrift.path` for the thrift binary, using the following command:"},"1752":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-66","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-a-tarball","content":"You can build a tarball without going through the release process described in Releasing Apache HBase, by running the following command:"},"1753":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-67","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-a-tarball","content":"The distribution tarball is built in `hbase-assembly/target/hbase-<version>-bin.tar.gz`."},"1754":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-68","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-a-tarball","content":"You can install or deploy the tarball by having the assembly:single goal before install or deploy in the maven command:"},"1755":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-69","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#maven-site-failure","content":"If you see `Unable to find resource 'VM_global_library.vm'`, ignore it.\nIt's not an error.\nIt is officially ugly though."},"1756":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-70","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#build-on-linux-aarch64","content":"HBase runs on both Windows and UNIX-like systems, and it should run on any platform\nthat runs a supported version of Java. This should include JVMs on x86\\_64 and aarch64.\nThe documentation below describes how to build hbase on aarch64 platform."},"1757":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-71","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#set-environment-variables","content":"Manually install Java and Maven on aarch64 servers if they are not installed,\nand set environment variables. For example:"},"1758":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-72","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#use-protobuf-supported-on-aarch64","content":"Now HBase uses protobuf of two versions. Version '3.11.4' of protobuf that hbase uses\ninternally and version '2.5.0' as external usage.\nPackage protoc-2.5.0 does not work on aarch64 platform, we should add maven\nprofile '-Paarch64' when building. It downloads protoc-2.5.0 package from maven\nrepository which we made on aarch64 platform locally."},"1759":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building-73","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/building","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/building#use-protobuf-supported-on-aarch64","content":"Protobuf is released with aarch64 protoc since version '3.5.0', and we are planning to upgrade\nprotobuf later, then we don't have to add the profile '-Paarch64' anymore."},"1760":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"page","content":"Developer Guidelines","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Building and Developing Apache HBase"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines"},"1761":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-0","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","content":"Code standards, interface classifications, formatting conventions, Git best practices, and patch submission guidelines for HBase contributors."},"1762":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-1","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines#branches","content":"Branches"},"1763":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-2","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines#policy-for-fix-version-in-jira","content":"Policy for Fix Version in JIRA"},"1764":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-3","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines#code-standards","content":"Code Standards"},"1765":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-4","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines#interface-classifications","content":"Interface Classifications"},"1766":{"id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines-5","page_id":"/docs/building-and-developing/developer-guidelines","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/building-and-develop