Case Studies","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Performance & Troubleshooting"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies"},"2376":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-0","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies","content":"Performance and troubleshooting case studies for diagnosing Apache HBase cluster issues."},"2377":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-1","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-studies-overview","content":"Overview"},"2378":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-2","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-studies-schema-design","content":"Schema Design"},"2379":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-3","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#performancetroubleshooting","content":"Performance/Troubleshooting"},"2380":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-4","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-1-performance-issue-on-a-single-node","content":"Case Study #1 (Performance Issue On A Single Node)"},"2381":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-5","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#scenario","content":"Scenario"},"2382":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-6","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Hardware"},"2383":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-7","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"Hypotheses"},"2384":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-8","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#resolution","content":"Resolution"},"2385":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-9","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-2-performance-research-2012","content":"Case Study #2 (Performance Research 2012)"},"2386":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-10","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-3-performance-research-2010","content":"Case Study #3 (Performance Research 2010)"},"2387":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-11","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-4-maxtransferthreads-config","content":"Case Study #4 (max.transfer.threads Config)"},"2388":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-12","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-studies-overview","content":"This chapter will describe a variety of performance and troubleshooting case studies that can provide a useful blueprint on diagnosing Apache HBase cluster issues."},"2389":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-13","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-studies-overview","content":"For more information on Performance and Troubleshooting, see Apache HBase Performance Tuning and Troubleshooting and Debugging Apache HBase."},"2390":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-14","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-studies-schema-design","content":"See the schema design case studies here: Schema Design Case Studies"},"2391":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-15","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#scenario","content":"Following a scheduled reboot, one data node began exhibiting unusual behavior.\nRoutine MapReduce jobs run against HBase tables which regularly completed in five or six minutes began taking 30 or 40 minutes to finish.\nThese jobs were consistently found to be waiting on map and reduce tasks assigned to the troubled data node (e.g., the slow map tasks all had the same Input Split). The situation came to a head during a distributed copy, when the copy was severely prolonged by the lagging node."},"2392":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-16","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Datanodes:"},"2393":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-17","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Two 12-core processors"},"2394":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-18","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Six Enterprise SATA disks"},"2395":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-19","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"24GB of RAM"},"2396":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-20","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Two bonded gigabit NICs"},"2397":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-21","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"Network:"},"2398":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-22","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"10 Gigabit top-of-rack switches"},"2399":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-23","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hardware","content":"20 Gigabit bonded interconnects between racks."},"2400":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-24","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"**HBase \"Hot Spot\" Region**\\\nWe hypothesized that we were experiencing a familiar point of pain: a \"hot spot\" region in an HBase table, where uneven key-space distribution can funnel a huge number of requests to a single HBase region, bombarding the RegionServer process and cause slow response time.\nExamination of the HBase Master status page showed that the number of HBase requests to the troubled node was almost zero.\nFurther, examination of the HBase logs showed that there were no region splits, compactions, or other region transitions in progress.\nThis effectively ruled out a \"hot spot\" as the root cause of the observed slowness."},"2401":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-25","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"**HBase Region With Non-Local Data**\\\nOur next hypothesis was that one of the MapReduce tasks was requesting data from HBase that was not local to the DataNode, thus forcing HDFS to request data blocks from other servers over the network.\nExamination of the DataNode logs showed that there were very few blocks being requested over the network, indicating that the HBase region was correctly assigned, and that the majority of the necessary data was located on the node.\nThis ruled out the possibility of non-local data causing a slowdown."},"2402":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-26","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"**Excessive I/O Wait Due To Swapping Or An Over-Worked Or Failing Hard Disk**\\\nAfter concluding that the Hadoop and HBase were not likely to be the culprits, we moved on to troubleshooting the DataNode's hardware.\nJava, by design, will periodically scan its entire memory space to do garbage collection.\nIf system memory is heavily overcommitted, the Linux kernel may enter a vicious cycle, using up all of its resources swapping Java heap back and forth from disk to RAM as Java tries to run garbage collection.\nFurther, a failing hard disk will often retry reads and/or writes many times before giving up and returning an error.\nThis can manifest as high iowait, as running processes wait for reads and writes to complete.\nFinally, a disk nearing the upper edge of its performance envelope will begin to cause iowait as it informs the kernel that it cannot accept any more data, and the kernel queues incoming data into the dirty write pool in memory.\nHowever, using `vmstat(1)` and `free(1)`, we could see that no swap was being used, and the amount of disk IO was only a few kilobytes per second."},"2403":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-27","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"**Slowness Due To High Processor Usage**\\\nNext, we checked to see whether the system was performing slowly simply due to very high computational load. `top(1)` showed that the system load was higher than normal, but `vmstat(1)` and `mpstat(1)` showed that the amount of processor being used for actual computation was low."},"2404":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-28","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"**Network Saturation (The Winner)**\\\nSince neither the disks nor the processors were being utilized heavily, we moved on to the performance of the network interfaces.\nThe DataNode had two gigabit ethernet adapters, bonded to form an active-standby interface. `ifconfig(8)` showed some unusual anomalies, namely interface errors, overruns, framing errors.\nWhile not unheard of, these kinds of errors are exceedingly rare on modern hardware which is operating as it should:"},"2405":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-29","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"These errors immediately lead us to suspect that one or more of the ethernet interfaces might have negotiated the wrong line speed.\nThis was confirmed both by running an ICMP ping from an external host and observing round-trip-time in excess of 700ms, and by running `ethtool(8)` on the members of the bond interface and discovering that the active interface was operating at 100Mbs/, full duplex."},"2406":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-30","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#hypotheses","content":"In normal operation, the ICMP ping round trip time should be around 20ms, and the interface speed and duplex should read, \"1000MB/s\", and, \"Full\", respectively."},"2407":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-31","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#resolution","content":"After determining that the active ethernet adapter was at the incorrect speed, we used the `ifenslave(8)` command to make the standby interface the active interface, which yielded an immediate improvement in MapReduce performance, and a 10 times improvement in network throughput:"},"2408":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-32","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#resolution","content":"On the next trip to the datacenter, we determined that the line speed issue was ultimately caused by a bad network cable, which was replaced."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-33","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-2-performance-research-2012","content":"Investigation results of a self-described \"we're not sure what's wrong, but it seems slow\" problem. http\\://gbif.blogspot.com/2012/03/hbase-performance-evaluation-continued.html"},"2410":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-34","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-3-performance-research-2010","content":"Investigation results of general cluster performance from 2010.\nAlthough this research is on an older version of the codebase, this writeup is still very useful in terms of approach. https\\://web.archive.org/web/20180503124332/http\\://hstack.org/hbase-performance-testing/"},"2411":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-35","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-4-maxtransferthreads-config","content":"Case study of configuring `max.transfer.threads` (previously known as `xcievers`) and diagnosing errors from misconfigurations. http\\://www\\.larsgeorge.com/2012/03/hadoop-hbase-and-xceivers.html"},"2412":{"id":"/docs/case-studies-36","page_id":"/docs/case-studies","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/case-studies#case-study-4-maxtransferthreads-config","content":"See also `dfs.datanode.max.transfer.threads`."},"2413":{"id":"/docs/community","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"page","content":"Community","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Infrastructure & Tools"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community"},"2414":{"id":"/docs/community-0","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community","content":"HBase community decisions, release managers, JIRA policies, commit message format, and guidelines for feature branches."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/community-1","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#decisions","content":"Decisions"},"2416":{"id":"/docs/community-2","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#feature-branches","content":"Feature Branches"},"2417":{"id":"/docs/community-3","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"How to set fix version in JIRA on issue resolve"},"2418":{"id":"/docs/community-4","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#policy-on-when-to-set-a-resolved-jira-as-closed","content":"Policy on when to set a RESOLVED JIRA as CLOSED"},"2419":{"id":"/docs/community-5","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#only-transient-state-in-zookeeper","content":"Only transient state in ZooKeeper!"},"2420":{"id":"/docs/community-6","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#community-roles","content":"Community Roles"},"2421":{"id":"/docs/community-7","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Release Managers"},"2422":{"id":"/docs/community-8","page_id":"/docs/community","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/community#commit-message-format","content":"Commit Message format"},"2423":{"id":"/docs/community-9","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#feature-branches","content":"Feature Branches are easy to make. You do not have to be a committer to make one. Just request the name of your branch be added to JIRA up on the developer's mailing list and a committer will add it for you. Thereafter you can file issues against your feature branch in Apache HBase JIRA. Your code you keep elsewhere — it should be public so it can be observed — and you can update dev mailing list on progress. When the feature is ready for commit, 3 +1s from committers will get your feature merged. See HBase, mail # dev - Thoughts about large feature dev branches"},"2424":{"id":"/docs/community-10","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Here is how we agreed to set versions in JIRA when we resolve an issue. If master is going to be 3.0.0, branch-2 will be 2.4.0, and branch-1 will be 1.7.0 then:"},"2425":{"id":"/docs/community-11","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit only to master (i.e., backward-incompatible new feature): Mark with 3.0.0"},"2426":{"id":"/docs/community-12","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit only to master and branch-2 (i.e., backward-compatible new feature, applicable only to 2.x+): Mark with 3.0.0 and 2.4.0"},"2427":{"id":"/docs/community-13","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit to master, branch-2, and branch-1 (i.e., backward-compatible new feature, applicable everywhere): Mark with 3.0.0, 2.4.0, and 1.7.0"},"2428":{"id":"/docs/community-14","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit to master, branch-2, and branch-2.3, branch-1, branch-1.4 (i.e., bug fix applicable to all active release lines): Mark with 3.0.0, 2.4.0, 2.3.x, 1.7.0, and 1.4.x"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/community-15","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit a fix to the website: no version"},"2430":{"id":"/docs/community-16","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#policy-on-when-to-set-a-resolved-jira-as-closed","content":"We agreed that for issues that list multiple releases in their *Fix Version/s* field, CLOSE the issue on the release of any of the versions listed; subsequent change to the issue must happen in a new JIRA."},"2431":{"id":"/docs/community-17","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#only-transient-state-in-zookeeper","content":"You should be able to kill the data in zookeeper and hbase should ride over it recreating the zk content as it goes. This is an old adage around these parts. We just made note of it now. We also are currently in violation of this basic tenet — replication at least keeps permanent state in zk — but we are working to undo this breaking of a golden rule."},"2432":{"id":"/docs/community-18","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Each maintained release branch has a release manager, who volunteers to coordinate new features and bug fixes are backported to that release. The release managers are committers. If you would like your feature or bug fix to be included in a given release, communicate with that release manager. If this list goes out of date or you can't reach the listed person, reach out to someone else on the list."},"2433":{"id":"/docs/community-19","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"**Release Managers:**"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/community-20","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Release"},"2435":{"id":"/docs/community-21","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Release Manager"},"2436":{"id":"/docs/community-22","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Latest Release"},"2437":{"id":"/docs/community-23","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"EOL"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/community-24","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"0.94"},"2439":{"id":"/docs/community-25","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"Lars Hofhansl"},"2440":{"id":"/docs/community-26","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"0.94.27"},"2441":{"id":"/docs/community-27","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","content":"April 2017"},"2442":{"id":"/docs/community-28","page_id":"/docs/community","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/community#release-managers","con