:{"id":"/docs/compression-289","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#compressor-microbenchmarks","content":"81.32%"},"2803":{"id":"/docs/compression-290","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#compressor-support-on-the-master","content":"A new configuration setting was introduced in HBase 0.95, to check the Master to determine which data block encoders are installed and configured on it, and assume that the entire cluster is configured the same.\nThis option, `hbase.master.check.compression`, defaults to `true`.\nThis prevents the situation described in HBASE-6370, where a table is created or modified to support a codec that a region server does not support, leading to failures that take a long time to occur and are difficult to debug."},"2804":{"id":"/docs/compression-291","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#compressor-support-on-the-master","content":"If `hbase.master.check.compression` is enabled, libraries for all desired compressors need to be installed and configured on the Master, even if the Master does not run a region server."},"2805":{"id":"/docs/compression-292","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-gz-support-via-native-libraries","content":"HBase uses Java's built-in GZip support unless the native Hadoop libraries are available on the CLASSPATH.\nThe recommended way to add libraries to the CLASSPATH is to set the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the user running HBase.\nIf native libraries are not available and Java's GZIP is used, `Got brand-new compressor` reports will be present in the logs.\nSee brand.new\\.compressor)."},"2806":{"id":"/docs/compression-293","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-lzo-support","content":"HBase cannot ship with the Hadoop native LZO codc because of incompatibility between HBase, which uses an Apache Software License (ASL) and LZO, which uses a GPL license.\nSee the Hadoop-LZO at Twitter for information on configuring LZO support for HBase."},"2807":{"id":"/docs/compression-294","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-lzo-support","content":"If you depend upon LZO compression, consider using the pure Java and ASL licensed\nAirCompressor LZO codec option instead of the Hadoop native default, or configure your\nRegionServers to fail to start if native LZO support is not available.\nSee hbase.regionserver.codecs."},"2808":{"id":"/docs/compression-295","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"LZ4 support is bundled with Hadoop and is the default LZ4 codec implementation.\nIt is not required that you make use of the Hadoop LZ4 codec. Our LZ4 codec implemented\nwith lz4-java offers superior performance, and the AirCompressor LZ4 codec offers a\npure Java option for use where native support is not available."},"2809":{"id":"/docs/compression-296","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"That said, if you prefer the Hadoop option, make sure the hadoop shared library\n(libhadoop.so) is accessible when you start HBase.\nAfter configuring your platform (see hadoop.native.lib), you can\nmake a symbolic link from HBase to the native Hadoop libraries. This assumes the two\nsoftware installs are colocated. For example, if my 'platform' is Linux-amd64-64:"},"2810":{"id":"/docs/compression-297","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"Use the compression tool to check that LZ4 is installed on all nodes.\nStart up (or restart) HBase.\nAfterward, you can create and alter tables to enable LZ4 as a compression codec.:"},"2811":{"id":"/docs/compression-298","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"Snappy support is bundled with Hadoop and is the default Snappy codec implementation.\nIt is not required that you make use of the Hadoop Snappy codec. Our Snappy codec\nimplemented with Xerial Snappy offers superior performance, and the AirCompressor\nSnappy codec offers a pure Java option for use where native support is not available."},"2812":{"id":"/docs/compression-299","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"That said, if you prefer the Hadoop codec option, you can install Snappy binaries (for\ninstance, by using +yum install snappy+ on CentOS) or build Snappy from source.\nAfter installing Snappy, search for the shared library, which will be called *libsnappy.so.X&#x2A; where X is a number.\nIf you built from source, copy the shared library to a known location on your system, such as &#x2A;/opt/snappy/lib/*."},"2813":{"id":"/docs/compression-300","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"In addition to the Snappy library, HBase also needs access to the Hadoop shared library, which will be called something like *libhadoop.so.X.Y*, where X and Y are both numbers.\nMake note of the location of the Hadoop library, or copy it to the same location as the Snappy library."},"2814":{"id":"/docs/compression-301","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"The Snappy and Hadoop libraries need to be available on each node of your cluster.\nSee compression.test to find out how to test that this is the case."},"2815":{"id":"/docs/compression-302","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"See hbase.regionserver.codecs to configure your RegionServers to fail to start if a given compressor is not available."},"2816":{"id":"/docs/compression-303","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"Each of these library locations need to be added to the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the operating system user that runs HBase.\nYou need to restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."},"2817":{"id":"/docs/compression-304","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#compressiontest","content":"You can use the CompressionTest tool to verify that your compressor is available to HBase:"},"2818":{"id":"/docs/compression-305","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#enforce-compression-settings-on-a-regionserver","content":"You can configure a RegionServer so that it will fail to restart if compression is configured incorrectly, by adding the option hbase.regionserver.codecs to the *hbase-site.xml*, and setting its value to a comma-separated list of codecs that need to be available.\nFor example, if you set this property to `lzo,gz`, the RegionServer would fail to start if both compressors were not available.\nThis would prevent a new server from being added to the cluster without having codecs configured properly."},"2819":{"id":"/docs/compression-306","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#enable-compression-on-a-columnfamily","content":"To enable compression for a ColumnFamily, use an `alter` command.\nYou do not need to re-create the table or copy data.\nIf you are changing codecs, be sure the old codec is still available until all the old StoreFiles have been compacted."},"2820":{"id":"/docs/compression-307","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#testing-compression-performance","content":"HBase includes a tool called LoadTestTool which provides mechanisms to test your compression performance.\nYou must specify either `-write` or `-update-read` as your first parameter, and if you do not specify another parameter, usage advice is printed for each option."},"2821":{"id":"/docs/compression-308","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#testing-compression-performance","content":"**`LoadTestTool` Usage**"},"2822":{"id":"/docs/compression-309","page_id":"/docs/compression","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/compression#enable-data-block-encoding","content":"Codecs are built into HBase so no extra configuration is needed.\nCodecs are enabled on a table by setting the `DATA_BLOCK_ENCODING` property.\nDisable the table before altering its DATA\\_BLOCK\\_ENCODING setting.\nFollowing is an example using HBase Shell:"},"2823":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"page","content":"Basic Prerequisites","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Configuration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites"},"2824":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-0","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","content":"This section lists required services and some required system configuration."},"2825":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-1","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"Java"},"2826":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-2","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#java-support-by-release-line","content":"Java support by release line"},"2827":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-3","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#operating-system-utilities","content":"Operating System Utilities"},"2828":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-4","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#ssh","content":"ssh"},"2829":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-5","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#dns","content":"DNS"},"2830":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-6","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#ntp","content":"NTP"},"2831":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-7","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#limits-on-number-of-files-and-processes-ulimit","content":"Limits on Number of Files and Processes (ulimit)"},"2832":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-8","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#example-ulimit-settings-on-ubuntu-toc","content":"Example: `ulimit` Settings on Ubuntu [!toc]"},"2833":{"id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites-9","page_id":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/basic-prerequisites#linux-shell","content":"Linux Shell"},"2834":{"id":"/docs/configuration/bas