ike."},"3837":{"id":"/docs/configuration/example-7","page_id":"/docs/configuration/example","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/example#regionservers","content":"In this file you list the nodes that will run RegionServers. In our case, these nodes are `example1`-`example9`."},"3838":{"id":"/docs/configuration/example-8","page_id":"/docs/configuration/example","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/example#configuration-example-basic-distributed-hbase-install-hbase-env-sh","content":"The following lines in the *hbase-env.sh* file show how to set the `JAVA_HOME` environment variable (required for HBase) and set the heap to 4 GB (rather than the default value of 1 GB). If you copy and paste this example, be sure to adjust the `JAVA_HOME` to suit your environment."},"3839":{"id":"/docs/configuration/example-9","page_id":"/docs/configuration/example","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/example#configuration-example-basic-distributed-hbase-install-hbase-env-sh","content":"Use rsync to copy the content of the *conf* directory to all nodes of the cluster."},"3840":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"page","content":"HBase run modes: Standalone and Distributed","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Configuration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models"},"3841":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-0","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","content":"Understanding HBase deployment modes: standalone mode for development and distributed mode for production clusters."},"3842":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-1","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase","content":"Standalone HBase"},"3843":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-2","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase-over-hdfs","content":"Standalone HBase over HDFS"},"3844":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-3","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed","content":"Distributed"},"3845":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-4","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#pseudo-distributed","content":"Pseudo-distributed"},"3846":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-5","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#fully-distributed","content":"Fully-distributed"},"3847":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-6","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed-regionservers","content":"Distributed RegionServers"},"3848":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-7","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#zookeeper-and-hbase","content":"ZooKeeper and HBase"},"3849":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-8","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#example-distributed-hbase-cluster","content":"Example: Distributed HBase Cluster"},"3850":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-9","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed-hbase-quickstart","content":"Distributed HBase Quickstart"},"3851":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-10","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"Procedure: HDFS Client Configuration"},"3852":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-11","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#choosing-between-the-classic-package-and-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"Choosing between the Classic Package and the BYO Hadoop Package"},"3853":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-12","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#advantages-of-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"Advantages of the BYO Hadoop package:"},"3854":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-13","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","content":"HBase has two run modes: standalone and distributed. Out of the box, HBase runs in standalone mode. Whatever your mode, you will need to configure HBase by editing files in the HBase *conf* directory. At a minimum, you must edit `conf/hbase-env.sh` to tell HBase which java to use. In this file you set HBase environment variables such as the heapsize and other options for the `JVM`, the preferred location for log files, etc. Set `JAVA_HOME` to point at the root of your java install."},"3855":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-14","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase","content":"This is the default mode. Standalone mode is what is described in the quickstart section. In standalone mode, HBase does not use HDFS — it uses the local filesystem instead — and it runs all HBase daemons and a local ZooKeeper all up in the same JVM. ZooKeeper binds to a well-known port so clients may talk to HBase."},"3856":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-15","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase-over-hdfs","content":"A sometimes useful variation on standalone hbase has all daemons running inside the one JVM but rather than persist to the local filesystem, instead they persist to an HDFS instance."},"3857":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-16","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase-over-hdfs","content":"You might consider this profile when you are intent on a simple deploy profile, the loading is light, but the data must persist across node comings and goings. Writing to HDFS where data is replicated ensures the latter."},"3858":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-17","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#standalone-hbase-over-hdfs","content":"To configure this standalone variant, edit your *hbase-site.xml* setting *hbase.rootdir* to point at a directory in your HDFS instance but then set *hbase.cluster.distributed* to *false*. For example:"},"3859":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-18","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed","content":"Distributed mode can be subdivided into distributed but all daemons run on a single node — a.k.a. *pseudo-distributed* — and *fully-distributed* where the daemons are spread across all nodes in the cluster. The *pseudo-distributed* vs. *fully-distributed* nomenclature comes from Hadoop."},"3860":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-19","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed","content":"Pseudo-distributed mode can run against the local filesystem or it can run against an instance of the *Hadoop Distributed File System* (HDFS). Fully-distributed mode can ONLY run on HDFS. See the Hadoop documentation for how to set up HDFS. A good walk-through for setting up HDFS on Hadoop 2 can be found at https\\://web.archive.org/web/20221007121526/https\\://www\\.alexjf.net/blog/distributed-systems/hadoop-yarn-installation-definitive-guide/."},"3861":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-20","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#pseudo-distributed","content":"A quickstart has been added to the\nquickstart chapter. See\nquickstart-pseudo. Some of the\ninformation that was originally in this section has been moved there."},"3862":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-21","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#pseudo-distributed","content":"A pseudo-distributed mode is simply a fully-distributed mode run on a single host. Use this HBase configuration for testing and prototyping purposes only. Do not use this configuration for production or for performance evaluation."},"3863":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-22","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#fully-distributed","content":"By default, HBase runs in stand-alone mode. Both stand-alone mode and pseudo-distributed mode are provided for the purposes of small-scale testing. For a production environment, distributed mode is advised. In distributed mode, multiple instances of HBase daemons run on multiple servers in the cluster."},"3864":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-23","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#fully-distributed","content":"Just as in pseudo-distributed mode, a fully distributed configuration requires that you set the `hbase.cluster.distributed` property to `true`. Typically, the `hbase.rootdir` is configured to point to a highly-available HDFS filesystem."},"3865":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-24","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#fully-distributed","content":"In addition, the cluster is configured so that multiple cluster nodes enlist as RegionServers, ZooKeeper QuorumPeers, and backup HMaster servers. These configuration basics are all demonstrated in quickstart-fully-distributed."},"3866":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-25","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed-regionservers","content":"Typically, your cluster will contain multiple RegionServers all running on different servers, as well as primary and backup Master and ZooKeeper daemons. The *conf/regionservers* file on the master server contains a list of hosts whose RegionServers are associated with this cluster. Each host is on a separate line. All hosts listed in this file will have their RegionServer processes started and stopped when the master server starts or stops."},"3867":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-26","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#zookeeper-and-hbase","content":"See the ZooKeeper section for ZooKeeper setup instructions for HBase."},"3868":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-27","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#example-distributed-hbase-cluster","content":"This is a bare-bones *conf/hbase-site.xml* for a distributed HBase cluster. A cluster that is used for real-world work would contain more custom configuration parameters. Most HBase configuration directives have default values, which are used unless the value is overridden in the *hbase-site.xml*. See \"Configuration Files\" for more information."},"3869":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-28","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#example-distributed-hbase-cluster","content":"This is an example *conf/regionservers* file, which contains a list of nodes that should run a RegionServer in the cluster. These nodes need HBase installed and they need to use the same contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory as the Master server."},"3870":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-29","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#example-distributed-hbase-cluster","content":"This is an example *conf/backup-masters* file, which contains a list of each node that should run a backup Master instance. The backup Master instances will sit idle unless the main Master becomes unavailable."},"3871":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-30","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#distributed-hbase-quickstart","content":"See quickstart-fully-distributed for a walk-through of a simple three-node cluster configuration with multiple ZooKeeper, backup HMaster, and RegionServer instances."},"3872":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-31","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"Of note, if you have made HDFS client configuration changes on your Hadoop cluster, such as configuration directives for HDFS clients, as opposed to server-side configurations, you must use one of the following methods to enable HBase to see and use these configuration changes:"},"3873":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-32","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"Add a pointer to your `HADOOP_CONF_DIR` to the `HBASE_CLASSPATH` environment variable in *hbase-env.sh*."},"3874":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-33","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"Add a copy of *hdfs-site.xml* (or *hadoop-site.xml*) or, better, symlinks, under `$HBASE_HOME/conf`, or"},"3875":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-34","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"if only a small set of HDFS client configurations, add them to *hbase-site.xml*."},"3876":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-35","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#procedure-hdfs-client-configuration","content":"An example of such an HDFS client configuration is `dfs.replication`. If for example, you want to run with a replication factor of 5, HBase will create files with the default of 3 unless you do the above to make the configuration available to HBase."},"3877":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-36","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#choosing-between-the-classic-package-and-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"Starting with HBase 3.0, HBase includes two binary packages. The classic package includes both the HBase and Hadoop components, while the Hadoop-less \"Bring Your Own Hadoop\" package omits the Hadoop components, and uses the files from an existing Hadoop installation. The classic binary package filename is named `hbase-VERSION-bin.tar.gz` (e.g. *hbase-3.0.0-bin.tar.gz*), while the Hadoop-less package is `hbase-byo-hadoop-VERSION-bin.tar.gz` (e.g. *hbase-byo-hadoop-3.0.0-bin.tar.gz*)."},"3878":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-37","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#choosing-between-the-classic-package-and-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"If the cluster nodes already have Hadoop installed, you can use the Hadoop-less package. In this case you need to make sure that the `HADOOP_HOME` environment variable is set and points to the Hadoop installation. The easiest way to ensure this is to set it in *hbase-env.sh*. You still need to make sure that the Hadoop configuration files are present on the HBase classpath, as described above."},"3879":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-38","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#advantages-of-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"There is no need to replace the Hadoop libraries."},"3880":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-39","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#advantages-of-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"It is easier to upgrade Hadoop and HBase independently (as long as compatible versions are used)."},"3881":{"id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models-40","page_id":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/hbase-run-models#advantages-of-the-byo-hadoop-package","content":"Both the package and installed size are about 100 MB smaller."},"3882":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"page","content":"The Important Configurations","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Configuration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important"},"3883":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-0","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important","content":"Critical HBase configurations divided into required settings and recommended optimizations for production clusters."},"3884":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-1","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#required-configurations","content":"Required Configurations"},"3885":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-2","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#big-cluster-configurations","content":"Big Cluster Configurations"},"3886":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-3","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#recommended-configurations","content":"Recommended Configurations"},"3887":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-4","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#zookeeper-configuration","content":"ZooKeeper Configuration"},"3888":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-5","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#zookeepersessiontimeout","content":"`zookeeper.session.timeout`"},"3889":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-6","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#number-of-zookeeper-instances","content":"Number of ZooKeeper Instances"},"3890":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-7","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#hdfs-configurations","content":"HDFS Configurations"},"3891":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-8","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#dfsdatanodefailedvolumestolerated","content":"`dfs.datanode.failed.volumes.tolerated`"},"3892":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-9","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"`hbase.regionserver.handler.count`"},"3893":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-10","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-for-large-memory-machines","content":"Configuration for large memory machines"},"3894":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-11","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-recommended-configurations-compression","content":"Compression"},"3895":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-12","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuring-the-size-and-number-of-wal-files","content":"Configuring the size and number of WAL files"},"3896":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-13","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-splitting","content":"Managed Splitting"},"3897":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-14","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#disable-automatic-splitting","content":"Disable Automatic Splitting"},"3898":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-15","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#determine-the-optimal-number-of-pre-split-regions","content":"Determine the Optimal Number of Pre-Split Regions"},"3899":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-16","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-compactions","content":"Managed Compactions"},"3900":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-17","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-recommended-configurations-speculative-execution","content":"Speculative Execution"},"3901":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-18","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#other-configurations","content":"Other Configurations"},"3902":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-19","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#balancer","content":"Balancer"},"3903":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-20","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#disabling-blockcache","content":"Disabling Blockcache"},"3904":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-21","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#nagles-or-the-small-package-problem","content":"Nagle's or the small package problem"},"3905":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-22","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#better-mean-time-to-recover-mttr","content":"Better Mean Time to Recover (MTTR)"},"3906":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-23","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"JMX"},"3907":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-24","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#required-configurations","content":"Review the os and hadoop sections."},"3908":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-25","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#big-cluster-configurations","content":"If you have a cluster with a lot of regions, it is possible that a Regionserver checks in briefly after the Master starts while all the remaining RegionServers lag behind. This first server to check in will be assigned all regions which is not optimal. To prevent the above scenario from happening, up the `hbase.master.wait.on.regionservers.mintostart` property from its default value of 1. See HBASE-6389 Modify the conditions to ensure that Master waits for sufficient number of Region Servers before starting region assignments for more detail."},"3909":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-26","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#zookeepersessiontimeout","content":"The default timeout is 90 seconds (specified in milliseconds). This means that if a server crashes, it will be 90 seconds before the Master notices the crash and starts recovery. You might need to tune the timeout down to a minute or even less so the Master notices failures sooner. Before changing this value, be sure you have your JVM garbage collection configuration under control, otherwise, a long garbage collection that lasts beyond the ZooKeeper session timeout will take out your RegionServer. (You might be fine with this — you probably want recovery to start on the server if a RegionServer has been in GC for a long period of time)."},"3910":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-27","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#zookeepersessiontimeout","content":"To change this configuration, edit *hbase-site.xml*, copy the changed file across the cluster and restart."},"3911":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-28","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#zookeepersessiontimeout","content":"We set this value high to save our having to field questions up on the mailing lists asking why a RegionServer went down during a massive import. The usual cause is that their JVM is untuned and they are running into long GC pauses. Our thinking is that while users are getting familiar with HBase, we'd save them having to know all of its intricacies. Later when they've built some confidence, then they can play with configuration such as this."},"3912":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-29","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#number-of-zookeeper-instances","content":"See zookeeper."},"3913":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-30","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#dfsdatanodefailedvolumestolerated","content":"This is the \"...number of volumes that are allowed to fail before a DataNode stops offering service. By default, any volume failure will cause a datanode to shutdown\" from the *hdfs-default.xml* description. You might want to set this to about half the amount of your available disks."},"3914":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-31","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"This setting defines the number of threads that are kept open to answer incoming requests to user tables. The rule of thumb is to keep this number low when the payload per request approaches the MB (big puts, scans using a large cache) and high when the payload is small (gets, small puts, ICVs, deletes). The total size of the queries in progress is limited by the setting `hbase.ipc.server.max.callqueue.size`."},"3915":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-32","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"It is safe to set that number to the maximum number of incoming clients if their payload is small, the typical example being a cluster that serves a website since puts aren't typically buffered and most of the operations are gets."},"3916":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-33","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"The reason why it is dangerous to keep this setting high is that the aggregate size of all the puts that are currently happening in a region server may impose too much pressure on its memory, or even trigger an OutOfMemoryError. A RegionServer running on low memory will trigger its JVM's garbage collector to run more frequently up to a point where GC pauses become noticeable (the reason being that all the memory used to keep all the requests' payloads cannot be trashed, no matter how hard the garbage collector tries). After some time, the overall cluster throughput is affected since every request that hits that RegionServer will take longer, which exacerbates the problem even more."},"3917":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-34","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"You can get a sense of whether you have too little or too many handlers by rpc.logging on an individual RegionServer then tailing its logs (Queued requests consume memory)."},"3918":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-35","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-for-large-memory-machines","content":"HBase ships with a reasonable, conservative configuration that will work on nearly all machine types that people might want to test with. If you have larger machines — HBase has 8G and larger heap — you might find the following configuration options helpful. TODO."},"3919":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-36","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-recommended-configurations-compression","content":"You should consider enabling ColumnFamily compression. There are several options that are near-frictionless and in most all cases boost performance by reducing the size of StoreFiles and thus reducing I/O."},"3920":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-37","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-recommended-configurations-compression","content":"See compression for more information."},"3921":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-38","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuring-the-size-and-number-of-wal-files","content":"HBase uses \\[wal]/docs/architecture/regionserver#write-ahead-log-wal to recover the memstore data that has not been flushed to disk in case of an RS failure. These WAL files should be configured to be slightly smaller than HDFS block (by default a HDFS block is 64Mb and a WAL file is \\~60Mb)."},"3922":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-39","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuring-the-size-and-number-of-wal-files","content":"HBase also has a limit on the number of WAL files, designed to ensure there's never too much data that needs to be replayed during recovery. This limit needs to be set according to memstore configuration, so that all the necessary data would fit. It is recommended to allocate enough WAL files to store at least that much data (when all memstores are close to full). For example, with 16Gb RS heap, default memstore settings (0.4), and default WAL file size (\\~60Mb), 16Gb\\*0.4/60, the starting point for WAL file count is \\~109. However, as all memstores are not expected to be full all the time, less WAL files can be allocated."},"3923":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-40","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-splitting","content":"HBase generally handles splitting of your regions based upon the settings in your *hbase-default.xml* and *hbase-site.xml* configuration files. Important settings include `hbase.regionserver.region.split.policy`, `hbase.hregion.max.filesize`, `hbase.regionserver.regionSplitLimit`. A simplistic view of splitting is that when a region grows to `hbase.hregion.max.filesize`, it is split. For most usage patterns, you should use automatic splitting. See manual region splitting decisions for more information about manual region splitting."},"3924":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-41","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-splitting","content":"Instead of allowing HBase to split your regions automatically, you can choose to manage the splitting yourself. Manually managing splits works if you know your keyspace well, otherwise let HBase figure where to split for you. Manual splitting can mitigate region creation and movement under load. It also makes it so region boundaries are known and invariant (if you disable region splitting). If you use manual splits, it is easier doing staggered, time-based major compactions to spread out your network IO load."},"3925":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-42","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#disable-automatic-splitting","content":"To disable automatic splitting, you can set region split policy in either cluster configuration or table configuration to be `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DisabledRegionSplitPolicy`"},"3926":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-43","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#disable-automatic-splitting","content":"If you disable automatic splits to diagnose a problem or during a period of fast data growth, it\nis recommended to re-enable them when your situation becomes more stable. The potential benefits\nof managing region splits yourself are not undisputed."},"3927":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-44","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#determine-the-optimal-number-of-pre-split-regions","content":"The optimal number of pre-split regions depends on your application and environment. A good rule of thumb is to start with 10 pre-split regions per server and watch as data grows over time. It is better to err on the side of too few regions and perform rolling splits later. The optimal number of regions depends upon the largest StoreFile in your region. The size of the largest StoreFile will increase with time if the amount of data grows. The goal is for the largest region to be just large enough that the compaction selection algorithm only compacts it during a timed major compaction. Otherwise, the cluster can be prone to compaction storms with a large number of regions under compaction at the same time. It is important to understand that the data growth causes compaction storms and not the manual split decision."},"3928":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-45","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#determine-the-optimal-number-of-pre-split-regions","content":"If the regions are split into too many large regions, you can increase the major compaction interval by configuring `HConstants.MAJOR_COMPACTION_PERIOD`. The `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.util.RegionSplitter` utility also provides a network-IO-safe rolling split of all regions."},"3929":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-46","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-compactions","content":"By default, major compactions are scheduled to run once in a 7-day period."},"3930":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-47","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-compactions","content":"If you need to control exactly when and how often major compaction runs, you can disable managed major compactions. See the entry for `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction` in the compaction.parameters table for details."},"3931":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-48","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-compactions","content":"Major compactions are absolutely necessary for StoreFile clean-up. Do not disable them altogether.\nYou can run major compactions manually via the HBase shell or via the Admin\nAPI."},"3932":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-49","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#managed-compactions","content":"For more information about compactions and the compaction file selection process, see compaction"},"3933":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-50","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#configuration-important-recommended-configurations-speculative-execution","content":"Speculative Execution of MapReduce tasks is on by default, and for HBase clusters it is generally advised to turn off Speculative Execution at a system-level unless you need it for a specific case, where it can be configured per-job. Set the properties `mapreduce.map.speculative` and `mapreduce.reduce.speculative` to false."},"3934":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-51","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#balancer","content":"The balancer is a periodic operation which is run on the master to redistribute regions on the cluster. It is configured via `hbase.balancer.period` and defaults to 300000 (5 minutes)."},"3935":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-52","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#balancer","content":"See master.processes.loadbalancer for more information on the LoadBalancer."},"3936":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-53","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#disabling-blockcache","content":"Do not turn off block cache (You'd do it by setting `hfile.block.cache.size` to zero). Currently, we do not do well if you do this because the RegionServer will spend all its time loading HFile indices over and over again. If your working set is such that block cache does you no good, at least size the block cache such that HFile indices will stay up in the cache (you can get a rough idea on the size you need by surveying RegionServer UIs; you'll see index block size accounted near the top of the webpage)."},"3937":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-54","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#nagles-or-the-small-package-problem","content":"If a big 40ms or so occasional delay is seen in operations against HBase, try the Nagle's setting. For example, see the user mailing list thread, Inconsistent scan performance with caching set to 1 and the issue cited therein where setting `notcpdelay` improved scan speeds. You might also see the graphs on the tail of HBASE-7008 Set scanner caching to a better default where our Lars Hofhansl tries various data sizes w/ Nagle's on and off measuring the effect."},"3938":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-55","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#better-mean-time-to-recover-mttr","content":"This section is about configurations that will make servers come back faster after a fail. See the Deveraj Das and Nicolas Liochon blog post Introduction to HBase Mean Time to Recover (MTTR) for a brief introduction."},"3939":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-56","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#better-mean-time-to-recover-mttr","content":"The issue HBASE-8354 forces Namenode into loop with lease recovery requests is messy but has a bunch of good discussion toward the end on low timeouts and how to cause faster recovery including citation of fixes added to HDFS. Read the Varun Sharma comments. The below suggested configurations are Varun's suggestions distilled and tested. Make sure you are running on a late-version HDFS so you have the fixes he refers to and himself adds to HDFS that help HBase MTTR (e.g. HDFS-3703, HDFS-3712, and HDFS-4791 — Hadoop 2 for sure has them and late Hadoop 1 has some). Set the following in the RegionServer."},"3940":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-57","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#better-mean-time-to-recover-mttr","content":"And on the NameNode/DataNode side, set the following to enable 'staleness' introduced in HDFS-3703, HDFS-3912."},"3941":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-58","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"JMX (Java Management Extensions) provides built-in instrumentation that enables you to monitor and manage the Java VM. To enable monitoring and management from remote systems, you need to set system property `com.sun.management.jmxremote.port` (the port number through which you want to enable JMX RMI connections) when you start the Java VM. See the official documentation for more information. Historically, besides above port mentioned, JMX opens two additional random TCP listening ports, which could lead to port conflict problem. (See HBASE-10289 for details)"},"3942":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-59","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"As an alternative, you can use the coprocessor-based JMX implementation provided by HBase. To enable it, add below property in *hbase-site.xml*:"},"3943":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-60","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"DO NOT set `com.sun.management.jmxremote.port` for Java VM at the same time."},"3944":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-61","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"Currently it supports Master and RegionServer Java VM. By default, the JMX listens on TCP port 10102, you can further configure the port using below properties:"},"3945":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-62","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"The registry port can be shared with connector port in most cases, so you only need to configure `regionserver.rmi.registry.port`. However, if you want to use SSL communication, the 2 ports must be configured to different values."},"3946":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-63","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"By default the password authentication and SSL communication is disabled. To enable password authentication, you need to update *hbase-env.sh* like below:"},"3947":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-64","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"See example password/access file under `$JRE_HOME/lib/management`."},"3948":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-65","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"To enable SSL communication with password authentication, follow below steps:"},"3949":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-66","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"And then update *hbase-env.sh* like below:"},"3950":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-67","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"Finally start `jconsole` on the client using the key store:"},"3951":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-68","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"To enable the HBase JMX implementation on Master, you also need to add below property in\n*hbase-site.xml*:"},"3952":{"id":"/docs/configuration/important-69","page_id":"/docs/configuration/important","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration/important#jmx","content":"The corresponding properties for port configuration are `master.rmi.registry.port` (by default 10101) and `master.rmi.connector.port` (by default the same as registry.port)"},"3953":{"id":"/docs/configuration","page_id":"/docs/configuration","type":"page","content":"Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Configuration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration"},"3954":{"id":"/docs/configuration-0","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration","content":"Complete guide to HBase configuration including all settings, tuning parameters, and best practices."},"3955":{"id":"/docs/configuration-1","page_id":"/docs/configuration","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"Configuration Files"},"3956":{"id":"/docs/configuration-2","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration","content":"This chapter expands upon the Getting Started chapter to further explain configuration of Apache HBase. Please read this chapter carefully, especially the Basic Prerequisites to ensure that your HBase testing and deployment goes smoothly. Familiarize yourself with Support and Testing Expectations as well."},"3957":{"id":"/docs/configuration-3","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"Apache HBase uses the same configuration system as Apache Hadoop. All configuration files are located in the &#x2A;conf/* directory, which needs to be kept in sync for each node on your cluster."},"3958":{"id":"/docs/configuration-4","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***backup-masters***\\\nNot present by default. A plain-text file which lists hosts on which the Master should start a backup Master process, one host per line."},"3959":{"id":"/docs/configuration-5","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***hadoop-metrics2-hbase.properties***\\\nUsed to connect HBase Hadoop's Metrics2 framework. See the Hadoop Wiki entry for more information on Metrics2. Contains only commented-out examples by default."},"3960":{"id":"/docs/configuration-6","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-env.cmd* and *hbase-env.sh***\\\nScript for Windows and Linux / Unix environments to set up the working environment for HBase, including the location of Java, Java options, and other environment variables. The file contains many commented-out examples to provide guidance."},"3961":{"id":"/docs/configuration-7","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-policy.xml***\\\nThe default policy configuration file used by RPC servers to make authorization decisions on client requests. Only used if HBase security is enabled."},"3962":{"id":"/docs/configuration-8","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-site.xml***\\\nThe main HBase configuration file. This file specifies configuration options which override HBase's default configuration. You can view (but do not edit) the default configuration file at *hbase-common/src/main/resources/hbase-default.xml*. You can also view the entire effective configuration for your cluster (defaults and overrides) in the **HBase Configuration** tab of the HBase Web UI."},"3963":{"id":"/docs/configuration-9","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***log4j2.properties***\\\nConfiguration file for HBase logging via `log4j2`."},"3964":{"id":"/docs/configuration-10","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"***regionservers***\\\nA plain-text file containing a list of hosts which should run a RegionServer in your HBase cluster. By default, this file contains the single entry `localhost`. It should contain a list of hostnames or IP addresses, one per line, and should only contain `localhost` if each node in your cluster will run a RegionServer on its `localhost` interface."},"3965":{"id":"/docs/configuration-11","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"When you edit XML, it is a good idea to use an XML-aware editor to be sure that your syntax is\ncorrect and your XML is well-formed. You can also use the `xmllint` utility to check that your XML\nis well-formed. By default, `xmllint` re-flows and prints the XML to standard output. To check for\nwell-formedness and only print output if errors exist, use the command `xmllint -noout\n  filename.xml`."},"3966":{"id":"/docs/configuration-12","page_id":"/docs/configuration","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/configuration#configuration-files","content":"When running in distributed mode, after you make an edit to an HBase configuration, make sure you\ncopy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to all nodes of the cluster. HBase will not do this for\nyou. Use a configuration management tool for managing and copying the configuration files to your\nnodes. For most configurations, a restart is needed for servers to pick up changes. Dynamic\nconfiguration is an exception to this, to be described later below."},"3967":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"page","content":"Appendix: Contributing to Documentation","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Appendices"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation"},"3968":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-0","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","content":"Guide for contributing to Apache HBase documentation, including patch submission procedures, website editing, style guidelines, and best practices."},"3969":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-1","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Contributing to Documentation"},"3970":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-2","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#contributing-to-other-strings","content":"Contributing to Other Strings"},"3971":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-3","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"Procedure: Suggested Work flow for Submitting Patches"},"3972":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-4","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"Editing the HBase Website and Documentation"},"3973":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-5","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#publishing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"Publishing the HBase Website and Documentation"},"3974":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-6","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#mdx-and-fumadocs-components","content":"MDX and Fumadocs Components"},"3975":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-7","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#fumadocs-components","content":"Fumadocs Components"},"3976":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-8","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#steps-component","content":"Steps Component"},"3977":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-9","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#callout-component","content":"Callout Component"},"3978":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-10","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#include-directive","content":"Include Directive"},"3979":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-11","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#auto-generated-content","content":"Auto-Generated Content"},"3980":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-12","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#images-in-the-documentation","content":"Images in the Documentation"},"3981":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-13","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Adding a New Section to the Documentation"},"3982":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-14","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#unique-headings-requirement","content":"Unique Headings Requirement"},"3983":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-15","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#hiding-headings-from-table-of-contents","content":"Hiding Headings from Table of Contents"},"3984":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-16","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#combining-custom-ids-and-toc-hiding","content":"Combining Custom IDs and TOC Hiding"},"3985":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-17","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"Common Documentation Issues"},"3986":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-18","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"The Apache HBase project welcomes contributions to all aspects of the project, including the documentation."},"3987":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-19","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"In HBase, documentation includes the following areas, and probably some others:"},"3988":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-20","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"The HBase Reference Guide (this book)"},"3989":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-21","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"The HBase website"},"3990":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-22","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"API documentation"},"3991":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-23","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Command-line utility output and help text"},"3992":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-24","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Web UI strings, explicit help text, context-sensitive strings, and others"},"3993":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-25","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Log messages"},"3994":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-26","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Comments in source files, configuration files, and others"},"3995":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-27","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"Localization of any of the above into target languages other than English"},"3996":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-28","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#appendix-contributing-to-documentation","content":"No matter which area you want to help out with, the first step is almost always to download (typically by cloning the Git repository) and familiarize yourself with the HBase source code. For information on downloading and building the source, see developer."},"3997":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-29","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#contributing-to-other-strings","content":"If you spot an error in a string in a UI, utility, script, log message, or elsewhere, or you think something could be made more clear, or you think text needs to be added where it doesn't currently exist, the first step is to file a JIRA. Be sure to set the component to `Documentation` in addition to any other involved components. Most components have one or more default owners, who monitor new issues which come into those queues. Regardless of whether you feel able to fix the bug, you should still file bugs where you see them."},"3998":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-30","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#contributing-to-other-strings","content":"If you want to try your hand at fixing your newly-filed bug, assign it to yourself. You will need to clone the HBase Git repository to your local system and work on the issue there. When you have developed a potential fix, submit it for review. If it addresses the issue and is seen as an improvement, one of the HBase committers will commit it to one or more branches, as appropriate."},"3999":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-31","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"This procedure goes into more detail than Git pros will need, but is included in this appendix so that people unfamiliar with Git can feel confident contributing to HBase while they learn."},"4000":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-32","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"If you have not already done so, clone the Git repository locally. You only need to do this once."},"4001":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-33","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"Fairly often, pull remote changes into your local repository by using the `git pull` command,\nwhile your tracking branch is checked out."},"4002":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-34","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"For each issue you work on, create a new branch. One convention that works well for naming the branches is to name a given branch the same as the JIRA it relates to:"},"4003":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-35","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"Make your suggested changes on your branch, committing your changes to your local repository\noften. If you need to switch to working on a different issue, remember to check out the\nappropriate branch."},"4004":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-36","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"When you are ready to submit your patch, first be sure that HBase builds cleanly and behaves as\nexpected in your modified branch."},"4005":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-37","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"If you have made documentation or website changes, verify that the site builds correctly by\nrunning the development server from the `hbase-website/` directory."},"4006":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-38","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"If it takes you several days or weeks to implement your fix, or you know that the area of the code you are working in has had a lot of changes lately, make sure you rebase your branch against the remote master and take care of any conflicts before submitting your patch."},"4007":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-39","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"Generate your patch against the remote master. Run the following command from the top level of your git repository (usually called `hbase`):"},"4008":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-40","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"The name of the patch should contain the JIRA ID."},"4009":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-41","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"Look over the patch file to be sure that you did not change any additional files by accident and\nthat there are no other surprises."},"4010":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-42","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"When you are satisfied, attach the patch to the JIRA and click the **Patch Available** button. A\nreviewer will review your patch."},"4011":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-43","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#procedure-suggested-work-flow-for-submitting-patches","content":"If you need to submit a new version of the patch, leave the old one on the JIRA and add a version\nnumber to the name of the new patch."},"4012":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-44","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"The HBase website and documentation are now part of a single application built with Remix and Fumadocs. The source files are located in the `hbase-website/` directory:"},"4013":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-45","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"**Documentation pages**: `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/(multi-page)/` - individual MDX files for each documentation section"},"4014":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-46","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"**Single-page view**: `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/single-page/index.mdx` - combines all documentation into one page"},"4015":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-47","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"**Website components**: `hbase-website/app/components/` - React components used throughout the site"},"4016":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-48","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"**Images**: `hbase-website/public/` - static assets including images"},"4017":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-49","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#editing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"You can edit MDX files in any text editor or IDE. To preview your changes locally, run the development server from the `hbase-website/` directory and navigate to the documentation pages in your browser. When you are satisfied with your changes, follow the procedure in submit doc patch procedure to submit your patch."},"4018":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-50","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#publishing-the-hbase-website-and-documentation","content":"The HBase website and documentation are built and deployed as a single Remix application. The deployment process is managed through the project's CI/CD pipeline, which builds the site from the `hbase-website/` directory and deploys it automatically when changes are merged to the main branch."},"4019":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-51","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#mdx-and-fumadocs-components","content":"The HBase documentation is written in MDX (Markdown with JSX), which allows you to use standard Markdown syntax along with React components. For comprehensive documentation on Markdown formatting and MDX features, refer to:"},"4020":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-52","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#mdx-and-fumadocs-components","content":"Fumadocs Markdown Documentation - Complete guide to MDX syntax and Fumadocs features"},"4021":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-53","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#mdx-and-fumadocs-components","content":"CommonMark specification - Standard Markdown syntax reference"},"4022":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-54","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#mdx-and-fumadocs-components","content":"GFM (GitHub Flavored Markdown) - GitHub-style Markdown extensions"},"4023":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-55","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#fumadocs-components","content":"Fumadocs provides several components that enhance the documentation:"},"4024":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-56","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#steps-component","content":"Use `<Steps>` to create numbered step-by-step instructions:"},"4025":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-57","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#steps-component","content":"**Example output:**"},"4026":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-58","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#callout-component","content":"Use `<Callout>` for notes, warnings, and important information:"},"4027":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-59","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#callout-component","content":"**Example output:**"},"4028":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-60","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#include-directive","content":"The single-page documentation view uses `<include>` tags to combine multiple MDX files:"},"4029":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-61","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#include-directive","content":"See `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/single-page/index.mdx` for examples of how all documentation sections are included in the single-page view."},"4030":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-62","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#auto-generated-content","content":"Some parts of the HBase documentation, such as the default configuration, are generated automatically to stay in sync with the code. The configuration documentation is generated from the `hbase-common/src/main/resources/hbase-default.xml` file."},"4031":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-63","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#auto-generated-content","content":"To add or modify configuration parameters, update the source XML file. To regenerate the documentation from the updated configuration, run:"},"4032":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-64","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#auto-generated-content","content":"This command is also executed automatically when you run `npm ci`."},"4033":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-65","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#images-in-the-documentation","content":"You can include images in the HBase documentation using standard Markdown syntax. Always include descriptive alt text for accessibility:"},"4034":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-66","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#images-in-the-documentation","content":"Save images to the `hbase-website/public/` directory or an appropriate subdirectory. Reference them in your MDX files using absolute paths from the public directory:"},"4035":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-67","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#images-in-the-documentation","content":"When submitting a patch that includes images, attach the images to the JIRA issue."},"4036":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-68","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"To add a new section to the HBase documentation:"},"4037":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-69","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Create a new MDX file in `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/(multi-page)/` with a descriptive name (e.g., `my-new-section.mdx`)"},"4038":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-70","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Add frontmatter at the top of the file with a title and description:"},"4039":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-71","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Add your new file to `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/(multi-page)/meta.json` in the appropriate location within the `pages` array (without the `.mdx` extension):"},"4040":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-72","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Add an `<include>` directive to `hbase-website/app/pages/_docs/docs/_mdx/single-page/index.mdx` in the appropriate location:"},"4041":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-73","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#adding-a-new-section-to-the-documentation","content":"Add your new file to Git before creating your patch."},"4042":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-74","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#unique-headings-requirement","content":"Since all documentation files are merged into a single-page view, **all heading IDs must be unique across the entire documentation**. A test will fail during the build if duplicate heading IDs are detected, marking the problematic headings."},"4043":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-75","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#unique-headings-requirement","content":"Headings don't have to be visually unique, but their link IDs must be unique. You can customize the heading ID using Fumadocs syntax:"},"4044":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-76","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#unique-headings-requirement","content":"This creates a heading that displays as \"Configuration\" but has the unique ID `#server-configuration` for linking purposes."},"4045":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-77","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#hiding-headings-from-table-of-contents","content":"You can hide specific headings from the right-side table of contents:"},"4046":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-78","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#hiding-headings-from-table-of-contents","content":"This heading will still appear in the document but won't show up in the table of contents navigation."},"4047":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-79","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#hiding-headings-from-table-of-contents","content":"Note: `[!toc]` becomes part of the heading ID. For example, `## Usage [!toc]` will have the ID\n`#usage-toc`."},"4048":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-80","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#combining-custom-ids-and-toc-hiding","content":"You can combine both attributes:"},"4049":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-81","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"The following documentation issues come up often:"},"4050":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-82","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"**Isolate Changes for Easy Diff Review**"},"4051":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-83","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"Avoid reformatting entire files when making content changes. If you need to reformat a file, do that in a separate JIRA where you do not change any content."},"4052":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-84","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"**Syntax Highlighting**"},"4053":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-85","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"MDX supports syntax highlighting for code blocks. Specify the language after the opening triple backticks:"},"4054":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-86","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"**Component Syntax**"},"4055":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-87","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"Remember to properly close Fumadocs components. Components like `<Steps>` and `<Callout>` must be properly closed:"},"4056":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-88","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"**Unique Heading IDs**"},"4057":{"id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation-89","page_id":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/contributing-to-documentation#common-documentation-issues","content":"Ensure all heading IDs are unique across the entire documentation. If you get a test failure about duplicate headings, customize the heading ID using `[#custom-id]` syntax as described in the Unique Headings Requirement section."},"4058":{"id":"/docs/cp","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"page","content":"Apache HBase Coprocessors","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","APIs & Integration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp"},"4059":{"id":"/docs/cp-0","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"The coprocessor framework provides mechanisms for running your custom code directly on the RegionServers managing your data."},"4060":{"id":"/docs/cp-1","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-overview","content":"Coprocessor Overview"},"4061":{"id":"/docs/cp-2","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-analogies","content":"Coprocessor Analogies"},"4062":{"id":"/docs/cp-3","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Coprocessor Implementation Overview"},"4063":{"id":"/docs/cp-4","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#types-of-coprocessors","content":"Types of Coprocessors"},"4064":{"id":"/docs/cp-5","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#observer-coprocessors","content":"Observer Coprocessors"},"4065":{"id":"/docs/cp-6","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"Use Cases for Observer Coprocessors"},"4066":{"id":"/docs/cp-7","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"Types of Observer Coprocessor"},"4067":{"id":"/docs/cp-8","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Endpoint Coprocessor"},"4068":{"id":"/docs/cp-9","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#loading-coprocessors","content":"Loading Coprocessors"},"4069":{"id":"/docs/cp-10","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Static Loading"},"4070":{"id":"/docs/cp-11","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#static-unloading","content":"Static Unloading"},"4071":{"id":"/docs/cp-12","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"Dynamic Loading"},"4072":{"id":"/docs/cp-13","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Using HBase Shell"},"4073":{"id":"/docs/cp-14","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api-all-hbase-versions","content":"Using the Java API (all HBase versions)"},"4074":{"id":"/docs/cp-15","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api-hbase-096-only","content":"Using the Java API (HBase 0.96+ only)"},"4075":{"id":"/docs/cp-16","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-unloading","content":"Dynamic Unloading"},"4076":{"id":"/docs/cp-17","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell-1","content":"Using HBase Shell"},"4077":{"id":"/docs/cp-18","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api","content":"Using the Java API"},"4078":{"id":"/docs/cp-19","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"Examples"},"4079":{"id":"/docs/cp-20","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#observer-example","content":"Observer Example"},"4080":{"id":"/docs/cp-21","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-example","content":"Endpoint Example"},"4081":{"id":"/docs/cp-22","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#guidelines-for-deploying-a-coprocessor","content":"Guidelines For Deploying A Coprocessor"},"4082":{"id":"/docs/cp-23","page_id":"/docs/cp","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/cp#restricting-coprocessor-usage","content":"Restricting Coprocessor Usage"},"4083":{"id":"/docs/cp-24","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"HBase Coprocessors are modeled after Google BigTable's coprocessor implementation\n(http\\://research.google.com/people/jeff/SOCC2010-keynote-slides.pdf pages 41-42.).\nEfforts are ongoing to bridge gaps between HBase's\nimplementation and BigTable's architecture. For more information see\nHBASE-4047."},"4084":{"id":"/docs/cp-25","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"The information in this chapter is primarily sourced and heavily reused from the following\nresources:"},"4085":{"id":"/docs/cp-26","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"Mingjie Lai's blog post\nCoprocessor Introduction."},"4086":{"id":"/docs/cp-27","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"Gaurav Bhardwaj's blog post\nThe How To Of HBase Coprocessors."},"4087":{"id":"/docs/cp-28","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"Coprocessors are an advanced feature of HBase and are intended to be used by system\ndevelopers only. Because coprocessor code runs directly on the RegionServer and has\ndirect access to your data, they introduce the risk of data corruption, man-in-the-middle\nattacks, or other malicious data access. Currently, there is no mechanism to prevent\ndata corruption by coprocessors, though work is underway on\nHBASE-4047."},"4088":{"id":"/docs/cp-29","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp","content":"In addition, there is no resource isolation, so a well-intentioned but misbehaving\ncoprocessor can severely degrade cluster performance and stability."},"4089":{"id":"/docs/cp-30","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-overview","content":"In HBase, you fetch data using a `Get` or `Scan`, whereas in an RDBMS you use a SQL\nquery. In order to fetch only the relevant data, you filter it using a HBase\nFilter\n, whereas in an RDBMS you use a `WHERE` predicate."},"4090":{"id":"/docs/cp-31","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-overview","content":"After fetching the data, you perform computations on it. This paradigm works well\nfor \"small data\" with a few thousand rows and several columns. However, when you scale\nto billions of rows and millions of columns, moving large amounts of data across your\nnetwork will create bottlenecks at the network layer, and the client needs to be powerful\nenough and have enough memory to handle the large amounts of data and the computations.\nIn addition, the client code can grow large and complex."},"4091":{"id":"/docs/cp-32","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-overview","content":"In this scenario, coprocessors might make sense. You can put the business computation\ncode into a coprocessor which runs on the RegionServer, in the same location as the\ndata, and returns the result to the client."},"4092":{"id":"/docs/cp-33","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-overview","content":"This is only one scenario where using coprocessors can provide benefit. Following\nare some analogies which may help to explain some of the benefits of coprocessors."},"4093":{"id":"/docs/cp-34","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**Triggers and Stored Procedure**\\\nAn Observer coprocessor is similar to a trigger in a RDBMS in that it executes\nyour code either before or after a specific event (such as a `Get` or `Put`)\noccurs. An endpoint coprocessor is similar to a stored procedure in a RDBMS\nbecause it allows you to perform custom computations on the data on the\nRegionServer itself, rather than on the client."},"4094":{"id":"/docs/cp-35","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**MapReduce**\\\nMapReduce operates on the principle of moving the computation to the location of\nthe data. Coprocessors operate on the same principal."},"4095":{"id":"/docs/cp-36","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**AOP**\\\nIf you are familiar with Aspect Oriented Programming (AOP), you can think of a coprocessor\nas applying advice by intercepting a request and then running some custom code,\nbefore passing the request on to its final destination (or even changing the destination)."},"4096":{"id":"/docs/cp-37","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Your class should implement one of the Coprocessor interfaces -\nCoprocessor,\nRegionObserver,\nCoprocessorService - to name a few."},"4097":{"id":"/docs/cp-38","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Load the coprocessor, either statically (from the configuration) or dynamically,\nusing HBase Shell. For more details see Loading Coprocessors."},"4098":{"id":"/docs/cp-39","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Call the coprocessor from your client-side code. HBase handles the coprocessor\ntransparently."},"4099":{"id":"/docs/cp-40","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"The framework API is provided in the\ncoprocessor\npackage."},"4100":{"id":"/docs/cp-41","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#observer-coprocessors","content":"Observer coprocessors are triggered either before or after a specific event occurs.\nObservers that happen before an event use methods that start with a `pre` prefix,\nsuch as `prePut`. Observers that happen just after an event override methods that start\nwith a `post` prefix, such as `postPut`."},"4101":{"id":"/docs/cp-42","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Security**\\\nBefore performing a `Get` or `Put` operation, you can check for permission using\n`preGet` or `prePut` methods."},"4102":{"id":"/docs/cp-43","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Referential Integrity**\\\nHBase does not directly support the RDBMS concept of refential integrity, also known\nas foreign keys. You can use a coprocessor to enforce such integrity. For instance,\nif you have a business rule that every insert to the `users` table must be followed\nby a corresponding entry in the `user_daily_attendance` table, you could implement\na coprocessor to use the `prePut` method on `user` to insert a record into `user_daily_attendance`."},"4103":{"id":"/docs/cp-44","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Secondary Indexes**\\\nYou can use a coprocessor to maintain secondary indexes. For more information, see\nSecondaryIndexing."},"4104":{"id":"/docs/cp-45","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**RegionObserver**\\\nA RegionObserver coprocessor allows you to observe events on a region, such as `Get`\nand `Put` operations. See\nRegionObserver."},"4105":{"id":"/docs/cp-46","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**RegionServerObserver**\\\nA RegionServerObserver allows you to observe events related to the RegionServer's\noperation, such as starting, stopping, or performing merges, commits, or rollbacks.\nSee\nRegionServerObserver."},"4106":{"id":"/docs/cp-47","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**MasterObserver**\\\nA MasterObserver allows you to observe events related to the HBase Master, such\nas table creation, deletion, or schema modification. See\nMasterObserver."},"4107":{"id":"/docs/cp-48","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**WalObserver**\\\nA WalObserver allows you to observe events related to writes to the Write-Ahead\nLog (WAL). See\nWALObserver."},"4108":{"id":"/docs/cp-49","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"Examples provides working examples of observer coprocessors."},"4109":{"id":"/docs/cp-50","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Endpoint processors allow you to perform computation at the location of the data.\nSee Coprocessor Analogy. An example is the need to calculate a running\naverage or summation for an entire table which spans hundreds of regions."},"4110":{"id":"/docs/cp-51","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In contrast to observer coprocessors, where your code is run transparently, endpoint\ncoprocessors must be explicitly invoked using the\nCoprocessorService()\nmethod available in\nAsyncTable."},"4111":{"id":"/docs/cp-52","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"The coprocessorService method in Table\nhas been deprecated."},"4112":{"id":"/docs/cp-53","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In HBASE-21512\nwe reimplement the sync client based on the async client. The coprocessorService\nmethod defined in `Table` interface directly references a method from protobuf's\n`BlockingInterface`, which means we need to use a separate thread pool to execute\nthe method so we avoid blocking the async client(We want to avoid blocking calls in\nour async implementation)."},"4113":{"id":"/docs/cp-54","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Since coprocessor is an advanced feature, we believe it is OK for coprocessor users to\ninstead switch over to use `AsyncTable`. There is a lightweight\ntoAsyncConnection\nmethod to get an `AsyncConnection` from `Connection` if needed."},"4114":{"id":"/docs/cp-55","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Starting with HBase 0.96, endpoint coprocessors are implemented using Google Protocol\nBuffers (protobuf). For more details on protobuf, see Google's\nProtocol Buffer Guide.\nEndpoints Coprocessor written in version 0.94 are not compatible with version 0.96 or later.\nSee\nHBASE-5448). To upgrade your\nHBase cluster from 0.94 or earlier to 0.96 or later, you need to reimplement your\ncoprocessor."},"4115":{"id":"/docs/cp-56","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In HBase 2.x, we made use of a shaded version of protobuf 3.x, but kept the\nprotobuf for coprocessors on 2.5.0. In HBase 3.0.0, we removed all dependencies on\nnon-shaded protobuf so you need to reimplement your coprocessor to make use of the\nshaded protobuf version provided in hbase-thirdparty. Please see\nthe protobuf section for more details."},"4116":{"id":"/docs/cp-57","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Coprocessor Endpoints should make no use of HBase internals and\nonly avail of public APIs; ideally a CPEP should depend on Interfaces\nand data structures only. This is not always possible but beware\nthat doing so makes the Endpoint brittle, liable to breakage as HBase\ninternals evolve. HBase internal APIs annotated as private or evolving\ndo not have to respect semantic versioning rules or general java rules on\ndeprecation before removal. While generated protobuf files are\nabsent the hbase audience annotations — they are created by the\nprotobuf protoc tool which knows nothing of how HBase works —\nthey should be consided `@InterfaceAudience.Private` so are liable to\nchange."},"4117":{"id":"/docs/cp-58","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Examples provides working examples of endpoint coprocessors."},"4118":{"id":"/docs/cp-59","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#loading-coprocessors","content":"To make your coprocessor available to HBase, it must be *loaded*, either statically\n(through the HBase configuration) or dynamically (using HBase Shell or the Java API)."},"4119":{"id":"/docs/cp-60","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Follow these steps to statically load your coprocessor. Keep in mind that you must\nrestart HBase to unload a coprocessor that has been loaded statically."},"4120":{"id":"/docs/cp-61","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Define the Coprocessor in *hbase-site.xml*, with a `<property>` element with a `<name>`\nand a `<value>` sub-element. The `<name>` should be one of the following:"},"4121":{"id":"/docs/cp-62","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.region.classes` for RegionObservers and Endpoints."},"4122":{"id":"/docs/cp-63","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.wal.classes` for WALObservers."},"4123":{"id":"/docs/cp-64","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.master.classes` for MasterObservers."},"4124":{"id":"/docs/cp-65","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"`<value>` must contain the fully-qualified class name of your coprocessor's implementation\nclass."},"4125":{"id":"/docs/cp-66","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"For example to load a Coprocessor (implemented in class SumEndPoint.java) you have to create\nfollowing entry in RegionServer's 'hbase-site.xml' file (generally located under 'conf' directory):"},"4126":{"id":"/docs/cp-67","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"If multiple classes are specified for loading, the class names must be comma-separated.\nThe framework attempts to load all the configured classes using the default class loader.\nTherefore, the jar file must reside on the server-side HBase classpath."},"4127":{"id":"/docs/cp-68","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Coprocessors which are loaded in this way will be active on all regions of all tables.\nThese are also called system Coprocessor.\nThe first listed Coprocessors will be assigned the priority `Coprocessor.Priority.SYSTEM`.\nEach subsequent coprocessor in the list will have its priority value incremented by one (which\nreduces its priority, because priorities have the natural sort order of Integers)."},"4128":{"id":"/docs/cp-69","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"These priority values can be manually overriden in hbase-site.xml. This can be useful if you\nwant to guarantee that a coprocessor will execute after another. For example, in the following\nconfiguration `SumEndPoint` would be guaranteed to go last, except in the case of a tie with\nanother coprocessor:"},"4129":{"id":"/docs/cp-70","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"When calling out to registered observers, the framework executes their callbacks methods in the\nsorted order of their priority.\nTies are broken arbitrarily."},"4130":{"id":"/docs/cp-71","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Put your code on HBase's classpath. One easy way to do this is to drop the jar\n(containing you code and all the dependencies) into the `lib/` directory in the\nHBase installation."},"4131":{"id":"/docs/cp-72","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-loading","content":"Restart HBase."},"4132":{"id":"/docs/cp-73","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-unloading","content":"Delete the coprocessor's `<property>` element, including sub-elements, from `hbase-site.xml`."},"4133":{"id":"/docs/cp-74","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-unloading","content":"Restart HBase."},"4134":{"id":"/docs/cp-75","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#static-unloading","content":"Optionally, remove the coprocessor's JAR file from the classpath or HBase's `lib/`\ndirectory."},"4135":{"id":"/docs/cp-76","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"You can also load a coprocessor dynamically, without restarting HBase. This may seem\npreferable to static loading, but dynamically loaded coprocessors are loaded on a\nper-table basis, and are only available to the table for which they were loaded. For\nthis reason, dynamically loaded tables are sometimes called **Table Coprocessor**."},"4136":{"id":"/docs/cp-77","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"In addition, dynamically loading a coprocessor acts as a schema change on the table,\nand the table must be taken offline to load the coprocessor."},"4137":{"id":"/docs/cp-78","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"There are three ways to dynamically load Coprocessor."},"4138":{"id":"/docs/cp-79","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"The below mentioned instructions makes the following assumptions:"},"4139":{"id":"/docs/cp-80","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"A JAR called `coprocessor.jar` contains the Coprocessor implementation along with all of its\ndependencies."},"4140":{"id":"/docs/cp-81","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#dynamic-loading","content":"The JAR is available in HDFS in some location like\n`hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/coprocessor.jar`."},"4141":{"id":"/docs/cp-82","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Load the Coprocessor, using a command like the following:"},"4142":{"id":"/docs/cp-83","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"The Coprocessor framework will try to read the class information from the coprocessor table\nattribute value.\nThe value contains four pieces of information which are separated by the pipe (`|`) character."},"4143":{"id":"/docs/cp-84","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"File path: The jar file containing the Coprocessor implementation must be in a location where\nall region servers can read it.\nYou could copy the file onto the local disk on each region server, but it is recommended to store\nit in HDFS.\nHBASE-14548 allows a directory containing the jars\nor some wildcards to be specified, such as: `hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/` or\n`hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/*.jar`. Please note that if a directory is specified,\nall jar files(.jar) in the directory are added. It does not search for files in sub-directories.\nDo not use a wildcard if you would like to specify a directory. This enhancement applies to the\nusage via the JAVA API as well."},"4144":{"id":"/docs/cp-85","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Class name: The full class name of the Coprocessor."},"4145":{"id":"/docs/cp-86","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Priority: An integer. The framework will determine the execution sequence of all configured\nobservers registered at the same hook using priorities. This field can be left blank. In that\ncase the framework will assign a default priority value."},"4146":{"id":"/docs/cp-87","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Arguments (Optional): This field is passed to the Coprocessor implementation. This is optional."},"4147":{"id":"/docs/cp-88","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"Verify that the coprocessor loaded:"},"4148":{"id":"/docs/cp-89","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell","content":"The coprocessor should be listed in the `TABLE_ATTRIBUTES`."},"4149":{"id":"/docs/cp-90","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api-all-hbase-versions","content":"The following Java code shows how to use the `setValue()` method of `HTableDescriptor`\nto load a coprocessor on the `users` table."},"4150":{"id":"/docs/cp-91","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api-hbase-096-only","content":"In HBase 0.96 and newer, the `addCoprocessor()` method of `HTableDescriptor` provides\nan easier way to load a coprocessor dynamically."},"4151":{"id":"/docs/cp-92","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api-hbase-096-only","content":"There is no guarantee that the framework will load a given Coprocessor successfully. For example,\nthe shell command neither guarantees a jar file exists at a particular location nor verifies\nwhether the given class is actually contained in the jar file."},"4152":{"id":"/docs/cp-93","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell-1","content":"Alter the table to remove the coprocessor with `table_att_unset`."},"4153":{"id":"/docs/cp-94","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-hbase-shell-1","content":"Alter the table to remove the coprocessor with `table_remove_coprocessor` introduced in\nHBASE-26524 by specifying an explicit\nclassname"},"4154":{"id":"/docs/cp-95","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api","content":"Reload the table definition without setting the value of the coprocessor either by\nusing `setValue()` or `addCoprocessor()` methods. This will remove any coprocessor\nattached to the table."},"4155":{"id":"/docs/cp-96","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#using-the-java-api","content":"In HBase 0.96 and newer, you can instead use the `removeCoprocessor()` method of the\n`HTableDescriptor` class."},"4156":{"id":"/docs/cp-97","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"HBase ships examples for Observer Coprocessor."},"4157":{"id":"/docs/cp-98","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"A more detailed example is given below."},"4158":{"id":"/docs/cp-99","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"These examples assume a table called `users`, which has two column families `personalDet`\nand `salaryDet`, containing personal and salary details. Below is the graphical representation\nof the `users` table."},"4159":{"id":"/docs/cp-100","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**Users Table**"},"4160":{"id":"/docs/cp-101","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**personalDet**"},"4161":{"id":"/docs/cp-102","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**salaryDet**"},"4162":{"id":"/docs/cp-103","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**rowkey**"},"4163":{"id":"/docs/cp-104","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**name**"},"4164":{"id":"/docs/cp-105","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**lastname**"},"4165":{"id":"/docs/cp-106","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**dob**"},"4166":{"id":"/docs/cp-107","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**gross**"},"4167":{"id":"/docs/cp-108","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**net**"},"4168":{"id":"/docs/cp-109","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"**allowances**"},"4169":{"id":"/docs/cp-110","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"admin"},"4170":{"id":"/docs/cp-111","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples","content":"Admin"},"4171":{"id":"/docs/cp-112","page_id":"/docs/cp","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cp#cp-examples"