ge"],"url":"/docs/datamodel#major-compactions-change-query-results","content":"Major compactions change query results"},"4273":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-46","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/datamodel#sort-order","content":"Sort Order"},"4274":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-47","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/datamodel#column-metadata","content":"Column Metadata"},"4275":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-48","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/datamodel#datamodel-joins","content":"Joins"},"4276":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-49","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/datamodel#acid","content":"ACID"},"4277":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-50","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-toc","content":"An HBase table consists of multiple rows."},"4278":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-51","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#row-toc","content":"A row in HBase consists of a row key and one or more columns with values associated with them.\nRows are sorted alphabetically by the row key as they are stored.\nFor this reason, the design of the row key is very important.\nThe goal is to store data in such a way that related rows are near each other.\nA common row key pattern is a website domain.\nIf your row keys are domains, you should probably store them in reverse (org.apache.www, org.apache.mail, org.apache.jira). This way, all of the Apache domains are near each other in the table, rather than being spread out based on the first letter of the subdomain."},"4279":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-52","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-toc","content":"A column in HBase consists of a column family and a column qualifier, which are delimited by a `:` (colon) character."},"4280":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-53","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-family-toc","content":"Column families physically colocate a set of columns and their values, often for performance reasons.\nEach column family has a set of storage properties, such as whether its values should be cached in memory, how its data is compressed or its row keys are encoded, and others.\nEach row in a table has the same column families, though a given row might not store anything in a given column family."},"4281":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-54","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-qualifier-toc","content":"A column qualifier is added to a column family to provide the index for a given piece of data.\nGiven a column family `content`, a column qualifier might be `content:html`, and another might be `content:pdf`.\nThough column families are fixed at table creation, column qualifiers are mutable and may differ greatly between rows."},"4282":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-55","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#cell-toc","content":"A cell is a combination of row, column family, and column qualifier, and contains a value and a timestamp, which represents the value's version."},"4283":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-56","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#timestamp-toc","content":"A timestamp is written alongside each value, and is the identifier for a given version of a value.\nBy default, the timestamp represents the time on the RegionServer when the data was written, but you can specify a different timestamp value when you put data into the cell."},"4284":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-57","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#conceptual-view","content":"You can read a very understandable explanation of the HBase data model in the blog post Understanding HBase and BigTable by Jim R. Wilson.\nAnother good explanation is available in the PDF Introduction to Basic Schema Design by Amandeep Khurana."},"4285":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-58","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#conceptual-view","content":"It may help to read different perspectives to get a solid understanding of HBase schema design.\nThe linked articles cover the same ground as the information in this section."},"4286":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-59","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#conceptual-view","content":"The following example is a slightly modified form of the one on page 2 of the BigTable paper.\nThere is a table called `webtable` that contains two rows (`com.cnn.www` and `com.example.www`) and three column families named `contents`, `anchor`, and `people`.\nIn this example, for the first row (`com.cnn.www`), `anchor` contains two columns (`anchor:cssnsi.com`, `anchor:my.look.ca`) and `contents` contains one column (`contents:html`). This example contains 5 versions of the row with the row key `com.cnn.www`, and one version of the row with the row key `com.example.www`.\nThe `contents:html` column qualifier contains the entire HTML of a given website.\nQualifiers of the `anchor` column family each contain the external site which links to the site represented by the row, along with the text it used in the anchor of its link.\nThe `people` column family represents people associated with the site."},"4287":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-60","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#conceptual-view","content":"By convention, a column name is made of its column family prefix and a *qualifier*. For example,\nthe column *contents:html* is made up of the column family `contents` and the `html` qualifier.\nThe colon character (`:`) delimits the column family from the column family *qualifier*."},"4288":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-61","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"Row Key"},"4289":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-62","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"4290":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-63","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `contents`"},"4291":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-64","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `anchor`"},"4292":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-65","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `people`"},"4293":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-66","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4294":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-67","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"t9"},"4295":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-68","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"anchor:cnnsi.com = \"CNN\""},"4296":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-69","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4297":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-70","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"t8"},"4298":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-71","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"anchor:my.look.ca = \"CNN.com\""},"4299":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-72","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4300":{"id":