webtable-toc","content":"people:author = \"John Doe\""},"4312":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-85","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table-webtable-toc","content":"Cells in this table that appear to be empty do not take space, or in fact exist, in HBase.\nThis is what makes HBase \"sparse.\" A tabular view is not the only possible way to look at data in HBase, or even the most accurate.\nThe following represents the same information as a multi-dimensional map.\nThis is only a mock-up for illustrative purposes and may not be strictly accurate."},"4313":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-86","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#physical-view","content":"Although at a conceptual level tables may be viewed as a sparse set of rows, they are physically stored by column family.\nA new column qualifier (column\\_family:column\\_qualifier) can be added to an existing column family at any time."},"4314":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-87","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Row Key"},"4315":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-88","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"4316":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-89","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Column Family `anchor`"},"4317":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-90","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4318":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-91","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"t9"},"4319":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-92","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"`anchor:cnnsi.com = \"CNN\"`"},"4320":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-93","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4321":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-94","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"t8"},"4322":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-95","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"`anchor:my.look.ca = \"CNN.com\"`"},"4323":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-96","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"Row Key"},"4324":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-97","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"4325":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-98","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `contents:`"},"4326":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-99","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4327":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-100","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t6"},"4328":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-101","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"4329":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-102","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4330":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-103","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t5"},"4331":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-104","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"4332":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-105","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"4333":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-106","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t3"},"4334":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-107","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"4335":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-108","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"The empty cells shown in the conceptual view are not stored at all.\nThus a request for the value of the `contents:html` column at time stamp `t8` would return no value.\nSimilarly, a request for an `anchor:my.look.ca` value at time stamp `t9` would return no value.\nHowever, if no timestamp is supplied, the most recent value for a particular column would be returned.\nGiven multiple versions, the most recent is also the first one found, since timestamps are stored in descending order.\nThus a request for the values of all columns in the row `com.cnn.www` if no timestamp is specified would be: the value of `contents:html` from timestamp `t6`, the value of `anchor:cnnsi.com` from timestamp `t9`, the value of `anchor:my.look.ca` from timestamp `t8`."},"4336":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-109","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"For more information about the internals of how Apache HBase stores data, see regions.arch."},"4337":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-110","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#namespace","content":"A namespace is a logical grouping of tables analogous to a database in relation database systems.\nThis abstraction lays the groundwork for upcoming multi-tenancy related features:"},"4338":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-111","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#namespace","content":"Quota Management (HBASE-8410) - Restrict the amount of resources (i.e. regions, tables) a namespace can consume."},"4339":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-112","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#namespace","content":"Namespace Security Administration (HBASE-9206) - Provide another level of security administration for tenants."},"4340":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-113","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#namespace","content":"Region server groups (HBASE-6721) - A namespace/table can be pinned onto a subset of RegionServers thus guaranteeing a coarse level of isolation."},"4341":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-114","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#namespace-management","content":"A namespace can be created, removed or altered.\nNamespace membership is determined during table creation by specifying a fully-qualified table name of the form:"},"4342":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-115","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#predefined-namespaces","content":"There are two predefined special namespaces:"},"4343":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-116","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#predefined-namespaces","content":"hbase - system namespace, used to contain HBase internal tables"},"4344":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-117","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#predefined-namespaces","content":"default - tables with no explicit specified namespace will automatically fall into this namespace"},"4345":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-118","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"We used to have a system table called `hbase:namespace` for storing the namespace information."},"4346":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-119","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"It introduced some painful bugs in the past, especially that it may hang the master startup thus\nhang the whole cluster. This is because meta table also has a namespace, so it depends on namespace\ntable. But namespace table also depends on meta table as meta table stores the location of all\nregions. This is a cyclic dependency so sometimes namespace and meta table will wait for each other\nto online and hang the master start up."},"4347":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-120","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"It is not easy to fix so in 3.0.0, we decided to completely remove the `hbase:namespace` table and\nfold its content into the `ns` family in `hbase:meta` table. When upgrading from 2.x to 3.x, the\nmigration will be done automatically and the `hbase:namespace` table will be disabled after the\nmigration is done. You are free to leave it there for sometime and finally drop it."},"4348":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-121","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"For more tails, please see https\\://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/HBASE-21154."},"4349":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-122","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#table","content":"Tables are declared up front at schema definition time."},"4350":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-123","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#row","content":"Row keys are uninterpreted bytes.\nRows are lexicographically sorted with the lowest order appearing first in a table.\nThe empty byte array is used to denote both the start and end of a tables' namespace."},"4351":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-124","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-family","content":"Columns in Apache HBase are grouped into *column families*.\nAll column members of a column family have the same prefix.\nFor example, the columns *courses:history* and *courses:math* are both members of the *courses* column family.\nThe colon character (`:`) delimits the column family from the column family qualifier.\nThe column family prefix must be composed of *printable* characters.\nThe qualifying tail, the column family *qualifier*, can be made of any arbitrary bytes.\nColumn families must be declared up front at schema definition time whereas columns do not need to be defined at schema time but can be conjured on the fly while the table is up and running."},"4352":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-125","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-family","content":"Physically, all column family members are stored together on the filesystem.\nBecause tunings and storage specifications are done at the column family level, it is advised that all column family members have the same general access pattern and size characteristics."},"4353":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-126","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#cells","content":"A &#x2A;\\{row, column, version}* tuple exactly specifies a `cell` in HBase.\nCell content is uninterpreted bytes"},"4354":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-127","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#data-model-operations","content":"The four primary data model operations are Get, Put, Scan, and Delete.\nOperations are applied via Table instances."},"4355":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-128","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#get","content":"Get returns attributes for a specified row.\nGets are executed via Table.get"},"4356":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-129","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#put","content":"Put either adds new rows to a table (if the key is new) or can update existing rows (if the key already exists). Puts are executed via Table.put (non-writeBuffer) or Table.batch (non-writeBuffer)"},"4357":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-130","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#data-model-operations-scans","content":"Scan allow iteration over multiple rows for specified attributes."},"4358":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-131","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#data-model-operations-scans","content":"The following is an example of a Scan on a Table instance.\nAssume that a table is populated with rows with keys \"row1\", \"row2\", \"row3\", and then another set of rows with the keys \"abc1\", \"abc2\", and \"abc3\". The following example shows how to set a Scan instance to return the rows beginning with \"row\"."},"4359":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-132","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#data-model-operations-scans","content":"Note that generally the easiest way to specify a specific stop point for a scan is by using the InclusiveStopFilter class."},"4360":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-133","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete","content":"Delete removes a row from a table.\nDeletes are executed via Table.delete."},"4361":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-134","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete","content":"HBase does not modify data in place, and so deletes are handled by creating new markers called *tombstones*.\nThese tombstones, along with the dead values, are cleaned up on major compactions."},"4362":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-135","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete","content":"See version.delete for more information on deleting versions of columns, and see compaction for more information on compactions."},"4363":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-136","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"A &#x2A;\\{row, column, version}* tuple exactly specifies a `cell` in HBase.\nIt's possible to have an unbounded number of cells where the row and column are the same but the cell address differs only in its version dimension."},"4364":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-137","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"While rows and column keys are expressed as bytes, the version is specified using a long integer.\nTypically this long contains time instances such as those returned by `java.util.Date.getTime()` or `System.currentTimeMillis()`, that is: *the difference, measured in milliseconds, between the current time and midnight, January 1, 1970 UTC*."},"4365":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-138","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"The HBase version dimension is stored in decreasing order, so that when reading from a store file, the most recent values are found first."},"4366":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-139","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"There is a lot of confusion over the semantics of `cell` versions, in HBase.\nIn particular:"},"4367":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-140","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"If multiple writes to a cell have the same version, only the last written is fetchable."},"4368":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-141","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"It is OK to write cells in a non-increasing version order."},"4369":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-142","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"Below we describe how the version dimension in HBase currently works.\nSee HBASE-2406 for discussion of HBase versions. Bending time in HBase makes for a good read on the version, or time, dimension in HBase.\nIt has more detail on versioning than is provided here."},"4370":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-143","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions","content":"As of this writing, the limitation *Overwriting values at existing timestamps* mentioned in the article no longer holds in HBase.\nThis section is basically a synopsis of this article by Bruno Dumon."},"4371":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-144","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#specifying-the-number-of-versions-to-store","content":"The maximum number of versions to store for a given column is part of the column schema and is specified at table creation, or via an `alter` command, via `HColumnDescriptor.DEFAULT_VERSIONS`.\nPrior to HBase 0.96, the default number of versions kept was `3`, but in 0.96 and newer has been changed to `1`."},"4372":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-145","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#example-modify-the-maximum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"This example uses HBase Shell to keep a maximum of 5 versions of all columns in column family `f1`.\nYou could also use ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."},"4373":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-146","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#example-modify-the-minimum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"You can also specify the minimum number of versions to store per column family.\nBy default, this is set to 0, which means the feature is disabled.\nThe following example sets the minimum number of versions on all columns in column family `f1` to `2`, via HBase Shell.\nYou could also use ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."},"4374":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-147","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#example-modify-the-minimum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"Starting with HBase 0.98.2, you can specify a global default for the maximum number of versions kept for all newly-created columns, by setting `hbase.column.max.version` in *hbase-site.xml*.\nSee hbase.column.max.version."},"4375":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-148","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versions-and-hbase-operations","content":"In this section we look at the behavior of the version dimension for each of the core HBase operations."},"4376":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-149","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#getscan","content":"Gets are implemented on top of Scans.\nThe below discussion of Get applies equally to Scans."},"4377":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-150","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#getscan","content":"By default, i.e. if you specify no explicit version, when doing a `get`, the cell whose version has the largest value is returned (which may or may not be the latest one written, see later). The default behavior can be modified in the following ways:"},"4378":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-151","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#getscan","content":"to return more than one version, see Get.readVersions(int)"},"4379":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-152","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#getscan","content":"to return versions other than the latest, see Get.setTimeRange(long,long)"},"4380":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-153","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#getscan","content":"To retrieve the latest version that is less than or equal to a given value, thus giving the 'latest' state of the record at a certain point in time, just use a range from 0 to the desired version and set the max versions to 1."},"4381":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-154","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#default-get-example","content":"The following Get will only retrieve the current version of the row"},"4382":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-155","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#versioned-get-example","content":"The following Get will return the last 3 versions of the row."},"4383":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-156","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#put-1","content":"Doing a put always creates a new version of a `cell`, at a certain timestamp.\nBy default the system uses the server's `currentTimeMillis`, but you can specify the version (= the long integer) yourself, on a per-column level.\nThis means you could assign a time in the past or the future, or use the long value for non-time purposes."},"4384":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-157","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#put-1","content":"To overwrite an existing value, do a put at exactly the same row, column, and version as that of the cell you want to overwrite."},"4385":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-158","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#implicit-version-example-toc","content":"The following Put will be implicitly versioned by HBase with the current time."},"4386":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-159","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#explicit-version-example","content":"The following Put has the version timestamp explicitly set."},"4387":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-160","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#explicit-version-example","content":"Caution: the version timestamp is used internally by HBase for things like time-to-live calculations.\nIt's usually best to avoid setting this timestamp yourself.\nPrefer using a separate timestamp attribute of the row, or have the timestamp as a part of the row key, or both."},"4388":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-161","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#cell-version-example-toc","content":"The following Put uses a method getCellBuilder() to get a CellBuilder instance\nthat already has relevant Type and Row set."},"4389":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-162","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"There are three different types of internal delete markers.\nSee Lars Hofhansl's blog for discussion of his attempt adding another, Scanning in HBase: Prefix Delete Marker."},"4390":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-163","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Delete: for a specific version of a column."},"4391":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-164","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Delete column: for all versions of a column."},"4392":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-165","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Delete family: for all columns of a particular ColumnFamily"},"4393":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-166","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"When deleting an entire row, HBase will internally create a tombstone for each ColumnFamily (i.e., not each individual column)."},"4394":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-167","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Deletes work by creating *tombstone* markers.\nFor example, let's suppose we want to delete a row.\nFor this you can specify a version, or else by default the `currentTimeMillis` is used.\nWhat this means is *delete all cells where the version is less than or equal to this version*.\nHBase never modifies data in place, so for example a delete will not immediately delete (or mark as deleted) the entries in the storage file that correspond to the delete condition.\nRather, a so-called *tombstone* is written, which will mask the deleted values.\nWhen HBase does a major compaction, the tombstones are processed to actually remove the dead values, together with the tombstones themselves.\nIf the version you specified when deleting a row is larger than the version of any value in the row, then you can consider the complete row to be deleted."},"4395":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-168","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"For an informative discussion on how deletes and versioning interact, see the thread Put w/timestamp -> Deleteall -> Put w/ timestamp fails up on the user mailing list."},"4396":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-169","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Also see keyvalue for more information on the internal KeyValue format."},"4397":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-170","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"Delete markers are purged during the next major compaction of the store, unless the `KEEP_DELETED_CELLS` option is set in the column family (See Keeping Deleted Cells).\nTo keep the deletes for a configurable amount of time, you can set the delete TTL via the `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes` property in *hbase-site.xml*.\nIf `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes` is not set, or set to 0, all delete markers, including those with timestamps in the future, are purged during the next major compaction.\nOtherwise, a delete marker with a timestamp in the future is kept until the major compaction which occurs after the time represented by the marker's timestamp plus the value of `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes`, in milliseconds."},"4398":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-171","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#delete-toc","content":"This behavior represents a fix for an unexpected change that was introduced in HBase 0.94, and was\nfixed in HBASE-10118. The change has been\nbackported to HBase 0.94 and newer branches."},"4399":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-172","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"In `hbase-2.0.0`, the operator can specify an alternate version and\ndelete treatment by setting the column descriptor property\n`NEW_VERSION_BEHAVIOR` to true (To set a property on a column family\ndescriptor, you must first disable the table and then alter the\ncolumn family descriptor; see Keeping Deleted Cells for an example\nof editing an attribute on a column family descriptor)."},"4400":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-173","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"The 'new version behavior', undoes the limitations listed below\nwhereby a `Delete` ALWAYS overshadows a `Put` if at the same\nlocation — i.e. same row, column family, qualifier and timestamp\n\\-- regardless of which arrived first. Version accounting is also\nchanged as deleted versions are considered toward total version count.\nThis is done to ensure results are not changed should a major\ncompaction intercede. See `HBASE-15968` and linked issues for\ndiscussion."},"4401":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-174","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"Running with this new configuration currently costs; we factor\nthe Cell MVCC on every compare so we burn more CPU. The slow\ndown will depend. In testing we've seen between 0% and 25%\ndegradation."},"4402":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-175","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"If replicating, it is advised that you run with the new\nserial replication feature (See `HBASE-9465`; the serial\nreplication feature did NOT make it into `hbase-2.0.0` but\nshould arrive in a subsequent hbase-2.x release) as now\nthe order in which Mutations arrive is a factor."},"4403":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-176","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#current-limitations","content":"The below limitations are addressed in hbase-2.0.0. See\nthe section above, Optional New Version and Delete behavior in HBase-2.0.0."},"4404":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-177","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#deletes-mask-puts","content":"Deletes mask puts, even puts that happened after the delete was entered.\nSee HBASE-2256.\nRemember that a delete writes a tombstone, which only disappears after then next major compaction has run.\nSuppose you do a delete of everything `<= T`.\nAfter this you do a new put with a timestamp `<= T`.\nThis put, even if it happened after the delete, will be masked by the delete tombstone.\nPerforming the put will not fail, but when you do a get you will notice the put did have no effect.\nIt will start working again after the major compaction has run.\nThese issues should not be a problem if you use always-increasing versions for new puts to a row.\nBut they can occur even if you do not care about time: just do delete and put immediately after each other, and there is some chance they happen within the same millisecond."},"4405":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-178","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#major-compactions-change-query-results","content":"*...create three cell versions at t1, t2 and t3, with a maximum-versions\nsetting of 2. So when getting all versions, only the values at t2 and t3 will be\nreturned. But if you delete the version at t2 or t3, the one at t1 will appear again.\nObviously, once a major compaction has run, such behavior will not be the case\nanymore...* (See *Garbage Collection* in Bending time in HBase.)"},"4406":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-179","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#sort-order","content":"All data model operations HBase return data in sorted order.\nFirst by row, then by ColumnFamily, followed by column qualifier, and finally timestamp (sorted in reverse, so newest records are returned first)."},"4407":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-180","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-metadata","content":"There is no store of column metadata outside of the internal KeyValue instances for a ColumnFamily.\nThus, while HBase can support not only a wide number of columns per row, but a heterogeneous set of columns between rows as well, it is your responsibility to keep track of the column names."},"4408":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-181","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#column-metadata","content":"The only way to get a complete set of columns that exist for a ColumnFamily is to process all the rows.\nFor more information about how HBase stores data internally, see keyvalue."},"4409":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-182","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#datamodel-joins","content":"Whether HBase supports joins is a common question on the dist-list, and there is a simple answer: it doesn't, at not least in the way that RDBMS' support them (e.g., with equi-joins or outer-joins in SQL). As has been illustrated in this chapter, the read data model operations in HBase are Get and Scan."},"4410":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-183","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#datamodel-joins","content":"However, that doesn't mean that equivalent join functionality can't be supported in your application, but you have to do it yourself.\nThe two primary strategies are either denormalizing the data upon writing to HBase, or to have lookup tables and do the join between HBase tables in your application or MapReduce code (and as RDBMS' demonstrate, there are several strategies for this depending on the size of the tables, e.g., nested loops vs.\nhash-joins). So which is the best approach? It depends on what you are trying to do, and as such there isn't a single answer that works for every use case."},"4411":{"id":"/docs/datamodel-184","page_id":"/docs/datamodel","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/datamodel#acid","content":"See ACID Semantics.\nLars Hofhansl has also written a note on ACID in HBase."},"4412":{"id":"/docs/external-apis","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"page","content":"Apache HBase External APIs","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","APIs & Integration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis"},"4413":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-0","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis","content":"This chapter will cover access to Apache HBase either through non-Java languages and through custom protocols."},"4414":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-1","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#rest","content":"REST"},"4415":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-2","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#starting-and-stopping-the-rest-server","content":"Starting and Stopping the REST Server"},"4416":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-3","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#configuring-the-rest-server-and-client","content":"Configuring the REST Server and Client"},"4417":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-4","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#using-rest-endpoints","content":"Using REST Endpoints"},"4418":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-5","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Cluster-Wide Endpoints"},"4419":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-6","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#namespace-endpoints","content":"Namespace Endpoints"},"4420":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-7","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#table-endpoints","content":"Table Endpoints"},"4421":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-8","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-get-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Get` Operations"},"4422":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-9","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-delete-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Delete` Operations"},"4423":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-10","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#stateful-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"Stateful endpoints for `Scan` Operations"},"4424":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-11","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"Stateless endpoints for `Scan` Operations"},"4425":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-12","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Put` Operations"},"4426":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-13","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Check-And-Put` Operations"},"4427":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-14","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Check-And-Delete` Operations"},"4428":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-15","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Append` Operations"},"4429":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-16","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"Endpoints for `Increment` Operations"},"4430":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-17","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#rest-xml-schema","content":"REST XML Schema"},"4431":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-18","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#rest-protobufs-schema","content":"REST Protobufs Schema"},"4432":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-19","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#thrift","content":"Thrift"},"4433":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-20","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#cc-apache-hbase-client","content":"C/C++ Apache HBase Client"},"4434":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-21","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"Using Java Data Objects (JDO) with HBase"},"4435":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-22","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#scala","content":"Scala"},"4436":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-23","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#setting-the-classpath","content":"Setting the Classpath"},"4437":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-24","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#scala-sbt-file","content":"Scala SBT File"},"4438":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-25","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#example-scala-code","content":"Example Scala Code"},"4439":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-26","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#jython","content":"Jython"},"4440":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-27","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#setting-the-classpath-1","content":"Setting the Classpath"},"4441":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-28","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/external-apis#jython-code-examples","content":"Jython Code Examples"},"4442":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-29","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis","content":"For information on using the native HBase APIs, refer to User API Reference and the HBase APIs chapter."},"4443":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-30","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#rest","content":"Representational State Transfer (REST) was introduced in 2000 in the doctoral dissertation of Roy Fielding, one of the principal authors of the HTTP specification."},"4444":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-31","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#rest","content":"REST itself is out of the scope of this documentation, but in general, REST allows client-server interactions via an API that is tied to the URL itself. This section discusses how to configure and run the REST server included with HBase, which exposes HBase tables, rows, cells, and metadata as URL specified resources. There is also a nice series of blogs on How-to: Use the Apache HBase REST Interface by Jesse Anderson."},"4445":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-32","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#starting-and-stopping-the-rest-server","content":"The included REST server can run as a daemon which starts an embedded Jetty servlet container and deploys the servlet into it. Use one of the following commands to start the REST server in the foreground or background. The port is optional, and defaults to 8080."},"4446":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-33","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#starting-and-stopping-the-rest-server","content":"To stop the REST server, use Ctrl-C if you were running it in the foreground, or the following command if you were running it in the background."},"4447":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-34","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#configuring-the-rest-server-and-client","content":"For information about configuring the REST server and client for SSL, as well as `doAs` impersonation for the REST server, see Configure the Thrift Gateway to Authenticate on Behalf of the Client and other portions of the Securing Apache HBase chapter."},"4448":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-35","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#using-rest-endpoints","content":"The following examples use the placeholder server http\\://example.com:8000, and the following commands can all be run using `curl` or `wget` commands. You can request plain text (the default), XML , or JSON output by adding no header for plain text, or the header \"Accept: text/xml\" for XML, \"Accept: application/json\" for JSON, or \"Accept: application/x-protobuf\" to for protocol buffers."},"4449":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-36","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#using-rest-endpoints","content":"Unless specified, use `GET` requests for queries, `PUT` or `POST` requests for creation or\nmutation, and `DELETE` for deletion."},"4450":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-37","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Endpoint"},"4451":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-38","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"HTTP Verb"},"4452":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-39","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Description"},"4453":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-40","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"`/version/cluster`"},"4454":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-41","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"`GET`"},"4455":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-42","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Version of HBase running on this cluster"},"4456":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-43","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"`/version/rest`"},"4457":{"id":"/docs/external-apis-44","page_id":"/docs/external-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/external-apis#cluster-wide-endpoints","c