ing","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-zookeeper","content":"Configure ZooKeeper"},"4813":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-35","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-prepare-node-b-and-node-c","content":"Procedure: Prepare `node-b` and `node-c`"},"4814":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-36","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#download-and-unpack-hbase","content":"Download and unpack HBase"},"4815":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-37","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#copy-the-configuration-files-from-node-a-to-node-band-node-c","content":"Copy the configuration files from `node-a` to `node-b`.and `node-c`"},"4816":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-38","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-start-and-test-your-cluster","content":"Procedure: Start and Test Your Cluster"},"4817":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-39","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#be-sure-hbase-is-not-running-on-any-node","content":"Be sure HBase is not running on any node"},"4818":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-40","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#start-the-cluster","content":"Start the cluster"},"4819":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-41","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"Verify that the processes are running"},"4820":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-42","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#browse-to-the-web-ui","content":"Browse to the Web UI"},"4821":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-43","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#test-what-happens-when-nodes-or-services-disappear","content":"Test what happens when nodes or services disappear"},"4822":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-44","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/getting-started#where-to-go-next","content":"Where to go next"},"4823":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-45","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#quick-start---standalone-hbase","content":"This section describes the setup of a single-node standalone HBase. A *standalone* instance has all HBase daemons — the Master, RegionServers, and ZooKeeper — running in a single JVM persisting to the local filesystem. It is our most basic deploy profile. We will show you how to create a table in HBase using the `hbase shell` CLI, insert rows into the table, perform put and scan operations against the table, enable or disable the table, and start and stop HBase."},"4824":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-46","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#quick-start---standalone-hbase","content":"Apart from downloading HBase, this procedure should take less than 10 minutes."},"4825":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-47","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#getting-started-jdk-version-requirements","content":"HBase requires that a JDK be installed. See Java for information about supported JDK versions."},"4826":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-48","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"Choose a download site from this list of Apache Download Mirrors. Click on the suggested top link. This will take you to a mirror of *HBase Releases*. Click on the folder named *stable&#x2A; and then download the binary file that ends in *.tar.gz* to your local filesystem. Do not download the file ending in *src.tar.gz* for now."},"4827":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-49","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"Extract the downloaded file, and change to the newly-created directory."},"4828":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-50","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"You must set the `JAVA_HOME` environment variable before starting HBase. To make this easier, HBase lets you set it within the *conf/hbase-env.sh* file. You must locate where Java is installed on your machine, and one way to find this is by using the *whereis java* command. Once you have the location, edit the *conf/hbase-env.sh&#x2A; file and uncomment the line starting with &#x2A;#export JAVA\\_HOME=*, and then set it to your Java installation path."},"4829":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-51","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"*Example extract from *hbase-env.sh* where *JAVA\\_HOME* is set*"},"4830":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-52","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"The *bin/start-hbase.sh* script is provided as a convenient way to start HBase. Issue the command, and if all goes well, a message is logged to standard output showing that HBase started successfully. You can use the `jps` command to verify that you have one running process called `HMaster`. In standalone mode HBase runs all daemons within this single JVM, i.e. the HMaster, a single HRegionServer, and the ZooKeeper daemon. Go to *http\\://localhost:16010* to view the HBase Web UI."},"4831":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-53","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#connect-to-hbase","content":"Connect to your running instance of HBase using the `hbase shell` command, located in the &#x2A;bin/* directory of your HBase install. In this example, some usage and version information that is printed when you start HBase Shell has been omitted. The HBase Shell prompt ends with a `>` character."},"4832":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-54","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#display-hbase-shell-help-text","content":"Type `help` and press Enter, to display some basic usage information for HBase Shell, as well as several example commands. Notice that table names, rows, columns all must be enclosed in quote characters."},"4833":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-55","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#create-a-table","content":"Use the `create` command to create a new table. You must specify the table name and the ColumnFamily name."},"4834":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-56","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#list-information-about-your-table","content":"Use the `list` command to confirm your table exists"},"4835":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-57","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#list-information-about-your-table","content":"Now use the `describe` command to see details, including configuration defaults"},"4836":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-58","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#put-data-into-your-table","content":"To put data into your table, use the `put` command."},"4837":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-59","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#put-data-into-your-table","content":"Here, we insert three values, one at a time. The first insert is at `row1`, column `cf:a`, with a value of `value1`. Columns in HBase are comprised of a column family prefix, `cf` in this example, followed by a colon and then a column qualifier suffix, `a` in this case."},"4838":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-60","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#scan-the-table-for-all-data-at-once","content":"One of the ways to get data from HBase is to scan. Use the `scan` command to scan the table for data. You can limit your scan, but for now, all data is fetched."},"4839":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-61","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#get-a-single-row-of-data","content":"To get a single row of data at a time, use the `get` command."},"4840":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-62","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#disable-a-table","content":"If you want to delete a table or change its settings, as well as in some other situations, you need to disable the table first, using the `disable` command. You can re-enable it using the `enable` command."},"4841":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-63","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#disable-a-table","content":"Disable the table again if you tested the `enable` command above:"},"4842":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-64","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#drop-the-table","content":"To drop (delete) a table, use the `drop` command."},"4843":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-65","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#exit-the-hbase-shell","content":"To exit the HBase Shell and disconnect from your cluster, use the `quit` command. HBase is still running in the background."},"4844":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-66","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"In the same way that the *bin/start-hbase.sh* script is provided to conveniently start all HBase daemons, the *bin/stop-hbase.sh* script stops them."},"4845":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-67","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"After issuing the command, it can take several minutes for the processes to shut down. Use the `jps` to be sure that the HMaster and HRegionServer processes are shut down."},"4846":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-68","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"The above has shown you how to start and stop a standalone instance of HBase. In the next sections we give a quick overview of other modes of hbase deploy."},"4847":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-69","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing","content":"After working your way through quickstart standalone mode, you can re-configure HBase to run in pseudo-distributed mode. Pseudo-distributed mode means that HBase still runs completely on a single host, but each HBase daemon (HMaster, HRegionServer, and ZooKeeper) runs as a separate process: in standalone mode all daemons ran in one jvm process/instance. By default, unless you configure the `hbase.rootdir` property as described in quickstar&#x74;, your data is still stored in &#x2A;/tmp/*. In this walk-through, we store your data in HDFS instead, assuming you have HDFS available. You can skip the HDFS configuration to continue storing your data in the local filesystem."},"4848":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-70","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing","content":"This procedure assumes that you have configured Hadoop and HDFS on your local system and/or a\nremote system, and that they are running and available. It also assumes you are using Hadoop 2.\nThe guide on Setting up a Single Node\nCluster\nin the Hadoop documentation is a good starting point."},"4849":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-71","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#stop-hbase-if-it-is-running","content":"If you have just finished quickstart and HBase is still running, stop it. This procedure will create a totally new directory where HBase will store its data, so any databases you created before will be lost."},"4850":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-72","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-hbase","content":"Edit the *hbase-site.xml* configuration. First, add the following property which directs HBase to run in distributed mode, with one JVM instance per daemon."},"4851":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-73","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-hbase","content":"Next, add a configuration for `hbase.rootdir`, pointing to the address of your HDFS instance, using the `hdfs:////` URI syntax. In this example, HDFS is running on the localhost at port 8020."},"4852":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-74","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-hbase","content":"You do not need to create the directory in HDFS. HBase will do this for you. If you create the directory, HBase will attempt to do a migration, which is not what you want."},"4853":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-75","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-hbase","content":"Finally, remove existing configuration for `hbase.tmp.dir` and `hbase.unsafe.stream.capability.enforce`."},"4854":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-76","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-hbase","content":"Use the *bin/start-hbase.sh* command to start HBase. If your system is configured correctly, the `jps` command should show the HMaster and HRegionServer processes running."},"4855":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-77","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#check-the-hbase-directory-in-hdfs","content":"If everything worked correctly, HBase created its directory in HDFS. In the configuration above, it is stored in &#x2A;/hbase/* on HDFS. You can use the `hadoop fs` command in Hadoop's &#x2A;bin/* directory to list this directory."},"4856":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-78","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#create-a-table-and-populate-it-with-data","content":"You can use the HBase Shell to create a table, populate it with data, scan and get values from it, using the same procedure as in shell exercises."},"4857":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-79","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"Running multiple HMaster instances on the same hardware does not make sense in a production\nenvironment, in the same way that running a pseudo-distributed cluster does not make sense for\nproduction. This step is offered for testing and learning purposes only."},"4858":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-80","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"The HMaster server controls the HBase cluster. You can start up to 9 backup HMaster servers, which makes 10 total HMasters, counting the primary. To start a backup HMaster, use the `local-master-backup.sh`. For each backup master you want to start, add a parameter representing the port offset for that master. Each HMaster uses two ports (16000 and 16010 by default). The port offset is added to these ports, so using an offset of 2, the backup HMaster would use ports 16002 and 16012. The following command starts 3 backup servers using ports 16002/16012, 16003/16013, and 16005/16015."},"4859":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-81","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"To kill a backup master without killing the entire cluster, you need to find its process ID (PID). The PID is stored in a file with a name like */tmp/hbase-USER-X-master.pid*. The only contents of the file is the PID. You can use the `kill -9` command to kill that PID. The following command will kill the master with port offset 1, but leave the cluster running:"},"4860":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-82","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop-additional-regionservers","content":"The HRegionServer manages the data in its StoreFiles as directed by the HMaster. Generally, one HRegionServer runs per node in the cluster. Running multiple HRegionServers on the same system can be useful for testing in pseudo-distributed mode. The `local-regionservers.sh` command allows you to run multiple RegionServers. It works in a similar way to the `local-master-backup.sh` command, in that each parameter you provide represents the port offset for an instance. Each RegionServer requires two ports, and the default ports are 16020 and 16030. Since HBase version 1.1.0, HMaster doesn't use region server ports, this leaves 10 ports (16020 to 16029 and 16030 to 16039) to be used for RegionServers. For supporting additional RegionServers, set environment variables HBASE\\_RS\\_BASE\\_PORT and HBASE\\_RS\\_INFO\\_BASE\\_PORT to appropriate values before running script `local-regionservers.sh`. e.g. With values 16200 and 16300 for base ports, 99 additional RegionServers can be supported, on a server. The following command starts four additional RegionServers, running on sequential ports starting at 16022/16032 (base ports 16020/16030 plus 2)."},"4861":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-83","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop-additional-regionservers","content":"To stop a RegionServer manually, use the `local-regionservers.sh` command with the `stop` parameter and the offset of the server to stop."},"4862":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-84","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#getting-started-pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing-stop-hbase","content":"You can stop HBase the same way as in the quickstart procedure, using the *bin/stop-hbase.sh* command."},"4863":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-85","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"In reality, you need a fully-distributed configuration to fully test HBase and to use it in real-world scenarios. In a distributed configuration, the cluster contains multiple nodes, each of which runs one or more HBase daemon. These include primary and backup Master instances, multiple ZooKeeper nodes, and multiple RegionServer nodes."},"4864":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-86","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"This advanced quickstart adds two more nodes to your cluster. The architecture will be as follows:"},"4865":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-87","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Node Name"},"4866":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-88","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Master"},"4867":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-89","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"ZooKeeper"},"4868":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-90","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"RegionServer"},"4869":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-91","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-a.example.com"},"4870":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-92","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4871":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-93","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4872":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-94","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"no"},"4873":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-95","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-b.example.com"},"4874":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-96","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"backup"},"4875":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-97","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4876":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-98","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4877":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-99","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-c.example.com"},"4878":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-100","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"no"},"4879":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-101","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4880":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-102","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"4881":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-103","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Distributed Cluster Demo Architecture"},"4882":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-104","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"This quickstart assumes that each node is a virtual machine and that they are all on the same network. It builds upon the previous quickstart, Pseudo-Distributed for Local Testing, assuming that the system you configured in that procedure is now `node-a`. Stop HBase on `node-a` before continuing."},"4883":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-105","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Be sure that all the nodes have full access to communicate, and that no firewall rules are in\nplace which could prevent them from talking to each other. If you see any errors like `no route to\n  host`, check your firewall."},"4884":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-106","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-configure-passwordless-ssh-access","content":"`node-a` needs to be able to log into `node-b` and `node-c` (and to itself) in order to start the daemons. The easiest way to accomplish this is to use the same username on all hosts, and configure password-less SSH login from `node-a` to each of the others."},"4885":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-107","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#on-node-a-generate-a-key-pair","content":"While logged in as the user who will run HBase, generate a SSH key pair, using the following command:"},"4886":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-108","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#on-node-a-generate-a-key-pair","content":"If the command succeeds, the location of the key pair is printed to standard output. The default name of the public key is *id\\_rsa.pub*."},"4887":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-109","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#create-the-directory-that-will-hold-the-shared-keys-on-the-other-nodes","content":"On `node-b` and `node-c&#x60;, log in as the HBase user and create a &#x2A;.ssh/* directory in the user's home directory, if it does not already exist. If it already exists, be aware that it may already contain other keys."},"4888":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-110","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#copy-the-public-key-to-the-other-nodes","content":"Securely copy the public key from `node-a` to each of the nodes, by using the `scp&#x60; or some other secure means. On each of the other nodes, create a new file called *.ssh/authorized\\_keys* *if it does not already exist*, and append the contents of the *id\\_rsa.pub* file to the end of it. Note that you also need to do this for `node-a` itself."},"4889":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-111","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#test-password-less-login","content":"If you performed the procedure correctly, you should not be prompted for a password when you SSH from `node-a` to either of the other nodes using the same username."},"4890":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-112","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#test-password-less-login","content":"Since `node-b` will run a backup Master, repeat the procedure above, substituting `node-b` everywhere you see `node-a&#x60;. Be sure not to overwrite your existing *.ssh/authorized\\_keys* files, but concatenate the new key onto the existing file using the `>>` operator rather than the `>` operator."},"4891":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-113","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"`node-a` will run your primary master and ZooKeeper processes, but no RegionServers. Stop the RegionServer from starting on `node-a`."},"4892":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-114","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"Edit *conf/regionservers* and remove the line which contains `localhost`. Add lines with the hostnames or IP addresses for `node-b` and `node-c`."},"4893":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-115","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"Even if you did want to run a RegionServer on `node-a`, you should refer to it by the hostname the other servers would use to communicate with it. In this case, that would be `node-a.example.com`. This enables you to distribute the configuration to each node of your cluster any hostname conflicts. Save the file."},"4894":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-116","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-hbase-to-use-node-b-as-a-backup-master","content":"Create a new file in &#x2A;conf/* called *backup-masters*, and add a new line to it with the hostname for `node-b`. In this demonstration, the hostname is `node-b.example.com`."},"4895":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-117","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-zookeeper","content":"In reality, you should carefully consider your ZooKeeper configuration. You can find out more about configuring ZooKeeper in zookeeper section. This configuration will direct HBase to start and manage a ZooKeeper instance on each node of the cluster."},"4896":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-118","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-zookeeper","content":"On `node-a`, edit *conf/hbase-site.xml* and add the following properties."},"4897":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-119","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#configure-zookeeper","content":"Everywhere in your configuration that you have referred to `node-a` as `localhost`, change the reference to point to the hostname that the other nodes will use to refer to `node-a`. In these examples, the hostname is `node-a.example.com`."},"4898":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-120","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#procedure-prepare-node-b-and-node-c","content":"`node-b` will run a backup master server and a ZooKeeper instance."},"4899":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-121","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#download-and-unpack-hbase","content":"Download and unpack HBase to `node-b`, just as you did for the standalone and pseudo-distributed quickstarts."},"4900":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-122","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#copy-the-configuration-files-from-node-a-to-node-band-node-c","content":"Each node of your cluster needs to have the same configuration information. Copy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to the &#x2A;conf/* directory on `node-b` and `node-c`."},"4901":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-123","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#be-sure-hbase-is-not-running-on-any-node","content":"If you forgot to stop HBase from previous testing, you will have errors. Check to see whether HBase is running on any of your nodes by using the `jps` command. Look for the processes `HMaster`, `HRegionServer`, and `HQuorumPeer`. If they exist, kill them."},"4902":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-124","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-the-cluster","content":"On `node-a`, issue the `start-hbase.sh` command. Your output will be similar to that below."},"4903":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-125","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#start-the-cluster","content":"ZooKeeper starts first, followed by the master, then the RegionServers, and finally the backup masters."},"4904":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-126","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"On each node of the cluster, run the `jps` command and verify that the correct processes are running on each server. You may see additional Java processes running on your servers as well, if they are used for other purposes."},"4905":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-127","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-a` `jps` Output"},"4906":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-128","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-b` `jps` Output"},"4907":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-129","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-c` `jps` Output"},"4908":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-130","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"The `HQuorumPeer` process is a ZooKeeper instance which is controlled and started by HBase. If you\nuse ZooKeeper this way, it is limited to one instance per cluster node and is appropriate for\ntesting only. If ZooKeeper is run outside of HBase, the process is called `QuorumPeer`. For more\nabout ZooKeeper configuration, including using an external ZooKeeper instance with HBase, see\nzookeeper section."},"4909":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-131","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#browse-to-the-web-ui","content":"If everything is set up correctly, you should be able to connect to the UI for the Master `http://node-a.example.com:16010/` or the secondary master at `http://node-b.example.com:16010/` using a web browser. If you can connect via `localhost` but not from another host, check your firewall rules. You can see the web UI for each of the RegionServers at port 16030 of their IP addresses, or by clicking their links in the web UI for the Master."},"4910":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-132","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#test-what-happens-when-nodes-or-services-disappear","content":"With a three-node cluster you have configured, things will not be very resilient. You can still test the behavior of the primary Master or a RegionServer by killing the associated processes and watching the logs."},"4911":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-133","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#where-to-go-next","content":"The next chapter, configuration, gives more information about the different HBase run modes, system requirements for running HBase, and critical configuration areas for setting up a distributed HBase cluster."},"4912":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","type":"page","content":"Apache HBase APIs","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","APIs & Integration"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hbase-apis"},"4913":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis-0","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hbase-apis","content":"This chapter provides information about performing operations using HBase native APIs."},"4914":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis-1","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hbase-apis#hbase-apis-examples","content":"Examples"},"4915":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis-2","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hbase-apis#create-modify-and-delete-a-table-using-java","content":"Create, modify and delete a Table Using Java"},"4916":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis-3","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hbase-apis","content":"This information is not exhaustive, and provides a quick reference in addition to the User API Reference.\nThe examples here are not comprehensive or complete, and should be used for purposes of illustration only."},"4917":{"id":"/docs/hbase-apis-4","page_id":"/docs/hbase-apis","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hbase-apis","content":"Apache HBase also works with multiple external APIs.\nSee Apach