file-format#fixed-file-trailer-format-differences-between-versions-1-and-2","content":"Fixed file trailer format differences between versions 1 and 2"},"5782":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-16","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Differences between HFile Versions 1 and 2"},"5783":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-17","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"getShortMidpointKey (an optimization for data index block)"},"5784":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-18","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#hbase-file-format-with-security-enhancements-version-3","content":"HBase File Format with Security Enhancements (version 3)"},"5785":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-19","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation-1","content":"Motivation"},"5786":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-20","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#hfile-format-with-inline-blocks-v2-overview","content":"Overview"},"5787":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-21","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"File Info Block in Version 3"},"5788":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-22","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Data Blocks in Version 3"},"5789":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-23","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/hfile-format#fixed-file-trailer-in-version-3","content":"Fixed File Trailer in Version 3"},"5790":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-24","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#hbase-file-format-version-1","content":"As we will be discussing changes to the HFile format, it is useful to give a short overview of the original (HFile version 1) format."},"5791":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-25","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#overview-of-version-1","content":"An HFile in version 1 format is structured as follows:"},"5792":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-26","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"The block index in version 1 is very straightforward.\nFor each entry, it contains:"},"5793":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-27","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Offset (long)"},"5794":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-28","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Uncompressed size (int)"},"5795":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-29","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key (a serialized byte array written using Bytes.writeByteArray)"},"5796":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-30","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key length as a variable-length integer (VInt)"},"5797":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-31","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key bytes"},"5798":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-32","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"The number of entries in the block index is stored in the fixed file trailer, and has to be passed in to the method that reads the block index.\nOne of the limitations of the block index in version 1 is that it does not provide the compressed size of a block, which turns out to be necessary for decompression.\nTherefore, the HFile reader has to infer this compressed size from the offset difference between blocks.\nWe fix this limitation in version 2, where we store on-disk block size instead of uncompressed size, and get uncompressed size from the block header."},"5799":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-33","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#hbase-file-format-with-inline-blocks-version-2","content":"Note: this feature was introduced in HBase 0.92"},"5800":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-34","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation","content":"We found it necessary to revise the HFile format after encountering high memory usage and slow startup times caused by large Bloom filters and block indexes in the region server.\nBloom filters can get as large as 100 MB per HFile, which adds up to 2 GB when aggregated over 20 regions.\nBlock indexes can grow as large as 6 GB in aggregate size over the same set of regions.\nA region is not considered opened until all of its block index data is loaded.\nLarge Bloom filters produce a different performance problem: the first get request that requires a Bloom filter lookup will incur the latency of loading the entire Bloom filter bit array."},"5801":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-35","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation","content":"To speed up region server startup we break Bloom filters and block indexes into multiple blocks and write those blocks out as they fill up, which also reduces the HFile writer's memory footprint.\nIn the Bloom filter case, \"filling up a block\" means accumulating enough keys to efficiently utilize a fixed-size bit array, and in the block index case we accumulate an \"index block\" of the desired size.\nBloom filter blocks and index blocks (we call these \"inline blocks\") become interspersed with data blocks, and as a side effect we can no longer rely on the difference between block offsets to determine data block length, as it was done in version 1."},"5802":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-36","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation","content":"HFile is a low-level file format by design, and it should not deal with application-specific details such as Bloom filters, which are handled at StoreFile level.\nTherefore, we call Bloom filter blocks in an HFile \"inline\" blocks.\nWe also supply HFile with an interface to write those inline blocks."},"5803":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-37","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation","content":"Another format modification aimed at reducing the region server startup time is to use a contiguous \"load-on-open\" section that has to be loaded in memory at the time an HFile is being opened.\nCurrently, as an HFile opens, there are separate seek operations to read the trailer, data/meta indexes, and file info.\nTo read the Bloom filter, there are two more seek operations for its \"data\" and \"meta\" portions.\nIn version 2, we seek once to read the trailer and seek again to read everything else we need to open the file from a contiguous block."},"5804":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-38","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#overview-of-version-2","content":"The version of HBase introducing the above features reads both version 1 and 2 HFiles, but only writes version 2 HFiles.\nA version 2 HFile is structured as follows:"},"5805":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-39","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"In the version 2 every block in the data section contains the following fields:"},"5806":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-40","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"8 bytes: Block type, a sequence of bytes equivalent to version 1's \"magic records\". Supported block types are:"},"5807":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-41","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"DATA – data blocks"},"5808":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-42","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"LEAF\\_INDEX – leaf-level index blocks in a multi-level-block-index"},"5809":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-43","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"BLOOM\\_CHUNK – Bloom filter chunks"},"5810":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-44","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"META – meta blocks (not used for Bloom filters in version 2 anymore)"},"5811":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-45","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"INTERMEDIATE\\_INDEX – intermediate-level index blocks in a multi-level blockindex"},"5812":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-46","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"ROOT\\_INDEX – root-level index blocks in a multi-level block index"},"5813":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-47","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"FILE\\_INFO – the \"file info\" block, a small key-value map of metadata"},"5814":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-48","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"BLOOM\\_META – a Bloom filter metadata block in the load-on-open section"},"5815":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-49","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"TRAILER – a fixed-size file trailer. As opposed to the above, this is not an HFile v2 block but a fixed-size (for each HFile version) data structure"},"5816":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-50","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"INDEX\\_V1 – this block type is only used for legacy HFile v1 block"},"5817":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-51","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Compressed size of the block's data, not including the header (int).\\\nCan be used for skipping the current data block when scanning HFile data."},"5818":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-52","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Uncompressed size of the block's data, not including the header (int)\\\nThis is equal to the compressed size if the compression algorithm is NONE"},"5819":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-53","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"File offset of the previous block of the same type (long)\\\nCan be used for seeking to the previous data/index block"},"5820":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-54","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Compressed data (or uncompressed data if the compression algorithm is NONE)."},"5821":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-55","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"The above format of blocks is used in the following HFile sections:"},"5822":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-56","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#scanned-block-section","content":"The section is named so because it contains all data blocks that need to be read when an HFile is scanned sequentially.\nAlso contains Leaf index blocks and Bloom chunk blocks."},"5823":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-57","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-scanned-block-section","content":"This section still contains unified-format v2 blocks but it does not have to be read when doing a sequential scan.\nThis section contains \"meta\" blocks and intermediate-level index blocks."},"5824":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-58","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-scanned-block-section","content":"We are supporting \"meta\" blocks in version 2 the same way they were supported in version 1, even though we do not store Bloom filter data in these blocks anymore."},"5825":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-59","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"There are three types of block indexes in HFile version 2, stored in two different formats (root and non-root):"},"5826":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-60","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Data index — version 2 multi-level block index, consisting of:"},"5827":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-61","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Version 2 root index, stored in the data block index section of the file"},"5828":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-62","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Optionally, version 2 intermediate levels, stored in the non-root format in the data index section of the file. Intermediate levels can only be present if leaf level blocks are present"},"5829":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-63","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Optionally, version 2 leaf levels, stored in the non-root format inline with data blocks"},"5830":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-64","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Meta index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the meta index section of the file"},"5831":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-65","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Bloom index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the \"load-on-open\" section as part of Bloom filter metadata."},"5832":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-66","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"This format applies to:"},"5833":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-67","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Root level of the version 2 data index"},"5834":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-68","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Entire meta and Bloom indexes in version 2, which are always single-level."},"5835":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-69","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"A version 2 root index block is a sequence of entries of the following format, similar to entries of a version 1 block index, but storing on-disk size instead of uncompressed size."},"5836":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-70","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Offset (long)\\\nThis offset may point to a data block or to a deeper-level index block."},"5837":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-71","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"On-disk size (int)"},"5838":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-72","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key (a serialized byte array stored using Bytes.writeByteArray)"},"5839":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-73","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key (VInt)"},"5840":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-74","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key bytes"},"5841":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-75","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"A single-level version 2 block index consists of just a single root index block.\nTo read a root index block of version 2, one needs to know the number of entries.\nFor the data index and the meta index the number of entries is stored in the trailer, and for the Bloom index it is stored in the compound Bloom filter metadata."},"5842":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-76","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"For a multi-level block index we also store the following fields in the root index block in the load-on-open section of the HFile, in addition to the data structure described above:"},"5843":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-77","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Middle leaf index block offset"},"5844":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-78","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Middle leaf block on-disk size (meaning the leaf index block containing the reference to the \"middle\" data block of the file)"},"5845":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-79","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"The index of the mid-key (defined below) in the middle leaf-level block."},"5846":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-80","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"These additional fields are used to efficiently retrieve the mid-key of the HFile used in HFile splits, which we define as the first key of the block with a zero-based index of (n – 1) / 2, if the total number of blocks in the HFile is n.\nThis definition is consistent with how the mid-key was determined in HFile version 1, and is reasonable in general, because blocks are likely to be the same size on average, but we don't have any estimates on individual key/value pair sizes."},"5847":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-81","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"When writing a version 2 HFile, the total number of data blocks pointed to by every leaf-level index block is kept track of.\nWhen we finish writing and the total number of leaf-level blocks is determined, it is clear which leaf-level block contains the mid-key, and the fields listed above are computed.\nWhen reading the HFile and the mid-key is requested, we retrieve the middle leaf index block (potentially from the block cache) and get the mid-key value from the appropriate position inside that leaf block."},"5848":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-82","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"This format applies to intermediate-level and leaf index blocks of a version 2 multi-level data block index.\nEvery non-root index block is structured as follows."},"5849":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-83","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"numEntries: the number of entries (int)."},"5850":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-84","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"entryOffsets: the \"secondary index\" of offsets of entries in the block, to facilitate\na quick binary search on the key (`numEntries + 1` int values). The last value\nis the total length of all entries in this index block. For example, in a non-root\nindex block with entry sizes 60, 80, 50 the \"secondary index\" will contain the\nfollowing int array: `{0, 60, 140, 190}`."},"5851":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-85","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Entries. Each entry contains:"},"5852":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-86","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Offset of the block referenced by this entry in the file (long)"},"5853":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-87","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"On-disk size of the referenced block (int)"},"5854":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-88","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key. The length can be calculated from entryOffsets."},"5855":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-89","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"In contrast with version 1, in a version 2 HFile Bloom filter metadata is stored in the load-on-open section of the HFile for quick startup."},"5856":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-90","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"A compound Bloom filter."},"5857":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-91","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Bloom filter version = 3 (int). There used to be a DynamicByteBloomFilter class that had the Bloom filter version number 2"},"5858":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-92","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The total byte size of all compound Bloom filter chunks (long)"},"5859":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-93","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Number of hash functions (int)"},"5860":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-94","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Type of hash functions (int)"},"5861":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-95","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The total key count inserted into the Bloom filter (long)"},"5862":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-96","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The maximum total number of keys in the Bloom filter (long)"},"5863":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-97","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The number of chunks (int)"},"5864":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-98","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Comparator class used for Bloom filter keys, a UTF-8 encoded string stored using Bytes.writeByteArray"},"5865":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-99","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Bloom block index in the version 2 root block index format"},"5866":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-100","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The file info block is a serialized map from byte arrays to byte arrays, with the following keys, among others.\nStoreFile-level logic adds more keys to this."},"5867":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-101","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Key"},"5868":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-102","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Description"},"5869":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-103","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.LASTKEY"},"5870":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-104","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The last key of the file (byte array)"},"5871":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-105","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.AVG\\_KEY\\_LEN"},"5872":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-106","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The average key length in the file (int)"},"5873":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-107","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.AVG\\_VALUE\\_LEN"},"5874":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-108","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The average value length in the file (int)"},"5875":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-109","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"In version 2, we did not change the file format, but we moved the file info to\nthe final section of the file, which can be loaded as one block when the HFile\nis being opened."},"5876":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-110","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Also, we do not store the comparator in the version 2 file info anymore.\nInstead, we store it in the fixed file trailer.\nThis is because we need to know the comparator at the time of parsing the load-on-open section of the HFile."},"5877":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-111","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#fixed-file-trailer-format-differences-between-versions-1-and-2","content":"The following table shows common and different fields between fixed file trailers in versions 1 and 2.\nNote that the size of the trailer is different depending on the version, so it is \"fixed\" only within one version.\nHowever, the version is always stored as the last four-byte integer in the file."},"5878":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-112","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version 1"},"5879":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-113","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version 2"},"5880":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-114","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"File info offset (long)"},"5881":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-115","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Data index offset (long)"},"5882":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-116","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"loadOnOpenOffset (long) &#x2A;The offset of the section that we need to load when opening the file.*"},"5883":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-117","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Number of data index entries (int)"},"5884":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-118","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"metaIndexOffset (long) &#x2A;This field is not being used by the version 1 reader, so we removed it from version 2.*"},"5885":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-119","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"uncompressedDataIndexSize (long) &#x2A;The total uncompressed size of the whole data block index, including root-level, intermediate-level, and leaf-level blocks.*"},"5886":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-120","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Number of meta index entries (int)"},"5887":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-121","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Total uncompressed bytes (long)"},"5888":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-122","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"numEntries (int)"},"5889":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-123","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"numEntries (long)"},"5890":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-124","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Compression codec: 0 = LZO, 1 = GZ, 2 = NONE (int)"},"5891":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-125","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Compression codec: 0 = LZO, 1 = GZ, 2 = NONE (int)"},"5892":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-126","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"The number of levels in the data block index (int)"},"5893":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-127","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"firstDataBlockOffset (long) &#x2A;The offset of the first data block. Used when scanning.*"},"5894":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-128","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"lastDataBlockEnd (long) &#x2A;The offset of the first byte after the last key/value data block. We don't need to go beyond this offset when scanning.*"},"5895":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-129","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version: 1 (int)"},"5896":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-130","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version: 2 (int)"},"5897":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-131","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"Note: this optimization was introduced in HBase 0.95+"},"5898":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-132","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"HFiles contain many blocks that contain a range of sorted Cells.\nEach cell has a key.\nTo save IO when reading Cells, the HFile also has an index that maps a Cell's start key to the offset of the beginning of a particular block.\nPrior to this optimization, HBase would use the key of the first cell in each data block as the index key."},"5899":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-133","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"In HBASE-7845, we generate a new key that is lexicographically larger than the last key of the previous block and lexicographically equal or smaller than the start key of the current block.\nWhile actual keys can potentially be very long, this \"fake key\" or \"virtual key\" can be much shorter.\nFor example, if the stop key of previous block is \"the quick brown fox\", the start key of current block is \"the who\", we could use \"the r\" as our virtual key in our hfile index."},"5900":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-134","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"There are two benefits to this:"},"5901":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-135","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"having shorter keys reduces the hfile index size, (allowing us to keep more indexes in memory), and"},"5902":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-136","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"using something closer to the end key of the previous block allows us to avoid a potential extra IO when the target key lives in between the \"virtual key\" and the key of the first element in the target block."},"5903":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-137","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"This optimization (implemented by the getShortMidpointKey method) is inspired by LevelDB's ByteWiseComparatorImpl::FindShortestSeparator() and FindShortSuccessor()."},"5904":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-138","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#hbase-file-format-with-security-enhancements-version-3","content":"Note: this feature was introduced in HBase 0.98"},"5905":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-139","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#motivation-1","content":"Version 3 of HFile makes changes needed to ease management of encryption at rest and cell-level metadata (which in turn is needed for cell-level ACLs and cell-level visibility labels). For more information see hbase.encryption.server, hbase.tags, hbase.accesscontrol.configuration, and hbase.visibility.labels."},"5906":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-140","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#hfile-format-with-inline-blocks-v2-overview","content":"The version of HBase introducing the above features reads HFiles in versions 1, 2, and 3 but only writes version 3 HFiles.\nVersion 3 HFiles are structured the same as version 2 HFiles.\nFor more information see hfilev2.overview."},"5907":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-141","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Version 3 added two additional pieces of information to the reserved keys in the file info block."},"5908":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-142","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Key"},"5909":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-143","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Description"},"5910":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-144","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"hfile.MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"5911":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-145","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"The maximum number of bytes needed to store the serialized tags for any single cell in this hfile (int)"},"5912":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-146","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"hfile.TAGS\\_COMPRESSED"},"5913":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-147","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Does the block encoder for this hfile compress tags? (boolean). Should only be present if hfile.MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN is also present."},"5914":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-148","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When reading a Version 3 HFile the presence of `MAX_TAGS_LEN` is used to determine how to deserialize the cells within a data block.\nTherefore, consumers must read the file's info block prior to reading any data blocks."},"5915":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-149","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When writing a Version 3 HFile, HBase will always include `MAX_TAGS_LEN` when flushing the memstore to underlying filesystem."},"5916":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-150","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When compacting extant files, the default writer will omit `MAX_TAGS_LEN` if all of the files selected do not themselves contain any cells with tags."},"5917":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-151","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"See compaction for details on the compaction file selection algorithm."},"5918":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-152","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Within an HFile, HBase cells are stored in data blocks as a sequence of KeyValues (see hfilev1.overview, or Lars George's\nexcellent introduction to HBase Storage). In version 3, these KeyValue optionally will include a set of 0 or more tags:"},"5919":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-153","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Version 1 & 2, Version 3 without MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"5920":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-154","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Version 3 with MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"5921":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-155","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Key Length (4 bytes)"},"5922":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-156","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5923":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-157","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5924":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-158","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Value Length (4 bytes)"},"5925":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-159","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5926":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-160","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5927":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-161","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Key bytes (variable)"},"5928":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-162","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5929":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-163","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5930":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-164","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Value bytes (variable)"},"5931":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-165","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5932":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-166","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5933":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-167","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Tags Length (2 bytes)"},"5934":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-168","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5935":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-169","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Tags bytes (variable)"},"5936":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-170","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"5937":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-171","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"If the info block for a given HFile contains an entry for `MAX_TAGS_LEN` each cell will have the length of that cell's tags included, even if that length is zero.\nThe actual tags are stored as a sequence of tag length (2 bytes), tag type (1 byte), tag bytes (variable). The format an individual tag's bytes depends on the tag type."},"5938":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-172","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Note that the dependence on the contents of the info block implies that prior to reading any data blocks you must first process a file's info block.\nIt also implies that prior to writing a data block you must know if the file's info block will include `MAX_TAGS_LEN`."},"5939":{"id":"/docs/hfile-format-173","page_id":"/docs/hfile-format","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hfile-format#fixed-file-trailer-in-version-3","content":"The fixed file trailers written with HFile version 3 are always serialized with protocol buffers.\nAdditionally, it adds an optional field to the version 2 protocol buffer named encryption\\_key.\nIf HBase is configured to encrypt HFiles this field will store a data encryption key for this particular HFile, encrypted with the current cluster master key using AES.\nFor more information see hbase.encryption.server."},"5940":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Preface","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Getting Started"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs"},"5941":{"id":"/docs-0","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"This is the official reference guide for the HBase version it ships with."},"5942":{"id":"/docs-1","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#about-this-guide","content":"About This Guide"},"5943":{"id":"/docs-2","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#contributing-to-the-documentation","content":"Contributing to the Documentation"},"5944":{"id":"/docs-3","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"Heads-up if this is your first foray into the world of distributed computing..."},"5945":{"id":"/docs-4","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#reporting-bugs","content":"Reporting Bugs"},"5946":{"id":"/docs-5","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#support-and-testing-expectations","content":"Support and Testing Expectations"},"5947":{"id":"/docs-6","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#supported","content":"Supported"},"5948":{"id":"/docs-7","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#not-supported","content":"Not Supported"},"5949":{"id":"/docs-8","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#tested","content":"Tested"},"5950":{"id":"/docs-9","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs#not-tested","content":"Not Tested"},"5951":{"id":"/docs-10","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Herein you will find either the definitive documentation on an HBase topic as of its\nstanding when the referenced HBase version shipped, or it will point to the location\nin Javadoc or\nJIRA where the pertinent information can be found."},"5952":{"id":"/docs-11","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#about-this-guide","content":"This reference guide is a work in progress. The source for this guide can be found in the\n&#x2A;hbase-website/app/pages/\\_docs/docs/\\_mdx/(multi-page)* directory of the HBase source. This reference guide is marked up\nusing MDX (just extended markdown) powered by Fumadocs from which the finished guide is generated as part of the\n'site' build target. Run"},"5953":{"id":"/docs-12","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#about-this-guide","content":"to generate this documentation.\nAmendments and improvements to the documentation are welcomed.\nClick\nthis link\nto file a new documentation bug against Apache HBase with some values pre-selected."},"5954":{"id":"/docs-13","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#contributing-to-the-documentation","content":"For an overview and suggestions to get started contributing to the documentation,\nsee the relevant section later in this documentation."},"5955":{"id":"/docs-14","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"If this is your first foray into the wonderful world of Distributed Computing, then you are in for some interesting times.\nFirst off, distributed systems are hard; making a distributed system hum requires a disparate skillset that spans systems (hardware and software) and networking."},"5956":{"id":"/docs-15","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"Your cluster's operation can hiccup because of any of a myriad set of reasons from bugs in HBase itself through misconfigurations — misconfiguration of HBase but also operating system misconfigurations — through to hardware problems whether it be a bug in your network card drivers or an underprovisioned RAM bus (to mention two recent examples of hardware issues that manifested as \"HBase is slow\"). You will also need to do a recalibration if up to this your computing has been bound to a single box.\nHere is one good starting point: Fallacies of Distributed Computing."},"5957":{"id":"/docs-16","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"That said, you are welcome.\nIt's a fun place to be.\nYours, the HBase Community."},"5958":{"id":"/docs-17","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#reporting-bugs","content":"Please use JIRA to report non-security-related bugs."},"5959":{"id":"/docs-18","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#reporting-bugs","content":"To protect existing HBase installations from new vulnerabilities, please **do not** use JIRA to report security-related bugs. Instead, send your report to the mailing list private\\@hbase.apache.org, which allows anyone to send messages, but restricts who can read them. Someone on that list will contact you to follow up on your report."},"5960":{"id":"/docs-19","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#support-and-testing-expectations","content":"The phrases *supported*, *not supported*, *tested*, and *not tested* occur several\nplaces throughout this guide. In the interest of clarity, here is a brief explanation\nof what is generally meant by these phrases, in the context of HBase."},"5961":{"id":"/docs-20","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#support-and-testing-expectations","content":"Commercial technical support for Apache HBase is provided by many Hadoop vendors. This is not the\nsense in which the term *support* is used in the context of the Apache HBase project. The Apache\nHBase team assumes no responsibility for your HBase clusters, your configuration, or your data."},"5962":{"id":"/docs-21","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#supported","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *supported* means that HBase is designed to work\nin the way described, and deviation from the defined behavior or functionality should\nbe reported as a bug."},"5963":{"id":"/docs-22","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#not-supported","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *not supported* means that a use case or use pattern\nis not expected to work and should be considered an antipattern. If you think this\ndesignation should be reconsidered for a given feature or use pattern, file a JIRA\nor start a discussion on one of the mailing lists."},"5964":{"id":"/docs-23","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#tested","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *tested* means that a feature is covered by unit\nor integration tests, and has been proven to work as expected."},"5965":{"id":"/docs-24","page_id":"/docs","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs#not-tested","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *not tested* means that a feature or use pattern\nmay or may not work in a given way, and may or may not corrupt your data or cause\noperational issues. It is an unknown, and there are no guarantees. If you can provide\nproof that a feature designated as *not tested* does work in a given way, please\nsubmit the tests and/or the metrics so that other users can gain certainty about\nsuch features or use patterns."},"5966":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","type":"page","content":"In-memory Compaction","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction"},"5967":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-0","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","content":"Accordion feature that applies LSM compaction to MemStore while data is in RAM, reducing write amplification and improving performance."},"5968":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-1","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"Overview"},"5969":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-2","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"Enabling"},"5970":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-3","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In-memory Compaction (A.K.A Accordion) is a new feature in hbase-2.0.0.\nIt was first introduced on the Apache HBase Blog at\nAccordion: HBase Breathes with In-Memory Compaction.\nQuoting the blog:"},"5971":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-4","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"> Accordion reapplies the LSM principal \\[*Log-Structured-Merge Tree*, the design pattern upon which HBase is based] to MemStore, in order to eliminate redundancies and other overhead while the data is still in RAM. Doing so decreases the frequency of flushes to HDFS, thereby reducing the write amplification and the overall disk footprint. With less flushes, the write operations are stalled less frequently as the MemStore overflows, therefore the write performance is improved. Less data on disk also implies less pressure on the block cache, higher hit rates, and eventually better read response times. Finally, having less disk writes also means having less compaction happening in the background, i.e., less cycles are stolen from productive (read and write) work. All in all, the effect of in-memory compaction can be envisioned as a catalyst that enables the system move faster as a whole."},"5972":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-5","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"A developer view is available at\nAccordion: Developer View of In-Memory Compaction."},"5973":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-6","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In-memory compaction works best when high data churn; overwrites or over-versions\ncan be eliminated while the data is still in memory. If the writes are all uniques,\nit may drag write throughput (In-memory compaction costs CPU). We suggest you test\nand compare before deploying to production."},"5974":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-7","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In this section we describe how to enable Accordion and the available configurations."},"5975":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-8","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"To enable in-memory compactions, set the *IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION* attribute\non per column family where you want the behavior. The *IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION*\nattribute can have one of four values."},"5976":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-9","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"*NONE*: No in-memory compaction."},"5977":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-10","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"*BASIC*: Basic policy enables flushing and keeps a pipeline of flushes until we trip the pipeline maximum threshold and then we flush to disk. No in-memory compaction but can help throughput as data is moved from the profligate, native ConcurrentSkipListMap data-type to more compact (and efficient) data types."},"5978":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-11","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"*EAGER*: This is *BASIC* policy plus in-memory compaction of flushes (much like the on-disk compactions done to hfiles); on compaction we apply on-disk rules eliminating versions, duplicates, ttl'd cells, etc."},"5979":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-12","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"*ADAPTIVE*: Adaptive compaction adapts to the workload. It applies either index compaction or data compaction based on the ratio of duplicate cells in the data. Experimental."},"5980":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-13","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"To enable *BASIC* on the *info* column family in the table *radish*, add the attribute to the *info* column family:"},"5981":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-14","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"Note how the IN*MEMORY\\_COMPACTION attribute shows as part of the \\_METADATA* map."},"5982":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-15","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"There is also a global configuration, *hbase.hregion.compacting.memstore.type* which you can set in your *hbase-site.xml* file. Use it to set the\ndefault on creation of a new table (On creation of a column family Store, we look first to the column family configuration looking for the\n*IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION* setting, and if none, we then consult the *hbase.hregion.compacting.memstore.type* value using its content; default is\n*NONE*)."},"5983":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-16","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"By default, new hbase system tables will have *NONE* in-memory compaction set. To specify otherwise,\non new table-creation, set *hbase.systemtables.compacting.memstore.type* to *BASIC* / *EAGER* / *ADAPTIVE* (Note, setting this value\npost-creation of system tables will not have a retroactive effect; you will have to alter your tables\nto set the in-memory attribute)."},"5984":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-17","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"When an in-memory flush happens is calculated by dividing the configured region flush size (Set in the table descriptor\nor read from *hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size*) by the number of column families and then multiplying by\n*hbase.memstore.inmemoryflush.threshold.factor*. Default is 0.014."},"5985":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-18","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"The number of flushes carried by the pipeline is monitored so as to fit within the bounds of memstore sizing\nbut you can also set a maximum on the number of flushes total by setting\n*hbase.hregion.compacting.pipeline.segments.limit*. Default is 2."},"5986":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-19","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"When a column family Store is created, it says what memstore type is in effect. As of this writing\nthere is the old-school *DefaultMemStore* which fills a *ConcurrentSkipListMap* and then flushes\nto disk or the new *CompactingMemStore* that is the implementation that provides this new\nin-memory compactions facility. Here is a log-line from a RegionServer that shows a column\nfamily Store named *family* configured to use a *CompactingMemStore*:"},"5987":{"id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction-20","page_id":"/docs/inmemory-compaction","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/inmemory-compaction#enabling","content":"Enable TRACE-level logging on the CompactingMemStore class (*org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.CompactingMemStore*) to see detail on its operation."},"5988":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"page","content":"HBase and MapReduce","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Core Concepts"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce"},"5989":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-0","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce","content":"Guide to using Apache HBase with MapReduce including configuration, examples, and best practices."},"5990":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-1","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"HBase, MapReduce, and the CLASSPATH"},"5991":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-2","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"MapReduce Scan Caching"},"5992":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-3","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#bundled-hbase-mapreduce-jobs","content":"Bundled HBase MapReduce Jobs"},"5993":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-4","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"HBase as a MapReduce Job Data Source and Data Sink"},"5994":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-5","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#writing-hfiles-directly-during-bulk-import","content":"Writing HFiles Directly During Bulk Import"},"5995":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-6","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#rowcounter-example","content":"RowCounter Example"},"5996":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-7","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#map-task-splitting","content":"Map-Task Splitting"},"5997":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-8","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#the-default-hbase-mapreduce-splitter","content":"The Default HBase MapReduce Splitter"},"5998":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-9","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#custom-splitters","content":"Custom Splitters"},"5999":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-10","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-examples","content":"HBase MapReduce Examples"},"6000":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-11","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-read-example","content":"HBase MapReduce Read Example"},"6001":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-12","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"HBase MapReduce Read/Write Example"},"6002":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-13","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example-with-multi-table-output","content":"HBase MapReduce Read/Write Example With Multi-Table Output"},"6003":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-14","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"HBase MapReduce Summary to HBase Example"},"6004":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-15","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-file-example","content":"HBase MapReduce Summary to File Example"},"6005":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-16","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"HBase MapReduce Summary to HBase Without Reducer"},"6006":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-17","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"HBase MapReduce Summary to RDBMS"},"6007":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-18","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#accessing-other-hbase-tables-in-a-mapreduce-job","content":"Accessing Other HBase Tables in a MapReduce Job"},"6008":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-19","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-speculative-execution","content":"Speculative Execution"},"6009":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-20","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce","content":"Apache MapReduce is a software framework used to analyze large amounts of data. It is provided by Apache Hadoop.\nMapReduce itself is out of the scope of this document.\nA good place to get started with MapReduce is https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r2.6.0/hadoop-mapreduce-client/hadoop-mapreduce-client-core/MapReduceTutorial.html.\nMapReduce version 2 (MR2)is now part of YARN."},"6010":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-21","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce","content":"This chapter discusses specific configuration steps you need to take to use MapReduce on data within HBase.\nIn addition, it discusses other interactions and issues between HBase and MapReduce jobs."},"6011":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-22","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce","content":"There are two mapreduce packages in HBase as in MapReduce itself: *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapred*\nand *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce*. The former does old-style API and the latter the new\nmode. The latter has more facility though you can usually find an equivalent in the older package.\nPick the package that goes with your MapReduce deploy. When in doubt or starting over, pick\n*org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce*. In the notes below, we refer to *o.a.h.h.mapreduce* but\nreplace with *o.a.h.h.mapred* if that is what you are using."},"6012":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-23","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"By default, MapReduce jobs deployed to a MapReduce cluster do not have access to\neither the HBase configuration under `$HBASE_CONF_DIR` or the HBase classes."},"6013":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-24","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"To give the MapReduce jobs the access they need, you could add \\_hbase-site.xml\\_to \\_\\$HADOOP*HOME/conf&#x2A; and add HBase jars to the *\\$HADOOP\\_HOME/lib&#x2A; directory.\nYou would then need to copy these changes across your cluster. Or you could edit *\\$HADOOP\\_HOME/conf/hadoop-env.sh* and add hbase dependencies to the `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` variable.\nNeither of these approaches is recommended because it will pollute your Hadoop install with HBase references.\nIt also requires you restart the Hadoop cluster before Hadoop can use the HBase data."},"6014":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-25","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The recommended approach is to let HBase add its dependency jars and use `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` or `-libjars`."},"6015":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-26","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"Since HBase `0.90.x`, HBase adds its dependency JARs to the job configuration itself.\nThe dependencies only need to be available on the local `CLASSPATH` and from here they'll be picked\nup and bundled into the fat job jar deployed to the MapReduce cluster. A basic trick just passes\nthe full hbase classpath — all hbase and dependent jars as well as configurations — to the mapreduce\njob runner letting hbase utility pick out from the full-on classpath what it needs adding them to the\nMapReduce job configuration (See the source at `TableMapReduceUtil#addDependencyJars(org.apache.hadoop.mapreduce.Job)` for how this is done)."},"6016":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-27","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The following example runs the bundled HBase RowCounter MapReduce job against a table named `usertable`.\nIt sets into `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` the jars hbase needs to run in an MapReduce context (including configuration files such as hbase-site.xml).\nBe sure to use the correct version of the HBase JAR for your system; replace the VERSION string in the below command line w/ the version of\nyour local hbase install. The backticks (\\`\\`\\`) cause the shell to execute the sub-commands, setting the output of `hbase classpath` into `HADOOP_CLASSPATH`.\nThis example assumes you use a BASH-compatible shell."},"6017":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-28","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The above command will launch a row counting mapreduce job against the hbase cluster that is pointed to by your local configuration on a cluster that the hadoop configs are pointing to."},"6018":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-29","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The main for the `hbase-mapreduce.jar` is a Driver that lists a few basic mapreduce tasks that ship with hbase.\nFor example, presuming your install is hbase `2.0.0-SNAPSHOT`:"},"6019":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-30","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"You can use the above listed shortnames for mapreduce jobs as in the below re-run of the row counter job (again, presuming your install is hbase `2.0.0-SNAPSHOT`):"},"6020":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-31","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"You might find the more selective `hbase mapredcp` tool output of interest; it lists the minimum set of jars needed\nto run a basic mapreduce job against an hbase install. It does not include configuration. You'll probably need to add\nthese if you want your MapReduce job to find the target cluster. You'll probably have to also add pointers to extra jars\nonce you start to do anything of substance. Just specify the extras by passing the system propery `-Dtmpjars` when\nyou run `hbase mapredcp`."},"6021":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-32","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"For jobs that do not package their dependencies or call `TableMapReduceUtil#addDependencyJars`, the following command structure is necessary:"},"6022":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-33","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The example may not work if you are running HBase from its build directory rather than an installed location.\nYou may see an error like the following:"},"6023":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-34","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"If this occurs, try modifying the command as follows, so that it uses the HBase JARs from the &#x2A;target/* directory within the build environment."},"6024":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-35","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"Some MapReduce jobs that use HBase fail to launch.\nThe symptom is an exception similar to the following:"},"6025":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-36","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This is caused by an optimization introduced in HBASE-9867 that inadvertently introduced a classloader dependency."},"6026":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-37","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This affects both jobs using the `-libjars` option and \"fat jar,\" those which package their runtime dependencies in a nested `lib` folder."},"6027":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-38","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"In order to satisfy the new classloader requirements, `hbase-protocol.jar` must be included in Hadoop's classpath.\nSee HBase, MapReduce, and the CLASSPATH for current recommendations for resolving classpath errors.\nThe following is included for historical purposes."},"6028":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-39","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This can be resolved system-wide by including a reference to the `hbase-protocol.jar` in Hadoop's lib directory, via a symlink or by copying the jar into the new location."},"6029":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-40","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This can also be achieved on a per-job launch basis by including it in the `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` environment variable at job submission time.\nWhen launching jobs that package their dependencies, all three of the following job launching commands satisfy this requirement:"},"6030":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-41","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"For jars that do not package their dependencies, the following command structure is necessary:"},"6031":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-42","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"See also HBASE-10304 for further discussion of this issue."},"6032":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-43","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"TableMapReduceUtil now restores the option to set scanner caching (the number of rows which are cached before returning the result to the client) on the Scan object that is passed in.\nThis functionality was lost due to a bug in HBase 0.95 (HBASE-11558), which is fixed for HBase 0.98.5 and 0.96.3.\nThe priority order for choosing the scanner caching is as follows:"},"6033":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-44","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Caching settings which are set on the scan object."},"6034":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-45","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Caching settings which are specified via the configuration option `hbase.client.scanner.caching`, which can either be set manually in *hbase-site.xml* or via the helper method `TableMapReduceUtil.setScannerCaching()`."},"6035":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-46","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"The default value `HConstants.DEFAULT_HBASE_CLIENT_SCANNER_CACHING`, which is set to `100`."},"6036":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-47","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Optimizing the caching settings is a balance between the time the client waits for a result and the number of sets of results the client needs to receive.\nIf the caching setting is too large, the client could end up waiting for a long time or the request could even time out.\nIf the setting is too small, the scan needs to return results in several pieces.\nIf you think of the scan as a shovel, a bigger cache setting is analogous to a bigger shovel, and a smaller cache setting is equivalent to more shoveling in order to fill the bucket."},"6037":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-48","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"The list of priorities mentioned above allows you to set a reasonable default, and override it for specific operations."},"6038":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-49","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"See the API documentation for Scan for more details."},"6039":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-50","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#bundled-hbase-mapreduce-jobs","content":"The HBase JAR also serves as a Driver for some bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo learn about the bundled MapReduce jobs, run the following command."},"6040":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-51","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#bundled-hbase-mapreduce-jobs","content":"Each of the valid program names are bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo run one of the jobs, model your command after the following example."},"6041":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-52","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"HBase can be used as a data source, TableInputFormat, and data sink, TableOutputFormat or MultiTableOutputFormat, for MapReduce jobs.\nWriting MapReduce jobs that read or write HBase, it is advisable to subclass TableMapper and/or TableReducer.\nSee the do-nothing pass-through classes IdentityTableMapper and IdentityTableReducer for basic usage.\nFor a more involved example, see RowCounter or review the `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.TestTableMapReduce` unit test."},"6042":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-53","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"If you run MapReduce jobs that use HBase as source or sink, need to specify source and sink table and column names in your configuration."},"6043":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-54","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"When you read from HBase, the `TableInputFormat` requests the list of regions from HBase and makes a map, which is either a `map-per-region` or `mapreduce.job.maps` map, whichever is smaller.\nIf your job only has two maps, raise `mapreduce.job.maps` to a number greater than the number of regions.\nMaps will run on the adjacent TaskTracker/NodeManager if you are running a TaskTracer/NodeManager and RegionServer per node.\nWhen writing to HBase, it may make sense to avoid the Reduce step and write back into HBase from within your map.\nThis approach works when your job does not need the sort and collation that MapReduce does on the map-emitted data.\nOn insert, HBase 'sorts' so there is no point double-sorting (and shuffling data around your MapReduce cluster) unless you need to.\nIf you do not need the Reduce, your map might emit counts of records processed for reporting at the end of the job, or set the number of Reduces to zero and use TableOutputFormat.\nIf running the Reduce step makes sense in your case, you should typically use multiple reducers so that load is spread across the HBase cluster."},"6044":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-55","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"A new HBase partitioner, the HRegionPartitioner, can run as many reducers the number of existing regions.\nThe HRegionPartitioner is suitable when your table is large and your upload will not greatly alter the number of existing regions upon completion.\nOtherwise use the default partitioner."},"6045":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-56","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#writing-hfiles-directly-during-bulk-import","content":"If you are importing into a new table, you can bypass the HBase API and write your content directly to the filesystem, formatted into HBase data files (HFiles). Your import will run faster, perhaps an order of magnitude faster.\nFor more on how this mechanism works, see Bulk Load."},"6046":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-57","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#rowcounter-example","content":"The included RowCounter MapReduce job uses `TableInputFormat` and does a count of all rows in the specified table.\nTo run it, use the following command:"},"6047":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-58","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#rowcounter-example","content":"This will invoke the HBase MapReduce Driver class.\nSelect `rowcounter` from the choice of jobs offered.\nThis will print rowcounter usage advice to standard output.\nSpecify the tablename, column to count, and output directory.\nIf you have classpath errors, see HBase, MapReduce, and the CLASSPATH."},"6048":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-59","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#the-default-hbase-mapreduce-splitter","content":"When TableInputFormat is used to source an HBase table in a MapReduce job, its splitter will make a map task for each region of the table.\nThus, if there are 100 regions in the table, there will be 100 map-tasks for the job - regardless of how many column families are selected in the Scan."},"6049":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-60","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#custom-splitters","content":"For those interested in implementing custom splitters, see the method `getSplits` in TableInputFormatBase.\nThat is where the logic for map-task assignment resides."},"6050":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-61","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-read-example","content":"The following is an example of using HBase as a MapReduce source in read-only manner.\nSpecifically, there is a Mapper instance but no Reducer, and nothing is being emitted from the Mapper.\nThe job would be defined as follows..."},"6051":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-62","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-read-example","content":"...and the mapper instance would extend TableMapper..."},"6052":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-63","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"The following is an example of using HBase both as a source and as a sink with MapReduce.\nThis example will simply copy data from one table to another."},"6053":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-64","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"An explanation is required of what `TableMapReduceUtil` is doing, especially with the reducer. TableOutputFormat is being used as the outputFormat class, and several parameters are being set on the config (e.g., `TableOutputFormat.OUTPUT_TABLE`), as well as setting the reducer output key to `ImmutableBytesWritable` and reducer value to `Writable`.\nThese could be set by the programmer on the job and conf, but `TableMapReduceUtil` tries to make things easier."},"6054":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-65","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"The following is the example mapper, which will create a `Put` and matching the input `Result` and emit it.\nNote: this is what the CopyTable utility does."},"6055":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-66","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"There isn't actually a reducer step, so `TableOutputFormat` takes care of sending the `Put` to the target table."},"6056":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-67","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"This is just an example, developers could choose not to use `TableOutputFormat` and connect to the target table themselves."},"6057":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-68","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example-with-multi-table-output","content":"TODO: example for `MultiTableOutputFormat`."},"6058":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-69","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"The following example uses HBase as a MapReduce source and sink with a summarization step.\nThis example will count the number of distinct instances of a value in a table and write those summarized counts in another table."},"6059":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-70","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"In this example mapper a column with a String-value is chosen as the value to summarize upon.\nThis value is used as the key to emit from the mapper, and an `IntWritable` represents an instance counter."},"6060":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-71","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"In the reducer, the \"ones\" are counted (just like any other MR example that does this), and then emits a `Put`."},"6061":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-72","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-file-example","content":"This very similar to the summary example above, with exception that this is using HBase as a MapReduce source but HDFS as the sink.\nThe differences are in the job setup and in the reducer.\nThe mapper remains the same."},"6062":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-73","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-file-example","content":"As stated above, the previous Mapper can run unchanged with this example.\nAs for the Reducer, it is a \"generic\" Reducer instead of extending TableMapper and emitting Puts."},"6063":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-74","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"It is also possible to perform summaries without a reducer - if you use HBase as the reducer."},"6064":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-75","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"An HBase target table would need to exist for the job summary.\nThe Table method `incrementColumnValue` would be used to atomically increment values.\nFrom a performance perspective, it might make sense to keep a Map of values with their values to be incremented for each map-task, and make one update per key at during the `cleanup` method of the mapper.\nHowever, your mileage may vary depending on the number of rows to be processed and unique keys."},"6065":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-76","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"In the end, the summary results are in HBase."},"6066":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-77","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"Sometimes it is more appropriate to generate summaries to an RDBMS.\nFor these cases, it is possible to generate summaries directly to an RDBMS via a custom reducer.\nThe `setup` method can connect to an RDBMS (the connection information can be passed via custom parameters in the context) and the cleanup method can close the connection."},"6067":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-78","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"It is critical to understand that number of reducers for the job affects the summarization implementation, and you'll have to design this into your reducer.\nSpecifically, whether it is designed to run as a singleton (one reducer) or multiple reducers.\nNeither is right or wrong, it depends on your use-case.\nRecognize that the more reducers that are assigned to the job, the more simultaneous connections to the RDBMS will be created - this will scale, but only to a point."},"6068":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-79","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"In the end, the summary results are written to your RDBMS table/s."},"6069":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-80","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#accessing-other-hbase-tables-in-a-mapreduce-job","content":"Although the framework currently allows one HBase table as input to a MapReduce job, other HBase tables can be accessed as lookup tables, etc., in a MapReduce job via creating an Table instance in the setup method of the Mapper."},"6070":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-81","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-speculative-execution","content":"It is generally advisable to turn off speculative execution for MapReduce jobs that use HBase as a source.\nThis can either be done on a per-Job basis through properties, or on the entire cluster.\nEspecially for longer running jobs, speculative execution will create duplicate map-tasks which will double-write your data to HBase; this is probably not what you want."},"6071":{"id":"/docs/mapreduce-82","page_id":"/docs/mapreduce","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/mapreduce#mapreduce-speculative-execution","content":"See Speculative Execution for more information."},"6072":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"page","content":"RegionServer Off-Heap Read/Write Path","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Advanced Features"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write"},"6073":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-0","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write","content":"Using off-heap memory for HBase read and write paths to reduce GC pressure and improve P99/P999 RPC latencies."},"6074":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-1","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-write-overview","content":"Overview"},"6075":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-2","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-path","content":"Offheap read-path"},"6076":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-3","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"Tuning the RPC buffer pool"},"6077":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-4","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Read block from HDFS to offheap directly"},"6078":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-5","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-write-path","content":"Offheap write-path"},"6079":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-6","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-write-overview","content":"To help reduce P99/P999 RPC latencies, HBase 2.x has made the read and write path use a pool of offheap buffers. Cells are\nallocated in offheap memory outside of the purview of the JVM garbage collector with attendent reduction in GC pressure.\nIn the write path, the request packet received from client will be read in on a pre-allocated offheap buffer and retained\noffheap until those cells are successfully persisted to the WAL and Memstore. The memory data structure in Memstore does\nnot directly store the cell memory, but references the cells encoded in the offheap buffers. Similarly for the read path.\nWe'll try to read the block cache first and if a cache misses, we'll go to the HFile and read the respective block. The\nworkflow from reading blocks to sending cells to client does its best to avoid on-heap memory allocations reducing the\namount of work the GC has to do."},"6080":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-7","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-write-overview","content":"For redress for the single mention of onheap in the read-section of the diagram above see Read block from HDFS to offheap directly."},"6081":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-8","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-path","content":"In HBase-2.0.0, HBASE-11425 changed the HBase read path so it\ncould hold the read-data off-heap avoiding copying of cached data (BlockCache) on to the java heap (for uncached data,\nsee note under the diagram in the section above). This reduces GC pauses given there is less garbage made and so less\nto clear. The off-heap read path can have a performance that is similar or better to that of the on-heap LRU cache.\nThis feature is available since HBase 2.0.0. Refer to below blogs for more details and test results on off heaped read path\nOffheaping the Read Path in Apache HBase: Part 1 of 2\nand Offheap Read-Path in Production - The Alibaba story"},"6082":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-9","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-path","content":"For an end-to-end off-heaped read-path, all you have to do is enable an off-heap backed Off-heap Block Cache(BC).\nTo do this, configure *hbase.bucketcache.ioengine* to be *offheap* in *hbase-site.xml* (See BucketCache Deploy Modes to learn\nmore about *hbase.bucketcache.ioengine* options). Also specify the total capacity of the BC using `hbase.bucketcache.size`.\nPlease remember to adjust value of 'HBASE*OFFHEAPSIZE' in \\_hbase-env.sh* (See BucketCache Example Configuration for help sizing and an example\nenabling). This configuration is for specifying the maximum possible off-heap memory allocation for the RegionServer java\nprocess. This should be bigger than the off-heap BC size to accommodate usage by other features making use of off-heap memory\nsuch as Server RPC buffer pool and short-circuit reads (See discussion in BucketCache Example Configuration)."},"6083":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-10","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-path","content":"Please keep in mind that there is no default for `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` which means the `BlockCache` is OFF by default\n(See the \"Direct Memory Usage In HBase\" info section in BucketCache Example Configuration)."},"6084":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-11","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-read-path","content":"This is all you need to do to enable off-heap read path. Most buffers in HBase are already off-heap. With BC off-heap,\nthe read pipeline will copy data between HDFS and the server socket — caveat hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max —\nsending results back to the client."},"6085":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-12","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"It is possible to tune the ByteBuffer pool on the RPC server side used to accumulate the cell bytes and create result\ncell blocks to send back to the client side. Use `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled` to turn this pool ON or OFF. By\ndefault this pool is ON and available. HBase will create off-heap ByteBuffers and pool them them by default. Please\nmake sure not to turn this OFF if you want end-to-end off-heaping in read path."},"6086":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-13","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If this pool is turned off, the server will create temp buffers onheap to accumulate the cell bytes and\nmake a result cell block. This can impact the GC on a highly read loaded server."},"6087":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-14","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The config keys which start with prefix `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir` are deprecated in hbase-3.x\n(the internal pool implementation changed). If you are still in hbase-2.2.x or older, then just\nuse the old config keys. Otherwise if in hbase-3.x or hbase-2.3.x+, please use the new config keys\n(See deprecated and new configs in\nHBase3.x)"},"6088":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-15","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"Next thing to tune is the ByteBuffer pool on the RPC server side. The user can tune this pool with respect to how\nmany buffers are in the pool and what should be the size of each ByteBuffer. Use the config\n`hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.buffer.size` to tune each of the buffer sizes. Default is 64KB for hbase-2.2.x\nand less, changed to 65KB by default for hbase-2.3.x+\n(see HBASE-22532)"},"6089":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-16","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"When the result size is larger than one 64KB (Default) ByteBuffer size, the server will try to grab more than one\nByteBuffer and make a result cell block out of a collection of fixed-sized ByteBuffers. When the pool is running\nout of buffers, the server will skip the pool and create temporary on-heap buffers."},"6090":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-17","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The maximum number of ByteBuffers in the pool can be tuned using the config `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max`.\nIts default is a factor of region server handlers count (See the config `hbase.regionserver.handler.count`). The\nmath is such that by default we consider 2 MB as the result cell block size per read result and each handler will be\nhandling a read. For 2 MB size, we need 32 buffers each of size 64 KB (See default buffer size in pool). So per handler\n32 ByteBuffers(BB). We allocate twice this size as the max BBs count such that one handler can be creating the response\nand handing it to the RPC Responder thread and then handling a new request creating a new response cell block (using\npooled buffers). Even if the responder could not send back the first TCP reply immediately, our count should allow that\nwe should still have enough buffers in our pool without having to make temporary buffers on the heap. Again for smaller\nsized random row reads, tune this max count. These are lazily created buffers and the count is the max count to be pooled."},"6091":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-18","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If you still see GC issues even after making end-to-end read path off-heap, look for issues in the appropriate buffer\npool. Check for the below RegionServer log line at INFO level in HBase2.x:"},"6092":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-19","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"Or the following log message in HBase3.x:"},"6093":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-20","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The setting for *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* in *hbase-env.sh* should consider this off heap buffer pool on the server side also.\nWe need to config this max off heap size for the RegionServer as a bit higher than the sum of this max pool size and\nthe off heap cache size. The TCP layer will also need to create direct bytebuffers for TCP communication. Also the DFS\nclient will need some off-heap to do its workings especially if short-circuit reads are configured. Allocating an extra\n1 - 2 GB for the max direct memory size has worked in tests."},"6094":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-21","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If you are using coprocessors and refer to the Cells in the read results, DO NOT store reference to these Cells out of\nthe scope of the CP hook methods. Some times the CPs want to store info about the cell (Like its row key) for considering\nin the next CP hook call etc. For such cases, pls clone the required fields of the entire Cell as per the use cases.\n\\[ See CellUtil#cloneXXX(Cell) APIs ]"},"6095":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-22","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"In HBase-2.x, the RegionServer will read blocks from HDFS to a temporary onheap ByteBuffer and then flush to\nthe BucketCache. Even if the BucketCache is offheap, we will first pull the HDFS read onheap before writing\nit out to the offheap BucketCache. We can observe much GC pressure when cache hit ratio low (e.g. a cacheHitRatio \\~ 60% ).\nHBASE-21879 addresses this issue (Requires hbase-2.3.x/hbase-3.x).\nIt depends on there being a supporting HDFS being in place (hadoop-2.10.x or hadoop-3.3.x) and it may require patching\nHBase itself (as of this writing); see\nHBASE-21879 Read HFile's block to ByteBuffer directly instead of to byte for reducing young gc purpose.\nAppropriately setup, reads from HDFS can be into offheap buffers passed offheap to the offheap BlockCache to cache."},"6096":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-23","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"For more details about the design and performance improvement, please see the\nDesign Doc -Read HFile's block to Offheap."},"6097":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-24","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Here we will share some best practice about the performance tuning but first we introduce new (hbase-3.x/hbase-2.3.x) configuration names\nthat go with the new internal pool implementation (`ByteBuffAllocator` vs the old `ByteBufferPool`), some of which mimic now deprecated\nhbase-2.2.x configurations discussed above in the Tuning the RPC buffer pool. Much of the advice here overlaps that given above\nin the Tuning the RPC buffer pool since the implementations have similar configurations."},"6098":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-25","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.pool.enabled` is for whether the RegionServer will use the pooled offheap ByteBuffer allocator. Default\nvalue is true. In hbase-2.x, the deprecated `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled` did similar and is mapped to this config\nuntil support for the old configuration is removed. This new name will be used in hbase-3.x and hbase-2.3.x+."},"6099":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-26","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.minimal.allocate.size` is the threshold at which we start allocating from the pool. Otherwise the\nrequest will be allocated from onheap directly because it would be wasteful allocating small stuff from our pool of fixed-size\nByteBuffers. The default minimum is `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size/6`."},"6100":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-27","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`: The `ByteBuffAllocator`, the new pool/reservoir implementation, has fixed-size\nByteBuffers. This config is for how many buffers to pool. Its default value is 2MB \\_ 2 \\_ hbase.regionserver.handler.count / 65KB\n(similar to the discussion above in Tuning the RPC buffer pool). If the default `hbase.regionserver.handler.count` is 30, then the default will be 1890."},"6101":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-28","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`: The byte size of each ByteBuffer. The default value is 66560 (65KB), here we choose 65KB instead of 64KB\nbecause of HBASE-22532."},"6102":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-29","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"The three config keys —`hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled`, `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.buffer.size` and `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max` — introduced in hbase-2.x\nhave been renamed and deprecated in hbase-3.x/hbase-2.3.x. Please use the new config keys instead:\n`hbase.server.allocator.pool.enabled`, `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size` and `hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`."},"6103":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-30","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Next, we have some suggestions regards performance."},"6104":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-31","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"**Please make sure that there are enough pooled DirectByteBuffer in your ByteBuffAllocator.**\\\nThe ByteBuffAllocator will allocate ByteBuffer from the DirectByteBuffer pool first. If\nthere's no available ByteBuffer in the pool, then we will allocate the ByteBuffers from onheap.\nBy default, we will pre-allocate 4MB for each RPC handler (The handler count is determined by the config:\n`hbase.regionserver.handler.count`, it has the default value 30) . That's to say, if your `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`\nis 65KB, then your pool will have 2MB \\_ 2 / 65KB \\_ 30 = 945 DirectByteBuffer. If you have a large scan and a big cache,\nyou may have a RPC response whose bytes size is greater than 2MB (another 2MB for receiving rpc request), then it will\nbe better to increase the `hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`."},"6105":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-32","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"The RegionServer web UI has statistics on ByteBuffAllocator:"},"6106":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-33","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"If the following condition is met, you may need to increase your max buffer.count:"},"6107":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-34","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"**Please make sure the buffer size is greater than your block size.**\\\nWe have the default block size of 64KB, so almost all of the data blocks will be 64KB + a small delta, where the delta is\nvery small, depending on the size of the last Cell. If we set `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`=64KB,\nthen each block will be allocated as two ByteBuffers: one 64KB DirectByteBuffer and one HeapByteBuffer for the delta bytes.\nIdeally, we should let the data block to be allocated as one ByteBuffer; it has a simpler data structure, faster access speed,\nand less heap usage. Also, if the blocks are a composite of multiple ByteBuffers, to validate the checksum\nwe have to perform a temporary heap copy (see HBASE-21917)\nwhereas if it's a single ByteBuffer we can speed the checksum by calling the hadoop' checksum native lib; it's more faster."},"6108":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-35","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Please also see: HBASE-22483"},"6109":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-36","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Don't forget to up your *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* accordingly."},"6110":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-37","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-write-path","content":"In hbase-2.x, HBASE-15179 made the HBase write path work off-heap. By default, the MemStores in\nHBase have always used MemStore Local Allocation Buffers (MSLABs) to avoid memory fragmentation; an MSLAB creates bigger fixed sized chunks and then the\nMemStores Cell's data gets copied into these MSLAB chunks. These chunks can be pooled also and from hbase-2.x on, the MSLAB pool is by default ON.\nWrite off-heaping makes use of the MSLAB pool. It creates MSLAB chunks as Direct ByteBuffers and pools them."},"6111":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-38","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-write-path","content":"`hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is the configuration key which controls the amount of off-heap data. Its value is the number of megabytes\nof off-heap memory that should be used by MSLAB (e.g. `25` would result in 25MB of off-heap). Be sure to increase *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* which will set the JVM's\nMaxDirectMemorySize property (see Tuning the RPC buffer pool for more on *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE*). The default value of\n`hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is 0 which means MSLAB uses onheap, not offheap, chunks by default."},"6112":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-39","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-write-path","content":"`hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.chunksize` controls the size of each off-heap chunk. Default is `2097152` (2MB)."},"6113":{"id":"/docs/offheap-read-write-40","page_id":"/docs/offheap-read-write","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/offheap-read-write#offheap-write-path","content":"When a Cell is added to a MemStore, the bytes for that Cell are copied into these off-heap buffers (if `hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is non-zero)\nand a Cell POJO will refer to this memory area. This can greatly reduce the on-heap occupancy of the MemStores and reduce the total heap utilization for RegionServers\nin a write-heavy workload. On-heap and off-heap memory utiliazation are tracked at multiple levels to implement low level and high level memory management.\nThe decision to flush a MemStore considers both the on-heap and off-heap usage of that MemStore. At the Region level, we sum the on-heap and off-heap usages and\ncompare them against the region flush size (128MB, by default). Globally, on-heap size occupancy of all memstores are tracked as well as off-heap size. When any of\nthese sizes breache the lower mark (`hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size.lower.limit`) or the maximum size `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size`), all\nregions are selected for forced flushes."},"6114":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"page","content":"Backup & Snapshots","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots"},"6115":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","content":"HBase backup strategies and snapshot operations for data protection, including full cluster backups and live cluster snapshots."},"6116":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-backup","content":"HBase Backup"},"6117":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#full-shutdown-backup","content":"Full Shutdown Backup"},"6118":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stop-hbase","content":"Stop HBase"},"6119":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#distcp","content":"Distcp"},"6120":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-if-needed","content":"Restore (if needed)"},"6121":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---replication","content":"Live Cluster Backup - Replication"},"6122":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---copytable","content":"Live Cluster Backup - CopyTable"},"6123":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---export","content":"Live Cluster Backup - Export"},"6124":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-snapshots","content":"HBase Snapshots"},"6125":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-configuration","content":"Configuration"},"6126":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot","content":"Take a Snapshot"},"6127":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-without-flushing","content":"Take a Snapshot Without Flushing"},"6128":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-with-ttl","content":"Take a Snapshot With TTL"},"6129":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"Default Snapshot TTL:"},"6130":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-with-custom-max_filesize","content":"Take a snapshot with custom MAX_FILESIZE"},"6131":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-16","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"Enable/Disable Snapshot Auto Cleanup on running cluster:"},"6132":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-17","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#listing-snapshots","content":"Listing Snapshots"},"6133":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-18","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#deleting-snapshots","content":"Deleting Snapshots"},"6134":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-19","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#clone-a-table-from-snapshot","content":"Clone a table from snapshot"},"6135":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-20","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-a-snapshot","content":"Restore a snapshot"},"6136":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-21","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#snapshots-operations-and-acls","content":"Snapshots operations and ACLs"},"6137":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-22","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#export-to-another-cluster","content":"Export to another cluster"},"6138":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-23","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#limiting-bandwidth-consumption","content":"Limiting Bandwidth Consumption"},"6139":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-24","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-an-amazon-s3-bucket","content":"Storing Snapshots in an Amazon S3 Bucket"},"6140":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-25","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"6141":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-26","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-microsoft-azure-blob-storage","content":"Storing Snapshots in Microsoft Azure Blob Storage"},"6142":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-27","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-azure-prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"6143":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-28","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-aliyun-object-storage-service","content":"Storing Snapshots in Aliyun Object Storage Service"},"6144":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-29","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-aliyun-prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"6145":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-30","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-backup","content":"There are two broad strategies for performing HBase backups: backing up with a full cluster shutdown, and backing up on a live cluster. Each approach has pros and cons."},"6146":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-31","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-backup","content":"For additional information, see HBase Backup Options over on the Sematext Blog."},"6147":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-32","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#full-shutdown-backup","content":"Some environments can tolerate a periodic full shutdown of their HBase cluster, for example if it is being used a back-end analytic capacity and not serving front-end web-pages. The benefits are that the NameNode/Master are RegionServers are down, so there is no chance of missing any in-flight changes to either StoreFiles or metadata. The obvious con is that the cluster is down. The steps include:"},"6148":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-33","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#distcp","content":"Distcp could be used to either copy the contents of the HBase directory in HDFS to either the same cluster in another directory, or to a different cluster.\\\nNote: Distcp works in this situation because the cluster is down and there are no in-flight edits to files. Distcp-ing of files in the HBase directory is not generally recommended on a live cluster."},"6149":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-34","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-if-needed","content":"The backup of the hbase directory from HDFS is copied onto the 'real' hbase directory via distcp. The act of copying these files creates new HDFS metadata, which is why a restore of the NameNode edits from the time of the HBase backup isn't required for this kind of restore, because it's a restore (via distcp) of a specific HDFS directory (i.e., the HBase part) not the entire HDFS file-system."},"6150":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-35","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---replication","content":"This approach assumes that there is a second cluster. See the HBase page on replication for more information."},"6151":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-36","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---copytable","content":"The copytable utility could either be used to copy data from one table to another on the same cluster, or to copy data to another table on another cluster."},"6152":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-37","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---copytable","content":"Since the cluster is up, there is a risk that edits could be missed in the copy process."},"6153":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-38","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---export","content":"The export approach dumps the content of a table to HDFS on the same cluster. To restore the data, the import utility would be used."},"6154":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-39","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#live-cluster-backup---export","content":"Since the cluster is up, there is a risk that edits could be missed in the export process. If you want to know more about HBase back-up and restore see the page on Backup and Restore."},"6155":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-40","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-snapshots","content":"HBase Snapshots allow you to take a copy of a table (both contents and metadata)with a very small performance impact. A Snapshot is an immutable collection of table metadata and a list of HFiles that comprised the table at the time the Snapshot was taken. A \"clone\" of a snapshot creates a new table from that snapshot, and a \"restore\" of a snapshot returns the contents of a table to what it was when the snapshot was created. The \"clone\" and \"restore\" operations do not require any data to be copied, as the underlying HFiles (the files which contain the data for an HBase table) are not modified with either action. Simiarly, exporting a snapshot to another cluster has little impact on RegionServers of the local cluster."},"6156":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-41","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#hbase-snapshots","content":"Prior to version 0.94.6, the only way to backup or to clone a table is to use CopyTable/ExportTable, or to copy all the hfiles in HDFS after disabling the table. The disadvantages of these methods are that you can degrade region server performance (Copy/Export Table) or you need to disable the table, that means no reads or writes; and this is usually unacceptable."},"6157":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-configuration","content":"To turn on the snapshot support just set the `hbase.snapshot.enabled` property to true. (Snapshots are enabled by default in 0.95+ and off by default in 0.94.6+)"},"6158":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot","content":"You can take a snapshot of a table regardless of whether it is enabled or disabled. The snapshot operation doesn't involve any data copying."},"6159":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-without-flushing","content":"The default behavior is to perform a flush of data in memory before the snapshot is taken. This means that data in memory is included in the snapshot. In most cases, this is the desired behavior. However, if your set-up can tolerate data in memory being excluded from the snapshot, you can use the `SKIP_FLUSH` option of the `snapshot` command to disable and flushing while taking the snapshot."},"6160":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-45","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-without-flushing","content":"There is no way to determine or predict whether a very concurrent insert or update will be\nincluded in a given snapshot, whether flushing is enabled or disabled. A snapshot is only a\nrepresentation of a table during a window of time. The amount of time the snapshot operation will\ntake to reach each Region Server may vary from a few seconds to a minute, depending on the\nresource load and speed of the hardware or network, among other factors. There is also no way to\nknow whether a given insert or update is in memory or has been flushed."},"6161":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-46","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-with-ttl","content":"Snapshots have a lifecycle that is independent from the table from which they are created. Although data in a table may be stored with TTL the data files containing them become frozen by the snapshot. Space consumed by expired cells will not be reclaimed by normal table housekeeping like compaction. While this is expected it can be inconvenient at scale. When many snapshots are under management and the data in various tables is expired by TTL some notion of optional TTL (and optional default TTL) for snapshots could be useful."},"6162":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-47","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-with-ttl","content":"The above command creates snapshot `snapshot1234` with TTL of 86400 sec (24 hours) and hence, the snapshot is supposed to be cleaned up after 24 hours"},"6163":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-48","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"User specified default TTL with config `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl`"},"6164":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-49","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"FOREVER if `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl` is not set"},"6165":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-50","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"While creating a snapshot, if TTL in seconds is not explicitly specified, the above logic will be followed to determine the TTL. If no configs are changed, the default behavior is that all snapshots will be retained forever (until manual deletion). If a different default TTL behavior is desired, `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl` can be set to a default TTL in seconds. Any snapshot created without an explicit TTL will take this new value."},"6166":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-51","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"If `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl` is set, a snapshot with an explicit \\{TTL ⇒ 0} or \\{TTL ⇒ -1} will also take this value. In this case, a TTL \\< -1 (such as \\{TTL ⇒ -2}) should be used to indicate FOREVER."},"6167":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-52","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"To summarize concisely,"},"6168":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-53","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"Snapshot with TTL value \\< -1 will stay forever regardless of any server side config changes (until deleted manually by user)."},"6169":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-54","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"Snapshot with TTL value > 0 will be deleted automatically soon after TTL expires."},"6170":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-55","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"Snapshot created without specifying TTL will always have TTL value represented by config `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl`. Default value of this config is 0, which represents: keep the snapshot forever (until deleted manually by user)."},"6171":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-56","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#default-snapshot-ttl","content":"From client side, TTL value 0 or -1 should never be explicitly provided because they will be treated same as snapshot without TTL (same as above point 3) and hence will use TTL as per value represented by config `hbase.master.snapshot.ttl`."},"6172":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-57","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#take-a-snapshot-with-custom-max_filesize","content":"Optionally, snapshots can be created with a custom max file size configuration that will be used by cloned tables, instead of the global `hbase.hregion.max.filesize` configuration property. This is mostly useful when exporting snapshots between different clusters. If the HBase cluster where the snapshot is originally taken has a much larger value set for `hbase.hregion.max.filesize` than one or more clusters where the snapshot is being exported to, a storm of region splits may occur when restoring the snapshot on destination clusters. Specifying `MAX_FILESIZE` on properties passed to `snapshot` command will save informed value into the table's `MAX_FILESIZE` decriptor at snapshot creation time. If the table already defines `MAX_FILESIZE` descriptor, this property would be ignored and have no effect."},"6173":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-58","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"By default, snapshot auto cleanup based on TTL would be enabled for any new cluster. At any point in time, if snapshot cleanup is supposed to be stopped due to some snapshot restore activity or any other reason, it is advisable to disable it using shell command:"},"6174":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-59","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"We can re-enable it using:"},"6175":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-60","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"The shell command with switch false would disable snapshot auto cleanup activity based on TTL and return the previous state of the activity(true: running already, false: disabled already)"},"6176":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-61","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"A sample output for above commands:"},"6177":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-62","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"We can query whether snapshot auto cleanup is enabled for cluster using:"},"6178":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-63","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#enabledisable-snapshot-auto-cleanup-on-running-cluster","content":"The command would return output in true/false."},"6179":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-64","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#listing-snapshots","content":"List all snapshots taken (by printing the names and relative information)."},"6180":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-65","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#deleting-snapshots","content":"You can remove a snapshot, and the files retained for that snapshot will be removed if no longer needed."},"6181":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-66","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#clone-a-table-from-snapshot","content":"From a snapshot you can create a new table (clone operation) with the same data that you had when the snapshot was taken. The clone operation, doesn't involve data copies, and a change to the cloned table doesn't impact the snapshot or the original table."},"6182":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-67","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-a-snapshot","content":"The restore operation requires the table to be disabled, and the table will be restored to the state at the time when the snapshot was taken, changing both data and schema if required."},"6183":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-68","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-a-snapshot","content":"Since Replication works at log level and snapshots at file-system level, after a restore, the\nreplicas will be in a different state from the master. If you want to use restore, you need to\nstop replication and redo the bootstrap."},"6184":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-69","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#restore-a-snapshot","content":"In case of partial data-loss due to misbehaving client, instead of a full restore that requires the table to be disabled, you can clone the table from the snapshot and use a Map-Reduce job to copy the data that you need, from the clone to the main one."},"6185":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-70","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#snapshots-operations-and-acls","content":"If you are using security with the AccessController Coprocessor (See hbase.accesscontrol.configuration), only a global administrator can take, clone, or restore a snapshot, and these actions do not capture the ACL rights. This means that restoring a table preserves the ACL rights of the existing table, while cloning a table creates a new table that has no ACL rights until the administrator adds them."},"6186":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-71","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#export-to-another-cluster","content":"The ExportSnapshot tool copies all the data related to a snapshot (hfiles, logs, snapshot metadata) to another cluster. The tool executes a Map-Reduce job, similar to distcp, to copy files between the two clusters, and since it works at file-system level the hbase cluster does not have to be online."},"6187":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-72","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#export-to-another-cluster","content":"To copy a snapshot called MySnapshot to an HBase cluster srv2 (hdfs\\:///srv2:8082/hbase) using 16 mappers:"},"6188":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-73","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#limiting-bandwidth-consumption","content":"You can limit the bandwidth consumption when exporting a snapshot, by specifying the `-bandwidth` parameter, which expects an integer representing megabytes per second. The following example limits the above example to 200 MB/sec."},"6189":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-74","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-an-amazon-s3-bucket","content":"You can store and retrieve snapshots from Amazon S3, using the following procedure."},"6190":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-75","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-an-amazon-s3-bucket","content":"You can also store snapshots in Microsoft Azure Blob Storage. See Storing Snapshots in Microsoft\nAzure Blob\nStorage."},"6191":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-76","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"You must be using HBase 1.0 or higher and Hadoop 2.6.1 or higher, which is the first configuration that uses the Amazon AWS SDK."},"6192":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-77","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"You must use the `s3a://` protocol to connect to Amazon S3. The older `s3n://` and `s3://` protocols have various limitations and do not use the Amazon AWS SDK."},"6193":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-78","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"The `s3a://` URI must be configured and available on the server where you run the commands to export and restore the snapshot."},"6194":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-79","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"After you have fulfilled the prerequisites, take the snapshot like you normally would. Afterward, you can export it using the `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.snapshot.ExportSnapshot` command like the one below, substituting your own `s3a://` path in the `copy-from` or `copy-to` directive and substituting or modifying other options as required:"},"6195":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-80","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-s3-prerequisites","content":"You can also use the `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.snapshot.SnapshotInfo` utility with the `s3a://` path by including the `-remote-dir` option."},"6196":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-81","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-microsoft-azure-blob-storage","content":"You can store snapshots in Microsoft Azure Blog Storage using the same techniques as in Storing Snapshots in an Amazon S3 Bucket."},"6197":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-82","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-azure-prerequisites","content":"You must be using HBase 1.2 or higher with Hadoop 2.7.1 or higher. No version of HBase supports Hadoop 2.7.0."},"6198":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-83","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-azure-prerequisites","content":"Your hosts must be configured to be aware of the Azure blob storage filesystem. See https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r2.7.1/hadoop-azure/index.html."},"6199":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-84","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-azure-prerequisites","content":"After you meet the prerequisites, follow the instructions in Storing Snapshots in an Amazon S3 Bucket, replacingthe protocol specifier with `wasb://` or `wasbs://`."},"6200":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-85","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#storing-snapshots-in-aliyun-object-storage-service","content":"You can store snapshots in Aliyun Object Storage Service(Aliyun OSS) using the same techniques as in Storing Snapshots in an Amazon S3 Bucket."},"6201":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-86","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-aliyun-prerequisites","content":"You must be using HBase 1.2 or higher with Hadoop 2.9.1 or higher."},"6202":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-87","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-aliyun-prerequisites","content":"Your hosts must be configured to be aware of the Aliyun oss filesystem. See https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/stable/hadoop-aliyun/tools/hadoop-aliyun/index.html."},"6203":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots-88","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/backup-and-snapshots#operational-management-backup-and-snapshots-stroing-in-aliyun-prerequisites","content":"After you meet the prerequisites, follow the instructions in Storing Snapshots in an Amazon S3 Bucket, replacing the protocol specifier with `oss://`."},"6204":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"page","content":"Cluster Replication","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication"},"6205":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","content":"Setting up and managing HBase cluster replication for disaster recovery, data aggregation, and geographic distribution of data."},"6206":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Cluster Replication"},"6207":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"Replication Overview"},"6208":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"Cluster Topologies"},"6209":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#managing-and-configuring-cluster-replication","content":"Managing and Configuring Cluster Replication"},"6210":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"Cluster Configuration Overview"},"6211":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration","content":"Serial Replication Configuration"},"6212":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"Cluster Management Commands"},"6213":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#migrate-across-different-replication-peer-storages","content":"Migrate Across Different Replication Peer Storages"},"6214":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication","content":"Serial Replication"},"6215":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"Why need serial replication?"},"6216":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"Serial replication configuration"},"6217":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#verifying-replicated-data","content":"Verifying Replicated Data"},"6218":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#detailed-information-about-cluster-replication","content":"Detailed Information About Cluster Replication"},"6219":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Life of a WAL Edit"},"6220":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"Replication Internals"},"6221":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-16","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#choosing-region-servers-to-replicate-to","content":"Choosing Region Servers to Replicate To"},"6222":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-17","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-zookeeper","content":"Keeping Track of Logs(based on ZooKeeper)"},"6223":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-18","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-hbase-table","content":"Keeping Track of Logs(based on hbase table)"},"6224":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-19","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#reading-filtering-and-sending-edits","content":"Reading, Filtering and Sending Edits"},"6225":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-20","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cleaning-logs","content":"Cleaning Logs"},"6226":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-21","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#region-server-failover","content":"Region Server Failover"},"6227":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-22","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claiming-based-on-zookeeper","content":"The replication queue claiming (based on ZooKeeper)"},"6228":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-23","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claimingbased-on-hbase-table","content":"The replication queue claiming(based on hbase table)"},"6229":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-24","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"Replication Metrics"},"6230":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-25","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-configuration-options","content":"Replication Configuration Options"},"6231":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-26","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#monitoring-replication-status","content":"Monitoring Replication Status"},"6232":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-27","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#understanding-the-output","content":"Understanding the output"},"6233":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-28","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-observability-framework","content":"Replication Observability Framework"},"6234":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-29","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replicationwaleventtracker-table","content":"REPLICATION.WALEVENTTRACKER table"},"6235":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-30","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replicationsink_tracker-table","content":"REPLICATION.SINK_TRACKER table"},"6236":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-31","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replicationmarker-chore","content":"ReplicationMarker Chore"},"6237":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-32","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#how-to-enable-end-to-end-feature-","content":"How to enable end-to-end feature ?"},"6238":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-33","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"HBase provides a cluster replication mechanism which allows you to keep one cluster's state synchronized with that of another cluster, using the write-ahead log (WAL) of the source cluster to propagate the changes. Some use cases for cluster replication include:"},"6239":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-34","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Backup and disaster recovery"},"6240":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-35","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Data aggregation"},"6241":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-36","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Geographic data distribution"},"6242":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-37","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Online data ingestion combined with offline data analytics"},"6243":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-38","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Replication is enabled at the granularity of the column family. Before enabling replication for a\ncolumn family, create the table and all column families to be replicated, on the destination\ncluster."},"6244":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-39","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"Replication is asynchronous as we send WAL to another cluster in background, which means that when\nyou want to do recovery through replication, you could loss some data. To address this problem, we\nhave introduced a new feature called synchronous replication. As the mechanism is a bit different\nso we use a separated section to describe it. Please see Synchronous\nReplication."},"6245":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-40","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-replication","content":"At present, there is compatibility problem if Replication and WAL Compression are used together.\nIf you need to use Replication, it is recommended to set the\n`hbase.regionserver.wal.enablecompression` property to `false`. See\n(HBASE-26849) for details."},"6246":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-41","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"Cluster replication uses a source-push methodology. An HBase cluster can be a source (also called master or active, meaning that it is the originator of new data), a destination (also called slave or passive, meaning that it receives data via replication), or can fulfill both roles at once. Replication is asynchronous, and the goal of replication is eventual consistency. When the source receives an edit to a column family with replication enabled, that edit is propagated to all destination clusters using the WAL for that for that column family on the RegionServer managing the relevant region."},"6247":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"When data is replicated from one cluster to another, the original source of the data is tracked via a cluster ID which is part of the metadata. In HBase 0.96 and newer (HBASE-7709), all clusters which have already consumed the data are also tracked. This prevents replication loops."},"6248":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"The WALs for each region server must be kept in HDFS as long as they are needed to replicate data to any slave cluster. Each region server reads from the oldest log it needs to replicate and keeps track of its progress processing WALs inside ZooKeeper to simplify failure recovery. The position marker which indicates a slave cluster's progress, as well as the queue of WALs to process, may be different for every slave cluster."},"6249":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"The clusters participating in replication can be of different sizes. The master cluster relies on randomization to attempt to balance the stream of replication on the slave clusters. It is expected that the slave cluster has storage capacity to hold the replicated data, as well as any data it is responsible for ingesting. If a slave cluster does run out of room, or is inaccessible for other reasons, it throws an error and the master retains the WAL and retries the replication at intervals."},"6250":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-45","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"How your application builds on top of the HBase API matters when replication is in play. HBase's replication system provides at-least-once delivery of client edits for an enabled column family to each configured destination cluster. In the event of failure to reach a given destination, the replication system will retry sending edits in a way that might repeat a given message. HBase provides two ways of replication, one is the original replication and the other is serial replication. In the previous way of replication, there is not a guaranteed order of delivery for client edits. In the event of a RegionServer failing, recovery of the replication queue happens independent of recovery of the individual regions that server was previously handling. This means that it is possible for the not-yet-replicated edits to be serviced by a RegionServer that is currently slower to replicate than the one that handles edits from after the failure."},"6251":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-46","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"The combination of these two properties (at-least-once delivery and the lack of message ordering) means that some destination clusters may end up in a different state if your application makes use of operations that are not idempotent, e.g. Increments."},"6252":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-47","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"To solve the problem, HBase now supports serial replication, which sends edits to destination cluster as the order of requests from client. See Serial Replication."},"6253":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-48","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-overview","content":"Previously, terms such as **master-master**, **master-slave**, and **cyclical** were used to\ndescribe replication relationships in HBase. These terms added confusion, and have been abandoned\nin favor of discussions about cluster topologies appropriate for different scenarios."},"6254":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-49","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"A central source cluster might propagate changes out to multiple destination clusters, for failover or due to geographic distribution."},"6255":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-50","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"A source cluster might push changes to a destination cluster, which might also push its own changes back to the original cluster."},"6256":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-51","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"Many different low-latency clusters might push changes to one centralized cluster for backup or resource-intensive data analytics jobs. The processed data might then be replicated back to the low-latency clusters."},"6257":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-52","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"Multiple levels of replication may be chained together to suit your organization's needs. The following diagram shows a hypothetical scenario. Use the arrows to follow the data paths."},"6258":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-53","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"***Example of a Complex Cluster Replication Configuration***"},"6259":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-54","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-topologies","content":"HBase replication borrows many concepts from the **statement-based replication** design used by MySQL. Instead of SQL statements, entire WALEdits (consisting of multiple cell inserts coming from Put and Delete operations on the clients) are replicated in order to maintain atomicity."},"6260":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-55","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"Configure and start the source and destination clusters. Create tables with the same names and column families on both the source and destination clusters, so that the destination cluster knows where to store data it will receive."},"6261":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-56","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"All hosts in the source and destination clusters should be reachable to each other."},"6262":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-57","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"If both clusters use the same ZooKeeper cluster, you must use a different `zookeeper.znode.parent`, because they cannot write in the same folder."},"6263":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-58","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"On the source cluster, in HBase Shell, add the destination cluster as a peer, using the `add_peer` command."},"6264":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-59","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"On the source cluster, in HBase Shell, enable the table replication, using the `enable_table_replication` command."},"6265":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-60","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-configuration-overview","content":"Check the logs to see if replication is taking place. If so, you will see messages like the following, coming from the ReplicationSource."},"6266":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-61","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration","content":"See Serial Replication"},"6267":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-62","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**add\\_peer `<ID>` `<CLUSTER_KEY>`**\\\nAdds a replication relationship between two clusters."},"6268":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-63","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"ID — a unique string, which must not contain a hyphen."},"6269":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-64","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"CLUSTER\\_KEY: composed using the following template, with appropriate place-holders: `hbase.zookeeper.quorum:hbase.zookeeper.property.clientPort:zookeeper.znode.parent`. This value can be found on the Master UI info page."},"6270":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-65","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"STATE(optional): ENABLED or DISABLED, default value is ENABLED"},"6271":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-66","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**list\\_peers**\\\nlist all replication relationships known by this cluster"},"6272":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-67","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**enable\\_peer `<ID>`**\\\nEnable a previously-disabled replication relationship"},"6273":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-68","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**disable\\_peer `<ID>`**\\\nDisable a replication relationship. HBase will no longer send edits to that peer cluster, but it still keeps track of all the new WALs that it will need to replicate if and when it is re-enabled. WALs are retained when enabling or disabling replication as long as peers exist."},"6274":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-69","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**remove\\_peer `<ID>`**\\\nDisable and remove a replication relationship. HBase will no longer send edits to that peer cluster or keep track of WALs."},"6275":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-70","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**enable\\_table\\_replication `<TABLE_NAME>`**\\\nEnable the table replication switch for all its column families. If the table is not found in the destination cluster then it will create one with the same name and column families."},"6276":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-71","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**disable\\_table\\_replication `<TABLE_NAME>`**\\\nDisable the table replication switch for all its column families."},"6277":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-72","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**peer\\_modification\\_switch `<enable_or_disable>`, `<drain_procedures>`**\\\nEnabled/Disable peer modification operations, such as adding/removing replication peers. The second parameter means whether you want to wait until all existing peer modification procedures to finish before returning when disabling peer modification."},"6278":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-73","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cluster-management-commands","content":"**peer\\_modification\\_enabled**\\\nCheck whether peer modification is enabled."},"6279":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-74","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#migrate-across-different-replication-peer-storages","content":"Starting from 2.6.0, we introduce a file system based `ReplicationPeerStorage`, which stores the replication peer state with files on HFile file system, instead of znodes on ZooKeeper. And we have also implemented a tool to copy replication peer state across different replication peer storages."},"6280":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-75","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#migrate-across-different-replication-peer-storages","content":"To support doing the migrate online, we introduce a shell command called `peer_modification_switch`."},"6281":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-76","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#migrate-across-different-replication-peer-storages","content":"Use the above command can disable peer modification operations. The second `true` means you want to wait until all the existing replication peer modification procedures to finish before returning. After disabling the peer modification, it is safe for you to copy replication peer state with the above tool, and then update all the *hbase-site.xml* files in the cluster to specify the new replication peer storage, and finally trigger an online configuration update to load the new replication peer storage."},"6282":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-77","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication","content":"Note: this feature is introduced in HBase 2.1"},"6283":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-78","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication","content":"**Function of serial replication**\\\nSerial replication supports to push logs to the destination cluster in the same order as logs reach to the source cluster."},"6284":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-79","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"In replication of HBase, we push mutations to destination cluster by reading WAL in each region server. We have a queue for WAL files so we can read them in order of creation time. However, when region-move or RS failure occurs in source cluster, the hlog entries that are not pushed before region-move or RS-failure will be pushed by original RS(for region move) or another RS which takes over the remained hlog of dead RS(for RS failure), and the new entries for the same region(s) will be pushed by the RS which now serves the region(s), but they push the hlog entries of a same region concurrently without coordination."},"6285":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-80","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"This treatment can possibly lead to data inconsistency between source and destination clusters:"},"6286":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-81","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"there are put and then delete written to source cluster."},"6287":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-82","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"due to region-move / RS-failure, they are pushed by different replication-source threads to peer cluster."},"6288":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-83","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#why-need-serial-replication","content":"if delete is pushed to peer cluster before put, and flush and major-compact occurs in peer cluster before put is pushed to peer cluster, the delete is collected and the put remains in peer cluster, but in source cluster the put is masked by the delete, hence data inconsistency between source and destination clusters."},"6289":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-84","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"Set the serial flag to true for a repliation peer. And the default serial flag is false."},"6290":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-85","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"Add a new replication peer which serial flag is true"},"6291":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-86","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"Set a replication peer's serial flag to false"},"6292":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-87","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"Set a replication peer's serial flag to true"},"6293":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-88","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#serial-replication-configuration-1","content":"The serial replication feature had been done firstly in HBASE-9465 and then reverted and redone in HBASE-20046. You can find more details in these issues."},"6294":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-89","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#verifying-replicated-data","content":"The `VerifyReplication` MapReduce job, which is included in HBase, performs a systematic comparison of replicated data between two different clusters. Run the VerifyReplication job on the master cluster, supplying it with the peer ID and table name to use for validation. You can limit the verification further by specifying a time range or specific families. The job's short name is `verifyrep`. To run the job, use a command like the following:"},"6295":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-90","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#verifying-replicated-data","content":"The `VerifyReplication` command prints out `GOODROWS` and `BADROWS` counters to indicate rows that did and did not replicate correctly."},"6296":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-91","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#detailed-information-about-cluster-replication","content":"***Replication Architecture Overview***"},"6297":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-92","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"A single WAL edit goes through several steps in order to be replicated to a slave cluster."},"6298":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-93","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"An HBase client uses a Put or Delete operation to manipulate data in HBase."},"6299":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-94","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"The region server writes the request to the WAL in a way allows it to be replayed if it is not written successfully."},"6300":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-95","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"If the changed cell corresponds to a column family that is scoped for replication, the edit is added to the queue for replication."},"6301":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-96","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"In a separate thread, the edit is read from the log, as part of a batch process. Only the KeyValues that are eligible for replication are kept. Replicable KeyValues are part of a column family whose schema is scoped GLOBAL, are not part of a catalog such as `hbase:meta`, did not originate from the target slave cluster, and have not already been consumed by the target slave cluster."},"6302":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-97","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"The edit is tagged with the master's UUID and added to a buffer. When the buffer is filled, or the reader reaches the end of the file, the buffer is sent to a random region server on the slave cluster."},"6303":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-98","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"The region server reads the edits sequentially and separates them into buffers, one buffer per table. After all edits are read, each buffer is flushed using Table, HBase's normal client. The master's UUID and the UUIDs of slaves which have already consumed the data are preserved in the edits they are applied, in order to prevent replication loops."},"6304":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-99","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"In the master, the offset for the WAL that is currently being replicated is registered in ZooKeeper."},"6305":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-100","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"The first three steps, where the edit is inserted, are identical."},"6306":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-101","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Again in a separate thread, the region server reads, filters, and edits the log edits in the same way as above. The slave region server does not answer the RPC call."},"6307":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-102","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"The master sleeps and tries again a configurable number of times."},"6308":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-103","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"If the slave region server is still not available, the master selects a new subset of region server to replicate to, and tries again to send the buffer of edits."},"6309":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-104","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Meanwhile, the WALs are rolled and stored in a queue in ZooKeeper. Logs that are **archived** by their region server, by moving them from the region server's log directory to a central log directory, will update their paths in the in-memory queue of the replicating thread."},"6310":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-105","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"When the slave cluster is finally available, the buffer is applied in the same way as during normal processing. The master region server will then replicate the backlog of logs that accumulated during the outage."},"6311":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-106","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"**Spreading Queue Failover Load**\\\nWhen replication is active, a subset of region servers in the source cluster is responsible for shipping edits to the sink. This responsibility must be failed over like all other region server functions should a process or node crash. The following configuration settings are recommended for maintaining an even distribution of replication activity over the remaining live servers in the source cluster:"},"6312":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-107","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Set `replication.source.maxretriesmultiplier` to `300`."},"6313":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-108","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Set `replication.source.sleepforretries` to `1` (1 second). This value, combined with the value of `replication.source.maxretriesmultiplier`, causes the retry cycle to last about 5 minutes."},"6314":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-109","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"Set `replication.sleep.before.failover` to `30000` (30 seconds) in the source cluster site configuration."},"6315":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-110","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#life-of-a-wal-edit","content":"**Preserving Tags During Replication**\\\nBy default, the codec used for replication between clusters strips tags, such as cell-level ACLs, from cells. To prevent the tags from being stripped, you can use a different codec which does not strip them. Configure `hbase.replication.rpc.codec` to use `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.codec.KeyValueCodecWithTags`, on both the source and sink RegionServers involved in the replication. This option was introduced in HBASE-10322."},"6316":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-111","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**Replication State Storage**\\\nIn HBASE-15867, we abstract two interfaces for storing replication state, `ReplicationPeerStorage` and `ReplicationQueueStorage`. The former one is for storing the replication peer related states, and the latter one is for storing the replication queue related states. HBASE-15867 is only half done, as although we have abstract these two interfaces, we still only have zookeeper based implementations."},"6317":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-112","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"And in HBASE-27110, we have implemented a file system based replication peer storage, to store replication peer state on file system. Of course you can still use the zookeeper based replication peer storage."},"6318":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-113","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"And in HBASE-27109, we have changed the replication queue storage from zookeeper based to hbase table based. See the below `Replication Queue State` in hbase:replication table section for more details."},"6319":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-114","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**Replication State in ZooKeeper**\\&#xA;By default, the state is contained in the base node */hbase/replication*. Usually this nodes contains two child nodes, the peers znode is for storing replication peer state, and the rs znodes is for storing replication queue state. And if you choose the file system based replication peer storage, you will not see the peers znode. And starting from 3.0.0, we have moved the replication queue state to `hbase:replication` table (see below), so you will not see the rs znode."},"6320":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-115","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**The `Peers` Znode**\\\nThe `peers&#x60; znode is stored in */hbase/replication/peers* by default. It consists of a list of all peer replication clusters, along with the status of each of them. The value of each peer is its cluster key, which is provided in the HBase Shell. The cluster key contains a list of ZooKeeper nodes in the cluster's quorum, the client port for the ZooKeeper quorum, and the base znode for HBase in HDFS on that cluster. Starting from 3.0.0, you can also specify connection URI as a cluster key. See Connection URI for more details about connection URI."},"6321":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-116","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**The `RS` Znode**\\\nThe `rs` znode contains a list of WAL logs which need to be replicated. This list is divided into a set of queues organized by region server and the peer cluster the region server is shipping the logs to. The rs znode has one child znode for each region server in the cluster. The child znode name is the region server's hostname, client port, and start code. This list includes both live and dead region servers."},"6322":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-117","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**The hbase:replication Table**\\\nAfter 3.0.0, the `Queue` has been stored in the hbase:replication table, where the row key is `<PeerId>-<ServerName>[/<SourceServerName>]`, the WAL group will be the qualifier, and the serialized ReplicationGroupOffset will be the value. The ReplicationGroupOffset includes the wal file of the corresponding queue (`<PeerId>-<ServerName>[/<SourceServerName>]`) and its offset. Because we track replication offset per queue instead of per file, we only need to store one replication offset per queue."},"6323":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-118","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-internals","content":"**Other implementations for `ReplicationPeerStorage`**\\\nStarting from 2.6.0, we introduce a file system based `ReplicationPeerStorage`, which stores the replication peer state with files on HFile file system, instead of znodes on ZooKeeper. The layout is almost the same with znodes on zookeeper, the main difference is that, the HFile file system may not support atomic rename, so we use two files to store the state and when reading we will read them both and compare the timestamp to find out the newer one. So typically, you will see two peer config files. And for enable/disable state, we just touch a disabled file if the peer is disabled, and remove the file when enabling the peer."},"6324":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-119","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#choosing-region-servers-to-replicate-to","content":"When a master cluster region server initiates a replication source to a slave cluster, it first connects to the slave's ZooKeeper ensemble using the provided cluster key . It then scans the &#x2A;rs/* directory to discover all the available sinks (region servers that are accepting incoming streams of edits to replicate) and randomly chooses a subset of them using a configured ratio which has a default value of 10%. For example, if a slave cluster has 150 machines, 15 will be chosen as potential recipient for edits that this master cluster region server sends. Because this selection is performed by each master region server, the probability that all slave region servers are used is very high, and this method works for clusters of any size. For example, a master cluster of 10 machines replicating to a slave cluster of 5 machines with a ratio of 10% causes the master cluster region servers to choose one machine each at random."},"6325":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-120","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#choosing-region-servers-to-replicate-to","content":"A ZooKeeper watcher is placed on the `$zookeeper.znode.parent/rs` node of the slave cluster by each of the master cluster's region servers. This watch is used to monitor changes in the composition of the slave cluster. When nodes are removed from the slave cluster, or if nodes go down or come back up, the master cluster's region servers will respond by selecting a new pool of slave region servers to replicate to."},"6326":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-121","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-zookeeper","content":"Each master cluster region server has its own znode in the replication znodes hierarchy. It contains one znode per peer cluster (if 5 slave clusters, 5 znodes are created), and each of these contain a queue of WALs to process. Each of these queues will track the WALs created by that region server, but they can differ in size. For example, if one slave cluster becomes unavailable for some time, the WALs should not be deleted, so they need to stay in the queue while the others are processed. See rs.failover.details for an example."},"6327":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-122","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-zookeeper","content":"When a source is instantiated, it contains the current WAL that the region server is writing to. During log rolling, the new file is added to the queue of each slave cluster's znode just before it is made available. This ensures that all the sources are aware that a new log exists before the region server is able to append edits into it, but this operations is now more expensive. The queue items are discarded when the replication thread cannot read more entries from a file (because it reached the end of the last block) and there are other files in the queue. This means that if a source is up to date and replicates from the log that the region server writes to, reading up to the \"end\" of the current file will not delete the item in the queue."},"6328":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-123","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-zookeeper","content":"A log can be archived if it is no longer used or if the number of logs exceeds `hbase.regionserver.maxlogs` because the insertion rate is faster than regions are flushed. When a log is archived, the source threads are notified that the path for that log changed. If a particular source has already finished with an archived log, it will just ignore the message. If the log is in the queue, the path will be updated in memory. If the log is currently being replicated, the change will be done atomically so that the reader doesn't attempt to open the file when has already been moved. Because moving a file is a NameNode operation , if the reader is currently reading the log, it won't generate any exception."},"6329":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-124","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-hbase-table","content":"After 3.0.0, for table based implementation, we have server name in row key, which means we will have lots of rows for a given peer."},"6330":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-125","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-hbase-table","content":"For a normal replication queue, the WAL files belong to the region server that is still alive, all the WAL files are kept in memory, so we do not need to get the WAL files from replication queue storage. And for a recovered replication queue, we could get the WAL files of the dead region server by listing the old WAL directory on HDFS. So theoretically, we do not need to store every WAL file in replication queue storage. And what's more, we store the created time(usually) in the WAL file name, so for all the WAL files in a WAL group, we can sort them(actually we will sort them in the current replication framework), which means we only need to store one replication offset per queue. When starting a recovered replication queue, we will skip all the files before this offset, and start replicating from this offset."},"6331":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-126","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#keeping-track-of-logsbased-on-hbase-table","content":"For ReplicationLogCleaner, all the files before this offset can be deleted, otherwise not."},"6332":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-127","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#reading-filtering-and-sending-edits","content":"By default, a source attempts to read from a WAL and ship log entries to a sink as quickly as possible. Speed is limited by the filtering of log entries Only KeyValues that are scoped GLOBAL and that do not belong to catalog tables will be retained. Speed is also limited by total size of the list of edits to replicate per slave, which is limited to 64 MB by default. With this configuration, a master cluster region server with three slaves would use at most 192 MB to store data to replicate. This does not account for the data which was filtered but not garbage collected."},"6333":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-128","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#reading-filtering-and-sending-edits","content":"Once the maximum size of edits has been buffered or the reader reaches the end of the WAL, the source thread stops reading and chooses at random a sink to replicate to (from the list that was generated by keeping only a subset of slave region servers). It directly issues a RPC to the chosen region server and waits for the method to return. If the RPC was successful, the source determines whether the current file has been emptied or it contains more data which needs to be read. If the file has been emptied, the source deletes the znode in the queue. Otherwise, it registers the new offset in the log's znode. If the RPC threw an exception, the source will retry 10 times before trying to find a different sink."},"6334":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-129","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cleaning-logs","content":"If replication is not enabled, the master's log-cleaning thread deletes old logs using a configured TTL. This TTL-based method does not work well with replication, because archived logs which have exceeded their TTL may still be in a queue. The default behavior is augmented so that if a log is past its TTL, the cleaning thread looks up every queue until it finds the log, while caching queues it has found. If the log is not found in any queues, the log will be deleted. The next time the cleaning process needs to look for a log, it starts by using its cached list."},"6335":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-130","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#cleaning-logs","content":"WALs are saved when replication is enabled or disabled as long as peers exist."},"6336":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-131","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#region-server-failover","content":"When no region servers are failing, keeping track of the logs in ZooKeeper adds no value. Unfortunately, region servers do fail, and since ZooKeeper is highly available, it is useful for managing the transfer of the queues in the event of a failure. Each of the master cluster region servers keeps a watcher on every other region server, in order to be notified when one dies (just as the master does). When a failure happens, they all race to create a znode called `lock` inside the dead region server's znode that contains its queues. The region server that creates it successfully then transfers all the queues to its own znode, one at a time since ZooKeeper does not support renaming queues. After queues are all transferred, they are deleted from the old location. The znodes that were recovered are renamed with the ID of the slave cluster appended with the name of the dead server."},"6337":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-132","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#region-server-failover","content":"Next, the master cluster region server creates one new source thread per copied queue, and each of the source threads follows the read/filter/ship pattern. The main difference is that those queues will never receive new data, since they do not belong to their new region server. When the reader hits the end of the last log, the queue's znode is deleted and the master cluster region server closes that replication source."},"6338":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-133","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#region-server-failover","content":"And starting from 2.5.0, the failover logic has been moved to SCP, where we add a SERVER\\_CRASH\\_CLAIM\\_REPLICATION\\_QUEUES step in SCP to claim the replication queues for a dead server. And starting from 3.0.0, where we changed the replication queue storage from zookeeper to table, the update to the replication queue storage is async, so we also need an extra step to add the missing replication queues before claiming."},"6339":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-134","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claiming-based-on-zookeeper","content":"Given a master cluster with 3 region servers replicating to a single slave with id `2`, the following hierarchy represents what the znodes layout could be at some point in time. The region servers' znodes all contain a `peers` znode which contains a single queue. The znode names in the queues represent the actual file names on HDFS in the form `address,port.timestamp`."},"6340":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-135","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claiming-based-on-zookeeper","content":"Assume that 1.1.1.2 loses its ZooKeeper session. The survivors will race to create a lock, and, arbitrarily, 1.1.1.3 wins. It will then start transferring all the queues to its local peers znode by appending the name of the dead server. Right before 1.1.1.3 is able to clean up the old znodes, the layout will look like the following:"},"6341":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-136","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claiming-based-on-zookeeper","content":"Some time later, but before 1.1.1.3 is able to finish replicating the last WAL from 1.1.1.2, it dies too. Some new logs were also created in the normal queues. The last region server will then try to lock 1.1.1.3's znode and will begin transferring all the queues. The new layout will be:"},"6342":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-137","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claimingbased-on-hbase-table","content":"Given a master cluster with 3 region servers replicating to a single slave with id `2`, the following info represents what the storage layout of queue in the hbase:replication at some point in time. Row key is `<PeerId>-<ServerName>[/<SourceServerName>]`, and value is WAL && Offset."},"6343":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-138","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#the-replication-queue-claimingbased-on-hbase-table","content":"Assume that 1.1.1.2 failed. The survivors will claim queue of that, and, arbitrarily, 1.1.1.3 wins. It will claim all the queue of 1.1.1.2, including removing the row of a replication queue, and inserting a new row(where we change the server name to the region server which claims the queue). Finally, the layout will look like the following:"},"6344":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-139","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"The following metrics are exposed at the global region server level and at the peer level:"},"6345":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-140","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.sizeOfLogQueue`\\\nnumber of WALs to process (excludes the one which is being processed) at the Replication source"},"6346":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-141","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.shippedOps`\\\nnumber of mutations shipped"},"6347":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-142","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.logEditsRead`\\\nnumber of mutations read from WALs at the replication source"},"6348":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-143","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.ageOfLastShippedOp`\\\nage of last batch that was shipped by the replication source"},"6349":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-144","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.completedLogs`\\\nThe number of write-ahead-log files that have completed their acknowledged sending to the peer associated with this source. Increments to this metric are a part of normal operation of HBase replication."},"6350":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-145","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.completedRecoverQueues`\\\nThe number of recovery queues this source has completed sending to the associated peer. Increments to this metric are a part of normal recovery of HBase replication in the face of failed Region Servers."},"6351":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-146","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.uncleanlyClosedLogs`\\\nThe number of write-ahead-log files the replication system considered completed after reaching the end of readable entries in the face of an uncleanly closed file."},"6352":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-147","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.ignoredUncleanlyClosedLogContentsInBytes`\\\nWhen a write-ahead-log file is not closed cleanly, there will likely be some entry that has been partially serialized. This metric contains the number of bytes of such entries the HBase replication system believes were remaining at the end of files skipped in the face of an uncleanly closed file. Those bytes should either be in different file or represent a client write that was not acknowledged."},"6353":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-148","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.restartedLogReading`\\\nThe number of times the HBase replication system detected that it failed to correctly parse a cleanly closed write-ahead-log file. In this circumstance, the system replays the entire log from the beginning, ensuring that no edits fail to be acknowledged by the associated peer. Increments to this metric indicate that the HBase replication system is having difficulty correctly handling failures in the underlying distributed storage system. No dataloss should occur, but you should check Region Server log files for details of the failures."},"6354":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-149","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.repeatedLogFileBytes`\\\nWhen the HBase replication system determines that it needs to replay a given write-ahead-log file, this metric is incremented by the number of bytes the replication system believes had already been acknowledged by the associated peer prior to starting over."},"6355":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-150","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-metrics","content":"`source.closedLogsWithUnknownFileLength`\\\nIncremented when the HBase replication system believes it is at the end of a write-ahead-log file but it can not determine the length of that file in the underlying distributed storage system. Could indicate dataloss since the replication system is unable to determine if the end of readable entries lines up with the expected end of the file. You should check Region Server log files for details of the failures."},"6356":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-151","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-configuration-options","content":"Option"},"6357":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-152","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-configuration-options","content":"Description"},"6358":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-153","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-configuration-options","content":"Default"},"6359":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication-154","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-replication#replication-configuration-options","content":"zookeeper.znode.parent"},"6360":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/cluster-repli