aseslowlog","content":"Operator can use ColumnValueFilter to filter records based on region\\_name, username, client\\_address etc."},"6637":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-177","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#get-slowlarge-response-logs-from-system-table-hbaseslowlog","content":"Time range based queries will also be very useful. Example:"},"6638":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-178","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"Starting with HBase 0.98, the HBase Web UI includes the ability to monitor and report on the performance of the block cache. To view the block cache reports, see the Block Cache section of the region server UI. Following are a few examples of the reporting capabilities."},"6639":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-179","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"***Basic Info shows the cache implementation.***"},"6640":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-180","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"***Config shows all cache configuration options.***"},"6641":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-181","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"***Stats shows statistics about the performance of the cache.***"},"6642":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-182","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"***L1 and L2 show information about the L1 and L2 caches.***"},"6643":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-183","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#block-cache-monitoring","content":"This is not an exhaustive list of all the screens and reports available. Have a look in the Web UI."},"6644":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-184","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"Starting with HBase 0.95, Snapshot usage information on individual snapshots was shown in the HBase Master Web UI. This was further enhanced starting with HBase 1.3 to show the total Storefile size of the Snapshot Set. The following metrics are shown in the Master Web UI with HBase 1.3 and later."},"6645":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-185","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"Shared Storefile Size is the Storefile size shared between snapshots and active tables."},"6646":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-186","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"Mob Storefile Size is the Mob Storefile size shared between snapshots and active tables."},"6647":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-187","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"Archived Storefile Size is the Storefile size in Archive."},"6648":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-188","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"The format of Archived Storefile Size is NNN(MMM). NNN is the total Storefile size in Archive, MMM is the total Storefile size in Archive that is specific to the snapshot (not shared with other snapshots and tables)."},"6649":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-189","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"***Master Snapshot Overview***"},"6650":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-190","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"***Snapshot Storefile Stats Example 1***"},"6651":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-191","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"***Snapshot Storefile Stats Example 2***"},"6652":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-192","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"***Empty Snapshot Storfile Stats Example***"},"6653":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring-193","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/metrics-and-monitoring#snapshot-space-usage-monitoring","content":"The Metrics system was redone in HBase 0.96. See Migration to the New Metrics Hotness – Metrics2 by Elliot Clark for detail."},"6654":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"page","content":"Node Management","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node"},"6655":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node","content":"Managing HBase cluster nodes including decommissioning RegionServers, rolling restarts, and draining regions for maintenance."},"6656":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-management","content":"Node Management"},"6657":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"Node Decommission"},"6658":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#decommissioning-several-regions-servers-concurrently","content":"Decommissioning several Regions Servers concurrently"},"6659":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#bad-or-failing-disk","content":"Bad or Failing Disk"},"6660":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#rolling-restart","content":"Rolling Restart"},"6661":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"Using the `rolling-restart.sh` Script"},"6662":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#manual-rolling-restart","content":"Manual Rolling Restart"},"6663":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Logic for Crafting Your Own Rolling Restart Script"},"6664":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/node#adding-a-new-node","content":"Adding a New Node"},"6665":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"You can stop an individual RegionServer by running the following script in the HBase directory on the particular node:"},"6666":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"The RegionServer will first close all regions and then shut itself down. On shutdown, the RegionServer's ephemeral node in ZooKeeper will expire. The master will notice the RegionServer gone and will treat it as a 'crashed' server; it will reassign the nodes the RegionServer was carrying."},"6667":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"If the load balancer runs while a node is shutting down, then there could be contention between\nthe Load Balancer and the Master's recovery of the just decommissioned RegionServer. Avoid any\nproblems by disabling the balancer first. See the \"Load Balancer\" info section below."},"6668":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"In hbase-2.0, in the bin directory, we added a script named *considerAsDead.sh* that can be used\nto kill a regionserver. Hardware issues could be detected by specialized monitoring tools before\nthe zookeeper timeout has expired. *considerAsDead.sh* is a simple function to mark a RegionServer\nas dead. It deletes all the znodes of the server, starting the recovery process. Plug in the\nscript into your monitoring/fault detection tools to initiate faster failover. Be careful how you\nuse this disruptive tool. Copy the script if you need to make use of it in a version of hbase\nprevious to hbase-2.0."},"6669":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"A downside to the above stop of a RegionServer is that regions could be offline for a good period of time. Regions are closed in order. If many regions on the server, the first region to close may not be back online until all regions close and after the master notices the RegionServer's znode gone. A node can be asked to gradually shed its load and then shutdown itself using the *graceful\\_stop.sh* script. Here is its usage:"},"6670":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"To decommission a loaded RegionServer, run the following: \\$ ./bin/graceful\\_stop.sh HOSTNAME where `HOSTNAME` is the host carrying the RegionServer you would decommission."},"6671":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-16","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"The `HOSTNAME` passed to *graceful\\_stop.sh* must match the hostname that hbase is using to\nidentify RegionServers. HBase uses fully-qualified domain names usually. Check the list of\nRegionServers in the master UI for how HBase is referring to servers. Whatever HBase is using,\nthis is what you should pass the *graceful\\_stop.sh* decommission script. If you pass IPs, the\nscript is not yet smart enough to make a hostname (or FQDN) of it and so it will fail when it\nchecks if server is currently running; the graceful unloading of regions will not run."},"6672":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-17","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"The *graceful\\_stop.sh* script will move the regions off the decommissioned RegionServer one at a time to minimize region churn. It will verify the region deployed in the new location before it will moves the next region and so on until the decommissioned server is carrying zero regions. At this point, the *graceful\\_stop.sh* tells the RegionServer `stop`. The master will at this point notice the RegionServer gone but all regions will have already been redeployed and because the RegionServer went down cleanly, there will be no WAL logs to split."},"6673":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-18","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"It is assumed that the Region Load Balancer is disabled while the `graceful_stop` script runs (otherwise the balancer and the decommission script will end up fighting over region deployments). Use the shell to disable the balancer:"},"6674":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-19","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"This turns the balancer OFF. To reenable, do:"},"6675":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-20","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#node-decommission","content":"The `graceful_stop` will check the balancer and if enabled, will turn it off before it goes to work. If it exits prematurely because of error, it will not have reset the balancer. Hence, it is better to manage the balancer apart from `graceful_stop` reenabling it after you are done w/ graceful\\_stop."},"6676":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-21","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#decommissioning-several-regions-servers-concurrently","content":"If you have a large cluster, you may want to decommission more than one machine at a time by gracefully stopping multiple RegionServers concurrently. To gracefully drain multiple regionservers at the same time, RegionServers can be put into a \"draining\" state. This is done by marking a RegionServer as a draining node by creating an entry in ZooKeeper under the *hbase\\_root/draining* znode. This znode has format `name,port,startcode` just like the regionserver entries under *hbase\\_root/rs* znode."},"6677":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-22","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#decommissioning-several-regions-servers-concurrently","content":"Without this facility, decommissioning multiple nodes may be non-optimal because regions that are being drained from one region server may be moved to other regionservers that are also draining. Marking RegionServers to be in the draining state prevents this from happening. See this blog post for more details."},"6678":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-23","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#bad-or-failing-disk","content":"It is good having dfs.datanode.failed.volumes.tolerated set if you have a decent number of disks per machine for the case where a disk plain dies. But usually disks do the \"John Wayne\" — i.e. take a while to go down spewing errors in *dmesg* — or for some reason, run much slower than their companions. In this case you want to decommission the disk. You have two options. You can decommission the datanode or, less disruptive in that only the bad disks data will be rereplicated, can stop the datanode, unmount the bad volume (You can't umount a volume while the datanode is using it), and then restart the datanode (presuming you have set dfs.datanode.failed.volumes.tolerated > 0). The regionserver will throw some errors in its logs as it recalibrates where to get its data from — it will likely roll its WAL log too — but in general but for some latency spikes, it should keep on chugging."},"6679":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-24","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#bad-or-failing-disk","content":"If you are doing short-circuit reads, you will have to move the regions off the regionserver\nbefore you stop the datanode; when short-circuiting reading, though chmod'd so regionserver cannot\nhave access, because it already has the files open, it will be able to keep reading the file\nblocks from the bad disk even though the datanode is down. Move the regions back after you restart\nthe datanode."},"6680":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-25","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#rolling-restart","content":"Some cluster configuration changes require either the entire cluster, or the RegionServers, to be restarted in order to pick up the changes. In addition, rolling restarts are supported for upgrading to a minor or maintenance release, and to a major release if at all possible. See the release notes for release you want to upgrade to, to find out about limitations to the ability to perform a rolling upgrade."},"6681":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-26","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#rolling-restart","content":"There are multiple ways to restart your cluster nodes, depending on your situation. These methods are detailed below."},"6682":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-27","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"HBase ships with a script, *bin/rolling-restart.sh*, that allows you to perform rolling restarts on the entire cluster, the master only, or the RegionServers only. The script is provided as a template for your own script, and is not explicitly tested. It requires password-less SSH login to be configured and assumes that you have deployed using a tarball. The script requires you to set some environment variables before running it. Examine the script and modify it to suit your needs."},"6683":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-28","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"***rolling-restart.sh* General Usage**"},"6684":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-29","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"**Rolling Restart on RegionServers Only**\\\nTo perform a rolling restart on the RegionServers only, use the `--rs-only` option. This might be necessary if you need to reboot the individual RegionServer or if you make a configuration change that only affects RegionServers and not the other HBase processes."},"6685":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-30","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"**Rolling Restart on Masters Only**\\\nTo perform a rolling restart on the active and backup Masters, use the `--master-only` option. You might use this if you know that your configuration change only affects the Master and not the RegionServers, or if you need to restart the server where the active Master is running."},"6686":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-31","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"**Graceful Restart**\\\nIf you specify the `--graceful` option, RegionServers are restarted using the *bin/graceful\\_stop.sh* script, which moves regions off a RegionServer before restarting it. This is safer, but can delay the restart."},"6687":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-32","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#using-the-rolling-restartsh-script","content":"Limiting the Number of Threads\\\nTo limit the rolling restart to using only a specific number of threads, use the `--maxthreads` option."},"6688":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-33","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#manual-rolling-restart","content":"To retain more control over the process, you may wish to manually do a rolling restart across your cluster. This uses the `graceful-stop.sh` command decommission. In this method, you can restart each RegionServer individually and then move its old regions back into place, retaining locality. If you also need to restart the Master, you need to do it separately, and restart the Master before restarting the RegionServers using this method. The following is an example of such a command. You may need to tailor it to your environment. This script does a rolling restart of RegionServers only. It disables the load balancer before moving the regions."},"6689":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-34","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#manual-rolling-restart","content":"Monitor the output of the */tmp/log.txt* file to follow the progress of the script."},"6690":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-35","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Use the following guidelines if you want to create your own rolling restart script."},"6691":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-36","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Extract the new release, verify its configuration, and synchronize it to all nodes of your cluster using `rsync`, `scp`, or another secure synchronization mechanism."},"6692":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-37","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Restart the master first. You may need to modify these commands if your new HBase directory is different from the old one, such as for an upgrade."},"6693":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-38","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Gracefully restart each RegionServer, using a script such as the following, from the Master."},"6694":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-39","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"If you are running Thrift or REST servers, pass the --thrift or --rest options. For other available options, run the `bin/graceful-stop.sh --help` command."},"6695":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-40","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"It is important to drain HBase regions slowly when restarting multiple RegionServers. Otherwise, multiple regions go offline simultaneously and must be reassigned to other nodes, which may also go offline soon. This can negatively affect performance. You can inject delays into the script above, for instance, by adding a Shell command such as `sleep`. To wait for 5 minutes between each RegionServer restart, modify the above script to the following:"},"6696":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-41","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#logic-for-crafting-your-own-rolling-restart-script","content":"Restart the Master again, to clear out the dead servers list and re-enable the load balancer."},"6697":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#adding-a-new-node","content":"Adding a new regionserver in HBase is essentially free, you simply start it like this: `$ ./bin/hbase-daemon.sh start regionserver` and it will register itself with the master. Ideally you also started a DataNode on the same machine so that the RS can eventually start to have local files. If you rely on ssh to start your daemons, don't forget to add the new hostname in *conf/regionservers* on the master."},"6698":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#adding-a-new-node","content":"At this point the region server isn't serving data because no regions have moved to it yet. If the balancer is enabled, it will start moving regions to the new RS. On a small/medium cluster this can have a very adverse effect on latency as a lot of regions will be offline at the same time. It is thus recommended to disable the balancer the same way it's done when decommissioning a node and move the regions manually (or even better, using a script that moves them one by one)."},"6699":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/node-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/node","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/node#adding-a-new-node","content":"The moved regions will all have 0% locality and won't have any blocks in cache so the region server will have to use the network to serve requests. Apart from resulting in higher latency, it may also be able to use all of your network card's capacity. For practical purposes, consider that a standard 1GigE NIC won't be able to read much more than *100MB/s*. In this case, or if you are in a OLAP environment and require having locality, then it is recommended to major compact the moved regions."},"6700":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"page","content":"Region & Capacity Management","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity"},"6701":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","content":"Managing HBase regions including compaction, splits, merges, capacity planning, RegionServer grouping, and region normalization."},"6702":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-management","content":"Region Management"},"6703":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#major-compaction","content":"Major Compaction"},"6704":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#merge","content":"Merge"},"6705":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#capacity-planning-and-region-sizing","content":"Capacity Planning and Region Sizing"},"6706":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#node-count-and-hardwarevm-configuration","content":"Node count and hardware/VM configuration"},"6707":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Physical data size"},"6708":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#readwrite-throughput","content":"Read/Write throughput"},"6709":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#jvm-gc-limitations","content":"JVM GC limitations"},"6710":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#determining-region-count-and-size","content":"Determining region count and size"},"6711":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#viewing-the-current-number-of-regions","content":"Viewing the Current Number of Regions"},"6712":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"Number of regions per RS - upper bound"},"6713":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---lower-bound","content":"Number of regions per RS - lower bound"},"6714":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#maximum-region-size","content":"Maximum region size"},"6715":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#total-data-size-per-region-server","content":"Total data size per region server"},"6716":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#initial-configuration-and-tuning","content":"Initial configuration and tuning"},"6717":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-16","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#compactions","content":"Compactions"},"6718":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-17","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#pre-splitting-the-table","content":"Pre-splitting the table"},"6719":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-18","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"RegionServer Grouping"},"6720":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-19","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Regex-Based Membership"},"6721":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-20","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#best-practice","content":"Best Practice"},"6722":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-21","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#isolate-system-tables","content":"Isolate System Tables"},"6723":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-22","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#dead-nodes","content":"Dead Nodes"},"6724":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-23","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-and-capacity-troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"6725":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-24","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Remove RegionServer Grouping"},"6726":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-25","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#acl","content":"ACL"},"6727":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-26","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"Migrating From Old Implementation"},"6728":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-27","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"Region Normalizer"},"6729":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-28","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#auto-region-reopen","content":"Auto Region Reopen"},"6730":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-29","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#major-compaction","content":"Major compactions can be requested via the HBase shell or Admin.majorCompact."},"6731":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-30","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#major-compaction","content":"Note: major compactions do NOT do region merges. See compaction for more information about compactions."},"6732":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-31","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#merge","content":"Merge is a utility that can merge adjoining regions in the same table (see org.apache.hadoop.hbase.util.Merge)."},"6733":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-32","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#merge","content":"If you feel you have too many regions and want to consolidate them, Merge is the utility you need. Merge must run be done when the cluster is down. See the O'Reilly HBase Book for an example of usage."},"6734":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-33","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#merge","content":"You will need to pass 3 parameters to this application. The first one is the table name. The second one is the fully qualified name of the first region to merge, like \"table\\_name,\\x0A,1342956111995.7cef47f192318ba7ccc75b1bbf27a82b.\". The third one is the fully qualified name for the second region to merge."},"6735":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-34","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#merge","content":"Additionally, there is a Ruby script attached to HBASE-1621 for region merging."},"6736":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-35","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#capacity-planning-and-region-sizing","content":"There are several considerations when planning the capacity for an HBase cluster and performing the initial configuration. Start with a solid understanding of how HBase handles data internally."},"6737":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-36","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Physical data size on disk is distinct from logical size of your data and is affected by the following:"},"6738":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-37","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Increased by HBase overhead"},"6739":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-38","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"See keyvalue and keysize. At least 24 bytes per key-value (cell), can be more. Small keys/values means more relative overhead."},"6740":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-39","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"KeyValue instances are aggregated into blocks, which are indexed. Indexes also have to be stored. Blocksize is configurable on a per-ColumnFamily basis. See regions.arch."},"6741":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-40","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Decreased by compression and data block encoding, depending on data. You might want to test what compression and encoding (if any) make sense for your data."},"6742":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-41","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Increased by size of region server \\[wal]/docs/architecture/regionserver#write-ahead-log-wal (usually fixed and negligible - less than half of RS memory size, per RS)."},"6743":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Increased by HDFS replication - usually x3."},"6744":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#physical-data-size","content":"Aside from the disk space necessary to store the data, one RS may not be able to serve arbitrarily large amounts of data due to some practical limits on region count and size (see ops.capacity.regions)."},"6745":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#readwrite-throughput","content":"Number of nodes can also be driven by required throughput for reads and/or writes. The throughput one can get per node depends a lot on data (esp. key/value sizes) and request patterns, as well as node and system configuration. Planning should be done for peak load if it is likely that the load would be the main driver of the increase of the node count. PerformanceEvaluation and ycsb tools can be used to test single node or a test cluster."},"6746":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-45","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#readwrite-throughput","content":"For write, usually 5-15Mb/s per RS can be expected, since every region server has only one active WAL. There's no good estimate for reads, as it depends vastly on data, requests, and cache hit rate. perf.casestudy might be helpful."},"6747":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-46","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#jvm-gc-limitations","content":"RS cannot currently utilize very large heap due to cost of GC. There's also no good way of running multiple RS-es per server (other than running several VMs per machine). Thus, \\~20-24Gb or less memory dedicated to one RS is recommended. GC tuning is required for large heap sizes. See gcpause, trouble.log.gc and elsewhere (TODO: where?)"},"6748":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-47","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#determining-region-count-and-size","content":"Generally less regions makes for a smoother running cluster (you can always manually split the big regions later (if necessary) to spread the data, or request load, over the cluster); 20-200 regions per RS is a reasonable range. The number of regions cannot be configured directly (unless you go for fully disable.splitting); adjust the region size to achieve the target region size given table size."},"6749":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-48","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#determining-region-count-and-size","content":"When configuring regions for multiple tables, note that most region settings can be set on a per-table basis via TableDescriptorBuilder, as well as shell commands. These settings will override the ones in `hbase-site.xml`. That is useful if your tables have different workloads/use cases."},"6750":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-49","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#determining-region-count-and-size","content":"Also note that in the discussion of region sizes here, &#x2A;HDFS replication factor is not (and should not be) taken into account, whereas other factors ops.capacity.nodes.datasize should be.* So, if your data is compressed and replicated 3 ways by HDFS, \"9 Gb region\" means 9 Gb of compressed data. HDFS replication factor only affects your disk usage and is invisible to most HBase code."},"6751":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-50","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#viewing-the-current-number-of-regions","content":"You can view the current number of regions for a given table using the HMaster UI. In the **Tables** section, the number of online regions for each table is listed in the **Online Regions** column. This total only includes the in-memory state and does not include disabled or offline regions."},"6752":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-51","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"In production scenarios, where you have a lot of data, you are normally concerned with the maximum number of regions you can have per server. too many regions has technical discussion on the subject. Basically, the maximum number of regions is mostly determined by memstore memory usage. Each region has its own memstores; these grow up to a configurable size; usually in 128-256 MB range, see hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size. One memstore exists per column family (so there's only one per region if there's one CF in the table). The RS dedicates some fraction of total memory to its memstores (see hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size). If this memory is exceeded (too much memstore usage), it can cause undesirable consequences such as unresponsive server or compaction storms. A good starting point for the number of regions per RS (assuming one table) is:"},"6753":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-52","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"This formula is pseudo-code. Here are two formulas using the actual tunable parameters, first for HBase 0.98+ and second for HBase 0.94.x."},"6754":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-53","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"**HBase 0.98.x**"},"6755":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-54","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"**HBase 0.94.x**"},"6756":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-55","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"If a given RegionServer has 16 GB of RAM, with default settings, the formula works out to 16384\\*0.4/128 \\~ 51 regions per RS is a starting point. The formula can be extended to multiple tables; if they all have the same configuration, just use the total number of families."},"6757":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-56","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"This number can be adjusted; the formula above assumes all your regions are filled at approximately the same rate. If only a fraction of your regions are going to be actively written to, you can divide the result by that fraction to get a larger region count. Then, even if all regions are written to, all region memstores are not filled evenly, and eventually jitter appears even if they are (due to limited number of concurrent flushes). Thus, one can have as many as 2-3 times more regions than the starting point; however, increased numbers carry increased risk."},"6758":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-57","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---upper-bound","content":"For write-heavy workload, memstore fraction can be increased in configuration at the expense of block cache; this will also allow one to have more regions."},"6759":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-58","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---lower-bound","content":"HBase scales by having regions across many servers. Thus if you have 2 regions for 16GB data, on a 20 node machine your data will be concentrated on just a few machines - nearly the entire cluster will be idle. This really can't be stressed enough, since a common problem is loading 200MB data into HBase and then wondering why your awesome 10 node cluster isn't doing anything."},"6760":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-59","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#number-of-regions-per-rs---lower-bound","content":"On the other hand, if you have a very large amount of data, you may also want to go for a larger number of regions to avoid having regions that are too large."},"6761":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-60","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#maximum-region-size","content":"For large tables in production scenarios, maximum region size is mostly limited by compactions - very large compactions, esp. major, can degrade cluster performance. Currently, the recommended maximum region size is 10-20Gb, and 5-10Gb is optimal. For older 0.90.x codebase, the upper-bound of regionsize is about 4Gb, with a default of 256Mb."},"6762":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-61","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#maximum-region-size","content":"The size at which the region is split into two is generally configured via hbase.hregion.max.filesize; for details, see arch.region.splits."},"6763":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-62","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#maximum-region-size","content":"If you cannot estimate the size of your tables well, when starting off, it's probably best to stick to the default region size, perhaps going smaller for hot tables (or manually split hot regions to spread the load over the cluster), or go with larger region sizes if your cell sizes tend to be largish (100k and up)."},"6764":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-63","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#maximum-region-size","content":"In HBase 0.98, experimental stripe compactions feature was added that would allow for larger regions, especially for log data. See ops.stripe."},"6765":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-64","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#total-data-size-per-region-server","content":"According to above numbers for region size and number of regions per region server, in an optimistic estimate 10 GB x 100 regions per RS will give up to 1TB served per region server, which is in line with some of the reported multi-PB use cases. However, it is important to think about the data vs cache size ratio at the RS level. With 1TB of data per server and 10 GB block cache, only 1% of the data will be cached, which may barely cover all block indices."},"6766":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-65","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#initial-configuration-and-tuning","content":"First, see important configurations. Note that some configurations, more than others, depend on specific scenarios. Pay special attention to:"},"6767":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-66","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#initial-configuration-and-tuning","content":"hbase.regionserver.handler.count - request handler thread count, vital for high-throughput workloads."},"6768":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-67","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#initial-configuration-and-tuning","content":"config.wals - the blocking number of WAL files depends on your memstore configuration and should be set accordingly to prevent potential blocking when doing high volume of writes."},"6769":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-68","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#initial-configuration-and-tuning","content":"Then, there are some considerations when setting up your cluster and tables."},"6770":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-69","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#compactions","content":"Depending on read/write volume and latency requirements, optimal compaction settings may be different. See compaction for some details."},"6771":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-70","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#compactions","content":"When provisioning for large data sizes, however, it's good to keep in mind that compactions can affect write throughput. Thus, for write-intensive workloads, you may opt for less frequent compactions and more store files per regions. Minimum number of files for compactions (`hbase.hstore.compaction.min`) can be set to higher value; hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles should also be increased, as more files might accumulate in such case. You may also consider manually managing compactions: managed.compactions"},"6772":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-71","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#pre-splitting-the-table","content":"Based on the target number of the regions per RS (see ops.capacity.regions.count) and number of RSes, one can pre-split the table at creation time. This would both avoid some costly splitting as the table starts to fill up, and ensure that the table starts out already distributed across many servers."},"6773":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-72","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#pre-splitting-the-table","content":"If the table is expected to grow large enough to justify that, at least one region per RS should be created. It is not recommended to split immediately into the full target number of regions (e.g. 50 \\* number of RSes), but a low intermediate value can be chosen. For multiple tables, it is recommended to be conservative with presplitting (e.g. pre-split 1 region per RS at most), especially if you don't know how much each table will grow. If you split too much, you may end up with too many regions, with some tables having too many small regions."},"6774":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-73","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#pre-splitting-the-table","content":"For pre-splitting howto, see manual region splitting decisions and precreate.regions."},"6775":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-74","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"RegionServer Grouping (A.K.A `rsgroup`) is an advanced feature for partitioning regionservers into distinctive groups for strict isolation. It should only be used by users who are sophisticated enough to understand the full implications and have a sufficient background in managing HBase clusters. It was developed by Yahoo! and they run it at scale on their large grid cluster. See HBase at Yahoo! Scale."},"6776":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-75","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"RSGroups can be defined and managed with both admin methods and shell commands. A server can be added to a group with hostname and port pair and tables can be moved to this group so that only regionservers in the same rsgroup can host the regions of the table. The group for a table is stored in its TableDescriptor, the property name is `hbase.rsgroup.name`. You can also set this property on a namespace, so it will cause all the tables under this namespace to be placed into this group. RegionServers and tables can only belong to one rsgroup at a time. By default, all tables and regionservers belong to the `default` rsgroup. System tables can also be put into a rsgroup using the regular APIs. A custom balancer implementation tracks assignments per rsgroup and makes sure to move regions to the relevant regionservers in that rsgroup. The rsgroup information is stored in a regular HBase table, and a zookeeper-based read-only cache is used at cluster bootstrap time."},"6777":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-76","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"To enable, add the following to your hbase-site.xml and restart your Master:"},"6778":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-77","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"Then use the admin/shell *rsgroup* methods/commands to create and manipulate RegionServer groups: e.g. to add a rsgroup and then add a server to it. To see the list of rsgroup commands available in the hbase shell type:"},"6779":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-78","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"High level, you create a rsgroup that is other than the `default` group using *add\\_rsgroup* command. You then add servers and tables to this group with the *move\\_servers\\_rsgroup* and *move\\_tables\\_rsgroup* commands. If necessary, run a balance for the group if tables are slow to migrate to the groups dedicated server with the *balance\\_rsgroup* command (Usually this is not needed). To monitor effect of the commands, see the `Tables` tab toward the end of the Master UI home page. If you click on a table, you can see what servers it is deployed across. You should see here a reflection of the grouping done with your shell commands. View the master log if issues."},"6780":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-79","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"Here is example using a few of the rsgroup commands. To add a group, do as follows:"},"6781":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-80","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"If you have not enabled the rsgroup feature and you call any of the rsgroup admin methods or shell\ncommands the call will fail with a `DoNotRetryIOException` with a detail message that says the\nrsgroup feature is disabled."},"6782":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-81","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"Add a server (specified by hostname + port) to the just-made group using the *move\\_servers\\_rsgroup* command as follows:"},"6783":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-82","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"The rsgroup feature refers to servers in a cluster with hostname and port only. It does not make\nuse of the HBase ServerName type identifying RegionServers; i.e. hostname + port + starttime to\ndistinguish RegionServer instances. The rsgroup feature keeps working across RegionServer restarts\nso the starttime of ServerName — and hence the ServerName type — is not appropriate.\nAdministration"},"6784":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-83","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"Servers come and go over the lifetime of a Cluster. Currently, you must manually align the servers referenced in rsgroups with the actual state of nodes in the running cluster. What we mean by this is that if you decommission a server, then you must update rsgroups as part of your server decommission process removing references. Notice that, by calling `clearDeadServers` manually will also remove the dead servers from any rsgroups, but the problem is that we will lost track of the dead servers after master restarts, which means you still need to update the rsgroup by your own."},"6785":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-84","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"Please use `Admin.removeServersFromRSGroup` or shell command *remove\\_servers\\_rsgroup* to remove decommission servers from rsgroup."},"6786":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-85","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regionserver-grouping","content":"The `default` group, and every regex-governed group (see Regex-Based Membership), are dynamic. Their server lists mirror the current state of the cluster; i.e. if you shutdown a server that was part of one of them, and then do a *get\\_rsgroup* on it to list its content in the shell, the server will no longer be listed. For other non-default rsgroups, though a node may be offline, it will persist in the group's list of servers. But if you move the offline server from the non-default rsgroup to default, it will not show in the `default` list. It will just be dropped."},"6787":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-86","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Instead of moving each server into a group by hand, a non-default rsgroup can be made *regex-governed*: regionservers whose hostname matches a regular expression are placed in the group automatically as they register, and removed again when they go away. Set one property per group, where the suffix is the name of an existing rsgroup:"},"6788":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-87","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"The regex is matched against the server's hostname only (not the port), and must match the whole hostname."},"6789":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-88","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Regexes are evaluated by the master on every regionserver join and leave, with no timeout. Like the rest of `hbase-site.xml`, they are trusted configuration, so keep them simple: a pattern prone to catastrophic backtracking (for example nested quantifiers such as `(a+)+`) can stall regionserver registration and expiry."},"6790":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-89","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"The group must already exist (`add_rsgroup`); the `default` group cannot be regex-governed."},"6791":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-90","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"A hostname that matches the regexes of more than one group is treated as unmatched and stays in `default` until the overlap is fixed. Invalid regexes and invalid group names are ignored with a warning in the master log."},"6792":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-91","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Membership of a regex-governed group follows the live servers and is recomputed whenever a server joins or leaves, or when the HMaster restarts, like the `default` group. It is never stored in `hbase:rsgroup` or ZooKeeper: a regex-governed group is persisted with no servers, so a server event costs no extra I/O."},"6793":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-92","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"The regex always wins. A manual move that contradicts a regex is rejected, because the next server event or master restart would put the server back where its hostname says: a server matching a regex can only be moved to its regex-governed group, and a server that does not match cannot be moved into one. A regex-governed group cannot be removed while it has servers in the master's in-memory view. Remove the `hbase.rsgroup.regex.<group>` property from the configuration of all masters, including backup masters, and restart all HMasters. The group is then an ordinary admin-managed group with no servers, and its former servers go to `default`. This holds only if you did not reload the configuration first and a group change stored the servers in the meantime; if you did, move the servers out of the group by hand. Then remove the group. Removal also fails while a table or a namespace refers to the group."},"6794":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-93","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Set the same `hbase.rsgroup.regex.*` properties on every master, including backup masters. Membership is computed from the active master's configuration, so a master that takes over with different properties would regroup the servers."},"6795":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-94","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"To add, change or remove a regex, change the configuration of all masters and restart the HMasters. Do not reload the configuration; reload is not recommended for these properties. A restart recomputes the membership of every regex-governed group at once. A reload recomputes nothing: servers move only at the next server event, and the in-memory membership of a group stays as it was until then. Group operations do not recompute it either: `add_rsgroup` and a group rename do not apply a regex, so a regex for a group that does not exist yet is ignored until a later server event or restart."},"6796":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-95","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Adding or changing a regex for an existing group removes the servers that the regex does not match, but not at once. They leave the group at the next server event or master restart, and tables bound to the group then have fewer servers. Before you add or change a regex for an existing group, check the group's current servers against the regex."},"6797":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-96","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"**Removing a regex, or breaking it (typo, invalid regex), turns the group back into an ordinary\nadmin-managed group.** Its servers are not stored, so the group has none after a master restart;\nuntil then, servers it already holds stay in it, and a server that joins later is placed in\n`default`. Restart all masters, including backup masters, to settle it. A configuration reload is\nnot enough: it leaves the in-memory servers in the group, and a later change to a group stores\nthem as admin-managed members. Tables bound to the group may then have no regionserver of their\nown (see `hbase.rsgroup.fallback.enable` below). Invalid regexes and a regex-governed group with\nno online servers are both logged at WARN in the master log."},"6798":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-97","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Set `hbase.rsgroup.fallback.enable` to `true` whenever you use regex-based membership, unless you\nhave a very good reason not to. Membership follows the live servers, so a regex-governed group (or\n`default`) can lose all of its servers when pods go down, when a regex is mistyped or removed, or\nwhen the regexes cover every regionserver. Without fallback, regions of tables bound to that group\nstay unassigned until a matching server returns."},"6799":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-98","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"When you enable a regex for servers that were previously placed in groups by hand, no manual cleanup is needed: after the HMaster restart, the regex places each matching server in its group at once (a reload would do so only at the next server event), any stale copy stored for it is cleared on the next flush, and a server stored in a regex-governed group that no longer matches its regex returns to `default`. The master logs a WARN naming the group the stale copy was in."},"6800":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-99","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#regex-based-membership","content":"Regex placement is always applied, even if it leaves the `default` group without any online servers (for example, when the regexes cover every regionserver). The master logs a warning in that case. Tables in `default`, including system tables unless they live in another rsgroup, then have no regionserver of their own, so set `hbase.rsgroup.fallback.enable` to `true` to let their regions fall back to the servers of other groups. Without it, those regions stay unassigned until a server that matches no regex, and is in no other group, registers."},"6801":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-100","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#best-practice","content":"The authors of the rsgroup feature, the Yahoo! HBase Engineering team, have been running it on their grid for a good while now and have come up with a few best practices informed by their experience."},"6802":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-101","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#isolate-system-tables","content":"Either have a system rsgroup where all the system tables are or just leave the system tables in `default` rsgroup and have all user-space tables are in non-default rsgroups."},"6803":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-102","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#dead-nodes","content":"Yahoo! Have found it useful at their scale to keep a special rsgroup of dead or questionable nodes; this is one means of keeping them out of the running until repair."},"6804":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-103","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#dead-nodes","content":"Be careful replacing dead nodes in an rsgroup. Ensure there are enough live nodes before you start moving out the dead. Move in good live nodes first if you have to."},"6805":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-104","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#dead-nodes","content":"Servers that match a `hbase.rsgroup.regex.<group>` entry cannot be moved into a dead-nodes group; the move is rejected because the regex decides their group. To park such a server, change the regex (for example, exclude it by hostname) and restart the HMaster to apply the change. A configuration reload is not recommended."},"6806":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-105","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-and-capacity-troubleshooting","content":"Viewing the Master log will give you insight on rsgroup operation."},"6807":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-106","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-and-capacity-troubleshooting","content":"If it appears stuck, restart the Master process."},"6808":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-107","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Simply disable RegionServer Grouping feature is easy, just remove the 'hbase.balancer.rsgroup.enabled' from hbase-site.xml or explicitly set it to false in hbase-site.xml."},"6809":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-108","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"But if you change the 'hbase.balancer.rsgroup.enabled' to true, the old rsgroup configs will take effect again. So if you want to completely remove the RegionServer Grouping feature from a cluster, so that if the feature is re-enabled in the future, the old meta data will not affect the functioning of the cluster, there are more steps to do."},"6810":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-109","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Move all tables in non-default rsgroups to `default` regionserver group"},"6811":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-110","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Remove every `hbase.rsgroup.regex.*` property from the configuration on **all masters**, and restart all HMasters, including backup masters. Do not only reload the configuration. Moves of servers that match a regex are rejected while the property is set. After the restart, the regex-governed groups have no servers and their former servers are in `default`."},"6812":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-111","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Move all regionservers in non-default rsgroups to `default` regionserver group"},"6813":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-112","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Remove all non-default rsgroups. `default` rsgroup created implicitly doesn't have to be removed"},"6814":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-113","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Remove the changes made in `hbase-site.xml` and restart the cluster"},"6815":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-114","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Drop the table `hbase:rsgroup` from `hbase`"},"6816":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-115","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#remove-regionserver-grouping","content":"Remove znode `rsgroup` from the cluster ZooKeeper using zkCli.sh"},"6817":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-116","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#acl","content":"To enable ACL, add the following to your hbase-site.xml and restart your Master:"},"6818":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-117","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"The coprocessor `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.rsgroup.RSGroupAdminEndpoint` is deprected, but for compatible, if you want the pre 3.0.0 hbase client/shell to communicate with the new hbase cluster, you still need to add this coprocessor to master."},"6819":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-118","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"The `hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class` config has been deprecated, as now the top level load balancer will always be `RSGroupBasedLoadBalaner`, and the `hbase.master.loadbalancer.class` config is for configuring the balancer within a group. This also means you should not set `hbase.master.loadbalancer.class` to `RSGroupBasedLoadBalaner` any more even if rsgroup feature is enabled."},"6820":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-119","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"And we have done some special changes for compatibility. First, if coprocessor `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.rsgroup.RSGroupAdminEndpoint` is specified, the `hbase.balancer.rsgroup.enabled` flag will be set to true automatically to enable rs group feature. Second, we will load `hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class` prior to `hbase.master.loadbalancer.class`. And last, if you do not set `hbase.rsgroup.grouploadbalancer.class` but only set `hbase.master.loadbalancer.class` to `RSGroupBasedLoadBalancer`, we will load the default load balancer to avoid infinite nesting. This means you do not need to change anything when upgrading if you have already enabled rs group feature."},"6821":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-120","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"The main difference comparing to the old implementation is that, now the rsgroup for a table is stored in `TableDescriptor`, instead of in `RSGroupInfo`, so the `getTables` method of `RSGroupInfo` has been deprecated. And if you use the `Admin` methods to get the `RSGroupInfo`, its `getTables` method will always return empty. This is because that in the old implementation, this method is a bit broken as you can set rsgroup on namespace and make all the tables under this namespace into this group but you can not get these tables through `RSGroupInfo.getTables`. Now you should use the two new methods `listTablesInRSGroup` and `getConfiguredNamespacesAndTablesInRSGroup` in `Admin` to get tables and namespaces in a rsgroup."},"6822":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-121","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"Of course the behavior for the old RSGroupAdminEndpoint is not changed, we will fill the tables field of the RSGroupInfo before returning, to make it compatible with old hbase client/shell."},"6823":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-122","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#migrating-from-old-implementation","content":"When upgrading, the migration between the RSGroupInfo and TableDescriptor will be done automatically. It will take sometime, but it is fine to restart master in the middle, the migration will continue after restart. And during the migration, the rs group feature will still work and in most cases the region will not be misplaced(since this is only a one time job and will not last too long so we have not test it very seriously to make sure the region will not be misplaced always, so we use the word 'in most cases'). The implementation is a bit tricky, you can see the code in `RSGroupInfoManagerImpl.migrate` if interested."},"6824":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-123","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"The Region Normalizer tries to make Regions all in a table about the same in size. It does this by first calculating total table size and average size per region. It splits any region that is larger than twice this size. Any region that is much smaller is merged into an adjacent region. The Normalizer runs on a regular schedule, which is configurable. It can also be disabled entirely via a runtime \"switch\". It can be run manually via the shell or Admin API call. Even if normally disabled, it is good to run manually after the cluster has been running a while or say after a burst of activity such as a large delete."},"6825":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-124","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"The Normalizer works well for bringing a table's region boundaries into alignment with the reality of data distribution after an initial effort at pre-splitting a table. It is also a nice compliment to the data TTL feature when the schema includes timestamp in the rowkey, as it will automatically merge away regions whose contents have expired."},"6826":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-125","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"(The bulk of the below detail was copied wholesale from the blog by Romil Choksi at HBase Region Normalizer)."},"6827":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-126","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"The Region Normalizer is feature available since HBase-1.2. It runs a set of pre-calculated merge/split actions to resize regions that are either too large or too small compared to the average region size for a given table. Region Normalizer when invoked computes a normalization 'plan' for all of the tables in HBase. System tables (such as hbase:meta, hbase:namespace, Phoenix system tables etc) and user tables with normalization disabled are ignored while computing the plan. For normalization enabled tables, normalization plan is carried out in parallel across multiple tables."},"6828":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-127","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"Normalizer can be enabled or disabled globally for the entire cluster using the ‘normalizer\\_switch' command in the HBase shell. Normalization can also be controlled on a per table basis, which is disabled by default when a table is created. Normalization for a table can be enabled or disabled by setting the NORMALIZATION\\_ENABLED table attribute to true or false."},"6829":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-128","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"To check normalizer status and enable/disable normalizer"},"6830":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-129","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"When enabled, Normalizer is invoked in the background every 5 mins (by default), which can be configured using `hbase.normalization.period` in `hbase-site.xml`. Normalizer can also be invoked manually/programmatically at will using HBase shell's `normalize` command. HBase by default uses `SimpleRegionNormalizer`, but users can design their own normalizer as long as they implement the RegionNormalizer Interface. Details about the logic used by `SimpleRegionNormalizer` to compute its normalization plan can be found here."},"6831":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-130","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"The below example shows a normalization plan being computed for an user table, and merge action being taken as a result of the normalization plan computed by SimpleRegionNormalizer."},"6832":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-131","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"Consider an user table with some pre-split regions having 3 equally large regions (about 100K rows) and 1 relatively small region (about 25K rows). Following is the snippet from an hbase meta table scan showing each of the pre-split regions for the user table."},"6833":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-132","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"Invoking the normalizer using 'normalize' int the HBase shell, the below log snippet from HMaster log shows the normalization plan computed as per the logic defined for SimpleRegionNormalizer. Since the total region size (in MB) for the adjacent smallest regions in the table is less than the average region size, the normalizer computes a plan to merge these two regions."},"6834":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-133","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"Region normalizer as per it's computed plan, merged the region with start key as ‘' and end key as ‘1', with another region having start key as ‘1' and end key as ‘3'. Now, that these regions have been merged we see a single new region with start key as ‘' and end key as ‘3'"},"6835":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-134","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#region-normalizer","content":"A similar example can be seen for an user table with 3 smaller regions and 1 relatively large region. For this example, we have an user table with 1 large region containing 100K rows, and 3 relatively smaller regions with about 33K rows each. As seen from the normalization plan, since the larger region is more than twice the average region size it ends being split into two regions – one with start key as ‘1' and end key as ‘154717' and the other region with start key as '154717' and end key as ‘3'"},"6836":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-135","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#auto-region-reopen","content":"We can leak store reader references if a coprocessor or core function somehow opens a scanner, or wraps one, and then does not take care to call close on the scanner or the wrapped instance. Leaked store files can not be removed even after it is invalidated via compaction. A reasonable mitigation for a reader reference leak would be a fast reopen of the region on the same server. This will release all resources, like the refcount, leases, etc. The clients should gracefully ride over this like any other region in transition. By default this auto reopen of region feature would be disabled. To enabled it, please provide high ref count value for config `hbase.regions.recovery.store.file.ref.count`."},"6837":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity-136","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/region-and-capacity#auto-region-reopen","content":"Please refer to config descriptions for `hbase.master.regions.recovery.check.interval` and `hbase.regions.recovery.store.file.ref.count`."},"6838":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"page","content":"Running Multiple Workloads On a Single Cluster","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster"},"6839":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","content":"Managing multiple workloads on HBase clusters using quotas, request queues, and priority mechanisms for resource allocation."},"6840":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#quotas","content":"Quotas"},"6841":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-quotas","content":"Enabling Quotas"},"6842":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"General Quota Syntax"},"6843":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-request-quotas","content":"Setting Request Quotas"},"6844":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-disabling-and-checking-rpc-throttling","content":"Enabling, Disabling, and Checking RPC Throttling"},"6845":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-namespace-quotas","content":"Setting Namespace Quotas"},"6846":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#limiting-tables-per-namespace","content":"Limiting Tables Per Namespace"},"6847":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#limiting-regions-per-namespace","content":"Limiting Regions Per Namespace"},"6848":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#request-queues","content":"Request Queues"},"6849":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-typed-queues","content":"Multiple-Typed Queues"},"6850":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-queues","content":"Multiple Queues"},"6851":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#read-and-write-queues","content":"Read and Write Queues"},"6852":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#get-and-scan-queues","content":"Get and Scan Queues"},"6853":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"Space Quotas"},"6854":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-simple-space-quotas","content":"Setting simple space quotas"},"6855":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-16","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#table-and-namespace-space-quotas","content":"Table and Namespace space quotas"},"6856":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-17","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#disabling-automatic-space-quota-deletion","content":"Disabling Automatic Space Quota Deletion"},"6857":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-18","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"HBase Snapshots with Space Quotas"},"6858":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-19","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","content":"HBase provides the following mechanisms for managing the performance of a cluster handling multiple workloads:"},"6859":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-20","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","content":"Quotas"},"6860":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-21","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","content":"Request Queues"},"6861":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-22","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","content":"Multiple-Typed Queues"},"6862":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-23","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#quotas","content":"HBASE-11598 introduces RPC quotas, which allow you to throttle requests based on the following limits:"},"6863":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-24","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#quotas","content":"The number or size of requests(read, write, or read+write) in a given timeframe"},"6864":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-25","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#quotas","content":"The number of tables allowed in a namespace"},"6865":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-26","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#quotas","content":"These limits can be enforced for a specified user, table, or namespace."},"6866":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-27","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-quotas","content":"Quotas are disabled by default. To enable the feature, set the `hbase.quota.enabled` property to `true` in *hbase-site.xml* file for all cluster nodes."},"6867":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-28","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"THROTTLE\\_TYPE can be expressed as READ, WRITE, or the default type(read + write)."},"6868":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-29","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"Timeframes can be expressed in the following units: `sec`, `min`, `hour`, `day`"},"6869":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-30","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"Request sizes can be expressed in the following units: `B` (bytes), `K` (kilobytes), `M` (megabytes), `G` (gigabytes), `T` (terabytes), `P` (petabytes)"},"6870":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-31","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"Numbers of requests are expressed as an integer followed by the string `req`"},"6871":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-32","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"Limits relating to time are expressed as req/time or size/time. For instance `10req/day` or `100P/hour`."},"6872":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-33","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#general-quota-syntax","content":"Numbers of tables or regions are expressed as integers."},"6873":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-34","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-request-quotas","content":"You can set quota rules ahead of time, or you can change the throttle at runtime. The change will propagate after the quota refresh period has expired. This expiration period defaults to 5 minutes. To change it, modify the `hbase.quota.refresh.period` property in `hbase-site.xml`. This property is expressed in milliseconds and defaults to `300000`."},"6874":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-35","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-request-quotas","content":"You can also place a global limit and exclude a user or a table from the limit by applying the `GLOBAL_BYPASS` property."},"6875":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-36","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-disabling-and-checking-rpc-throttling","content":"HBase provides shell commands to control RPC throttling at runtime. When throttling is disabled, HBase will not apply any request throttling. This can be useful in production environments that require temporary unthrottled operation."},"6876":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-37","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-disabling-and-checking-rpc-throttling","content":"The following HBase shell commands are available:"},"6877":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-38","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-disabling-and-checking-rpc-throttling","content":"`enable_rpc_throttle` and `disable_rpc_throttle` return the previous RPC throttling state as a boolean value. `rpc_throttle_enabled` returns the current state."},"6878":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-39","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#enabling-disabling-and-checking-rpc-throttling","content":"If no quotas are configured, RPC throttling is not applied, and enabling or disabling throttling\nwill always return false."},"6879":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-40","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-namespace-quotas","content":"You can specify the maximum number of tables or regions allowed in a given namespace, either when you create the namespace or by altering an existing namespace, by setting the `hbase.namespace.quota.maxtables property` on the namespace."},"6880":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-41","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#request-queues","content":"If no throttling policy is configured, when the RegionServer receives multiple requests, they are now placed into a queue waiting for a free execution slot (HBASE-6721). The simplest queue is a FIFO queue, where each request waits for all previous requests in the queue to finish before running. Fast or interactive queries can get stuck behind large requests."},"6881":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#request-queues","content":"If you are able to guess how long a request will take, you can reorder requests by pushing the long requests to the end of the queue and allowing short requests to preempt them. Eventually, you must still execute the large requests and prioritize the new requests behind them. The short requests will be newer, so the result is not terrible, but still suboptimal compared to a mechanism which allows large requests to be split into multiple smaller ones."},"6882":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#request-queues","content":"HBASE-10993 introduces such a system for deprioritizing long-running scanners. There are two types of queues, `fifo` and `deadline`. To configure the type of queue used, configure the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.type` property in `hbase-site.xml`. There is no way to estimate how long each request may take, so de-prioritization only affects scans, and is based on the number of “next” calls a scan request has made. An assumption is made that when you are doing a full table scan, your job is not likely to be interactive, so if there are concurrent requests, you can delay long-running scans up to a limit tunable by setting the `hbase.ipc.server.queue.max.call.delay` property. The slope of the delay is calculated by a simple square root of `(numNextCall * weight)` where the weight is configurable by setting the `hbase.ipc.server.scan.vtime.weight` property."},"6883":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-typed-queues","content":"You can also prioritize or deprioritize different kinds of requests by configuring a specified number of dedicated handlers and queues. You can segregate the scan requests in a single queue with a single handler, and all the other available queues can service short `Get` requests."},"6884":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-45","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-typed-queues","content":"You can adjust the IPC queues and handlers based on the type of workload, using static tuning options. This approach is an interim first step that will eventually allow you to change the settings at runtime, and to dynamically adjust values based on the load."},"6885":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-46","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-queues","content":"To avoid contention and separate different kinds of requests, configure the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor` property, which allows you to increase the number of queues and control how many handlers can share the same queue., allows admins to increase the number of queues and decide how many handlers share the same queue."},"6886":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-47","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#multiple-queues","content":"Using more queues reduces contention when adding a task to a queue or selecting it from a queue. You can even configure one queue per handler. The trade-off is that if some queues contain long-running tasks, a handler may need to wait to execute from that queue rather than stealing from another queue which has waiting tasks."},"6887":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-48","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#read-and-write-queues","content":"With multiple queues, you can now divide read and write requests, giving more priority (more queues) to one or the other type. Use the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio` property to choose to serve more reads or more writes."},"6888":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-49","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#get-and-scan-queues","content":"Similar to the read/write split, you can split gets and scans by tuning the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.scan.ratio` property to give more priority to gets or to scans. A scan ratio of `0.1` will give more queue/handlers to the incoming gets, which means that more gets can be processed at the same time and that fewer scans can be executed at the same time. A value of `0.9` will give more queue/handlers to scans, so the number of scans executed will increase and the number of gets will decrease."},"6889":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-50","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"HBASE-16961 introduces a new type of quotas for HBase to leverage: filesystem quotas. These \"space\" quotas limit the amount of space on the filesystem that HBase namespaces and tables can consume. If a user, malicious or ignorant, has the ability to write data into HBase, with enough time, that user can effectively crash HBase (or worse HDFS) by consuming all available space. When there is no filesystem space available, HBase crashes because it can no longer create/sync data to the write-ahead log."},"6890":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-51","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"This feature allows a for a limit to be set on the size of a table or namespace. When a space quota is set on a namespace, the quota's limit applies to the sum of usage of all tables in that namespace. When a table with a quota exists in a namespace with a quota, the table quota takes priority over the namespace quota. This allows for a scenario where a large limit can be placed on a collection of tables, but a single table in that collection can have a fine-grained limit set."},"6891":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-52","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"The existing `set_quota` and `list_quota` HBase shell commands can be used to interact with space quotas. Space quotas are quotas with a `TYPE` of `SPACE` and have `LIMIT` and `POLICY` attributes. The `LIMIT` is a string that refers to the amount of space on the filesystem that the quota subject (e.g. the table or namespace) may consume. For example, valid values of `LIMIT` are `'10G'`, `'2T'`, or `'256M'`. The `POLICY` refers to the action that HBase will take when the quota subject's usage exceeds the `LIMIT`. The following are valid `POLICY` values."},"6892":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-53","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"`NO_INSERTS` - No new data may be written (e.g. `Put`, `Increment`, `Append`)."},"6893":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-54","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"`NO_WRITES` - Same as `NO_INSERTS` but `Deletes` are also disallowed."},"6894":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-55","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"`NO_WRITES_COMPACTIONS` - Same as `NO_WRITES` but compactions are also disallowed."},"6895":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-56","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"This policy only prevents user-submitted compactions. System can still run compactions."},"6896":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-57","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#space-quotas","content":"`DISABLE` - The table(s) are disabled, preventing all read/write access."},"6897":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-58","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#setting-simple-space-quotas","content":"Consider the following scenario to set up quotas on a namespace, overriding the quota on tables in that namespace"},"6898":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-59","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#table-and-namespace-space-quotas","content":"In the above scenario, the tables in the namespace `ns1` will not be allowed to consume more than 100TB of space on the filesystem among each other. The table 'ns1:t2' is only allowed to be 200GB in size, and will disallow all writes when the usage exceeds this limit. The table 'ns1:t3' is allowed to grow to 20TB in size and also will disallow all writes then the usage exceeds this limit. Because there is no table quota on 'ns1:t1', this table can grow up to 100TB, but only if 'ns1:t2' and 'ns1:t3' have a usage of zero bytes. Practically, it's limit is 100TB less the current usage of 'ns1:t2' and 'ns1:t3'."},"6899":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-60","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#disabling-automatic-space-quota-deletion","content":"By default, if a table or namespace is deleted that has a space quota, the quota itself is also deleted. In some cases, it may be desirable for the space quota to not be automatically deleted. In these cases, the user may configure the system to not delete any space quota automatically via hbase-site.xml."},"6900":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-61","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#disabling-automatic-space-quota-deletion","content":"The value is set to `true` by default."},"6901":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-62","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"One common area of unintended-filesystem-use with HBase is via HBase snapshots. Because snapshots exist outside of the management of HBase tables, it is not uncommon for administrators to suddenly realize that hundreds of gigabytes or terabytes of space is being used by HBase snapshots which were forgotten and never removed."},"6902":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-63","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"HBASE-17748 is the umbrella JIRA issue which expands on the original space quota functionality to also include HBase snapshots. While this is a confusing subject, the implementation attempts to present this support in as reasonable and simple of a manner as possible for administrators. This feature does not make any changes to administrator interaction with space quotas, only in the internal computation of table/namespace usage. Table and namespace usage will automatically incorporate the size taken by a snapshot per the rules defined below."},"6903":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-64","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"As a review, let's cover a snapshot's lifecycle: a snapshot is metadata which points to a list of HFiles on the filesystem. This is why creating a snapshot is a very cheap operation; no HBase table data is actually copied to perform a snapshot. Cloning a snapshot into a new table or restoring a table is a cheap operation for the same reason; the new table references the files which already exist on the filesystem without a copy. To include snapshots in space quotas, we need to define which table \"owns\" a file when a snapshot references the file (\"owns\" refers to encompassing the filesystem usage of that file)."},"6904":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-65","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"Consider a snapshot which was made against a table. When the snapshot refers to a file and the table no longer refers to that file, the \"originating\" table \"owns\" that file. When multiple snapshots refer to the same file and no table refers to that file, the snapshot with the lowest-sorting name (lexicographically) is chosen and the table which that snapshot was created from \"owns\" that file. HFiles are not \"double-counted\" hen a table and one or more snapshots refer to that HFile."},"6905":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-66","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"When a table is \"rematerialized\" (via `clone_snapshot` or `restore_snapshot`), a similar problem of file ownership arises. In this case, while the rematerialized table references a file which a snapshot also references, the table does not \"own\" the file. The table from which the snapshot was created still \"owns\" that file. When the rematerialized table is compacted or the snapshot is deleted, the rematerialized table will uniquely refer to a new file and \"own\" the usage of that file. Similarly, when a table is duplicated via a snapshot and `restore_snapshot`, the new table will not consume any quota size until the original table stops referring to the files, either due to a compaction on the original table, a compaction on the new table, or the original table being deleted."},"6906":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster-67","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/running-multiple-workloads-on-a-single-cluster#hbase-snapshots-with-space-quotas","content":"One new HBase shell command was added to inspect the computed sizes of each snapshot in an HBase instance."},"6907":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","type":"page","content":"Table Rename","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename"},"6908":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","content":"How to rename HBase tables using snapshots without downtime or data loss."},"6909":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","content":"In versions 0.90.x of hbase and earlier, we had a simple script that would rename the hdfs table directory and then do an edit of the hbase:meta table replacing all mentions of the old table name with the new. The script was called `./bin/rename_table.rb`. The script was deprecated and removed mostly because it was unmaintained and the operation performed by the script was brutal."},"6910":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","content":"As of hbase 0.94.x, you can use the snapshot facility renaming a table. Here is how you would do it using the hbase shell:"},"6911":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/table-rename","content":"or in code it would be as follows:"},"6912":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"page","content":"HBase Tools and Utilities","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development","Apache HBase Operational Management"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools"},"6913":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-0","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools","content":"Command-line tools for HBase administration, analysis, and debugging including canary, HBCK2, WAL tools, and MapReduce utilities."},"6914":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-1","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"Canary"},"6915":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-2","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-for-every-column-family-store-of-every-region-of-every-table","content":"Canary test for every column family (store) of every region of every table"},"6916":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-3","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-for-every-column-family-store-of-every-region-of-a-specific-tables","content":"Canary test for every column family (store) of every region of a specific table(s)"},"6917":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-4","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-with-regionserver-granularity","content":"Canary test with RegionServer granularity"},"6918":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-5","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-with-regular-expression-pattern","content":"Canary test with regular expression pattern"},"6919":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-6","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#run-canary-test-as-a-daemon","content":"Run canary test as a \"daemon\""},"6920":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-7","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#force-timeout-if-canary-test-stuck","content":"Force timeout if canary test stuck"},"6921":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-8","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#enable-write-sniffing-in-canary","content":"Enable write sniffing in canary"},"6922":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-9","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#treat-read--write-failure-as-error","content":"Treat read / write failure as error"},"6923":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-10","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"Running Canary in a Kerberos-enabled Cluster"},"6924":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-11","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#regionsplitter","content":"RegionSplitter"},"6925":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-12","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#health-checker","content":"Health Checker"},"6926":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-13","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"Driver"},"6927":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-14","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck","content":"HBase `hbck`"},"6928":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-15","page_id":"/docs/operational-manage