tional-management/tools-42","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#pre-upgrade-validator","content":"Pre-Upgrade validator"},"6956":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-43","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"Coprocessor validation"},"6957":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-44","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#datablockencoding-validation","content":"DataBlockEncoding validation"},"6958":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-45","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hfile-content-validation","content":"HFile Content validation"},"6959":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-46","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixing-prefix_tree-errors","content":"Fixing PREFIX_TREE errors"},"6960":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-47","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#data-block-encoding-tool","content":"Data Block Encoding Tool"},"6961":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-48","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-conf-tool","content":"HBase Conf Tool"},"6962":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-49","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools","content":"HBase provides several tools for administration, analysis, and debugging of your cluster. The entry-point to most of these tools is the *bin/hbase* command, though some tools are available in the &#x2A;dev-support/* directory."},"6963":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-50","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools","content":"To see usage instructions for *bin/hbase* command, run it with no arguments, or with the `-h` argument. These are the usage instructions for HBase 0.98.x. Some commands, such as `version`, `pe`, `ltt`, `clean`, are not available in previous versions."},"6964":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-51","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools","content":"Some of the tools and utilities below are Java classes which are passed directly to the *bin/hbase* command, as referred to in the last line of the usage instructions. Others, such as `hbase shell` (The Apache HBase Shell), `hbase upgrade` (Upgrading), and `hbase thrift` (Thrift API and Filter Language), are documented elsewhere in this guide."},"6965":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-52","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"The Canary tool can help users \"canary-test\" the HBase cluster status. The default \"region mode\" fetches a row from every column-family of every regions. In \"regionserver mode\", the Canary tool will fetch a row from a random region on each of the cluster's RegionServers. In \"zookeeper mode\", the Canary will read the root znode on each member of the zookeeper ensemble."},"6966":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-53","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"To see usage, pass the `-help` parameter (if you pass no parameters, the Canary tool starts executing in the default region \"mode\" fetching a row from every region in the cluster)."},"6967":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-54","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"The `Sink` class is instantiated using the `hbase.canary.sink.class` configuration property."},"6968":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-55","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"This tool will return non zero error codes to user for collaborating with other monitoring tools, such as Nagios. The error code definitions are:"},"6969":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-56","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"Here are some examples based on the following given case: given two Table objects called test-01 and test-02 each with two column family cf1 and cf2 respectively, deployed on 3 RegionServers. See the following table."},"6970":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-57","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"RegionServer"},"6971":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-58","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"test-01"},"6972":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-59","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"test-02"},"6973":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-60","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"rs1"},"6974":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-61","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"r1"},"6975":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-62","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"r2"},"6976":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-63","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"rs2"},"6977":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-64","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"r2"},"6978":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-65","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"rs3"},"6979":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-66","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"r2"},"6980":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-67","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"r1"},"6981":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-68","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary","content":"Following are some example outputs based on the previous given case."},"6982":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-69","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-for-every-column-family-store-of-every-region-of-every-table","content":"So you can see, table test-01 has two regions and two column families, so the Canary tool in the default \"region mode\" will pick 4 small piece of data from 4 (2 region \\* 2 store) different stores. This is a default behavior."},"6983":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-70","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-for-every-column-family-store-of-every-region-of-a-specific-tables","content":"You can also test one or more specific tables by passing table names."},"6984":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-71","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-with-regionserver-granularity","content":"In \"regionserver mode\", the Canary tool will pick one small piece of data from each RegionServer (You can also pass one or more RegionServer names as arguments to the canary-test when in \"regionserver mode\")."},"6985":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-72","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#canary-test-with-regular-expression-pattern","content":"You can pass regexes for table names when in \"region mode\" or for servernames when in \"regionserver mode\". The below will test both table test-01 and test-02."},"6986":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-73","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#run-canary-test-as-a-daemon","content":"Run repeatedly with an interval defined via the option `-interval` (default value is 60 seconds). This daemon will stop itself and return non-zero error code if any error occur. To have the daemon keep running across errors, pass the -f flag with its value set to false (see usage above)."},"6987":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-74","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#run-canary-test-as-a-daemon","content":"To run repeatedly with 5 second intervals and not stop on errors, do the following."},"6988":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-75","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#force-timeout-if-canary-test-stuck","content":"In some cases the request is stuck and no response is sent back to the client. This can happen with dead RegionServers which the master has not yet noticed. Because of this we provide a timeout option to kill the canary test and return a non-zero error code. The below sets the timeout value to 60 seconds (the default value is 600 seconds)."},"6989":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-76","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#enable-write-sniffing-in-canary","content":"By default, the canary tool only checks read operations. To enable the write sniffing, you can run the canary with the `-writeSniffing` option set. When write sniffing is enabled, the canary tool will create an hbase table and make sure the regions of the table are distributed to all region servers. In each sniffing period, the canary will try to put data to these regions to check the write availability of each region server."},"6990":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-77","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#enable-write-sniffing-in-canary","content":"The default write table is `hbase:canary` and can be specified with the option `-writeTable`."},"6991":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-78","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#enable-write-sniffing-in-canary","content":"The default value size of each put is 10 bytes. You can set it via the config key: `hbase.canary.write.value.size`."},"6992":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-79","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#treat-read--write-failure-as-error","content":"By default, the canary tool only logs read failures — due to e.g. RetriesExhaustedException, etc. — and will return the 'normal' exit code. To treat read/write failure as errors, you can run canary with the `-treatFailureAsError` option. When enabled, read/write failures will result in an error exit code."},"6993":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-80","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"To run the Canary in a Kerberos-enabled cluster, configure the following two properties in *hbase-site.xml*:"},"6994":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-81","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"`hbase.client.keytab.file`"},"6995":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-82","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"`hbase.client.kerberos.principal`"},"6996":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-83","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"Kerberos credentials are refreshed every 30 seconds when Canary runs in daemon mode."},"6997":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-84","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"To configure the DNS interface for the client, configure the following optional properties in *hbase-site.xml*."},"6998":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-85","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"`hbase.client.dns.interface`"},"6999":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-86","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"`hbase.client.dns.nameserver`"},"7000":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-87","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#running-canary-in-a-kerberos-enabled-cluster","content":"**Example Canary in a Kerberos-Enabled Cluster**\\\nThis example shows each of the properties with valid values."},"7001":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-88","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#regionsplitter","content":"For additional detail, see Manual Region Splitting."},"7002":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-89","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#health-checker","content":"You can configure HBase to run a script periodically and if it fails N times (configurable), have the server exit. See *HBASE-7351 Periodic health check script* for configurations and detail."},"7003":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-90","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"Several frequently-accessed utilities are provided as `Driver` classes, and executed by the *bin/hbase* command. These utilities represent MapReduce jobs which run on your cluster. They are run in the following way, replacing *UtilityName* with the utility you want to run. This command assumes you have set the environment variable `HBASE_HOME` to the directory where HBase is unpacked on your server."},"7004":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-91","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"The following utilities are available:"},"7005":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-92","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`LoadIncrementalHFiles`\\\nComplete a bulk data load."},"7006":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-93","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`CopyTable`\\\nExport a table from the local cluster to a peer cluster."},"7007":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-94","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`Export`\\\nWrite table data to HDFS."},"7008":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-95","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`Import`\\\nImport data written by a previous `Export` operation."},"7009":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-96","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`ImportTsv`\\\nImport data in TSV format."},"7010":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-97","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`RowCounter`\\\nCount rows in an HBase table."},"7011":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-98","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`CellCounter`\\\nCount cells in an HBase table."},"7012":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-99","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"`replication.VerifyReplication`\\\nCompare the data from tables in two different clusters. WARNING: It doesn't work for incrementColumnValues'd cells since the timestamp is changed. Note that this command is in a different package than the others."},"7013":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-100","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#driver","content":"Each command except `RowCounter` and `CellCounter` accept a single `--help` argument to print usage instructions."},"7014":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-101","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck","content":"The `hbck` tool that shipped with hbase-1.x has been made read-only in hbase-2.x. It is not able to repair hbase-2.x clusters as hbase internals have changed. Nor should its assessments in read-only mode be trusted as it does not understand hbase-2.x operation."},"7015":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-102","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck","content":"A new tool, HBase `HBCK2`, described in the next section, replaces `hbck`."},"7016":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-103","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck2","content":"`HBCK2` is the successor to HBase `HBCK`, the hbase-1.x fix tool (A.K.A `hbck1`). Use it in place of `hbck1` making repairs against hbase-2.x installs."},"7017":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-104","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck2","content":"`HBCK2` does not ship as part of hbase. It can be found as a subproject of the companion hbase-operator-tools repository at Apache HBase HBCK2 Tool. `HBCK2` was moved out of hbase so it could evolve at a cadence apart from that of hbase core."},"7018":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-105","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck2","content":"See the HBCK2 Home Page for how `HBCK2` differs from `hbck1`, and for how to build and use it."},"7019":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-106","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck2","content":"Once built, you can run `HBCK2` as follows:"},"7020":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-107","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-hbck2","content":"This will generate `HBCK2` usage describing commands and options."},"7021":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-108","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#operational-management-tools-hfile-tool","content":"See HFile Tool."},"7022":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-109","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#wal-tools","content":"For bulk replaying WAL files or *recovered.edits* files, see WALPlayer. For reading/verifying individual files, read on."},"7023":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-110","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walprettyprinter","content":"The `WALPrettyPrinter` is a tool with configurable options to print the contents of a WAL or a *recovered.edits* file. You can invoke it via the HBase cli with the 'wal' command."},"7024":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-111","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walprettyprinter","content":"Prior to version 2.0, the `WALPrettyPrinter` was called the `HLogPrettyPrinter`, after an internal name for HBase's write ahead log. In those versions, you can print the contents of a WAL using the same configuration as above, but with the 'hlog' command."},"7025":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-112","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#compression-tool","content":"See compression.test."},"7026":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-113","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"CopyTable is a utility that can copy part or of all of a table, either to the same cluster or another cluster. The target table must first exist. The usage is as follows:"},"7027":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-114","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"Starting from 3.0.0, we introduce a `peer.uri` option so the `peer.adr` option is deprecated. Please use connection URI for specifying HBase clusters. For all previous versions, you should still use the `peer.adr` option."},"7028":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-115","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"Caching for the input Scan is configured via `hbase.client.scanner.caching` in the job\nconfiguration."},"7029":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-116","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"By default, CopyTable utility only copies the latest version of row cells unless `--versions=n` is\nexplicitly specified in the command."},"7030":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-117","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"CopyTable does not perform a diff, it copies all Cells in between the specified startrow/stoprow\nstarttime/endtime range. This means that already existing cells with same values will still be\ncopied."},"7031":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-118","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#copytable","content":"See Jonathan Hsieh's Online HBase Backups with CopyTable blog post for more on `CopyTable`."},"7032":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-119","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hashtablesynctable","content":"HashTable/SyncTable is a two steps tool for synchronizing table data, where each of the steps are implemented as MapReduce jobs. Similarly to CopyTable, it can be used for partial or entire table data syncing, under same or remote cluster. However, it performs the sync in a more efficient way than CopyTable. Instead of copying all cells in specified row key/time period range, HashTable (the first step) creates hashed indexes for batch of cells on source table and output those as results. On the next stage, SyncTable scans the source table and now calculates hash indexes for table cells, compares these hashes with the outputs of HashTable, then it just scans (and compares) cells for diverging hashes, only updating mismatching cells. This results in less network traffic/data transfers, which can be impacting when syncing large tables on remote clusters."},"7033":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-120","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-1-hashtable","content":"First, run HashTable on the source table cluster (this is the table whose state will be copied to its counterpart)."},"7034":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-121","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-1-hashtable","content":"Usage:"},"7035":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-122","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-1-hashtable","content":"The **batchsize** property defines how much cell data for a given region will be hashed together in a single hash value. Sizing this properly has a direct impact on the sync efficiency, as it may lead to less scans executed by mapper tasks of SyncTable (the next step in the process). The rule of thumb is that, the smaller the number of cells out of sync (lower probability of finding a diff), larger batch size values can be determined."},"7036":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-123","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Once HashTable has completed on source cluster, SyncTable can be ran on target cluster. Just like replication and other synchronization jobs, it requires that all RegionServers/DataNodes on source cluster be accessible by NodeManagers on the target cluster (where SyncTable job tasks will be running)."},"7037":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-124","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Usage:"},"7038":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-125","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Starting from 3.0.0, we introduce `sourceuri` and `targeturi` options so `sourcezkcluster` and `targetzkcluster` are deprecated. Please use connection URI for specifying HBase clusters. For all previous versions, you should still use `sourcezkcluster` and `targetzkcluster`."},"7039":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-126","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Cell comparison takes ROW/FAMILY/QUALIFIER/TIMESTAMP/VALUE into account for equality. When syncing at the target, missing cells will be added with original timestamp value from source. That may cause unexpected results after SyncTable completes, for example, if missing cells on target have a delete marker with a timestamp T2 (say, a bulk delete performed by mistake), but source cells timestamps have an older value T1, then those cells would still be unavailable at target because of the newer delete marker timestamp. Since cell timestamps might not be relevant to all use cases, *ignoreTimestamps* option adds the flexibility to avoid using cells timestamp in the comparison. When using *ignoreTimestamps* set to true, this option must be specified for both HashTable and SyncTable steps."},"7040":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-127","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"The **dryrun** option is useful when a read only, diff report is wanted, as it will produce only COUNTERS indicating the differences, but will not perform any actual changes. It can be used as an alternative to VerifyReplication tool."},"7041":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-128","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"By default, SyncTable will cause target table to become an exact copy of source table (at least, for the specified startrow/stoprow or/and starttime/endtime)."},"7042":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-129","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Setting doDeletes to false modifies default behaviour to not delete target cells that are missing on source. Similarly, setting doPuts to false modifies default behaviour to not add missing cells on target. Setting both doDeletes and doPuts to false would give same effect as setting dryrun to true."},"7043":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-130","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"\"doDeletes/doPuts\" were only added by\nHBASE-20305, so these may not be available on\nall released versions. For major 1.x versions, minimum minor release including it is **1.4.10**.\nFor major 2.x versions, minimum minor release including it is **2.1.5**."},"7044":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-131","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"\"ignoreTimestamps\" was only added by\nHBASE-24302, so it may not be available on\nall released versions. For major 1.x versions, minimum minor release including it is **1.4.14**.\nFor major 2.x versions, minimum minor release including it is **2.2.5**."},"7045":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-132","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"On Two-Way Replication or other scenarios where both source and target clusters can have data\ningested, it's advisable to always set doDeletes option to false, as any additional cell inserted\non SyncTable target cluster and not yet replicated to source would be deleted, and potentially\nlost permanently."},"7046":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-133","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Although not required, if sourcezkcluster is not set, SyncTable will connect to local HBase\ncluster for both source and target, which does not give any meaningful result."},"7047":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-134","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#step-2-synctable","content":"Often, remote clusters may be deployed on different Kerberos Realms.\nHBASE-20586 added SyncTable support for cross\nrealm authentication, allowing a SyncTable process running on target cluster to connect to source\ncluster and read both HashTable output files and the given HBase table when performing the\nrequired comparisons."},"7048":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-135","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Export is a utility that will dump the contents of table to HDFS in a sequence file. The Export can be run via a Coprocessor Endpoint or MapReduce. Invoke via:"},"7049":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-136","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"**mapreduce-based Export**"},"7050":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-137","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"**endpoint-based Export**"},"7051":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-138","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Make sure the Export coprocessor is enabled by adding `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.coprocessor.Export`\nto `hbase.coprocessor.region.classes`."},"7052":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-139","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"The outputdir is a HDFS directory that does not exist prior to the export. When done, the exported files will be owned by the user invoking the export command."},"7053":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-140","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"**The Comparison of Endpoint-based Export And Mapreduce-based Export**"},"7054":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-141","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Endpoint-based Export"},"7055":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-142","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Mapreduce-based Export"},"7056":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-143","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"HBase version requirement"},"7057":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-144","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"2.0+"},"7058":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-145","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"0.2.1+"},"7059":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-146","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Maven dependency"},"7060":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-147","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"hbase-endpoint"},"7061":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-148","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"hbase-mapreduce (2.0+), hbase-server(prior to 2.0)"},"7062":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-149","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Requirement before dump"},"7063":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-150","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"mount the endpoint.Export on the target table"},"7064":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-151","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"deploy the MapReduce framework"},"7065":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-152","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Read latency"},"7066":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-153","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"low, directly read the data from region"},"7067":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-154","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"normal, traditional RPC scan"},"7068":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-155","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Read Scalability"},"7069":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-156","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"depend on number of regions"},"7070":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-157","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"depend on number of mappers (see TableInputFormatBase#getSplits)"},"7071":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-158","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Timeout"},"7072":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-159","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"operation timeout. configured by hbase.client.operation.timeout"},"7073":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-160","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"scan timeout. configured by hbase.client.scanner.timeout.period"},"7074":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-161","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Permission requirement"},"7075":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-162","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"READ, EXECUTE"},"7076":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-163","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"READ"},"7077":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-164","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Fault tolerance"},"7078":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-165","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"no"},"7079":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-166","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"depend on MapReduce"},"7080":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-167","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"To see usage instructions, run the command with no options. Available options include specifying\ncolumn families and applying filters during the export."},"7081":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-168","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"By default, the `Export&#x60; tool only exports the newest version of a given cell, regardless of the number of versions stored. To export more than one version, replace &#x2A;**\\<versions>*** with the desired number of versions."},"7082":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-169","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"For mapreduce based Export, if you want to export cell tags then set the following config property `hbase.client.rpc.codec` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.codec.KeyValueCodecWithTags`"},"7083":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-170","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#export","content":"Note: caching for the input Scan is configured via `hbase.client.scanner.caching` in the job configuration."},"7084":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-171","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#import","content":"Import is a utility that will load data that has been exported back into HBase. Invoke via:"},"7085":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-172","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#import","content":"To import 0.94 exported files in a 0.96 cluster or onwards, you need to set system property \"hbase.import.version\" when running the import command as below:"},"7086":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-173","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#import","content":"If you want to import cell tags then set the following config property `hbase.client.rpc.codec` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.codec.KeyValueCodecWithTags`"},"7087":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-174","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv","content":"ImportTsv is a utility that will load data in TSV format into HBase. It has two distinct usages: loading data from TSV format in HDFS into HBase via Puts, and preparing StoreFiles to be loaded via the `completebulkload`."},"7088":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-175","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv","content":"To load data via Puts (i.e., non-bulk loading):"},"7089":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-176","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv","content":"To generate StoreFiles for bulk-loading:"},"7090":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-177","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv","content":"These generated StoreFiles can be loaded into HBase via completebulkload."},"7091":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-178","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-options","content":"Running `ImportTsv` with no arguments prints brief usage information:"},"7092":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-179","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-example","content":"For example, assume that we are loading data into a table called 'datatsv' with a ColumnFamily called 'd' with two columns \"c1\" and \"c2\"."},"7093":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-180","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-example","content":"Assume that an input file exists as follows:"},"7094":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-181","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-example","content":"For ImportTsv to use this input file, the command line needs to look like this:"},"7095":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-182","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-example","content":"... and in this example the first column is the rowkey, which is why the HBASE\\_ROW\\_KEY is used. The second and third columns in the file will be imported as \"d:c1\" and \"d:c2\", respectively."},"7096":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-183","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-warning","content":"If you have preparing a lot of data for bulk loading, make sure the target HBase table is pre-split appropriately."},"7097":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-184","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#importtsv-see-also","content":"For more information about bulk-loading HFiles into HBase, see arch.bulk.load"},"7098":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-185","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload","content":"The `completebulkload` utility will move generated StoreFiles into an HBase table. This utility is often used in conjunction with output from importtsv."},"7099":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-186","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload","content":"There are two ways to invoke this utility, with explicit classname and via the driver:"},"7100":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-187","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload","content":"**Explicit Classname**"},"7101":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-188","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload","content":"**Driver**"},"7102":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-189","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload-warning","content":"Data generated via MapReduce is often created with file permissions that are not compatible with the running HBase process. Assuming you're running HDFS with permissions enabled, those permissions will need to be updated before you run CompleteBulkLoad."},"7103":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-190","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#completebulkload-warning","content":"For more information about bulk-loading HFiles into HBase, see arch.bulk.load."},"7104":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-191","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"WALPlayer is a utility to replay WAL files into HBase."},"7105":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-192","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"The WAL can be replayed for a set of tables or all tables, and a timerange can be provided (in milliseconds). The WAL is filtered to this set of tables. The output can optionally be mapped to another set of tables."},"7106":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-193","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"WALPlayer can also generate HFiles for later bulk importing, in that case only a single table and no mapping can be specified."},"7107":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-194","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"Finally, you can use WALPlayer to replay the content of a Regions `recovered.edits` directory (the files under `recovered.edits` directory have the same format as WAL files)."},"7108":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-195","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"To read or verify single WAL files or *recovered.edits* files, since they share the WAL format,\nsee WAL Tools."},"7109":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-196","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"Invoke via:"},"7110":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-197","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"For example:"},"7111":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-198","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer","content":"WALPlayer, by default, runs as a mapreduce job. To NOT run WALPlayer as a mapreduce job on your cluster, force it to run all in the local process by adding the flags `-Dmapreduce.jobtracker.address=local` on the command line."},"7112":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-199","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#walplayer-options","content":"Running `WALPlayer` with no arguments prints brief usage information:"},"7113":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-200","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#rowcounter","content":"RowCounter is a mapreduce job to count all the rows of a table. This is a good utility to use as a sanity check to ensure that HBase can read all the blocks of a table if there are any concerns of metadata inconsistency. It will run the mapreduce all in a single process but it will run faster if you have a MapReduce cluster in place for it to exploit. It is possible to limit the time range of data to be scanned by using the `--starttime=[starttime]` and `--endtime=[endtime]` flags. The scanned data can be limited based on keys using the `--range=[startKey],[endKey][;[startKey],[endKey]...]` option."},"7114":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-201","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#rowcounter","content":"RowCounter only counts one version per cell."},"7115":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-202","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#rowcounter","content":"For performance consider to use `-Dhbase.client.scanner.caching=100` and `-Dmapreduce.map.speculative=false` options."},"7116":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-203","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"HBase ships another diagnostic mapreduce job called CellCounter. Like RowCounter, it gathers more fine-grained statistics about your table. The statistics gathered by CellCounter are more fine-grained and include:"},"7117":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-204","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total number of rows in the table."},"7118":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-205","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total number of CFs across all rows."},"7119":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-206","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total qualifiers across all rows."},"7120":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-207","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total occurrence of each CF."},"7121":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-208","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total occurrence of each qualifier."},"7122":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-209","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Total number of versions of each qualifier."},"7123":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-210","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"The program allows you to limit the scope of the run. Provide a row regex or prefix to limit the rows to analyze. Specify a time range to scan the table by using the `--starttime=<starttime>` and `--endtime=<endtime>` flags."},"7124":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-211","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Use `hbase.mapreduce.scan.column.family` to specify scanning a single column family."},"7125":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-212","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#cellcounter","content":"Note: just like RowCounter, caching for the input Scan is configured via `hbase.client.scanner.caching` in the job configuration."},"7126":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-213","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#mlockall","content":"It is possible to optionally pin your servers in physical memory making them less likely to be swapped out in oversubscribed environments by having the servers call mlockall on startup. See HBASE-4391 Add ability to start RS as root and call mlockall for how to build the optional library and have it run on startup."},"7127":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-214","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"**CompactionTool** provides a way of running compactions (either minor or major) as an independent process from the RegionServer. It reuses same internal implementation classes executed by RegionServer compaction feature. However, since this runs on a complete separate independent java process, it releases RegionServers from the overhead involved in rewrite a set of hfiles, which can be critical for latency sensitive use cases."},"7128":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-215","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"Usage:"},"7129":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-216","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"As shown by usage options above, **CompactionTool** can run as a standalone client or a mapreduce job. When running as mapreduce job, each family dir is handled as an input split, and is processed by a separate map task."},"7130":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-217","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"The **compactionOnce** parameter controls how many compaction cycles will be performed until **CompactionTool** program decides to finish its work. If omitted, it will assume it should keep running compactions on each specified family as determined by the given compaction policy configured. For more info on compaction policy, see compaction."},"7131":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-218","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"If a major compaction is desired, **major** flag can be specified. If omitted, **CompactionTool** will assume minor compaction is wanted by default."},"7132":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-219","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"It also allows for configuration overrides with `-D` flag. In the usage section above, for example, `-Dhbase.compactiontool.delete=false` option will instruct compaction engine to not delete original files from temp folder."},"7133":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-220","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"Files targeted for compaction must be specified as parent hdfs dirs. It allows for multiple dirs definition, as long as each for these dirs are either a **family**, a **region**, or a **table** dir. If a table or region dir is passed, the program will recursively iterate through related sub-folders, effectively running compaction for each family found below the table/region level."},"7134":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-221","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"Since these dirs are nested under **hbase** hdfs directory tree, **CompactionTool** requires hbase super user permissions in order to have access to required hfiles."},"7135":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-222","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"MapReduce mode offers the ability to process each family dir in parallel, as a separate map task.\nGenerally, it would make sense to run in this mode when specifying one or more table dirs as\ntargets for compactions. The caveat, though, is that if number of families to be compacted become\ntoo large, the related mapreduce job may have indirect impacts on **RegionServers** performance .\nSince **NodeManagers** are normally co-located with RegionServers, such large jobs could compete\nfor IO/Bandwidth resources with the **RegionServers**."},"7136":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-223","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"**Major compactions** can be a costly operation (see\ncompaction), and can indeed impact performance on\nRegionServers, leading operators to completely disable it for critical low latency application.\n**CompactionTool** could be used as an alternative in such scenarios, although, additional custom\napplication logic would need to be implemented, such as deciding scheduling and selection of\ntables/regions/families target for a given compaction run."},"7137":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-224","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#offline-compaction-tool","content":"For additional details about CompactionTool, see also CompactionTool."},"7138":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-225","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-clean","content":"The `hbase clean` command cleans HBase data from ZooKeeper, HDFS, or both. It is appropriate to use for testing. Run it with no options for usage instructions. The `hbase clean` command was introduced in HBase 0.98."},"7139":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-226","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-pe","content":"The `hbase pe` command runs the PerformanceEvaluation tool, which is used for testing."},"7140":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-227","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-pe","content":"The PerformanceEvaluation tool accepts many different options and commands. For usage instructions, run the command with no options."},"7141":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-228","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-pe","content":"The PerformanceEvaluation tool has received many updates in recent HBase releases, including support for namespaces, support for tags, cell-level ACLs and visibility labels, multiget support for RPC calls, increased sampling sizes, an option to randomly sleep during testing, and ability to \"warm up\" the cluster before testing starts."},"7142":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-229","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-ltt","content":"The `hbase ltt` command runs the LoadTestTool utility, which is used for testing."},"7143":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-230","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-ltt","content":"You must specify either `-init_only` or at least one of `-write`, `-update`, or `-read`. For general usage instructions, pass the `-h` option."},"7144":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-231","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hbase-ltt","content":"The LoadTestTool has received many updates in recent HBase releases, including support for namespaces, support for tags, cell-level ACLS and visibility labels, testing security-related features, ability to specify the number of regions per server, tests for multi-get RPC calls, and tests relating to replication."},"7145":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-232","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#pre-upgrade-validator","content":"Pre-Upgrade validator tool can be used to check the cluster for known incompatibilities before upgrading from HBase 1 to HBase 2."},"7146":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-233","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"HBase supports co-processors for a long time, but the co-processor API can be changed between major releases. Co-processor validator tries to determine whether the old co-processors are still compatible with the actual HBase version."},"7147":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-234","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"The co-processor classes can be explicitly declared by `-class` option, or they can be obtained from HBase configuration by `-config` option. Table level co-processors can be also checked by `-table` option. The tool searches for co-processors on its classpath, but it can be extended by the `-jar` option. It is possible to test multiple classes with multiple `-class`, multiple tables with multiple `-table` options as well as adding multiple jars to the classpath with multiple `-jar` options."},"7148":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-235","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"The tool can report errors and warnings. Errors mean that HBase won't be able to load the coprocessor, because it is incompatible with the current version of HBase. Warnings mean that the co-processors can be loaded, but they won't work as expected. If `-e` option is given, then the tool will also fail for warnings."},"7149":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-236","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"Please note that this tool cannot validate every aspect of jar files, it just does some static checks."},"7150":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-237","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"For example:"},"7151":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-238","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"It validates `MyMasterObserver` and `MyRegionObserver` classes which are located in `my-coprocessor.jar`."},"7152":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-239","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#coprocessor-validation","content":"It validates every table level co-processors where the table name matches to `.*` regular expression."},"7153":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-240","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#datablockencoding-validation","content":"HBase 2.0 removed `PREFIX_TREE` Data Block Encoding from column families. For further information please check *prefix-tree* encoding removed. To verify that none of the column families are using incompatible Data Block Encodings in the cluster run the following command."},"7154":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-241","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#datablockencoding-validation","content":"This check validates all column families and print out any incompatibilities. For example:"},"7155":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-242","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#datablockencoding-validation","content":"Which means that Data Block Encoding of table `t`, column family `f` is incompatible. To fix, use `alter` command in HBase shell:"},"7156":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-243","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#datablockencoding-validation","content":"Please also validate HFiles, which is described in the next section."},"7157":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-244","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hfile-content-validation","content":"Even though Data Block Encoding is changed from `PREFIX_TREE` it is still possible to have HFiles that contain data encoded that way. To verify that HFiles are readable with HBase 2 please use *HFile content validator*."},"7158":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-245","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hfile-content-validation","content":"The tool will log the corrupt HFiles and details about the root cause. If the problem is about PREFIX\\_TREE encoding it is necessary to change encodings before upgrading to HBase 2."},"7159":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-246","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#hfile-content-validation","content":"The following log message shows an example of incorrect HFiles."},"7160":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-247","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixing-prefix_tree-errors","content":"It's possible to get `PREFIX_TREE` errors after changing Data Block Encoding to a supported one. It can happen because there are some HFiles which still encoded with `PREFIX_TREE` or there are still some snapshots."},"7161":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-248","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixing-prefix_tree-errors","content":"For fixing HFiles, please run a major compaction on the table (it was `default:t` according to the log message):"},"7162":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-249","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixing-prefix_tree-errors","content":"HFiles can be referenced from snapshots, too. It's the case when the HFile is located under `archive/data`. The first step is to determine which snapshot references that HFile (the name of the file was `29c641ae91c34fc3bee881f45436b6d1` according to the logs):"},"7163":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-250","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixing-prefix_tree-errors","content":"The output of this shell script is:"},"7164":{"id":"/docs/operational-management/tools-251","page_id":"/docs/operational-management/tools","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/operational-management/tools#fixi