llocating HBase and MapReduce"},"7272":{"id":"/docs/performance-73","page_id":"/docs/performance","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/performance#performance-case-studies","content":"Case Studies"},"7273":{"id":"/docs/performance-74","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#memory","content":"RAM, RAM, RAM. Don't starve HBase."},"7274":{"id":"/docs/performance-75","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#64-bit","content":"Use a 64-bit platform (and 64-bit JVM)."},"7275":{"id":"/docs/performance-76","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#swapping","content":"Watch out for swapping. Set `swappiness` to 0."},"7276":{"id":"/docs/performance-77","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#cpu","content":"Make sure you have set up your Hadoop to use native, hardware checksumming. See hadoop.native.lib."},"7277":{"id":"/docs/performance-78","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network","content":"Perhaps the most important factor in avoiding network issues degrading Hadoop and HBase performance is the switching hardware that is used, decisions made early in the scope of the project can cause major problems when you double or triple the size of your cluster (or more)."},"7278":{"id":"/docs/performance-79","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network","content":"Important items to consider:"},"7279":{"id":"/docs/performance-80","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network","content":"Switching capacity of the device"},"7280":{"id":"/docs/performance-81","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network","content":"Number of systems connected"},"7281":{"id":"/docs/performance-82","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network","content":"Uplink capacity"},"7282":{"id":"/docs/performance-83","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#single-switch","content":"The single most important factor in this configuration is that the switching capacity of the hardware is capable of handling the traffic which can be generated by all systems connected to the switch. Some lower priced commodity hardware can have a slower switching capacity than could be utilized by a full switch."},"7283":{"id":"/docs/performance-84","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-switches","content":"Multiple switches are a potential pitfall in the architecture. The most common configuration of lower priced hardware is a simple 1Gbps uplink from one switch to another. This often overlooked pinch point can easily become a bottleneck for cluster communication. Especially with MapReduce jobs that are both reading and writing a lot of data the communication across this uplink could be saturated."},"7284":{"id":"/docs/performance-85","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-switches","content":"Mitigation of this issue is fairly simple and can be accomplished in multiple ways:"},"7285":{"id":"/docs/performance-86","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-switches","content":"Use appropriate hardware for the scale of the cluster which you're attempting to build."},"7286":{"id":"/docs/performance-87","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-switches","content":"Use larger single switch configurations i.e. single 48 port as opposed to 2x 24 port"},"7287":{"id":"/docs/performance-88","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-switches","content":"Configure port trunking for uplinks to utilize multiple interfaces to increase cross switch bandwidth."},"7288":{"id":"/docs/performance-89","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-racks","content":"Multiple rack configurations carry the same potential issues as multiple switches, and can suffer performance degradation from two main areas:"},"7289":{"id":"/docs/performance-90","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-racks","content":"Poor switch capacity performance"},"7290":{"id":"/docs/performance-91","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-racks","content":"Insufficient uplink to another rack"},"7291":{"id":"/docs/performance-92","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-racks","content":"If the switches in your rack have appropriate switching capacity to handle all the hosts at full speed, the next most likely issue will be caused by homing more of your cluster across racks. The easiest way to avoid issues when spanning multiple racks is to use port trunking to create a bonded uplink to other racks. The downside of this method however, is in the overhead of ports that could potentially be used. An example of this is, creating an 8Gbps port channel from rack A to rack B, using 8 of your 24 ports to communicate between racks gives you a poor ROI, using too few however can mean you're not getting the most out of your cluster."},"7292":{"id":"/docs/performance-93","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#multiple-racks","content":"Using 10Gbe links between racks will greatly increase performance, and assuming your switches support a 10Gbe uplink or allow for an expansion card will allow you to save your ports for machines as opposed to uplinks."},"7293":{"id":"/docs/performance-94","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-network-interfaces","content":"Are all the network interfaces functioning correctly? Are you sure? See the Troubleshooting Case Study in Case Study #1 (Performance Issue On A Single Node)."},"7294":{"id":"/docs/performance-95","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"The CAP Theorem states that a distributed system can maintain two out of the following three characteristics:"},"7295":{"id":"/docs/performance-96","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"***C***&#x6F;nsistency — all nodes see the same data."},"7296":{"id":"/docs/performance-97","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"***A***&#x76;ailability — every request receives a response about whether it succeeded or failed."},"7297":{"id":"/docs/performance-98","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"***P***&#x61;rtition tolerance — the system continues to operate even if some of its components become unavailable to the others."},"7298":{"id":"/docs/performance-99","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"HBase favors consistency and partition tolerance, where a decision has to be made. Coda Hale explains why partition tolerance is so important, in http\\://codahale.com/you-cant-sacrifice-partition-tolerance/."},"7299":{"id":"/docs/performance-100","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#network-consistency-and-partition-tolerance","content":"Robert Yokota used an automated testing framework called Jepson to test HBase's partition tolerance in the face of network partitions, using techniques modeled after Aphyr's Call Me Maybe series. The results, available as a blog post and an addendum, show that HBase performs correctly."},"7300":{"id":"/docs/performance-101","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"In his presentation, Avoiding Full GCs with MemStore-Local Allocation Buffers, Todd Lipcon describes two cases of stop-the-world garbage collections common in HBase, especially during loading; CMS failure modes and old generation heap fragmentation brought."},"7301":{"id":"/docs/performance-102","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"To address the first, start the CMS earlier than default by adding `-XX:CMSInitiatingOccupancyFraction` and setting it down from defaults. Start at 60 or 70 percent (The lower you bring down the threshold, the more GCing is done, the more CPU used). To address the second fragmentation issue, Todd added an experimental facility, (MSLAB), that must be explicitly enabled in Apache HBase 0.90.x (It's defaulted to be *on* in Apache 0.92.x HBase). Set `hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.enabled` to true in your `Configuration`. See the cited slides for background and detail. The latest JVMs do better regards fragmentation so make sure you are running a recent release. Read down in the message, Identifying concurrent mode failures caused by fragmentation. Be aware that when enabled, each MemStore instance will occupy at least an MSLAB instance of memory. If you have thousands of regions or lots of regions each with many column families, this allocation of MSLAB may be responsible for a good portion of your heap allocation and in an extreme case cause you to OOME. Disable MSLAB in this case, or lower the amount of memory it uses or float less regions per server."},"7302":{"id":"/docs/performance-103","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"If you have a write-heavy workload, check out HBASE-8163 MemStoreChunkPool: An improvement for JAVA GC when using MSLAB. It describes configurations to lower the amount of young GC during write-heavy loadings. If you do not have HBASE-8163 installed, and you are trying to improve your young GC times, one trick to consider — courtesy of our Liang Xie — is to set the GC config `-XX:PretenureSizeThreshold` in *hbase-env.sh* to be just smaller than the size of `hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.chunksize` so MSLAB allocations happen in the tenured space directly rather than first in the young gen. You'd do this because these MSLAB allocations are going to likely make it to the old gen anyways and rather than pay the price of a copies between s0 and s1 in eden space followed by the copy up from young to old gen after the MSLABs have achieved sufficient tenure, save a bit of YGC churn and allocate in the old gen directly."},"7303":{"id":"/docs/performance-104","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"Other sources of long GCs can be the JVM itself logging. See Eliminating Large JVM GC Pauses Caused by Background IO Traffic"},"7304":{"id":"/docs/performance-105","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"For more information about GC logs, see JVM Garbage Collection Logs."},"7305":{"id":"/docs/performance-106","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#long-gc-pauses","content":"Consider also enabling the off-heap Block Cache. This has been shown to mitigate GC pause times. See Block Cache"},"7306":{"id":"/docs/performance-107","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbase-configurations","content":"See Recommended Configurations."},"7307":{"id":"/docs/performance-108","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#improving-the-99th-percentile","content":"Try hedged\\_reads."},"7308":{"id":"/docs/performance-109","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#managing-compactions","content":"For larger systems, managing compactions and splits may be something you want to consider."},"7309":{"id":"/docs/performance-110","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-hbaseregionserverhandlercount","content":"See hbase.regionserver.handler.count."},"7310":{"id":"/docs/performance-111","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hfileblockcachesize","content":"See hfile.block.cache.size. A memory setting for the RegionServer process."},"7311":{"id":"/docs/performance-112","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"HBASE-9857 adds a new option to prefetch HFile contents when opening the BlockCache, if a Column family or RegionServer property is set. This option is available for HBase 0.98.3 and later. The purpose is to warm the BlockCache as rapidly as possible after the cache is opened, using in-memory table data, and not counting the prefetching as cache misses. This is great for fast reads, but is not a good idea if the data to be preloaded will not fit into the BlockCache. It is useful for tuning the IO impact of prefetching versus the time before all data blocks are in cache."},"7312":{"id":"/docs/performance-113","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"To enable prefetching on a given column family, you can use HBase Shell or use the API."},"7313":{"id":"/docs/performance-114","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"**Enable Prefetch Using HBase Shell**"},"7314":{"id":"/docs/performance-115","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"**Enable Prefetch Using the API**"},"7315":{"id":"/docs/performance-116","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"See the API documentation for CacheConfig."},"7316":{"id":"/docs/performance-117","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#prefetch-option-for-blockcache","content":"To see prefetch in operation, enable TRACE level logging on `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.io.hfile.HFileReaderImpl` in hbase-2.0+ or on `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.io.hfile.HFileReaderV2` in earlier versions, hbase-1.x, of HBase."},"7317":{"id":"/docs/performance-118","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbaseregionserverglobalmemstoresize","content":"See hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size. This memory setting is often adjusted for the RegionServer process depending on needs."},"7318":{"id":"/docs/performance-119","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbaseregionserverglobalmemstoresizelowerlimit","content":"See hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size.lower.limit. This memory setting is often adjusted for the RegionServer process depending on needs."},"7319":{"id":"/docs/performance-120","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbasehstoreblockingstorefiles","content":"See hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles. If there is blocking in the RegionServer logs, increasing this can help."},"7320":{"id":"/docs/performance-121","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbasehregionmemstoreblockmultiplier","content":"See hbase.hregion.memstore.block.multiplier. If there is enough RAM, increasing this can help."},"7321":{"id":"/docs/performance-122","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbaseregionserverchecksumverify","content":"Have HBase write the checksum into the datablock and save having to do the checksum seek whenever you read."},"7322":{"id":"/docs/performance-123","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbaseregionserverchecksumverify","content":"See hbase.regionserver.checksum.verify, hbase.hstore.bytes.per.checksum and hbase.hstore.checksum.algorithm. For more information see the release note on HBASE-5074 support checksums in HBase block cache."},"7323":{"id":"/docs/performance-124","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"HBASE-11355 introduces several callQueue tuning mechanisms which can increase performance. See the JIRA for some benchmarking information."},"7324":{"id":"/docs/performance-125","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"To increase the number of callqueues, set `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` to a value greater than `1`. To split the callqueue into separate read and write queues, set `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio` to a value between `0` and `1`. This factor weights the queues toward writes (if below .5) or reads (if above .5). Another way to say this is that the factor determines what percentage of the split queues are used for reads. The following examples illustrate some of the possibilities. Note that you always have at least one write queue, no matter what setting you use."},"7325":{"id":"/docs/performance-126","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"The default value of `0` does not split the queue."},"7326":{"id":"/docs/performance-127","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.3` uses 30% of the queues for reading and 70% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 3 queues would be used for reads and 7 for writes."},"7327":{"id":"/docs/performance-128","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.5` uses the same number of read queues and write queues. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 5 queues would be used for reads and 5 for writes."},"7328":{"id":"/docs/performance-129","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.6` uses 60% of the queues for reading and 40% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 6 queues would be used for reads and 4 for writes."},"7329":{"id":"/docs/performance-130","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `1.0` uses one queue to process write requests, and all other queues process read requests. A value higher than `1.0` has the same effect as a value of `1.0`. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 9 queues would be used for reads and 1 for writes."},"7330":{"id":"/docs/performance-131","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"You can also split the read queues so that separate queues are used for short reads (from Get operations) and long reads (from Scan operations), by setting the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.scan.ratio` option. This option is a factor between 0 and 1, which determine the ratio of read queues used for Gets and Scans. More queues are used for Gets if the value is below `.5` and more are used for scans if the value is above `.5`. No matter what setting you use, at least one read queue is used for Get operations."},"7331":{"id":"/docs/performance-132","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `0` does not split the read queue."},"7332":{"id":"/docs/performance-133","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.3` uses 70% of the read queues for Gets and 30% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 7 would be used for Gets and 3 for Scans."},"7333":{"id":"/docs/performance-134","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.5` uses half the read queues for Gets and half for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 5 would be used for Gets and 5 for Scans."},"7334":{"id":"/docs/performance-135","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `.7` uses 30% of the read queues for Gets and 70% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 3 would be used for Gets and 7 for Scans."},"7335":{"id":"/docs/performance-136","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `1.0` uses all but one of the read queues for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 1 would be used for Gets and 9 for Scans."},"7336":{"id":"/docs/performance-137","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"You can use the new option `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor` to programmatically tune the number of queues:"},"7337":{"id":"/docs/performance-138","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `0` uses a single shared queue between all the handlers."},"7338":{"id":"/docs/performance-139","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value of `1` uses a separate queue for each handler."},"7339":{"id":"/docs/performance-140","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"A value between `0` and `1` tunes the number of queues against the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers.\\\nHaving more queues, such as in a situation where you have one queue per handler, reduces contention when adding a task to a queue or selecting it from a queue. The trade-off is that if you have some queues with long-running tasks, a handler may end up waiting to execute from that queue rather than processing another queue which has waiting tasks."},"7340":{"id":"/docs/performance-141","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#tuning-callqueue-options","content":"For these values to take effect on a given RegionServer, the RegionServer must be restarted. These parameters are intended for testing purposes and should be used carefully."},"7341":{"id":"/docs/performance-142","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-zookeeper","content":"See ZooKeeper for information on configuring ZooKeeper, and see the part about having a dedicated disk."},"7342":{"id":"/docs/performance-143","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#number-of-column-families","content":"See On the number of column families."},"7343":{"id":"/docs/performance-144","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#key-and-attribute-lengths","content":"See Try to minimize row and column sizes. See also However... for compression caveats."},"7344":{"id":"/docs/performance-145","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-regionsize","content":"The regionsize can be set on a per-table basis via `setMaxFileSize` on TableDescriptorBuilder in the event where certain tables require different regionsizes than the configured default regionsize."},"7345":{"id":"/docs/performance-146","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-regionsize","content":"See Determining region count and size for more information."},"7346":{"id":"/docs/performance-147","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"A Bloom filter, named for its creator, Burton Howard Bloom, is a data structure which is designed to predict whether a given element is a member of a set of data. A positive result from a Bloom filter is not always accurate, but a negative result is guaranteed to be accurate. Bloom filters are designed to be \"accurate enough\" for sets of data which are so large that conventional hashing mechanisms would be impractical. For more information about Bloom filters in general, refer to http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloom\\_filter."},"7347":{"id":"/docs/performance-148","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"In terms of HBase, Bloom filters provide a lightweight in-memory structure to reduce the number of disk reads for a given Get operation (Bloom filters do not work with Scans) to only the StoreFiles likely to contain the desired Row. The potential performance gain increases with the number of parallel reads."},"7348":{"id":"/docs/performance-149","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"The Bloom filters themselves are stored in the metadata of each HFile and never need to be updated. When an HFile is opened because a region is deployed to a RegionServer, the Bloom filter is loaded into memory."},"7349":{"id":"/docs/performance-150","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"HBase includes some tuning mechanisms for folding the Bloom filter to reduce the size and keep the false positive rate within a desired range."},"7350":{"id":"/docs/performance-151","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"Bloom filters were introduced in HBASE-1200. Since HBase 0.96, row-based Bloom filters are enabled by default. (HBASE-8450)"},"7351":{"id":"/docs/performance-152","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters","content":"For more information on Bloom filters in relation to HBase, see Bloom Filters for more information, or the following Quora discussion: How are bloom filters used in HBase?."},"7352":{"id":"/docs/performance-153","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#when-to-use-bloom-filters","content":"Since HBase 0.96, row-based Bloom filters are enabled by default. You may choose to disable them or to change some tables to use row+column Bloom filters, depending on the characteristics of your data and how it is loaded into HBase."},"7353":{"id":"/docs/performance-154","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#when-to-use-bloom-filters","content":"To determine whether Bloom filters could have a positive impact, check the value of `blockCacheHitRatio` in the RegionServer metrics. If Bloom filters are enabled, the value of `blockCacheHitRatio` should increase, because the Bloom filter is filtering out blocks that are definitely not needed."},"7354":{"id":"/docs/performance-155","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#when-to-use-bloom-filters","content":"You can choose to enable Bloom filters for a row or for a row+column combination. If you generally scan entire rows, the row+column combination will not provide any benefit. A row-based Bloom filter can operate on a row+column Get, but not the other way around. However, if you have a large number of column-level Puts, such that a row may be present in every StoreFile, a row-based filter will always return a positive result and provide no benefit. Unless you have one column per row, row+column Bloom filters require more space, in order to store more keys. Bloom filters work best when the size of each data entry is at least a few kilobytes in size."},"7355":{"id":"/docs/performance-156","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#when-to-use-bloom-filters","content":"Overhead will be reduced when your data is stored in a few larger StoreFiles, to avoid extra disk IO during low-level scans to find a specific row."},"7356":{"id":"/docs/performance-157","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#when-to-use-bloom-filters","content":"Bloom filters need to be rebuilt upon deletion, so may not be appropriate in environments with a large number of deletions."},"7357":{"id":"/docs/performance-158","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#enabling-bloom-filters","content":"Bloom filters are enabled on a Column Family. You can do this by using the setBloomFilterType method of HColumnDescriptor or using the HBase API. Valid values are `NONE`, `ROW` (default), or `ROWCOL`. See When To Use Bloom Filters for more information on `ROW` versus `ROWCOL`. See also the API documentation for ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."},"7358":{"id":"/docs/performance-159","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#enabling-bloom-filters","content":"The following example creates a table and enables a ROWCOL Bloom filter on the `colfam1` column family."},"7359":{"id":"/docs/performance-160","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"You can configure the following settings in the *hbase-site.xml*."},"7360":{"id":"/docs/performance-161","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Parameter"},"7361":{"id":"/docs/performance-162","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Default"},"7362":{"id":"/docs/performance-163","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Description"},"7363":{"id":"/docs/performance-164","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.bloom.enabled"},"7364":{"id":"/docs/performance-165","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"yes"},"7365":{"id":"/docs/performance-166","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Set to no to kill bloom filters server-wide if something goes wrong"},"7366":{"id":"/docs/performance-167","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.bloom.error.rate"},"7367":{"id":"/docs/performance-168","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":".01"},"7368":{"id":"/docs/performance-169","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"The average false positive rate for bloom filters. Folding is used to maintain the false positive rate. Expressed as a decimal representation of a percentage."},"7369":{"id":"/docs/performance-170","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.bloom.max.fold"},"7370":{"id":"/docs/performance-171","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"7"},"7371":{"id":"/docs/performance-172","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"The guaranteed maximum fold rate. Changing this setting should not be necessary and is not recommended."},"7372":{"id":"/docs/performance-173","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.bloom.max.keys"},"7373":{"id":"/docs/performance-174","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"128000000"},"7374":{"id":"/docs/performance-175","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"For default (single-block) Bloom filters, this specifies the maximum number of keys."},"7375":{"id":"/docs/performance-176","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.delete.family.bloom.enabled"},"7376":{"id":"/docs/performance-177","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"true"},"7377":{"id":"/docs/performance-178","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Master switch to enable Delete Family Bloom filters and store them in the StoreFile."},"7378":{"id":"/docs/performance-179","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"io.storefile.bloom.block.size"},"7379":{"id":"/docs/performance-180","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"131072"},"7380":{"id":"/docs/performance-181","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Target Bloom block size. Bloom filter blocks of approximately this size are interleaved with data blocks."},"7381":{"id":"/docs/performance-182","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"hfile.block.bloom.cacheonwrite"},"7382":{"id":"/docs/performance-183","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"false"},"7383":{"id":"/docs/performance-184","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#configuring-server-wide-behavior-of-bloom-filters","content":"Enables cache-on-write for inline blocks of a compound Bloom filter."},"7384":{"id":"/docs/performance-185","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#columnfamily-blocksize","content":"The blocksize can be configured for each ColumnFamily in a table, and defaults to 64k. Larger cell values require larger blocksizes. There is an inverse relationship between blocksize and the resulting StoreFile indexes (i.e., if the blocksize is doubled then the resulting indexes should be roughly halved)."},"7385":{"id":"/docs/performance-186","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#columnfamily-blocksize","content":"See ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder and Store for more information."},"7386":{"id":"/docs/performance-187","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#in-memory-columnfamilies","content":"ColumnFamilies can optionally be defined as in-memory. Data is still persisted to disk, just like any other ColumnFamily. In-memory blocks have the highest priority in the Block Cache, but it is not a guarantee that the entire table will be in memory."},"7387":{"id":"/docs/performance-188","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#in-memory-columnfamilies","content":"See ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder for more information."},"7388":{"id":"/docs/performance-189","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-schema-design-compression","content":"Production systems should use compression with their ColumnFamily definitions. See Compression and Data Block Encoding In HBase for more information."},"7389":{"id":"/docs/performance-190","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#however","content":"Compression deflates data *on disk*. When it's in-memory (e.g., in the MemStore) or on the wire (e.g., transferring between RegionServer and Client) it's inflated. So while using ColumnFamily compression is a best practice, but it's not going to completely eliminate the impact of over-sized Keys, over-sized ColumnFamily names, or over-sized Column names."},"7390":{"id":"/docs/performance-191","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#however","content":"See Try to minimize row and column sizes on for schema design tips, and KeyValue for more information on HBase stores data internally."},"7391":{"id":"/docs/performance-192","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#constants","content":"When people get started with HBase they have a tendency to write code that looks like this:"},"7392":{"id":"/docs/performance-193","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#constants","content":"But especially when inside loops (and MapReduce jobs), converting the columnFamily and column-names to byte-arrays repeatedly is surprisingly expensive. It's better to use constants for the byte-arrays, like this:"},"7393":{"id":"/docs/performance-194","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#batch-loading","content":"Use the bulk load tool if you can. See Bulk Loading. Otherwise, pay attention to the below."},"7394":{"id":"/docs/performance-195","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-pre-creating-regions","content":"Tables in HBase are initially created with one region by default. For bulk imports, this means that all clients will write to the same region until it is large enough to split and become distributed across the cluster. A useful pattern to speed up the bulk import process is to pre-create empty regions. Be somewhat conservative in this, because too-many regions can actually degrade performance."},"7395":{"id":"/docs/performance-196","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-pre-creating-regions","content":"There are two different approaches to pre-creating splits using the HBase API. The first approach is to rely on the default `Admin` strategy (which is implemented in `Bytes.split`)..."},"7396":{"id":"/docs/performance-197","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-pre-creating-regions","content":"And the other approach, using the HBase API, is to define the splits yourself..."},"7397":{"id":"/docs/performance-198","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-pre-creating-regions","content":"You can achieve a similar effect using the HBase Shell to create tables by specifying split options."},"7398":{"id":"/docs/performance-199","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-pre-creating-regions","content":"See Relationship Between RowKeys and Region Splits for issues related to understanding your keyspace and pre-creating regions. See manual region splitting decisions for discussion on manually pre-splitting regions. See Pre-splitting tables with the HBase Shell for more details of using the HBase Shell to pre-split tables."},"7399":{"id":"/docs/performance-200","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-deferred-log-flush","content":"The default behavior for Puts using the Write Ahead Log (WAL) is that `WAL` edits will be written immediately. If deferred log flush is used, WAL edits are kept in memory until the flush period. The benefit is aggregated and asynchronous `WAL`- writes, but the potential downside is that if the RegionServer goes down the yet-to-be-flushed edits are lost. This is safer, however, than not using WAL at all with Puts."},"7400":{"id":"/docs/performance-201","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#table-creation-deferred-log-flush","content":"Deferred log flush can be configured on tables via TableDescriptorBuilder. The default value of `hbase.regionserver.optionallogflushinterval` is 1000ms."},"7401":{"id":"/docs/performance-202","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbase-client-turn-off-wal-on-puts","content":"A frequent request is to disable the WAL to increase performance of Puts. This is only appropriate for bulk loads, as it puts your data at risk by removing the protection of the WAL in the event of a region server crash. Bulk loads can be re-run in the event of a crash, with little risk of data loss."},"7402":{"id":"/docs/performance-203","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbase-client-turn-off-wal-on-puts","content":"If you disable the WAL for anything other than bulk loads, your data is at risk."},"7403":{"id":"/docs/performance-204","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbase-client-turn-off-wal-on-puts","content":"In general, it is best to use WAL for Puts, and where loading throughput is a concern to use bulk loading techniques instead. For normal Puts, you are not likely to see a performance improvement which would outweigh the risk. To disable the WAL, see Disabling the WAL."},"7404":{"id":"/docs/performance-205","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hbase-client-group-puts-by-regionserver","content":"In addition to using the writeBuffer, grouping `Put`s by RegionServer can reduce the number of client RPC calls per writeBuffer flush. There is a utility `HTableUtil` currently on MASTER that does this, but you can either copy that or implement your own version for those still on 0.90.x or earlier."},"7405":{"id":"/docs/performance-206","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#mapreduce-skip-the-reducer","content":"When writing a lot of data to an HBase table from a MR job (e.g., with TableOutputFormat), and specifically where Puts are being emitted from the Mapper, skip the Reducer step. When a Reducer step is used, all of the output (Puts) from the Mapper will get spooled to disk, then sorted/shuffled to other Reducers that will most likely be off-node. It's far more efficient to just write directly to HBase."},"7406":{"id":"/docs/performance-207","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#mapreduce-skip-the-reducer","content":"For summary jobs where HBase is used as a source and a sink, then writes will be coming from the Reducer step (e.g., summarize values then write out result). This is a different processing problem than from the above case."},"7407":{"id":"/docs/performance-208","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#anti-pattern-one-hot-region","content":"If all your data is being written to one region at a time, then re-read the section on processing timeseries data."},"7408":{"id":"/docs/performance-209","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#anti-pattern-one-hot-region","content":"Also, if you are pre-splitting regions and all your data is *still* winding up in a single region even though your keys aren't monotonically increasing, confirm that your keyspace actually works with the split strategy. There are a variety of reasons that regions may appear \"well split\" but won't work with your data. As the HBase client communicates directly with the RegionServers, this can be obtained via RegionLocator.getRegionLocation."},"7409":{"id":"/docs/performance-210","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#anti-pattern-one-hot-region","content":"See Table Creation: Pre-Creating Regions, as well as HBase Configurations"},"7410":{"id":"/docs/performance-211","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#reading-from-hbase","content":"The mailing list can help if you are having performance issues."},"7411":{"id":"/docs/performance-212","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#scan-caching","content":"If HBase is used as an input source for a MapReduce job, for example, make sure that the input Scan instance to the MapReduce job has `setCaching` set to something greater than the default (which is 1). Using the default value means that the map-task will make call back to the region-server for every record processed. Setting this value to 500, for example, will transfer 500 rows at a time to the client to be processed. There is a cost/benefit to have the cache value be large because it costs more in memory for both client and RegionServer, so bigger isn't always better."},"7412":{"id":"/docs/performance-213","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#scan-caching-in-mapreduce-jobs","content":"Scan settings in MapReduce jobs deserve special attention. Timeouts can result (e.g., UnknownScannerException) in Map tasks if it takes longer to process a batch of records before the client goes back to the RegionServer for the next set of data. This problem can occur because there is non-trivial processing occurring per row. If you process rows quickly, set caching higher. If you process rows more slowly (e.g., lots of transformations per row, writes), then set caching lower."},"7413":{"id":"/docs/performance-214","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#scan-caching-in-mapreduce-jobs","content":"Timeouts can also happen in a non-MapReduce use case (i.e., single threaded HBase client doing a Scan), but the processing that is often performed in MapReduce jobs tends to exacerbate this issue."},"7414":{"id":"/docs/performance-215","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#scan-attribute-selection","content":"Whenever a Scan is used to process large numbers of rows (and especially when used as a MapReduce source), be aware of which attributes are selected. If `scan.addFamily` is called then *all* of the attributes in the specified ColumnFamily will be returned to the client. If only a small number of the available attributes are to be processed, then only those attributes should be specified in the input scan because attribute over-selection is a non-trivial performance penalty over large datasets."},"7415":{"id":"/docs/performance-216","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#avoid-scan-seeks","content":"When columns are selected explicitly with `scan.addColumn`, HBase will schedule seek operations to seek between the selected columns. When rows have few columns and each column has only a few versions this can be inefficient. A seek operation is generally slower if does not seek at least past 5-10 columns/versions or 512-1024 bytes."},"7416":{"id":"/docs/performance-217","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#avoid-scan-seeks","content":"In order to opportunistically look ahead a few columns/versions to see if the next column/version can be found that way before a seek operation is scheduled, a new attribute `Scan.HINT_LOOKAHEAD` can be set on the Scan object. The following code instructs the RegionServer to attempt two iterations of next before a seek is scheduled:"},"7417":{"id":"/docs/performance-218","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#mapreduce---input-splits","content":"For MapReduce jobs that use HBase tables as a source, if there a pattern where the \"slow\" map tasks seem to have the same Input Split (i.e., the RegionServer serving the data), see the Troubleshooting Case Study in Case Study #1 (Performance Issue On A Single Node)."},"7418":{"id":"/docs/performance-219","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#close-resultscanners","content":"This isn't so much about improving performance but rather *avoiding* performance problems. If you forget to close ResultScanners you can cause problems on the RegionServers. Always have ResultScanner processing enclosed in try/catch blocks."},"7419":{"id":"/docs/performance-220","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-reading-from-hbase-block-cache","content":"Scan instances can be set to use the block cache in the RegionServer via the `setCacheBlocks` method. For input Scans to MapReduce jobs, this should be `false`. For frequently accessed rows, it is advisable to use the block cache."},"7420":{"id":"/docs/performance-221","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-reading-from-hbase-block-cache","content":"Cache more data by moving your Block Cache off-heap. See Off-heap Block Cache"},"7421":{"id":"/docs/performance-222","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#optimal-loading-of-row-keys","content":"When performing a table scan where only the row keys are needed (no families, qualifiers, values or timestamps), add a FilterList with a `MUST_PASS_ALL` operator to the scanner using `setFilter`. The filter list should include both a FirstKeyOnlyFilter and a KeyOnlyFilter. Using this filter combination will result in a worst case scenario of a RegionServer reading a single value from disk and minimal network traffic to the client for a single row."},"7422":{"id":"/docs/performance-223","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#concurrency-monitor-data-spread","content":"When performing a high number of concurrent reads, monitor the data spread of the target tables. If the target table(s) have too few regions then the reads could likely be served from too few nodes."},"7423":{"id":"/docs/performance-224","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#concurrency-monitor-data-spread","content":"See Table Creation: Pre-Creating Regions, as well as HBase Configurations"},"7424":{"id":"/docs/performance-225","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters-1","content":"Enabling Bloom Filters can save your having to go to disk and can help improve read latencies."},"7425":{"id":"/docs/performance-226","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters-1","content":"Bloom filters were developed over in HBase-1200 Add bloomfilters. For description of the development process — why static blooms rather than dynamic — and for an overview of the unique properties that pertain to blooms in HBase, as well as possible future directions, see the *Development Process* section of the document BloomFilters in HBase attached to HBASE-1200. The bloom filters described here are actually version two of blooms in HBase. In versions up to 0.19.x, HBase had a dynamic bloom option based on work done by the European Commission One-Lab Project 034819. The core of the HBase bloom work was later pulled up into Hadoop to implement org.apache.hadoop.io.BloomMapFile. Version 1 of HBase blooms never worked that well. Version 2 is a rewrite from scratch though again it starts with the one-lab work."},"7426":{"id":"/docs/performance-227","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filters-1","content":"See also Bloom Filters."},"7427":{"id":"/docs/performance-228","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-storefile-footprint","content":"Bloom filters add an entry to the `StoreFile` general `FileInfo` data structure and then two extra entries to the `StoreFile` metadata section."},"7428":{"id":"/docs/performance-229","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-storefile-footprint","content":"**BloomFilter in the `StoreFile` `FileInfo` data structure**\\\n`FileInfo` has a `BLOOM_FILTER_TYPE` entry which is set to `NONE`, `ROW` or `ROWCOL.`"},"7429":{"id":"/docs/performance-230","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-storefile-footprint","content":"**BloomFilter entries in `StoreFile` metadata**\\\n`BLOOM_FILTER_META` holds Bloom Size, Hash Function used, etc. It's small in size and is cached on `StoreFile.Reader` load.\\\n`BLOOM_FILTER_DATA` is the actual bloomfilter data. Obtained on-demand. Stored in the LRU cache, if it is enabled (It's enabled by default)."},"7430":{"id":"/docs/performance-231","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filter-configuration","content":"**`io.storefile.bloom.enabled` global kill switch**\\\n`io.storefile.bloom.enabled` in `Configuration` serves as the kill switch in case something goes wrong. Default = `true`."},"7431":{"id":"/docs/performance-232","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filter-configuration","content":"`io.storefile.bloom.error.rate`\\\n`io.storefile.bloom.error.rate` = average false positive rate. Default = 1%. Decrease rate by ½ (e.g. to .5%) == +1 bit per bloom entry."},"7432":{"id":"/docs/performance-233","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#bloom-filter-configuration","content":"`io.storefile.bloom.max.fold`\\\n`io.storefile.bloom.max.fold` = guaranteed minimum fold rate. Most people should leave this alone. Default = 7, or can collapse to at least 1/128th of original size. See the *Development Process* section of the document BloomFilters in HBase for more on what this option means."},"7433":{"id":"/docs/performance-234","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Hedged reads are a feature of HDFS, introduced in Hadoop 2.4.0 with HDFS-5776. Normally, a single thread is spawned for each read request. However, if hedged reads are enabled, the client waits some configurable amount of time, and if the read does not return, the client spawns a second read request, against a different block replica of the same data. Whichever read returns first is used, and the other read request is discarded."},"7434":{"id":"/docs/performance-235","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Hedged reads are \"...very good at eliminating outlier datanodes, which in turn makes them very good choice for latency sensitive setups. But, if you are looking for maximizing throughput, hedged reads tend to create load amplification as things get slower in general. In short, the thing to watch out for is the non-graceful performance degradation when you are running close a certain throughput threshold.\" (Quote from Ashu Pachauri in HBASE-17083)."},"7435":{"id":"/docs/performance-236","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Other concerns to keep in mind while running with hedged reads enabled include:"},"7436":{"id":"/docs/performance-237","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"They may lead to network congestion. See HBASE-17083"},"7437":{"id":"/docs/performance-238","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Make sure you set the thread pool large enough so as blocking on the pool does not become a bottleneck (Again see HBASE-17083)"},"7438":{"id":"/docs/performance-239","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"(From Yu Li up in HBASE-17083)"},"7439":{"id":"/docs/performance-240","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Because an HBase RegionServer is a HDFS client, you can enable hedged reads in HBase, by adding the following properties to the RegionServer's hbase-site.xml and tuning the values to suit your environment."},"7440":{"id":"/docs/performance-241","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"**Configuration for Hedged Reads**"},"7441":{"id":"/docs/performance-242","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"`dfs.client.hedged.read.threadpool.size` - the number of threads dedicated to servicing hedged reads. If this is set to 0 (the default), hedged reads are disabled."},"7442":{"id":"/docs/performance-243","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"`dfs.client.hedged.read.threshold.millis` - the number of milliseconds to wait before spawning a second read thread."},"7443":{"id":"/docs/performance-244","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"**Hedged Reads Configuration Example**"},"7444":{"id":"/docs/performance-245","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"Use the following metrics to tune the settings for hedged reads on your cluster. See HBase Metrics for more information."},"7445":{"id":"/docs/performance-246","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"**Metrics for Hedged Reads**"},"7446":{"id":"/docs/performance-247","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"hedgedReadOps - the number of times hedged read threads have been triggered. This could indicate that read requests are often slow, or that hedged reads are triggered too quickly."},"7447":{"id":"/docs/performance-248","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"hedgeReadOpsWin - the number of times the hedged read thread was faster than the original thread. This could indicate that a given RegionServer is having trouble servicing requests."},"7448":{"id":"/docs/performance-249","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#hedged-reads","content":"hedgedReadOpsInCurThread - the number of times hedged read was rejected from executor and needed to fallback to be executed in current thread. This could indicate that current hedged read thread pool size is not appropriate."},"7449":{"id":"/docs/performance-250","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#using-hbase-tables-as-queues","content":"HBase tables are sometimes used as queues. In this case, special care must be taken to regularly perform major compactions on tables used in this manner. As is documented in Data Model, marking rows as deleted creates additional StoreFiles which then need to be processed on reads. Tombstones only get cleaned up with major compactions."},"7450":{"id":"/docs/performance-251","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#using-hbase-tables-as-queues","content":"See also Compaction and Admin.majorCompact."},"7451":{"id":"/docs/performance-252","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#delete-rpc-behavior","content":"Be aware that `Table.delete(Delete)` doesn't use the writeBuffer. It will execute an RegionServer RPC with each invocation. For a large number of deletes, consider `Table.delete(List)`."},"7452":{"id":"/docs/performance-253","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#delete-rpc-behavior","content":"See hbase.client.Delete"},"7453":{"id":"/docs/performance-254","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-hdfs","content":"Because HBase runs on HDFS it is important to understand how it works and how it affects HBase."},"7454":{"id":"/docs/performance-255","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#current-issues-with-low-latency-reads","content":"The original use-case for HDFS was batch processing. As such, there low-latency reads were historically not a priority. With the increased adoption of Apache HBase this is changing, and several improvements are already in development. See the Umbrella Jira Ticket for HDFS Improvements for HBase."},"7455":{"id":"/docs/performance-256","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"Since Hadoop 1.0.0 (also 0.22.1, 0.23.1, CDH3u3 and HDP 1.0) via HDFS-2246, it is possible for the DFSClient to take a \"short circuit\" and read directly from the disk instead of going through the DataNode when the data is local. What this means for HBase is that the RegionServers can read directly off their machine's disks instead of having to open a socket to talk to the DataNode, the former being generally much faster. See JD's Performance Talk. Also see HBase, mail # dev - read short circuit thread for more discussion around short circuit reads."},"7456":{"id":"/docs/performance-257","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"To enable \"short circuit\" reads, it will depend on your version of Hadoop. The original shortcircuit read patch was much improved upon in Hadoop 2 in HDFS-347. See http\\://blog.cloudera.com/blog/2013/08/how-improved-short-circuit-local-reads-bring-better-performance-and-security-to-hadoop/ for details on the difference between the old and new implementations. See Hadoop shortcircuit reads configuration page for how to enable the latter, better version of shortcircuit. For example, here is a minimal config. enabling short-circuit reads added to *hbase-site.xml*:"},"7457":{"id":"/docs/performance-258","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"Be careful about permissions for the directory that hosts the shared domain socket; dfsclient will complain if open to other than the hbase user."},"7458":{"id":"/docs/performance-259","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"If you are running on an old Hadoop, one that is without HDFS-347 but that has HDFS-2246, you must set two configurations. First, the hdfs-site.xml needs to be amended. Set the property `dfs.block.local-path-access.user` to be the *only* user that can use the shortcut. This has to be the user that started HBase. Then in hbase-site.xml, set `dfs.client.read.shortcircuit` to be `true`"},"7459":{"id":"/docs/performance-260","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"Services — at least the HBase RegionServers — will need to be restarted in order to pick up the new configurations."},"7460":{"id":"/docs/performance-261","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#leveraging-local-data","content":"The default for this value is too high when running on a highly trafficked HBase. In HBase, if\nthis value has not been set, we set it down from the default of 1M to 128k (Since HBase 0.98.0 and\n0.96.1). See HBASE-8143 HBase on Hadoop 2 with local short circuit reads (ssr) causes\nOOM. The Hadoop DFSClient in HBase will\nallocate a direct byte buffer of this size for *each* block it has open; given HBase keeps its\nHDFS files open all the time, this can add up quickly."},"7461":{"id":"/docs/performance-262","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-comparisons-of-hbase-vs-hdfs","content":"A fairly common question on the dist-list is why HBase isn't as performant as HDFS files in a batch context (e.g., as a MapReduce source or sink). The short answer is that HBase is doing a lot more than HDFS (e.g., reading the KeyValues, returning the most current row or specified timestamps, etc.), and as such HBase is 4-5 times slower than HDFS in this processing context. There is room for improvement and this gap will, over time, be reduced, but HDFS will always be faster in this use-case."},"7462":{"id":"/docs/performance-263","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-amazon-ec2","content":"Performance questions are common on Amazon EC2 environments because it is a shared environment. You will not see the same throughput as a dedicated server. In terms of running tests on EC2, run them several times for the same reason (i.e., it's a shared environment and you don't know what else is happening on the server)."},"7463":{"id":"/docs/performance-264","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-amazon-ec2","content":"If you are running on EC2 and post performance questions on the dist-list, please state this fact up-front that because EC2 issues are practically a separate class of performance issues."},"7464":{"id":"/docs/performance-265","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#collocating-hbase-and-mapreduce","content":"It is often recommended to have different clusters for HBase and MapReduce. A better qualification of this is: don't collocate an HBase that serves live requests with a heavy MR workload. OLTP and OLAP-optimized systems have conflicting requirements and one will lose to the other, usually the former. For example, short latency-sensitive disk reads will have to wait in line behind longer reads that are trying to squeeze out as much throughput as possible. MR jobs that write to HBase will also generate flushes and compactions, which will in turn invalidate blocks in the Block Cache."},"7465":{"id":"/docs/performance-266","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#collocating-hbase-and-mapreduce","content":"If you need to process the data from your live HBase cluster in MR, you can ship the deltas with CopyTable or use replication to get the new data in real time on the OLAP cluster. In the worst case, if you really need to collocate both, set MR to use less Map and Reduce slots than you'd normally configure, possibly just one."},"7466":{"id":"/docs/performance-267","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#collocating-hbase-and-mapreduce","content":"When HBase is used for OLAP operations, it's preferable to set it up in a hardened way like configuring the ZooKeeper session timeout higher and giving more memory to the MemStores (the argument being that the Block Cache won't be used much since the workloads are usually long scans)."},"7467":{"id":"/docs/performance-268","page_id":"/docs/performance","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/performance#performance-case-studies","content":"For Performance and Troubleshooting Case Studies, see Apache HBase Case Studies."},"7468":{"id":"/docs/profiler","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"page","content":"Profiler Servlet","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Performance & Troubleshooting"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler"},"7469":{"id":"/docs/profiler-0","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler","content":"Integrated, on-demand profiling via the Async Profiler project."},"7470":{"id":"/docs/profiler-1","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-background","content":"Background"},"7471":{"id":"/docs/profiler-2","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"7472":{"id":"/docs/profiler-3","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Usage"},"7473":{"id":"/docs/profiler-4","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-ui","content":"UI"},"7474":{"id":"/docs/profiler-5","page_id":"/docs/profiler","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-notes","content":"Notes"},"7475":{"id":"/docs/profiler-6","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-background","content":"HBASE-21926 introduced a new servlet that\nsupports integrated, on-demand profiling via the\nAsync Profiler project."},"7476":{"id":"/docs/profiler-7","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-prerequisites","content":"Go to the Async Profiler Home Page, download\na release appropriate for your platform, and install on every cluster host. If running a Linux\nkernel v4.6 or later, be sure to set proc variables as per the\nBasic Usage section. Not doing\nso will result in flame graphs that contain no content."},"7477":{"id":"/docs/profiler-8","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-prerequisites","content":"Set `ASYNC_PROFILER_HOME` in the environment (put it in hbase-env.sh) to the root directory of the\nasync-profiler install location, or pass it on the HBase daemon's command line as a system property\nas `-Dasync.profiler.home=/path/to/async-profiler`."},"7478":{"id":"/docs/profiler-9","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Once the prerequisites are satisfied, access to async-profiler is available by way of the HBase UI\nor direct interaction with the infoserver."},"7479":{"id":"/docs/profiler-10","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Examples:"},"7480":{"id":"/docs/profiler-11","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"To collect 30 second CPU profile of current process (returns FlameGraph svg)\n`curl http://localhost:16030/prof`"},"7481":{"id":"/docs/profiler-12","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"To collect 1 minute CPU profile of current process and output in tree format (html)\n`curl http://localhost:16030/prof?output=tree&duration=60`"},"7482":{"id":"/docs/profiler-13","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"To collect 30 second heap allocation profile of current process (returns FlameGraph svg)\n`curl http://localhost:16030/prof?event=alloc`"},"7483":{"id":"/docs/profiler-14","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"To collect lock contention profile of current process (returns FlameGraph svg)\n`curl http://localhost:16030/prof?event=lock`"},"7484":{"id":"/docs/profiler-15","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"The following event types are supported by async-profiler. Use the 'event' parameter to specify. Default is 'cpu'. Not all operating systems will support all types."},"7485":{"id":"/docs/profiler-16","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Perf events:"},"7486":{"id":"/docs/profiler-17","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"cpu"},"7487":{"id":"/docs/profiler-18","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"page-faults"},"7488":{"id":"/docs/profiler-19","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"context-switches"},"7489":{"id":"/docs/profiler-20","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"cycles"},"7490":{"id":"/docs/profiler-21","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"instructions"},"7491":{"id":"/docs/profiler-22","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"cache-references"},"7492":{"id":"/docs/profiler-23","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"cache-misses"},"7493":{"id":"/docs/profiler-24","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"branches"},"7494":{"id":"/docs/profiler-25","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"branch-misses"},"7495":{"id":"/docs/profiler-26","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"bus-cycles"},"7496":{"id":"/docs/profiler-27","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"L1-dcache-load-misses"},"7497":{"id":"/docs/profiler-28","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"LLC-load-misses"},"7498":{"id":"/docs/profiler-29","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"dTLB-load-misses"},"7499":{"id":"/docs/profiler-30","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Java events:"},"7500":{"id":"/docs/profiler-31","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"alloc"},"7501":{"id":"/docs/profiler-32","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"lock"},"7502":{"id":"/docs/profiler-33","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"The following output formats are supported. Use the 'output' parameter to specify. Default is 'flamegraph'."},"7503":{"id":"/docs/profiler-34","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"Output formats:"},"7504":{"id":"/docs/profiler-35","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"summary: A dump of basic profiling statistics."},"7505":{"id":"/docs/profiler-36","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"traces: Call traces."},"7506":{"id":"/docs/profiler-37","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"flat: Flat profile (top N hot methods)."},"7507":{"id":"/docs/profiler-38","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"collapsed: Collapsed call traces in the format used by FlameGraph script. This is a collection of call stacks, where each line is a semicolon separated list of frames followed by a counter."},"7508":{"id":"/docs/profiler-39","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"svg: FlameGraph in SVG format."},"7509":{"id":"/docs/profiler-40","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"tree: Call tree in HTML format."},"7510":{"id":"/docs/profiler-41","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"jfr: Call traces in Java Flight Recorder format."},"7511":{"id":"/docs/profiler-42","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-usage","content":"The 'duration' parameter specifies how long to collect trace data before generating output, specified in seconds. The default is 10 seconds."},"7512":{"id":"/docs/profiler-43","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-ui","content":"In the UI, there is a new entry 'Profiler' in the top menu that will run the default action, which is to profile the CPU usage of the local process for thirty seconds and then produce FlameGraph SVG output."},"7513":{"id":"/docs/profiler-44","page_id":"/docs/profiler","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/profiler#profiler-notes","content":"The query parameter `pid` can be used to specify the process id of a specific process to be profiled. If this parameter is missing the local process in which the infoserver is embedded will be profiled. Profile targets that are not JVMs might work but is not specifically supported. There are security implications. Access to the infoserver should be appropriately restricted."},"7514":{"id":"/docs/protobuf","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","type":"page","content":"Protobuf in HBase","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Internals"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/protobuf"},"7515":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-0","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf","content":"Detailed guide on how HBase uses Protocol Buffers for serialization, RPC interfaces, and coprocessor endpoints, including shading and versioning considerations."},"7516":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-1","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"Protobuf"},"7517":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-2","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"hbase-2.0.0 and the shading of protobufs (HBASE-15638)"},"7518":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-3","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"protobuf changes for hbase-3.0.0 (HBASE-23797)"},"7519":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-4","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"HBase uses Google's protobufs wherever\nit persists metadata — in the tail of hfiles or Cells written by\nHBase into the system hbase:meta table or when HBase writes znodes\nto zookeeper, etc. — and when it passes objects over the wire making\nRPCs. HBase uses protobufs to describe the RPC\nInterfaces (Services) we expose to clients, for example the `Admin` and `Client`\nInterfaces that the RegionServer fields,\nor specifying the arbitrary extensions added by developers via our\nCoprocessor Endpoint mechanism."},"7520":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-5","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"With protobuf, you describe serializations and services in a `.protos` file.\nYou then feed these descriptors to a protobuf tool, the `protoc` binary,\nto generate classes that can marshall and unmarshall the described serializations\nand field the specified Services."},"7521":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-6","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"See the `README.txt` in the HBase sub-modules for details on how\nto run the class generation on a per-module basis;\ne.g. see `hbase-protocol/README.txt` for how to generate protobuf classes\nin the hbase-protocol module."},"7522":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-7","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"In HBase, `.proto` files are either in the `hbase-protocol` module; a module\ndedicated to hosting the common proto files and the protoc generated classes\nthat HBase uses internally serializing metadata. For extensions to hbase\nsuch as REST or Coprocessor Endpoints that need their own descriptors; their\nprotos are located inside the function's hosting module: e.g. `hbase-rest`\nis home to the REST proto files and the `hbase-rsgroup` table grouping\nCoprocessor Endpoint has all protos that have to do with table grouping."},"7523":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-8","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"Protos are hosted by the module that makes use of them. While\nthis makes it so generation of protobuf classes is distributed, done\nper module, we do it this way so modules encapsulate all to do with\nthe functionality they bring to hbase."},"7524":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-9","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-overview","content":"Extensions whether REST or Coprocessor Endpoints will make use\nof core HBase protos found back in the hbase-protocol module. They'll\nuse these core protos when they want to serialize a Cell or a Put or\nrefer to a particular node via ServerName, etc., as part of providing the\nCPEP Service. Going forward, after the release of hbase-2.0.0, this\npractice needs to whither. We'll explain why in the later\nhbase-2.0.0 section."},"7525":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-10","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"As of hbase-2.0.0, our protobuf usage gets a little more involved. HBase\ncore protobuf references are offset so as to refer to a private,\nbundled protobuf. Core stops referring to protobuf\nclasses at com.google.protobuf.\\_ and instead references protobuf at\nthe HBase-specific offset\norg.apache.hadoop.hbase.shaded.com.google.protobuf.\\_. We do this indirection\nso hbase core can evolve its protobuf version independent of whatever our\ndependencies rely on. For instance, HDFS serializes using protobuf.\nHDFS is on our CLASSPATH. Without the above described indirection, our\nprotobuf versions would have to align. HBase would be stuck\non the HDFS protobuf version until HDFS decided to upgrade. HBase\nand HDFS versions would be tied."},"7526":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-11","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"We had to move on from protobuf-2.5.0 because we need facilities\nadded in protobuf-3.1.0; in particular being able to save on\ncopies and avoiding bringing protobufs onheap for\nserialization/deserialization."},"7527":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-12","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"In hbase-2.0.0, we introduced a new module, `hbase-protocol-shaded`\ninside which we contained all to do with protobuf and its subsequent\nrelocation/shading. This module is in essence a copy of much of the old\n`hbase-protocol` but with an extra shading/relocation step.\nCore was moved to depend on this new module."},"7528":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-13","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"That said, a complication arises around Coprocessor Endpoints (CPEPs).\nCPEPs depend on public HBase APIs that reference protobuf classes at\n`com.google.protobuf.*` explicitly. For example, in our Table Interface\nwe have the below as the means by which you obtain a CPEP Service\nto make invocations against:"},"7529":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-14","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"Existing CPEPs will have made reference to core HBase protobufs\nspecifying ServerNames or carrying Mutations.\nSo as to continue being able to service CPEPs and their references\nto `com.google.protobuf.*` across the upgrade to hbase-2.0.0 and beyond,\nHBase needs to be able to deal with both\n`com.google.protobuf.*` references and its internal offset\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.shaded.com.google.protobuf.*` protobufs."},"7530":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-15","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"The `hbase-protocol-shaded` module hosts all\nprotobufs used by HBase core."},"7531":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-16","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"But for the vestigial CPEP references to the (non-shaded) content of\n`hbase-protocol`, we keep around most of this module going forward\njust so it is available to CPEPs. Retaining the most of `hbase-protocol`\nmakes for overlapping, 'duplicated' proto instances where some exist as\nnon-shaded/non-relocated here in their old module\nlocation but also in the new location, shaded under\n`hbase-protocol-shaded`. In other words, there is an instance\nof the generated protobuf class\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.protobuf.generated.ServerName`\nin hbase-protocol and another generated instance that is the same in all\nregards except its protobuf references are to the internal shaded\nversion at `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.shaded.protobuf.generated.ServerName`\n(note the 'shaded' addition in the middle of the package name)."},"7532":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-17","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"If you extend a proto in `hbase-protocol-shaded` for internal use,\nconsider extending it also in\n`hbase-protocol` (and regenerating)."},"7533":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-18","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#hbase-200-and-the-shading-of-protobufs-hbase-15638","content":"Going forward, we will provide a new module of common types for use\nby CPEPs that will have the same guarantees against change as does our\npublic API. TODO."},"7534":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-19","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"Since hadoop(start from 3.3.x) also shades protobuf and bumps the version to\n3.x, there is no reason for us to stay on protobuf 2.5.0 any more."},"7535":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-20","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"In HBase 3.0.0, the hbase-protocol module has been purged, the CPEP\nimplementation should use the protos in hbase-protocol-shaded module, and also\nmake use of the shaded protobuf in hbase-thirdparty. In general, we will keep\nthe protobuf version compatible for a whole major release, unless there are\ncritical problems, for example, a critical CVE on protobuf."},"7536":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-21","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"Add this dependency to your pom:"},"7537":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-22","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"And typically you also need to add this plugin to your pom to make your\ngenerated protobuf code also use the shaded and relocated protobuf version\nin hbase-thirdparty."},"7538":{"id":"/docs/protobuf-23","page_id":"/docs/protobuf","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/protobuf#protobuf-changes-for-hbase-300-hbase-23797","content":"In hbase-examples module, we have some examples under the\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.coprocessor.example` package. You can see\n`BulkDeleteEndpoint` and `BulkDelete.proto` for more details, and you can also\ncheck the `pom.xml` of hbase-examples module to see how to make use of the above\nplugin."},"7539":{"id":"/docs/pv2","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"page","content":"Procedure Framework (PV2)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Internals"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2"},"7540":{"id":"/docs/pv2-0","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2","content":"Procedure v2 ...aims to provide a unified way to build...multi-step procedures with a rollback/roll-forward ability in case of failure (e.g. create/delete table) — Matteo Bertozzi, the author of Pv2."},"7541":{"id":"/docs/pv2-1","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"Procedures"},"7542":{"id":"/docs/pv2-2","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#implementation","content":"Implementation"},"7543":{"id":"/docs/pv2-3","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-rollback","content":"Rollback"},"7544":{"id":"/docs/pv2-4","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"Metrics"},"7545":{"id":"/docs/pv2-5","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#baggage","content":"Baggage"},"7546":{"id":"/docs/pv2-6","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#resultstate-and-queries","content":"Result/State and Queries"},"7547":{"id":"/docs/pv2-7","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#subprocedures","content":"Subprocedures"},"7548":{"id":"/docs/pv2-8","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"ProcedureExecutor"},"7549":{"id":"/docs/pv2-9","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#nonces","content":"Nonces"},"7550":{"id":"/docs/pv2-10","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#waitwakesuspendyield","content":"Wait/Wake/Suspend/Yield"},"7551":{"id":"/docs/pv2-11","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Locking"},"7552":{"id":"/docs/pv2-12","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#procedure-types","content":"Procedure Types"},"7553":{"id":"/docs/pv2-13","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#statemachineprocedure","content":"StateMachineProcedure"},"7554":{"id":"/docs/pv2-14","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#remoteproceduredispatcher","content":"RemoteProcedureDispatcher"},"7555":{"id":"/docs/pv2-15","page_id":"/docs/pv2","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/pv2#references","content":"References"},"7556":{"id":"/docs/pv2-16","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2","content":"See HBASE-12439 for the original implementation."},"7557":{"id":"/docs/pv2-17","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2","content":"With Pv2 you can build and run state machines. It was built by Matteo to make distributed state transitions in HBase resilient in the face of process failures. Previous to Pv2, state transition handling was spread about the codebase with implementation varying by transition-type and context. Pv2 was inspired by FATE, of Apache Accumulo."},"7558":{"id":"/docs/pv2-18","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2","content":"Early Pv2 aspects have been shipping in HBase with a good while now but it has continued to evolve as it takes on more involved scenarios. What we have now is powerful but intricate in operation and incomplete, in need of cleanup and hardening. In this doc we have given overview on the system so you can make use of it (and help with its polishing)."},"7559":{"id":"/docs/pv2-19","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2","content":"This system has the awkward name of Pv2 because HBase already had the notion of a Procedure used in snapshots (see hbase-server *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.procedure* as opposed to hbase-procedure *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.procedure2*). Pv2 supercedes and is to replace Procedure."},"7560":{"id":"/docs/pv2-20","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"A Procedure is a transform made on an HBase entity. Examples of HBase entities would be Regions and Tables.\nProcedures are run by a ProcedureExecutor instance. Procedure current state is kept in the ProcedureStore.\nThe ProcedureExecutor has but a primitive view on what goes on inside a Procedure. From its PoV, Procedures are submitted and then the ProcedureExecutor keeps calling &#x2A;#execute(Object)* until the Procedure is done. Execute may be called multiple times in the case of failure or restart, so Procedure code must be idempotent yielding the same result each time it run. Procedure code can also implement *rollback* so steps can be undone if failure. A call to &#x2A;execute()* can result in one of following possibilities:"},"7561":{"id":"/docs/pv2-21","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*execute()* returns"},"7562":{"id":"/docs/pv2-22","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*null*: indicates we are done."},"7563":{"id":"/docs/pv2-23","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*this*: indicates there is more to do so, persist current procedure state and re-&#x2A;execute()*."},"7564":{"id":"/docs/pv2-24","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*Array* of sub-procedures: indicates a set of procedures needed to be run to completion before we can proceed (after which we expect the framework to call our execute again)."},"7565":{"id":"/docs/pv2-25","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*execute()* throws exception"},"7566":{"id":"/docs/pv2-26","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*suspend*: indicates execution of procedure is suspended and can be resumed due to some external event. The procedure state is persisted."},"7567":{"id":"/docs/pv2-27","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*yield*: procedure is added back to scheduler. The procedure state is not persisted."},"7568":{"id":"/docs/pv2-28","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"*interrupted*: currently same as *yield*."},"7569":{"id":"/docs/pv2-29","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"Any *exception* not listed above: Procedure *state* is changed to *FAILED* (after which we expect the framework will attempt rollback)."},"7570":{"id":"/docs/pv2-30","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"The ProcedureExecutor stamps the frameworks notions of Procedure State into the Procedure itself; e.g. it marks Procedures as INITIALIZING on submit. It moves the state to RUNNABLE when it goes to execute. When done, a Procedure gets marked FAILED or SUCCESS depending. Here is the list of all states as of this writing:"},"7571":{"id":"/docs/pv2-31","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***INITIALIZING*** Procedure in construction, not yet added to the executor"},"7572":{"id":"/docs/pv2-32","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***RUNNABLE*** Procedure added to the executor, and ready to be executed."},"7573":{"id":"/docs/pv2-33","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***WAITING*** The procedure is waiting on children (subprocedures) to be completed"},"7574":{"id":"/docs/pv2-34","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***WAITING\\_TIMEOUT*** The procedure is waiting a timeout or an external event"},"7575":{"id":"/docs/pv2-35","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***ROLLEDBACK*** The procedure failed and was rolledback."},"7576":{"id":"/docs/pv2-36","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***SUCCESS*** The procedure execution completed successfully."},"7577":{"id":"/docs/pv2-37","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"***FAILED*** The procedure execution failed, may need to rollback."},"7578":{"id":"/docs/pv2-38","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedures","content":"After each execute, the Procedure state is persisted to the ProcedureStore. Hooks are invoked on Procedures so they can preserve custom state. Post-fault, the ProcedureExecutor re-hydrates its pre-crash state by replaying the content of the ProcedureStore. This makes the Procedure Framework resilient against process failure."},"7579":{"id":"/docs/pv2-39","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#implementation","content":"In implementation, Procedures tend to divide transforms into finer-grained tasks and while some of these work items are handed off to sub-procedures,\nthe bulk are done as processing *steps* in-Procedure; each invocation of the execute is used to perform a single step, and then the Procedure relinquishes returning to the framework. The Procedure does its own tracking of where it is in the processing."},"7580":{"id":"/docs/pv2-40","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#implementation","content":"What comprises a sub-task, or *step* in the execution is up to the Procedure author but generally it is a small piece of work that cannot be further decomposed and that moves the processing forward toward its end state. Having procedures made of many small steps rather than a few large ones allows the Procedure framework give out insight on where we are in the processing. It also allows the framework be more fair in its execution. As stated per above, each step may be called multiple times (failure/restart) so steps must be implemented idempotent.\nIt is easy to confuse the state that the Procedure itself is keeping with that of the Framework itself. Try to keep them distinct."},"7581":{"id":"/docs/pv2-41","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-rollback","content":"Rollback is called when the procedure or one of the sub-procedures has failed. The rollback step is supposed to cleanup the resources created during the execute() step. In case of failure and restart, rollback() may be called multiple times, so again the code must be idempotent."},"7582":{"id":"/docs/pv2-42","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"There are hooks for collecting metrics on submit of the procedure and on finish."},"7583":{"id":"/docs/pv2-43","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"updateMetricsOnSubmit()"},"7584":{"id":"/docs/pv2-44","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"updateMetricsOnFinish()"},"7585":{"id":"/docs/pv2-45","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"Individual procedures can override these methods to collect procedure specific metrics. The default implementations of these methods try to get an object implementing an interface ProcedureMetrics which encapsulates following set of generic metrics:"},"7586":{"id":"/docs/pv2-46","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"SubmittedCount (Counter): Total number of procedure instances submitted of a type."},"7587":{"id":"/docs/pv2-47","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"Time (Histogram): Histogram of runtime for procedure instances."},"7588":{"id":"/docs/pv2-48","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"FailedCount (Counter): Total number of failed procedure instances."},"7589":{"id":"/docs/pv2-49","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#pv2-procedures-metrics","content":"Individual procedures can implement this object and define these generic set of metrics."},"7590":{"id":"/docs/pv2-50","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#baggage","content":"Procedures can carry baggage. One example is the *step* the procedure last attained (see previous section); procedures persist the enum that marks where they are currently. Other examples might be the Region or Server name the Procedure is currently working. After each call to execute, the Procedure#serializeStateData is called. Procedures can persist whatever."},"7591":{"id":"/docs/pv2-51","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#resultstate-and-queries","content":"(From Matteo's ProcedureV2 and Notification Bus doc)\\\nIn the case of asynchronous operations, the result must be kept around until the client asks for it. Once we receive a \"get\" of the result we can schedule the delete of the record. For some operations the result may be \"unnecessary\" especially in case of failure (e.g. if the create table fail, we can query the operation result or we can just do a list table to see if it was created) so in some cases we can schedule the delete after a timeout. On the client side the operation will return a \"Procedure ID\", this ID can be used to wait until the procedure is completed and get the result/exception."},"7592":{"id":"/docs/pv2-52","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#resultstate-and-queries","content":"If the master goes down while performing the operation the backup master will pickup the half in­progress operation and complete it. The client will not notice the failure."},"7593":{"id":"/docs/pv2-53","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#subprocedures","content":"Subprocedures are *Procedure&#x2A; instances created and returned by &#x2A;#execute(Object)* method of a procedure instance (parent procedure). As subprocedures are of type *Procedure*, they can instantiate their own subprocedures. As its a recursive, procedure stack is maintained by the framework. The framework makes sure that the parent procedure does not proceed till all sub-procedures and their subprocedures in a procedure stack are successfully finished."},"7594":{"id":"/docs/pv2-54","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"*ProcedureExecutor* uses *ProcedureStore* and *ProcedureScheduler* and executes procedures submitted to it. Some of the basic operations supported are:"},"7595":{"id":"/docs/pv2-55","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"*abort(procId)*: aborts specified procedure if its not finished"},"7596":{"id":"/docs/pv2-56","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"*submit(Procedure)*: submits procedure for execution"},"7597":{"id":"/docs/pv2-57","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"*retrieve:* list of get methods to get *Procedure* instances and results"},"7598":{"id":"/docs/pv2-58","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"*register/ unregister* listeners: for listening on Procedure related notifications"},"7599":{"id":"/docs/pv2-59","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#procedureexecutor","content":"When *ProcedureExecutor* starts it loads procedure instances persisted in *ProcedureStore* from previous run. All unfinished procedures are resumed from the last stored state."},"7600":{"id":"/docs/pv2-60","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#nonces","content":"You can pass the nonce that came in with the RPC to the Procedure on submit at the executor. This nonce will then be serialized along w/ the Procedure on persist. If a crash, on reload, the nonce will be put back into a map of nonces to pid in case a client tries to run same procedure for a second time (it will be rejected). See the base Procedure and how nonce is a base data member."},"7601":{"id":"/docs/pv2-61","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#waitwakesuspendyield","content":"'suspend' means stop processing a procedure because we can make no more progress until a condition changes; i.e. we sent RPC and need to wait on response. The way this works is that a Procedure throws a suspend exception from down in its guts as a GOTO the end-of-the-current-processing step. Suspend also puts the Procedure back on the scheduler. Problematic is we do some accounting on our way out even on suspend making it so it can take time exiting (We have to update state in the WAL)."},"7602":{"id":"/docs/pv2-62","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#waitwakesuspendyield","content":"RegionTransitionProcedure#reportTransition is called on receipt of a report from a RS. For Assign and Unassign, this event response from the server we sent an RPC wakes up suspended Assign/Unassigns."},"7603":{"id":"/docs/pv2-63","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Procedure Locks are not about concurrency! They are about giving a Procedure read/write access to an HBase Entity such as a Table or Region so that is possible to shut out other Procedures from making modifications to an HBase Entity state while the current one is running."},"7604":{"id":"/docs/pv2-64","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Locking is optional, up to the Procedure implementor but if an entity is being operated on by a Procedure, all transforms need to be done via Procedures using the same locking scheme else havoc."},"7605":{"id":"/docs/pv2-65","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Two ProcedureExecutor Worker threads can actually end up both processing the same Procedure instance. If it happens, the threads are meant to be running different parts of the one Procedure — changes that do not stamp on each other (This gets awkward around the procedure frameworks notion of ‘suspend'. More on this below)."},"7606":{"id":"/docs/pv2-66","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Locks optionally may be held for the life of a Procedure. For example, if moving a Region, you probably want to have exclusive access to the HBase Region until the Region completes (or fails). This is used in conjunction with \\{@link #holdLock(Object)}. If \\{@link #holdLock(Object)} returns true, the procedure executor will call acquireLock() once and thereafter not call \\{@link #releaseLock(Object)} until the Procedure is done (Normally, it calls release/acquire around each invocation of \\{@link #execute(Object)}."},"7607":{"id":"/docs/pv2-67","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Locks also may live the life of a procedure; i.e. once an Assign Procedure starts, we do not want another procedure meddling w/ the region under assignment. Procedures that hold the lock for the life of the procedure set Procedure#holdLock to true. AssignProcedure does this as do Split and Move (If in the middle of a Region move, you do not want it Splitting)."},"7608":{"id":"/docs/pv2-68","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Locking can be for life of Procedure."},"7609":{"id":"/docs/pv2-69","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#locking","content":"Some locks have a hierarchy. For example, taking a region lock also takes (read) lock on its containing table and namespace to prevent another Procedure obtaining an exclusive lock on the hosting table (or namespace)."},"7610":{"id":"/docs/pv2-70","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#statemachineprocedure","content":"One can consider each call to &#x2A;#execute(Object)* method as transitioning from one state to another in a state machine. Abstract class *StateMachineProcedure* is wrapper around base *Procedure* class which provides constructs for implementing a state machine as a *Procedure*. After each state transition current state is persisted so that, in case of crash/ restart, the state transition can be resumed from the previous state of a procedure before crash/ restart. Individual procedures need to define initial and terminus states and hooks &#x2A;executeFromState()* and &#x2A;setNextState()* are provided for state transitions."},"7611":{"id":"/docs/pv2-71","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#remoteproceduredispatcher","content":"A new RemoteProcedureDispatcher (+ subclass RSProcedureDispatcher) primitive takes care of running the Procedure-based Assignments 'remote' component. This dispatcher knows about 'servers'. It does aggregation of assignments by time on a time/count basis so can send procedures in batches rather than one per RPC. Procedure status comes back on the back of the RegionServer heartbeat reporting online/offline regions (No more notifications via ZK). The response is passed to the AMv2 to 'process'. It will check against the in-memory state. If there is a mismatch, it fences out the RegionServer on the assumption that something went wrong on the RS side. Timeouts trigger retries (Not Yet Implemented!). The Procedure machine ensures only one operation at a time on any one Region/Table using entity *locking* and smarts about what is serial and what can be run concurrently (Locking was zk-based — you'd put a znode in zk for a table — but now has been converted to be procedure-based as part of this project)."},"7612":{"id":"/docs/pv2-72","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#references","content":"Matteo had a slide deck on what it the Procedure Framework would look like and the problems it addresses initially attached to the Pv2 issue."},"7613":{"id":"/docs/pv2-73","page_id":"/docs/pv2","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/pv2#references","content":"A good doc by Matteo on problem and how Pv2 addresses it w/ roadmap (from the Pv2 JIRA). We should go back to the roadmap to do the Notification Bus, convertion of log splitting to Pv2, etc."},"7614":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"page","content":"Read Replica Cluster","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Operations & Development"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster"},"7615":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-0","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","content":"Running a secondary HBase cluster in read-only mode against shared cloud storage to scale read workloads."},"7616":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-1","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"Background"},"7617":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-2","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-design","content":"Design"},"7618":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-3","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#custom-hbasemeta-per-cluster","content":"Custom `hbase:meta` per cluster"},"7619":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-4","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Global read-only mode"},"7620":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-5","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#preventing-multiple-active-clusters-activeclustersuffixid","content":"Preventing Multiple Active Clusters (active.cluster.suffix.id)"},"7621":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-6","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-configuration","content":"Configuration"},"7622":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-7","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-operation-and-maintenance","content":"Operation and maintenance"},"7623":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-8","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-1-bring-up-a-new-read-replica-cluster","content":"Case 1. Bring up a new read replica cluster"},"7624":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-9","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-2-routine-sync-after-writes-on-the-active-cluster","content":"Case 2. Routine sync after writes on the active cluster"},"7625":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-10","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-3-dynamically-toggle-read-only-mode","content":"Case 3. Dynamically toggle read-only mode"},"7626":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-11","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-4-promote-a-replica-when-the-active-cluster-is-lost","content":"Case 4. Promote a replica when the active cluster is lost"},"7627":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-12","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"Case 5. Recover from a blocked read-only transition"},"7628":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-13","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#configurations-and-commands","content":"Configurations and Commands"},"7629":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-14","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"New configs"},"7630":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-15","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#new-commands","content":"New commands"},"7631":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-16","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"A *Read Replica Cluster* is an entire HBase cluster running in global read-only mode against the same shared\nstorage (`hbase.rootdir`) as an active read-write cluster. Both clusters list the same HFiles in the same\nHDFS / cloud-object-store location; no data is copied. Reads can be served from either cluster, letting the\nread workload be fanned out across multiple clusters without doubling storage cost."},"7632":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-17","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"Typical use cases:"},"7633":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-18","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"Fan out heavy scan / analytical workloads off the primary cluster."},"7634":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-19","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"Add cross-availability-zone read capacity backed by a single shared bucket."},"7635":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-20","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"Stand up an isolated cluster for read-mostly experiments without copying data."},"7636":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-21","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"**Eventual consistency.** A replica only sees data once (a) the active cluster has flushed the\ndata to HFiles in shared storage, and (b) the replica has been told to re-read shared storage via\nthe `refresh_meta` and `refresh_hfiles` commands. MemStore data on the active cluster is invisible\nto the replica until flushed."},"7637":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-22","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-background","content":"The parent design lives on HBASE-29081."},"7638":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-23","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-design","content":"The feature has three parts."},"7639":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-24","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#custom-hbasemeta-per-cluster","content":"Every cluster sharing a `hbase.rootdir` needs its own `hbase:meta` and its own master local region\ndirectory, because region assignments and master-local state are node-scoped and cannot be shared. Other\nsystem tables (`hbase:acl`, `hbase:replication`) *are* safe to share because their contents are storage-wide\nand the replica never writes to them."},"7640":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-25","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#custom-hbasemeta-per-cluster","content":"The configuration key `hbase.meta.table.suffix` selects a per-cluster suffix; the meta table becomes\n`hbase:meta_<suffix>` and the master's local store directory becomes `MasterData_<suffix>`. Each cluster\nsharing the same `hbase.rootdir` must be configured with a distinct suffix so its `hbase:meta` and\n`MasterData` directory do not collide with any other cluster's. The suffix must match `[a-zA-Z0-9]+`."},"7641":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-26","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`hbase.global.readonly.enabled=true` puts a cluster into read-only mode. Five coprocessor controllers under\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.security.access` intercept every user-table mutation path and throw\n`WriteAttemptedOnReadOnlyClusterException` (a `DoNotRetryIOException`) with the message\n`Operation not allowed in Read-Only Mode`:"},"7642":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-27","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Class"},"7643":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-28","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Coprocessor host"},"7644":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-29","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Blocks"},"7645":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-30","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`MasterReadOnlyController`"},"7646":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-31","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Master"},"7647":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-32","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"DDL, snapshots, splits, merges, namespace ops, ACL/quota ops, replication-peer ops"},"7648":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-33","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`RegionServerReadOnlyController`"},"7649":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-34","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"RegionServer"},"7650":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-35","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"WAL roll, replication sink mutations, log replay"},"7651":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-36","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`RegionReadOnlyController`"},"7652":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-37","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Region"},"7653":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-38","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"put, delete, batchMutate, checkAnd\\*, append, increment, flush, compaction, WAL append, commit/replay"},"7654":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-39","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`BulkLoadReadOnlyController`"},"7655":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-40","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Region"},"7656":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-41","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"bulk-load prepare/cleanup"},"7657":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-42","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"`EndpointReadOnlyController`"},"7658":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-43","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Region"},"7659":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-44","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"all coprocessor endpoint invocations"},"7660":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-45","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#global-read-only-mode","content":"Operators do not load these classes manually. `CoprocessorConfigurationUtil.syncReadOnlyConfigurations`\nadds them to `hbase.coprocessor.master.classes`, `hbase.coprocessor.regionserver.classes`, and\n`hbase.coprocessor.region.classes` at startup and on every dynamic\n`ConfigurationManager.notifyAllObservers` event — so the flag can be flipped at runtime with\n`update_all_config` (see Case 3)."},"7661":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-46","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#preventing-multiple-active-clusters-activeclustersuffixid","content":"Two clusters writing to the same `hbase.rootdir` would corrupt shared storage. To enforce a single writer, an\nactive master creates a protobuf-serialized sentinel at `<hbase.rootdir>/active.cluster.suffix.id` recording\nits cluster ID and meta suffix. `MasterFileSystem.negotiateActiveClusterSuffixFile` runs at master startup:"},"7662":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-47","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#preventing-multiple-active-clusters-activeclustersuffixid","content":"An **active** cluster (`hbase.global.readonly.enabled=false`) creates the file if absent, or verifies its\ncontents match its own identity. If the file belongs to another cluster, startup aborts with an\n`IOException`."},"7663":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-48","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#preventing-multiple-active-clusters-activeclustersuffixid","content":"A **replica** cluster (`hbase.global.readonly.enabled=true`) does not read or write the file; it logs\n`[Read-replica feature] Replica cluster is being started in Read Only Mode` and continues."},"7664":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-49","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#preventing-multiple-active-clusters-activeclustersuffixid","content":"`AbstractReadOnlyController.manageActiveClusterIdFile` handles the dynamic toggle: switching to read-only\ndeletes the file if this cluster owns it, and switching back to read-write creates the file if absent."},"7665":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-50","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-configuration","content":"On every node of the **read replica cluster**, add the following to `hbase-site.xml`:"},"7666":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-51","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-configuration","content":"The **active cluster** uses the same `hbase.rootdir` but its own `hbase.meta.table.suffix` (distinct from\nevery replica's suffix), and leaves `hbase.global.readonly.enabled` unset or `false`."},"7667":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-52","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-configuration","content":"`hbase.global.readonly.enabled` is a dynamic configuration — a config-change event reloads the read-only\ncoprocessors without restarting the process. All nodes must agree on the value; operators are responsible for\nkeeping every `hbase-site.xml` in sync before issuing `update_all_config`."},"7668":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-53","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-1-bring-up-a-new-read-replica-cluster","content":"Provision the replica cluster on hardware that can reach the active cluster's `hbase.rootdir` (typically\nthe same HDFS or object store)."},"7669":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-54","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-1-bring-up-a-new-read-replica-cluster","content":"Set `hbase.global.readonly.enabled=true` in the replica's `hbase-site.xml`. And set up\n`hbase.meta.table.suffix`, to distinguish the replica cluster's meta table on the shared storage."},"7670":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-55","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-1-bring-up-a-new-read-replica-cluster","content":"Start the cluster and verify if the Master log shows\n`[Read-replica feature] Replica cluster is being started in Read Only Mode`."},"7671":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-56","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-1-bring-up-a-new-read-replica-cluster","content":"From the replica shell, run `refresh_meta` and then `refresh_hfiles` to materialize the active cluster's\ncurrent state on the replica."},"7672":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-57","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-2-routine-sync-after-writes-on-the-active-cluster","content":"Always run `refresh_meta` first, then `refresh_hfiles`. `refresh_hfiles` only refreshes regions that are open\non the replica, so newly discovered regions must be in meta (and assigned) before their HFiles can be picked\nup. `refresh_hfiles` supports three scopes:"},"7673":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-58","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-2-routine-sync-after-writes-on-the-active-cluster","content":"Passing both `TABLE_NAME` and `NAMESPACE` to `refresh_hfiles` is rejected. Both commands return a procedure\nID that can be tracked through the master UI or `Admin.getProcedures()`."},"7674":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-59","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-2-routine-sync-after-writes-on-the-active-cluster","content":"If the replica's block cache holds stale entries for a table that has just been refreshed, evict them with\nthe pre-existing `clear_block_cache 'my_namespace:my_table'` shell command."},"7675":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-60","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-2-routine-sync-after-writes-on-the-active-cluster","content":"`Admin` and `AsyncAdmin` expose the same operations programmatically:"},"7676":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-61","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-3-dynamically-toggle-read-only-mode","content":"`hbase.global.readonly.enabled` can be changed without a restart. Edit `hbase-site.xml` then\ntrigger a configuration refresh (for example, `update_all_config` from the shell). `ConfigurationManager`\nnotifies its observers, which load or unload the read-only coprocessors and call\n`AbstractReadOnlyController.manageActiveClusterIdFile`:"},"7677":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-62","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-3-dynamically-toggle-read-only-mode","content":"**false → true (becoming a replica):** the `active.cluster.suffix.id` file is deleted only if its contents\nmatch this cluster; if another cluster owns the file, it is left in place."},"7678":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-63","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-3-dynamically-toggle-read-only-mode","content":"**true → false (becoming active):** the file is recreated with this cluster's identity, unless it already\nexists."},"7679":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-64","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-3-dynamically-toggle-read-only-mode","content":"In-flight batch operations are not interrupted; write operations submitted *after* the toggle throw\n`WriteAttemptedOnReadOnlyClusterException`. The caller is responsible for handling and (if desired) resubmitting the failed\nmutations."},"7680":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-65","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-4-promote-a-replica-when-the-active-cluster-is-lost","content":"Confirm the original active cluster is fully down."},"7681":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-66","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-4-promote-a-replica-when-the-active-cluster-is-lost","content":"If a stale `active.cluster.suffix.id` from the previous active is still present, remove it manually\n(e.g. `hdfs dfs -rm <hbase.rootdir>/active.cluster.suffix.id`, or the equivalent CLI for your object\nstore). The new active master will refuse to start while a foreign sentinel file is in place."},"7682":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-67","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-4-promote-a-replica-when-the-active-cluster-is-lost","content":"Set `hbase.global.readonly.enabled=false` on the replica and apply the change (dynamic update or\nrestart). The master writes a fresh sentinel file with this cluster's identity."},"7683":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-68","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"If you attempt to promote a replica to active (`hbase.global.readonly.enabled=false` + `update_all_config`)\nwhile a different cluster's `active.cluster.suffix.id` file is still present, the transition is blocked. The\nshell returns a `ReadOnlyTransitionException` and the cluster remains in read-only mode — writes continue to be\nrejected with `WriteAttemptedOnReadOnlyClusterException`."},"7684":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-69","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"The server ERROR log includes the foreign cluster's ID:"},"7685":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-70","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"A cluster whose promotion was blocked is still a replica — the read-only coprocessors remain\nloaded and writes are still rejected — even though its `hbase-site.xml` has\n`hbase.global.readonly.enabled=false`."},"7686":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-71","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"To recover, choose one of the following paths depending on whether the existing active cluster is still\nrunning."},"7687":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-72","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"**Path A — the active cluster is still running:**"},"7688":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-73","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"On the currently active cluster, set `hbase.global.readonly.enabled=true` in `hbase-site.xml` and run\n`update_all_config`. This converts it to a replica and deletes its `active.cluster.suffix.id` file."},"7689":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-74","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"On the cluster you want to promote, re-run `update_all_config`. The transition will now succeed — the\ncluster writes a fresh sentinel file with its own identity and unloads the read-only coprocessors."},"7690":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-75","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"**Path B — the active cluster is down or has already been converted to a replica:**"},"7691":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-76","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"Confirm the cluster that owns the sentinel file is fully stopped or has already been converted to a replica."},"7692":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-77","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"Remove the foreign sentinel file:"},"7693":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-78","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"On the cluster you want to promote, re-run `update_all_config`. The transition will now succeed — the\ncluster writes a fresh sentinel file with its own identity and unloads the read-only coprocessors."},"7694":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-79","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#case-5-recover-from-a-blocked-read-only-transition","content":"**Do not remove the sentinel file while the cluster that wrote it is still running as an active\ncluster.** Doing so would allow two clusters to write to the same shared storage simultaneously,\nrisking data corruption."},"7695":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-80","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"Config"},"7696":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-81","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"Default"},"7697":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-82","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"Description"},"7698":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-83","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"`hbase.meta.table.suffix`"},"7699":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-84","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"`\"\"`"},"7700":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-85","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"Adds a suffix to the meta table name. `value='test'` produces the table name `hbase:meta_test`."},"7701":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-86","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"`hbase.global.readonly.enabled`"},"7702":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-87","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"`false`"},"7703":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-88","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#read-replica-cluster-new-configs","content":"Puts the entire cluster into read-only mode."},"7704":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-89","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#new-commands","content":"Command"},"7705":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-90","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#new-commands","content":"Usage"},"7706":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster-91","page_id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/read-replica-cluster#new-commands","content":"Description"},"7707":{"id":"/docs/read-replica-cluster