pgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Upgrade process from 1.x to 2.x"},"9329":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-44","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-171","content":"Upgrading to 1.7.1+"},"9330":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-45","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-from-pre-14-to-14","content":"Upgrading from pre-1.4 to 1.4+"},"9331":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-46","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#region-server-memory-consumption-changes","content":"Region Server memory consumption changes."},"9332":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-47","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Replication peer's TableCFs config"},"9333":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-48","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#raw-scan-now-ignores-ttl","content":"Raw scan now ignores TTL"},"9334":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-49","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-from-pre-13-to-13","content":"Upgrading from pre-1.3 to 1.3+"},"9335":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-50","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-1x","content":"Upgrading to 1.x"},"9336":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-51","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"The RegionServer Grouping feature has been reimplemented. See section Migrating From Old Implementation in Apache HBase Operational Management for more details."},"9337":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-52","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"The `hbase:namespace` table has been removed and fold into `hbase:meta`. See Data Model for more details."},"9338":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-53","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"There is no special consideration upgrading to hbase-2.4.x from 2.3.x. And for earlier versions, just follow the Upgrade from 2.0.x-2.2.x to 2.3+ guide. In general, 2.2.x should be rolling upgradeable, for 2.1.x or 2.0.x, you will need to clear the Upgrade from 2.0 or 2.1 to 2.2+ hurdle first."},"9339":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-54","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20x-22x-to-23","content":"There is no special consideration upgrading to hbase-2.3.x from earlier versions. From 2.2.x, it should be rolling upgradeable. From 2.1.x or 2.0.x, you will need to clear the Upgrade from 2.0 or 2.1 to 2.2+ hurdle first."},"9340":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-55","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgraded-zookeeper-dependency-version","content":"Our dependency on Apache ZooKeeper has been upgraded to 3.5.7 (HBASE-24132), as 3.4.x is EOL. The newer 3.5.x client is compatible with the older 3.4.x server. However, if you're using HBase in stand-alone mode and perform an in-place upgrade, there are some upgrade steps documented by the ZooKeeper community. This doesn't impact a production deployment, but would impact a developer's local environment."},"9341":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-56","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#new-in-master-procedure-store","content":"Of note, HBase 2.3.0 changes the in-Master Procedure Store implementation. It was a dedicated custom store to instead use a standard HBase Region (HBASE-23326). The migration from the old to new format is automatic run by the new 2.3.0 Master on startup. The old *MasterProcWALs&#x2A; dir which hosted the old custom implementation files in &#x2A;$\\{hbase.rootdir}* is deleted on successful migration. A new *MasterProc&#x2A; sub-directory replaces it to host the Store files and WALs for the new Procedure Store in-Master Region. The in-Master Region is unusual in that it writes to an alternate location at *$\\{hbase.rootdir}/MasterProc&#x2A; rather than under *$\\{hbase.rootdir}/data* in the filesystem and the special Procedure Store in-Master Region is hidden from all clients other than the active Master itself. Otherwise, it is like any other with the Master process running flushes and compactions, archiving WALs when over-flushed, and so on. Its files are readable by standard Region and Store file tooling for triage and analysis as long as they are pointed to the appropriate location in the filesystem."},"9342":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-57","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#new-in-master-procedure-store","content":"Notice that, after the migration, you should make sure to not start an active master with old code, as it can not recognize the new procedure store. So it is suggested to upgrade backup master(s) to new 2.3 first, and then upgrade the active master. And unless explicitly mentioned, this is the suggested way for all upgrading, i.e, upgrading backup master(s) first, then active master, and then region servers."},"9343":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-58","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"HBase 2.2+ uses a new Procedure form assigning/unassigning/moving Regions. It does not process HBase 2.1 and 2.0's Unassign/Assign Procedure types. Upgrade requires that we first drain the Master Procedure Store of old style Procedures before starting the new 2.2 Master. So you need to make sure that before you kill the old version (2.0 or 2.1) Master, there is no region in transition. And once the new version (2.2+) Master is up, you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one."},"9344":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-59","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"And there is a more safer way if you are running 2.1.1+ or 2.0.3+ cluster. It need four steps to upgrade Master."},"9345":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-60","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Shutdown both active and standby Masters (Your cluster will continue to server reads and writes without interruption)."},"9346":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-61","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Set the property hbase.procedure.upgrade-to-2-2 to true in hbase-site.xml for the Master, and start only one Master, still using the 2.1.1+ (or 2.0.3+) version."},"9347":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-62","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Wait until the Master quits. Confirm that there is a 'UPGRADE OK: All existed procedures have been finished, quit...' message in the Master log as the cause of the shutdown. The Procedure Store is now empty."},"9348":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-63","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Start new Masters with the new 2.2+ version."},"9349":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-64","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Then you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one. See HBASE-21075 for more details."},"9350":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-65","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"In case these steps are not done, on starting 2.2+ master, you would see the following exception in the master logs:"},"9351":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-66","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.HBaseIOException: Unsupported procedure type class org.apache.hadoop.hbase.master.assignment.UnassignProcedure found`"},"9352":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-67","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-from-1x-to-2x","content":"In this section we will first call out significant changes compared to the prior stable HBase release and then go over the upgrade process. Be sure to read the former with care so you avoid surprises."},"9353":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-68","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changes-of-note","content":"First we'll cover deployment / operational changes that you might hit when upgrading to HBase 2.0+. After that we'll call out changes for downstream applications. Please note that Coprocessors are covered in the operational section. Also note that this section is not meant to convey information about new features that may be of interest to you. For a complete summary of changes, please see the CHANGES.txt file in the source release artifact for the version you are planning to upgrade to."},"9354":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-69","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#update-to-basic-prerequisite-minimums-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"As noted in the Basic Prerequisites section, HBase 2.0+ requires a minimum of Java 8 and Hadoop 2.6. The HBase community recommends ensuring you have already completed any needed upgrades in prerequisites prior to upgrading your HBase version."},"9355":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-70","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"You **must not** use an HBase 1.x version of HBCK against an HBase 2.0+ cluster. HBCK is strongly tied to the HBase server version. Using the HBCK tool from an earlier release against an HBase 2.0+ cluster will destructively alter said cluster in unrecoverable ways."},"9356":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-71","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"As of HBase 2.0, HBCK (A.K.A *HBCK1* or *hbck1*) is a read-only tool that can report the status of some non-public system internals but will often misread state because it does not understand the workings of hbase2."},"9357":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-72","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"To read about HBCK's replacement, see Operations and Management documentation."},"9358":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-73","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"Related, before you upgrade, ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type\ninconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process."},"9359":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-74","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following configuration settings are no longer applicable or available. For details, please see the detailed release notes."},"9360":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-75","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.config.read.zookeeper.config (see upgrade2.0.zkconfig for migration details)"},"9361":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-76","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.zookeeper.useMulti (HBase now always uses ZK's multi functionality)"},"9362":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-77","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.client.threads.max"},"9363":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-78","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.client.nativetransport"},"9364":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-79","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.fs.tmp.dir"},"9365":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-80","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bucketcache.combinedcache.enabled"},"9366":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-81","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bucketcache.ioengine no longer supports the 'heap' value."},"9367":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-82","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bulkload.staging.dir"},"9368":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-83","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster wasn't removed, strictly speaking, but its meaning has fundamentally changed and users should not set it. See the section upgrade2.0.regions.on.master for details."},"9369":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-84","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.distributed.log.replay See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"9370":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-85","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.disallow\\.writes.when.recovering See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"9371":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-86","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.wal.logreplay.batch.size See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"9372":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-87","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.catalog.timeout"},"9373":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-88","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.catalog.timeout"},"9374":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-89","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.metrics.exposeOperationTimes"},"9375":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-90","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.metrics.showTableName"},"9376":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-91","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.online.schema.update.enable (HBase now always supports this)"},"9377":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-92","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.thrift.htablepool.size.max"},"9378":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-93","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following properties have been renamed. Attempts to set the old property will be ignored at run time."},"9379":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-94","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"Old name"},"9380":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-95","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"New name"},"9381":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-96","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.server.nativetransport"},"9382":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-97","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.nativetransport"},"9383":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-98","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.rpc.server.worker.count"},"9384":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-99","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.worker.count"},"9385":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-100","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hfile.compactions.discharger.interval"},"9386":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-101","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hfile.compaction.discharger.interval"},"9387":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-102","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.percolumnfamilyflush.size.lower.bound"},"9388":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-103","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.percolumnfamilyflush.size.lower.bound.min"},"9389":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-104","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following configuration settings changed their default value. Where applicable, the value to set to restore the behavior of HBase 1.2 is given."},"9390":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-105","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.security.authorization now defaults to false. set to true to restore same behavior as previous default."},"9391":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-106","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.retries.number is now set to 10. Previously it was 35. Downstream users are advised to use client timeouts as described in Configuration instead."},"9392":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-107","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.serverside.retries.multiplier is now set to 3. Previously it was 10. Downstream users are advised to use client timeouts as described in Configuration instead."},"9393":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-108","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.fileSplitTimeout is now set to 10 minutes. Previously it was 30 seconds."},"9394":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-109","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.logroll.multiplier is now set to 0.5. Previously it was 0.95. This change is tied with the following doubling of block size. Combined, these two configuration changes should make for WALs of about the same size as those in hbase-1.x but there should be less incidence of small blocks because we fail to roll the WAL before we hit the blocksize threshold. See HBASE-19148 for discussion."},"9395":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-110","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.hlog.blocksize defaults to 2x the HDFS default block size for the WAL dir. Previously it was equal to the HDFS default block size for the WAL dir."},"9396":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-111","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.start.log.errors.counter changed to 5. Previously it was 9."},"9397":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-112","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.type changed to 'fifo'. In HBase versions 1.0 - 1.2 it was 'deadline'. In prior and later 1.x versions it already defaults to 'fifo'."},"9398":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-113","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.memstore.chunkpool.maxsize is 1.0 by default. Previously it was 0.0. Effectively, this means previously we would not use a chunk pool when our memstore is onheap and now we will. See the section Long GC pauses for more information about the MSLAB chunk pool."},"9399":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-114","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.cleaner.interval is now set to 10 minutes. Previously it was 1 minute."},"9400":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-115","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.procedure.threads will now default to 1/4 of the number of available CPUs, but not less than 16 threads. Previously it would be number of threads equal to number of CPUs."},"9401":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-116","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles is now 16. Previously it was 10."},"9402":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-117","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.http.max.threads is now 16. Previously it was 10."},"9403":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-118","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.max.perserver.tasks is now 2. Previously it was 5."},"9404":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-119","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.normalizer.period is now 5 minutes. Previously it was 30 minutes."},"9405":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-120","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.region.split.policy is now SteppingSplitPolicy. Previously it was IncreasingToUpperBoundRegionSplitPolicy."},"9406":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-121","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"replication.source.ratio is now 0.5. Previously it was 0.1."},"9407":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-122","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"The feature \"Master acts as region server\" and associated follow-on work available in HBase 1.y is non-functional in HBase 2.y and should not be used in a production setting due to deadlock on Master initialization. Downstream users are advised to treat related configuration settings as experimental and the feature as inappropriate for production settings."},"9408":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-123","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"A brief summary of related changes:"},"9409":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-124","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"Master no longer carries regions by default"},"9410":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-125","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster is a boolean, default false (if it holds an HBase 1.x list of tables, will default to false)"},"9411":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-126","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster.systemTablesOnly is boolean to keep user tables off master. default false"},"9412":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-127","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"those wishing to replicate old list-of-servers config should deploy a stand-alone RegionServer process and then rely on Region Server Groups"},"9413":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-128","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#distributed-log-replay-feature-broken-and-removed-toc","content":"The Distributed Log Replay feature was broken and has been removed from HBase 2.y+. As a consequence all related configs, metrics, RPC fields, and logging have also been removed. Note that this feature was found to be unreliable in the run up to HBase 1.0, defaulted to being unused, and was effectively removed in HBase 1.2.0 when we started ignoring the config that turns it on (HBASE-14465). If you are currently using the feature, be sure to perform a clean shutdown, ensure all DLR work is complete, and disable the feature prior to upgrading."},"9414":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-129","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"The prefix-tree encoding was removed from HBase 2.0.0 (HBASE-19179). It was (late!) deprecated in hbase-1.2.7, hbase-1.4.0, and hbase-1.3.2."},"9415":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-130","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"This feature was removed because it as not being actively maintained. If interested in reviving this sweet facility which improved random read latencies at the expensive of slowed writes, write the HBase developers list at *dev at hbase dot apache dot org*."},"9416":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-131","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"The prefix-tree encoding needs to be removed from all tables before upgrading to HBase 2.0+. To do that first you need to change the encoding from PREFIX\\_TREE to something else that is supported in HBase 2.0. After that you have to major compact the tables that were using PREFIX\\_TREE encoding before. To check which column families are using incompatible data block encoding you can use Pre-Upgrade Validator."},"9417":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-132","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have changed names:"},"9418":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-133","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"Metrics previously published under the name \"AssignmentManger\" \\[sic] are now published under the name \"AssignmentManager\""},"9419":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-134","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have changed their meaning:"},"9420":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-135","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The metric 'blockCacheEvictionCount' published on a per-region server basis no longer includes blocks removed from the cache due to the invalidation of the hfiles they are from (e.g. via compaction)."},"9421":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-136","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The metric 'totalRequestCount' increments once per request; previously it incremented by the number of `Actions` carried in the request; e.g. if a request was a `multi` made of four Gets and two Puts, we'd increment 'totalRequestCount' by six; now we increment by one regardless. Expect to see lower values for this metric in hbase-2.0.0."},"9422":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-137","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The 'readRequestCount' now counts reads that return a non-empty row where in older hbases, we'd increment 'readRequestCount' whether a Result or not. This change will flatten the profile of the read-requests graphs if requests for non-existent rows. A YCSB read-heavy workload can do this dependent on how the database was loaded."},"9423":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-138","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have been removed:"},"9424":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-139","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"Metrics related to the Distributed Log Replay feature are no longer present. They were previously found in the region server context under the name 'replay'. See the section \"Distributed Log Replay\" feature broken and removed for details."},"9425":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-140","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have been added:"},"9426":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-141","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-metrics-toc","content":"'totalRowActionRequestCount' is a count of region row actions summing reads and writes."},"9427":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-142","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-logging-toc","content":"HBase-2.0.0 now uses slf4j as its logging frontend. Previously, we used log4j (1.2). For most the transition should be seamless; slf4j does a good job interpreting *log4j.properties* logging configuration files such that you should not notice any difference in your log system emissions."},"9428":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-143","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changed-logging-toc","content":"That said, your *log4j.properties* may need freshening. See HBASE-20351 for example, where a stale log configuration file manifest as netty configuration being dumped at DEBUG level as preamble on every shell command invocation."},"9429":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-144","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#zookeeper-configs-no-longer-read-from-zoocfg-toc","content":"HBase no longer optionally reads the 'zoo.cfg' file for ZooKeeper related configuration settings. If you previously relied on the 'hbase.config.read.zookeeper.config' config for this functionality, you should migrate any needed settings to the hbase-site.xml file while adding the prefix 'hbase.zookeeper.property.' to each property name."},"9430":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-145","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"The following permission related changes either altered semantics or defaults:"},"9431":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-146","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"Permissions granted to a user now merge with existing permissions for that user, rather than over-writing them. (see the release note on HBASE-17472 for details)"},"9432":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-147","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"Region Server Group commands (added in 1.4.0) now require admin privileges."},"9433":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-148","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"A number of admin commands are known to not work when used from a pre-HBase 2.0 client. This includes an HBase Shell that has the library jars from pre-HBase 2.0. You will need to plan for an outage of use of admin APIs and commands until you can also update to the needed client version."},"9434":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-149","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"The following client operations do not work against HBase 2.0+ cluster when executed from a pre-HBase 2.0 client:"},"9435":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-150","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"list\\_procedures"},"9436":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-151","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"split"},"9437":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-152","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"merge\\_region"},"9438":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-153","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"list\\_quotas"},"9439":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-154","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"enable\\_table\\_replication"},"9440":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-155","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"disable\\_table\\_replication"},"9441":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-156","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"Snapshot related commands"},"9442":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-157","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#deprecated-in-10-admin-commands-have-been-removed-toc","content":"The following commands that were deprecated in 1.0 have been removed. Where applicable the replacement command is listed."},"9443":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-158","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#deprecated-in-10-admin-commands-have-been-removed-toc","content":"The 'hlog' command has been removed. Downstream users should rely on the 'wal' command instead."},"9444":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-159","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#region-server-memory-consumption-changes-toc","content":"Users upgrading from versions prior to HBase 1.4 should read the instructions in section Region Server memory consumption changes.."},"9445":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-160","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#region-server-memory-consumption-changes-toc","content":"Additionally, HBase 2.0 has changed how memstore memory is tracked for flushing decisions. Previously, both the data size and overhead for storage were used to calculate utilization against the flush threshold. Now, only data size is used to make these per-region decisions. Globally the addition of the storage overhead is used to make decisions about forced flushes."},"9446":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-161","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#web-ui-for-splitting-and-merging-operate-on-row-prefixes-toc","content":"Previously, the Web UI included functionality on table status pages to merge or split based on an encoded region name. In HBase 2.0, instead this functionality works by taking a row prefix."},"9447":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-162","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#special-upgrading-for-replication-users-from-pre-hbase-14-toc","content":"User running versions of HBase prior to the 1.4.0 release that make use of replication should be sure to read the instructions in the section Replication peer's TableCFs config."},"9448":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-163","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-shell-changes-toc","content":"The HBase shell command relies on a bundled JRuby instance. This bundled JRuby been updated from version 1.6.8 to version 9.1.10.0. The represents a change from Ruby 1.8 to Ruby 2.3.3, which introduces non-compatible language changes for user scripts."},"9449":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-164","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-shell-changes-toc","content":"The HBase shell command now ignores the '--return-values' flag that was present in early HBase 1.4 releases. Instead the shell always behaves as though that flag were passed. If you wish to avoid having expression results printed in the console you should alter your IRB configuration."},"9450":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-165","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"All Coprocessor APIs have been refactored to improve supportability around binary API compatibility for future versions of HBase. If you or applications you rely on have custom HBase coprocessors, you should read the release notes for HBASE-18169 for details of changes you will need to make prior to upgrading to HBase 2.0+."},"9451":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-166","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"For example, if you had a BaseRegionObserver in HBase 1.2 then at a minimum you will need to update it to implement both RegionObserver and RegionCoprocessor and add the method"},"9452":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-167","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"For more information, see Upgrading Coprocessors to 2.0."},"9453":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-168","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-20-can-no-longer-write-hfile-v2-files-toc","content":"HBase has simplified our internal HFile handling. As a result, we can no longer write HFile versions earlier than the default of version 3. Upgrading users should ensure that hfile.format.version is not set to 2 in hbase-site.xml before upgrading. Failing to do so will cause Region Server failure. HBase can still read HFiles written in the older version 2 format."},"9454":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-169","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"HBase can no longer read the deprecated WAL files written in the Apache Hadoop Sequence File format. The hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and hbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configuration entries should be set to use the Protobuf based WAL reader / writer classes. This implementation has been the default since HBase 0.96, so legacy WAL files should not be a concern for most downstream users."},"9455":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-170","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"Starting from 2.6.0, the hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and hbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configuration entries are removed since the only valid values are protobuf based reader/writer. Setting them in *hbase-site.xml* will have no real effect."},"9456":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-171","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"A clean cluster shutdown should ensure there are no WAL files. If you are unsure of a given WAL file's format you can use the `hbase wal` command to parse files while the HBase cluster is offline. In HBase 2.0+, this command will not be able to read a Sequence File based WAL."},"9457":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-172","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#change-in-behavior-for-filters-toc","content":"The Filter ReturnCode NEXT\\_ROW has been redefined as skipping to next row in current family, not to next row in all family. it's more reasonable, because ReturnCode is a concept in store level, not in region level."},"9458":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-173","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#downstream-hbase-20-users-should-use-the-shaded-client-toc","content":"Downstream users are strongly urged to rely on the Maven coordinates org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-client for their runtime use. This artifact contains all the needed implementation details for talking to an HBase cluster while minimizing the number of third party dependencies exposed."},"9459":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-174","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#downstream-hbase-20-users-should-use-the-shaded-client-toc","content":"Note that this artifact exposes some classes in the org.apache.hadoop package space (e.g. o.a.h.configuration.Configuration) so that we can maintain source compatibility with our public API. Those classes are included so that they can be altered to use the same relocated third party dependencies as the rest of the HBase client code. In the event that you need to **also** use Hadoop in your code, you should ensure all Hadoop related jars precede the HBase client jar in your classpath."},"9460":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-175","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#downstream-hbase-20-users-of-mapreduce-must-switch-to-new-artifact-toc","content":"Downstream users of HBase's integration for Apache Hadoop MapReduce must switch to relying on the org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-mapreduce module for their runtime use. Historically, downstream users relied on either the org.apache.hbase:hbase-server or org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-server artifacts for these classes. Both uses are no longer supported and in the vast majority of cases will fail at runtime."},"9461":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-176","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#downstream-hbase-20-users-of-mapreduce-must-switch-to-new-artifact-toc","content":"Note that this artifact exposes some classes in the org.apache.hadoop package space (e.g. o.a.h.configuration.Configuration) so that we can maintain source compatibility with our public API. Those classes are included so that they can be altered to use the same relocated third party dependencies as the rest of the HBase client code. In the event that you need to **also** use Hadoop in your code, you should ensure all Hadoop related jars precede the HBase client jar in your classpath."},"9462":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-177","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#significant-changes-to-runtime-classpath-toc","content":"A number of internal dependencies for HBase were updated or removed from the runtime classpath. Downstream client users who do not follow the guidance in Downstream HBase 2.0+ users should use the shaded client will have to examine the set of dependencies Maven pulls in for impact. Downstream users of LimitedPrivate Coprocessor APIs will need to examine the runtime environment for impact."},"9463":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-178","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#multiple-breaking-changes-to-source-and-binary-compatibility-for-client-api-toc","content":"The Java client API for HBase has a number of changes that break both source and binary compatibility for details see the Compatibility Check Report for the release you'll be upgrading to."},"9464":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-179","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"The backing implementation of HBase's tracing features was updated from Apache HTrace 3 to HTrace 4, which includes several breaking changes. While HTrace 3 and 4 can coexist in the same runtime, they will not integrate with each other, leading to disjoint trace information."},"9465":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-180","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"The internal changes to HBase during this upgrade were sufficient for compilation, but it has not been confirmed that there are no regressions in tracing functionality. Please consider this feature experimental for the immediate future."},"9466":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-181","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"If you previously relied on client side tracing integrated with HBase operations, it is recommended that you upgrade your usage to HTrace 4 as well."},"9467":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-182","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"After the Apache HTrace project moved to the Attic/retired, the traces in HBase are left broken and unmaintained since HBase 2.0. A new project HBASE-22120 will replace HTrace with OpenTelemetry. It will be shipped in 3.0.0 release. Please see the Tracing documentation for more details."},"9468":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-183","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#hfile-lose-forward-compatibility-toc","content":"HFiles generated by 2.0.0, 2.0.1, 2.1.0 are not forward compatible to 1.4.6-, 1.3.2.1-, 1.2.6.1-, and other inactive releases. Why HFile lose compatibility is hbase in new versions (2.0.0, 2.0.1, 2.1.0) use protobuf to serialize/deserialize TimeRangeTracker (TRT) while old versions use DataInput/DataOutput. To solve this, We have to put HBASE-21012 to 2.x and put HBASE-21013 in 1.x. For more information, please check HBASE-21008."},"9469":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-184","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#paths-upgrading-from-1-to-2-changes-of-note-performance","content":"You will likely see a change in the performance profile on upgrade to hbase-2.0.0 given read and write paths have undergone significant change. On release, writes may be slower with reads about the same or much better, dependent on context. Be prepared to spend time re-tuning (See Performance). Performance is also an area that is now under active review so look forward to improvement in coming releases (See HBASE-20188 TESTING Performance)."},"9470":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-185","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#integration-tests-and-kerberos-toc","content":"Integration Tests (`IntegrationTests*`) used to rely on the Kerberos credential cache for authentication against secured clusters. This used to lead to tests failing due to authentication failures when the tickets in the credential cache expired. As of hbase-2.0.0 (and hbase-1.3.0+), the integration test clients will make use of the configuration properties `hbase.client.keytab.file` and `hbase.client.kerberos.principal`. They are required. The clients will perform a login from the configured keytab file and automatically refresh the credentials in the background for the process lifetime (See HBASE-16231)."},"9471":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-186","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"HBase 2.x comes with default limits to the speed at which compactions can execute. This limit is defined per RegionServer. In previous versions of HBase earlier than 1.5, there was no limit to the speed at which a compaction could run by default. Applying a limit to the throughput of a compaction should ensure more stable operations from RegionServers."},"9472":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-187","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"Take care to notice that this limit is *per RegionServer*, not *per compaction*."},"9473":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-188","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The throughput limit is defined as a range of bytes written per second, and is allowed to vary within the given lower and upper bound. RegionServers observe the current throughput of a compaction and apply a linear formula to adjust the allowed throughput, within the lower and upper bound, with respect to external pressure. For compactions, external pressure is defined as the number of store files with respect to the maximum number of allowed store files. The more store files, the higher the compaction pressure."},"9474":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-189","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"Configuration of this throughput is governed by the following properties."},"9475":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-190","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The lower bound is defined by `hbase.hstore.compaction.throughput.lower.bound` and defaults to 50 MB/s (`52428800`)."},"9476":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-191","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The upper bound is defined by `hbase.hstore.compaction.throughput.higher.bound` and defaults to 100 MB/s (`104857600`)."},"9477":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-192","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"To revert this behavior to the unlimited compaction throughput of earlier versions of HBase, please set the following property to the implementation that applies no limits to compactions."},"9478":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-193","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"`hbase.regionserver.throughput.controller=org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.throttle.NoLimitThroughputController`"},"9479":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-194","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Coprocessors have changed substantially in 2.0 ranging from top level design changes in class hierarchies to changed/removed methods, interfaces, etc. (Parent jira: HBASE-18169 Coprocessor fix and cleanup before 2.0.0 release). Some of the reasons for such widespread changes:"},"9480":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-195","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Pass Interfaces instead of Implementations; e.g. TableDescriptor instead of HTableDescriptor and Region instead of HRegion (HBASE-18241 Change client.Table and client.Admin to not use HTableDescriptor)."},"9481":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-196","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Design refactor so implementers need to fill out less boilerplate and so we can do more compile-time checking (HBASE-17732)"},"9482":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-197","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Purge Protocol Buffers from Coprocessor API (HBASE-18859, HBASE-16769, etc)"},"9483":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-198","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Cut back on what we expose to Coprocessors removing hooks on internals that were too private to expose (for eg. HBASE-18453 CompactionRequest should not be exposed to user directly; HBASE-18298 RegionServerServices Interface cleanup for CP expose; etc)"},"9484":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-199","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"To use coprocessors in 2.0, they should be rebuilt against new API otherwise they will fail to load and HBase processes will die."},"9485":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-200","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Suggested order of changes to upgrade the coprocessors:"},"9486":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-201","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Directly implement observer interfaces instead of extending Base\\*Observer classes. Change `Foo extends BaseXXXObserver` to `Foo implements XXXObserver`. (HBASE-17312)."},"9487":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-202","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Adapt to design change from Inheritence to Composition (HBASE-17732) by following this example."},"9488":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-203","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"getTable() has been removed from the CoprocessorEnvrionment, coprocessors should self-manage Table instances."},"9489":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-204","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Some examples of writing coprocessors with new API can be found in hbase-example module here."},"9490":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-205","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Lastly, if an api has been changed/removed that breaks you in an irreparable way, and if there's a good justification to add it back, bring it our notice dev\\@hbase.apache.org."},"9491":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-206","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#rolling-upgrade-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Rolling upgrades are currently an experimental feature. They have had limited testing. There are likely corner cases as yet uncovered in our limited experience so you should be careful if you go this route. The stop/upgrade/start described in the next section, Upgrade process from 1.x to 2.x, is the safest route."},"9492":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-207","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#rolling-upgrade-from-1x-to-2x","content":"That said, the below is a prescription for a rolling upgrade of a 1.4 cluster."},"9493":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-208","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#pre-requirements","content":"Upgrade to the latest 1.4.x release. Pre 1.4 releases may also work but are not tested, so please upgrade to 1.4.3+ before upgrading to 2.x, unless you are an expert and familiar with the region assignment and crash processing. See the section Upgrading from pre-1.4 to 1.4+ on how to upgrade to 1.4.x."},"9494":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-209","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#pre-requirements","content":"Make sure that the zk-less assignment is enabled, i.e, set `hbase.assignment.usezk` to `false`. This is the most important thing. It allows the 1.x master to assign/unassign regions to/from 2.x region servers. See the release note section of HBASE-11059 on how to migrate from zk based assignment to zk less assignment."},"9495":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-210","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#pre-requirements","content":"Before you upgrade, ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type inconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process."},"9496":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-211","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#pre-requirements","content":"We have tested rolling upgrading from 1.4.3 to 2.1.0, but it should also work if you want to upgrade to 2.0.x."},"9497":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-212","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"Unload a region server and upgrade it to 2.1.0. With HBASE-17931 in place, the meta region and regions for other system tables will be moved to this region server immediately. If not, please move them manually to the new region server. This is very important because"},"9498":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-213","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"The schema of meta region is hard coded, if meta is on an old region server, then the new region servers can not access it as it does not have some families, for example, table state."},"9499":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-214","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"Client with lower version can communicate with server with higher version, but not vice versa. If the meta region is on an old region server, the new region server will use a client with higher version to communicate with a server with lower version, this may introduce strange problems."},"9500":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-215","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"Rolling upgrade all other region servers."},"9501":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-216","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"Upgrading masters."},"9502":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-217","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"It is OK that during the rolling upgrading there are region server crashes. The 1.x master can assign regions to both 1.x and 2.x region servers, and HBASE-19166 fixed a problem so that 1.x region server can also read the WALs written by 2.x region server and split them."},"9503":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-218","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"Please read the Changes of Note! section carefully before\nrolling upgrading. Make sure that you do not use the removed features in 2.0, for example, the\nprefix-tree encoding, the old hfile format, etc. They could both fail the upgrading and leave the\ncluster in an intermediate state and hard to recover."},"9504":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-219","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#instructions","content":"If you have success running this prescription, please notify the dev list with a note on your\nexperience and/or update the above with any deviations you may have taken so others going this\nroute can benefit from your efforts."},"9505":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-220","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"To upgrade an existing HBase 1.x cluster, you should:"},"9506":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-221","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type inconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process. Fix all *hbck1* complaints before proceeding."},"9507":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-222","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Clean shutdown of existing 1.x cluster"},"9508":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-223","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Update coprocessors"},"9509":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-224","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Upgrade Master roles first"},"9510":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-225","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Upgrade RegionServers"},"9511":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-226","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"(Eventually) Upgrade Clients"},"9512":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-227","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-171","content":"HBase release 1.7.0 introduced an incompatible table metadata serialization format that broke the minor release compatibility guarantees. The issue was reported in HBASE-26021 and the problematic serialization patch was reverted in HBase 1.7.1. Some important notes about 1.7.x upgrades below."},"9513":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-228","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you are considering an upgrade to 1.7.x version, skip 1.7.0 completely and upgrade to 1.7.1+ version. 1.7.0 was withdrawn and removed from the Apache sites."},"9514":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-229","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you already installed a 1.7.0 cluster from scratch and are looking to migrate to 1.7.1+, you cannot follow the regular rolling upgrade procedures due to broken compatibility contracts. Instead shutdown the cluster and reboot with 1.7.1+ binaries. Newer versions detect any existing tables with incompatible serialization and rewrite them using the correct format at bootstrap."},"9515":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-230","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you are already on 1.7.1+ version, everything is good and no additional steps need to be performed."},"9516":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-231","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#region-server-memory-consumption-changes","content":"Users upgrading from versions prior to HBase 1.4 should be aware that the estimates of heap usage by the memstore objects (KeyValue, object and array header sizes, etc) have been made more accurate for heap sizes up to 32G (using CompressedOops), resulting in them dropping by 10-50% in practice. This also results in less number of flushes and compactions due to \"fatter\" flushes. YMMV. As a result, the actual heap usage of the memstore before being flushed may increase by up to 100%. If configured memory limits for the region server had been tuned based on observed usage, this change could result in worse GC behavior or even OutOfMemory errors. Set the environment property (not hbase-site.xml) \"hbase.memorylayout.use.unsafe\" to false to disable."},"9517":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-232","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Before 1.4, the table name can't include namespace for replication peer's TableCFs config. It was fixed by add TableCFs to ReplicationPeerConfig which was stored on Zookeeper. So when upgrade to 1.4, you have to update the original ReplicationPeerConfig data on Zookeeper firstly. There are four steps to upgrade when your cluster have a replication peer with TableCFs config."},"9518":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-233","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Disable the replication peer."},"9519":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-234","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"If master has permission to write replication peer znode, then rolling update master directly. If not, use TableCFsUpdater tool to update the replication peer's config."},"9520":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-235","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Rolling update regionservers."},"9521":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-236","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Enable the replication peer."},"9522":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-237","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Notes:"},"9523":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-238","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Can't use the old client(before 1.4) to change the replication peer's config. Because the client will write config to Zookeeper directly, the old client will miss TableCFs config. And the old client write TableCFs config to the old tablecfs znode, it will not work for new version regionserver."},"9524":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-239","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#raw-scan-now-ignores-ttl","content":"Doing a raw scan will now return results that have expired according to TTL settings."},"9525":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-240","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-from-pre-13-to-13","content":"If running Integration Tests under Kerberos, see upgrade2.0.it.kerberos."},"9526":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/paths-241","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/paths","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/paths#upgrading-to-1x","content":"Please consult the documentation published specifically for the version of HBase that you are upgrading to for details on the upgrade process."},"9527":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"page","content":"Rollback","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Upgrading"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback"},"9528":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-0","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","content":"How to rollback an HBase upgrade to restore the cluster to a previous version, including backup procedures and data recovery steps."},"9529":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-1","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#caveats","content":"Caveats"},"9530":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-2","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-vs-downgrade","content":"Rollback vs Downgrade"},"9531":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-3","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#replication","content":"Replication"},"9532":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-4","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#data-locality","content":"Data Locality"},"9533":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-5","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#configurable-locations","content":"Configurable Locations"},"9534":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-6","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#all-service-rollback","content":"All service rollback"},"9535":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-7","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements","content":"Requirements"},"9536":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-8","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade","content":"Before upgrade"},"9537":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-9","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Performing a rollback"},"9538":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-10","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-after-hdfs-rollback-and-zookeeper-downgrade","content":"Rollback after HDFS rollback and ZooKeeper downgrade"},"9539":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-11","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-1","content":"Requirements"},"9540":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-12","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-1","content":"Before upgrade"},"9541":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-13","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Performing a rollback"},"9542":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-14","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-after-hdfs-downgrade","content":"Rollback after HDFS downgrade"},"9543":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-15","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-2","content":"Requirements"},"9544":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-16","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"Before upgrade"},"9545":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-17","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Performing a rollback"},"9546":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-18","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","content":"Sometimes things don't go as planned when attempting an upgrade. This section explains how to perform a *rollback* to an earlier HBase release. Note that this should only be needed between Major and some Minor releases. You should always be able to *downgrade* between HBase Patch releases within the same Minor version. These instructions may require you to take steps before you start the upgrade process, so be sure to read through this section beforehand."},"9547":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-19","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-vs-downgrade","content":"This section describes how to perform a *rollback* on an upgrade between HBase minor and major versions. In this document, rollback refers to the process of taking an upgraded cluster and restoring it to the old version *while losing all changes that have occurred since upgrade*. By contrast, a cluster *downgrade* would restore an upgraded cluster to the old version while maintaining any data written since the upgrade. We currently only offer instructions to rollback HBase clusters. Further, rollback only works when these instructions are followed prior to performing the upgrade."},"9548":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-20","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-vs-downgrade","content":"When these instructions talk about rollback vs downgrade of prerequisite cluster services (i.e. HDFS), you should treat leaving the service version the same as a degenerate case of downgrade."},"9549":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-21","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#replication","content":"Unless you are doing an all-service rollback, the HBase cluster will lose any configured peers for HBase replication. If your cluster is configured for HBase replication, then prior to following these instructions you should document all replication peers. After performing the rollback you should then add each documented peer back to the cluster. Note also that data written to the cluster since the upgrade may or may not have already been replicated to any peers. Determining which, if any, peers have seen replication data as well as rolling back the data in those peers is out of the scope of this guide."},"9550":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-22","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#data-locality","content":"Unless you are doing an all-service rollback, going through a rollback procedure will likely destroy all locality for Region Servers. You should expect degraded performance until after the cluster has had time to go through compactions to restore data locality. Optionally, you can force a compaction to speed this process up at the cost of generating cluster load."},"9551":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-23","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#configurable-locations","content":"The instructions below assume default locations for the HBase data directory and the HBase znode. Both of these locations are configurable and you should verify the value used in your cluster before proceeding. In the event that you have a different value, just replace the default with the one found in your configuration \\* HBase data directory is configured via the key 'hbase.rootdir' and has a default value of '/hbase'. \\* HBase znode is configured via the key 'zookeeper.znode.parent' and has a default value of '/hbase'."},"9552":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-24","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#all-service-rollback","content":"If you will be performing a rollback of both the HDFS and ZooKeeper services, then HBase's data will be rolled back in the process."},"9553":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-25","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements","content":"Ability to rollback HDFS and ZooKeeper"},"9554":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-26","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade","content":"No additional steps are needed pre-upgrade. As an extra precautionary measure, you may wish to use distcp to back up the HBase data off of the cluster to be upgraded. To do so, follow the steps in the 'Before upgrade' section of 'Rollback after HDFS downgrade' but copy to another HDFS instance instead of within the same instance."},"9555":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-27","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Stop HBase"},"9556":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-28","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Perform a rollback for HDFS and ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"9557":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-29","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"9558":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-30","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Start HBase"},"9559":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-31","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback","content":"Verify HBase contents — use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"9560":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-32","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-after-hdfs-rollback-and-zookeeper-downgrade","content":"If you will be rolling back HDFS but going through a ZooKeeper downgrade, then HBase will be in an inconsistent state. You must ensure the cluster is not started until you complete this process."},"9561":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-33","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-1","content":"Ability to rollback HDFS"},"9562":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-34","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-1","content":"Ability to downgrade ZooKeeper"},"9563":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-35","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-1","content":"No additional steps are needed pre-upgrade. As an extra precautionary measure, you may wish to use distcp to back up the HBase data off of the cluster to be upgraded. To do so, follow the steps in the 'Before upgrade' section of 'Rollback after HDFS downgrade' but copy to another HDFS instance instead of within the same instance."},"9564":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-36","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Stop HBase"},"9565":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-37","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Perform a rollback for HDFS and a downgrade for ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"9566":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-38","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"9567":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-39","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Clean out ZooKeeper information related to HBase. WARNING: This step will permanently destroy all replication peers. Please see the section on HBase Replication under Caveats for more information."},"9568":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-40","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"**Clean HBase information out of ZooKeeper:**"},"9569":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-41","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Start HBase"},"9570":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-42","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Verify HBase contents—use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"9571":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-43","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#rollback-after-hdfs-downgrade","content":"If you will be performing an HDFS downgrade, then you'll need to follow these instructions regardless of whether ZooKeeper goes through rollback, downgrade, or reinstallation."},"9572":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-44","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-2","content":"Ability to downgrade HDFS"},"9573":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-45","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-2","content":"Pre-upgrade cluster must be able to run MapReduce jobs"},"9574":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-46","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-2","content":"HDFS super user access"},"9575":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-47","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#requirements-2","content":"Sufficient space in HDFS for at least two copies of the HBase data directory"},"9576":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-48","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"Before beginning the upgrade process, you must take a complete backup of HBase's backing data. The following instructions cover backing up the data within the current HDFS instance. Alternatively, you can use the distcp command to copy the data to another HDFS cluster."},"9577":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-49","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"Stop the HBase cluster"},"9578":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-50","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"Copy the HBase data directory to a backup location using the distcp command as the HDFS super user (shown below on a security enabled cluster)"},"9579":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-51","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"**Using distcp to backup the HBase data directory:**"},"9580":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-52","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#before-upgrade-2","content":"Distcp will launch a mapreduce job to handle copying the files in a distributed fashion. Check the output of the distcp command to ensure this job completed successfully."},"9581":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-53","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Stop HBase"},"9582":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-54","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Perform a downgrade for HDFS and a downgrade/rollback for ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"9583":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-55","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"9584":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-56","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Restore the HBase data directory from prior to the upgrade. Restore the HBase data directory from prior to the upgrade as the HDFS super user (shown below on a security enabled cluster). If you backed up your data on another HDFS cluster instead of locally, you will need to use the distcp command to copy it back to the current HDFS cluster."},"9585":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-57","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"**Restore the HBase data directory:**"},"9586":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-58","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Clean out ZooKeeper information related to HBase. WARNING: This step will permanently destroy all replication peers. Please see the section on HBase Replication under Caveats for more information."},"9587":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-59","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"**Clean HBase information out of ZooKeeper:**"},"9588":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-60","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Start HBase"},"9589":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback-61","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rollback","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rollback#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Verify HBase contents–use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"9590":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","type":"page","content":"Rolling Upgrades","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Upgrading"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling"},"9591":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-0","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","content":"How to perform rolling upgrades across HBase versions that are binary or wire compatible without cluster downtime."},"9592":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-1","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling#rolling-upgrade-between-versions-that-are-binarywire-compatible-toc","content":"Rolling Upgrade Between Versions that are Binary/Wire Compatible [!toc]"},"9593":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-2","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","content":"A rolling upgrade is the process by which you update the servers in your cluster a server at a time. You can rolling upgrade across HBase versions if they are binary or wire compatible. See the \"Rolling Upgrade Between Versions that are Binary/Wire Compatible\" section below for more on what this means. Coarsely, a rolling upgrade is a graceful stop each server, update the software, and then restart. You do this for each server in the cluster. Usually you upgrade the Master first and then the RegionServers. See Rolling Restart for tools that can help use the rolling upgrade process."},"9594":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-3","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","content":"For example, in the below, HBase was symlinked to the actual HBase install. On upgrade, before running a rolling restart over the cluster, we changed the symlink to point at the new HBase software version and then ran"},"9595":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-4","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","content":"The rolling-restart script will first gracefully stop and restart the master, and then each of the RegionServers in turn. Because the symlink was changed, on restart the server will come up using the new HBase version. Check logs for errors as the rolling upgrade proceeds."},"9596":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-5","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling#rolling-upgrade-between-versions-that-are-binarywire-compatible-toc","content":"Unless otherwise specified, HBase minor versions are binary compatible. You can do a rolling upgrades between HBase point versions. For example, you can go to 1.4.4 from 1.4.6 by doing a rolling upgrade across the cluster replacing the 1.4.4 binary with a 1.4.6 binary."},"9597":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling-6","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/rolling","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/rolling#rolling-upgrade-between-versions-that-are-binarywire-compatible-toc","content":"In the minor version-particular sections below, we call out where the versions are wire/protocol compatible and in this case, it is also possible to do a rolling upgrade."},"9598":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"page","content":"HBase version number and compatibility","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Multi-Page Documentation","Configuration & Setup","Upgrading"],"tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number"},"9599":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-0","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","content":"Understanding HBase semantic versioning scheme and compatibility guarantees across major, minor, and patch versions."},"9600":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-1","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#aspirational-semantic-versioning","content":"Aspirational Semantic Versioning"},"9601":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-2","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#given-a-version-number-majorminorpatch-increment-the-toc","content":"Given a version number MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH, increment the: [!toc]"},"9602":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-3","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-dimensions-toc","content":"Compatibility Dimensions [!toc]"},"9603":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-4","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-server-wire-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Client-Server wire protocol compatibility [!toc]"},"9604":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-5","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Server-Server protocol compatibility [!toc]"},"9605":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-6","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#file-format-compatibility-toc","content":"File format compatibility [!toc]"},"9606":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-7","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"Client API compatibility [!toc]"},"9607":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-8","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-binary-compatibility-toc","content":"Client Binary compatibility [!toc]"},"9608":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-9","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-side-limited-api-compatibility-taken-from-hadoop-toc","content":"Server-Side Limited API compatibility (taken from Hadoop) [!toc]"},"9609":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-10","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"Dependency Compatibility [!toc]"},"9610":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-11","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#operational-compatibility-toc","content":"Operational Compatibility [!toc]"},"9611":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-12","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#summary-toc","content":"Summary [!toc]"},"9612":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-13","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Compatibility Matrix: [!toc]"},"9613":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-14","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#hbase-api-surface","content":"HBase API Surface"},"9614":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-15","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#hbase-client-api","content":"HBase Client API"},"9615":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-16","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#hbase-limitedprivate-api","content":"HBase LimitedPrivate API"},"9616":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-17","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#hbase-private-api","content":"HBase Private API"},"9617":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-18","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","type":"heading","tags":["multi-page"],"url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#binary-compatibility","content":"Binary Compatibility"},"9618":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-19","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#aspirational-semantic-versioning","content":"Starting with the 1.0.0 release, HBase is working towards Semantic Versioning for its release versioning. In summary:"},"9619":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-20","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#given-a-version-number-majorminorpatch-increment-the-toc","content":"MAJOR version when you make incompatible API changes,"},"9620":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-21","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#given-a-version-number-majorminorpatch-increment-the-toc","content":"MINOR version when you add functionality in a backwards-compatible manner, and"},"9621":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-22","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#given-a-version-number-majorminorpatch-increment-the-toc","content":"PATCH version when you make backwards-compatible bug fixes."},"9622":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-23","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#given-a-version-number-majorminorpatch-increment-the-toc","content":"Additional labels for pre-release and build metadata are available as extensions to the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH format."},"9623":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-24","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-dimensions-toc","content":"In addition to the usual API versioning considerations HBase has other compatibility dimensions that we need to consider."},"9624":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-25","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-server-wire-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Allows updating client and server out of sync."},"9625":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-26","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-server-wire-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"We could only allow upgrading the server first. I.e. the server would be backward compatible to an old client, that way new APIs are OK."},"9626":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-27","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-server-wire-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: A user should be able to use an old client to connect to an upgraded cluster."},"9627":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-28","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Servers of different versions can co-exist in the same cluster."},"9628":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-29","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"The wire protocol between servers is compatible."},"9629":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-30","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Workers for distributed tasks, such as replication and log splitting, can co-exist in the same cluster."},"9630":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-31","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Dependent protocols (such as using ZK for coordination) will also not be changed."},"9631":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-32","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-server-protocol-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: A user can perform a rolling upgrade."},"9632":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-33","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#file-format-compatibility-toc","content":"Support file formats backward and forward compatible"},"9633":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-34","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#file-format-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: File, ZK encoding, directory layout is upgraded automatically as part of an HBase upgrade. User can downgrade to the older version and everything will continue to work."},"9634":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-35","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"Allow changing or removing existing client APIs."},"9635":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-36","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"An API needs to be deprecated for a whole major version before we will change/remove it."},"9636":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-37","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"An example: An API was deprecated in 2.0.1 and will be marked for deletion in 4.0.0. On the other hand, an API deprecated in 2.0.0 can be removed in 3.0.0."},"9637":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-38","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"Occasionally mistakes are made and internal classes are marked with a higher access level than they should. In these rare circumstances, we will accelerate the deprecation schedule to the next major version (i.e., deprecated in 2.2.x, marked `IA.Private` 3.0.0). Such changes are communicated and explained via release note in Jira."},"9638":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-39","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"APIs available in a patch version will be available in all later patch versions. However, new APIs may be added which will not be available in earlier patch versions."},"9639":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-40","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"New APIs introduced in a patch version will only be added in a source compatible way: i.e. code that implements public APIs will continue to compile. [^1]"},"9640":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-41","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-api-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: A user using a newly deprecated API does not need to modify application code with HBase API calls until the next major version. \\*"},"9641":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-42","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-binary-compatibility-toc","content":"Client code written to APIs available in a given patch release can run unchanged (no recompilation needed) against the new jars of later patch versions."},"9642":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-43","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-binary-compatibility-toc","content":"Client code written to APIs available in a given patch release might not run against the old jars from an earlier patch version."},"9643":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-44","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-binary-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: Old compiled client code will work unchanged with the new jars."},"9644":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-45","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#client-binary-compatibility-toc","content":"If a Client implements an HBase Interface, a recompile MAY be required upgrading to a newer minor version (See release notes for warning about incompatible changes). All effort will be made to provide a default implementation so this case should not arise."},"9645":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-46","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-side-limited-api-compatibility-taken-from-hadoop-toc","content":"Internal APIs are marked as Stable, Evolving, or Unstable"},"9646":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-47","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-side-limited-api-compatibility-taken-from-hadoop-toc","content":"This implies binary compatibility for coprocessors and plugins (pluggable classes, including replication) as long as these are only using marked interfaces/classes."},"9647":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-48","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#server-side-limited-api-compatibility-taken-from-hadoop-toc","content":"Example: Old compiled Coprocessor, Filter, or Plugin code will work unchanged with the new jars."},"9648":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-49","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"An upgrade of HBase will not require an incompatible upgrade of a dependent project, except for Apache Hadoop."},"9649":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-50","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"An upgrade of HBase will not require an incompatible upgrade of the Java runtime."},"9650":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-51","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: Upgrading HBase to a version that supports *Dependency Compatibility* won't require that you upgrade your Apache ZooKeeper service."},"9651":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-52","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"Example: If your current version of HBase supported running on JDK 8, then an upgrade to a version that supports *Dependency Compatibility* will also run on JDK 8."},"9652":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-53","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#dependency-compatibility-toc","content":"Previously, we tried to maintain dependency compatibility for the underly Hadoop service but over\nthe last few years this has proven untenable. While the HBase project attempts to maintain support\nfor older versions of Hadoop, we drop the \"supported\" designator for minor versions that fail to\ncontinue to see releases. Additionally, the Hadoop project has its own set of compatibility\nguidelines, which means in some cases having to update to a newer supported minor release might\nbreak some of our compatibility promises."},"9653":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-54","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#operational-compatibility-toc","content":"Metric changes"},"9654":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-55","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#operational-compatibility-toc","content":"Behavioral changes of services"},"9655":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-56","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#operational-compatibility-toc","content":"JMX APIs exposed via the `/jmx/` endpoint"},"9656":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-57","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#summary-toc","content":"A patch upgrade is a drop-in replacement. Any change that is not Java binary and source compatible would not be allowed.[^2] Downgrading versions within patch releases may not be compatible."},"9657":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-58","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#summary-toc","content":"A minor upgrade requires no application/client code modification. Ideally it would be a drop-in replacement but client code, coprocessors, filters, etc might have to be recompiled if new jars are used."},"9658":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-59","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#summary-toc","content":"A major upgrade allows the HBase community to make breaking changes."},"9659":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-60","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"*Please note, this indicates what could break, not that it will break. We will/should add\nspecifics in our release notes.*"},"9660":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-61","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Major"},"9661":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-62","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Minor"},"9662":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-63","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Patch"},"9663":{"id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number-64","page_id":"/docs/upgrading/version-number","tags":["multi-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/upgrading/version-number#c