/docs/single-page#hbasesnapshotexceptiongetsnapshotdescription----hbaseprotossnapshotdescription-1-toc","content":"HBaseSnapshotException.getSnapshotDescription ( ) : HBaseProtos.SnapshotDescription 1 [!toc]"},"11761":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1961","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#resultgetstats----clientprotosregionloadstats-1-toc","content":"Result.getStats ( ) : ClientProtos.RegionLoadStats 1 [!toc]"},"11762":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1962","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#rest-changes","content":"REST changes:"},"11763":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1963","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#clientgethttpclient----httpclient-1-toc","content":"Client.getHttpClient ( ) : HttpClient 1 [!toc]"},"11764":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1964","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#responsegetheaders----header--1-toc","content":"Response.getHeaders ( ) : Header[ ] 1 [!toc]"},"11765":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1965","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#prettyprinter-changes","content":"PrettyPrinter changes:"},"11766":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1966","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#hfileprettyprinterprocessfile--path-file---void-1-toc","content":"HFilePrettyPrinter.processFile ( Path file ) : void 1 [!toc]"},"11767":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1967","page_id":"/docs/single-page","type":"heading","tags":["single-page"],"url":"/docs/single-page#accesscontrolclient-changes","content":"AccessControlClient changes:"},"11768":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1968","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preface","content":"Herein you will find either the definitive documentation on an HBase topic as of its\nstanding when the referenced HBase version shipped, or it will point to the location\nin Javadoc or\nJIRA where the pertinent information can be found."},"11769":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1969","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-this-guide","content":"This reference guide is a work in progress. The source for this guide can be found in the\n&#x2A;hbase-website/app/pages/\\_docs/docs/\\_mdx/(multi-page)* directory of the HBase source. This reference guide is marked up\nusing MDX (just extended markdown) powered by Fumadocs from which the finished guide is generated as part of the\n'site' build target. Run"},"11770":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1970","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-this-guide","content":"to generate this documentation.\nAmendments and improvements to the documentation are welcomed.\nClick\nthis link\nto file a new documentation bug against Apache HBase with some values pre-selected."},"11771":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1971","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#contributing-to-the-documentation","content":"For an overview and suggestions to get started contributing to the documentation,\nsee the relevant section later in this documentation."},"11772":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1972","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"If this is your first foray into the wonderful world of Distributed Computing, then you are in for some interesting times.\nFirst off, distributed systems are hard; making a distributed system hum requires a disparate skillset that spans systems (hardware and software) and networking."},"11773":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1973","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"Your cluster's operation can hiccup because of any of a myriad set of reasons from bugs in HBase itself through misconfigurations — misconfiguration of HBase but also operating system misconfigurations — through to hardware problems whether it be a bug in your network card drivers or an underprovisioned RAM bus (to mention two recent examples of hardware issues that manifested as \"HBase is slow\"). You will also need to do a recalibration if up to this your computing has been bound to a single box.\nHere is one good starting point: Fallacies of Distributed Computing."},"11774":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1974","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#heads-up-if-this-is-your-first-foray-into-the-world-of-distributed-computing","content":"That said, you are welcome.\nIt's a fun place to be.\nYours, the HBase Community."},"11775":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1975","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reporting-bugs","content":"Please use JIRA to report non-security-related bugs."},"11776":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1976","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reporting-bugs","content":"To protect existing HBase installations from new vulnerabilities, please **do not** use JIRA to report security-related bugs. Instead, send your report to the mailing list private\\@hbase.apache.org, which allows anyone to send messages, but restricts who can read them. Someone on that list will contact you to follow up on your report."},"11777":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1977","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#support-and-testing-expectations","content":"The phrases *supported*, *not supported*, *tested*, and *not tested* occur several\nplaces throughout this guide. In the interest of clarity, here is a brief explanation\nof what is generally meant by these phrases, in the context of HBase."},"11778":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#support-and-testing-expectations","content":"Commercial technical support for Apache HBase is provided by many Hadoop vendors. This is not the\nsense in which the term *support* is used in the context of the Apache HBase project. The Apache\nHBase team assumes no responsibility for your HBase clusters, your configuration, or your data."},"11779":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *supported* means that HBase is designed to work\nin the way described, and deviation from the defined behavior or functionality should\nbe reported as a bug."},"11780":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#not-supported","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *not supported* means that a use case or use pattern\nis not expected to work and should be considered an antipattern. If you think this\ndesignation should be reconsidered for a given feature or use pattern, file a JIRA\nor start a discussion on one of the mailing lists."},"11781":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tested","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *tested* means that a feature is covered by unit\nor integration tests, and has been proven to work as expected."},"11782":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#not-tested","content":"In the context of Apache HBase, *not tested* means that a feature or use pattern\nmay or may not work in a given way, and may or may not corrupt your data or cause\noperational issues. It is an unknown, and there are no guarantees. If you can provide\nproof that a feature designated as *not tested* does work in a given way, please\nsubmit the tests and/or the metrics so that other users can gain certainty about\nsuch features or use patterns."},"11783":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#quick-start---standalone-hbase","content":"This section describes the setup of a single-node standalone HBase. A *standalone* instance has all HBase daemons — the Master, RegionServers, and ZooKeeper — running in a single JVM persisting to the local filesystem. It is our most basic deploy profile. We will show you how to create a table in HBase using the `hbase shell` CLI, insert rows into the table, perform put and scan operations against the table, enable or disable the table, and start and stop HBase."},"11784":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#quick-start---standalone-hbase","content":"Apart from downloading HBase, this procedure should take less than 10 minutes."},"11785":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getting-started-jdk-version-requirements","content":"HBase requires that a JDK be installed. See Java for information about supported JDK versions."},"11786":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"Choose a download site from this list of Apache Download Mirrors. Click on the suggested top link. This will take you to a mirror of *HBase Releases*. Click on the folder named *stable&#x2A; and then download the binary file that ends in *.tar.gz* to your local filesystem. Do not download the file ending in *src.tar.gz* for now."},"11787":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"Extract the downloaded file, and change to the newly-created directory."},"11788":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"You must set the `JAVA_HOME` environment variable before starting HBase. To make this easier, HBase lets you set it within the *conf/hbase-env.sh* file. You must locate where Java is installed on your machine, and one way to find this is by using the *whereis java* command. Once you have the location, edit the *conf/hbase-env.sh&#x2A; file and uncomment the line starting with &#x2A;#export JAVA\\_HOME=*, and then set it to your Java installation path."},"11789":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"*Example extract from *hbase-env.sh* where *JAVA\\_HOME* is set*"},"11790":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-download-configure-and-start-hbase-in-standalone-mode","content":"The *bin/start-hbase.sh* script is provided as a convenient way to start HBase. Issue the command, and if all goes well, a message is logged to standard output showing that HBase started successfully. You can use the `jps` command to verify that you have one running process called `HMaster`. In standalone mode HBase runs all daemons within this single JVM, i.e. the HMaster, a single HRegionServer, and the ZooKeeper daemon. Go to *http\\://localhost:16010* to view the HBase Web UI."},"11791":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connect-to-hbase","content":"Connect to your running instance of HBase using the `hbase shell` command, located in the &#x2A;bin/* directory of your HBase install. In this example, some usage and version information that is printed when you start HBase Shell has been omitted. The HBase Shell prompt ends with a `>` character."},"11792":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#display-hbase-shell-help-text","content":"Type `help` and press Enter, to display some basic usage information for HBase Shell, as well as several example commands. Notice that table names, rows, columns all must be enclosed in quote characters."},"11793":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1993","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#create-a-table","content":"Use the `create` command to create a new table. You must specify the table name and the ColumnFamily name."},"11794":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1994","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#list-information-about-your-table","content":"Use the `list` command to confirm your table exists"},"11795":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1995","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#list-information-about-your-table","content":"Now use the `describe` command to see details, including configuration defaults"},"11796":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1996","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#put-data-into-your-table","content":"To put data into your table, use the `put` command."},"11797":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1997","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#put-data-into-your-table","content":"Here, we insert three values, one at a time. The first insert is at `row1`, column `cf:a`, with a value of `value1`. Columns in HBase are comprised of a column family prefix, `cf` in this example, followed by a colon and then a column qualifier suffix, `a` in this case."},"11798":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1998","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scan-the-table-for-all-data-at-once","content":"One of the ways to get data from HBase is to scan. Use the `scan` command to scan the table for data. You can limit your scan, but for now, all data is fetched."},"11799":{"id":"/docs/single-page-1999","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#get-a-single-row-of-data","content":"To get a single row of data at a time, use the `get` command."},"11800":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2000","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-a-table","content":"If you want to delete a table or change its settings, as well as in some other situations, you need to disable the table first, using the `disable` command. You can re-enable it using the `enable` command."},"11801":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2001","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-a-table","content":"Disable the table again if you tested the `enable` command above:"},"11802":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2002","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#drop-the-table","content":"To drop (delete) a table, use the `drop` command."},"11803":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2003","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#exit-the-hbase-shell","content":"To exit the HBase Shell and disconnect from your cluster, use the `quit` command. HBase is still running in the background."},"11804":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2004","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"In the same way that the *bin/start-hbase.sh* script is provided to conveniently start all HBase daemons, the *bin/stop-hbase.sh* script stops them."},"11805":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2005","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"After issuing the command, it can take several minutes for the processes to shut down. Use the `jps` to be sure that the HMaster and HRegionServer processes are shut down."},"11806":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2006","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-stop-hbase","content":"The above has shown you how to start and stop a standalone instance of HBase. In the next sections we give a quick overview of other modes of hbase deploy."},"11807":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2007","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing","content":"After working your way through quickstart standalone mode, you can re-configure HBase to run in pseudo-distributed mode. Pseudo-distributed mode means that HBase still runs completely on a single host, but each HBase daemon (HMaster, HRegionServer, and ZooKeeper) runs as a separate process: in standalone mode all daemons ran in one jvm process/instance. By default, unless you configure the `hbase.rootdir` property as described in quickstar&#x74;, your data is still stored in &#x2A;/tmp/*. In this walk-through, we store your data in HDFS instead, assuming you have HDFS available. You can skip the HDFS configuration to continue storing your data in the local filesystem."},"11808":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2008","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing","content":"This procedure assumes that you have configured Hadoop and HDFS on your local system and/or a\nremote system, and that they are running and available. It also assumes you are using Hadoop 2.\nThe guide on Setting up a Single Node\nCluster\nin the Hadoop documentation is a good starting point."},"11809":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2009","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stop-hbase-if-it-is-running","content":"If you have just finished quickstart and HBase is still running, stop it. This procedure will create a totally new directory where HBase will store its data, so any databases you created before will be lost."},"11810":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2010","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hbase","content":"Edit the *hbase-site.xml* configuration. First, add the following property which directs HBase to run in distributed mode, with one JVM instance per daemon."},"11811":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2011","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hbase","content":"Next, add a configuration for `hbase.rootdir`, pointing to the address of your HDFS instance, using the `hdfs:////` URI syntax. In this example, HDFS is running on the localhost at port 8020."},"11812":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2012","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hbase","content":"You do not need to create the directory in HDFS. HBase will do this for you. If you create the directory, HBase will attempt to do a migration, which is not what you want."},"11813":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2013","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hbase","content":"Finally, remove existing configuration for `hbase.tmp.dir` and `hbase.unsafe.stream.capability.enforce`."},"11814":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2014","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-hbase","content":"Use the *bin/start-hbase.sh* command to start HBase. If your system is configured correctly, the `jps` command should show the HMaster and HRegionServer processes running."},"11815":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2015","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#check-the-hbase-directory-in-hdfs","content":"If everything worked correctly, HBase created its directory in HDFS. In the configuration above, it is stored in &#x2A;/hbase/* on HDFS. You can use the `hadoop fs` command in Hadoop's &#x2A;bin/* directory to list this directory."},"11816":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2016","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#create-a-table-and-populate-it-with-data","content":"You can use the HBase Shell to create a table, populate it with data, scan and get values from it, using the same procedure as in shell exercises."},"11817":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2017","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"Running multiple HMaster instances on the same hardware does not make sense in a production\nenvironment, in the same way that running a pseudo-distributed cluster does not make sense for\nproduction. This step is offered for testing and learning purposes only."},"11818":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2018","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"The HMaster server controls the HBase cluster. You can start up to 9 backup HMaster servers, which makes 10 total HMasters, counting the primary. To start a backup HMaster, use the `local-master-backup.sh`. For each backup master you want to start, add a parameter representing the port offset for that master. Each HMaster uses two ports (16000 and 16010 by default). The port offset is added to these ports, so using an offset of 2, the backup HMaster would use ports 16002 and 16012. The following command starts 3 backup servers using ports 16002/16012, 16003/16013, and 16005/16015."},"11819":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2019","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-and-stop-a-backup-hbase-master-hmaster-server","content":"To kill a backup master without killing the entire cluster, you need to find its process ID (PID). The PID is stored in a file with a name like */tmp/hbase-USER-X-master.pid*. The only contents of the file is the PID. You can use the `kill -9` command to kill that PID. The following command will kill the master with port offset 1, but leave the cluster running:"},"11820":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2020","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-and-stop-additional-regionservers","content":"The HRegionServer manages the data in its StoreFiles as directed by the HMaster. Generally, one HRegionServer runs per node in the cluster. Running multiple HRegionServers on the same system can be useful for testing in pseudo-distributed mode. The `local-regionservers.sh` command allows you to run multiple RegionServers. It works in a similar way to the `local-master-backup.sh` command, in that each parameter you provide represents the port offset for an instance. Each RegionServer requires two ports, and the default ports are 16020 and 16030. Since HBase version 1.1.0, HMaster doesn't use region server ports, this leaves 10 ports (16020 to 16029 and 16030 to 16039) to be used for RegionServers. For supporting additional RegionServers, set environment variables HBASE\\_RS\\_BASE\\_PORT and HBASE\\_RS\\_INFO\\_BASE\\_PORT to appropriate values before running script `local-regionservers.sh`. e.g. With values 16200 and 16300 for base ports, 99 additional RegionServers can be supported, on a server. The following command starts four additional RegionServers, running on sequential ports starting at 16022/16032 (base ports 16020/16030 plus 2)."},"11821":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2021","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-and-stop-additional-regionservers","content":"To stop a RegionServer manually, use the `local-regionservers.sh` command with the `stop` parameter and the offset of the server to stop."},"11822":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2022","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getting-started-pseudo-distributed-for-local-testing-stop-hbase","content":"You can stop HBase the same way as in the quickstart procedure, using the *bin/stop-hbase.sh* command."},"11823":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2023","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"In reality, you need a fully-distributed configuration to fully test HBase and to use it in real-world scenarios. In a distributed configuration, the cluster contains multiple nodes, each of which runs one or more HBase daemon. These include primary and backup Master instances, multiple ZooKeeper nodes, and multiple RegionServer nodes."},"11824":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2024","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"This advanced quickstart adds two more nodes to your cluster. The architecture will be as follows:"},"11825":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2025","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Node Name"},"11826":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2026","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Master"},"11827":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2027","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"ZooKeeper"},"11828":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2028","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"RegionServer"},"11829":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2029","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-a.example.com"},"11830":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2030","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11831":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2031","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11832":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2032","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"no"},"11833":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2033","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-b.example.com"},"11834":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2034","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"backup"},"11835":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2035","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11836":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2036","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11837":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2037","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"node-c.example.com"},"11838":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2038","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"no"},"11839":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2039","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11840":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2040","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"yes"},"11841":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2041","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Distributed Cluster Demo Architecture"},"11842":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2042","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"This quickstart assumes that each node is a virtual machine and that they are all on the same network. It builds upon the previous quickstart, Pseudo-Distributed for Local Testing, assuming that the system you configured in that procedure is now `node-a`. Stop HBase on `node-a` before continuing."},"11843":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2043","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fully-distributed-for-production","content":"Be sure that all the nodes have full access to communicate, and that no firewall rules are in\nplace which could prevent them from talking to each other. If you see any errors like `no route to\n  host`, check your firewall."},"11844":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2044","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-configure-passwordless-ssh-access","content":"`node-a` needs to be able to log into `node-b` and `node-c` (and to itself) in order to start the daemons. The easiest way to accomplish this is to use the same username on all hosts, and configure password-less SSH login from `node-a` to each of the others."},"11845":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2045","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#on-node-a-generate-a-key-pair","content":"While logged in as the user who will run HBase, generate a SSH key pair, using the following command:"},"11846":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2046","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#on-node-a-generate-a-key-pair","content":"If the command succeeds, the location of the key pair is printed to standard output. The default name of the public key is *id\\_rsa.pub*."},"11847":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2047","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#create-the-directory-that-will-hold-the-shared-keys-on-the-other-nodes","content":"On `node-b` and `node-c&#x60;, log in as the HBase user and create a &#x2A;.ssh/* directory in the user's home directory, if it does not already exist. If it already exists, be aware that it may already contain other keys."},"11848":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2048","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#copy-the-public-key-to-the-other-nodes","content":"Securely copy the public key from `node-a` to each of the nodes, by using the `scp&#x60; or some other secure means. On each of the other nodes, create a new file called *.ssh/authorized\\_keys* *if it does not already exist*, and append the contents of the *id\\_rsa.pub* file to the end of it. Note that you also need to do this for `node-a` itself."},"11849":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2049","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#test-password-less-login","content":"If you performed the procedure correctly, you should not be prompted for a password when you SSH from `node-a` to either of the other nodes using the same username."},"11850":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2050","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#test-password-less-login","content":"Since `node-b` will run a backup Master, repeat the procedure above, substituting `node-b` everywhere you see `node-a&#x60;. Be sure not to overwrite your existing *.ssh/authorized\\_keys* files, but concatenate the new key onto the existing file using the `>>` operator rather than the `>` operator."},"11851":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2051","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"`node-a` will run your primary master and ZooKeeper processes, but no RegionServers. Stop the RegionServer from starting on `node-a`."},"11852":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2052","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"Edit *conf/regionservers* and remove the line which contains `localhost`. Add lines with the hostnames or IP addresses for `node-b` and `node-c`."},"11853":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2053","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-prepare-node-a","content":"Even if you did want to run a RegionServer on `node-a`, you should refer to it by the hostname the other servers would use to communicate with it. In this case, that would be `node-a.example.com`. This enables you to distribute the configuration to each node of your cluster any hostname conflicts. Save the file."},"11854":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2054","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hbase-to-use-node-b-as-a-backup-master","content":"Create a new file in &#x2A;conf/* called *backup-masters*, and add a new line to it with the hostname for `node-b`. In this demonstration, the hostname is `node-b.example.com`."},"11855":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2055","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-zookeeper","content":"In reality, you should carefully consider your ZooKeeper configuration. You can find out more about configuring ZooKeeper in zookeeper section. This configuration will direct HBase to start and manage a ZooKeeper instance on each node of the cluster."},"11856":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2056","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-zookeeper","content":"On `node-a`, edit *conf/hbase-site.xml* and add the following properties."},"11857":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2057","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-zookeeper","content":"Everywhere in your configuration that you have referred to `node-a` as `localhost`, change the reference to point to the hostname that the other nodes will use to refer to `node-a`. In these examples, the hostname is `node-a.example.com`."},"11858":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2058","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-prepare-node-b-and-node-c","content":"`node-b` will run a backup master server and a ZooKeeper instance."},"11859":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2059","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#download-and-unpack-hbase","content":"Download and unpack HBase to `node-b`, just as you did for the standalone and pseudo-distributed quickstarts."},"11860":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2060","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#copy-the-configuration-files-from-node-a-to-node-band-node-c","content":"Each node of your cluster needs to have the same configuration information. Copy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to the &#x2A;conf/* directory on `node-b` and `node-c`."},"11861":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2061","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#be-sure-hbase-is-not-running-on-any-node","content":"If you forgot to stop HBase from previous testing, you will have errors. Check to see whether HBase is running on any of your nodes by using the `jps` command. Look for the processes `HMaster`, `HRegionServer`, and `HQuorumPeer`. If they exist, kill them."},"11862":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2062","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-the-cluster","content":"On `node-a`, issue the `start-hbase.sh` command. Your output will be similar to that below."},"11863":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2063","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#start-the-cluster","content":"ZooKeeper starts first, followed by the master, then the RegionServers, and finally the backup masters."},"11864":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2064","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"On each node of the cluster, run the `jps` command and verify that the correct processes are running on each server. You may see additional Java processes running on your servers as well, if they are used for other purposes."},"11865":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2065","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-a` `jps` Output"},"11866":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2066","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-b` `jps` Output"},"11867":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2067","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"`node-c` `jps` Output"},"11868":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2068","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#verify-that-the-processes-are-running","content":"The `HQuorumPeer` process is a ZooKeeper instance which is controlled and started by HBase. If you\nuse ZooKeeper this way, it is limited to one instance per cluster node and is appropriate for\ntesting only. If ZooKeeper is run outside of HBase, the process is called `QuorumPeer`. For more\nabout ZooKeeper configuration, including using an external ZooKeeper instance with HBase, see\nzookeeper section."},"11869":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2069","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#browse-to-the-web-ui","content":"If everything is set up correctly, you should be able to connect to the UI for the Master `http://node-a.example.com:16010/` or the secondary master at `http://node-b.example.com:16010/` using a web browser. If you can connect via `localhost` but not from another host, check your firewall rules. You can see the web UI for each of the RegionServers at port 16030 of their IP addresses, or by clicking their links in the web UI for the Master."},"11870":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2070","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#test-what-happens-when-nodes-or-services-disappear","content":"With a three-node cluster you have configured, things will not be very resilient. You can still test the behavior of the primary Master or a RegionServer by killing the associated processes and watching the logs."},"11871":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2071","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#where-to-go-next","content":"The next chapter, configuration, gives more information about the different HBase run modes, system requirements for running HBase, and critical configuration areas for setting up a distributed HBase cluster."},"11872":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2072","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration","content":"This chapter expands upon the Getting Started chapter to further explain configuration of Apache HBase. Please read this chapter carefully, especially the Basic Prerequisites to ensure that your HBase testing and deployment goes smoothly. Familiarize yourself with Support and Testing Expectations as well."},"11873":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2073","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"Apache HBase uses the same configuration system as Apache Hadoop. All configuration files are located in the &#x2A;conf/* directory, which needs to be kept in sync for each node on your cluster."},"11874":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2074","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***backup-masters***\\\nNot present by default. A plain-text file which lists hosts on which the Master should start a backup Master process, one host per line."},"11875":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2075","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***hadoop-metrics2-hbase.properties***\\\nUsed to connect HBase Hadoop's Metrics2 framework. See the Hadoop Wiki entry for more information on Metrics2. Contains only commented-out examples by default."},"11876":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2076","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-env.cmd* and *hbase-env.sh***\\\nScript for Windows and Linux / Unix environments to set up the working environment for HBase, including the location of Java, Java options, and other environment variables. The file contains many commented-out examples to provide guidance."},"11877":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2077","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-policy.xml***\\\nThe default policy configuration file used by RPC servers to make authorization decisions on client requests. Only used if HBase security is enabled."},"11878":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2078","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***hbase-site.xml***\\\nThe main HBase configuration file. This file specifies configuration options which override HBase's default configuration. You can view (but do not edit) the default configuration file at *hbase-common/src/main/resources/hbase-default.xml*. You can also view the entire effective configuration for your cluster (defaults and overrides) in the **HBase Configuration** tab of the HBase Web UI."},"11879":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2079","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***log4j2.properties***\\\nConfiguration file for HBase logging via `log4j2`."},"11880":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2080","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"***regionservers***\\\nA plain-text file containing a list of hosts which should run a RegionServer in your HBase cluster. By default, this file contains the single entry `localhost`. It should contain a list of hostnames or IP addresses, one per line, and should only contain `localhost` if each node in your cluster will run a RegionServer on its `localhost` interface."},"11881":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2081","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"When you edit XML, it is a good idea to use an XML-aware editor to be sure that your syntax is\ncorrect and your XML is well-formed. You can also use the `xmllint` utility to check that your XML\nis well-formed. By default, `xmllint` re-flows and prints the XML to standard output. To check for\nwell-formedness and only print output if errors exist, use the command `xmllint -noout\n  filename.xml`."},"11882":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2082","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-files","content":"When running in distributed mode, after you make an edit to an HBase configuration, make sure you\ncopy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to all nodes of the cluster. HBase will not do this for\nyou. Use a configuration management tool for managing and copying the configuration files to your\nnodes. For most configurations, a restart is needed for servers to pick up changes. Dynamic\nconfiguration is an exception to this, to be described later below."},"11883":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2083","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"HBase runs on the Java Virtual Machine, thus all HBase deployments require a JVM runtime."},"11884":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2084","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"The following table summarizes the HBase community's recommendations for Java versions:"},"11885":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2085","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"✅ indicates a recommended deployment combination with a base level of testing. The community is willing to help diagnose and address issues."},"11886":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2086","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"⚠️ indicates possible compatibility challenges. Review the available guidance before choosing this combination."},"11887":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2087","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"❌ indicates an unsupported combination. The Java version is deprecated by the HBase community, or the combination is known not to work."},"11888":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2088","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"Treat any combination not listed in the table as ❌."},"11889":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2089","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"Newer JDKs may have compatibility issues with older HBase releases that cannot be fixed within HBase's compatibility guarantees."},"11890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2090","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"Check the table notes for limitations, including build failures, test failures, and operational issues."},"11891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2091","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"HBase recommends downstream users rely only on JDK releases that are marked as Long-Term Supported\n(LTS), either from the OpenJDK project or vendors. At the time of this writing, the following JDK\nreleases are NOT LTS releases and are NOT tested or advocated for use by the Apache HBase\ncommunity: JDK9, JDK10, JDK12, JDK13, and JDK14. Community discussion around this decision is\nrecorded on HBASE-20264."},"11892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2092","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-basic-prerequisites-java","content":"At this time, all testing performed by the Apache HBase project runs on the HotSpot variant of the\nJVM. When selecting your JDK distribution, please take this into consideration."},"11893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2093","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"HBase Version"},"11894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2094","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 6"},"11895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2095","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 7"},"11896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2096","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 8"},"11897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2097","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 11"},"11898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2098","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 17"},"11899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2099","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"JDK 21"},"11900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2100","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"HBase 3.1"},"11901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2101","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"❌"},"11902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2102","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"❌"},"11903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2103","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-support-by-release-line","content":"❌"},"11904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2104","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url