le-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Y"},"12778":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Operational Compatibility"},"12779":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"N"},"12780":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"N"},"12781":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"Y"},"12782":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compatibility-matrix-toc","content":"HBase 1.7.0 release violated client-server wire compatibility guarantees and was subsequently\nwithdrawn after the incompatibilities were reported and fixed in 1.7.1. If you are considering an\nupgrade to 1.7.x line, see Upgrading to 1.7.1+."},"12783":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"HBase has a lot of API points, but for the compatibility matrix above, we differentiate between Client API, Limited Private API, and Private API. HBase uses Apache Yetus Audience Annotations to guide downstream expectations for stability."},"12784":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"InterfaceAudience (javadocs): captures the intended audience, possible values include:"},"12785":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Public: safe for end users and external projects"},"12786":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"LimitedPrivate: used for internals we expect to be pluggable, such as coprocessors"},"12787":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Private: strictly for use within HBase itself Classes which are defined as `IA.Private` may be used as parameters or return values for interfaces which are declared `IA.LimitedPrivate`. Treat the `IA.Private` object as opaque; do not try to access its methods or fields directly."},"12788":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"InterfaceStability (javadocs): describes what types of interface changes are permitted. Possible values include:"},"12789":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Stable: the interface is fixed and is not expected to change"},"12790":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Evolving: the interface may change in future minor versions"},"12791":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Unstable: the interface may change at any time"},"12792":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"Please keep in mind the following interactions between the `InterfaceAudience` and `InterfaceStability` annotations within the HBase project:"},"12793":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2993","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"`IA.Public` classes are inherently stable and adhere to our stability guarantees relating to the type of upgrade (major, minor, or patch)."},"12794":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2994","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"`IA.LimitedPrivate` classes should always be annotated with one of the given `InterfaceStability` values. If they are not, you should presume they are `IS.Unstable`."},"12795":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2995","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-api-surface","content":"`IA.Private` classes should be considered implicitly unstable, with no guarantee of stability between releases."},"12796":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2996","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-client-api","content":"HBase Client API consists of all the classes or methods that are marked with InterfaceAudience.Public interface. All main classes in hbase-client and dependent modules have either InterfaceAudience.Public, InterfaceAudience.LimitedPrivate, or InterfaceAudience.Private marker. Not all classes in other modules (hbase-server, etc) have the marker. If a class is not annotated with one of these, it is assumed to be a InterfaceAudience.Private class."},"12797":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2997","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-limitedprivate-api","content":"LimitedPrivate annotation comes with a set of target consumers for the interfaces. Those consumers are coprocessors, phoenix, replication endpoint implementations or similar. At this point, HBase only guarantees source and binary compatibility for these interfaces between patch versions."},"12798":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2998","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-private-api","content":"All classes annotated with InterfaceAudience.Private or all classes that do not have the annotation are for HBase internal use only. The interfaces and method signatures can change at any point in time. If you are relying on a particular interface that is marked Private, you should open a jira to propose changing the interface to be Public or LimitedPrivate, or an interface exposed for this purpose."},"12799":{"id":"/docs/single-page-2999","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-compatibility","content":"When we say two HBase versions are compatible, we mean that the versions are wire and binary compatible. Compatible HBase versions means that clients can talk to compatible but differently versioned servers. It means too that you can just swap out the jars of one version and replace them with the jars of another, compatible version and all will just work. Unless otherwise specified, HBase point versions are (mostly) binary compatible. You can safely do rolling upgrades between binary compatible versions; i.e. across maintenance releases: e.g. from 1.4.4 to 1.4.6. See \"Does compatibility between versions also mean binary compatibility?\" discussion on the HBase dev mailing list."},"12800":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3000","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-compatibility","content":"See 'Source Compatibility' https\\://wiki.openjdk.org/spaces/csr/pages/32342052/Kinds+of+Compatibility"},"12801":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3001","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-compatibility","content":"See http\\://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se8/html/jls-13.html."},"12802":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3002","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-compatibility","content":"Running an offline upgrade tool without downgrade might be needed. We will typically only support migrating data from major version X to major version X+1."},"12803":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3003","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling","content":"A rolling upgrade is the process by which you update the servers in your cluster a server at a time. You can rolling upgrade across HBase versions if they are binary or wire compatible. See the \"Rolling Upgrade Between Versions that are Binary/Wire Compatible\" section below for more on what this means. Coarsely, a rolling upgrade is a graceful stop each server, update the software, and then restart. You do this for each server in the cluster. Usually you upgrade the Master first and then the RegionServers. See Rolling Restart for tools that can help use the rolling upgrade process."},"12804":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3004","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling","content":"For example, in the below, HBase was symlinked to the actual HBase install. On upgrade, before running a rolling restart over the cluster, we changed the symlink to point at the new HBase software version and then ran"},"12805":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3005","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling","content":"The rolling-restart script will first gracefully stop and restart the master, and then each of the RegionServers in turn. Because the symlink was changed, on restart the server will come up using the new HBase version. Check logs for errors as the rolling upgrade proceeds."},"12806":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3006","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling-upgrade-between-versions-that-are-binarywire-compatible-toc","content":"Unless otherwise specified, HBase minor versions are binary compatible. You can do a rolling upgrades between HBase point versions. For example, you can go to 1.4.4 from 1.4.6 by doing a rolling upgrade across the cluster replacing the 1.4.4 binary with a 1.4.6 binary."},"12807":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3007","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling-upgrade-between-versions-that-are-binarywire-compatible-toc","content":"In the minor version-particular sections below, we call out where the versions are wire/protocol compatible and in this case, it is also possible to do a rolling upgrade."},"12808":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3008","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rollback","content":"Sometimes things don't go as planned when attempting an upgrade. This section explains how to perform a *rollback* to an earlier HBase release. Note that this should only be needed between Major and some Minor releases. You should always be able to *downgrade* between HBase Patch releases within the same Minor version. These instructions may require you to take steps before you start the upgrade process, so be sure to read through this section beforehand."},"12809":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3009","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rollback-vs-downgrade","content":"This section describes how to perform a *rollback* on an upgrade between HBase minor and major versions. In this document, rollback refers to the process of taking an upgraded cluster and restoring it to the old version *while losing all changes that have occurred since upgrade*. By contrast, a cluster *downgrade* would restore an upgraded cluster to the old version while maintaining any data written since the upgrade. We currently only offer instructions to rollback HBase clusters. Further, rollback only works when these instructions are followed prior to performing the upgrade."},"12810":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3010","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rollback-vs-downgrade","content":"When these instructions talk about rollback vs downgrade of prerequisite cluster services (i.e. HDFS), you should treat leaving the service version the same as a degenerate case of downgrade."},"12811":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3011","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication","content":"Unless you are doing an all-service rollback, the HBase cluster will lose any configured peers for HBase replication. If your cluster is configured for HBase replication, then prior to following these instructions you should document all replication peers. After performing the rollback you should then add each documented peer back to the cluster. Note also that data written to the cluster since the upgrade may or may not have already been replicated to any peers. Determining which, if any, peers have seen replication data as well as rolling back the data in those peers is out of the scope of this guide."},"12812":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3012","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-locality","content":"Unless you are doing an all-service rollback, going through a rollback procedure will likely destroy all locality for Region Servers. You should expect degraded performance until after the cluster has had time to go through compactions to restore data locality. Optionally, you can force a compaction to speed this process up at the cost of generating cluster load."},"12813":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3013","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurable-locations","content":"The instructions below assume default locations for the HBase data directory and the HBase znode. Both of these locations are configurable and you should verify the value used in your cluster before proceeding. In the event that you have a different value, just replace the default with the one found in your configuration \\* HBase data directory is configured via the key 'hbase.rootdir' and has a default value of '/hbase'. \\* HBase znode is configured via the key 'zookeeper.znode.parent' and has a default value of '/hbase'."},"12814":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3014","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#all-service-rollback","content":"If you will be performing a rollback of both the HDFS and ZooKeeper services, then HBase's data will be rolled back in the process."},"12815":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3015","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements","content":"Ability to rollback HDFS and ZooKeeper"},"12816":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3016","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade","content":"No additional steps are needed pre-upgrade. As an extra precautionary measure, you may wish to use distcp to back up the HBase data off of the cluster to be upgraded. To do so, follow the steps in the 'Before upgrade' section of 'Rollback after HDFS downgrade' but copy to another HDFS instance instead of within the same instance."},"12817":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3017","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback","content":"Stop HBase"},"12818":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3018","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback","content":"Perform a rollback for HDFS and ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"12819":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3019","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"12820":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3020","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback","content":"Start HBase"},"12821":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3021","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback","content":"Verify HBase contents — use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"12822":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3022","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rollback-after-hdfs-rollback-and-zookeeper-downgrade","content":"If you will be rolling back HDFS but going through a ZooKeeper downgrade, then HBase will be in an inconsistent state. You must ensure the cluster is not started until you complete this process."},"12823":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3023","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-1","content":"Ability to rollback HDFS"},"12824":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3024","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-1","content":"Ability to downgrade ZooKeeper"},"12825":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3025","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-1","content":"No additional steps are needed pre-upgrade. As an extra precautionary measure, you may wish to use distcp to back up the HBase data off of the cluster to be upgraded. To do so, follow the steps in the 'Before upgrade' section of 'Rollback after HDFS downgrade' but copy to another HDFS instance instead of within the same instance."},"12826":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3026","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Stop HBase"},"12827":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3027","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Perform a rollback for HDFS and a downgrade for ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"12828":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3028","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"12829":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3029","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Clean out ZooKeeper information related to HBase. WARNING: This step will permanently destroy all replication peers. Please see the section on HBase Replication under Caveats for more information."},"12830":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3030","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"**Clean HBase information out of ZooKeeper:**"},"12831":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3031","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Start HBase"},"12832":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3032","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-1","content":"Verify HBase contents—use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"12833":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3033","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rollback-after-hdfs-downgrade","content":"If you will be performing an HDFS downgrade, then you'll need to follow these instructions regardless of whether ZooKeeper goes through rollback, downgrade, or reinstallation."},"12834":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3034","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-2","content":"Ability to downgrade HDFS"},"12835":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3035","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-2","content":"Pre-upgrade cluster must be able to run MapReduce jobs"},"12836":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3036","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-2","content":"HDFS super user access"},"12837":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3037","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#requirements-2","content":"Sufficient space in HDFS for at least two copies of the HBase data directory"},"12838":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3038","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-2","content":"Before beginning the upgrade process, you must take a complete backup of HBase's backing data. The following instructions cover backing up the data within the current HDFS instance. Alternatively, you can use the distcp command to copy the data to another HDFS cluster."},"12839":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3039","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-2","content":"Stop the HBase cluster"},"12840":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3040","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-2","content":"Copy the HBase data directory to a backup location using the distcp command as the HDFS super user (shown below on a security enabled cluster)"},"12841":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3041","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-2","content":"**Using distcp to backup the HBase data directory:**"},"12842":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3042","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#before-upgrade-2","content":"Distcp will launch a mapreduce job to handle copying the files in a distributed fashion. Check the output of the distcp command to ensure this job completed successfully."},"12843":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3043","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Stop HBase"},"12844":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3044","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Perform a downgrade for HDFS and a downgrade/rollback for ZooKeeper (HBase should remain stopped)"},"12845":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3045","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Change the installed version of HBase to the previous version"},"12846":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3046","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Restore the HBase data directory from prior to the upgrade. Restore the HBase data directory from prior to the upgrade as the HDFS super user (shown below on a security enabled cluster). If you backed up your data on another HDFS cluster instead of locally, you will need to use the distcp command to copy it back to the current HDFS cluster."},"12847":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3047","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"**Restore the HBase data directory:**"},"12848":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3048","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Clean out ZooKeeper information related to HBase. WARNING: This step will permanently destroy all replication peers. Please see the section on HBase Replication under Caveats for more information."},"12849":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3049","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"**Clean HBase information out of ZooKeeper:**"},"12850":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3050","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Start HBase"},"12851":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3051","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performing-a-rollback-2","content":"Verify HBase contents–use the HBase shell to list tables and scan some known values."},"12852":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3052","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"The RegionServer Grouping feature has been reimplemented. See section Migrating From Old Implementation in Apache HBase Operational Management for more details."},"12853":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3053","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"The `hbase:namespace` table has been removed and fold into `hbase:meta`. See Data Model for more details."},"12854":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3054","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-2x-to-3x","content":"There is no special consideration upgrading to hbase-2.4.x from 2.3.x. And for earlier versions, just follow the Upgrade from 2.0.x-2.2.x to 2.3+ guide. In general, 2.2.x should be rolling upgradeable, for 2.1.x or 2.0.x, you will need to clear the Upgrade from 2.0 or 2.1 to 2.2+ hurdle first."},"12855":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3055","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20x-22x-to-23","content":"There is no special consideration upgrading to hbase-2.3.x from earlier versions. From 2.2.x, it should be rolling upgradeable. From 2.1.x or 2.0.x, you will need to clear the Upgrade from 2.0 or 2.1 to 2.2+ hurdle first."},"12856":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3056","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgraded-zookeeper-dependency-version","content":"Our dependency on Apache ZooKeeper has been upgraded to 3.5.7 (HBASE-24132), as 3.4.x is EOL. The newer 3.5.x client is compatible with the older 3.4.x server. However, if you're using HBase in stand-alone mode and perform an in-place upgrade, there are some upgrade steps documented by the ZooKeeper community. This doesn't impact a production deployment, but would impact a developer's local environment."},"12857":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3057","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#new-in-master-procedure-store","content":"Of note, HBase 2.3.0 changes the in-Master Procedure Store implementation. It was a dedicated custom store to instead use a standard HBase Region (HBASE-23326). The migration from the old to new format is automatic run by the new 2.3.0 Master on startup. The old *MasterProcWALs&#x2A; dir which hosted the old custom implementation files in &#x2A;$\\{hbase.rootdir}* is deleted on successful migration. A new *MasterProc&#x2A; sub-directory replaces it to host the Store files and WALs for the new Procedure Store in-Master Region. The in-Master Region is unusual in that it writes to an alternate location at *$\\{hbase.rootdir}/MasterProc&#x2A; rather than under *$\\{hbase.rootdir}/data* in the filesystem and the special Procedure Store in-Master Region is hidden from all clients other than the active Master itself. Otherwise, it is like any other with the Master process running flushes and compactions, archiving WALs when over-flushed, and so on. Its files are readable by standard Region and Store file tooling for triage and analysis as long as they are pointed to the appropriate location in the filesystem."},"12858":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3058","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#new-in-master-procedure-store","content":"Notice that, after the migration, you should make sure to not start an active master with old code, as it can not recognize the new procedure store. So it is suggested to upgrade backup master(s) to new 2.3 first, and then upgrade the active master. And unless explicitly mentioned, this is the suggested way for all upgrading, i.e, upgrading backup master(s) first, then active master, and then region servers."},"12859":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3059","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"HBase 2.2+ uses a new Procedure form assigning/unassigning/moving Regions. It does not process HBase 2.1 and 2.0's Unassign/Assign Procedure types. Upgrade requires that we first drain the Master Procedure Store of old style Procedures before starting the new 2.2 Master. So you need to make sure that before you kill the old version (2.0 or 2.1) Master, there is no region in transition. And once the new version (2.2+) Master is up, you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one."},"12860":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3060","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"And there is a more safer way if you are running 2.1.1+ or 2.0.3+ cluster. It need four steps to upgrade Master."},"12861":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3061","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Shutdown both active and standby Masters (Your cluster will continue to server reads and writes without interruption)."},"12862":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3062","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Set the property hbase.procedure.upgrade-to-2-2 to true in hbase-site.xml for the Master, and start only one Master, still using the 2.1.1+ (or 2.0.3+) version."},"12863":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3063","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Wait until the Master quits. Confirm that there is a 'UPGRADE OK: All existed procedures have been finished, quit...' message in the Master log as the cause of the shutdown. The Procedure Store is now empty."},"12864":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3064","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Start new Masters with the new 2.2+ version."},"12865":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3065","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"Then you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one. See HBASE-21075 for more details."},"12866":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3066","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"In case these steps are not done, on starting 2.2+ master, you would see the following exception in the master logs:"},"12867":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3067","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-from-20-or-21-to-22","content":"`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.HBaseIOException: Unsupported procedure type class org.apache.hadoop.hbase.master.assignment.UnassignProcedure found`"},"12868":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3068","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-from-1x-to-2x","content":"In this section we will first call out significant changes compared to the prior stable HBase release and then go over the upgrade process. Be sure to read the former with care so you avoid surprises."},"12869":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3069","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changes-of-note","content":"First we'll cover deployment / operational changes that you might hit when upgrading to HBase 2.0+. After that we'll call out changes for downstream applications. Please note that Coprocessors are covered in the operational section. Also note that this section is not meant to convey information about new features that may be of interest to you. For a complete summary of changes, please see the CHANGES.txt file in the source release artifact for the version you are planning to upgrade to."},"12870":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3070","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#update-to-basic-prerequisite-minimums-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"As noted in the Basic Prerequisites section, HBase 2.0+ requires a minimum of Java 8 and Hadoop 2.6. The HBase community recommends ensuring you have already completed any needed upgrades in prerequisites prior to upgrading your HBase version."},"12871":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3071","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"You **must not** use an HBase 1.x version of HBCK against an HBase 2.0+ cluster. HBCK is strongly tied to the HBase server version. Using the HBCK tool from an earlier release against an HBase 2.0+ cluster will destructively alter said cluster in unrecoverable ways."},"12872":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3072","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"As of HBase 2.0, HBCK (A.K.A *HBCK1* or *hbck1*) is a read-only tool that can report the status of some non-public system internals but will often misread state because it does not understand the workings of hbase2."},"12873":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3073","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"To read about HBCK's replacement, see Operations and Management documentation."},"12874":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3074","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbck-must-match-hbase-server-version-toc","content":"Related, before you upgrade, ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type\ninconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process."},"12875":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3075","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following configuration settings are no longer applicable or available. For details, please see the detailed release notes."},"12876":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3076","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.config.read.zookeeper.config (see upgrade2.0.zkconfig for migration details)"},"12877":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3077","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.zookeeper.useMulti (HBase now always uses ZK's multi functionality)"},"12878":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3078","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.client.threads.max"},"12879":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3079","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.client.nativetransport"},"12880":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3080","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.fs.tmp.dir"},"12881":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3081","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bucketcache.combinedcache.enabled"},"12882":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3082","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bucketcache.ioengine no longer supports the 'heap' value."},"12883":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3083","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.bulkload.staging.dir"},"12884":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3084","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster wasn't removed, strictly speaking, but its meaning has fundamentally changed and users should not set it. See the section upgrade2.0.regions.on.master for details."},"12885":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3085","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.distributed.log.replay See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"12886":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3086","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.disallow\\.writes.when.recovering See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"12887":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3087","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.wal.logreplay.batch.size See the section upgrade2.0.distributed.log.replay for details"},"12888":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3088","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.catalog.timeout"},"12889":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3089","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.catalog.timeout"},"12890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3090","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.metrics.exposeOperationTimes"},"12891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3091","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.metrics.showTableName"},"12892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3092","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.online.schema.update.enable (HBase now always supports this)"},"12893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3093","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-no-longer-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.thrift.htablepool.size.max"},"12894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3094","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following properties have been renamed. Attempts to set the old property will be ignored at run time."},"12895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3095","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"Old name"},"12896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3096","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"New name"},"12897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3097","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.rpc.server.nativetransport"},"12898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3098","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.nativetransport"},"12899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3099","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.rpc.server.worker.count"},"12900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3100","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.netty.worker.count"},"12901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3101","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hfile.compactions.discharger.interval"},"12902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3102","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hfile.compaction.discharger.interval"},"12903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3103","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.percolumnfamilyflush.size.lower.bound"},"12904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3104","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties-that-were-renamed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.percolumnfamilyflush.size.lower.bound.min"},"12905":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3105","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"The following configuration settings changed their default value. Where applicable, the value to set to restore the behavior of HBase 1.2 is given."},"12906":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3106","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.security.authorization now defaults to false. set to true to restore same behavior as previous default."},"12907":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3107","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.retries.number is now set to 10. Previously it was 35. Downstream users are advised to use client timeouts as described in Configuration instead."},"12908":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3108","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.serverside.retries.multiplier is now set to 3. Previously it was 10. Downstream users are advised to use client timeouts as described in Configuration instead."},"12909":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3109","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.fileSplitTimeout is now set to 10 minutes. Previously it was 30 seconds."},"12910":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3110","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.logroll.multiplier is now set to 0.5. Previously it was 0.95. This change is tied with the following doubling of block size. Combined, these two configuration changes should make for WALs of about the same size as those in hbase-1.x but there should be less incidence of small blocks because we fail to roll the WAL before we hit the blocksize threshold. See HBASE-19148 for discussion."},"12911":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3111","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.hlog.blocksize defaults to 2x the HDFS default block size for the WAL dir. Previously it was equal to the HDFS default block size for the WAL dir."},"12912":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3112","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.start.log.errors.counter changed to 5. Previously it was 9."},"12913":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3113","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.type changed to 'fifo'. In HBase versions 1.0 - 1.2 it was 'deadline'. In prior and later 1.x versions it already defaults to 'fifo'."},"12914":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3114","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hregion.memstore.chunkpool.maxsize is 1.0 by default. Previously it was 0.0. Effectively, this means previously we would not use a chunk pool when our memstore is onheap and now we will. See the section Long GC pauses for more information about the MSLAB chunk pool."},"12915":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3115","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.cleaner.interval is now set to 10 minutes. Previously it was 1 minute."},"12916":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3116","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.master.procedure.threads will now default to 1/4 of the number of available CPUs, but not less than 16 threads. Previously it would be number of threads equal to number of CPUs."},"12917":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3117","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles is now 16. Previously it was 10."},"12918":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3118","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.http.max.threads is now 16. Previously it was 10."},"12919":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3119","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.client.max.perserver.tasks is now 2. Previously it was 5."},"12920":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3120","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.normalizer.period is now 5 minutes. Previously it was 30 minutes."},"12921":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3121","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"hbase.regionserver.region.split.policy is now SteppingSplitPolicy. Previously it was IncreasingToUpperBoundRegionSplitPolicy."},"12922":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3122","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-settings-with-different-defaults-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"replication.source.ratio is now 0.5. Previously it was 0.1."},"12923":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3123","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"The feature \"Master acts as region server\" and associated follow-on work available in HBase 1.y is non-functional in HBase 2.y and should not be used in a production setting due to deadlock on Master initialization. Downstream users are advised to treat related configuration settings as experimental and the feature as inappropriate for production settings."},"12924":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3124","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"A brief summary of related changes:"},"12925":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3125","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"Master no longer carries regions by default"},"12926":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3126","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster is a boolean, default false (if it holds an HBase 1.x list of tables, will default to false)"},"12927":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3127","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"hbase.balancer.tablesOnMaster.systemTablesOnly is boolean to keep user tables off master. default false"},"12928":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3128","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-hosting-regions-feature-broken-and-unsupported-toc","content":"those wishing to replicate old list-of-servers config should deploy a stand-alone RegionServer process and then rely on Region Server Groups"},"12929":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3129","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#distributed-log-replay-feature-broken-and-removed-toc","content":"The Distributed Log Replay feature was broken and has been removed from HBase 2.y+. As a consequence all related configs, metrics, RPC fields, and logging have also been removed. Note that this feature was found to be unreliable in the run up to HBase 1.0, defaulted to being unused, and was effectively removed in HBase 1.2.0 when we started ignoring the config that turns it on (HBASE-14465). If you are currently using the feature, be sure to perform a clean shutdown, ensure all DLR work is complete, and disable the feature prior to upgrading."},"12930":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3130","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"The prefix-tree encoding was removed from HBase 2.0.0 (HBASE-19179). It was (late!) deprecated in hbase-1.2.7, hbase-1.4.0, and hbase-1.3.2."},"12931":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3131","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"This feature was removed because it as not being actively maintained. If interested in reviving this sweet facility which improved random read latencies at the expensive of slowed writes, write the HBase developers list at *dev at hbase dot apache dot org*."},"12932":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3132","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#prefix-tree-encoding-removed-toc","content":"The prefix-tree encoding needs to be removed from all tables before upgrading to HBase 2.0+. To do that first you need to change the encoding from PREFIX\\_TREE to something else that is supported in HBase 2.0. After that you have to major compact the tables that were using PREFIX\\_TREE encoding before. To check which column families are using incompatible data block encoding you can use Pre-Upgrade Validator."},"12933":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3133","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have changed names:"},"12934":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3134","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"Metrics previously published under the name \"AssignmentManger\" \\[sic] are now published under the name \"AssignmentManager\""},"12935":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3135","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have changed their meaning:"},"12936":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3136","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The metric 'blockCacheEvictionCount' published on a per-region server basis no longer includes blocks removed from the cache due to the invalidation of the hfiles they are from (e.g. via compaction)."},"12937":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3137","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The metric 'totalRequestCount' increments once per request; previously it incremented by the number of `Actions` carried in the request; e.g. if a request was a `multi` made of four Gets and two Puts, we'd increment 'totalRequestCount' by six; now we increment by one regardless. Expect to see lower values for this metric in hbase-2.0.0."},"12938":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3138","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The 'readRequestCount' now counts reads that return a non-empty row where in older hbases, we'd increment 'readRequestCount' whether a Result or not. This change will flatten the profile of the read-requests graphs if requests for non-existent rows. A YCSB read-heavy workload can do this dependent on how the database was loaded."},"12939":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3139","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have been removed:"},"12940":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3140","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"Metrics related to the Distributed Log Replay feature are no longer present. They were previously found in the region server context under the name 'replay'. See the section \"Distributed Log Replay\" feature broken and removed for details."},"12941":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3141","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"The following metrics have been added:"},"12942":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3142","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-metrics-toc","content":"'totalRowActionRequestCount' is a count of region row actions summing reads and writes."},"12943":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3143","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-logging-toc","content":"HBase-2.0.0 now uses slf4j as its logging frontend. Previously, we used log4j (1.2). For most the transition should be seamless; slf4j does a good job interpreting *log4j.properties* logging configuration files such that you should not notice any difference in your log system emissions."},"12944":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3144","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changed-logging-toc","content":"That said, your *log4j.properties* may need freshening. See HBASE-20351 for example, where a stale log configuration file manifest as netty configuration being dumped at DEBUG level as preamble on every shell command invocation."},"12945":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3145","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#zookeeper-configs-no-longer-read-from-zoocfg-toc","content":"HBase no longer optionally reads the 'zoo.cfg' file for ZooKeeper related configuration settings. If you previously relied on the 'hbase.config.read.zookeeper.config' config for this functionality, you should migrate any needed settings to the hbase-site.xml file while adding the prefix 'hbase.zookeeper.property.' to each property name."},"12946":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3146","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"The following permission related changes either altered semantics or defaults:"},"12947":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3147","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"Permissions granted to a user now merge with existing permissions for that user, rather than over-writing them. (see the release note on HBASE-17472 for details)"},"12948":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3148","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#changes-in-permissions-toc","content":"Region Server Group commands (added in 1.4.0) now require admin privileges."},"12949":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3149","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"A number of admin commands are known to not work when used from a pre-HBase 2.0 client. This includes an HBase Shell that has the library jars from pre-HBase 2.0. You will need to plan for an outage of use of admin APIs and commands until you can also update to the needed client version."},"12950":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3150","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"The following client operations do not work against HBase 2.0+ cluster when executed from a pre-HBase 2.0 client:"},"12951":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3151","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"list\\_procedures"},"12952":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3152","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"split"},"12953":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3153","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"merge\\_region"},"12954":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3154","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"list\\_quotas"},"12955":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3155","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"enable\\_table\\_replication"},"12956":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3156","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"disable\\_table\\_replication"},"12957":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3157","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#most-admin-apis-dont-work-against-an-hbase-20-cluster-from-pre-hbase-20-clients-toc","content":"Snapshot related commands"},"12958":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3158","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#deprecated-in-10-admin-commands-have-been-removed-toc","content":"The following commands that were deprecated in 1.0 have been removed. Where applicable the replacement command is listed."},"12959":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3159","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#deprecated-in-10-admin-commands-have-been-removed-toc","content":"The 'hlog' command has been removed. Downstream users should rely on the 'wal' command instead."},"12960":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3160","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-server-memory-consumption-changes-toc","content":"Users upgrading from versions prior to HBase 1.4 should read the instructions in section Region Server memory consumption changes.."},"12961":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3161","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-server-memory-consumption-changes-toc","content":"Additionally, HBase 2.0 has changed how memstore memory is tracked for flushing decisions. Previously, both the data size and overhead for storage were used to calculate utilization against the flush threshold. Now, only data size is used to make these per-region decisions. Globally the addition of the storage overhead is used to make decisions about forced flushes."},"12962":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3162","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#web-ui-for-splitting-and-merging-operate-on-row-prefixes-toc","content":"Previously, the Web UI included functionality on table status pages to merge or split based on an encoded region name. In HBase 2.0, instead this functionality works by taking a row prefix."},"12963":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3163","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#special-upgrading-for-replication-users-from-pre-hbase-14-toc","content":"User running versions of HBase prior to the 1.4.0 release that make use of replication should be sure to read the instructions in the section Replication peer's TableCFs config."},"12964":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3164","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-shell-changes-toc","content":"The HBase shell command relies on a bundled JRuby instance. This bundled JRuby been updated from version 1.6.8 to version 9.1.10.0. The represents a change from Ruby 1.8 to Ruby 2.3.3, which introduces non-compatible language changes for user scripts."},"12965":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3165","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-shell-changes-toc","content":"The HBase shell command now ignores the '--return-values' flag that was present in early HBase 1.4 releases. Instead the shell always behaves as though that flag were passed. If you wish to avoid having expression results printed in the console you should alter your IRB configuration."},"12966":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3166","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"All Coprocessor APIs have been refactored to improve supportability around binary API compatibility for future versions of HBase. If you or applications you rely on have custom HBase coprocessors, you should read the release notes for HBASE-18169 for details of changes you will need to make prior to upgrading to HBase 2.0+."},"12967":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3167","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"For example, if you had a BaseRegionObserver in HBase 1.2 then at a minimum you will need to update it to implement both RegionObserver and RegionCoprocessor and add the method"},"12968":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3168","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-apis-have-changed-in-hbase-20-toc","content":"For more information, see Upgrading Coprocessors to 2.0."},"12969":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3169","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-20-can-no-longer-write-hfile-v2-files-toc","content":"HBase has simplified our internal HFile handling. As a result, we can no longer write HFile versions earlier than the default of version 3. Upgrading users should ensure that hfile.format.version is not set to 2 in hbase-site.xml before upgrading. Failing to do so will cause Region Server failure. HBase can still read HFiles written in the older version 2 format."},"12970":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3170","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"HBase can no longer read the deprecated WAL files written in the Apache Hadoop Sequence File format. The hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and hbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configuration entries should be set to use the Protobuf based WAL reader / writer classes. This implementation has been the default since HBase 0.96, so legacy WAL files should not be a concern for most downstream users."},"12971":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3171","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"Starting from 2.6.0, the hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and hbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configuration entries are removed since the only valid values are protobuf based reader/writer. Setting them in *hbase-site.xml* will have no real effect."},"12972":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3172","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-20-can-no-longer-read-sequence-file-based-wal-file-toc","content":"A clean cluster shutdown should ensure there are no WAL files. If you are unsure of a given WAL file's format you can use the `hbase wal` command to parse files while the HBase cluster is offline. In HBase 2.0+, this command will not be able to read a Sequence File based WAL."},"12973":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3173","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-in-behavior-for-filters-toc","content":"The Filter ReturnCode NEXT\\_ROW has been redefined as skipping to next row in current family, not to next row in all family. it's more reasonable, because ReturnCode is a concept in store level, not in region level."},"12974":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3174","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#downstream-hbase-20-users-should-use-the-shaded-client-toc","content":"Downstream users are strongly urged to rely on the Maven coordinates org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-client for their runtime use. This artifact contains all the needed implementation details for talking to an HBase cluster while minimizing the number of third party dependencies exposed."},"12975":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3175","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#downstream-hbase-20-users-should-use-the-shaded-client-toc","content":"Note that this artifact exposes some classes in the org.apache.hadoop package space (e.g. o.a.h.configuration.Configuration) so that we can maintain source compatibility with our public API. Those classes are included so that they can be altered to use the same relocated third party dependencies as the rest of the HBase client code. In the event that you need to **also** use Hadoop in your code, you should ensure all Hadoop related jars precede the HBase client jar in your classpath."},"12976":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3176","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#downstream-hbase-20-users-of-mapreduce-must-switch-to-new-artifact-toc","content":"Downstream users of HBase's integration for Apache Hadoop MapReduce must switch to relying on the org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-mapreduce module for their runtime use. Historically, downstream users relied on either the org.apache.hbase:hbase-server or org.apache.hbase:hbase-shaded-server artifacts for these classes. Both uses are no longer supported and in the vast majority of cases will fail at runtime."},"12977":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3177","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#downstream-hbase-20-users-of-mapreduce-must-switch-to-new-artifact-toc","content":"Note that this artifact exposes some classes in the org.apache.hadoop package space (e.g. o.a.h.configuration.Configuration) so that we can maintain source compatibility with our public API. Those classes are included so that they can be altered to use the same relocated third party dependencies as the rest of the HBase client code. In the event that you need to **also** use Hadoop in your code, you should ensure all Hadoop related jars precede the HBase client jar in your classpath."},"12978":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3178","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#significant-changes-to-runtime-classpath-toc","content":"A number of internal dependencies for HBase were updated or removed from the runtime classpath. Downstream client users who do not follow the guidance in Downstream HBase 2.0+ users should use the shaded client will have to examine the set of dependencies Maven pulls in for impact. Downstream users of LimitedPrivate Coprocessor APIs will need to examine the runtime environment for impact."},"12979":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3179","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiple-breaking-changes-to-source-and-binary-compatibility-for-client-api-toc","content":"The Java client API for HBase has a number of changes that break both source and binary compatibility for details see the Compatibility Check Report for the release you'll be upgrading to."},"12980":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3180","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"The backing implementation of HBase's tracing features was updated from Apache HTrace 3 to HTrace 4, which includes several breaking changes. While HTrace 3 and 4 can coexist in the same runtime, they will not integrate with each other, leading to disjoint trace information."},"12981":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3181","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"The internal changes to HBase during this upgrade were sufficient for compilation, but it has not been confirmed that there are no regressions in tracing functionality. Please consider this feature experimental for the immediate future."},"12982":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3182","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"If you previously relied on client side tracing integrated with HBase operations, it is recommended that you upgrade your usage to HTrace 4 as well."},"12983":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3183","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tracing-implementation-changes-toc","content":"After the Apache HTrace project moved to the Attic/retired, the traces in HBase are left broken and unmaintained since HBase 2.0. A new project HBASE-22120 will replace HTrace with OpenTelemetry. It will be shipped in 3.0.0 release. Please see the Tracing documentation for more details."},"12984":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3184","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hfile-lose-forward-compatibility-toc","content":"HFiles generated by 2.0.0, 2.0.1, 2.1.0 are not forward compatible to 1.4.6-, 1.3.2.1-, 1.2.6.1-, and other inactive releases. Why HFile lose compatibility is hbase in new versions (2.0.0, 2.0.1, 2.1.0) use protobuf to serialize/deserialize TimeRangeTracker (TRT) while old versions use DataInput/DataOutput. To solve this, We have to put HBASE-21012 to 2.x and put HBASE-21013 in 1.x. For more information, please check HBASE-21008."},"12985":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3185","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#paths-upgrading-from-1-to-2-changes-of-note-performance","content":"You will likely see a change in the performance profile on upgrade to hbase-2.0.0 given read and write paths have undergone significant change. On release, writes may be slower with reads about the same or much better, dependent on context. Be prepared to spend time re-tuning (See Performance). Performance is also an area that is now under active review so look forward to improvement in coming releases (See HBASE-20188 TESTING Performance)."},"12986":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3186","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#integration-tests-and-kerberos-toc","content":"Integration Tests (`IntegrationTests*`) used to rely on the Kerberos credential cache for authentication against secured clusters. This used to lead to tests failing due to authentication failures when the tickets in the credential cache expired. As of hbase-2.0.0 (and hbase-1.3.0+), the integration test clients will make use of the configuration properties `hbase.client.keytab.file` and `hbase.client.kerberos.principal`. They are required. The clients will perform a login from the configured keytab file and automatically refresh the credentials in the background for the process lifetime (See HBASE-16231)."},"12987":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3187","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"HBase 2.x comes with default limits to the speed at which compactions can execute. This limit is defined per RegionServer. In previous versions of HBase earlier than 1.5, there was no limit to the speed at which a compaction could run by default. Applying a limit to the throughput of a compaction should ensure more stable operations from RegionServers."},"12988":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3188","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"Take care to notice that this limit is *per RegionServer*, not *per compaction*."},"12989":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3189","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The throughput limit is defined as a range of bytes written per second, and is allowed to vary within the given lower and upper bound. RegionServers observe the current throughput of a compaction and apply a linear formula to adjust the allowed throughput, within the lower and upper bound, with respect to external pressure. For compactions, external pressure is defined as the number of store files with respect to the maximum number of allowed store files. The more store files, the higher the compaction pressure."},"12990":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3190","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"Configuration of this throughput is governed by the following properties."},"12991":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3191","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The lower bound is defined by `hbase.hstore.compaction.throughput.lower.bound` and defaults to 50 MB/s (`52428800`)."},"12992":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3192","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"The upper bound is defined by `hbase.hstore.compaction.throughput.higher.bound` and defaults to 100 MB/s (`104857600`)."},"12993":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3193","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"To revert this behavior to the unlimited compaction throughput of earlier versions of HBase, please set the following property to the implementation that applies no limits to compactions."},"12994":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3194","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-compaction-throughput-toc","content":"`hbase.regionserver.throughput.controller=org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.throttle.NoLimitThroughputController`"},"12995":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3195","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Coprocessors have changed substantially in 2.0 ranging from top level design changes in class hierarchies to changed/removed methods, interfaces, etc. (Parent jira: HBASE-18169 Coprocessor fix and cleanup before 2.0.0 release). Some of the reasons for such widespread changes:"},"12996":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3196","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Pass Interfaces instead of Implementations; e.g. TableDescriptor instead of HTableDescriptor and Region instead of HRegion (HBASE-18241 Change client.Table and client.Admin to not use HTableDescriptor)."},"12997":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3197","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Design refactor so implementers need to fill out less boilerplate and so we can do more compile-time checking (HBASE-17732)"},"12998":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3198","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Purge Protocol Buffers from Coprocessor API (HBASE-18859, HBASE-16769, etc)"},"12999":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3199","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Cut back on what we expose to Coprocessors removing hooks on internals that were too private to expose (for eg. HBASE-18453 CompactionRequest should not be exposed to user directly; HBASE-18298 RegionServerServices Interface cleanup for CP expose; etc)"},"13000":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3200","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"To use coprocessors in 2.0, they should be rebuilt against new API otherwise they will fail to load and HBase processes will die."},"13001":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3201","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Suggested order of changes to upgrade the coprocessors:"},"13002":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3202","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Directly implement observer interfaces instead of extending Base\\*Observer classes. Change `Foo extends BaseXXXObserver` to `Foo implements XXXObserver`. (HBASE-17312)."},"13003":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3203","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Adapt to design change from Inheritence to Composition (HBASE-17732) by following this example."},"13004":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3204","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"getTable() has been removed from the CoprocessorEnvrionment, coprocessors should self-manage Table instances."},"13005":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3205","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Some examples of writing coprocessors with new API can be found in hbase-example module here."},"13006":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3206","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-coprocessors-to-20","content":"Lastly, if an api has been changed/removed that breaks you in an irreparable way, and if there's a good justification to add it back, bring it our notice dev\\@hbase.apache.org."},"13007":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3207","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling-upgrade-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Rolling upgrades are currently an experimental feature. They have had limited testing. There are likely corner cases as yet uncovered in our limited experience so you should be careful if you go this route. The stop/upgrade/start described in the next section, Upgrade process from 1.x to 2.x, is the safest route."},"13008":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3208","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rolling-upgrade-from-1x-to-2x","content":"That said, the below is a prescription for a rolling upgrade of a 1.4 cluster."},"13009":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3209","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-requirements","content":"Upgrade to the latest 1.4.x release. Pre 1.4 releases may also work but are not tested, so please upgrade to 1.4.3+ before upgrading to 2.x, unless you are an expert and familiar with the region assignment and crash processing. See the section Upgrading from pre-1.4 to 1.4+ on how to upgrade to 1.4.x."},"13010":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3210","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-requirements","content":"Make sure that the zk-less assignment is enabled, i.e, set `hbase.assignment.usezk` to `false`. This is the most important thing. It allows the 1.x master to assign/unassign regions to/from 2.x region servers. See the release note section of HBASE-11059 on how to migrate from zk based assignment to zk less assignment."},"13011":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3211","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-requirements","content":"Before you upgrade, ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type inconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process."},"13012":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3212","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-requirements","content":"We have tested rolling upgrading from 1.4.3 to 2.1.0, but it should also work if you want to upgrade to 2.0.x."},"13013":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3213","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"Unload a region server and upgrade it to 2.1.0. With HBASE-17931 in place, the meta region and regions for other system tables will be moved to this region server immediately. If not, please move them manually to the new region server. This is very important because"},"13014":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3214","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"The schema of meta region is hard coded, if meta is on an old region server, then the new region servers can not access it as it does not have some families, for example, table state."},"13015":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3215","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"Client with lower version can communicate with server with higher version, but not vice versa. If the meta region is on an old region server, the new region server will use a client with higher version to communicate with a server with lower version, this may introduce strange problems."},"13016":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3216","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"Rolling upgrade all other region servers."},"13017":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3217","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"Upgrading masters."},"13018":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3218","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"It is OK that during the rolling upgrading there are region server crashes. The 1.x master can assign regions to both 1.x and 2.x region servers, and HBASE-19166 fixed a problem so that 1.x region server can also read the WALs written by 2.x region server and split them."},"13019":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3219","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"Please read the Changes of Note! section carefully before\nrolling upgrading. Make sure that you do not use the removed features in 2.0, for example, the\nprefix-tree encoding, the old hfile format, etc. They could both fail the upgrading and leave the\ncluster in an intermediate state and hard to recover."},"13020":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3220","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#instructions","content":"If you have success running this prescription, please notify the dev list with a note on your\nexperience and/or update the above with any deviations you may have taken so others going this\nroute can benefit from your efforts."},"13021":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3221","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"To upgrade an existing HBase 1.x cluster, you should:"},"13022":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3222","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Ensure that *hbck1* reports no `INCONSISTENCIES`. Fixing hbase1-type inconsistencies post-upgrade is an involved process. Fix all *hbck1* complaints before proceeding."},"13023":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3223","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Clean shutdown of existing 1.x cluster"},"13024":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3224","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Update coprocessors"},"13025":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3225","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Upgrade Master roles first"},"13026":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3226","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"Upgrade RegionServers"},"13027":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3227","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrade-process-from-1x-to-2x","content":"(Eventually) Upgrade Clients"},"13028":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3228","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-171","content":"HBase release 1.7.0 introduced an incompatible table metadata serialization format that broke the minor release compatibility guarantees. The issue was reported in HBASE-26021 and the problematic serialization patch was reverted in HBase 1.7.1. Some important notes about 1.7.x upgrades below."},"13029":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3229","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you are considering an upgrade to 1.7.x version, skip 1.7.0 completely and upgrade to 1.7.1+ version. 1.7.0 was withdrawn and removed from the Apache sites."},"13030":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3230","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you already installed a 1.7.0 cluster from scratch and are looking to migrate to 1.7.1+, you cannot follow the regular rolling upgrade procedures due to broken compatibility contracts. Instead shutdown the cluster and reboot with 1.7.1+ binaries. Newer versions detect any existing tables with incompatible serialization and rewrite them using the correct format at bootstrap."},"13031":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3231","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-171","content":"If you are already on 1.7.1+ version, everything is good and no additional steps need to be performed."},"13032":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3232","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-server-memory-consumption-changes","content":"Users upgrading from versions prior to HBase 1.4 should be aware that the estimates of heap usage by the memstore objects (KeyValue, object and array header sizes, etc) have been made more accurate for heap sizes up to 32G (using CompressedOops), resulting in them dropping by 10-50% in practice. This also results in less number of flushes and compactions due to \"fatter\" flushes. YMMV. As a result, the actual heap usage of the memstore before being flushed may increase by up to 100%. If configured memory limits for the region server had been tuned based on observed usage, this change could result in worse GC behavior or even OutOfMemory errors. Set the environment property (not hbase-site.xml) \"hbase.memorylayout.use.unsafe\" to false to disable."},"13033":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3233","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Before 1.4, the table name can't include namespace for replication peer's TableCFs config. It was fixed by add TableCFs to ReplicationPeerConfig which was stored on Zookeeper. So when upgrade to 1.4, you have to update the original ReplicationPeerConfig data on Zookeeper firstly. There are four steps to upgrade when your cluster have a replication peer with TableCFs config."},"13034":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3234","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Disable the replication peer."},"13035":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3235","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"If master has permission to write replication peer znode, then rolling update master directly. If not, use TableCFsUpdater tool to update the replication peer's config."},"13036":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3236","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Rolling update regionservers."},"13037":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3237","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Enable the replication peer."},"13038":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3238","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Notes:"},"13039":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3239","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replication-peers-tablecfs-config","content":"Can't use the old client(before 1.4) to change the replication peer's config. Because the client will write config to Zookeeper directly, the old client will miss TableCFs config. And the old client write TableCFs config to the old tablecfs znode, it will not work for new version regionserver."},"13040":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3240","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#raw-scan-now-ignores-ttl","content":"Doing a raw scan will now return results that have expired according to TTL settings."},"13041":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3241","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-from-pre-13-to-13","content":"If running Integration Tests under Kerberos, see upgrade2.0.it.kerberos."},"13042":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3242","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-1x","content":"Please consult the documentation published specifically for the version of HBase that you are upgrading to for details on the upgrade process."},"13043":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3243","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell","content":"The Apache HBase Shell is (J)Ruby's IRB with some HBase particular commands added.\nAnything you can do in IRB, you should be able to do in the HBase Shell."},"13044":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3244","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell","content":"To run the HBase shell, do as follows:"},"13045":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3245","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell","content":"Type `help` and then `<RETURN>` to see a listing of shell commands and options.\nBrowse at least the paragraphs at the end of the help output for the gist of how variables and command arguments are entered into the HBase shell; in particular note how table names, rows, and columns, etc., must be quoted."},"13046":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3246","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell","content":"See shell exercises for example basic shell operation."},"13047":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3247","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell","content":"Here is a nicely formatted listing of all shell commands by Rajeshbabu Chintaguntla."},"13048":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3248","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scripting-with-ruby","content":"For examples scripting Apache HBase, look in the HBase *bin&#x2A; directory.\nLook at the files that end in *\\*.rb*.\nTo run one of these files, do as follows:"},"13049":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3249","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#running-the-shell-in-non-interactive-mode","content":"A new non-interactive mode has been added to the HBase Shell (HBASE-11658).\nNon-interactive mode captures the exit status (success or failure) of HBase Shell commands and passes that status back to the command interpreter.\nIf you use the normal interactive mode, the HBase Shell will only ever return its own exit status, which will nearly always be `0` for success."},"13050":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3250","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#running-the-shell-in-non-interactive-mode","content":"To invoke non-interactive mode, pass the `-n` or `--non-interactive` option to HBase Shell."},"13051":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3251","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-shell-in-os-scripts","content":"You can use the HBase shell from within operating system script interpreters like the Bash shell which is the default command interpreter for most Linux and UNIX distributions.\nThe following guidelines use Bash syntax, but could be adjusted to work with C-style shells such as csh or tcsh, and could probably be modified to work with the Microsoft Windows script interpreter as well. Submissions are welcome."},"13052":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3252","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-shell-in-os-scripts","content":"Spawning HBase Shell commands in this way is slow, so keep that in mind when you are deciding when\ncombining HBase operations with the operating system command line is appropriate."},"13053":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3253","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#passing-commands-to-the-hbase-shell","content":"You can pass commands to the HBase Shell in non-interactive mode (see hbase.shell.noninteractive) using the `echo` command and the `|` (pipe) operator.\nBe sure to escape characters in the HBase commands which would otherwise be interpreted by the shell.\nSome debug-level output has been truncated from the example below."},"13054":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3254","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#passing-commands-to-the-hbase-shell","content":"To suppress all output, echo it to &#x2A;/dev/null:*"},"13055":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3255","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#checking-the-result-of-a-scripted-command","content":"Since scripts are not designed to be run interactively, you need a way to check whether your command failed or succeeded.\nThe HBase shell uses the standard convention of returning a value of `0` for successful commands, and some non-zero value for failed commands.\nBash stores a command's return value in a special environment variable called `$?`.\nBecause that variable is overwritten each time the shell runs any command, you should store the result in a different, script-defined variable."},"13056":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3256","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#checking-the-result-of-a-scripted-command","content":"This is a naive script that shows one way to store the return value and make a decision based upon it."},"13057":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3257","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#checking-for-success-or-failure-in-scripts","content":"Getting an exit code of `0` means that the command you scripted definitely succeeded.\nHowever, getting a non-zero exit code does not necessarily mean the command failed.\nThe command could have succeeded, but the client lost connectivity, or some other event obscured its success.\nThis is because RPC commands are stateless.\nThe only way to be sure of the status of an operation is to check.\nFor instance, if your script creates a table, but returns a non-zero exit value, you should check whether the table was actually created before trying again to create it."},"13058":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3258","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-hbase-shell-commands-from-a-command-file","content":"You can enter HBase Shell commands into a text file, one command per line, and pass that file to the HBase Shell."},"13059":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3259","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#directing-hbase-shell-to-execute-the-commands","content":"Pass the path to the command file as the only argument to the `hbase shell` command.\nEach command is executed and its output is shown.\nIf you do not include the `exit` command in your script, you are returned to the HBase shell prompt.\nThere is no way to programmatically check each individual command for success or failure.\nAlso, though you see the output for each command, the commands themselves are not echoed to the screen so it can be difficult to line up the command with its output."},"13060":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3260","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#passing-vm-options-to-the-shell","content":"You can pass VM options to the HBase Shell using the `HBASE_SHELL_OPTS&#x60; environment variable.\nYou can set this in your environment, for instance by editing *\\~/.bashrc*, or set it as part of the command to launch HBase Shell.\nThe following example sets several garbage-collection-related variables, just for the lifetime of the VM running the HBase Shell.\nThe command should be run all on a single line, but is broken by the `\\` character, for readability."},"13061":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3261","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overriding-configuration-starting-the-hbase-shell","content":"As of hbase-2.0.5/hbase-2.1.3/hbase-2.2.0/hbase-1.4.10/hbase-1.5.0, you can\npass or override hbase configuration as specified in `hbase-*.xml` by passing\nyour key/values prefixed with `-D` on the command-line as follows:"},"13062":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3262","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-variables","content":"HBase 0.95 adds shell commands that provides jruby-style object-oriented references for tables.\nPreviously all of the shell commands that act upon a table have a procedural style that always took the name of the table as an argument.\nHBase 0.95 introduces the ability to assign a table to a jruby variable.\nThe table reference can be used to perform data read write operations such as puts, scans, and gets well as admin functionality such as disabling, dropping, describing tables."},"13063":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3263","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-variables","content":"For example, previously you would always specify a table name:"},"13064":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3264","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-variables","content":"Now you can assign the table to a variable and use the results in jruby shell code."},"13065":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3265","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-variables","content":"If the table has already been created, you can assign a Table to a variable by using the get\\_table method:"},"13066":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3266","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-variables","content":"The list functionality has also been extended so that it returns a list of table names as strings.\nYou can then use jruby to script table operations based on these names.\nThe list\\_snapshots command also acts similarly."},"13067":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3267","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#irbrc","content":"Create an *.irbrc* file for yourself in your home directory.\nAdd customizations.\nA useful one is command history so commands are save across Shell invocations:"},"13068":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3268","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#irbrc","content":"If you'd like to avoid printing the result of evaluting each expression to stderr, for example the array of tables returned from the \"list\" command:"},"13069":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3269","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#irbrc","content":"See the `ruby&#x60; documentation of *.irbrc* to learn about other possible configurations."},"13070":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3270","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#log-data-to-timestamp","content":"To convert the date '08/08/16 20:56:29' from an hbase log into a timestamp, do:"},"13071":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3271","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#log-data-to-timestamp","content":"To go the other direction:"},"13072":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3272","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#log-data-to-timestamp","content":"To output in a format that is exactly like that of the HBase log format will take a little messing with SimpleDateFormat."},"13073":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3273","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-shell-configuration","content":"To set a config in the shell:"},"13074":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3274","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"You can use a variety of options to pre-split tables when creating them via the HBase Shell `create` command."},"13075":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3275","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"The simplest approach is to specify an array of split points when creating the table. Note that when specifying string literals as split points, these will create split points based on the underlying byte representation of the string. So when specifying a split point of '10', we are actually specifying the byte split point '\\x31\\30'."},"13076":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3276","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"The split points will define `n+1` regions where `n` is the number of split points. The lowest region will contain all keys from the lowest possible key up to but not including the first split point key.\nThe next region will contain keys from the first split point up to, but not including the next split point key.\nThis will continue for all split points up to the last. The last region will be defined from the last split point up to the maximum possible key."},"13077":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3277","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"In the above example, the table 't1' will be created with column family 'f', pre-split to four regions. Note the first region will contain all keys from '\\x00' up to '\\x30' (as '\\x31' is the ASCII code for '1')."},"13078":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3278","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"You can pass the split points in a file using following variation. In this example, the splits are read from a file corresponding to the local path on the local filesystem. Each line in the file specifies a split point key."},"13079":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3279","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"The other options are to automatically compute splits based on a desired number of regions and a splitting algorithm.\nHBase supplies algorithms for splitting the key range based on uniform splits or based on hexadecimal keys, but you can provide your own splitting algorithm to subdivide the key range."},"13080":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3280","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"As the HBase Shell is effectively a Ruby environment, you can use simple Ruby scripts to compute splits algorithmically."},"13081":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3281","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#pre-splitting-tables-with-the-hbase-shell","content":"Note that the HBase Shell command `truncate` effectively drops and recreates the table with default options which will discard any pre-splitting.\nIf you need to truncate a pre-split table, you must drop and recreate the table explicitly to re-specify custom split options."},"13082":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3282","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#shell-debug-switch","content":"You can set a debug switch in the shell to see more output — e.g.\nmore of the stack trace on exception — when you run a command:"},"13083":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3283","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#debug-log-level","content":"To enable DEBUG level logging in the shell, launch it with the `-d` option."},"13084":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3284","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#count","content":"Count command returns the number of rows in a table.\nIt's quite fast when configured with the right CACHE"},"13085":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3285","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#count","content":"The above count fetches 1000 rows at a time.\nSet CACHE lower if your rows are big.\nDefault is to fetch one row at a time."},"13086":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3286","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-toc","content":"An HBase table consists of multiple rows."},"13087":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3287","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#row-toc","content":"A row in HBase consists of a row key and one or more columns with values associated with them.\nRows are sorted alphabetically by the row key as they are stored.\nFor this reason, the design of the row key is very important.\nThe goal is to store data in such a way that related rows are near each other.\nA common row key pattern is a website domain.\nIf your row keys are domains, you should probably store them in reverse (org.apache.www, org.apache.mail, org.apache.jira). This way, all of the Apache domains are near each other in the table, rather than being spread out based on the first letter of the subdomain."},"13088":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3288","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-toc","content":"A column in HBase consists of a column family and a column qualifier, which are delimited by a `:` (colon) character."},"13089":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3289","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-family-toc","content":"Column families physically colocate a set of columns and their values, often for performance reasons.\nEach column family has a set of storage properties, such as whether its values should be cached in memory, how its data is compressed or its row keys are encoded, and others.\nEach row in a table has the same column families, though a given row might not store anything in a given column family."},"13090":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3290","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-qualifier-toc","content":"A column qualifier is added to a column family to provide the index for a given piece of data.\nGiven a column family `content`, a column qualifier might be `content:html`, and another might be `content:pdf`.\nThough column families are fixed at table creation, column qualifiers are mutable and may differ greatly between rows."},"13091":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3291","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cell-toc","content":"A cell is a combination of row, column family, and column qualifier, and contains a value and a timestamp, which represents the value's version."},"13092":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3292","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-toc","content":"A timestamp is written alongside each value, and is the identifier for a given version of a value.\nBy default, the timestamp represents the time on the RegionServer when the data was written, but you can specify a different timestamp value when you put data into the cell."},"13093":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3293","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#conceptual-view","content":"You can read a very understandable explanation of the HBase data model in the blog post Understanding HBase and BigTable by Jim R. Wilson.\nAnother good explanation is available in the PDF Introduction to Basic Schema Design by Amandeep Khurana."},"13094":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3294","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#conceptual-view","content":"It may help to read different perspectives to get a solid understanding of HBase schema design.\nThe linked articles cover the same ground as the information in this section."},"13095":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3295","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#conceptual-view","content":"The following example is a slightly modified form of the one on page 2 of the BigTable paper.\nThere is a table called `webtable` that contains two rows (`com.cnn.www` and `com.example.www`) and three column families named `contents`, `anchor`, and `people`.\nIn this example, for the first row (`com.cnn.www`), `anchor` contains two columns (`anchor:cssnsi.com`, `anchor:my.look.ca`) and `contents` contains one column (`contents:html`). This example contains 5 versions of the row with the row key `com.cnn.www`, and one version of the row with the row key `com.example.www`.\nThe `contents:html` column qualifier contains the entire HTML of a given website.\nQualifiers of the `anchor` column family each contain the external site which links to the site represented by the row, along with the text it used in the anchor of its link.\nThe `people` column family represents people associated with the site."},"13096":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3296","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#conceptual-view","content":"By convention, a column name is made of its column family prefix and a *qualifier*. For example,\nthe column *contents:html* is made up of the column family `contents` and the `html` qualifier.\nThe colon character (`:`) delimits the column family from the column family *qualifier*."},"13097":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3297","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"Row Key"},"13098":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3298","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"13099":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3299","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `contents`"},"13100":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3300","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `anchor`"},"13101":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3301","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `people`"},"13102":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3302","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13103":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3303","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t9"},"13104":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3304","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"anchor:cnnsi.com = \"CNN\""},"13105":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3305","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13106":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3306","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t8"},"13107":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3307","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"anchor:my.look.ca = \"CNN.com\""},"13108":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3308","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13109":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3309","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t6"},"13110":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3310","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13111":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3311","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13112":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3312","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t5"},"13113":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3313","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13114":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3314","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13115":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3315","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t3"},"13116":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3316","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13117":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3317","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"\"com.example.www\""},"13118":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3318","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"t5"},"13119":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3319","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13120":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3320","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"people:author = \"John Doe\""},"13121":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3321","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-webtable-toc","content":"Cells in this table that appear to be empty do not take space, or in fact exist, in HBase.\nThis is what makes HBase \"sparse.\" A tabular view is not the only possible way to look at data in HBase, or even the most accurate.\nThe following represents the same information as a multi-dimensional map.\nThis is only a mock-up for illustrative purposes and may not be strictly accurate."},"13122":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3322","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#physical-view","content":"Although at a conceptual level tables may be viewed as a sparse set of rows, they are physically stored by column family.\nA new column qualifier (column\\_family:column\\_qualifier) can be added to an existing column family at any time."},"13123":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3323","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Row Key"},"13124":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3324","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"13125":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3325","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"Column Family `anchor`"},"13126":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3326","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13127":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3327","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"t9"},"13128":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3328","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"`anchor:cnnsi.com = \"CNN\"`"},"13129":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3329","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13130":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3330","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"t8"},"13131":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3331","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-anchor-toc","content":"`anchor:my.look.ca = \"CNN.com\"`"},"13132":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3332","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"Row Key"},"13133":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3333","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"Time Stamp"},"13134":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3334","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"ColumnFamily `contents:`"},"13135":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3335","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13136":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3336","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t6"},"13137":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3337","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13138":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3338","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13139":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3339","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t5"},"13140":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3340","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13141":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3341","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"\"com.cnn.www\""},"13142":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3342","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"t3"},"13143":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3343","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"contents:html = \"\\<html>...\""},"13144":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3344","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"The empty cells shown in the conceptual view are not stored at all.\nThus a request for the value of the `contents:html` column at time stamp `t8` would return no value.\nSimilarly, a request for an `anchor:my.look.ca` value at time stamp `t9` would return no value.\nHowever, if no timestamp is supplied, the most recent value for a particular column would be returned.\nGiven multiple versions, the most recent is also the first one found, since timestamps are stored in descending order.\nThus a request for the values of all columns in the row `com.cnn.www` if no timestamp is specified would be: the value of `contents:html` from timestamp `t6`, the value of `anchor:cnnsi.com` from timestamp `t9`, the value of `anchor:my.look.ca` from timestamp `t8`."},"13145":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3345","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnfamily-contents-toc","content":"For more information about the internals of how Apache HBase stores data, see regions.arch."},"13146":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3346","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace","content":"A namespace is a logical grouping of tables analogous to a database in relation database systems.\nThis abstraction lays the groundwork for upcoming multi-tenancy related features:"},"13147":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3347","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace","content":"Quota Management (HBASE-8410) - Restrict the amount of resources (i.e. regions, tables) a namespace can consume."},"13148":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3348","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace","content":"Namespace Security Administration (HBASE-9206) - Provide another level of security administration for tenants."},"13149":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3349","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace","content":"Region server groups (HBASE-6721) - A namespace/table can be pinned onto a subset of RegionServers thus guaranteeing a coarse level of isolation."},"13150":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3350","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#namespace-management","content":"A namespace can be created, removed or altered.\nNamespace membership is determined during table creation by specifying a fully-qualified table name of the form:"},"13151":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3351","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#predefined-namespaces","content":"There are two predefined special namespaces:"},"13152":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3352","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#predefined-namespaces","content":"hbase - system namespace, used to contain HBase internal tables"},"13153":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3353","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#predefined-namespaces","content":"default - tables with no explicit specified namespace will automatically fall into this namespace"},"13154":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3354","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"We used to have a system table called `hbase:namespace` for storing the namespace information."},"13155":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3355","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"It introduced some painful bugs in the past, especially that it may hang the master startup thus\nhang the whole cluster. This is because meta table also has a namespace, so it depends on namespace\ntable. But namespace table also depends on meta table as meta table stores the location of all\nregions. This is a cyclic dependency so sometimes namespace and meta table will wait for each other\nto online and hang the master start up."},"13156":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3356","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"It is not easy to fix so in 3.0.0, we decided to completely remove the `hbase:namespace` table and\nfold its content into the `ns` family in `hbase:meta` table. When upgrading from 2.x to 3.x, the\nmigration will be done automatically and the `hbase:namespace` table will be disabled after the\nmigration is done. You are free to leave it there for sometime and finally drop it."},"13157":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3357","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#about-hbasenamespace-table","content":"For more tails, please see https\\://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/HBASE-21154."},"13158":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3358","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table","content":"Tables are declared up front at schema definition time."},"13159":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3359","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#row","content":"Row keys are uninterpreted bytes.\nRows are lexicographically sorted with the lowest order appearing first in a table.\nThe empty byte array is used to denote both the start and end of a tables' namespace."},"13160":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3360","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-family","content":"Columns in Apache HBase are grouped into *column families*.\nAll column members of a column family have the same prefix.\nFor example, the columns *courses:history* and *courses:math* are both members of the *courses* column family.\nThe colon character (`:`) delimits the column family from the column family qualifier.\nThe column family prefix must be composed of *printable* characters.\nThe qualifying tail, the column family *qualifier*, can be made of any arbitrary bytes.\nColumn families must be declared up front at schema definition time whereas columns do not need to be defined at schema time but can be conjured on the fly while the table is up and running."},"13161":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3361","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-family","content":"Physically, all column family members are stored together on the filesystem.\nBecause tunings and storage specifications are done at the column family level, it is advised that all column family members have the same general access pattern and size characteristics."},"13162":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3362","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cells","content":"A &#x2A;\\{row, column, version}* tuple exactly specifies a `cell` in HBase.\nCell content is uninterpreted bytes"},"13163":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3363","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-model-operations","content":"The four primary data model operations are Get, Put, Scan, and Delete.\nOperations are applied via Table instances."},"13164":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3364","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#get","content":"Get returns attributes for a specified row.\nGets are executed via Table.get"},"13165":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3365","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#put","content":"Put either adds new rows to a table (if the key is new) or can update existing rows (if the key already exists). Puts are executed via Table.put (non-writeBuffer) or Table.batch (non-writeBuffer)"},"13166":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3366","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-model-operations-scans","content":"Scan allow iteration over multiple rows for specified attributes."},"13167":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3367","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-model-operations-scans","content":"The following is an example of a Scan on a Table instance.\nAssume that a table is populated with rows with keys \"row1\", \"row2\", \"row3\", and then another set of rows with the keys \"abc1\", \"abc2\", and \"abc3\". The following example shows how to set a Scan instance to return the rows beginning with \"row\"."},"13168":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3368","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-model-operations-scans","content":"Note that generally the easiest way to specify a specific stop point for a scan is by using the InclusiveStopFilter class."},"13169":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3369","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete","content":"Delete removes a row from a table.\nDeletes are executed via Table.delete."},"13170":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3370","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete","content":"HBase does not modify data in place, and so deletes are handled by creating new markers called *tombstones*.\nThese tombstones, along with the dead values, are cleaned up on major compactions."},"13171":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3371","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete","content":"See version.delete for more information on deleting versions of columns, and see compaction for more information on compactions."},"13172":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3372","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"A &#x2A;\\{row, column, version}* tuple exactly specifies a `cell` in HBase.\nIt's possible to have an unbounded number of cells where the row and column are the same but the cell address differs only in its version dimension."},"13173":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3373","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"While rows and column keys are expressed as bytes, the version is specified using a long integer.\nTypically this long contains time instances such as those returned by `java.util.Date.getTime()` or `System.currentTimeMillis()`, that is: *the difference, measured in milliseconds, between the current time and midnight, January 1, 1970 UTC*."},"13174":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3374","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"The HBase version dimension is stored in decreasing order, so that when reading from a store file, the most recent values are found first."},"13175":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3375","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"There is a lot of confusion over the semantics of `cell` versions, in HBase.\nIn particular:"},"13176":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3376","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"If multiple writes to a cell have the same version, only the last written is fetchable."},"13177":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3377","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"It is OK to write cells in a non-increasing version order."},"13178":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3378","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"Below we describe how the version dimension in HBase currently works.\nSee HBASE-2406 for discussion of HBase versions. Bending time in HBase makes for a good read on the version, or time, dimension in HBase.\nIt has more detail on versioning than is provided here."},"13179":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3379","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions","content":"As of this writing, the limitation *Overwriting values at existing timestamps* mentioned in the article no longer holds in HBase.\nThis section is basically a synopsis of this article by Bruno Dumon."},"13180":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3380","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#specifying-the-number-of-versions-to-store","content":"The maximum number of versions to store for a given column is part of the column schema and is specified at table creation, or via an `alter` command, via `HColumnDescriptor.DEFAULT_VERSIONS`.\nPrior to HBase 0.96, the default number of versions kept was `3`, but in 0.96 and newer has been changed to `1`."},"13181":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3381","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-modify-the-maximum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"This example uses HBase Shell to keep a maximum of 5 versions of all columns in column family `f1`.\nYou could also use ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."},"13182":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3382","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-modify-the-minimum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"You can also specify the minimum number of versions to store per column family.\nBy default, this is set to 0, which means the feature is disabled.\nThe following example sets the minimum number of versions on all columns in column family `f1` to `2`, via HBase Shell.\nYou could also use ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."},"13183":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3383","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-modify-the-minimum-number-of-versions-for-a-column-family-toc","content":"Starting with HBase 0.98.2, you can specify a global default for the maximum number of versions kept for all newly-created columns, by setting `hbase.column.max.version` in *hbase-site.xml*.\nSee hbase.column.max.version."},"13184":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3384","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versions-and-hbase-operations","content":"In this section we look at the behavior of the version dimension for each of the core HBase operations."},"13185":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3385","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getscan","content":"Gets are implemented on top of Scans.\nThe below discussion of Get applies equally to Scans."},"13186":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3386","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getscan","content":"By default, i.e. if you specify no explicit version, when doing a `get`, the cell whose version has the largest value is returned (which may or may not be the latest one written, see later). The default behavior can be modified in the following ways:"},"13187":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3387","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getscan","content":"to return more than one version, see Get.readVersions(int)"},"13188":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3388","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getscan","content":"to return versions other than the latest, see Get.setTimeRange(long,long)"},"13189":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3389","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getscan","content":"To retrieve the latest version that is less than or equal to a given value, thus giving the 'latest' state of the record at a certain point in time, just use a range from 0 to the desired version and set the max versions to 1."},"13190":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3390","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-get-example","content":"The following Get will only retrieve the current version of the row"},"13191":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3391","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#versioned-get-example","content":"The following Get will return the last 3 versions of the row."},"13192":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3392","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#put-1","content":"Doing a put always creates a new version of a `cell`, at a certain timestamp.\nBy default the system uses the server's `currentTimeMillis`, but you can specify the version (= the long integer) yourself, on a per-column level.\nThis means you could assign a time in the past or the future, or use the long value for non-time purposes."},"13193":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3393","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#put-1","content":"To overwrite an existing value, do a put at exactly the same row, column, and version as that of the cell you want to overwrite."},"13194":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3394","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implicit-version-example-toc","content":"The following Put will be implicitly versioned by HBase with the current time."},"13195":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3395","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explicit-version-example","content":"The following Put has the version timestamp explicitly set."},"13196":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3396","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#explicit-version-example","content":"Caution: the version timestamp is used internally by HBase for things like time-to-live calculations.\nIt's usually best to avoid setting this timestamp yourself.\nPrefer using a separate timestamp attribute of the row, or have the timestamp as a part of the row key, or both."},"13197":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3397","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cell-version-example-toc","content":"The following Put uses a method getCellBuilder() to get a CellBuilder instance\nthat already has relevant Type and Row set."},"13198":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3398","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"There are three different types of internal delete markers.\nSee Lars Hofhansl's blog for discussion of his attempt adding another, Scanning in HBase: Prefix Delete Marker."},"13199":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3399","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Delete: for a specific version of a column."},"13200":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3400","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Delete column: for all versions of a column."},"13201":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3401","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Delete family: for all columns of a particular ColumnFamily"},"13202":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3402","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"When deleting an entire row, HBase will internally create a tombstone for each ColumnFamily (i.e., not each individual column)."},"13203":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3403","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Deletes work by creating *tombstone* markers.\nFor example, let's suppose we want to delete a row.\nFor this you can specify a version, or else by default the `currentTimeMillis` is used.\nWhat this means is *delete all cells where the version is less than or equal to this version*.\nHBase never modifies data in place, so for example a delete will not immediately delete (or mark as deleted) the entries in the storage file that correspond to the delete condition.\nRather, a so-called *tombstone* is written, which will mask the deleted values.\nWhen HBase does a major compaction, the tombstones are processed to actually remove the dead values, together with the tombstones themselves.\nIf the version you specified when deleting a row is larger than the version of any value in the row, then you can consider the complete row to be deleted."},"13204":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3404","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"For an informative discussion on how deletes and versioning interact, see the thread Put w/timestamp -> Deleteall -> Put w/ timestamp fails up on the user mailing list."},"13205":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3405","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Also see keyvalue for more information on the internal KeyValue format."},"13206":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3406","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"Delete markers are purged during the next major compaction of the store, unless the `KEEP_DELETED_CELLS` option is set in the column family (See Keeping Deleted Cells).\nTo keep the deletes for a configurable amount of time, you can set the delete TTL via the `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes` property in *hbase-site.xml*.\nIf `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes` is not set, or set to 0, all delete markers, including those with timestamps in the future, are purged during the next major compaction.\nOtherwise, a delete marker with a timestamp in the future is kept until the major compaction which occurs after the time represented by the marker's timestamp plus the value of `hbase.hstore.time.to.purge.deletes`, in milliseconds."},"13207":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3407","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#delete-toc","content":"This behavior represents a fix for an unexpected change that was introduced in HBase 0.94, and was\nfixed in HBASE-10118. The change has been\nbackported to HBase 0.94 and newer branches."},"13208":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3408","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"In `hbase-2.0.0`, the operator can specify an alternate version and\ndelete treatment by setting the column descriptor property\n`NEW_VERSION_BEHAVIOR` to true (To set a property on a column family\ndescriptor, you must first disable the table and then alter the\ncolumn family descriptor; see Keeping Deleted Cells for an example\nof editing an attribute on a column family descriptor)."},"13209":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3409","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"The 'new version behavior', undoes the limitations listed below\nwhereby a `Delete` ALWAYS overshadows a `Put` if at the same\nlocation — i.e. same row, column family, qualifier and timestamp\n\\-- regardless of which arrived first. Version accounting is also\nchanged as deleted versions are considered toward total version count.\nThis is done to ensure results are not changed should a major\ncompaction intercede. See `HBASE-15968` and linked issues for\ndiscussion."},"13210":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3410","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"Running with this new configuration currently costs; we factor\nthe Cell MVCC on every compare so we burn more CPU. The slow\ndown will depend. In testing we've seen between 0% and 25%\ndegradation."},"13211":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3411","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-new-version-and-delete-behavior-in-hbase-200","content":"If replicating, it is advised that you run with the new\nserial replication feature (See `HBASE-9465`; the serial\nreplication feature did NOT make it into `hbase-2.0.0` but\nshould arrive in a subsequent hbase-2.x release) as now\nthe order in which Mutations arrive is a factor."},"13212":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3412","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#current-limitations","content":"The below limitations are addressed in hbase-2.0.0. See\nthe section above, Optional New Version and Delete behavior in HBase-2.0.0."},"13213":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3413","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#deletes-mask-puts","content":"Deletes mask puts, even puts that happened after the delete was entered.\nSee HBASE-2256.\nRemember that a delete writes a tombstone, which only disappears after then next major compaction has run.\nSuppose you do a delete of everything `<= T`.\nAfter this you do a new put with a timestamp `<= T`.\nThis put, even if it happened after the delete, will be masked by the delete tombstone.\nPerforming the put will not fail, but when you do a get you will notice the put did have no effect.\nIt will start working again after the major compaction has run.\nThese issues should not be a problem if you use always-increasing versions for new puts to a row.\nBut they can occur even if you do not care about time: just do delete and put immediately after each other, and there is some chance they happen within the same millisecond."},"13214":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3414","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#major-compactions-change-query-results","content":"*...create three cell versions at t1, t2 and t3, with a maximum-versions\nsetting of 2. So when getting all versions, only the values at t2 and t3 will be\nreturned. But if you delete the version at t2 or t3, the one at t1 will appear again.\nObviously, once a major compaction has run, such behavior will not be the case\nanymore...* (See *Garbage Collection* in Bending time in HBase.)"},"13215":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3415","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sort-order","content":"All data model operations HBase return data in sorted order.\nFirst by row, then by ColumnFamily, followed by column qualifier, and finally timestamp (sorted in reverse, so newest records are returned first)."},"13216":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3416","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-metadata","content":"There is no store of column metadata outside of the internal KeyValue instances for a ColumnFamily.\nThus, while HBase can support not only a wide number of columns per row, but a heterogeneous set of columns between rows as well, it is your responsibility to keep track of the column names."},"13217":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3417","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-metadata","content":"The only way to get a complete set of columns that exist for a ColumnFamily is to process all the rows.\nFor more information about how HBase stores data internally, see keyvalue."},"13218":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3418","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#datamodel-joins","content":"Whether HBase supports joins is a common question on the dist-list, and there is a simple answer: it doesn't, at not least in the way that RDBMS' support them (e.g., with equi-joins or outer-joins in SQL). As has been illustrated in this chapter, the read data model operations in HBase are Get and Scan."},"13219":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3419","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#datamodel-joins","content":"However, that doesn't mean that equivalent join functionality can't be supported in your application, but you have to do it yourself.\nThe two primary strategies are either denormalizing the data upon writing to HBase, or to have lookup tables and do the join between HBase tables in your application or MapReduce code (and as RDBMS' demonstrate, there are several strategies for this depending on the size of the tables, e.g., nested loops vs.\nhash-joins). So which is the best approach? It depends on what you are trying to do, and as such there isn't a single answer that works for every use case."},"13220":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3420","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#acid","content":"See ACID Semantics.\nLars Hofhansl has also written a note on ACID in HBase."},"13221":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3421","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design","content":"A good introduction on the strength and weaknesses modelling on the various non-rdbms datastores is to be found in Ian Varley's Master thesis, No Relation: The Mixed Blessings of Non-Relational Databases. It is a little dated now but a good background read if you have a moment on how HBase schema modeling differs from how it is done in an RDBMS. Also, read keyvalue for how HBase stores data internally, and the section on schema.casestudies."},"13222":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3422","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design","content":"The documentation on the Cloud Bigtable website, Designing Your Schema, is pertinent and nicely done and lessons learned there equally apply here in HBase land; just divide any quoted values by \\~10 to get what works for HBase: e.g. where it says individual values can be \\~10MBs in size, HBase can do similar — perhaps best to go smaller if you can — and where it says a maximum of 100 column families in Cloud Bigtable, think \\~10 when modeling on HBase."},"13223":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3423","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design","content":"See also Robert Yokota's HBase Application Archetypes (an update on work done by other HBasers), for a helpful categorization of use cases that do well on top of the HBase model."},"13224":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3424","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-creation","content":"HBase schemas can be created or updated using the Apache HBase Shell or by using Admin in the Java API."},"13225":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3425","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-creation","content":"Tables must be disabled when making ColumnFamily modifications, for example:"},"13226":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3426","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-creation","content":"See client dependencies for more information about configuring client connections."},"13227":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3427","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-creation","content":"Online schema changes are supported in the 0.92.x codebase, but the 0.90.x codebase requires the\ntable to be disabled."},"13228":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3428","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-updates","content":"When changes are made to either Tables or ColumnFamilies (e.g. region size, block size), these changes take effect the next time there is a major compaction and the StoreFiles get re-written."},"13229":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3429","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-updates","content":"See store for more information on StoreFiles."},"13230":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3430","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"There are many different data sets, with different access patterns and service-level expectations. Therefore, these rules of thumb are only an overview. Read the rest of this chapter to get more details after you have gone through this list."},"13231":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3431","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"Aim to have regions sized between 10 and 50 GB."},"13232":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3432","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"Aim to have cells no larger than 10 MB, or 50 MB if you use mob. Otherwise, consider storing your cell data in HDFS and store a pointer to the data in HBase."},"13233":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3433","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"A typical schema has between 1 and 3 column families per table. HBase tables should not be designed to mimic RDBMS tables."},"13234":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3434","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"Around 50-100 regions is a good number for a table with 1 or 2 column families. Remember that a region is a contiguous segment of a column family."},"13235":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3435","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"Keep your column family names as short as possible. The column family names are stored for every value (ignoring prefix encoding). They should not be self-documenting and descriptive like in a typical RDBMS."},"13236":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3436","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"If you are storing time-based machine data or logging information, and the row key is based on device ID or service ID plus time, you can end up with a pattern where older data regions never have additional writes beyond a certain age. In this type of situation, you end up with a small number of active regions and a large number of older regions which have no new writes. For these situations, you can tolerate a larger number of regions because your resource consumption is driven by the active regions only."},"13237":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3437","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#table-schema-rules-of-thumb","content":"If only one column family is busy with writes, only that column family accomulates memory. Be aware of write patterns when allocating resources."},"13238":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3438","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-sizing","content":"Lars Hofhansl wrote a great blog post about RegionServer memory sizing. The upshot is that you probably need more memory than you think you need. He goes into the impact of region size, memstore size, HDFS replication factor, and other things to check."},"13239":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3439","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-sizing","content":"> Personally I would place the maximum disk space per machine that can be served exclusively with HBase around 6T, unless you have a very read-heavy workload. In that case the Java heap should be 32GB (20G regions, 128M memstores, the rest defaults).\n>\n> — Lars Hofhansl http\\://hadoop-hbase.blogspot.com/2013/01/hbase-region-server-memory-sizing.html"},"13240":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3440","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#on-the-number-of-column-families","content":"HBase currently does not do well with anything above two or three column families so keep the number of column families in your schema low. Currently, flushing is done on a per Region basis so if one column family is carrying the bulk of the data bringing on flushes, the adjacent families will also be flushed even though the amount of data they carry is small. When many column families exist the flushing interaction can make for a bunch of needless i/o (To be addressed by changing flushing to work on a per column family basis). In addition, compactions triggered at table/region level will happen per store too."},"13241":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3441","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#on-the-number-of-column-families","content":"Try to make do with one column family if you can in your schemas. Only introduce a second and third column family in the case where data access is usually column scoped; i.e. you query one column family or the other but usually not both at the one time."},"13242":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3442","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cardinality-of-columnfamilies","content":"Where multiple ColumnFamilies exist in a single table, be aware of the cardinality (i.e., number of rows). If ColumnFamilyA has 1 million rows and ColumnFamilyB has 1 billion rows, ColumnFamilyA's data will likely be spread across many, many regions (and RegionServers). This makes mass scans for ColumnFamilyA less efficient."},"13243":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3443","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hotspotting","content":"Rows in HBase are sorted lexicographically by row key. This design optimizes for scans, allowing you to store related rows, or rows that will be read together, near each other. However, poorly designed row keys are a common source of **hotspotting**. Hotspotting occurs when a large amount of client traffic is directed at one node, or only a few nodes, of a cluster. This traffic may represent reads, writes, or other operations. The traffic overwhelms the single machine responsible for hosting that region, causing performance degradation and potentially leading to region unavailability. This can also have adverse effects on other regions hosted by the same region server as that host is unable to service the requested load. It is important to design data access patterns such that the cluster is fully and evenly utilized."},"13244":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3444","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hotspotting","content":"To prevent hotspotting on writes, design your row keys such that rows that truly do need to be in the same region are, but in the bigger picture, data is being written to multiple regions across the cluster, rather than one at a time. Some common techniques for avoiding hotspotting are described below, along with some of their advantages and drawbacks."},"13245":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3445","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#salting-toc","content":"Salting in this sense has nothing to do with cryptography, but refers to adding random data to the start of a row key. In this case, salting refers to adding a randomly-assigned prefix to the row key to cause it to sort differently than it otherwise would. The number of possible prefixes correspond to the number of regions you want to spread the data across. Salting can be helpful if you have a few \"hot\" row key patterns which come up over and over amongst other more evenly-distributed rows. Consider the following example, which shows that salting can spread write load across multiple RegionServers, and illustrates some of the negative implications for reads."},"13246":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3446","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#salting-example-toc","content":"Suppose you have the following list of row keys, and your table is split such that there is one region for each letter of the alphabet. Prefix 'a' is one region, prefix 'b' is another. In this table, all rows starting with 'f' are in the same region. This example focuses on rows with keys like the following:"},"13247":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3447","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#salting-example-toc","content":"Now, imagine that you would like to spread these across four different regions. You decide to use four different salts: `a`, `b`, `c`, and `d`. In this scenario, each of these letter prefixes will be on a different region. After applying the salts, you have the following rowkeys instead. Since you can now write to four separate regions, you theoretically have four times the throughput when writing that you would have if all the writes were going to the same region."},"13248":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3448","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#salting-example-toc","content":"Then, if you add another row, it will randomly be assigned one of the four possible salt values and end up near one of the existing rows."},"13249":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3449","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#salting-example-toc","content":"Since this assignment will be random, you will need to do more work if you want to retrieve the rows in lexicographic order. In this way, salting attempts to increase throughput on writes, but has a cost during reads."},"13250":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3450","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hashing-toc","content":"Instead of a random assignment, you could use a one-way **hash** that would cause a given row to always be \"salted\" with the same prefix, in a way that would spread the load across the RegionServers, but allow for predictability during reads. Using a deterministic hash allows the client to reconstruct the complete rowkey and use a Get operation to retrieve that row as normal."},"13251":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3451","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hashing-example-toc","content":"Given the same situation in the salting example above, you could instead apply a one-way hash that would cause the row with key `foo0003` to always, and predictably, receive the `a` prefix. Then, to retrieve that row, you would already know the key. You could also optimize things so that certain pairs of keys were always in the same region, for instance."},"13252":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3452","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reversing-the-key-toc","content":"A third common trick for preventing hotspotting is to reverse a fixed-width or numeric row key so that the part that changes the most often (the least significant digit) is first. This effectively randomizes row keys, but sacrifices row ordering properties."},"13253":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3453","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reversing-the-key-toc","content":"See https\\://communities.intel.com/community/itpeernetwork/datastack/blog/2013/11/10/discussion-on-designing-hbase-tables, and article on Salted Tables from the Phoenix project, and the discussion in the comments of HBASE-11682 for more information about avoiding hotspotting."},"13254":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3454","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monotonically-increasing-row-keystimeseries-data","content":"In the HBase chapter of Tom White's book Hadoop: The Definitive Guide (O'Reilly) there is a an optimization note on watching out for a phenomenon where an import process walks in lock-step with all clients in concert pounding one of the table's regions (and thus, a single node), then moving onto the next region, etc. With monotonically increasing row-keys (i.e., using a timestamp), this will happen. See this comic by IKai Lan on why monotonically increasing row keys are problematic in BigTable-like datastores: monotonically increasing values are bad. The pile-up on a single region brought on by monotonically increasing keys can be mitigated by randomizing the input records to not be in sorted order, but in general it's best to avoid using a timestamp or a sequence (e.g. 1, 2, 3) as the row-key."},"13255":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3455","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monotonically-increasing-row-keystimeseries-data","content":"If you do need to upload time series data into HBase, you should study OpenTSDB as a successful example. It has a page describing the schema it uses in HBase. The key format in OpenTSDB is effectively \\[metric\\_type]\\[event\\_timestamp], which would appear at first glance to contradict the previous advice about not using a timestamp as the key. However, the difference is that the timestamp is not in the *lead* position of the key, and the design assumption is that there are dozens or hundreds (or more) of different metric types. Thus, even with a continual stream of input data with a mix of metric types, the Puts are distributed across various points of regions in the table."},"13256":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3456","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monotonically-increasing-row-keystimeseries-data","content":"See schema.casestudies for some rowkey design examples."},"13257":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3457","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#try-to-minimize-row-and-column-sizes","content":"In HBase, values are always freighted with their coordinates; as a cell value passes through the system, it'll be accompanied by its row, column name, and timestamp - always. If your rows and column names are large, especially compared to the size of the cell value, then you may run up against some interesting scenarios. One such is the case described by Marc Limotte at the tail of HBASE-3551 (recommended!). Therein, the indices that are kept on HBase storefiles (see HFile Format) to facilitate random access may end up occupying large chunks of the HBase allotted RAM because the cell value coordinates are large. Mark in the above cited comment suggests upping the block size so entries in the store file index happen at a larger interval or modify the table schema so it makes for smaller rows and column names. Compression will also make for larger indices. See the thread a question storefileIndexSize up on the user mailing list."},"13258":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3458","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#try-to-minimize-row-and-column-sizes","content":"Most of the time small inefficiencies don't matter all that much. Unfortunately, this is a case where they do. Whatever patterns are selected for ColumnFamilies, attributes, and rowkeys they could be repeated several billion times in your data."},"13259":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3459","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#try-to-minimize-row-and-column-sizes","content":"See keyvalue for more information on HBase stores data internally to see why this is important."},"13260":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3460","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-families","content":"Try to keep the ColumnFamily names as small as possible, preferably one character (e.g. \"d\" for data/default)."},"13261":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3461","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-families","content":"See KeyValue for more information on how HBase stores data internally."},"13262":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3462","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#attributes","content":"Although verbose attribute names (e.g., \"myVeryImportantAttribute\") are easier to read, prefer shorter attribute names (e.g., \"via\") to store in HBase."},"13263":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3463","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#attributes","content":"See keyvalue for more information on HBase stores data internally to see why this is important."},"13264":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3464","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowkey-length","content":"Keep them as short as is reasonable such that they can still be useful for required data access (e.g. Get vs. Scan). A short key that is useless for data access is not better than a longer key with better get/scan properties. Expect tradeoffs when designing rowkeys."},"13265":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3465","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#byte-patterns","content":"A long is 8 bytes. You can store an unsigned number up to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 in those eight bytes. If you stored this number as a String — presuming a byte per character — you need nearly 3x the bytes."},"13266":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3466","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#byte-patterns","content":"Not convinced? Below is some sample code that you can run on your own."},"13267":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3467","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#byte-patterns","content":"Unfortunately, using a binary representation of a type will make your data harder to read outside of your code. For example, this is what you will see in the shell when you increment a value:"},"13268":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3468","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#byte-patterns","content":"The shell makes a best effort to print a string, and it this case it decided to just print the hex. The same will happen to your row keys inside the region names. It can be okay if you know what's being stored, but it might also be unreadable if arbitrary data can be put in the same cells. This is the main trade-off."},"13269":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3469","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reverse-timestamps","content":"HBASE-4811 implements an API to scan a table\nor a range within a table in reverse, reducing the need to optimize your schema for forward or\nreverse scanning. This feature is available in HBase 0.98 and later. See\nScan.setReversed()\nfor more information."},"13270":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3470","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reverse-timestamps","content":"A common problem in database processing is quickly finding the most recent version of a value. A technique using reverse timestamps as a part of the key can help greatly with a special case of this problem. Also found in the HBase chapter of Tom White's book Hadoop: The Definitive Guide (O'Reilly), the technique involves appending (`Long.MAX_VALUE - timestamp`) to the end of any key, e.g. \\[key]\\[reverse\\_timestamp]."},"13271":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3471","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reverse-timestamps","content":"The most recent value for \\[key] in a table can be found by performing a Scan for \\[key] and obtaining the first record. Since HBase keys are in sorted order, this key sorts before any older row-keys for \\[key] and thus is first."},"13272":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3472","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reverse-timestamps","content":"This technique would be used instead of using Number of Versions where the intent is to hold onto all versions \"forever\" (or a very long time) and at the same time quickly obtain access to any other version by using the same Scan technique."},"13273":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3473","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowkeys-and-columnfamilies","content":"Rowkeys are scoped to ColumnFamilies. Thus, the same rowkey could exist in each ColumnFamily that exists in a table without collision."},"13274":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3474","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#immutability-of-rowkeys","content":"Rowkeys cannot be changed. The only way they can be \"changed\" in a table is if the row is deleted and then re-inserted. This is a fairly common question on the HBase dist-list so it pays to get the rowkeys right the first time (and/or before you've inserted a lot of data)."},"13275":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3475","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"If you pre-split your table, it is *critical* to understand how your rowkey will be distributed across the region boundaries. As an example of why this is important, consider the example of using displayable hex characters as the lead position of the key (e.g., \"0000000000000000\" to \"ffffffffffffffff\"). Running those key ranges through `Bytes.split` (which is the split strategy used when creating regions in `Admin.createTable(byte[] startKey, byte[] endKey, numRegions)` for 10 regions will generate the following splits..."},"13276":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3476","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"(note: the lead byte is listed to the right as a comment.) Given that the first split is a '0' and the last split is an 'f', everything is great, right? Not so fast."},"13277":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3477","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"The problem is that all the data is going to pile up in the first 2 regions and the last region thus creating a \"lumpy\" (and possibly \"hot\") region problem. To understand why, refer to an ASCII Table. '0' is byte 48, and 'f' is byte 102, but there is a huge gap in byte values (bytes 58 to 96) that will *never appear in this keyspace* because the only values are \\[0-9] and \\[a-f]. Thus, the middle regions will never be used. To make pre-splitting work with this example keyspace, a custom definition of splits (i.e., and not relying on the built-in split method) is required."},"13278":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3478","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"Lesson #1: Pre-splitting tables is generally a best practice, but you need to pre-split them in such a way that all the regions are accessible in the keyspace. While this example demonstrated the problem with a hex-key keyspace, the same problem can happen with *any* keyspace. Know your data."},"13279":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3479","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"Lesson #2: While generally not advisable, using hex-keys (and more generally, displayable data) can still work with pre-split tables as long as all the created regions are accessible in the keyspace."},"13280":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3480","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#relationship-between-rowkeys-and-region-splits","content":"To conclude this example, the following is an example of how appropriate splits can be pre-created for hex-keys:"},"13281":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3481","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#maximum-number-of-versions","content":"The maximum number of row versions to store is configured per column family via HColumnDescriptor. The default for max versions is 1. This is an important parameter because as described in Data Model section HBase does *not* overwrite row values, but rather stores different values per row by time (and qualifier). Excess versions are removed during major compactions. The number of max versions may need to be increased or decreased depending on application needs."},"13282":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3482","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#maximum-number-of-versions","content":"It is not recommended setting the number of max versions to an exceedingly high level (e.g., hundreds or more) unless those old values are very dear to you because this will greatly increase StoreFile size."},"13283":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3483","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minimum-number-of-versions","content":"Like maximum number of row versions, the minimum number of row versions to keep is configured per column family via ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder. The default for min versions is 0, which means the feature is disabled. The minimum number of row versions parameter is used together with the time-to-live parameter and can be combined with the number of row versions parameter to allow configurations such as \"keep the last T minutes worth of data, at most N versions, *but keep at least M versions around*\" (where M is the value for minimum number of row versions, M\\<N). This parameter should only be set when time-to-live is enabled for a column family and must be less than the number of row versions."},"13284":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3484","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-datatypes","content":"HBase supports a \"bytes-in/bytes-out\" interface via Put and Result, so anything that can be converted to an array of bytes can be stored as a value. Input could be strings, numbers, complex objects, or even images as long as they can rendered as bytes."},"13285":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3485","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-datatypes","content":"There are practical limits to the size of values (e.g., storing 10-50MB objects in HBase would probably be too much to ask); search the mailing list for conversations on this topic. All rows in HBase conform to the Data Model, and that includes versioning. Take that into consideration when making your design, as well as block size for the ColumnFamily."},"13286":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3486","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#counters","content":"One supported datatype that deserves special mention are \"counters\" (i.e., the ability to do atomic increments of numbers). See Increment in `Table`."},"13287":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3487","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#counters","content":"Synchronization on counters are done on the RegionServer, not in the client."},"13288":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3488","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-sizing-joins","content":"If you have multiple tables, don't forget to factor in the potential for Joins into the schema design."},"13289":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3489","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"ColumnFamilies can set a TTL length in seconds, and HBase will automatically delete rows once the expiration time is reached. This applies to *all* versions of a row - even the current one. The TTL time encoded in the HBase for the row is specified in UTC."},"13290":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3490","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"Store files which contains only expired rows are deleted on minor compaction. Setting `hbase.store.delete.expired.storefile` to `false` disables this feature. Setting minimum number of versions to other than 0 also disables this."},"13291":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3491","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"See HColumnDescriptor for more information."},"13292":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3492","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"Recent versions of HBase also support setting time to live on a per cell basis. See HBASE-10560 for more information. Cell TTLs are submitted as an attribute on mutation requests (Appends, Increments, Puts, etc.) using Mutation#setTTL. If the TTL attribute is set, it will be applied to all cells updated on the server by the operation. There are two notable differences between cell TTL handling and ColumnFamily TTLs:"},"13293":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3493","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"Cell TTLs are expressed in units of milliseconds instead of seconds."},"13294":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3494","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-to-live-ttl","content":"A cell TTLs cannot extend the effective lifetime of a cell beyond a ColumnFamily level TTL setting."},"13295":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3495","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keeping-deleted-cells","content":"By default, delete markers extend back to the beginning of time. Therefore, Get or Scan operations will not see a deleted cell (row or column), even when the Get or Scan operation indicates a time range before the delete marker was placed."},"13296":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3496","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keeping-deleted-cells","content":"ColumnFamilies can optionally keep deleted cells. In this case, deleted cells can still be retrieved, as long as these operations specify a time range that ends before the timestamp of any delete that would affect the cells. This allows for point-in-time queries even in the presence of deletes."},"13297":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3497","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keeping-deleted-cells","content":"Deleted cells are still subject to TTL and there will never be more than \"maximum number of versions\" deleted cells. A new \"raw\" scan options returns all deleted rows and the delete markers."},"13298":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3498","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-value-of-keep_deleted_cells-using-the-api-toc","content":"Let us illustrate the basic effect of setting the `KEEP_DELETED_CELLS` attribute on a table."},"13299":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3499","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-value-of-keep_deleted_cells-using-the-api-toc","content":"First, without:"},"13300":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3500","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-value-of-keep_deleted_cells-using-the-api-toc","content":"Notice how delete cells are let go."},"13301":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3501","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-value-of-keep_deleted_cells-using-the-api-toc","content":"Now let's run the same test only with `KEEP_DELETED_CELLS` set on the table (you can do table or per-column-family):"},"13302":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3502","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-value-of-keep_deleted_cells-using-the-api-toc","content":"KEEP*DELETED\\_CELLS is to avoid removing Cells from HBase when the \\_only* reason to remove them is the delete marker. So with KEEP\\_DELETED\\_CELLS enabled deleted cells would get removed if either you write more versions than the configured max, or you have a TTL and Cells are in excess of the configured timeout, etc."},"13303":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3503","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"This section could also be titled \"what if my table rowkey looks like *this* but I also want to query my table like *that*.\" A common example on the dist-list is where a row-key is of the format \"user-timestamp\" but there are reporting requirements on activity across users for certain time ranges. Thus, selecting by user is easy because it is in the lead position of the key, but time is not."},"13304":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3504","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"There is no single answer on the best way to handle this because it depends on..."},"13305":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3505","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Number of users"},"13306":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3506","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Data size and data arrival rate"},"13307":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3507","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Flexibility of reporting requirements (e.g., completely ad-hoc date selection vs. pre-configured ranges)"},"13308":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3508","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Desired execution speed of query (e.g., 90 seconds may be reasonable to some for an ad-hoc report, whereas it may be too long for others)"},"13309":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3509","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"and solutions are also influenced by the size of the cluster and how much processing power you have to throw at the solution. Common techniques are in sub-sections below. This is a comprehensive, but not exhaustive, list of approaches."},"13310":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3510","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"It should not be a surprise that secondary indexes require additional cluster space and processing. This is precisely what happens in an RDBMS because the act of creating an alternate index requires both space and processing cycles to update. RDBMS products are more advanced in this regard to handle alternative index management out of the box. However, HBase scales better at larger data volumes, so this is a feature trade-off."},"13311":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3511","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Pay attention to Apache HBase Performance Tuning when implementing any of these approaches."},"13312":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3512","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-indexes-and-alternate-query-paths","content":"Additionally, see the David Butler response in this dist-list thread HBase, mail # user - Stargate+hbase"},"13313":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3513","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#filter-query","content":"Depending on the case, it may be appropriate to use Client Request Filters. In this case, no secondary index is created. However, don't try a full-scan on a large table like this from an application (i.e., single-threaded client)."},"13314":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3514","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#periodic-update-secondary-index","content":"A secondary index could be created in another table which is periodically updated via a MapReduce job. The job could be executed intra-day, but depending on load-strategy it could still potentially be out of sync with the main data table."},"13315":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3515","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#periodic-update-secondary-index","content":"See mapreduce.example.readwrite for more information."},"13316":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3516","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dual-write-secondary-index","content":"Another strategy is to build the secondary index while publishing data to the cluster (e.g., write to data table, write to index table). If this is approach is taken after a data table already exists, then bootstrapping will be needed for the secondary index with a MapReduce job (see secondary.indexes.periodic)."},"13317":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3517","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#summary-tables","content":"Where time-ranges are very wide (e.g., year-long report) and where the data is voluminous, summary tables are a common approach. These would be generated with MapReduce jobs into another table."},"13318":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3518","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#summary-tables","content":"See mapreduce.example.summary for more information."},"13319":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3519","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-secondary-index","content":"Coprocessors act like RDBMS triggers. These were added in 0.92. For more information, see coprocessors"},"13320":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3520","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-sizing-constraints","content":"HBase currently supports 'constraints' in traditional (SQL) database parlance. The advised usage for Constraints is in enforcing business rules for attributes in the table (e.g. make sure values are in the range 1-10). Constraints could also be used to enforce referential integrity, but this is strongly discouraged as it will dramatically decrease the write throughput of the tables where integrity checking is enabled. Extensive documentation on using Constraints can be found at Constraint since version 0.94."},"13321":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3521","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"The following will describe some typical data ingestion use-cases with HBase, and how the rowkey design and construction can be approached. Note: this is just an illustration of potential approaches, not an exhaustive list. Know your data, and know your processing requirements."},"13322":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3522","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"It is highly recommended that you read the rest of the HBase and Schema Design first, before reading these case studies."},"13323":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3523","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"The following case studies are described:"},"13324":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3524","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"Log Data / Timeseries Data"},"13325":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3525","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"Log Data / Timeseries on Steroids"},"13326":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3526","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"Customer/Order"},"13327":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3527","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"Tall/Wide/Middle Schema Design"},"13328":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3528","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#schema-design-case-studies","content":"List Data"},"13329":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3529","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"Assume that the following data elements are being collected."},"13330":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3530","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"Hostname"},"13331":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3531","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"Timestamp"},"13332":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3532","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"Log event"},"13333":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3533","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"Value/message"},"13334":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3534","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data","content":"We can store them in an HBase table called LOG\\_DATA, but what will the rowkey be? From these attributes the rowkey will be some combination of hostname, timestamp, and log-event - but what specifically?"},"13335":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3535","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-in-the-rowkey-lead-position","content":"The rowkey `[timestamp][hostname][log-event]` suffers from the monotonically increasing rowkey problem described in Monotonically Increasing Row Keys/Timeseries Data."},"13336":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3536","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-in-the-rowkey-lead-position","content":"There is another pattern frequently mentioned in the dist-lists about \"bucketing\" timestamps, by performing a mod operation on the timestamp. If time-oriented scans are important, this could be a useful approach. Attention must be paid to the number of buckets, because this will require the same number of scans to return results."},"13337":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3537","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-in-the-rowkey-lead-position","content":"to construct:"},"13338":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3538","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-in-the-rowkey-lead-position","content":"As stated above, to select data for a particular timerange, a Scan will need to be performed for each bucket. 100 buckets, for example, will provide a wide distribution in the keyspace but it will require 100 Scans to obtain data for a single timestamp, so there are trade-offs."},"13339":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3539","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#host-in-the-rowkey-lead-position","content":"The rowkey `[hostname][log-event][timestamp]` is a candidate if there is a large-ish number of hosts to spread the writes and reads across the keyspace. This approach would be useful if scanning by hostname was a priority."},"13340":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3540","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-or-reverse-timestamp","content":"If the most important access path is to pull most recent events, then storing the timestamps as reverse-timestamps (e.g., `timestamp = Long.MAX_VALUE – timestamp`) will create the property of being able to do a Scan on `[hostname][log-event]` to obtain the most recently captured events."},"13341":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3541","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-or-reverse-timestamp","content":"Neither approach is wrong, it just depends on what is most appropriate for the situation."},"13342":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3542","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timestamp-or-reverse-timestamp","content":"HBASE-4811 implements an API to scan a table\nor a range within a table in reverse, reducing the need to optimize your schema for forward or\nreverse scanning. This feature is available in HBase 0.98 and later. See\nScan.setReversed()\nfor more information."},"13343":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3543","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"It is critical to remember that rowkeys are stamped on every column in HBase. If the hostname is `a` and the event type is `e1` then the resulting rowkey would be quite small. However, what if the ingested hostname is `myserver1.mycompany.com` and the event type is `com.package1.subpackage2.subsubpackage3.ImportantService`?"},"13344":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3544","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"It might make sense to use some substitution in the rowkey. There are at least two approaches: hashed and numeric. In the Hostname In The Rowkey Lead Position example, it might look like this:"},"13345":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3545","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"Composite Rowkey With Hashes:"},"13346":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3546","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[MD5 hash of hostname] = 16 bytes"},"13347":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3547","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[MD5 hash of event-type] = 16 bytes"},"13348":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3548","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[timestamp] = 8 bytes"},"13349":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3549","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"Composite Rowkey With Numeric Substitution:"},"13350":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3550","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"For this approach another lookup table would be needed in addition to LOG\\_DATA, called LOG\\_TYPES. The rowkey of LOG\\_TYPES would be:"},"13351":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3551","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"`[type]` (e.g., byte indicating hostname vs. event-type)"},"13352":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3552","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"`[bytes]` variable length bytes for raw hostname or event-type."},"13353":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3553","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"A column for this rowkey could be a long with an assigned number, which could be obtained by using an HBase counter"},"13354":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3554","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"So the resulting composite rowkey would be:"},"13355":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3555","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[substituted long for hostname] = 8 bytes"},"13356":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3556","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[substituted long for event type] = 8 bytes"},"13357":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3557","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"\\[timestamp] = 8 bytes"},"13358":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3558","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#variable-length-or-fixed-length-rowkeys","content":"In either the Hash or Numeric substitution approach, the raw values for hostname and event-type can be stored as columns."},"13359":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3559","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data-on-steroids","content":"This effectively is the OpenTSDB approach. What OpenTSDB does is re-write data and pack rows into columns for certain time-periods. For a detailed explanation, see: http\\://opentsdb.net/schema.html, and Lessons Learned from OpenTSDB from HBaseCon2012."},"13360":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3560","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data-on-steroids","content":"But this is how the general concept works: data is ingested, for example, in this manner..."},"13361":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3561","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data-on-steroids","content":"with separate rowkeys for each detailed event, but is re-written like this..."},"13362":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3562","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---log-data-and-timeseries-data-on-steroids","content":"and each of the above events are converted into columns stored with a time-offset relative to the beginning timerange (e.g., every 5 minutes). This is obviously a very advanced processing technique, but HBase makes this possible."},"13363":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3563","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Assume that HBase is used to store customer and order information. There are two core record-types being ingested: a Customer record type, and Order record type."},"13364":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3564","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"The Customer record type would include all the things that you'd typically expect:"},"13365":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3565","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Customer number"},"13366":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3566","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Customer name"},"13367":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3567","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Address (e.g., city, state, zip)"},"13368":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3568","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Phone numbers, etc."},"13369":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3569","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"The Order record type would include things like:"},"13370":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3570","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Customer number"},"13371":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3571","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Order number"},"13372":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3572","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Sales date"},"13373":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3573","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"A series of nested objects for shipping locations and line-items (see Order Object Design for details)"},"13374":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3574","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Assuming that the combination of customer number and sales order uniquely identify an order, these two attributes will compose the rowkey, and specifically a composite key such as:"},"13375":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3575","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"for an ORDER table. However, there are more design decisions to make: are the *raw* values the best choices for rowkeys?"},"13376":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3576","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"The same design questions in the Log Data use-case confront us here. What is the keyspace of the customer number, and what is the format (e.g., numeric? alphanumeric?) As it is advantageous to use fixed-length keys in HBase, as well as keys that can support a reasonable spread in the keyspace, similar options appear:"},"13377":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3577","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"Composite Rowkey With Hashes:"},"13378":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3578","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---customerorder","content":"\\[MD5 of customer number] = 16 bytes"},"13379":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3579","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---cu