 is likely to be the approach with an RDBMS, but that's not your only option with HBase. The cons of such an approach is that to retrieve information about any Order, you will need:"},"13406":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3606","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completely-normalized","content":"Get on the ORDER table for the Order"},"13407":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3607","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completely-normalized","content":"Scan on the SHIPPING\\_LOCATION table for that order to get the ShippingLocation instances"},"13408":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3608","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completely-normalized","content":"Scan on the LINE\\_ITEM for each ShippingLocation"},"13409":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3609","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completely-normalized","content":"granted, this is what an RDBMS would do under the covers anyway, but since there are no joins in HBase you're just more aware of this fact."},"13410":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3610","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"With this approach, there would exist a single table ORDER that would contain"},"13411":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3611","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"The Order rowkey was described above: schema.casestudies.custorder"},"13412":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3612","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[order-rowkey]`"},"13413":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3613","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[ORDER record type]`"},"13414":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3614","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"The ShippingLocation composite rowkey would be something like this:"},"13415":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3615","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[order-rowkey]`"},"13416":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3616","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[SHIPPING record type]`"},"13417":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3617","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[shipping location number]` (e.g., 1st location, 2nd, etc.)"},"13418":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3618","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"The LineItem composite rowkey would be something like this:"},"13419":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3619","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[order-rowkey]`"},"13420":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3620","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[LINE record type]`"},"13421":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3621","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[shipping location number]` (e.g., 1st location, 2nd, etc.)"},"13422":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3622","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#single-table-with-record-types","content":"`[line item number]` (e.g., 1st lineitem, 2nd, etc.)"},"13423":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3623","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"A variant of the Single Table With Record Types approach is to denormalize and flatten some of the object hierarchy, such as collapsing the ShippingLocation attributes onto each LineItem instance."},"13424":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3624","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"The LineItem composite rowkey would be something like this:"},"13425":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3625","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"`[order-rowkey]`"},"13426":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3626","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"`[LINE record type]`"},"13427":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3627","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"`[line item number]` (e.g., 1st lineitem, 2nd, etc., care must be taken that there are unique across the entire order)"},"13428":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3628","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"and the LineItem columns would be something like this:"},"13429":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3629","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"itemNumber"},"13430":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3630","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"quantity"},"13431":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3631","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"price"},"13432":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3632","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"shipToLine1 (denormalized from ShippingLocation)"},"13433":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3633","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"shipToLine2 (denormalized from ShippingLocation)"},"13434":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3634","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"shipToCity (denormalized from ShippingLocation)"},"13435":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3635","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"shipToState (denormalized from ShippingLocation)"},"13436":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3636","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"shipToZip (denormalized from ShippingLocation)"},"13437":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3637","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#denormalized","content":"The pros of this approach include a less complex object hierarchy, but one of the cons is that updating gets more complicated in case any of this information changes."},"13438":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3638","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#object-blob","content":"With this approach, the entire Order object graph is treated, in one way or another, as a BLOB. For example, the ORDER table's rowkey was described above: schema.casestudies.custorder, and a single column called \"order\" would contain an object that could be deserialized that contained a container Order, ShippingLocations, and LineItems."},"13439":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3639","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#object-blob","content":"There are many options here: JSON, XML, Java Serialization, Avro, Hadoop Writables, etc. All of them are variants of the same approach: encode the object graph to a byte-array. Care should be taken with this approach to ensure backward compatibility in case the object model changes such that older persisted structures can still be read back out of HBase."},"13440":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3640","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#object-blob","content":"Pros are being able to manage complex object graphs with minimal I/O (e.g., a single HBase Get per Order in this example), but the cons include the aforementioned warning about backward compatibility of serialization, language dependencies of serialization (e.g., Java Serialization only works with Java clients), the fact that you have to deserialize the entire object to get any piece of information inside the BLOB, and the difficulty in getting frameworks like Hive to work with custom objects like this."},"13441":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3641","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---tallwidemiddle-schema-design-smackdown","content":"This section will describe additional schema design questions that appear on the dist-list, specifically about tall and wide tables. These are general guidelines and not laws - each application must consider its own needs."},"13442":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3642","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rows-vs-versions","content":"A common question is whether one should prefer rows or HBase's built-in-versioning. The context is typically where there are \"a lot\" of versions of a row to be retained (e.g., where it is significantly above the HBase default of 1 max versions). The rows-approach would require storing a timestamp in some portion of the rowkey so that they would not overwrite with each successive update."},"13443":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3643","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rows-vs-versions","content":"Preference: Rows (generally speaking)."},"13444":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3644","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rows-vs-columns","content":"Another common question is whether one should prefer rows or columns. The context is typically in extreme cases of wide tables, such as having 1 row with 1 million attributes, or 1 million rows with 1 columns apiece."},"13445":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3645","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rows-vs-columns","content":"Preference: Rows (generally speaking). To be clear, this guideline is in the context is in extremely wide cases, not in the standard use-case where one needs to store a few dozen or hundred columns. But there is also a middle path between these two options, and that is \"Rows as Columns.\""},"13446":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3646","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rows-as-columns","content":"The middle path between Rows vs. Columns is packing data that would be a separate row into columns, for certain rows. OpenTSDB is the best example of this case where a single row represents a defined time-range, and then discrete events are treated as columns. This approach is often more complex, and may require the additional complexity of re-writing your data, but has the advantage of being I/O efficient. For an overview of this approach, see schema.casestudies.log-steroids."},"13447":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3647","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"The following is an exchange from the user dist-list regarding a fairly common question: how to handle per-user list data in Apache HBase."},"13448":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3648","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"QUESTION \\*"},"13449":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3649","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"We're looking at how to store a large amount of (per-user) list data in HBase, and we were trying to figure out what kind of access pattern made the most sense. One option is store the majority of the data in a key, so we could have something like:"},"13450":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3650","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"The other option we had was to do this entirely using:"},"13451":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3651","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"where each row would contain multiple values. So in one case reading the first thirty values would be:"},"13452":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3652","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"And in the second case it would be"},"13453":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3653","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"The general usage pattern would be to read only the first 30 values of these lists, with infrequent access reading deeper into the lists. Some users would have ⇐ 30 total values in these lists, and some users would have millions (i.e. power-law distribution)"},"13454":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3654","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"The single-value format seems like it would take up more space on HBase, but would offer some improved retrieval / pagination flexibility. Would there be any significant performance advantages to be able to paginate via gets vs paginating with scans?"},"13455":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3655","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"My initial understanding was that doing a scan should be faster if our paging size is unknown (and caching is set appropriately), but that gets should be faster if we'll always need the same page size. I've ended up hearing different people tell me opposite things about performance. I assume the page sizes would be relatively consistent, so for most use cases we could guarantee that we only wanted one page of data in the fixed-page-length case. I would also assume that we would have infrequent updates, but may have inserts into the middle of these lists (meaning we'd need to update all subsequent rows)."},"13456":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3656","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"Thanks for help / suggestions / follow-up questions."},"13457":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3657","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"ANSWER \\*"},"13458":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3658","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"If I understand you correctly, you're ultimately trying to store triples in the form \"user, valueid, value\", right? E.g., something like:"},"13459":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3659","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"(But the usernames are fixed width, and the valueids are fixed width)."},"13460":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3660","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"And, your access pattern is along the lines of: \"for user X, list the next 30 values, starting with valueid Y\". Is that right? And these values should be returned sorted by valueid?"},"13461":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3661","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"The tl;dr version is that you should probably go with one row per user+value, and not build a complicated intra-row pagination scheme on your own unless you're really sure it is needed."},"13462":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3662","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"Your two options mirror a common question people have when designing HBase schemas: should I go \"tall\" or \"wide\"? Your first schema is \"tall\": each row represents one value for one user, and so there are many rows in the table for each user; the row key is user + valueid, and there would be (presumably) a single column qualifier that means \"the value\". This is great if you want to scan over rows in sorted order by row key (thus my question above, about whether these ids are sorted correctly). You can start a scan at any user+valueid, read the next 30, and be done. What you're giving up is the ability to have transactional guarantees around all the rows for one user, but it doesn't sound like you need that. Doing it this way is generally recommended (see here)."},"13463":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3663","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"Your second option is \"wide\": you store a bunch of values in one row, using different qualifiers (where the qualifier is the valueid). The simple way to do that would be to just store ALL values for one user in a single row. I'm guessing you jumped to the \"paginated\" version because you're assuming that storing millions of columns in a single row would be bad for performance, which may or may not be true; as long as you're not trying to do too much in a single request, or do things like scanning over and returning all of the cells in the row, it shouldn't be fundamentally worse. The client has methods that allow you to get specific slices of columns."},"13464":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3664","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"Note that neither case fundamentally uses more disk space than the other; you're just \"shifting\" part of the identifying information for a value either to the left (into the row key, in option one) or to the right (into the column qualifiers in option 2). Under the covers, every key/value still stores the whole row key, and column family name. (If this is a bit confusing, take an hour and watch Lars George's excellent video about understanding HBase schema design: http\\://www\\.youtube.com/watch?v=\\_HLoH\\_PgrLk)."},"13465":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3665","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case-study---list-data","content":"A manually paginated version has lots more complexities, as you note, like having to keep track of how many things are in each page, re-shuffling if new values are inserted, etc. That seems significantly more complex. It might have some slight speed advantages (or disadvantages!) at extremely high throughput, and the only way to really know that would be to try it out. If you don't have time to build it both ways and compare, my advice would be to start with the simplest option (one row per user+value). Start simple and iterate! :)"},"13466":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3666","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"Set `hbase.regionserver.handler.count` (in `hbase-site.xml`) to cores x spindles for concurrency."},"13467":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3667","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"Optionally, split the call queues into separate read and write queues for differentiated service. The parameter `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor` specifies the number of call queues:"},"13468":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3668","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`0` means a single shared queue"},"13469":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3669","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`1` means one queue for each handler."},"13470":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3670","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"A value between `0` and `1` allocates the number of queues proportionally to the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers."},"13471":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3671","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"Use `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio` (`hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.share` in 0.98) to split the call queues into read and write queues:"},"13472":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3672","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`0.5` means there will be the same number of read and write queues"},"13473":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3673","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`< 0.5` for more write than read"},"13474":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3674","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`> 0.5` for more read than write"},"13475":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3675","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"Set `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.scan.ratio` (HBase 1.0+) to split read call queues into small-read and long-read queues:"},"13476":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3676","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"0.5 means that there will be the same number of short-read and long-read queues"},"13477":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3677","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`< 0.5` for more short-read"},"13478":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3678","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-hbase-server-rpc-handling","content":"`> 0.5` for more long-read"},"13479":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3679","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"Disable Nagle's algorithm. Delayed ACKs can add up to \\~200ms to RPC round trip time. Set the following parameters:"},"13480":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3680","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"In Hadoop's `core-site.xml`:"},"13481":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3681","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"`ipc.server.tcpnodelay = true`"},"13482":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3682","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"`ipc.client.tcpnodelay = true`"},"13483":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3683","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"In HBase's `hbase-site.xml`:"},"13484":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3684","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"`hbase.ipc.client.tcpnodelay = true`"},"13485":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3685","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-nagle-for-rpc","content":"`hbase.ipc.server.tcpnodelay = true`"},"13486":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3686","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"Detect regionserver failure as fast as reasonable. Set the following parameters:"},"13487":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3687","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"In `hbase-site.xml`, set `zookeeper.session.timeout` to 30 seconds or less to bound failure detection (20-30 seconds is a good start)."},"13488":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3688","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"Note: Zookeeper clients negotiate a session timeout with the server during client init. Server enforces this timeout to be in the range \\[`minSessionTimeout`, `maxSessionTimeout`] and both these timeouts (measured in milliseconds) are configurable in Zookeeper service configuration. If not configured, these default to 2 \\* `tickTime` and 20 \\* `tickTime` respectively (`tickTime` is the basic time unit used by ZooKeeper, as measured in milliseconds. It is used to regulate heartbeats, timeouts etc.). Refer to Zookeeper documentation for additional details."},"13489":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3689","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"Detect and avoid unhealthy or failed HDFS DataNodes: in `hdfs-site.xml` and `hbase-site.xml`, set the following parameters:"},"13490":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3690","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"`dfs.namenode.avoid.read.stale.datanode = true`"},"13491":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3691","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limit-server-failure-impact","content":"`dfs.namenode.avoid.write.stale.datanode = true`"},"13492":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3692","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"Skip the network for local blocks when the RegionServer goes to read from HDFS by exploiting HDFS's Short-Circuit Local Reads facility. Note how setup must be done both at the datanode and on the dfsclient ends of the conneciton — i.e. at the RegionServer and how both ends need to have loaded the hadoop native `.so` library. After configuring your hadoop setting *dfs.client.read.shortcircuit* to *true* and configuring the *dfs.domain.socket.path* path for the datanode and dfsclient to share and restarting, next configure the regionserver/dfsclient side."},"13493":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3693","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"In `hbase-site.xml`, set the following parameters:"},"13494":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3694","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.client.read.shortcircuit = true`"},"13495":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3695","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.skip.checksum = true` so we don't double checksum (HBase does its own checksumming to save on i/os)."},"13496":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3696","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.domain.socket.path` to match what was set for the datanodes."},"13497":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3697","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size = 131072` Important to avoid OOME — hbase has a default it uses if unset, see `hbase.dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size`; its default is 131072."},"13498":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3698","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"Ensure data locality. In `hbase-site.xml`, set `hbase.hstore.min.locality.to.skip.major.compact = 0.7` (Meaning that 0.7 \\<= n \\<= 1)"},"13499":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3699","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"Make sure DataNodes have enough handlers for block transfers. In `hdfs-site.xml`, set the following parameters:"},"13500":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3700","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.datanode.max.xcievers >= 8192`"},"13501":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3701","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"`dfs.datanode.handler.count =` number of spindles"},"13502":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3702","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"Check the RegionServer logs after restart. You should only see complaint if misconfiguration. Otherwise, shortcircuit read operates quietly in background. It does not provide metrics so no optics on how effective it is but read latencies should show a marked improvement, especially if good data locality, lots of random reads, and dataset is larger than available cache."},"13503":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3703","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"Other advanced configurations that you might play with, especially if shortcircuit functionality is complaining in the logs, include `dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.streams.cache.size` and `dfs.client.socketcache.capacity`. Documentation is sparse on these options. You'll have to read source code."},"13504":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3704","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"RegionServer metric system exposes HDFS short circuit read metrics `shortCircuitBytesRead`. Other HDFS read metrics, including `totalBytesRead` (The total number of bytes read from HDFS), `localBytesRead` (The number of bytes read from the local HDFS DataNode), `zeroCopyBytesRead` (The number of bytes read through HDFS zero copy) are available and can be used to troubleshoot short-circuit read issues."},"13505":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3705","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optimize-on-the-server-side-for-low-latency","content":"For more on short-circuit reads, see Colin's old blog on rollout, How Improved Short-Circuit Local Reads Bring Better Performance and Security to Hadoop. The HDFS-347 issue also makes for an interesting read showing the HDFS community at its best (caveat a few comments)."},"13506":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3706","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Use the CMS collector: `-XX:+UseConcMarkSweepGC`"},"13507":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3707","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Keep eden space as small as possible to minimize average collection time. Example:"},"13508":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3708","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Optimize for low collection latency rather than throughput: `-Xmn512m`"},"13509":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3709","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Collect eden in parallel: `-XX:+UseParNewGC`"},"13510":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3710","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Avoid collection under pressure: `-XX:+UseCMSInitiatingOccupancyOnly`"},"13511":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3711","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Limit per request scanner result sizing so everything fits into survivor space but doesn't tenure. In `hbase-site.xml`, set `hbase.client.scanner.max.result.size` to 1/8th of eden space (with -`Xmn512m` this is \\~51MB )"},"13512":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3712","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tune-jvm-gc-for-low-collection-latencies","content":"Set `max.result.size` x `handler.count` less than survivor space"},"13513":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3713","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#os-level-tuning","content":"Turn transparent huge pages (THP) off:"},"13514":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3714","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#os-level-tuning","content":"Set `vm.swappiness = 0`"},"13515":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3715","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#os-level-tuning","content":"Set `vm.min_free_kbytes` to at least 1GB (8GB on larger memory systems)"},"13516":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3716","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#os-level-tuning","content":"Disable NUMA zone reclaim with `vm.zone_reclaim_mode = 0`"},"13517":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3717","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"In `hbase-site.xml` on the client side, set the following parameters:"},"13518":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3718","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"Set `hbase.client.pause = 1000`"},"13519":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3719","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"Set `hbase.client.retries.number = 3`"},"13520":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3720","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"If you want to ride over splits and region moves, increase `hbase.client.retries.number` substantially (>= 20)"},"13521":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3721","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"Set the RecoverableZookeeper retry count: `zookeeper.recovery.retry = 1` (no retry)"},"13522":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3722","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-where-failing-quickly-is-better-than-waiting","content":"In `hbase-site.xml` on the server side, set the Zookeeper session timeout for detecting server failures: `zookeeper.session.timeout` ⇐ 30 seconds (20-30 is good)."},"13523":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3723","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"**HBase timeline consistency (HBASE-10070)** With read replicas enabled, read-only copies of regions (replicas) are distributed over the cluster. One RegionServer services the default or primary replica, which is the only replica that can service writes. Other RegionServers serve the secondary replicas, follow the primary RegionServer, and only see committed updates. The secondary replicas are read-only, but can serve reads immediately while the primary is failing over, cutting read availability blips from seconds to milliseconds. Phoenix supports timeline consistency as of 4.4.0 Tips:"},"13524":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3724","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"Deploy HBase 1.0.0 or later."},"13525":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3725","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"Enable timeline consistent replicas on the server side."},"13526":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3726","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"Use one of the following methods to set timeline consistency:"},"13527":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3727","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"Use `ALTER SESSION SET CONSISTENCY = 'TIMELINE'`"},"13528":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3728","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#for-applications-that-can-tolerate-slightly-out-of-date-information","content":"Set the connection property `Consistency` to `timeline` in the JDBC connect string"},"13529":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3729","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#more-information","content":"See the Performance section perf.schema for more information about operational and performance schema design options, such as Bloom Filters, Table-configured regionsizes, compression, and blocksizes."},"13530":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3730","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce","content":"Apache MapReduce is a software framework used to analyze large amounts of data. It is provided by Apache Hadoop.\nMapReduce itself is out of the scope of this document.\nA good place to get started with MapReduce is https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r2.6.0/hadoop-mapreduce-client/hadoop-mapreduce-client-core/MapReduceTutorial.html.\nMapReduce version 2 (MR2)is now part of YARN."},"13531":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3731","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce","content":"This chapter discusses specific configuration steps you need to take to use MapReduce on data within HBase.\nIn addition, it discusses other interactions and issues between HBase and MapReduce jobs."},"13532":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3732","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce","content":"There are two mapreduce packages in HBase as in MapReduce itself: *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapred*\nand *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce*. The former does old-style API and the latter the new\nmode. The latter has more facility though you can usually find an equivalent in the older package.\nPick the package that goes with your MapReduce deploy. When in doubt or starting over, pick\n*org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce*. In the notes below, we refer to *o.a.h.h.mapreduce* but\nreplace with *o.a.h.h.mapred* if that is what you are using."},"13533":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3733","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"By default, MapReduce jobs deployed to a MapReduce cluster do not have access to\neither the HBase configuration under `$HBASE_CONF_DIR` or the HBase classes."},"13534":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3734","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"To give the MapReduce jobs the access they need, you could add \\_hbase-site.xml\\_to \\_\\$HADOOP*HOME/conf&#x2A; and add HBase jars to the *\\$HADOOP\\_HOME/lib&#x2A; directory.\nYou would then need to copy these changes across your cluster. Or you could edit *\\$HADOOP\\_HOME/conf/hadoop-env.sh* and add hbase dependencies to the `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` variable.\nNeither of these approaches is recommended because it will pollute your Hadoop install with HBase references.\nIt also requires you restart the Hadoop cluster before Hadoop can use the HBase data."},"13535":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3735","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The recommended approach is to let HBase add its dependency jars and use `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` or `-libjars`."},"13536":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3736","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"Since HBase `0.90.x`, HBase adds its dependency JARs to the job configuration itself.\nThe dependencies only need to be available on the local `CLASSPATH` and from here they'll be picked\nup and bundled into the fat job jar deployed to the MapReduce cluster. A basic trick just passes\nthe full hbase classpath — all hbase and dependent jars as well as configurations — to the mapreduce\njob runner letting hbase utility pick out from the full-on classpath what it needs adding them to the\nMapReduce job configuration (See the source at `TableMapReduceUtil#addDependencyJars(org.apache.hadoop.mapreduce.Job)` for how this is done)."},"13537":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3737","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The following example runs the bundled HBase RowCounter MapReduce job against a table named `usertable`.\nIt sets into `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` the jars hbase needs to run in an MapReduce context (including configuration files such as hbase-site.xml).\nBe sure to use the correct version of the HBase JAR for your system; replace the VERSION string in the below command line w/ the version of\nyour local hbase install. The backticks (\\`\\`\\`) cause the shell to execute the sub-commands, setting the output of `hbase classpath` into `HADOOP_CLASSPATH`.\nThis example assumes you use a BASH-compatible shell."},"13538":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3738","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The above command will launch a row counting mapreduce job against the hbase cluster that is pointed to by your local configuration on a cluster that the hadoop configs are pointing to."},"13539":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3739","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The main for the `hbase-mapreduce.jar` is a Driver that lists a few basic mapreduce tasks that ship with hbase.\nFor example, presuming your install is hbase `2.0.0-SNAPSHOT`:"},"13540":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3740","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"You can use the above listed shortnames for mapreduce jobs as in the below re-run of the row counter job (again, presuming your install is hbase `2.0.0-SNAPSHOT`):"},"13541":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3741","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"You might find the more selective `hbase mapredcp` tool output of interest; it lists the minimum set of jars needed\nto run a basic mapreduce job against an hbase install. It does not include configuration. You'll probably need to add\nthese if you want your MapReduce job to find the target cluster. You'll probably have to also add pointers to extra jars\nonce you start to do anything of substance. Just specify the extras by passing the system propery `-Dtmpjars` when\nyou run `hbase mapredcp`."},"13542":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3742","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"For jobs that do not package their dependencies or call `TableMapReduceUtil#addDependencyJars`, the following command structure is necessary:"},"13543":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3743","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"The example may not work if you are running HBase from its build directory rather than an installed location.\nYou may see an error like the following:"},"13544":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3744","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"If this occurs, try modifying the command as follows, so that it uses the HBase JARs from the &#x2A;target/* directory within the build environment."},"13545":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3745","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"Some MapReduce jobs that use HBase fail to launch.\nThe symptom is an exception similar to the following:"},"13546":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3746","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This is caused by an optimization introduced in HBASE-9867 that inadvertently introduced a classloader dependency."},"13547":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3747","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This affects both jobs using the `-libjars` option and \"fat jar,\" those which package their runtime dependencies in a nested `lib` folder."},"13548":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3748","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"In order to satisfy the new classloader requirements, `hbase-protocol.jar` must be included in Hadoop's classpath.\nSee HBase, MapReduce, and the CLASSPATH for current recommendations for resolving classpath errors.\nThe following is included for historical purposes."},"13549":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3749","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This can be resolved system-wide by including a reference to the `hbase-protocol.jar` in Hadoop's lib directory, via a symlink or by copying the jar into the new location."},"13550":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3750","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"This can also be achieved on a per-job launch basis by including it in the `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` environment variable at job submission time.\nWhen launching jobs that package their dependencies, all three of the following job launching commands satisfy this requirement:"},"13551":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3751","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"For jars that do not package their dependencies, the following command structure is necessary:"},"13552":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3752","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-and-the-classpath","content":"See also HBASE-10304 for further discussion of this issue."},"13553":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3753","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"TableMapReduceUtil now restores the option to set scanner caching (the number of rows which are cached before returning the result to the client) on the Scan object that is passed in.\nThis functionality was lost due to a bug in HBase 0.95 (HBASE-11558), which is fixed for HBase 0.98.5 and 0.96.3.\nThe priority order for choosing the scanner caching is as follows:"},"13554":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3754","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Caching settings which are set on the scan object."},"13555":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3755","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Caching settings which are specified via the configuration option `hbase.client.scanner.caching`, which can either be set manually in *hbase-site.xml* or via the helper method `TableMapReduceUtil.setScannerCaching()`."},"13556":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3756","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"The default value `HConstants.DEFAULT_HBASE_CLIENT_SCANNER_CACHING`, which is set to `100`."},"13557":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3757","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"Optimizing the caching settings is a balance between the time the client waits for a result and the number of sets of results the client needs to receive.\nIf the caching setting is too large, the client could end up waiting for a long time or the request could even time out.\nIf the setting is too small, the scan needs to return results in several pieces.\nIf you think of the scan as a shovel, a bigger cache setting is analogous to a bigger shovel, and a smaller cache setting is equivalent to more shoveling in order to fill the bucket."},"13558":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3758","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"The list of priorities mentioned above allows you to set a reasonable default, and override it for specific operations."},"13559":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3759","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-scan-caching","content":"See the API documentation for Scan for more details."},"13560":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3760","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bundled-hbase-mapreduce-jobs","content":"The HBase JAR also serves as a Driver for some bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo learn about the bundled MapReduce jobs, run the following command."},"13561":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3761","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bundled-hbase-mapreduce-jobs","content":"Each of the valid program names are bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo run one of the jobs, model your command after the following example."},"13562":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3762","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"HBase can be used as a data source, TableInputFormat, and data sink, TableOutputFormat or MultiTableOutputFormat, for MapReduce jobs.\nWriting MapReduce jobs that read or write HBase, it is advisable to subclass TableMapper and/or TableReducer.\nSee the do-nothing pass-through classes IdentityTableMapper and IdentityTableReducer for basic usage.\nFor a more involved example, see RowCounter or review the `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.mapreduce.TestTableMapReduce` unit test."},"13563":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3763","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"If you run MapReduce jobs that use HBase as source or sink, need to specify source and sink table and column names in your configuration."},"13564":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3764","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"When you read from HBase, the `TableInputFormat` requests the list of regions from HBase and makes a map, which is either a `map-per-region` or `mapreduce.job.maps` map, whichever is smaller.\nIf your job only has two maps, raise `mapreduce.job.maps` to a number greater than the number of regions.\nMaps will run on the adjacent TaskTracker/NodeManager if you are running a TaskTracer/NodeManager and RegionServer per node.\nWhen writing to HBase, it may make sense to avoid the Reduce step and write back into HBase from within your map.\nThis approach works when your job does not need the sort and collation that MapReduce does on the map-emitted data.\nOn insert, HBase 'sorts' so there is no point double-sorting (and shuffling data around your MapReduce cluster) unless you need to.\nIf you do not need the Reduce, your map might emit counts of records processed for reporting at the end of the job, or set the number of Reduces to zero and use TableOutputFormat.\nIf running the Reduce step makes sense in your case, you should typically use multiple reducers so that load is spread across the HBase cluster."},"13565":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3765","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-as-a-mapreduce-job-data-source-and-data-sink","content":"A new HBase partitioner, the HRegionPartitioner, can run as many reducers the number of existing regions.\nThe HRegionPartitioner is suitable when your table is large and your upload will not greatly alter the number of existing regions upon completion.\nOtherwise use the default partitioner."},"13566":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3766","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#writing-hfiles-directly-during-bulk-import","content":"If you are importing into a new table, you can bypass the HBase API and write your content directly to the filesystem, formatted into HBase data files (HFiles). Your import will run faster, perhaps an order of magnitude faster.\nFor more on how this mechanism works, see Bulk Load."},"13567":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3767","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowcounter-example","content":"The included RowCounter MapReduce job uses `TableInputFormat` and does a count of all rows in the specified table.\nTo run it, use the following command:"},"13568":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3768","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowcounter-example","content":"This will invoke the HBase MapReduce Driver class.\nSelect `rowcounter` from the choice of jobs offered.\nThis will print rowcounter usage advice to standard output.\nSpecify the tablename, column to count, and output directory.\nIf you have classpath errors, see HBase, MapReduce, and the CLASSPATH."},"13569":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3769","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-default-hbase-mapreduce-splitter","content":"When TableInputFormat is used to source an HBase table in a MapReduce job, its splitter will make a map task for each region of the table.\nThus, if there are 100 regions in the table, there will be 100 map-tasks for the job - regardless of how many column families are selected in the Scan."},"13570":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3770","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-splitters","content":"For those interested in implementing custom splitters, see the method `getSplits` in TableInputFormatBase.\nThat is where the logic for map-task assignment resides."},"13571":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3771","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-read-example","content":"The following is an example of using HBase as a MapReduce source in read-only manner.\nSpecifically, there is a Mapper instance but no Reducer, and nothing is being emitted from the Mapper.\nThe job would be defined as follows..."},"13572":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3772","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-read-example","content":"...and the mapper instance would extend TableMapper..."},"13573":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3773","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"The following is an example of using HBase both as a source and as a sink with MapReduce.\nThis example will simply copy data from one table to another."},"13574":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3774","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"An explanation is required of what `TableMapReduceUtil` is doing, especially with the reducer. TableOutputFormat is being used as the outputFormat class, and several parameters are being set on the config (e.g., `TableOutputFormat.OUTPUT_TABLE`), as well as setting the reducer output key to `ImmutableBytesWritable` and reducer value to `Writable`.\nThese could be set by the programmer on the job and conf, but `TableMapReduceUtil` tries to make things easier."},"13575":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3775","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"The following is the example mapper, which will create a `Put` and matching the input `Result` and emit it.\nNote: this is what the CopyTable utility does."},"13576":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3776","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"There isn't actually a reducer step, so `TableOutputFormat` takes care of sending the `Put` to the target table."},"13577":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3777","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example","content":"This is just an example, developers could choose not to use `TableOutputFormat` and connect to the target table themselves."},"13578":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3778","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-readwrite-example-with-multi-table-output","content":"TODO: example for `MultiTableOutputFormat`."},"13579":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3779","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"The following example uses HBase as a MapReduce source and sink with a summarization step.\nThis example will count the number of distinct instances of a value in a table and write those summarized counts in another table."},"13580":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3780","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"In this example mapper a column with a String-value is chosen as the value to summarize upon.\nThis value is used as the key to emit from the mapper, and an `IntWritable` represents an instance counter."},"13581":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3781","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-example","content":"In the reducer, the \"ones\" are counted (just like any other MR example that does this), and then emits a `Put`."},"13582":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3782","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-file-example","content":"This very similar to the summary example above, with exception that this is using HBase as a MapReduce source but HDFS as the sink.\nThe differences are in the job setup and in the reducer.\nThe mapper remains the same."},"13583":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3783","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-file-example","content":"As stated above, the previous Mapper can run unchanged with this example.\nAs for the Reducer, it is a \"generic\" Reducer instead of extending TableMapper and emitting Puts."},"13584":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3784","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"It is also possible to perform summaries without a reducer - if you use HBase as the reducer."},"13585":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3785","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"An HBase target table would need to exist for the job summary.\nThe Table method `incrementColumnValue` would be used to atomically increment values.\nFrom a performance perspective, it might make sense to keep a Map of values with their values to be incremented for each map-task, and make one update per key at during the `cleanup` method of the mapper.\nHowever, your mileage may vary depending on the number of rows to be processed and unique keys."},"13586":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3786","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-hbase-without-reducer","content":"In the end, the summary results are in HBase."},"13587":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3787","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"Sometimes it is more appropriate to generate summaries to an RDBMS.\nFor these cases, it is possible to generate summaries directly to an RDBMS via a custom reducer.\nThe `setup` method can connect to an RDBMS (the connection information can be passed via custom parameters in the context) and the cleanup method can close the connection."},"13588":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3788","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"It is critical to understand that number of reducers for the job affects the summarization implementation, and you'll have to design this into your reducer.\nSpecifically, whether it is designed to run as a singleton (one reducer) or multiple reducers.\nNeither is right or wrong, it depends on your use-case.\nRecognize that the more reducers that are assigned to the job, the more simultaneous connections to the RDBMS will be created - this will scale, but only to a point."},"13589":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3789","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mapreduce-summary-to-rdbms","content":"In the end, the summary results are written to your RDBMS table/s."},"13590":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3790","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#accessing-other-hbase-tables-in-a-mapreduce-job","content":"Although the framework currently allows one HBase table as input to a MapReduce job, other HBase tables can be accessed as lookup tables, etc., in a MapReduce job via creating an Table instance in the setup method of the Mapper."},"13591":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3791","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-speculative-execution","content":"It is generally advisable to turn off speculative execution for MapReduce jobs that use HBase as a source.\nThis can either be done on a per-Job basis through properties, or on the entire cluster.\nEspecially for longer running jobs, speculative execution will create duplicate map-tasks which will double-write your data to HBase; this is probably not what you want."},"13592":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3792","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mapreduce-speculative-execution","content":"See Speculative Execution for more information."},"13593":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3793","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"**Reporting Security Bugs**"},"13594":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3794","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"HBase adheres to the Apache Software Foundation's policy on reported vulnerabilities, available at http\\://apache.org/security/."},"13595":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3795","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"If you wish to send an encrypted report, you can use the GPG details provided for the general ASF security list. This will likely increase the response time to your report."},"13596":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3796","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"To protect existing HBase installations from exploitation, please **do not** use JIRA to report\nsecurity-related bugs. Instead, send your report to the mailing list `private@hbase.apache.org`,\nwhich allows anyone to send messages, but restricts who can read them. Someone on that list will\ncontact you to follow up on your report."},"13597":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3797","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"**Security Model**"},"13598":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3798","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security","content":"Before reporting a security issue, please review the Apache HBase Security Model page, which describes the project's security assumptions, trust boundaries, and what constitutes a valid vulnerability report."},"13599":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3799","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"A default HBase install uses insecure HTTP connections for Web UIs for the master and region servers. To enable secure HTTP (HTTPS) connections instead, set `hbase.ssl.enabled` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml*(Please prepare SSL certificate and ssl configuration file in advance). This does not change the port used by the Web UI. To change the port for the web UI for a given HBase component, configure that port's setting in hbase-site.xml. These settings are:"},"13600":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3800","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"`hbase.master.info.port`"},"13601":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3801","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"`hbase.regionserver.info.port`"},"13602":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3802","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"If you enable secure HTTP, clients should connect to HBase using the `https://` URL. Clients using the `http://` URL will receive an HTTP response of `200`, but will not receive any data. The following exception is logged:"},"13603":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3803","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"This is because the same port is used for HTTP and HTTPS."},"13604":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3804","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-secure-http-https-for-the-web-ui","content":"HBase uses Jetty for the Web UI. Without modifying Jetty itself, it does not seem possible to configure Jetty to redirect one port to another on the same host. See Nick Dimiduk's contribution on this Stack Overflow thread for more information. If you know how to fix this without opening a second port for HTTPS, patches are appreciated."},"13605":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3805","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-cache-in-hbase-ui","content":"Set the following configuration in hbase-site to set max age to zero and disable cache for the web UI:"},"13606":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3806","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-spnego-for-kerberos-authentication-with-web-uis","content":"Kerberos-authentication to HBase Web UIs can be enabled via configuring SPNEGO with the `hbase.security.authentication.ui` property in *hbase-site.xml*. Enabling this authentication requires that HBase is also configured to use Kerberos authentication for RPCs (e.g `hbase.security.authentication` = `kerberos`)."},"13607":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3807","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-spnego-for-kerberos-authentication-with-web-uis","content":"A number of properties exist to configure SPNEGO authentication for the web server:"},"13608":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3808","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"In the previous section, we cover how to enable authentication for the Web UI via SPNEGO. However, some portions of the Web UI could be used to impact the availability and performance of an HBase cluster. As such, it is desirable to ensure that only those with proper authority can interact with these sensitive endpoints."},"13609":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3809","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"HBase allows the adminstrators to be defined via a list of usernames or groups in hbase-site.xml"},"13610":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3810","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"The usernames are those which the Kerberos identity maps to, given the Hadoop `auth_to_local` rules in core-site.xml. The groups here are the Unix groups associated with the mapped usernames."},"13611":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3811","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"Consider the following scenario to describe how the configuration properties operate. Consider three users which are defined in the Kerberos KDC:"},"13612":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3812","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`alice@COMPANY.COM`"},"13613":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3813","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`bob@COMPANY.COM`"},"13614":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3814","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`charlie@COMPANY.COM`"},"13615":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3815","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"The default Hadoop `auth_to_local` rules map these principals to the \"shortname\":"},"13616":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3816","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`alice`"},"13617":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3817","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`bob`"},"13618":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3818","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"`charlie`"},"13619":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3819","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"Unix groups membership define that `alice` is a member of the group `admins`. `bob` and `charlie` are not members of the `admins` group."},"13620":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3820","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"Given the above configuration, `alice` is allowed to access sensitive endpoints in the Web UI as she is a member of the `admins` group. `charlie` is also allowed to access sensitive endpoints because he is explicitly listed as an admin in the configuration. `bob` is not allowed to access sensitive endpoints because he is not a member of the `admins` group nor is listed as an explicit admin user via `hbase.security.authentication.spnego.admin.users`, but can still use any non-sensitive endpoints in the Web UI."},"13621":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3821","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-spnego","content":"If it doesn't go without saying: non-authenticated users cannot access any part of the Web UI."},"13622":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3822","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-ldap-authentication-with-web-uis","content":"LDAP authentication to HBase Web UIs can be enabled via configuring LDAP with the `hbase.security.authentication.ui` property in *hbase-site.xml*. The `hbase.http.filter.initializers` property also needs to have the `AuthenticationFilterInitializer` class."},"13623":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3823","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-ldap-authentication-with-web-uis","content":"**IMPORTANT:** A LDAP server must be configured and running. When TLS is enabled for communication with LDAP server (either via ldaps scheme or ‘start TLS' extension), configure the public certificate of the LDAP server in the local truststore. The LDAP authentication mechanism uses HTTP Basic authentication scheme to verify user specified credentials against a configured LDAP (or Active Directory) server. The authentication filter must be configured with the following init parameters:"},"13624":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3824","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-ldap-authentication-with-web-uis","content":"A number of properties exist to configure LDAP authentication for the web server:"},"13625":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3825","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-ldap","content":"In the previous section, we discussed enabling authentication for the Web UI via LDAP. Certain portions of the Web UI can impact the availability and performance of an HBase cluster. To safeguard these sensitive endpoints, it is essential to restrict access to authorized administrators only."},"13626":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3826","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-ldap","content":"HBase provides a mechanism to define administrators for the Web UI through a list of usernames in the `hbase-site.xml` configuration file."},"13627":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3827","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-ldap","content":"To specify the administrators, use the following property in `hbase-site.xml`:"},"13628":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3828","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#defining-administrators-of-the-web-ui-with-ldap","content":"The usernames listed in the above property should correspond to the LDAP usernames of the administrators."},"13629":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3829","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-web-ui-notes","content":"This feature is supported by only versions of HBase having HBASE-29244"},"13630":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3830","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-web-ui-notes","content":"Ensure that the LDAP server is properly configured and running. See the previous section for details."},"13631":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3831","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-web-ui-notes","content":"Only users explicitly listed in the `hbase.security.authentication.ldap.admin.users` property will have access to sensitive endpoints."},"13632":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3832","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-web-ui-notes","content":"Non-administrative users can still access non-sensitive endpoints, provided they are authenticated."},"13633":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3833","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-web-ui-notes","content":"By defining administrators in this way, you can ensure that only authorized personnel can interact with critical Web UI functionalities, thereby enhancing the security and stability of your HBase cluster."},"13634":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3834","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#other-ui-security-related-configuration","content":"While it is a clear anti-pattern for HBase developers, the developers acknowledge that the HBase configuration (including Hadoop configuration files) may contain sensitive information. As such, a user may find that they do not want to expose the HBase service-level configuration to all authenticated users. They may configure HBase to require a user must be an admin to access the service-level configuration via the HBase UI. This configuration is **false** by default (any authenticated user may access the configuration)."},"13635":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3835","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#other-ui-security-related-configuration","content":"Users who wish to change this would set the following in their hbase-site.xml:"},"13636":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3836","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#other-ui-security-related-configuration","content":"To disable showing stack traces in HBase UI for hiding sensitive information, set the following in hbase-site:"},"13637":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3837","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-access","content":"Newer releases of Apache HBase (>= 0.92) support optional SASL authentication of clients. See also Matteo Bertozzi's article on Understanding User Authentication and Authorization in Apache HBase."},"13638":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3838","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-access","content":"This describes how to set up Apache HBase and clients for connection to secure HBase resources."},"13639":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3839","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hadoop-authentication-configuration-toc","content":"To run HBase RPC with strong authentication, you must set `hbase.security.authentication` to `kerberos`. In this case, you must also set `hadoop.security.authentication` to `kerberos` in core-site.xml. Otherwise, you would be using strong authentication for HBase but not for the underlying HDFS, which would cancel out any benefit."},"13640":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3840","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#kerberos-kdc-toc","content":"You need to have a working Kerberos KDC."},"13641":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3841","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"First, refer to security.prerequisites and ensure that your underlying HDFS configuration is secure."},"13642":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3842","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every server machine in the cluster:"},"13643":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3843","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"A full shutdown and restart of HBase service is required when deploying these configuration changes."},"13644":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3844","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"First, refer to Prerequisites and ensure that your underlying HDFS configuration is secure."},"13645":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3845","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every client:"},"13646":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3846","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Before 2.2.0 version, the client environment must be logged in to Kerberos from KDC or keytab via the `kinit` command before communication with the HBase cluster will be possible."},"13647":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3847","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Since 2.2.0, client can specify the following configurations in `hbase-site.xml`:"},"13648":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3848","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Then application can automatically do the login and credential renewal jobs without client interference."},"13649":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3849","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"It's optional feature, client, who upgrades to 2.2.0, can still keep their login and credential renewal logic already did in older version, as long as keeping `hbase.client.keytab.file` and `hbase.client.keytab.principal` are unset."},"13650":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3850","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Be advised that if the `hbase.security.authentication` in the client- and server-side site files do not match, the client will not be able to communicate with the cluster."},"13651":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3851","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Once HBase is configured for secure RPC it is possible to optionally configure encrypted communication. To do so, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every client:"},"13652":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3852","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"This configuration property can also be set on a per-connection basis. Set it in the `Configuration` supplied to `Table`:"},"13653":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3853","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation","content":"Expect a \\~10% performance penalty for encrypted communication."},"13654":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3854","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every Thrift gateway:"},"13655":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3855","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"When `hbase.unsafe.thrift.hostname` is set to a non-empty value, the Thrift gateway uses the\nconfigured value instead of the hostname discovered through `hbase.thrift.dns.interface` and\n`hbase.thrift.dns.nameserver`. The configured hostname is used for Kerberos `_HOST`\nsubstitution and as the SASL server hostname. This can be useful when multiple Thrift gateways\nare exposed through a common DNS name, VIP, or layer-4 load balancer. Each gateway using the\ncommon hostname must have a keytab containing the corresponding service principal."},"13656":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3856","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"Substitute the appropriate credential and keytab for *\\$USER&#x2A; and *\\$KEYTAB* respectively."},"13657":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3857","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"In order to use the Thrift API principal to interact with HBase, it is also necessary to add the `hbase.thrift.kerberos.principal` to the `acl` table. For example, to give the Thrift API principal, `thrift_server`, administrative access, a command such as this one will suffice:"},"13658":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3858","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"For more information about ACLs, please see the Access Control Labels (ACLs) section"},"13659":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3859","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"The Thrift gateway will authenticate with HBase using the supplied credential. No authentication will be performed by the Thrift gateway itself. All client access via the Thrift gateway will use the Thrift gateway's credential and have its privilege."},"13660":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3860","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"Client-side Configuration for Secure Operation - Thrift Gateway describes how to authenticate a Thrift client to HBase using a fixed user. As an alternative, you can configure the Thrift gateway to authenticate to HBase on the client's behalf, and to access HBase using a proxy user. This was implemented in HBASE-11349 for Thrift 1, and HBASE-11474 for Thrift 2."},"13661":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3861","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"If you use framed transport, you cannot yet take advantage of this feature, because SASL does not\nwork with Thrift framed transport at this time."},"13662":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3862","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"To enable it, do the following."},"13663":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3863","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"Be sure Thrift is running in secure mode, by following the procedure described in Client-side Configuration for Secure Operation - Thrift Gateway."},"13664":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3864","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"Be sure that HBase is configured to allow proxy users, as described in REST Gateway Impersonation Configuration."},"13665":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3865","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"In *hbase-site.xml* for each cluster node running a Thrift gateway, set the property `hbase.thrift.security.qop` to one of the following three values:"},"13666":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3866","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"`privacy` - authentication, integrity, and confidentiality checking."},"13667":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3867","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"`integrity` - authentication and integrity checking"},"13668":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3868","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"`authentication` - authentication checking only"},"13669":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3869","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-authenticate-on-behalf-of-the-client","content":"Restart the Thrift gateway processes for the changes to take effect. If a node is running Thrift, the output of the `jps` command will list a `ThriftServer` process. To stop Thrift on a node, run the command `bin/hbase-daemon.sh stop thrift`. To start Thrift on a node, run the command `bin/hbase-daemon.sh start thrift`."},"13670":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3870","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-use-the-doas-feature","content":"Configure the Thrift Gateway to Authenticate on Behalf of the Client describes how to configure the Thrift gateway to authenticate to HBase on the client's behalf, and to access HBase using a proxy user. The limitation of this approach is that after the client is initialized with a particular set of credentials, it cannot change these credentials during the session. The `doAs` feature provides a flexible way to impersonate multiple principals using the same client. This feature was implemented in HBASE-12640 for Thrift 1, but is currently not available for Thrift 2."},"13671":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3871","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-use-the-doas-feature","content":"**To enable the `doAs` feature**, add the following to the *hbase-site.xml* file for every Thrift gateway:"},"13672":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3872","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-use-the-doas-feature","content":"**To allow proxy users** when using `doAs` impersonation, add the following to the *hbase-site.xml* file for every HBase node:"},"13673":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3873","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-the-thrift-gateway-to-use-the-doas-feature","content":"Take a look at the demo client to get an overall idea of how to use this feature in your client."},"13674":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3874","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every REST gateway:"},"13675":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3875","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"Substitute the appropriate credential and keytab for *\\$USER&#x2A; and *\\$KEYTAB* respectively."},"13676":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3876","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"The REST gateway will authenticate with HBase using the supplied credential."},"13677":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3877","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"In order to use the REST API principal to interact with HBase, it is also necessary to add the `hbase.rest.kerberos.principal` to the `acl` table. For example, to give the REST API principal, `rest_server`, administrative access, a command such as this one will suffice:"},"13678":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3878","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"For more information about ACLs, please see the Access Control Labels (ACLs) section"},"13679":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3879","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"HBase REST gateway supports SPNEGO HTTP authentication for client access to the gateway. To enable REST gateway Kerberos authentication for client access, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every REST gateway."},"13680":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3880","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"Substitute the keytab for HTTP for *\\$KEYTAB*."},"13681":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3881","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-secure-operation---rest-gateway","content":"HBase REST gateway supports different 'hbase.rest.authentication.type': simple, kerberos. You can also implement a custom authentication by implementing Hadoop AuthenticationHandler, then specify the full class name as 'hbase.rest.authentication.type' value. For more information, refer to SPNEGO HTTP authentication."},"13682":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3882","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"By default, the REST gateway doesn't support impersonation. It accesses the HBase on behalf of clients as the user configured as in the previous section. To the HBase server, all requests are from the REST gateway user. The actual users are unknown. You can turn on the impersonation support. With impersonation, the REST gateway user is a proxy user. The HBase server knows the actual/real user of each request. So it can apply proper authorizations."},"13683":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3883","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"To turn on REST gateway impersonation, we need to configure HBase servers (masters and region servers) to allow proxy users; configure REST gateway to enable impersonation."},"13684":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3884","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"To allow proxy users, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every HBase server:"},"13685":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3885","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"Substitute the REST gateway proxy user for *\\$USER&#x2A;, and the allowed group list for *\\$GROUPS*."},"13686":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3886","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"To enable REST gateway impersonation, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every REST gateway."},"13687":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3887","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest-gateway-impersonation-configuration","content":"Substitute the keytab for HTTP for *\\$KEYTAB*."},"13688":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3888","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-access","content":"Newer releases of Apache HBase (>= 0.92) support optional SASL authentication of clients. See also Matteo Bertozzi's article on Understanding User Authentication and Authorization in Apache HBase."},"13689":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3889","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-access","content":"This describes how to set up Apache HBase and clients for simple user access to HBase resources."},"13690":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3890","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#simple-versus-secure-access","content":"The following section shows how to set up simple user access. Simple user access is not a secure method of operating HBase. This method is used to prevent users from making mistakes. It can be used to mimic the Access Control using on a development system without having to set up Kerberos."},"13691":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3891","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#simple-versus-secure-access","content":"This method is not used to prevent malicious or hacking attempts. To make HBase secure against these types of attacks, you must configure HBase for secure operation. Refer to the section Secure Client Access to Apache HBase and complete all of the steps described there."},"13692":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3892","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-user-access-prerequisites","content":"None"},"13693":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3893","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every server machine in the cluster:"},"13694":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3894","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"For 0.94, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every server machine in the cluster:"},"13695":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3895","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"A full shutdown and restart of HBase service is required when deploying these configuration changes."},"13696":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3896","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"Add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every client:"},"13697":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3897","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"For 0.94, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file on every server machine in the cluster:"},"13698":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3898","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation","content":"Be advised that if the `hbase.security.authentication` in the client- and server-side site files do not match, the client will not be able to communicate with the cluster."},"13699":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3899","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"The Thrift gateway user will need access. For example, to give the Thrift API user, `thrift_server`, administrative access, a command such as this one will suffice:"},"13700":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3900","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"For more information about ACLs, please see the Access Control Labels (ACLs) section"},"13701":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3901","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---thrift-gateway","content":"The Thrift gateway will authenticate with HBase using the supplied credential. No authentication will be performed by the Thrift gateway itself. All client access via the Thrift gateway will use the Thrift gateway's credential and have its privilege."},"13702":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3902","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---rest-gateway","content":"The REST gateway will authenticate with HBase using the supplied credential. No authentication will be performed by the REST gateway itself. All client access via the REST gateway will use the REST gateway's credential and have its privilege."},"13703":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3903","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---rest-gateway","content":"The REST gateway user will need access. For example, to give the REST API user, `rest_server`, administrative access, a command such as this one will suffice:"},"13704":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3904","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---rest-gateway","content":"For more information about ACLs, please see the Access Control Labels (ACLs) section"},"13705":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3905","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration-for-simple-user-access-operation---rest-gateway","content":"It should be possible for clients to authenticate with the HBase cluster through the REST gateway in a pass-through manner via SPNEGO HTTP authentication. This is future work."},"13706":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3906","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"Since version `2.6.0` HBase supports TLS encryption in server-client and Master-RegionServer communication. Transport Layer Security (TLS) is a standard cryptographic protocol designed to provide communications security over a computer network. HBase TLS implementation works exactly how secure websites are accessed via **https** prefix in a web browser: once established all communication on the channel will be securely hidden from malicious access."},"13707":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3907","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"The encryption works at the transport level which means it's independent of the configured authentication method. Secure client access mentioned in the previous section requires Kerberos to be configured and used in HBase authentication, while TLS can be configured with any other SASL mechanism or even with simple client access methods, effectively preventing attackers from eavesdropping the communication. No Kerberos KDC or other complicated infrastructure required."},"13708":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3908","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"HBase TLS is based on the Netty library therefore it only works with Netty client and server RPC implementations. Netty's powerful SSL implementation is a great foundation for highly secure and performant communication providing the latest and greatest cryptographic solution at all times."},"13709":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3909","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"Since Region Servers effectively work as clients from Master's perspective, TLS supports encrypted communication between cluster members too."},"13710":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3910","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"From version 2.6.0 HBase supports the Hadoop CredentialProvider API to avoid storing sensitive information in HBase configuration files. The recommended way of storing keystore / truststore passwords is to use one of the supported credential providers e.g. the local jceks file provider. You can find more information about how to setup credential providers in the Hadoop documentation."},"13711":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3911","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls","content":"The CLI interface for accessing the Hadoop Credential Shell is also available in HBase CLI. Type `hbase credential` to get help."},"13712":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3912","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration","content":"We need to set up Java key store for the server. Key store is the list of private keys that a server can use to configure TLS encryption. See TLS wikipedia page for further details of the protocol. Add the following configuration to `hbase-site.xml` on Master, Region Servers and HBase clients:"},"13713":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3913","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration","content":"Use `hbase.rpc.tls.keystore.password` alias to retrieve key store password from Hadoop credential provider."},"13714":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3914","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-configuration","content":"The supported storefile formats are based on the registered security providers and the loader can\nbe autodetected from the file extension. If needed, the file format can be explicitly specified\nwith the `hbase.rpc.tls.keystore.type` property."},"13715":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3915","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"We need to configure trust store for the client. Trust store contains the list of certificates that the client should trust when doing the handshake with the server. Add the following to `hbase-site.xml`."},"13716":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3916","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"Use `hbase.rpc.tls.truststore.password` alias to retrieve trust store password from Hadoop credential provider."},"13717":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3917","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"The supported storefile formats are based on the registered security providers and the loader can\nbe autodetected from the file extension. If needed, the file format can be explicitly specified\nwith the `hbase.rpc.tls.truststore.type` property."},"13718":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3918","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"However, specifying a trust store is not always required. Standard JDK implementations are shipped with a standard list of trusted certificates (the certificates of Certificate Authorities) and if your private key is provided by one of them, you don't need to configure your clients to trust it. Similarly to an internet browser, you don't need to set up the certificates of every single website you're planning to visit. Later in this documentation we'll walk through the steps of creating self-signed certificates which requires a trust store setup."},"13719":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3919","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"You can check the list of public certificate authorities shipped with your JDK implementation:"},"13720":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3920","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-configuration","content":"Password is empty by default."},"13721":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3921","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"While obtaining globally trusted certificates from Certificate Authorities is convenient, it's perfectly valid to generate your own private/public keypairs and set them up specifically for the HBase cluster. Especially if you don't want to enable public access to the cluster, paying money for a certificate doesn't make sense."},"13722":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3922","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"Follow the following steps to generate self-signed certificates."},"13723":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3923","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"Create SSL key store JKS to store local credentials"},"13724":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3924","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"Please note that the alias (-alias) and the distinguished name (-dname) must match the hostname of the machine that is associated with, otherwise hostname verification won't work."},"13725":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3925","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"At the end of this operation you'll have as many key store files as many servers you have in your cluster. Each cluster member will have its own key store."},"13726":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3926","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"Extract the signed public key (certificate) from each key store"},"13727":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3927","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"Create SSL trust store JKS containing certificates for the clients"},"13728":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3928","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-self-signed-certificates","content":"The same truststore (storing all accepted certs) should be shared on participants of the cluster. You need to use different aliases to store multiple certificates in the same truststore. Name of the aliases doesn't matter."},"13729":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3929","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Here are the steps needed to upgrade an already running HBase cluster to TLS without downtime by taking advantage of port unification functionality. There's a property on server side called `hbase.server.netty.tls.supportplaintext` which makes possible to accept TLS and plaintext connections on the same socket port."},"13730":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3930","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Create the necessary key stores and trust stores for all server participants as described in the previous section."},"13731":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3931","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Enable secure communication on the Master node in *server-only* mode with plaintext support"},"13732":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3932","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Restart the Master. Master now accepts both TLS/non-TLS connections and works with non-TLS in client mode."},"13733":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3933","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Enable secure communication on the Region Servers in both *server and client* mode with plaintext support"},"13734":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3934","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Client mode here will ensure that RegionServer's communication to Master is encrypted."},"13735":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3935","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"**Replication**"},"13736":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3936","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"If you have read replicas enabled in your cluster or replication between two different clusters, you have to break this into two steps. Secure communication has to be enabled on the *server side* first with plaintext support and once all Region Servers are upgraded you can repeat the upgrade by enabling *client side* as well."},"13737":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3937","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"You have to prepare all Region Servers for secure communication before upgrading the client side."},"13738":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3938","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Restart Region Servers in rolling restart fashion. They send requests with TLS and accept both TLS and non-TLS communication."},"13739":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3939","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Enable secure communication on the clients"},"13740":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3940","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Enable client-mode TLS on master and disable plaintext mode"},"13741":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3941","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Restart Master."},"13742":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3942","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Disable plaintext communication on the Region Servers"},"13743":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3943","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Disable plaintext communication on the Region Servers by removing `supportplaintext` property. Restart RSs in rolling restart fashion."},"13744":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3944","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-existing-non-tls-cluster-with-no-downtime","content":"Once `hbase.client.netty.tls.enabled` is enabled on the server side, the cluster will only be able\nto communicate with other clusters which have TLS enabled. For example, this would impact\ninter-cluster replication."},"13745":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3945","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-automatic-certificate-reloading","content":"Certificates usually expire after some time to improve security. In this case we need to replace them by modifying Keystore / Truststore files and HBase processes have to be restarted. In order to avoid that you can enable automatic file change detection and certificate reloading with the following option. Default: false."},"13746":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3946","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabled-protocols","content":"Comma-separated list of TLS protocol versions to enable. Default is empty."},"13747":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3947","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#default-protocol","content":"Set the default TLS protocol version to use. Default is TLSv1.2. Use this protocol if enabled protocols is not defined."},"13748":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3948","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabled-cipher-suites","content":"List of enabled cipher suites in TLS protocol. Useful when you want to disable certain cipher suites due to recent security concerns. Default value is a mix of CBC and GCM ciphers. Due to performance reasons we prefer CBC ciphers for Java 8 and GCM ciphers for Java 9+."},"13749":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3949","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#certificate-revocation-checking","content":"There's a built-in mechanism in JDK's TrustManager which automatically checks certificates for revocation. See Managing Server Certificates. Disabled by default."},"13750":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3950","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#online-certificate-status-protocol","content":"Enables OCSP stapling. Please note that not all `SSLProvider` implementations support OCSP stapling and an exception will be thrown upon. Disabled by default."},"13751":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3951","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-handshake-timeout","content":"Set the TLS client handshake timeout is milliseconds. Default is 5 seconds."},"13752":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3952","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#securing-zookeeper-data","content":"ZooKeeper has a pluggable authentication mechanism to enable access from clients using different methods. ZooKeeper even allows authenticated and un-authenticated clients at the same time. The access to znodes can be restricted by providing Access Control Lists (ACLs) per znode. An ACL contains two components, the authentication method and the principal. ACLs are NOT enforced hierarchically. See ZooKeeper Programmers Guide for details."},"13753":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3953","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#securing-zookeeper-data","content":"HBase daemons authenticate to ZooKeeper via SASL and kerberos (See ZooKeeper). HBase sets up the znode ACLs so that only the HBase user and the configured hbase superuser (`hbase.superuser`) can access and modify the data. In cases where ZooKeeper is used for service discovery or sharing state with the client, the znodes created by HBase will also allow anyone (regardless of authentication) to read these znodes (clusterId, master address, meta location, etc), but only the HBase user can modify them."},"13754":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3954","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#securing-file-system-hdfs-data","content":"All of the data under management is kept under the root directory in the file system (`hbase.rootdir`). Access to the data and WAL files in the filesystem should be restricted so that users cannot bypass the HBase layer, and peek at the underlying data files from the file system. HBase assumes the filesystem used (HDFS or other) enforces permissions hierarchically. If sufficient protection from the file system (both authorization and authentication) is not provided, HBase level authorization control (ACLs, visibility labels, etc) is meaningless since the user can always access the data from the file system."},"13755":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3955","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#securing-file-system-hdfs-data","content":"HBase enforces the posix-like permissions 700 (`rwx------`) to its root directory. It means that only the HBase user can read or write the files in FS. The default setting can be changed by configuring `hbase.rootdir.perms` in hbase-site.xml. A restart of the active master is needed so that it changes the used permissions. For versions before 1.2.0, you can check whether HBASE-13780 is committed, and if not, you can manually set the permissions for the root directory if needed. Using HDFS, the command would be:"},"13756":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3956","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#securing-file-system-hdfs-data","content":"You should change `/hbase` if you are using a different `hbase.rootdir`."},"13757":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3957","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"After you have configured secure authentication between HBase client and server processes and gateways, you need to consider the security of your data itself. HBase provides several strategies for securing your data:"},"13758":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3958","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"Role-based Access Control (RBAC) controls which users or groups can read and write to a given HBase resource or execute a coprocessor endpoint, using the familiar paradigm of roles."},"13759":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3959","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"Visibility Labels which allow you to label cells and control access to labelled cells, to further restrict who can read or write to certain subsets of your data. Visibility labels are stored as tags. See hbase.tags for more information."},"13760":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3960","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"Transparent encryption of data at rest on the underlying filesystem, both in HFiles and in the WAL. This protects your data at rest from an attacker who has access to the underlying filesystem, without the need to change the implementation of the client. It can also protect against data leakage from improperly disposed disks, which can be important for legal and regulatory compliance."},"13761":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3961","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"Server-side configuration, administration, and implementation details of each of these features are discussed below, along with any performance trade-offs. An example security configuration is given at the end, to show these features all used together, as they might be in a real-world scenario."},"13762":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3962","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"All aspects of security in HBase are in active development and evolving rapidly. Any strategy you\nemploy for security of your data should be thoroughly tested. In addition, some of these features\nare still in the experimental stage of development. To take advantage of many of these features,\nyou must be running HBase 0.98+ and using the HFile v3 file format."},"13763":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3963","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access","content":"Several procedures in this section require you to copy files between cluster nodes. When copying\nkeys, configuration files, or other files containing sensitive strings, use a secure method, such\nas `ssh`, to avoid leaking sensitive data."},"13764":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3964","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-basic-server-side-configuration","content":"Enable HFile v3, by setting `hfile.format.version` to 3 in *hbase-site.xml*. This is the default for HBase 1.0 and newer."},"13765":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3965","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-basic-server-side-configuration","content":"Enable SASL and Kerberos authentication for RPC and ZooKeeper, as described in security.prerequisites and ZooKeeper."},"13766":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3966","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tags","content":"*Tags* are a feature of HFile v3. A tag is a piece of metadata which is part of a cell, separate from the key, value, and version. Tags are an implementation detail which provides a foundation for other security-related features such as cell-level ACLs and visibility labels. Tags are stored in the HFiles themselves. It is possible that in the future, tags will be used to implement other HBase features. You don't need to know a lot about tags in order to use the security features they enable."},"13767":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3967","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details","content":"Every cell can have zero or more tags. Every tag has a type and the actual tag byte array."},"13768":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3968","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details","content":"Just as row keys, column families, qualifiers and values can be encoded (see data.block.encoding.types), tags can also be encoded as well. You can enable or disable tag encoding at the level of the column family, and it is enabled by default. Use the `HColumnDescriptor#setCompressionTags(boolean compressTags)` method to manage encoding settings on a column family. You also need to enable the DataBlockEncoder for the column family, for encoding of tags to take effect."},"13769":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3969","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details","content":"You can enable compression of each tag in the WAL, if WAL compression is also enabled, by setting the value of `hbase.regionserver.wal.tags.enablecompression` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml*. Tag compression uses dictionary encoding."},"13770":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3970","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details","content":"Coprocessors that run server-side on RegionServers can perform get and set operations on cell Tags. Tags are stripped out at the RPC layer before the read response is sent back, so clients do not see these tags. Tag compression is not supported when using WAL encryption."},"13771":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3971","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works","content":"ACLs in HBase are based upon a user's membership in or exclusion from groups, and a given group's permissions to access a given resource. ACLs are implemented as a coprocessor called AccessController."},"13772":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3972","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works","content":"HBase does not maintain a private group mapping, but relies on a &#x2A;**Hadoop group mapper***, which maps between entities in a directory such as LDAP or Active Directory, and HBase users. Any supported Hadoop group mapper will work. Users are then granted specific permissions (Read, Write, Execute, Create, Admin) against resources (global, namespaces, tables, cells, or endpoints)."},"13773":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3973","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works","content":"With Kerberos and Access Control enabled, client access to HBase is authenticated and user data is\nprivate unless access has been explicitly granted."},"13774":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3974","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works","content":"HBase has a simpler security model than relational databases, especially in terms of client operations. No distinction is made between an insert (new record) and update (of existing record), for example, as both collapse down into a Put."},"13775":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3975","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"HBase access levels are granted independently of each other and allow for different types of operations at a given scope."},"13776":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3976","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Read (R)* - can read data at the given scope"},"13777":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3977","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Write (W)* - can write data at the given scope"},"13778":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Execute (X)* - can execute coprocessor endpoints at the given scope"},"13779":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Create (C)* - can create tables or drop tables (even those they did not create) at the given scope"},"13780":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Admin (A)* - can perform cluster operations such as balancing the cluster or assigning regions at the given scope"},"13781":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"The possible scopes are:"},"13782":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Superuser* - superusers can perform any operation available in HBase, to any resource. The user who runs HBase on your cluster is a superuser, as are any principals assigned to the configuration property `hbase.superuser` in *hbase-site.xml* on the HMaster."},"13783":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Global* - permissions granted at *global* scope allow the admin to operate on all tables of the cluster."},"13784":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Namespace* - permissions granted at *namespace* scope apply to all tables within a given namespace."},"13785":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Table* - permissions granted at *table* scope apply to data or metadata within a given table."},"13786":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*ColumnFamily* - permissions granted at *ColumnFamily* scope apply to cells within that ColumnFamily."},"13787":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Cell* - permissions granted at *cell* scope apply to that exact cell coordinate (key, value, timestamp). This allows for policy evolution along with data.\nTo change an ACL on a specific cell, write an updated cell with new ACL to the precise coordinates of the original.\\\nIf you have a multi-versioned schema and want to update ACLs on all visible versions, you need to write new cells for all visible versions. The application has complete control over policy evolution.\\\nThe exception to the above rule is `append` and `increment` processing. Appends and increments can carry an ACL in the operation. If one is included in the operation, then it will be applied to the result of the `append` or `increment`. Otherwise, the ACL of the existing cell you are appending to or incrementing is preserved."},"13788":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"The combination of access levels and scopes creates a matrix of possible access levels that can be granted to a user. In a production environment, it is useful to think of access levels in terms of what is needed to do a specific job. The following list describes appropriate access levels for some common types of HBase users. It is important not to grant more access than is required for a given user to perform their required tasks."},"13789":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Superusers* - In a production system, only the HBase user should have superuser access. In a development environment, an administrator may need superuser access in order to quickly control and manage the cluster. However, this type of administrator should usually be a Global Admin rather than a superuser."},"13790":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Global Admins* - A global admin can perform tasks and access every table in HBase. In a typical production environment, an admin should not have Read or Write permissions to data within tables."},"13791":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"A global admin with Admin permissions can perform cluster-wide operations on the cluster, such as balancing, assigning or unassigning regions, or calling an explicit major compaction. This is an operations role."},"13792":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"A global admin with Create permissions can create or drop any table within HBase. This is more of a DBA-type role.\nIn a production environment, it is likely that different users will have only one of Admin and Create permissions."},"13793":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3993","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"In the current implementation, a Global Admin with `Admin` permission can grant himself `Read`\nand `Write` permissions on a table and gain access to that table's data. For this reason, only\ngrant `Global Admin` permissions to trusted user who actually need them. Also be aware that a\n`Global Admin` with `Create` permission can perform a `Put` operation on the ACL table,\nsimulating a `grant` or `revoke` and circumventing the authorization check for `Global Admin`\npermissions. Due to these issues, be cautious with granting `Global Admin` privileges."},"13794":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3994","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Namespace Admins* - a namespace admin with `Create` permissions can create or drop tables within that namespace, and take and restore snapshots. A namespace admin with `Admin` permissions can perform operations such as splits or major compactions on tables within that namespace."},"13795":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3995","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Table Admins* - A table admin can perform administrative operations only on that table. A table admin with `Create` permissions can create snapshots from that table or restore that table from a snapshot. A table admin with `Admin` permissions can perform operations such as splits or major compactions on that table."},"13796":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3996","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#understanding-access-levels","content":"*Users* - Users can read or write data, or both. Users can also execute coprocessor endpoints, if given `Executable` permissions."},"13797":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3997","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Job Title"},"13798":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3998","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Scope"},"13799":{"id":"/docs/single-page-3999","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Permissions"},"13800":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4000","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Description"},"13801":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4001","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Senior Administrator"},"13802":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4002","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Global"},"13803":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4003","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Access, Create"},"13804":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4004","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Manages the cluster and gives access to Junior Administrators."},"13805":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4005","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Junior Administrator"},"13806":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4006","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Global"},"13807":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4007","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Create"},"13808":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4008","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Creates tables and gives access to Table Administrators."},"13809":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4009","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Table Administrator"},"13810":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4010","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Table"},"13811":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4011","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Access"},"13812":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4012","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Maintains a table from an operations point of view."},"13813":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4013","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Data Analyst"},"13814":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4014","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Table"},"13815":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4015","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Read"},"13816":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4016","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Creates reports from HBase data."},"13817":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4017","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Web Application"},"13818":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4018","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Table"},"13819":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4019","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Read, Write"},"13820":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4020","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#real-world-example-of-access-levels-toc","content":"Puts data into HBase and uses HBase data to perform operations."},"13821":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4021","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#acl-matrix-toc","content":"For more details on how ACLs map to specific HBase operations and tasks, see appendix acl matrix."},"13822":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4022","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details-1","content":"Cell-level ACLs are implemented using tags (see Tags). In order to use cell-level ACLs, you must be using HFile v3 and HBase 0.98 or newer."},"13823":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4023","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details-1","content":"Files created by HBase are owned by the operating system user running the HBase process. To interact with HBase files, you should use the API or bulk load facility."},"13824":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4024","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-details-1","content":"HBase does not model \"roles\" internally in HBase. Instead, group names can be granted permissions. This allows external modeling of roles via group membership. Groups are created and manipulated externally to HBase, via the Hadoop group mapping service."},"13825":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4025","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"As a prerequisite, perform the steps in Procedure: Basic Server-Side Configuration."},"13826":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4026","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"Install and configure the AccessController coprocessor, by setting the following properties in *hbase-site.xml*. These properties take a list of classes."},"13827":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4027","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"If you use the AccessController along with the VisibilityController, the AccessController must\ncome first in the list, because with both components active, the VisibilityController will\ndelegate access control on its system tables to the AccessController. For an example of using both\ntogether, see Security Configuration Example."},"13828":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4028","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"Optionally, you can enable transport security, by setting `hbase.rpc.protection` to\n`privacy`. This requires HBase 0.98.4 or newer."},"13829":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4029","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"Set up the Hadoop group mapper in the Hadoop namenode's *core-site.xml*. This is a Hadoop file, not an HBase file. Customize it to your site's needs. Following is an example."},"13830":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4030","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"Optionally, enable the early-out evaluation strategy. Prior to HBase 0.98.0, if a user was not granted access to a column family, or at least a column qualifier, an AccessDeniedException would be thrown. HBase 0.98.0 removed this exception in order to allow cell-level exceptional grants. To restore the old behavior in HBase 0.98.0-0.98.6, set `hbase.security.access.early_out` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml*. In HBase 0.98.6, the default has been returned to `true`."},"13831":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4031","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"Distribute your configuration and restart your cluster for changes to take effect."},"13832":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4032","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-acl-server-side-configuration","content":"To test your configuration, log into HBase Shell as a given user and use the `whoami` command to report the groups your user is part of. In this example, the user is reported as being a member of the `services` group."},"13833":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4033","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Administration tasks can be performed from HBase Shell or via an API."},"13834":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4034","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Many of the API examples below are taken from source files *hbase-server/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/security/access/TestAccessController.java* and *hbase-server/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/security/access/SecureTestUtil.java*."},"13835":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4035","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Neither the examples, nor the source files they are taken from, are part of the public HBase API, and are provided for illustration only. Refer to the official API for usage instructions."},"13836":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4036","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"As a prerequisite, perform the steps in Procedure: Basic Server-Side Configuration.."},"13837":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4037","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Install and configure the AccessController coprocessor, by setting the following properties in *hbase-site.xml*. These properties take a list of classes."},"13838":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4038","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"If you use the AccessController along with the VisibilityController, the AccessController must\ncome first in the list, because with both components active, the VisibilityController will\ndelegate access control on its system tables to the AccessController. For an example of using both\ntogether, see Security Configuration Example."},"13839":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4039","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Optionally, you can enable transport security, by setting `hbase.rpc.protection` to\n`privacy`. This requires HBase 0.98.4 or newer."},"13840":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4040","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Set up the Hadoop group mapper in the Hadoop namenode's *core-site.xml*. This is a Hadoop file, not an HBase file. Customize it to your site's needs. Following is an example."},"13841":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4041","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Optionally, enable the early-out evaluation strategy. Prior to HBase 0.98.0, if a user was not granted access to a column family, or at least a column qualifier, an AccessDeniedException would be thrown. HBase 0.98.0 removed this exception in order to allow cell-level exceptional grants. To restore the old behavior in HBase 0.98.0-0.98.6, set `hbase.security.access.early_out` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml*. In HBase 0.98.6, the default has been returned to `true`."},"13842":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4042","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"Distribute your configuration and restart your cluster for changes to take effect."},"13843":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4043","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-shell","content":"To test your configuration, log into HBase Shell as a given user and use the `whoami` command to report the groups your user is part of. In this example, the user is reported as being a member of the `services` group."},"13844":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4044","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"Visibility labels control can be used to only permit users or principals associated with a given label to read or access cells with that label. For instance, you might label a cell `top-secret`, and only grant access to that label to the `managers` group. Visibility labels are implemented using Tags, which are a feature of HFile v3, and allow you to store metadata on a per-cell basis. A label is a string, and labels can be combined into expressions by using logical operators (&, |, or !), and using parentheses for grouping. HBase does not do any kind of validation of expressions beyond basic well-formedness. Visibility labels have no meaning on their own, and may be used to denote sensitivity level, privilege level, or any other arbitrary semantic meaning."},"13845":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4045","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"If a user's labels do not match a cell's label or expression, the user is denied access to the cell."},"13846":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4046","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"In HBase 0.98.6 and newer, UTF-8 encoding is supported for visibility labels and expressions. When creating labels using the `addLabels(conf, labels)` method provided by the `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.security.visibility.VisibilityClient` class and passing labels in Authorizations via Scan or Get, labels can contain UTF-8 characters, as well as the logical operators normally used in visibility labels, with normal Java notations, without needing any escaping method. However, when you pass a CellVisibility expression via a Mutation, you must enclose the expression with the `CellVisibility.quote()` method if you use UTF-8 characters or logical operators. See `TestExpressionParser` and the source file *hbase-client/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/client/TestScan.java*."},"13847":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4047","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"A user adds visibility expressions to a cell during a Put operation. In the default configuration, the user does not need to have access to a label in order to label cells with it. This behavior is controlled by the configuration option `hbase.security.visibility.mutations.checkauths`. If you set this option to `true`, the labels the user is modifying as part of the mutation must be associated with the user, or the mutation will fail. Whether a user is authorized to read a labelled cell is determined during a Get or Scan, and results which the user is not allowed to read are filtered out. This incurs the same I/O penalty as if the results were returned, but reduces load on the network."},"13848":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4048","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"Visibility labels can also be specified during Delete operations. For details about visibility labels and Deletes, see HBASE-10885."},"13849":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4049","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"The user's effective label set is built in the RPC context when a request is first received by the RegionServer. The way that users are associated with labels is pluggable. The default plugin passes through labels specified in Authorizations added to the Get or Scan and checks those against the calling user's authenticated labels list. When the client passes labels for which the user is not authenticated, the default plugin drops them. You can pass a subset of user authenticated labels via the `Get#setAuthorizations(Authorizations(String,...))` and `Scan#setAuthorizations(Authorizations(String,...));` methods."},"13850":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4050","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"Groups can be granted visibility labels the same way as users. Groups are prefixed with an @ symbol. When checking visibility labels of a user, the server will include the visibility labels of the groups of which the user is a member, together with the user's own labels. When the visibility labels are retrieved using API `VisibilityClient#getAuths` or Shell command `get_auths` for a user, we will return labels added specifically for that user alone, not the group level labels."},"13851":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4051","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"Visibility label access checking is performed by the VisibilityController coprocessor. You can use interface `VisibilityLabelService` to provide a custom implementation and/or control the way that visibility labels are stored with cells. See the source file *hbase-server/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/security/visibility/TestVisibilityLabelsWithCustomVisLabService.java* for one example."},"13852":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4052","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"Visibility labels can be used in conjunction with ACLs."},"13853":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4053","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"The labels have to be explicitly defined before they can be used in visibility labels. See below\nfor an example of how this can be done."},"13854":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4054","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#visibility-labels","content":"There is currently no way to determine which labels have been applied to a cell. See\nHBASE-12470 for details."},"13855":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4055","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"Expression"},"13856":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4056","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"Interpretation"},"13857":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4057","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"`fulltime`"},"13858":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4058","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"Allow access to users associated with the fulltime label."},"13859":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4059","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"`!public`"},"13860":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4060","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"Allow access to users not associated with the public label."},"13861":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4061","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"`( secret \\| topsecret ) & !probationary`"},"13862":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4062","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#examples-of-visibility-expressions","content":"Allow access to users associated with either the secret or topsecret label and not associated with the probationary label."},"13863":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4063","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"As a prerequisite, perform the steps in Procedure: Basic Server-Side Configuration.."},"13864":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4064","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"Install and configure the VisibilityController coprocessor by setting the following properties in *hbase-site.xml*. These properties take a list of class names."},"13865":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4065","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"If you use the AccessController and VisibilityController coprocessors together, the\nAccessController must come first in the list, because with both components active, the\nVisibilityController will delegate access control on its system tables to the AccessController."},"13866":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4066","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"Adjust Configuration"},"13867":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4067","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"By default, users can label cells with any label, including labels they are not associated with, which means that a user can Put data that he cannot read. For example, a user could label a cell with the (hypothetical) 'topsecret' label even if the user is not associated with that label. If you only want users to be able to label cells with labels they are associated with, set `hbase.security.visibility.mutations.checkauths` to `true`. In that case, the mutation will fail if it makes use of labels the user is not associated with."},"13868":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4068","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-visibility-labels-server-side-configuration","content":"Distribute your configuration and restart your cluster for changes to take effect."},"13869":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4069","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Administration tasks can be performed using the HBase Shell or the Java API. For defining the list of visibility labels and associating labels with users, the HBase Shell is probably simpler."},"13870":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4070","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Many of the Java API examples in this section are taken from the source file *hbase-server/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/security/visibility/TestVisibilityLabels.java*. Refer to that file or the API documentation for more context."},"13871":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4071","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Neither these examples, nor the source file they were taken from, are part of the public HBase API, and are provided for illustration only. Refer to the official API for usage instructions."},"13872":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4072","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Define the List of Visibility Labels"},"13873":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4073","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**HBase Shell:**"},"13874":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4074","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**Java API:**"},"13875":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4075","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Associate Labels with Users"},"13876":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4076","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**HBase Shell:**"},"13877":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4077","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**Java API:**"},"13878":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4078","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Clear Labels From Users"},"13879":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4079","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**HBase Shell:**"},"13880":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4080","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**Java API:**"},"13881":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4081","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"Apply a Label or Expression to a Cell"},"13882":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4082","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"The label is only applied when data is written. The label is associated with a given version of the cell."},"13883":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4083","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**HBase Shell:**"},"13884":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4084","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"HBase Shell support for applying labels or permissions to cells is for testing and verification\nsupport, and should not be employed for production use because it won't apply the labels to cells\nthat don't exist yet. The correct way to apply cell level labels is to do so in the application\ncode when storing the values."},"13885":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4085","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-visibility","content":"**Java API example:**"},"13886":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4086","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reading-cells-with-labels","content":"When you issue a Scan or Get, HBase uses your default set of authorizations to filter out cells that you do not have access to. A superuser can set the default set of authorizations for a given user by using the `set_auths` HBase Shell command or the VisibilityClient.setAuths() method."},"13887":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4087","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#reading-cells-with-labels","content":"You can specify a different authorization during the Scan or Get, by passing the AUTHORIZATIONS option in HBase Shell, or the Scan.setAuthorizations() method if you use the API. This authorization will be combined with your default set as an additional filter. It will further filter your results, rather than giving you additional authorization."},"13888":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4088","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementing-your-own-visibility-label-algorithm","content":"Interpreting the labels authenticated for a given get/scan request is a pluggable algorithm."},"13889":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4089","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementing-your-own-visibility-label-algorithm","content":"You can specify a custom plugin or plugins by using the property `hbase.regionserver.scan.visibility.label.generator.class`. The output for the first `ScanLabelGenerator` will be the input for the next one, until the end of the list."},"13890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4090","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementing-your-own-visibility-label-algorithm","content":"The default implementation, which was implemented in HBASE-12466, loads two plugins, `FeedUserAuthScanLabelGenerator` and `DefinedSetFilterScanLabelGenerator`. See Reading Cells with Labels."},"13891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4091","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#replicating-visibility-tags-as-strings","content":"As mentioned in the above sections, the interface `VisibilityLabelService` could be used to implement a different way of storing the visibility expressions in the cells. Clusters with replication enabled also must replicate the visibility expressions to the peer cluster. If `DefaultVisibilityLabelServiceImpl` is used as the implementation for `VisibilityLabelService`, all the visibility expression are converted to the corresponding expression based on the ordinals for each visibility label stored in the labels table. During replication, visible cells are also replicated with the ordinal-based expression intact. The peer cluster may not have the same `labels` table with the same ordinal mapping for the visibility labels. In that case, replicating the ordinals makes no sense. It would be better if the replication occurred with the visibility expressions transmitted as strings. To replicate the visibility expression as strings to the peer cluster, create a `RegionServerObserver` configuration which works based on the implementation of the `VisibilityLabelService` interface. The configuration below enables replication of visibility expressions to peer clusters as strings. See HBASE-11639 for more details."},"13892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4092","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#transparent-encryption-of-data-at-rest","content":"HBase provides a mechanism for protecting your data at rest, in HFiles and the WAL, which reside within HDFS or another distributed filesystem. A two-tier architecture is used for flexible and non-intrusive key rotation. \"Transparent\" means that no implementation changes are needed on the client side. When data is written, it is encrypted. When it is read, it is decrypted on demand."},"13893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4093","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works-1","content":"The administrator provisions a master key for the cluster, which is stored in a key provider accessible to every trusted HBase process, including the HMaster, RegionServers, and clients (such as HBase Shell) on administrative workstations. The default key provider is integrated with the Java KeyStore API and any key management systems with support for it. Other custom key provider implementations are possible. The key retrieval mechanism is configured in the *hbase-site.xml* configuration file. The master key may be stored on the cluster servers, protected by a secure KeyStore file, or on an external keyserver, or in a hardware security module. This master key is resolved as needed by HBase processes through the configured key provider."},"13894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4094","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works-1","content":"Next, encryption use can be specified in the schema, per column family, by creating or modifying a column descriptor to include two additional attributes: the name of the encryption algorithm to use (currently only \"AES\" is supported), and optionally, a data key wrapped (encrypted) with the cluster master key. If a data key is not explicitly configured for a ColumnFamily, HBase will create a random data key per HFile. This provides an incremental improvement in security over the alternative. Unless you need to supply an explicit data key, such as in a case where you are generating encrypted HFiles for bulk import with a given data key, only specify the encryption algorithm in the ColumnFamily schema metadata and let HBase create data keys on demand. Per Column Family keys facilitate low impact incremental key rotation and reduce the scope of any external leak of key material. The wrapped data key is stored in the ColumnFamily schema metadata, and in each HFile for the Column Family, encrypted with the cluster master key. After the Column Family is configured for encryption, any new HFiles will be written encrypted. To ensure encryption of all HFiles, trigger a major compaction after enabling this feature."},"13895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4095","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works-1","content":"When the HFile is opened, the data key is extracted from the HFile, decrypted with the cluster master key, and used for decryption of the remainder of the HFile. The HFile will be unreadable if the master key is not available. If a remote user somehow acquires access to the HFile data because of some lapse in HDFS permissions, or from inappropriately discarded media, it will not be possible to decrypt either the data key or the file data."},"13896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4096","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-it-works-1","content":"It is also possible to encrypt the WAL. Even though WALs are transient, it is necessary to encrypt the WALEdits to avoid circumventing HFile protections for encrypted column families, in the event that the underlying filesystem is compromised. When WAL encryption is enabled, all WALs are encrypted, regardless of whether the relevant HFiles are encrypted."},"13897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4097","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-or-disable-the-feature","content":"The \"Transparent Encryption of Data At Rest\" feature is enabled by default, meaning the users can define tables with column families where the HFiles and WAL files will be encrypted by HBase, assuming the feature is properly configured (see Server-Side Configuration)."},"13898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4098","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-or-disable-the-feature","content":"In some cases (e.g. due to custom security policies), the operator of the HBase cluster might wish to only rely on an encryption at rest mechanism outside of HBase (e.g. those offered by HDFS) and wants to ensure that HBase's encryption at rest system is inactive. Since HBASE-25181 it is possible to explicitly disable HBase's own encryption by setting `hbase.crypto.enabled` to `false`. This configuration is `true` by default. If it is set to `false`, the users won't be able to create any table (column family) with HFile and WAL file encryption and the related create table shell (or API) commands will fail if they try."},"13899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4099","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"This procedure assumes you are using the default Java keystore implementation. If you are using a custom implementation, check its documentation and adjust accordingly."},"13900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4100","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Create a secret key of appropriate length for AES encryption, using the `keytool` utility."},"13901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4101","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Replace \\*\\*\\*\\* with the password for the keystore file and \\<alias> with the username of the HBase service account, or an arbitrary string. If you use an arbitrary string, you will need to configure HBase to use it, and that is covered below. Specify a keysize that is appropriate. Do not specify a separate password for the key, but press `Return` when prompted."},"13902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4102","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Set appropriate permissions on the keyfile and distribute it to all the HBase servers."},"13903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4103","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"The previous command created a file called *hbase.jks* in the HBase &#x2A;conf/* directory. Set the permissions and ownership on this file such that only the HBase service account user can read the file, and securely distribute the key to all HBase servers."},"13904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4104","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Configure the HBase daemons."},"13905":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4105","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Set the following properties in *hbase-site.xml* on the region servers, to configure HBase daemons to use a key provider backed by the KeyStore file or retrieving the cluster master key. In the example below, replace \\*\\*\\*\\* with the password."},"13906":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4106","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"By default, the HBase service account name will be used to resolve the cluster master key. However, you can store it with an arbitrary alias (in the `keytool` command). In that case, set the following property to the alias you used."},"13907":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4107","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"You also need to be sure your HFiles use HFile v3, in order to use transparent encryption. This is the default configuration for HBase 1.0 onward. For previous versions, set the following property in your *hbase-site.xml* file."},"13908":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4108","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Optionally, you can use a different cipher provider, either a Java Cryptography Encryption (JCE) algorithm provider or a custom HBase cipher implementation."},"13909":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4109","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"JCE:"},"13910":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4110","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Install a signed JCE provider (supporting `AES/CTR/NoPadding` mode with 128 bit keys)"},"13911":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4111","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Add it with highest preference to the JCE site configuration file *\\$JAVA\\_HOME/lib/security/java.security*."},"13912":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4112","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Update `hbase.crypto.algorithm.aes.provider` and `hbase.crypto.algorithm.rng.provider` options in *hbase-site.xml*."},"13913":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4113","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Custom HBase Cipher:"},"13914":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4114","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Implement `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.io.crypto.CipherProvider`."},"13915":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4115","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Add the implementation to the server classpath."},"13916":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4116","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Update `hbase.crypto.cipherprovider` in *hbase-site.xml*."},"13917":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4117","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Configure WAL encryption."},"13918":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4118","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Configure WAL encryption in every RegionServer's *hbase-site.xml*, by setting the following properties. You can include these in the HMaster's *hbase-site.xml* as well, but the HMaster does not have a WAL and will not use them."},"13919":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4119","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Starting from 2.6.0, the hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and\nhbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configurations are removed, you do not need to specify them\nany more. Just set hbase.regionserver.wal.encryption to true is enough to enable WAL encryption."},"13920":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4120","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"(Optional) Configure encryption key hash algorithm."},"13921":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4121","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Since HBASE-25181 it is possible to use custom encryption key hash algorithm instead of the default MD5 algorithm. This hash is needed to verify the secret key during decryption. The MD5 algorithm is considered weak, and can not be used in some (e.g. FIPS compliant) clusters."},"13922":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4122","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"The hash is set via the configuration option `hbase.crypto.key.hash.algorithm`. It should be set to a JDK `MessageDigest` algorithm like \"MD5\", \"SHA-384\" or \"SHA-512\". The default is \"MD5\" for backward compatibility. An example of this configuration parameter on a FIPS-compliant cluster:"},"13923":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4123","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Configure permissions on the *hbase-site.xml* file."},"13924":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4124","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Because the keystore password is stored in the hbase-site.xml, you need to ensure that only the HBase user can read the *hbase-site.xml* file, using file ownership and permissions."},"13925":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4125","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Restart your cluster."},"13926":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4126","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-data-access-transparent-encryption-of-data-at-test-server-side-configuration","content":"Distribute the new configuration file to all nodes and restart your cluster."},"13927":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4127","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-encryption","content":"Administrative tasks can be performed in HBase Shell or the Java API."},"13928":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4128","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-encryption","content":"Java API examples in this section are taken from the source file *hbase-server/src/test/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/util/TestHBaseFsckEncryption.java*. ."},"13929":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4129","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-access-administration-encryption","content":"Neither these examples, nor the source files they are taken from, are part of the public HBase API, and are provided for illustration only. Refer to the official API for usage instructions."},"13930":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4130","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-encryption-on-a-column-family","content":"To enable encryption on a column family, you can either use HBase Shell or the Java API. After enabling encryption, trigger a major compaction. When the major compaction completes, the compacted new HFiles will be encrypted. However, depending on the compaction settings, it is possible that not all the HFiles will be rewritten during a major compaction and there still might remain some old unencrypted HFiles. Also please note, that the snapshots are immutable. So the snapshots taken before you enabled the encryption will still contain the unencrypted HFiles."},"13931":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4131","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rotate-the-data-key","content":"To rotate the data key, first change the ColumnFamily key in the column descriptor, then trigger a major compaction. Until the compaction completes, the old HFiles will still be readable using the old key. During compaction, the compacted HFiles will be re-encrypted using the new data key. However, depending on the compaction settings, it is possible that not all the HFiles will be rewritten during a major compaction and there still might remain some old HFiles encrypted with the old key. Also please note, that the snapshots are immutable. So the snapshots taken before the changing of the encryption key will still contain the HFiles written using the old key."},"13932":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4132","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#switching-between-using-a-random-data-key-and-specifying-a-key","content":"If you configured a column family to use a specific key and you want to return to the default behavior of using a randomly-generated key for that column family, use the Java API to alter the `HColumnDescriptor` so that no value is sent with the key `ENCRYPTION_KEY`."},"13933":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4133","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rotate-the-master-key","content":"To rotate the master key, first generate and distribute the new key. Then update the KeyStore to contain a new master key, and keep the old master key in the KeyStore using a different alias. Next, configure fallback to the old master key in the *hbase-site.xml* file."},"13934":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4134","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secure-enable","content":"After hbase-2.x, the default 'hbase.security.authorization' changed. Before hbase-2.x, it defaulted to true, in later HBase versions, the default became false. So to enable hbase authorization, the following propertie must be configured in *hbase-site.xml*. See HBASE-19483;"},"13935":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4135","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-security-settings-in-hbase-sitexml","content":"Starting from 2.6.0, the hbase.regionserver.hlog.reader.impl and\nhbase.regionserver.hlog.writer.impl configurations are removed, you do not need to specify them\nany more. Just set hbase.regionserver.wal.encryption to true is enough to enable WAL encryption."},"13936":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4136","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-group-mapper-in-hadoop-core-sitexml","content":"Adjust these settings to suit your environment."},"13937":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4137","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-resources","content":"More information about the design and implementation can be found at the jira issue: HBASE-10070"},"13938":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4138","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-resources","content":"HBaseCon 2014 talk: HBase Read High Availability Using Timeline-Consistent Region Replicas also contains some details and slides."},"13939":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4139","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"HBase is a type of \"NoSQL\" database. \"NoSQL\" is a general term meaning that the database isn't an RDBMS which supports SQL as its primary access language, but there are many types of NoSQL databases: BerkeleyDB is an example of a local NoSQL database, whereas HBase is very much a distributed database. Technically speaking, HBase is really more a \"Data Store\" than \"Data Base\" because it lacks many of the features you find in an RDBMS, such as typed columns, secondary indexes, triggers, and advanced query languages, etc."},"13940":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4140","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"However, HBase has many features which supports both linear and modular scaling. HBase clusters expand by adding RegionServers that are hosted on commodity class servers. If a cluster expands from 10 to 20 RegionServers, for example, it doubles both in terms of storage and as well as processing capacity. An RDBMS can scale well, but only up to a point - specifically, the size of a single database server - and for the best performance requires specialized hardware and storage devices. HBase features of note are:"},"13941":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4141","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Strongly consistent reads/writes: HBase is not an \"eventually consistent\" DataStore. This makes it very suitable for tasks such as high-speed counter aggregation."},"13942":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4142","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Automatic sharding: HBase tables are distributed on the cluster via regions, and regions are automatically split and re-distributed as your data grows."},"13943":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4143","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Automatic RegionServer failover"},"13944":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4144","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Hadoop/HDFS Integration: HBase supports HDFS out of the box as its distributed file system."},"13945":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4145","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"MapReduce: HBase supports massively parallelized processing via MapReduce for using HBase as both source and sink."},"13946":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4146","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Java Client API: HBase supports an easy to use Java API for programmatic access."},"13947":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4147","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Thrift/REST API: HBase also supports Thrift and REST for non-Java front-ends."},"13948":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4148","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Block Cache and Bloom Filters: HBase supports a Block Cache and Bloom Filters for high volume query optimization."},"13949":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4149","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#nosql","content":"Operational Management: HBase provides build-in web-pages for operational insight as well as JMX metrics."},"13950":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4150","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"HBase isn't suitable for every problem."},"13951":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4151","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"First, make sure you have enough data. If you have hundreds of millions or billions of rows, then HBase is a good candidate. If you only have a few thousand/million rows, then using a traditional RDBMS might be a better choice due to the fact that all of your data might wind up on a single node (or two) and the rest of the cluster may be sitting idle."},"13952":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4152","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"Second, make sure you can live without all the extra features that an RDBMS provides (e.g., typed columns, secondary indexes, transactions, advanced query languages, etc.) An application built against an RDBMS cannot be \"ported\" to HBase by simply changing a JDBC driver, for example. Consider moving from an RDBMS to HBase as a complete redesign as opposed to a port."},"13953":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4153","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"Third, make sure you have enough hardware. Even HDFS doesn't do well with anything less than 5 DataNodes (due to things such as HDFS block replication which has a default of 3), plus a NameNode."},"13954":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4154","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-overview-when-should-i-use-hbase","content":"HBase can run quite well stand-alone on a laptop - but this should be considered a development configuration only."},"13955":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4155","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#what-is-the-difference-between-hbase-and-hadoophdfs","content":"HDFS is a distributed file system that is well suited for the storage of large files. Its documentation states that it is not, however, a general purpose file system, and does not provide fast individual record lookups in files. HBase, on the other hand, is built on top of HDFS and provides fast record lookups (and updates) for large tables. This can sometimes be a point of conceptual confusion. HBase internally puts your data in indexed \"StoreFiles\" that exist on HDFS for high-speed lookups. See the Data Model and the rest of this chapter for more information on how HBase achieves its goals."},"13956":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4156","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#catalog-tables","content":"The catalog table `hbase:meta` exists as an HBase table and is filtered out of the HBase shell's `list` command, but is in fact a table just like any other."},"13957":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4157","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"The `hbase:meta` table (previously called `.META.`) keeps a list of all regions in the system, and the location of `hbase:meta` is stored in ZooKeeper."},"13958":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4158","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"The `hbase:meta` table structure is as follows:"},"13959":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4159","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"**Key:**"},"13960":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4160","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"Region key of the format (`[table],[region start key],[region id]`)"},"13961":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4161","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"**Values:**"},"13962":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4162","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"`info:regioninfo` (serialized RegionInfo instance for this region)"},"13963":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4163","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"`info:server` (server:port of the RegionServer containing this region)"},"13964":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4164","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"`info:serverstartcode` (start-time of the RegionServer process containing this region)"},"13965":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4165","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"When a table is in the process of splitting, two other columns will be created, called `info:splitA` and `info:splitB`. These columns represent the two daughter regions. The values for these columns are also serialized HRegionInfo instances. After the region has been split, eventually this row will be deleted."},"13966":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4166","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"The empty key is used to denote table start and table end. A region with an empty start key is the\nfirst region in a table. If a region has both an empty start and an empty end key, it is the only\nregion in the table"},"13967":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4167","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasemeta","content":"In the (hopefully unlikely) event that programmatic processing of catalog metadata is required, see the RegionInfo.parseFrom utility."},"13968":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4168","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup-sequencing","content":"First, the location of `hbase:meta` is looked up in ZooKeeper. Next, `hbase:meta` is updated with server and startcode values."},"13969":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4169","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup-sequencing","content":"For information on region-RegionServer assignment, see Region-RegionServer Assignment."},"13970":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4170","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client","content":"The HBase client finds the RegionServers that are serving the particular row range of interest. It does this by querying the `hbase:meta` table. See hbase:meta for details. After locating the required region(s), the client contacts the RegionServer serving that region, rather than going through the master, and issues the read or write request. This information is cached in the client so that subsequent requests need not go through the lookup process. Should a region be reassigned either by the master load balancer or because a RegionServer has died, the client will requery the catalog tables to determine the new location of the user region."},"13971":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4171","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client","content":"See Runtime Impact for more information about the impact of the Master on HBase Client communication."},"13972":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4172","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client","content":"Administrative functions are done via an instance of Admin"},"13973":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4173","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-connections","content":"The API changed in HBase 1.0. For connection configuration information, see Client configuration and dependencies connecting to an HBase cluster."},"13974":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4174","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#api-as-of-hbase-100","content":"It's been cleaned up and users are returned Interfaces to work against rather than particular types. In HBase 1.0, obtain a `Connection` object from `ConnectionFactory` and thereafter, get from it instances of `Table`, `Admin`, and `RegionLocator` on an as-need basis. When done, close the obtained instances. Finally, be sure to cleanup your `Connection` instance before exiting. `Connections` are heavyweight objects but thread-safe so you can create one for your application and keep the instance around. `Table`, `Admin` and `RegionLocator` instances are lightweight. Create as you go and then let go as soon as you are done by closing them. See the Client Package Javadoc Description for example usage of the new HBase 1.0 API."},"13975":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4175","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#api-before-hbase-100","content":"Instances of `HTable` are the way to interact with an HBase cluster earlier than 1.0.0. *Table instances are not thread-safe*. Only one thread can use an instance of Table at any given time. When creating Table instances, it is advisable to use the same HBaseConfiguration instance. This will ensure sharing of ZooKeeper and socket instances to the RegionServers which is usually what you want. For example, this is preferred:"},"13976":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4176","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#api-before-hbase-100","content":"as opposed to this:"},"13977":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4177","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#api-before-hbase-100","content":"For more information about how connections are handled in the HBase client, see ConnectionFactory."},"13978":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4178","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connection-pooling","content":"For applications which require high-end multithreaded access (e.g., web-servers or application servers that may serve many application threads in a single JVM), you can pre-create a `Connection`, as shown in the following example:"},"13979":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4179","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connection-pooling","content":"**Example 24. Pre-Creating a `Connection`**"},"13980":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4180","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connection-pooling","content":"Previous versions of this guide discussed `HTablePool`, which was deprecated in HBase 0.94, 0.95,\nand 0.96, and removed in 0.98.1, by\nHBASE-6580, or `HConnection`, which is\ndeprecated in HBase 1.0 by `Connection`. Please use\nConnection\ninstead."},"13981":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4181","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"In HBase 1.0 and later, HTable is deprecated in favor of Table. `Table` does not use autoflush. To do buffered writes, use the BufferedMutator class."},"13982":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4182","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"In HBase 2.0 and later, HTable does not use BufferedMutator to execute the `Put` operation. Refer to HBASE-18500 for more information."},"13983":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4183","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"For additional information on write durability, review the ACID semantics page."},"13984":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4184","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#writebuffer-and-batch-methods","content":"For fine-grained control of batching of `Put`s or `Delete`s, see the batch methods on Table."},"13985":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4185","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"It is a new API introduced in HBase 2.0 which aims to provide the ability to access HBase asynchronously."},"13986":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4186","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"You can obtain an `AsyncConnection` from `ConnectionFactory`, and then get a asynchronous table instance from it to access HBase. When done, close the `AsyncConnection` instance(usually when your program exits)."},"13987":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4187","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"For the asynchronous table, most methods have the same meaning with the old `Table` interface, expect that the return value is wrapped with a CompletableFuture usually. We do not have any buffer here so there is no close method for asynchronous table, you do not need to close it. And it is thread safe."},"13988":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4188","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"There are several differences for scan:"},"13989":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4189","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"There is still a `getScanner` method which returns a `ResultScanner`. You can use it in the old way and it works like the old `ClientAsyncPrefetchScanner`."},"13990":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4190","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"There is a `scanAll` method which will return all the results at once. It aims to provide a simpler way for small scans which you want to get the whole results at once usually."},"13991":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4191","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"The Observer Pattern. There is a scan method which accepts a `ScanResultConsumer` as a parameter. It will pass the results to the consumer."},"13992":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4192","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"Notice that `AsyncTable` interface is templatized. The template parameter specifies the type of `ScanResultConsumerBase` used by scans, which means the observer style scan APIs are different. The two types of scan consumers are - `ScanResultConsumer` and `AdvancedScanResultConsumer`."},"13993":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4193","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"`ScanResultConsumer` needs a separate thread pool which is used to execute the callbacks registered to the returned CompletableFuture. Because the use of separate thread pool frees up RPC threads, callbacks are free to do anything. Use this if the callbacks are not quick, or when in doubt."},"13994":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4194","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-client","content":"`AdvancedScanResultConsumer` executes callbacks inside the framework thread. It is not allowed to do time consuming work in the callbacks else it will likely block the framework threads and cause very bad performance impact. As its name, it is designed for advanced users who want to write high performance code. See `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.client.example.HttpProxyExample` for how to write fully asynchronous code with it."},"13995":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4195","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-admin","content":"You can obtain an `AsyncConnection` from `ConnectionFactory`, and then get a `AsyncAdmin` instance from it to access HBase. Notice that there are two `getAdmin` methods to get a `AsyncAdmin` instance. One method has one extra thread pool parameter which is used to execute callbacks. It is designed for normal users. Another method doesn't need a thread pool and all the callbacks are executed inside the framework thread so it is not allowed to do time consuming works in the callbacks. It is designed for advanced users."},"13996":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4196","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-admin","content":"The default `getAdmin` methods will return a `AsyncAdmin` instance which use default configs. If you want to customize some configs, you can use `getAdminBuilder` methods to get a `AsyncAdminBuilder` for creating `AsyncAdmin` instance. Users are free to only set the configs they care about to create a new `AsyncAdmin` instance."},"13997":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4197","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-admin","content":"For the `AsyncAdmin` interface, most methods have the same meaning with the old `Admin` interface, expect that the return value is wrapped with a CompletableFuture usually."},"13998":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4198","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-admin","content":"For most admin operations, when the returned CompletableFuture is done, it means the admin operation has also been done. But for compact operation, it only means the compact request was sent to HBase and may need some time to finish the compact operation. For `rollWALWriter` method, it only means the rollWALWriter request was sent to the region server and may need some time to finish the `rollWALWriter` operation."},"13999":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4199","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asynchronous-admin","content":"For region name, we only accept `byte[]` as the parameter type and it may be a full region name or a encoded region name. For server name, we only accept `ServerName` as the parameter type. For table name, we only accept `TableName` as the parameter type. For `list*` operations, we only accept `Pattern` as the parameter type if you want to do regex matching."},"14000":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4200","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#external-clients","content":"Information on non-Java clients and custom protocols is covered in Apache HBase External APIs"},"14001":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4201","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Starting from 2.5.0, MasterRegistry is deprecated. It's functionality is completely superseded by the RpcConnectionRegistry. Please see Rpc Connection Registry (new as of 2.5.0) for more details."},"14002":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4202","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Client internally works with a *connection registry* to fetch the metadata needed by connections. This connection registry implementation is responsible for fetching the following metadata."},"14003":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4203","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Active master address"},"14004":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4204","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Current meta region(s) locations"},"14005":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4205","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Cluster ID (unique to this cluster)"},"14006":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4206","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"This information is needed as a part of various client operations like connection set up, scans, gets, etc. Traditionally, the connection registry implementation has been based on ZooKeeper as the source of truth and clients fetched the metadata directly from the ZooKeeper quorum. HBase 2.3.0 introduces a new connection registry implementation based on direct communication with the Masters. With this implementation, clients now fetch required metadata via master RPC end points instead of maintaining connections to ZooKeeper. This change was done for the following reasons."},"14007":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4207","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Reduce load on ZooKeeper since that is critical for cluster operation."},"14008":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4208","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Holistic client timeout and retry configurations since the new registry brings all the client operations under HBase rpc framework."},"14009":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4209","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Remove the ZooKeeper client dependency on HBase client library."},"14010":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4210","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"This means:"},"14011":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4211","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"At least a single active or stand by master is needed for cluster connection setup. Refer to Runtime Impact for more details."},"14012":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4212","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Master can be in a critical path of read/write operations, especially if the client metadata cache is empty or stale."},"14013":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4213","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"There is higher connection load on the masters that before since the clients talk directly to HMasters instead of ZooKeeper ensemble\\`"},"14014":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4214","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"To reduce hot-spotting on a single master, all the masters (active & stand-by) expose the needed service to fetch the connection metadata. This lets the client connect to any master (not just active). Both ZooKeeper-based and Master-based connection registry implementations are available in 2.3+. For 2.x and earlier, the ZooKeeper-based implementation remains the default configuration. For 3.0.0, RpcConnectionRegistry becomes the default configuration, as the alternate to MasterRegistry."},"14015":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4215","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"Change the connection registry implementation by updating the value configured for `hbase.client.registry.impl`. To explicitly enable the ZooKeeper-based registry, use"},"14016":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4216","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master-registry-new-as-of-230","content":"To explicitly enable the Master-based registry, use"},"14017":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4217","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#masterregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"MasterRegistry implements hedging of connection registry RPCs across active and stand-by masters. This lets the client make the same request to multiple servers and which ever responds first is returned back to the client immediately. This improves performance, especially when a subset of servers are under load. The hedging fan out size is configurable, meaning the number of requests that are hedged in a single attempt, using the configuration key *hbase.client.master\\_registry.hedged.fanout* in the client configuration. It defaults to 2. With this default, the RPCs are tried in batches of 2. The hedging policy is still primitive and does not adapt to any sort of live rpc performance metrics."},"14018":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4218","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-notes","content":"Clients hedge the requests in a randomized order to avoid hot-spotting a single master."},"14019":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4219","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-notes","content":"Cluster internal connections (masters ↔ regionservers) still use ZooKeeper based connection registry."},"14020":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4220","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-notes","content":"Cluster internal state is still tracked in Zookeeper, hence ZK availability requirements are same as before."},"14021":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4221","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-notes","content":"Inter cluster replication still uses ZooKeeper based connection registry to simplify configuration management."},"14022":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4222","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-notes","content":"For more implementation details, please refer to the design doc and HBASE-18095."},"14023":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4223","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"As said in the Master Registry (new as of 2.3.0) section, there are some disadvantages and limitations for MasterRegistry, especially that it puts master in the critical path of read/write operations. In order to address these problems, we introduced a more generic RpcConnectionRegistry."},"14024":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4224","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"It is also rpc based, like MasterRegistry, with several differences"},"14025":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4225","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"Region server also implements the necessary rpc service, so you can config any nodes in the cluster as bootstrap nodes, not only masters"},"14026":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4226","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"Support refreshing bootstrap nodes, for spreading loads across the nodes in the cluster, and also remove the dead nodes in bootstrap nodes."},"14027":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4227","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"To explicitly enable the rpc-based registry, use"},"14028":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4228","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"To configure the bootstrap nodes, use"},"14029":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4229","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"If not configured, we will fallback to use master addresses as the bootstrap nodes."},"14030":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4230","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-registry-new-as-of-250","content":"RpcConnectionRegistry is available in 2.5+, and becomes the default client registry implementation in 3.0.0."},"14031":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4231","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-rpc-hedging","content":"Hedged read is still supported, the configuration key is now *hbase.client.bootstrap.hedged.fanout*, and its default value is still 2."},"14032":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4232","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"There are basically two reasons for us to refresh the bootstrap nodes"},"14033":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4233","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"Periodically. This is for spreading loads across the nodes in the cluster. There are two configurations"},"14034":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4234","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"*hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*: the refresh interval in seconds, default 300. A value less than or equal to zero means disable refreshing."},"14035":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4235","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"*hbase.client.bootstrap.initial\\_refresh\\_delay\\_secs*: the initial refresh interval in seconds, the default value is 1/10 of *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*. The reason why we want to introduce a separated configuration for the delay for first refreshing is that, as end users could configure any nodes in a cluster as the initial bootstrap nodes, it is possible that different end users will configure the same machine which makes the machine over load. So we should have a shorter delay for the initial refresh, to let users quickly switch to the bootstrap nodes we want them to connect to."},"14036":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4236","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"When there is a connection error while requesting the nodes, we will refresh immediately, to remove the dead nodes. To avoid putting too much pressure to the cluster, there is a configuration *hbase.client.bootstrap.min\\_secs\\_between\\_refreshes*, to control the minimum interval between two refreshings. The default value is 60, but notice that, if you change *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs* to a small value, you need to make sure to also change *hbase.client.bootstrap.min\\_secs\\_between\\_refreshes* to a value smaller than *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*, otherwise an IllegalArgumentException will be thrown."},"14037":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4237","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpcconnectionregistry-bootstrap-nodes-refreshing","content":"(Advanced) In case of any issues with the rpc/master based registry, use the following\nconfiguration to fallback to the ZooKeeper based connection registry implementation."},"14038":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4238","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connection-uri","content":"Starting from 2.7.0, we add the support for specifying the connection information for a HBase cluster through an URI, which we call a \"connection URI\". And we've added several methods in *ConnectionFactory* to let you get a connection to the cluster specified by the URI. It looks like:"},"14039":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4239","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-schemes","content":"Currently there are two schemes supported, *hbase+rpc* for *RpcConnectionRegistry* and *hbase+zk* for *ZKConnectionRegistry*. *MasterRegistry* is deprecated so we do not expose it through connection URI."},"14040":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4240","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-schemes","content":"For *hbase+rpc*, it looks like"},"14041":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4241","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-schemes","content":"The authority part *server1:16020,server2:16020,server3:16020* specifies the bootstrap nodes and their rpc ports, i.e, the configuration value for *hbase.client.bootstrap.servers* in the past."},"14042":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4242","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-schemes","content":"For *hbase+zk*, it looks like"},"14043":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4243","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#supported-schemes","content":"The authority part *zk1:2181,zk2:2181,zk3:2181* is the zk quorum, i.e, the configuration value for *hbase.zookeeper.quorum&#x2A; in the past. The path part */hbase* is the znode parent, i.e, the configuration value for *zookeeper.znode.parent* in the past."},"14044":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4244","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#specify-configuration-through-uri-queries","content":"To let users fully specify the connection information through a connection URI, we support specifying configuration values through URI Queries. It looks like:"},"14045":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4245","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#specify-configuration-through-uri-queries","content":"In this way you can set the operation timeout to 10 seconds. Notice that, the configuration values specified in the connection URI will override the ones in the configuration file."},"14046":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4246","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"We use *ServiceLoader* to load different connection registry implementations, the entry point is *org.apache.hadoop.hbase.client.ConnectionRegistryURIFactory*. So if you implement your own *ConnectionRegistryURIFactory* which has a different scheme, and register it in the services file, we can load it at runtime."},"14047":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4247","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"Connection URI is still a very new feature which has not been used extensively in production, so we do not want to expose the ability to customize *ConnectionRegistryURIFactory* yet as the API may be changed frequently in the beginning."},"14048":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4248","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implement-your-own-connection-registry","content":"If you really want to implement your own connection registry, you can use the above way but take your own risk."},"14049":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4249","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-request-filters","content":"Get and Scan instances can be optionally configured with filters which are applied on the RegionServer."},"14050":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4250","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-request-filters","content":"Filters can be confusing because there are many different types, and it is best to approach them by understanding the groups of Filter functionality."},"14051":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4251","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#structural","content":"Structural Filters contain other Filters."},"14052":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4252","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#filterlist","content":"FilterList represents a list of Filters with a relationship of `FilterList.Operator.MUST_PASS_ALL` or `FilterList.Operator.MUST_PASS_ONE` between the Filters. The following example shows an 'or' between two Filters (checking for either 'my value' or 'my other value' on the same attribute)."},"14053":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4253","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#singlecolumnvaluefilter","content":"A SingleColumnValueFilter (see: https\\://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/filter/SingleColumnValueFilter.html) can be used to test column values for equivalence (`CompareOperaor.EQUAL`), inequality (`CompareOperaor.NOT_EQUAL`), or ranges (e.g., `CompareOperaor.GREATER`). The following is an example of testing equivalence of a column to a String value \"my value\"..."},"14054":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4254","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnvaluefilter","content":"Introduced in HBase-2.0.0 version as a complementation of SingleColumnValueFilter, ColumnValueFilter gets matched cell only, while SingleColumnValueFilter gets the entire row (has other columns and values) to which the matched cell belongs. Parameters of constructor of ColumnValueFilter are the same as SingleColumnValueFilter."},"14055":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4255","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnvaluefilter","content":"Note. For simple query like \"equals to a family:qualifier:value\", we highly recommend to use the following way instead of using SingleColumnValueFilter or ColumnValueFilter:"},"14056":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4256","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnvaluefilter","content":"This scan will restrict to the specified column 'family:qualifier', avoiding scan of unrelated families and columns, which has better performance, and `ValueFilter` is the condition used to do the value filtering."},"14057":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4257","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnvaluefilter","content":"But if query is much more complicated beyond this book, then please make your good choice case by case."},"14058":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4258","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#column-value-comparators","content":"There are several Comparator classes in the Filter package that deserve special mention. These Comparators are used in concert with other Filters, such as SingleColumnValueFilter."},"14059":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4259","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regexstringcomparator","content":"RegexStringComparator supports regular expressions for value comparisons."},"14060":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4260","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regexstringcomparator","content":"See the Oracle JavaDoc for supported RegEx patterns in Java."},"14061":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4261","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#substringcomparator","content":"SubstringComparator can be used to determine if a given substring exists in a value. The comparison is case-insensitive."},"14062":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4262","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binaryprefixcomparator","content":"See BinaryPrefixComparator."},"14063":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4263","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binarycomparator","content":"See BinaryComparator."},"14064":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4264","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binarycomponentcomparator","content":"BinaryComponentComparator can be used to compare specific value at specific location with in the cell value. The comparison can be done for both ascii and binary data."},"14065":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4265","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binarycomponentcomparator","content":"See HBASE-22969 for other use cases and details."},"14066":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4266","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue-metadata","content":"As HBase stores data internally as KeyValue pairs, KeyValue Metadata Filters evaluate the existence of keys (i.e., ColumnFamily:Column qualifiers) for a row, as opposed to values the previous section."},"14067":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4267","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#familyfilter","content":"FamilyFilter can be used to filter on the ColumnFamily. It is generally a better idea to select ColumnFamilies in the Scan than to do it with a Filter."},"14068":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4268","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#qualifierfilter","content":"QualifierFilter can be used to filter based on Column (aka Qualifier) name."},"14069":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4269","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnprefixfilter","content":"ColumnPrefixFilter can be used to filter based on the lead portion of Column (aka Qualifier) names."},"14070":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4270","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnprefixfilter","content":"A ColumnPrefixFilter seeks ahead to the first column matching the prefix in each row and for each involved column family. It can be used to efficiently get a subset of the columns in very wide rows."},"14071":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4271","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnprefixfilter","content":"Note: The same column qualifier can be used in different column families. This filter returns all matching columns."},"14072":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4272","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnprefixfilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family that start with \"abc\""},"14073":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4273","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"MultipleColumnPrefixFilter behaves like ColumnPrefixFilter but allows specifying multiple prefixes."},"14074":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4274","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"Like ColumnPrefixFilter, MultipleColumnPrefixFilter efficiently seeks ahead to the first column matching the lowest prefix and also seeks past ranges of columns between prefixes. It can be used to efficiently get discontinuous sets of columns from very wide rows."},"14075":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4275","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiplecolumnprefixfilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family that start with \"abc\" or \"xyz\""},"14076":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4276","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnrangefilter","content":"A ColumnRangeFilter allows efficient intra row scanning."},"14077":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4277","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnrangefilter","content":"A ColumnRangeFilter can seek ahead to the first matching column for each involved column family. It can be used to efficiently get a 'slice' of the columns of a very wide row. i.e. you have a million columns in a row but you only want to look at columns bbbb-bbdd."},"14078":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4278","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnrangefilter","content":"Note: The same column qualifier can be used in different column families. This filter returns all matching columns."},"14079":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4279","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnrangefilter","content":"Example: Find all columns in a row and family between \"bbbb\" (inclusive) and \"bbdd\" (inclusive)"},"14080":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4280","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#columnrangefilter","content":"Note: Introduced in HBase 0.92"},"14081":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4281","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowfilter","content":"It is generally a better idea to use the startRow/stopRow methods on Scan for row selection, however RowFilter can also be used."},"14082":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4282","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rowfilter","content":"You can supplement a scan (both bounded and unbounded) with RowFilter constructed from BinaryComponentComparator for further filtering out or filtering in rows. See HBASE-22969 for use cases and other details."},"14083":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4283","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#firstkeyonlyfilter","content":"This is primarily used for rowcount jobs. See FirstKeyOnlyFilter."},"14084":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4284","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#master","content":"`HMaster` is the implementation of the Master Server. The Master server is responsible for monitoring all RegionServer instances in the cluster, and is the interface for all metadata changes. In a distributed cluster, the Master typically runs on the NameNode. J Mohamed Zahoor goes into some more detail on the Master Architecture in this blog posting, HBase HMaster Architecture."},"14085":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4285","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup-behavior","content":"If run in a multi-Master environment, all Masters compete to run the cluster. If the active Master loses its lease in ZooKeeper (or the Master shuts down), then the remaining Masters jostle to take over the Master role."},"14086":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4286","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#runtime-impact","content":"A common dist-list question involves what happens to an HBase cluster when the Master goes down. This information has changed starting 3.0.0."},"14087":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4287","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#up-until-releases-2xy","content":"Because the HBase client talks directly to the RegionServers, the cluster can still function in a \"steady state\". Additionally, per Catalog Tables, `hbase:meta` exists as an HBase table and is not resident in the Master. However, the Master controls critical functions such as RegionServer failover and completing region splits. So while the cluster can still run for a short time without the Master, the Master should be restarted as soon as possible."},"14088":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4288","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#staring-release-300","content":"As mentioned in section Master Registry (new as of 2.3.0), the default connection registry for clients is now based on master rpc end points. Hence the requirements for masters' uptime are even tighter starting this release."},"14089":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4289","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#staring-release-300","content":"At least one active or stand by master is needed for a connection set up, unlike before when all the clients needed was a ZooKeeper ensemble."},"14090":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4290","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#staring-release-300","content":"Master is now in critical path for read/write operations. For example, if the meta region bounces off to a different region server, clients need master to fetch the new locations. Earlier this was done by fetching this information directly from ZooKeeper."},"14091":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4291","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#staring-release-300","content":"Masters will now have higher connection load than before. So, the server side configuration might need adjustment depending on the load."},"14092":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4292","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#staring-release-300","content":"Overall, the master uptime requirements, when this feature is enabled, are even higher for the client operations to go through."},"14093":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4293","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-master-interface","content":"The methods exposed by `HMasterInterface` are primarily metadata-oriented methods:"},"14094":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4294","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-master-interface","content":"Table (createTable, modifyTable, removeTable, enable, disable)"},"14095":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4295","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-master-interface","content":"ColumnFamily (addColumn, modifyColumn, removeColumn)"},"14096":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4296","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-master-interface","content":"Region (move, assign, unassign) For example, when the `Admin` method `disableTable` is invoked, it is serviced by the Master server."},"14097":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4297","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-master-processes","content":"The Master runs several background threads:"},"14098":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4298","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#loadbalancer","content":"Periodically, and when there are no regions in transition, a load balancer will run and move regions around to balance the cluster's load. See Balancer for configuring this property."},"14099":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4299","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#loadbalancer","content":"See Region-RegionServer Assignment for more information on region assignment."},"14100":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4300","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#catalogjanitor","content":"Periodically checks and cleans up the `hbase:meta` table. See hbase:meta for more information on the meta table."},"14101":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4301","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#masterprocwal","content":"*MasterProcWAL is replaced in hbase-2.3.0 by an alternate Procedure Store implementation; see in-master-procedure-store-region. This section pertains to hbase-2.0.0 through hbase-2.2.x*"},"14102":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4302","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#masterprocwal","content":"HMaster records administrative operations and their running states, such as the handling of a crashed server, table creation, and other DDLs, into a Procedure Store. The Procedure Store WALs are stored under the MasterProcWALs directory. The Master WALs are not like RegionServer WALs. Keeping up the Master WAL allows us to run a state machine that is resilient across Master failures. For example, if a HMaster was in the middle of creating a table encounters an issue and fails, the next active HMaster can take up where the previous left off and carry the operation to completion. Since hbase-2.0.0, a new AssignmentManager (A.K.A AMv2) was introduced and the HMaster handles region assignment operations, server crash processing, balancing, etc., all via AMv2 persisting all state and transitions into MasterProcWALs rather than up into ZooKeeper, as we do in hbase-1.x."},"14103":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4303","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#masterprocwal","content":"See AMv2 Description for Devs (and Procedure Framework (Pv2): HBASE-12439 for its basis) if you would like to learn more about the new AssignmentManager."},"14104":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4304","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"Here are the list of configurations that effect MasterProcWAL operation. You should not have to change your defaults."},"14105":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4305","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.periodic.roll.msec`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Frequency of generating a new WAL\\\n&#x2A;*Default:** `1h (3600000 in msec)`"},"14106":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4306","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.roll.threshold`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Threshold in size before the WAL rolls. Every time the WAL reaches this size or the above period, 1 hour, passes since last log roll, the HMaster will generate a new WAL.\\\n&#x2A;*Default:** `32MB (33554432 in byte)`"},"14107":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4307","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.warn.threshold`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** If the number of WALs goes beyond this threshold, the following message should appear in the HMaster log with WARN level when rolling."},"14108":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4308","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `64`"},"14109":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4309","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.max.retries.before.roll`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** Max number of retry when syncing slots (records) to its underlying storage, such as HDFS. Every attempt, the following message should appear in the HMaster log."},"14110":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4310","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `3`"},"14111":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4311","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**`hbase.procedure.store.wal.sync.failure.roll.max`**\\\n&#x2A;*Description:** After the above 3 retrials, the log is rolled and the retry count is reset to 0, thereon a new set of retrial starts. This configuration controls the max number of attempts of log rolling upon sync failure. That is, HMaster is allowed to fail to sync 9 times in total. Once it exceeds, the following log should appear in the HMaster log."},"14112":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4312","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configurations-for-masterprocwal","content":"**Default:** `3`"},"14113":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4313","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver","content":"`HRegionServer` is the RegionServer implementation. It is responsible for serving and managing regions. In a distributed cluster, a RegionServer runs on a DataNode."},"14114":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4314","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"The methods exposed by `HRegionRegionInterface` contain both data-oriented and region-maintenance methods:"},"14115":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4315","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"Data (get, put, delete, next, etc.)"},"14116":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4316","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-interface","content":"Region (splitRegion, compactRegion, etc.) For example, when the `Admin` method `majorCompact` is invoked on a table, the client is actually iterating through all regions for the specified table and requesting a major compaction directly to each region."},"14117":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4317","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-processes","content":"The RegionServer runs a variety of background threads:"},"14118":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4318","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compactsplitthread","content":"Checks for splits and handle minor compactions."},"14119":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4319","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#majorcompactionchecker","content":"Checks for major compactions."},"14120":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4320","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstoreflusher","content":"Periodically flushes in-memory writes in the MemStore to StoreFiles."},"14121":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4321","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#logroller","content":"Periodically checks the RegionServer's WAL."},"14122":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4322","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessors","content":"Coprocessors were added in 0.92. There is a thorough Blog Overview of CoProcessors posted. Documentation will eventually move to this reference guide, but the blog is the most current information available at this time."},"14123":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4323","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-block-cache","content":"HBase provides two different BlockCache implementations to cache data read from HDFS: the default on-heap `LruBlockCache` and the `BucketCache`, which is (usually) off-heap. This section discusses benefits and drawbacks of each implementation, how to choose the appropriate option, and configuration options for each."},"14124":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4324","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-block-cache","content":"See the RegionServer UI for detail on caching deploy. See configurations, sizings, current usage,\ntime-in-the-cache, and even detail on block counts and types."},"14125":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4325","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-choices","content":"`LruBlockCache` is the original implementation, and is entirely within the Java heap. `BucketCache` is optional and mainly intended for keeping block cache data off-heap, although `BucketCache` can also be a file-backed cache. In file-backed we can either use it in the file mode or the mmaped mode. We also have pmem mode where the bucket cache resides on the persistent memory device."},"14126":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4326","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-choices","content":"When you enable BucketCache, you are enabling a two tier caching system. We used to describe the tiers as \"L1\" and \"L2\" but have deprecated this terminology as of hbase-2.0.0. The \"L1\" cache referred to an instance of LruBlockCache and \"L2\" to an off-heap BucketCache. Instead, when BucketCache is enabled, all DATA blocks are kept in the BucketCache tier and meta blocks — INDEX and BLOOM blocks — are on-heap in the `LruBlockCache`. Management of these two tiers and the policy that dictates how blocks move between them is done by `CombinedBlockCache`."},"14127":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4327","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-cache-configurations","content":"Apart from the cache implementation itself, you can set some general configuration options to control how the cache performs. See CacheConfig. After setting any of these options, restart or rolling restart your cluster for the configuration to take effect. Check logs for errors or unexpected behavior."},"14128":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4328","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-cache-configurations","content":"See also Prefetch Option for Blockcache, which discusses a new option introduced in HBASE-9857."},"14129":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4329","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"The LruBlockCache is an LRU cache that contains three levels of block priority to allow for scan-resistance and in-memory ColumnFamilies:"},"14130":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4330","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"Single access priority: The first time a block is loaded from HDFS it normally has this priority and it will be part of the first group to be considered during evictions. The advantage is that scanned blocks are more likely to get evicted than blocks that are getting more usage."},"14131":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4331","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"Multi access priority: If a block in the previous priority group is accessed again, it upgrades to this priority. It is thus part of the second group considered during evictions."},"14132":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4332","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"In-memory access priority: If the block's family was configured to be \"in-memory\", it will be part of this priority disregarding the number of times it was accessed. Catalog tables are configured like this. This group is the last one considered during evictions.\\\nTo mark a column family as in-memory, call"},"14133":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4333","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"if creating a table from java, or set `IN_MEMORY ⇒ true` when creating or altering a table in the shell: e.g."},"14134":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4334","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-design","content":"For more information, see the LruBlockCache source"},"14135":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4335","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Block caching is enabled by default for all the user tables which means that any read operation will load the LRU cache. This might be good for a large number of use cases, but further tunings are usually required in order to achieve better performance. An important concept is the working set size, or WSS, which is: \"the amount of memory needed to compute the answer to a problem\". For a website, this would be the data that's needed to answer the queries over a short amount of time."},"14136":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4336","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The way to calculate how much memory is available in HBase for caching is:"},"14137":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4337","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The default value for the block cache is 0.4 which represents 40% of the available heap. The last value (99%) is the default acceptable loading factor in the LRU cache after which eviction is started. The reason it is included in this equation is that it would be unrealistic to say that it is possible to use 100% of the available memory since this would make the process blocking from the point where it loads new blocks. Here are some examples:"},"14138":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4338","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"One region server with the heap size set to 1 GB and the default block cache size will have 405 MB of block cache available."},"14139":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4339","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"20 region servers with the heap size set to 8 GB and a default block cache size will have 63.3 GB of block cache."},"14140":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4340","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"100 region servers with the heap size set to 24 GB and a block cache size of 0.5 will have about 1.16 TB of block cache."},"14141":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4341","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Your data is not the only resident of the block cache. Here are others that you may have to take into account:"},"14142":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4342","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Catalog Tables**\\\nThe `hbase:meta` table is forced into the block cache and have the in-memory priority which means that they are harder to evict."},"14143":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4343","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"The hbase:meta tables can occupy a few MBs depending on the number of regions."},"14144":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4344","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**HFiles Indexes**\\\nAn *HFile* is the file format that HBase uses to store data in HDFS. It contains a multi-layered index which allows HBase to seek the data without having to read the whole file. The size of those indexes is a factor of the block size (64KB by default), the size of your keys and the amount of data you are storing. For big data sets it's not unusual to see numbers around 1GB per region server, although not all of it will be in cache because the LRU will evict indexes that aren't used."},"14145":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4345","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Keys**\\\nThe values that are stored are only half the picture, since each value is stored along with its keys (row key, family qualifier, and timestamp). See Try to minimize row and column sizes."},"14146":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4346","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"**Bloom Filters**\\\nJust like the HFile indexes, those data structures (when enabled) are stored in the LRU."},"14147":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4347","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Currently the recommended way to measure HFile indexes and bloom filters sizes is to look at the region server web UI and checkout the relevant metrics. For keys, sampling can be done by using the HFile command line tool and look for the average key size metric. Since HBase 0.98.3, you can view details on BlockCache stats and metrics in a special Block Cache section in the UI. As of HBase 2.4.14, you can estimate HFile indexes and bloom filters vs other DATA blocks using blockCacheCount and blockCacheDataBlockCount in JMX. The formula `(blockCacheCount - blockCacheDataBlockCount) * blockSize` will give you an estimate which can be useful when trying to enable the BucketCache. You should make sure the post-BucketCache config gives enough memory to the on-heap LRU cache to hold at least the same number of non-DATA blocks from pre-BucketCache. Once BucketCache is enabled, the L1 metrics like l1CacheSize, l1CacheCount, and l1CacheEvictionCount can help you further tune the size."},"14148":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4348","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"It's generally bad to use block caching when the WSS doesn't fit in memory. This is the case when you have for example 40GB available across all your region servers' block caches but you need to process 1TB of data. One of the reasons is that the churn generated by the evictions will trigger more garbage collections unnecessarily. Here are two use cases:"},"14149":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4349","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Fully random reading pattern: This is a case where you almost never access the same row twice within a short amount of time such that the chance of hitting a cached block is close to 0. Setting block caching on such a table is a waste of memory and CPU cycles, more so that it will generate more garbage to pick up by the JVM. For more information on monitoring GC, see JVM Garbage Collection Logs."},"14150":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4350","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#lrublockcache-usage","content":"Mapping a table: In a typical MapReduce job that takes a table in input, every row will be read only once so there's no need to put them into the block cache. The Scan object has the option of turning this off via the setCacheBlocks method (set it to false). You can still keep block caching turned on on this table if you need fast random read access. An example would be counting the number of rows in a table that serves live traffic, caching every block of that table would create massive churn and would surely evict data that's currently in use."},"14151":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4351","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#caching-meta-blocks-only-data-blocks-in-fscache","content":"An interesting setup is one where we cache META blocks only and we read DATA blocks in on each access. If the DATA blocks fit inside fscache, this alternative may make sense when access is completely random across a very large dataset. To enable this setup, alter your table and for each column family set `BLOCKCACHE ⇒ 'false'`. You are 'disabling' the BlockCache for this column family only. You can never disable the caching of META blocks. Since HBASE-4683 Always cache index and bloom blocks, we will cache META blocks even if the BlockCache is disabled."},"14152":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4352","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"The usual deployment of BucketCache is via a managing class that sets up two caching tiers: an on-heap cache implemented by LruBlockCache and a second cache implemented with BucketCache. The managing class is CombinedBlockCache by default. The previous link describes the caching 'policy' implemented by CombinedBlockCache. In short, it works by keeping meta blocks — INDEX and BLOOM in the on-heap LruBlockCache tier — and DATA blocks are kept in the BucketCache tier."},"14153":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4353","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"**Pre-hbase-2.0.0 versions**"},"14154":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4354","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"Fetching will always be slower when fetching from BucketCache in pre-hbase-2.0.0, as compared to the native on-heap LruBlockCache. However, latencies tend to be less erratic across time, because there is less garbage collection when you use BucketCache since it is managing BlockCache allocations, not the GC. If the BucketCache is deployed in off-heap mode, this memory is not managed by the GC at all. This is why you'd use BucketCache in pre-2.0.0, so your latencies are less erratic, to mitigate GCs and heap fragmentation, and so you can safely use more memory. See Nick Dimiduk's BlockCache 101 for comparisons running on-heap vs off-heap tests. Also see Comparing BlockCache Deploys which finds that if your dataset fits inside your LruBlockCache deploy, use it otherwise if you are experiencing cache churn (or you want your cache to exist beyond the vagaries of java GC), use BucketCache."},"14155":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4355","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"In pre-2.0.0, one can configure the BucketCache so it receives the `victim` of an LruBlockCache eviction. All Data and index blocks are cached in L1 first. When eviction happens from L1, the blocks (or `victims`) will get moved to L2. Set `cacheDataInL1` via `(HColumnDescriptor.setCacheDataInL1(true)` or in the shell, creating or amending column families setting `CACHE_DATA_IN_L1` to true: e.g."},"14156":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4356","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"**hbase-2.0.0+ versions**"},"14157":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4357","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"HBASE-11425 changed the HBase read path so it could hold the read-data off-heap avoiding copying of cached data on to the java heap. See Offheap read-path. In hbase-2.0.0, off-heap latencies approach those of on-heap cache latencies with the added benefit of NOT provoking GC."},"14158":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4358","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-enable-bucketcache","content":"From HBase 2.0.0 onwards, the notions of L1 and L2 have been deprecated. When BucketCache is turned on, the DATA blocks will always go to BucketCache and INDEX/BLOOM blocks go to on heap LRUBlockCache. `cacheDataInL1` support has been removed."},"14159":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4359","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"The BucketCache Block Cache can be deployed *offheap*, *file* or *mmaped* file mode."},"14160":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4360","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"You set which via the `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` setting. Setting it to `offheap` will have BucketCache make its allocations off-heap, and an ioengine setting of `file:PATH_TO_FILE` will direct BucketCache to use file caching (Useful in particular if you have some fast I/O attached to the box such as SSDs). From 2.0.0, it is possible to have more than one file backing the BucketCache. This is very useful especially when the Cache size requirement is high. For multiple backing files, configure ioengine as `files:PATH_TO_FILE1,PATH_TO_FILE2,PATH_TO_FILE3`. BucketCache can be configured to use an mmapped file also. Configure ioengine as `mmap:PATH_TO_FILE` for this."},"14161":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4361","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"It is possible to deploy a tiered setup where we bypass the CombinedBlockCache policy and have BucketCache working as a strict L2 cache to the L1 LruBlockCache. For such a setup, set `hbase.bucketcache.combinedcache.enabled` to `false`. In this mode, on eviction from L1, blocks go to L2. When a block is cached, it is cached first in L1. When we go to look for a cached block, we look first in L1 and if none found, then search L2. Let us call this deploy format, *Raw L1+L2*. NOTE: This L1+L2 mode is removed from 2.0.0. When BucketCache is used, it will be strictly the DATA cache and the LruBlockCache will cache INDEX/META blocks."},"14162":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4362","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"Other BucketCache configs include: specifying a location to persist cache to across restarts, how many threads to use writing the cache, etc. See the CacheConfig.html class for configuration options and descriptions."},"14163":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4363","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-deploy-modes","content":"To check it enabled, look for the log line describing cache setup; it will detail how BucketCache has been deployed. Also see the UI. It will detail the cache tiering and their configuration."},"14164":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4364","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"This sample provides a configuration for a 4 GB off-heap BucketCache with a 1 GB on-heap cache."},"14165":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4365","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Configuration is performed on the RegionServer."},"14166":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4366","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Setting `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` and `hbase.bucketcache.size` > 0 enables `CombinedBlockCache`. Let us presume that the RegionServer has been set to run with a 5G heap: i.e. `HBASE_HEAPSIZE=5g`."},"14167":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4367","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"First, edit the RegionServer's *hbase-env.sh* and set `HBASE_OFFHEAPSIZE` to a value greater than the off-heap size wanted, in this case, 4 GB (expressed as 4G). Let's set it to 5G. That'll be 4G for our off-heap cache and 1G for any other uses of off-heap memory (there are other users of off-heap memory other than BlockCache; e.g. DFSClient in RegionServer can make use of off-heap memory). See Direct Memory Usage In HBase below."},"14168":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4368","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Next, add the following configuration to the RegionServer's *hbase-site.xml*."},"14169":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4369","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"Restart or rolling restart your cluster, and check the logs for any issues."},"14170":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4370","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"In the above, we set the BucketCache to be 4G. We configured the on-heap LruBlockCache have 20% (0.2) of the RegionServer's heap size (0.2 \\* 5G = 1G). In other words, you configure the L1 LruBlockCache as you would normally (as if there were no L2 cache present)."},"14171":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4371","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"HBASE-10641 introduced the ability to configure multiple sizes for the buckets of the BucketCache, in HBase 0.98 and newer. To configurable multiple bucket sizes, configure the new property `hbase.bucketcache.bucket.sizes` to a comma-separated list of block sizes, ordered from smallest to largest, with no spaces. The goal is to optimize the bucket sizes based on your data access patterns. The following example configures buckets of size 4096 and 8192."},"14172":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4372","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"The default maximum direct memory varies by JVM. Traditionally it is 64M or some relation to allocated heap size (-Xmx) or no limit at all (JDK7 apparently). HBase servers use direct memory, in particular short-circuit reading (See Leveraging local data), the hosted DFSClient will allocate direct memory buffers. How much the DFSClient uses is not easy to quantify; it is the number of open HFiles \\* `hbase.dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size` where `hbase.dfs.client.read.shortcircuit.buffer.size` is set to 128k in HBase — see *hbase-default.xml* default configurations. If you do off-heap block caching, you'll be making use of direct memory. The RPCServer uses a ByteBuffer pool. From 2.0.0, these buffers are off-heap ByteBuffers. Starting your JVM, make sure the `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize` setting in *conf/hbase-env.sh* considers off-heap BlockCache (`hbase.bucketcache.size`), DFSClient usage, RPC side ByteBufferPool max size. This has to be bit higher than sum of off heap BlockCache size and max ByteBufferPool size. Allocating an extra of 1-2 GB for the max direct memory size has worked in tests. Direct memory, which is part of the Java process heap, is separate from the object heap allocated by -Xmx. The value allocated by `MaxDirectMemorySize` must not exceed physical RAM, and is likely to be less than the total available RAM due to other memory requirements and system constraints."},"14173":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4373","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"You can see how much memory — on-heap and off-heap/direct — a RegionServer is configured to use and how much it is using at any one time by looking at the *Server Metrics: Memory* tab in the UI. It can also be gotten via JMX. In particular the direct memory currently used by the server can be found on the `java.nio.type=BufferPool,name=direct` bean. Terracotta has a good write up on using off-heap memory in Java. It is for their product BigMemory but a lot of the issues noted apply in general to any attempt at going off-heap. Check it out."},"14174":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4374","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"This is a pre-HBase 1.0 configuration removed because it was confusing. It was a float that you would set to some value between 0.0 and 1.0. Its default was 0.9. If the deploy was using CombinedBlockCache, then the LruBlockCache L1 size was calculated to be `(1 - hbase.bucketcache.percentage.in.combinedcache) * size-of-bucketcache` and the BucketCache size was `hbase.bucketcache.percentage.in.combinedcache * size-of-bucket-cache`. where size-of-bucket-cache itself is EITHER the value of the configuration `hbase.bucketcache.size` IF it was specified as Megabytes OR `hbase.bucketcache.size` \\* `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize` if `hbase.bucketcache.size` is between 0 and 1.0."},"14175":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4375","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bucketcache-example-configuration","content":"In 1.0, it should be more straight-forward. Onheap LruBlockCache size is set as a fraction of java heap using `hfile.block.cache.size setting` (not the best name) and BucketCache is set as above in absolute Megabytes."},"14176":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4376","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"HBASE-28463 introduced time based priority for blocks in BucketCache. It allows for defining an age threshold at individual column families' configuration, whereby blocks older than this configured threshold would be targeted first for eviction."},"14177":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4377","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Blocks from column families that don't define the age threshold wouldn't be evaluated by the time based priority, and would only be evicted following the LRU eviction logic."},"14178":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4378","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"This feature is mostly useful for use cases where most recent data is more frequently accessed, and therefore should get higher priority in the cache. Configuring Time Based Priority with the \"age\" of most accessed data would then give a finer control over blocks allocation in the BucketCache than the built-in LRU eviction logic."},"14179":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4379","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Time Based Priority for BucketCache provides three different strategies for defining data age:"},"14180":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4380","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Cell timestamps: Uses the timestamp portion of HBase cells for comparing the data age."},"14181":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4381","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Custom cell qualifiers: Uses a custom-defined date qualifier for comparing the data age. It uses that value to tier the entire row containing the given qualifier value. This requires that the custom qualifier be a valid Java long timestamp."},"14182":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4382","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Custom value provider: Allows for defining a pluggable implementation that contains the logic for identifying the date value to be used for comparison. This also provides additional flexibility for different use cases that might have the date stored in other formats or embedded with other data in various portions of a given row."},"14183":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4383","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"For use cases where priority is determined by the order of record ingestion in HBase (with the most recent being the most relevant), the built-in cell timestamp offers the most convenient and efficient method for configuring age-based priority. See Using Cell timestamps for Time Based Priority."},"14184":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4384","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Some applications may utilize a custom date column to define the priority of table records. In such instances, a custom cell qualifier-based priority is advisable. See Using Custom Cell Qualifiers for Time Based Priority."},"14185":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4385","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Finally, more intricate schemas may incorporate domain-specific logic for defining the age of each record. The custom value provider facilitates the integration of custom code to implement the appropriate parsing of the date value that should be used for the priority comparison. See Using a Custom value provider for Time Based Priority."},"14186":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4386","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"With Time Based Priority for BucketCache, blocks age is evaluated when deciding if a block should be cached (i.e. during reads, writes, compaction and prefetch), as well as during the cache freeSpace run (mass eviction), prior to executing the LRU logic."},"14187":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4387","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Because blocks don't hold any specific meta information other than type, it's necessary to group blocks of the same \"age group\" on separate files, using specialized compaction implementations (see more details in the configuration section below). The time range of all blocks in each file is then appended at the file meta info section, and is used for evaluating the age of blocks that should be considered in the Time Based Priority logic."},"14188":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4388","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Finding the age of each block involves an extra overhead, therefore the feature is disabled by default at a global configuration level."},"14189":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4389","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"To enable it, the following configuration should be set on RegionServers' *hbase-site.xml*:"},"14190":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4390","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-time-based-priority-for-bucketcache","content":"Once enabled globally, it's necessary to define the desired strategy-specific settings at the individual column family level."},"14191":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4391","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"This strategy is the most efficient to run, as it uses the timestamp portion of each cell containing the data for comparing the age of blocks. It requires DateTieredCompaction for splitting the blocks into separate files according to blocks' ages."},"14192":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4392","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"The example below sets the hot age threshold to one week (in milliseconds) for the column family 'cf1' in table 'orders':"},"14193":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4393","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"In the example above, the properties governing the number of windows and period of each window in the date tiered compaction were not set. With the default settings, the compaction will create initially four windows of six hours, then four windows of one day each, then another four windows of four days each and so on until the minimum timestamp among the selected files is covered. This can create a large number of files, therefore, additional changes to the 'hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles', 'hbase.hstore.compaction.min' and 'hbase.hstore.compaction.max' are recommended."},"14194":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4394","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-cell-timestamps-for-time-based-priority","content":"Alternatively, consider adjusting the initial window size to the same as the hot age threshold, and two windows only per tier:"},"14195":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4395","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"This strategy uses a new compaction implementation designed for Time Based Priority. It extends date tiered compaction, but instead of producing multiple tiers of various time windows, it simply splits files into two groups: the \"cold\" group, where all blocks are older than the defined threshold age, and the \"hot\" group, where all blocks are newer than the threshold age."},"14196":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4396","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"The example below defines a cell qualifier 'event\\_date' to be used for comparing the age of blocks within the custom cell qualifier strategy:"},"14197":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4397","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-custom-cell-qualifiers-for-time-based-priority","content":"Note that there are two different configurations for defining the hot age threshold. This is\nbecause the Time Based Priority enforcer operates independently of the compaction implementation."},"14198":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4398","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"It's also possible to hook in domain-specific logic for defining the data age of each row to be used for comparing blocks priorities. The Custom Time Based Priority framework defines the `CustomTieredCompactor.TieringValueProvider` interface, which can be implemented to provide the specific date value to be used by compaction for grouping the blocks according to the threshold age."},"14199":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4399","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"In the following example, the `RowKeyPortionTieringValueProvider` implements the `getTieringValue` method. This method parses the date from a segment of the row key value, specifically between positions 14 and 29, using the \"yyyyMMddHHmmss\" format. The parsed date is then returned as a long timestamp, which is then used by custom tiered compaction to group the blocks based on the defined hot age threshold:"},"14200":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4400","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"The Tiering Value Provider above can then be configured for Time Based Priority as follows:"},"14201":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4401","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"Upon enabling Custom Time Based Priority (either the custom qualifier or custom value provider) in\nthe column family configuration, it is imperative that major compaction be executed twice on the\nspecified tables to ensure the effective application of the newly configured priorities within the\nbucket cache."},"14202":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4402","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"Time Based Priority was originally implemented with the cell timestamp strategy only. The original design covering cell timestamp based strategy is available here."},"14203":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4403","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-a-custom-value-provider-for-time-based-priority","content":"The second phase including the two custom strategies mentioned above is detailed in this separate design doc."},"14204":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4404","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compressed-blockcache","content":"HBASE-11331 introduced lazy BlockCache decompression, more simply referred to as compressed BlockCache. When compressed BlockCache is enabled data and encoded data blocks are cached in the BlockCache in their on-disk format, rather than being decompressed and decrypted before caching."},"14205":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4405","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compressed-blockcache","content":"For a RegionServer hosting more data than can fit into cache, enabling this feature with SNAPPY compression has been shown to result in 50% increase in throughput and 30% improvement in mean latency while, increasing garbage collection by 80% and increasing overall CPU load by 2%. See HBASE-11331 for more details about how performance was measured and achieved. For a RegionServer hosting data that can comfortably fit into cache, or if your workload is sensitive to extra CPU or garbage-collection load, you may receive less benefit."},"14206":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4406","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compressed-blockcache","content":"The compressed BlockCache is disabled by default. To enable it, set `hbase.block.data.cachecompressed` to `true` in *hbase-site.xml* on all RegionServers."},"14207":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4407","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Depending on the data size and the configured cache size, the cache warm up can take anywhere from a few minutes to a few hours. This becomes even more critical for HBase deployments over cloud storage, where compute is separated from storage. Doing this everytime the region server starts can be a very expensive process. To eliminate this, HBASE-27313 implemented the cache persistence feature where the region servers periodically persist the blocks cached in the bucket cache. This persisted information is then used to resurrect the cache in the event of a region server restart because of normal restart or crash."},"14208":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4408","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"HBASE-27999 implements the cache aware load balancer, which adds to the load balancer the ability to consider the cache allocation of each region on region servers when calculating a new assignment plan, using the region/region server cache allocation information reported by region servers to calculate the percentage of HFiles cached for each region on the hosting server. This information is then used by the balancer as a factor when deciding on an optimal, new assignment plan."},"14209":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4409","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The master node captures the caching information from all the region servers and uses this information to decide on new region assignments while ensuring a minimal impact on the current cache allocation. A region is assigned to the region server where it has a better cache ratio as compared to the region server where it is currently hosted."},"14210":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4410","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The CacheAwareLoadBalancer uses two cost elements for deciding the region allocation. These are described below:"},"14211":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4411","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"**Cache Cost**\\\nThe cache cost is calculated as the percentage of data for a region cached on the region server where it is either currently hosted or was previously hosted. A region may have multiple HFiles, each of different sizes. A HFile is considered to be fully prefetched when all the data blocks in this file are in the cache. The region server hosting this region calculates the ratio of number of HFiles fully cached in the cache to the total number of HFiles in the region. This ratio will vary from 0 (region hosted on this server, but none of its HFiles are cached into the cache) to 1 (region hosted on this server and all the HFiles for this region are cached into the cache).\\\nEvery region server maintains this information for all the regions currently hosted there. In addition to that, this cache ratio is also maintained for the regions which were previously hosted on this region server giving historical information about the regions."},"14212":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4412","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Skewness Cost"},"14213":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4413","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The cache aware balancer will consider cache cost with the skewness cost to decide on the region assignment plan under following conditions:"},"14214":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4414","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"There is an idle server in the cluster. This can happen when an existing server is restarted or a new server is added to the cluster."},"14215":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4415","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"When the cost of maintaining the balance in the cluster is greater than the minimum threshold defined by the configuration *hbase.master.balancer.stochastic.minCostNeedBalance*."},"14216":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4416","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"The CacheAwareLoadBalancer can be enabled in the cluster by setting the following configuration properties in the master master configuration:"},"14217":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4417","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cache-aware-load-balancer","content":"Within HBASE-29168, the CacheAwareLoadBalancer implements region move throttling. This mitigates the impact of \"losing\" cache factor when balancing mainly due to region skewness, i.e. when new region servers are added to the cluster, a large bulk of cached regions may move to the new servers at once, which can cause noticeable read performance impacts for cache sensitive use cases. The throttling sleep time is determined by the **hbase.master.balancer.move.throttlingMillis** property, and it defaults to 60000 millis. If a region planned to be moved has a cache ratio on the target server above the thershold configurable by the **hbase.master.balancer.stochastic.throttling.cacheRatio** property (80% by default), no throttling will be applied in this region move."},"14218":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4418","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"As write requests are handled by the region server, they accumulate in an in-memory storage system called the *memstore*. Once the memstore fills, its content are written to disk as additional store files. This event is called a *memstore flush*. As store files accumulate, the RegionServer will compact them into fewer, larger files. After each flush or compaction finishes, the amount of data stored in the region has changed. The RegionServer consults the region split policy to determine if the region has grown too large or should be split for another policy-specific reason. A region split request is enqueued if the policy recommends it."},"14219":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4419","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"Logically, the process of splitting a region is simple. We find a suitable point in the keyspace of the region where we should divide the region in half, then split the region's data into two new regions at that point. The details of the process however are not simple. When a split happens, the newly created *daughter regions* do not rewrite all the data into new files immediately. Instead, they create small files similar to symbolic link files, named Reference files, which point to either the top or bottom part of the parent store file according to the split point. The reference file is used just like a regular data file, but only half of the records are considered. The region can only be split if there are no more references to the immutable data files of the parent region. Those reference files are cleaned gradually by compactions, so that the region will stop referring to its parents files, and can be split further."},"14220":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4420","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"Although splitting the region is a local decision made by the RegionServer, the split process itself must coordinate with many actors. The RegionServer notifies the Master before and after the split, updates the `.META.` table so that clients can discover the new daughter regions, and rearranges the directory structure and data files in HDFS. Splitting is a multi-task process. To enable rollback in case of an error, the RegionServer keeps an in-memory journal about the execution state. The steps taken by the RegionServer to execute the split are illustrated in the \"RegionServer Split Process\" schema below. Each step is labeled with its step number. Actions from RegionServers or Master are shown in red, while actions from the clients are shown in green."},"14221":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4421","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer decides locally to split the region, and prepares the split. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS STARTED.** As a first step, the RegionServer acquires a shared read lock on the table to prevent schema modifications during the splitting process. Then it creates a znode in zookeeper under `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name`, and sets the znode's state to `SPLITTING`."},"14222":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4422","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The Master learns about this znode, since it has a watcher for the parent `region-in-transition` znode."},"14223":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4423","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates a sub-directory named `.splits` under the parent's `region` directory in HDFS."},"14224":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4424","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer closes the parent region and marks the region as offline in its local data structures. &#x2A;*THE SPLITTING REGION IS NOW OFFLINE.** At this point, client requests coming to the parent region will throw `NotServingRegionException`. The client will retry with some backoff. The closing region is flushed."},"14225":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4425","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates region directories under the `.splits` directory, for daughter regions A and B, and creates necessary data structures. Then it splits the store files, in the sense that it creates two Reference files per store file in the parent region. Those reference files will point to the parent region's files."},"14226":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4426","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer creates the actual region directory in HDFS, and moves the reference files for each daughter."},"14227":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4427","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer sends a `Put` request to the `.META.` table, to set the parent as offline in the `.META.` table and add information about daughter regions. At this point, there won't be individual entries in `.META.` for the daughters. Clients will see that the parent region is split if they scan `.META.`, but won't know about the daughters until they appear in `.META.`. Also, if this `Put` to `.META`. succeeds, the parent will be effectively split. If the RegionServer fails before this RPC succeeds, Master and the next Region Server opening the region will clean dirty state about the region split. After the `.META.` update, though, the region split will be rolled-forward by Master."},"14228":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4428","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer opens daughters A and B in parallel."},"14229":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4429","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer adds the daughters A and B to `.META.`, together with information that it hosts the regions. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT REGIONS (DAUGHTERS WITH REFERENCES TO PARENT) ARE NOW ONLINE.** After this point, clients can discover the new regions and issue requests to them. Clients cache the `.META.` entries locally, but when they make requests to the RegionServer or `.META.`, their caches will be invalidated, and they will learn about the new regions from `.META.`."},"14230":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4430","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"The RegionServer updates znode `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name` in ZooKeeper to state `SPLIT`, so that the master can learn about it. The balancer can freely re-assign the daughter regions to other region servers if necessary. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS NOW FINISHED.**"},"14231":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4431","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regionserver-splitting-implementation","content":"After the split, `.META.` and HDFS will still contain references to the parent region. Those references will be removed when compactions in daughter regions rewrite the data files. Garbage collection tasks in the master periodically check whether the daughter regions still refer to the parent region's files. If not, the parent region will be removed."},"14232":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4432","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#note-on-split-directory-behavior-hbase-26187","content":"The numbered split procedure above documents the original, pre-HBASE-26187 implementation, in\nwhich daughter regions are first created under a `.splits` sub-directory of the parent region\ndirectory. In newer HBase implementations that include\nHBASE-26187, this on-disk layout has changed:\ndaughter region directories are created directly under the table directory, rather than under a\nparent region's `.splits` directory. When reading the numbered procedure for such newer versions,\ninterpret any steps that mention creating or using a `.splits` directory as instead preparing and\npromoting daughter region files directly under the table directory. This update does not change\nthe high-level split semantics (references, meta updates, and online transition of daughters); it\nonly affects where temporary/initial daughter files are prepared in HDFS."},"14233":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4433","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#purpose","content":"The &#x2A;Write Ahead Log (WAL)* records all changes to data in HBase, to file-based storage. Under normal operations, the WAL is not needed because data changes move from the MemStore to StoreFiles. However, if a RegionServer crashes or becomes unavailable before the MemStore is flushed, the WAL ensures that the changes to the data can be replayed. If writing to the WAL fails, the entire operation to modify the data fails."},"14234":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4434","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#purpose","content":"HBase uses an implementation of the WAL interface. Usually, there is only one instance of a WAL per RegionServer. An exception is the RegionServer that is carrying *hbase:meta*; the *meta* table gets its own dedicated WAL. The RegionServer records Puts and Deletes to its WAL, before recording them these Mutations MemStore for the affected Store."},"14235":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4435","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#purpose","content":"Prior to 2.0, the interface for WALs in HBase was named `HLog`. In 0.94, HLog was the name of the\nimplementation of the WAL. You will likely find references to the HLog in documentation tailored\nto these older versions."},"14236":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4436","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#purpose","content":"The WAL resides in HDFS in the &#x2A;/hbase/WALs/* directory, with subdirectories per RegionServer."},"14237":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4437","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#purpose","content":"For more general information about the concept of write ahead logs, see the Wikipedia Write-Ahead Log article."},"14238":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4438","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"In HBase, there are a number of WAL implementations (or 'Providers'). Each is known by a short name label (that unfortunately is not always descriptive). You set the provider in *hbase-site.xml* passing the WAL provider short-name as the value on the *hbase.wal.provider* property (Set the provider for *hbase:meta* using the *hbase.wal.meta\\_provider* property, otherwise it uses the same provider configured by *hbase.wal.provider*)."},"14239":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4439","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"*asyncfs*: The **default**. New since hbase-2.0.0 (HBASE-15536, HBASE-14790). This *AsyncFSWAL* provider, as it identifies itself in RegionServer logs, is built on a new non-blocking dfsclient implementation. It is currently resident in the hbase codebase but intent is to move it back up into HDFS itself. WALs edits are written concurrently (\"fan-out\") style to each of the WAL-block replicas on each DataNode rather than in a chained pipeline as the default client does. Latencies should be better. See Apache HBase Improvements and Practices at Xiaomi at slide 14 onward for more detail on implementation."},"14240":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4440","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"*filesystem*: This was the default in hbase-1.x releases. It is built on the blocking *DFSClient* and writes to replicas in classic *DFSCLient* pipeline mode. In logs it identifies as *FSHLog* or *FSHLogProvider*."},"14241":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4441","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"*multiwal*: This provider is made of multiple instances of *asyncfs* or *filesystem*. See the next section for more on *multiwal*."},"14242":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4442","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"Look for the lines like the below in the RegionServer log to see which provider is in place (The below shows the default AsyncFSWALProvider):"},"14243":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4443","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"As the *AsyncFSWAL* hacks into the internal of DFSClient implementation, it will be easily broken\nby upgrading the hadoop dependencies, even for a simple patch release. So if you do not specify\nthe wal provider explicitly, we will first try to use the *asyncfs*, if failed, we will fall back\nto use *filesystem*. And notice that this may not always work, so if you still have problem\nstarting HBase due to the problem of starting *AsyncFSWAL*, please specify *filesystem* explicitly\nin the config file."},"14244":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4444","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-providers","content":"EC support has been added to hadoop-3.x, and it is incompatible with WAL as the EC output stream\ndoes not support hflush/hsync. In order to create a non-EC file in an EC directory, we need to use\nthe new builder-based create API for *FileSystem*, but it is only introduced in hadoop-2.9+ and\nfor HBase we still need to support hadoop-2.7.x. So please do not enable EC for the WAL directory\nuntil we find a way to deal with it."},"14245":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4445","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiwal","content":"With a single WAL per RegionServer, the RegionServer must write to the WAL serially, because HDFS files must be sequential. This causes the WAL to be a performance bottleneck."},"14246":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4446","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiwal","content":"HBase 1.0 introduces support MultiWal in HBASE-5699. MultiWAL allows a RegionServer to write multiple WAL streams in parallel, by using multiple pipelines in the underlying HDFS instance, which increases total throughput during writes. This parallelization is done by partitioning incoming edits by their Region. Thus, the current implementation will not help with increasing the throughput to a single Region."},"14247":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4447","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#multiwal","content":"RegionServers using the original WAL implementation and those using the MultiWAL implementation can each handle recovery of either set of WALs, so a zero-downtime configuration update is possible through a rolling restart."},"14248":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4448","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-multiwal","content":"To configure MultiWAL for a RegionServer, set the value of the property `hbase.wal.provider` to `multiwal` by pasting in the following XML:"},"14249":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4449","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-multiwal","content":"Restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."},"14250":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4450","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-multiwal","content":"To disable MultiWAL for a RegionServer, unset the property and restart the RegionServer."},"14251":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4451","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-flushing","content":"TODO (describe)."},"14252":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4452","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-splitting","content":"A RegionServer serves many regions. All of the regions in a region server share the same active WAL file. Each edit in the WAL file includes information about which region it belongs to. When a region is opened, the edits in the WAL file which belong to that region need to be replayed. Therefore, edits in the WAL file must be grouped by region so that particular sets can be replayed to regenerate the data in a particular region. The process of grouping the WAL edits by region is called *log splitting*. It is a critical process for recovering data if a region server fails."},"14253":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4453","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-splitting","content":"Log splitting is done by the HMaster during cluster start-up or by the ServerShutdownHandler as a region server shuts down. So that consistency is guaranteed, affected regions are unavailable until data is restored. All WAL edits need to be recovered and replayed before a given region can become available again. As a result, regions affected by log splitting are unavailable until the process completes."},"14254":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4454","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"Renaming the directory is important because a RegionServer may still be up and accepting requests even if the HMaster thinks it is down. If the RegionServer does not respond immediately and does not heartbeat its ZooKeeper session, the HMaster may interpret this as a RegionServer failure. Renaming the logs directory ensures that existing, valid WAL files which are still in use by an active but busy RegionServer are not written to by accident."},"14255":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4455","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"The new directory is named according to the following pattern:"},"14256":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4456","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-hbasewalshostportstartcode-directory-is-renamed","content":"An example of such a renamed directory might look like the following:"},"14257":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4457","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"The log splitter reads the log file one edit entry at a time and puts each edit entry into the buffer corresponding to the edit's region. At the same time, the splitter starts several writer threads. Writer threads pick up a corresponding buffer and write the edit entries in the buffer to a temporary recovered edit file. The temporary edit file is stored to disk with the following naming pattern:"},"14258":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4458","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"This file is used to store all the edits in the WAL log for this region. After log splitting completes, the *.temp* file is renamed to the sequence ID of the first log written to the file."},"14259":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4459","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#each-log-file-is-split-one-at-a-time","content":"To determine whether all edits have been written, the sequence ID is compared to the sequence of the last edit that was written to the HFile. If the sequence of the last edit is greater than or equal to the sequence ID included in the file name, it is clear that all writes from the edit file have been completed."},"14260":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4460","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#after-log-splitting-is-complete-each-affected-region-is-assigned-to-a-regionserver","content":"When the region is opened, the *recovered.edits* folder is checked for recovered edits files. If any such files are present, they are replayed by reading the edits and saving them to the MemStore. After all edit files are replayed, the contents of the MemStore are written to disk (HFile) and the edit files are deleted."},"14261":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4461","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"If you set the `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` option to `true`, errors are treated as follows:"},"14262":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4462","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"Any error encountered during splitting will be logged."},"14263":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4463","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"The problematic WAL log will be moved into the *.corrupt* directory under the hbase `rootdir`,"},"14264":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4464","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"Processing of the WAL will continue"},"14265":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4465","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#handling-of-errors-during-log-splitting","content":"If the `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` option is set to `false`, the default, the exception will be propagated and the split will be logged as failed. See HBASE-2958 When hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors is set to false, we fail the split but that's it. We need to do more than just fail split if this flag is set."},"14266":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4466","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-eofexceptions-are-treated-when-splitting-a-crashed-regionservers-wals","content":"If an EOFException occurs while splitting logs, the split proceeds even when `hbase.hlog.split.skip.errors` is set to `false`. An EOFException while reading the last log in the set of files to split is likely, because the RegionServer was likely in the process of writing a record at the time of a crash. For background, see HBASE-2643 Figure how to deal with eof splitting logs"},"14267":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4467","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#performance-improvements-during-log-splitting","content":"WAL log splitting and recovery can be resource intensive and take a long time, depending on the number of RegionServers involved in the crash and the size of the regions. Distributed log splitting was developed to improve performance during log splitting."},"14268":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4468","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-or-disabling-distributed-log-splitting","content":"Distributed log processing is enabled by default since HBase 0.92. The setting is controlled by the `hbase.master.distributed.log.splitting` property, which can be set to `true` or `false`, but defaults to `true`."},"14269":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4469","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-splitting-based-on-procedurev2","content":"After HBASE-20610, we introduce a new way to do WAL splitting coordination by procedureV2 framework. This can simplify the process of WAL splitting and no need to connect zookeeper any more."},"14270":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4470","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#background-toc","content":"Currently, splitting WAL processes are coordinated by zookeeper. Each region server are trying to grab tasks from zookeeper. And the burden becomes heavier when the number of region server increase."},"14271":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4471","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-on-master-side-toc","content":"During ServerCrashProcedure, SplitWALManager will create one SplitWALProcedure for each WAL file which should be split. Then each SplitWALProcedure will spawn a SplitWalRemoteProcedure to send the request to region server. SplitWALProcedure is a StateMachineProcedure and here is the state transfer diagram."},"14272":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4472","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#implementation-on-region-server-side-toc","content":"Region Server will receive a SplitWALCallable and execute it, which is much more straightforward than before. It will return null if success and return exception if there is any error."},"14273":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4473","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regionserver-wal-splitting-based-on-procedurev2-performance","content":"According to tests on a cluster which has 5 regionserver and 1 master. procedureV2 coordinated WAL splitting has a better performance than ZK coordinated WAL splitting no master when restarting the whole cluster or one region server crashing."},"14274":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4474","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"To enable this feature, first we should ensure our package of HBase already contains these code. If not, please upgrade the package of HBase cluster without any configuration change first. Then change configuration 'hbase.split.wal.zk.coordinated' to false. Rolling upgrade the master with new configuration. Now WAL splitting are handled by our new implementation. But region server are still trying to grab tasks from zookeeper, we can rolling upgrade the region servers with the new configuration to stop that."},"14275":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4475","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Steps as follows:"},"14276":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4476","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade whole cluster to get the new Implementation."},"14277":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4477","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade Master with new configuration 'hbase.split.wal.zk.coordinated'=false."},"14278":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4478","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-this-feature-toc","content":"Upgrade region server to stop grab tasks from zookeeper."},"14279":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4479","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-compression","content":"The content of the WAL can be compressed using LRU Dictionary compression. This can be used to speed up WAL replication to different datanodes. The dictionary can store up to 215 elements; eviction starts after this number is exceeded."},"14280":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4480","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-compression","content":"To enable WAL compression, set the `hbase.regionserver.wal.enablecompression` property to `true`. The default value for this property is `false`. By default, WAL tag compression is turned on when WAL compression is enabled. You can turn off WAL tag compression by setting the `hbase.regionserver.wal.tags.enablecompression` property to 'false'."},"14281":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4481","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#wal-compression","content":"A possible downside to WAL compression is that we lose more data from the last block in the WAL if it is ill-terminated mid-write. If entries in this last block were added with new dictionary entries but we failed persist the amended dictionary because of an abrupt termination, a read of this last block may not be able to resolve last-written entries."},"14282":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4482","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"It is possible to set *durability* on each Mutation or on a Table basis. Options include:"},"14283":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4483","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"*SKIP\\_WAL*: Do not write Mutations to the WAL (See the next section, Disabling the WAL)."},"14284":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4484","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"*ASYNC\\_WAL*: Write the WAL asynchronously; do not hold-up clients waiting on the sync of their write to the filesystem but return immediately. The edit becomes visible. Meanwhile, in the background, the Mutation will be flushed to the WAL at some time later. This option currently may lose data. See HBASE-16689."},"14285":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4485","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"*SYNC\\_WAL*: The **default**. Each edit is sync'd to HDFS before we return success to the client."},"14286":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4486","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"*FSYNC\\_WAL*: Each edit is fsync'd to HDFS and the filesystem before we return success to the client."},"14287":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4487","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#durability","content":"Do not confuse the *ASYNC\\_WAL* option on a Mutation or Table with the *AsyncFSWAL* writer; they are distinct options unfortunately closely named"},"14288":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4488","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"HBASE-17437 added support for specifying a WAL directory outside the HBase root directory or even in a different FileSystem since 1.3.3/2.0+. Some FileSystems (such as Amazon S3) don't support append or consistent writes, in such scenario WAL directory needs to be configured in a different FileSystem to avoid loss of writes."},"14289":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4489","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"Following configurations are added to accomplish this:"},"14290":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4490","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.wal.dir`\\\nThis defines where the root WAL directory is located, could be on a different FileSystem than the root directory. WAL directory can not be set to a subdirectory of the root directory. The default value of this is the root directory if unset."},"14291":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4491","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.rootdir.perms`\\\nConfigures FileSystem permissions to set on the root directory. This is '700' by default."},"14292":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4492","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"`hbase.wal.dir.perms`\\\nConfigures FileSystem permissions to set on the WAL directory FileSystem. This is '700' by default."},"14293":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4493","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-wal-directory","content":"While migrating to custom WAL dir (outside the HBase root directory or a different FileSystem)\nexisting WAL files must be copied manually to new WAL dir, otherwise it may lead to data\nloss/inconsistency as HMaster has no information about previous WAL directory."},"14294":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4494","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disabling-the-wal","content":"It is possible to disable the WAL, to improve performance in certain specific situations. However, disabling the WAL puts your data at risk. The only situation where this is recommended is during a bulk load. This is because, in the event of a problem, the bulk load can be re-run with no risk of data loss."},"14295":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4495","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disabling-the-wal","content":"The WAL is disabled by calling the HBase client field `Mutation.writeToWAL(false)`. Use the `Mutation.setDurability(Durability.SKIP_WAL)` and Mutation.getDurability() methods to set and get the field's value. There is no way to disable the WAL for only a specific table."},"14296":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4496","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disabling-the-wal","content":"If you disable the WAL for anything other than bulk loads, your data is at risk."},"14297":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4497","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regions","content":"Regions are the basic element of availability and distribution for tables, and are comprised of a Store per Column Family. The hierarchy of objects is as follows:"},"14298":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4498","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#regions","content":"For a description of what HBase files look like when written to HDFS, see Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects."},"14299":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4499","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#considerations-for-number-of-regions","content":"In general, HBase is designed to run with a small (20-200) number of relatively large (5-20Gb) regions per server. The considerations for this are as follows:"},"14300":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4500","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"Typically you want to keep your region count low on HBase for numerous reasons. Usually right around 100 regions per RegionServer has yielded the best results. Here are some of the reasons below for keeping region count low:"},"14301":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4501","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"MSLAB (MemStore-local allocation buffer) requires 2MB per MemStore (that's 2MB per family per region). 1000 regions that have 2 families each is 3.9GB of heap used, and it's not even storing data yet. NB: the 2MB value is configurable."},"14302":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4502","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"If you fill all the regions at somewhat the same rate, the global memory usage makes it that it forces tiny flushes when you have too many regions which in turn generates compactions. Rewriting the same data tens of times is the last thing you want. An example is filling 1000 regions (with one family) equally and let's consider a lower bound for global MemStore usage of 5GB (the region server would have a big heap). Once it reaches 5GB it will force flush the biggest region, at that point they should almost all have about 5MB of data so it would flush that amount. 5MB inserted later, it would flush another region that will now have a bit over 5MB of data, and so on. This is currently the main limiting factor for the number of regions; see Number of regions per RS - upper bound for detailed formula."},"14303":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4503","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"The master as is is allergic to tons of regions, and will take a lot of time assigning them and moving them around in batches. The reason is that it's heavy on ZK usage, and it's not very async at the moment (could really be improved — and has been improved a bunch in 0.96 HBase)."},"14304":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4504","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"In older versions of HBase (pre-HFile v2, 0.90 and previous), tons of regions on a few RS can cause the store file index to rise, increasing heap usage and potentially creating memory pressure or OOME on the RSs"},"14305":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4505","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"Another issue is the effect of the number of regions on MapReduce jobs; it is typical to have one mapper per HBase region. Thus, hosting only 5 regions per RS may not be enough to get sufficient number of tasks for a MapReduce job, while 1000 regions will generate far too many tasks."},"14306":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4506","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#why-should-i-keep-my-region-count-low","content":"See Determining region count and size for configuration guidelines."},"14307":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4507","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-assignment","content":"This section describes how Regions are assigned to RegionServers."},"14308":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4508","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"When HBase starts regions are assigned as follows (short version):"},"14309":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4509","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"The Master invokes the `AssignmentManager` upon startup."},"14310":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4510","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"The `AssignmentManager` looks at the existing region assignments in `hbase:meta`."},"14311":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4511","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"If the region assignment is still valid (i.e., if the RegionServer is still online) then the assignment is kept."},"14312":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4512","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"If the assignment is invalid, then the `LoadBalancerFactory` is invoked to assign the region. The load balancer (`StochasticLoadBalancer` by default in HBase 1.0) assign the region to a RegionServer."},"14313":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4513","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#startup","content":"`hbase:meta` is updated with the RegionServer assignment (if needed) and the RegionServer start codes (start time of the RegionServer process) upon region opening by the RegionServer."},"14314":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4514","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"When a RegionServer fails:"},"14315":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4515","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"The regions immediately become unavailable because the RegionServer is down."},"14316":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4516","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"The Master will detect that the RegionServer has failed."},"14317":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4517","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"The region assignments will be considered invalid and will be re-assigned just like the startup sequence."},"14318":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4518","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"In-flight queries are re-tried, and not lost."},"14319":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4519","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#failover","content":"Operations are switched to a new RegionServer within the following amount of time:"},"14320":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4520","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-load-balancing","content":"Regions can be periodically moved by the LoadBalancer."},"14321":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4521","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"HBase maintains a state for each region and persists the state in `hbase:meta`. The state of the `hbase:meta` region itself is persisted in ZooKeeper. You can see the states of regions in transition in the Master web UI. Following is the list of possible region states."},"14322":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4522","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"**Possible Region States:**"},"14323":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4523","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`OFFLINE`: the region is offline and not opening"},"14324":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4524","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`OPENING`: the region is in the process of being opened"},"14325":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4525","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`OPEN`: the region is open and the RegionServer has notified the master"},"14326":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4526","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`FAILED_OPEN`: the RegionServer failed to open the region"},"14327":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4527","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`CLOSING`: the region is in the process of being closed"},"14328":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4528","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`CLOSED`: the RegionServer has closed the region and notified the master"},"14329":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4529","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`FAILED_CLOSE`: the RegionServer failed to close the region"},"14330":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4530","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLITTING`: the RegionServer notified the master that the region is splitting"},"14331":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4531","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLIT`: the RegionServer notified the master that the region has finished splitting"},"14332":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4532","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`SPLITTING_NEW`: this region is being created by a split which is in progress"},"14333":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4533","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGING`: the RegionServer notified the master that this region is being merged with another region"},"14334":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4534","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGED`: the RegionServer notified the master that this region has been merged"},"14335":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4535","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"`MERGING_NEW`: this region is being created by a merge of two regions"},"14336":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4536","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"**Graph Legend:**"},"14337":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4537","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Brown: Offline state, a special state that can be transient (after closed before opening), terminal (regions of disabled tables), or initial (regions of newly created tables)"},"14338":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4538","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Palegreen: Online state that regions can serve requests"},"14339":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4539","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Lightblue: Transient states"},"14340":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4540","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Red: Failure states that need OPS attention"},"14341":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4541","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Gold: Terminal states of regions split/merged"},"14342":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4542","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Grey: Initial states of regions created through split/merge"},"14343":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4543","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"**Transition State Descriptions:**"},"14344":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4544","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"The master moves a region from `OFFLINE` to `OPENING` state and tries to assign the region to a RegionServer. The RegionServer may or may not have received the open region request. The master retries sending the open region request to the RegionServer until the RPC goes through or the master runs out of retries. After the RegionServer receives the open region request, the RegionServer begins opening the region."},"14345":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4545","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the master is running out of retries, the master prevents the RegionServer from opening the region by moving the region to `CLOSING` state and trying to close it, even if the RegionServer is starting to open the region."},"14346":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4546","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"After the RegionServer opens the region, it continues to try to notify the master until the master moves the region to `OPEN` state and notifies the RegionServer. The region is now open."},"14347":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4547","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer cannot open the region, it notifies the master. The master moves the region to `CLOSED` state and tries to open the region on a different RegionServer."},"14348":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4548","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the master cannot open the region on any of a certain number of regions, it moves the region to `FAILED_OPEN` state, and takes no further action until an operator intervenes from the HBase shell, or the server is dead."},"14349":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4549","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"The master moves a region from `OPEN` to `CLOSING` state. The RegionServer holding the region may or may not have received the close region request. The master retries sending the close request to the server until the RPC goes through or the master runs out of retries."},"14350":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4550","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer is not online, or throws `NotServingRegionException`, the master moves the region to `OFFLINE` state and re-assigns it to a different RegionServer."},"14351":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4551","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer is online, but not reachable after the master runs out of retries, the master moves the region to `FAILED_CLOSE` state and takes no further action until an operator intervenes from the HBase shell, or the server is dead."},"14352":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4552","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the RegionServer gets the close region request, it closes the region and notifies the master. The master moves the region to `CLOSED` state and re-assigns it to a different RegionServer."},"14353":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4553","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"Before assigning a region, the master moves the region to `OFFLINE` state automatically if it is in `CLOSED` state."},"14354":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4554","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"When a RegionServer is about to split a region, it notifies the master. The master moves the region to be split from `OPEN` to `SPLITTING` state and add the two new regions to be created to the RegionServer. These two regions are in `SPLITTING_NEW` state initially."},"14355":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4555","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"After notifying the master, the RegionServer starts to split the region. Once past the point of no return, the RegionServer notifies the master again so the master can update the `hbase:meta` table. However, the master does not update the region states until it is notified by the server that the split is done. If the split is successful, the splitting region is moved from `SPLITTING` to `SPLIT` state and the two new regions are moved from `SPLITTING_NEW` to `OPEN` state."},"14356":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4556","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the split fails, the splitting region is moved from `SPLITTING` back to `OPEN` state, and the two new regions which were created are moved from `SPLITTING_NEW` to `OFFLINE` state."},"14357":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4557","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"When a RegionServer is about to merge two regions, it notifies the master first. The master moves the two regions to be merged from `OPEN` to `MERGING` state, and adds the new region which will hold the contents of the merged regions region to the RegionServer. The new region is in `MERGING_NEW` state initially."},"14358":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4558","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"After notifying the master, the RegionServer starts to merge the two regions. Once past the point of no return, the RegionServer notifies the master again so the master can update the META. However, the master does not update the region states until it is notified by the RegionServer that the merge has completed. If the merge is successful, the two merging regions are moved from `MERGING` to `MERGED` state and the new region is moved from `MERGING_NEW` to `OPEN` state."},"14359":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4559","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"If the merge fails, the two merging regions are moved from `MERGING` back to `OPEN` state, and the new region which was created to hold the contents of the merged regions is moved from `MERGING_NEW` to `OFFLINE` state."},"14360":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4560","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-state-transition","content":"For regions in `FAILED_OPEN` or `FAILED_CLOSE` states, the master tries to close them again when they are reassigned by an operator via HBase Shell."},"14361":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4561","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Over time, Region-RegionServer locality is achieved via HDFS block replication. The HDFS client does the following by default when choosing locations to write replicas:"},"14362":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4562","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"First replica is written to local node"},"14363":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4563","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Second replica is written to a random node on another rack"},"14364":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4564","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Third replica is written on the same rack as the second, but on a different node chosen randomly"},"14365":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4565","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Subsequent replicas are written on random nodes on the cluster. See *Replica Placement: The First Baby Steps* on this page: HDFS Architecture"},"14366":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4566","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"Thus, HBase eventually achieves locality for a region after a flush or a compaction. In a RegionServer failover situation a RegionServer may be assigned regions with non-local StoreFiles (because none of the replicas are local), however as new data is written in the region, or the table is compacted and StoreFiles are re-written, they will become \"local\" to the RegionServer."},"14367":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4567","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-regionserver-locality","content":"For more information, see *Replica Placement: The First Baby Steps* on this page: HDFS Architecture and also Lars George's blog on HBase and HDFS locality."},"14368":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4568","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-splits","content":"Regions split when they reach a configured threshold. Below we treat the topic in short. For a longer exposition, see Apache HBase Region Splitting and Merging by our Enis Soztutar."},"14369":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4569","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-splits","content":"Splits run unaided on the RegionServer; i.e. the Master does not participate. The RegionServer splits a region, offlines the split region and then adds the daughter regions to `hbase:meta`, opens daughters on the parent's hosting RegionServer and then reports the split to the Master. See Managed Splitting for how to manually manage splits (and for why you might do this)."},"14370":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4570","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-split-policies","content":"You can override the default split policy using a custom RegionSplitPolicy (HBase 0.94+). Typically a custom split policy should extend HBase's default split policy: IncreasingToUpperBoundRegionSplitPolicy."},"14371":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4571","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#custom-split-policies","content":"The policy can set globally through the HBase configuration or on a per-table basis."},"14372":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4572","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-the-split-policy-on-a-table-using-hbase-shell","content":"The policy can be set globally through the HBaseConfiguration used or on a per table basis:"},"14373":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4573","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-the-split-policy-on-a-table-using-hbase-shell","content":"The `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` policy blocks manual region splitting."},"14374":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4574","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"It is possible to manually split your table, either at table creation (pre-splitting), or at a later time as an administrative action. You might choose to split your region for one or more of the following reasons. There may be other valid reasons, but the need to manually split your table might also point to problems with your schema design."},"14375":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4575","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"**Reasons to Manually Split Your Table:**"},"14376":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4576","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"Your data is sorted by timeseries or another similar algorithm that sorts new data at the end of the table. This means that the Region Server holding the last region is always under load, and the other Region Servers are idle, or mostly idle. See also Monotonically Increasing Row Keys/Timeseries Data."},"14377":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4577","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"You have developed an unexpected hotspot in one region of your table. For instance, an application which tracks web searches might be inundated by a lot of searches for a celebrity in the event of news about that celebrity. See perf.one.region for more discussion about this particular scenario."},"14378":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4578","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"After a big increase in the number of RegionServers in your cluster, to get the load spread out quickly."},"14379":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4579","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"Before a bulk-load which is likely to cause unusual and uneven load across regions."},"14380":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4580","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"See Managed Splitting for a discussion about the dangers and possible benefits of managing splitting completely manually."},"14381":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4581","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manual-region-splitting","content":"The `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` policy blocks manual region splitting."},"14382":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4582","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#determining-split-points","content":"The goal of splitting your table manually is to improve the chances of balancing the load across the cluster in situations where good rowkey design alone won't get you there. Keeping that in mind, the way you split your regions is very dependent upon the characteristics of your data. It may be that you already know the best way to split your table. If not, the way you split your table depends on what your keys are like."},"14383":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4583","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#determining-split-points","content":"**Alphanumeric Rowkeys**\\\nIf your rowkeys start with a letter or number, you can split your table at letter or number boundaries. For instance, the following command creates a table with regions that split at each vowel, so the first region has A-D, the second region has E-H, the third region has I-N, the fourth region has O-V, and the fifth region has U-Z."},"14384":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4584","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#determining-split-points","content":"**Using a Custom Algorithm**\\\nThe RegionSplitter tool is provided with HBase, and uses a *SplitAlgorithm* to determine split points for you. As parameters, you give it the algorithm, desired number of regions, and column families. It includes three split algorithms. The first is the `HexStringSplit` algorithm, which assumes the row keys are hexadecimal strings. The second is the `DecimalStringSplit` algorithm, which assumes the row keys are decimal strings in the range 00000000 to 99999999. The third, `UniformSplit`, assumes the row keys are random byte arrays. You will probably need to develop your own `SplitAlgorithm`, using the provided ones as models."},"14385":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4585","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#online-region-merges","content":"Both Master and RegionServer participate in the event of online region merges. Client sends merge RPC to the master, then the master moves the regions together to the RegionServer where the more heavily loaded region resided. Finally the master sends the merge request to this RegionServer which then runs the merge. Similar to process of region splitting, region merges run as a local transaction on the RegionServer. It offlines the regions and then merges two regions on the file system, atomically delete merging regions from `hbase:meta` and adds the merged region to `hbase:meta`, opens the merged region on the RegionServer and reports the merge to the Master."},"14386":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4586","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#online-region-merges","content":"An example of region merges in the HBase shell"},"14387":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4587","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#online-region-merges","content":"It's an asynchronous operation and call returns immediately without waiting merge completed. Passing `true` as the optional third parameter will force a merge. Normally only adjacent regions can be merged. The `force` parameter overrides this behaviour and is for expert use only."},"14388":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4588","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#store","content":"A Store hosts a MemStore and 0 or more StoreFiles (HFiles). A Store corresponds to a column family for a table for a given region."},"14389":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4589","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstore","content":"The MemStore holds in-memory modifications to the Store. Modifications are Cells/KeyValues. When a flush is requested, the current MemStore is moved to a snapshot and is cleared. HBase continues to serve edits from the new MemStore and backing snapshot until the flusher reports that the flush succeeded. At this point, the snapshot is discarded. Note that when the flush happens, MemStores that belong to the same region will all be flushed."},"14390":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4590","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstore-flush","content":"A MemStore flush can be triggered under any of the conditions listed below. The minimum flush unit is per region, not at individual MemStore level."},"14391":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4591","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstore-flush","content":"When a MemStore reaches the size specified by `hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size`, all MemStores that belong to its region will be flushed out to disk."},"14392":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4592","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstore-flush","content":"When the overall MemStore usage reaches the value specified by `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.upperLimit`, MemStores from various regions will be flushed out to disk to reduce overall MemStore usage in a RegionServer.\\\nThe flush order is based on the descending order of a region's MemStore usage. Regions will have their MemStores flushed until the overall MemStore usage drops to or slightly below `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.lowerLimit`."},"14393":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4593","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memstore-flush","content":"When the number of WAL log entries in a given region server's WAL reaches the value specified in `hbase.regionserver.max.logs`, MemStores from various regions will be flushed out to disk to reduce the number of logs in the WAL.\\\nThe flush order is based on time. Regions with the oldest MemStores are flushed first until WAL count drops below `hbase.regionserver.max.logs`."},"14394":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4594","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"When a client issues a scan against a table, HBase generates `RegionScanner` objects, one per region, to serve the scan request."},"14395":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4595","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"The `RegionScanner` object contains a list of `StoreScanner` objects, one per column family."},"14396":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4596","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"Each `StoreScanner` object further contains a list of `StoreFileScanner` objects, corresponding to each StoreFile and HFile of the corresponding column family, and a list of `KeyValueScanner` objects for the MemStore."},"14397":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4597","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"The two lists are merged into one, which is sorted in ascending order with the scan object for the MemStore at the end of the list."},"14398":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4598","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-scans","content":"When a `StoreFileScanner` object is constructed, it is associated with a `MultiVersionConcurrencyControl` read point, which is the current `memstoreTS`, filtering out any new updates beyond the read point."},"14399":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4599","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storefile-hfile","content":"StoreFiles are where your data lives."},"14400":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4600","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-hfile-format","content":"The *HFile* file format is based on the SSTable file described in the BigTable \\[2006] paper and on Hadoop's TFile (The unit test suite and the compression harness were taken directly from TFile). Schubert Zhang's blog post on HFile: A Block-Indexed File Format to Store Sorted Key-Value Pairs makes for a thorough introduction to HBase's HFile. Matteo Bertozzi has also put up a helpful description, HBase I/O: HFile."},"14401":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4601","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-hfile-format","content":"For more information, see the HFile source code. Also see HBase file format with inline blocks (version 2) for information about the HFile v2 format that was included in 0.92."},"14402":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4602","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"To view a textualized version of HFile content, you can use the `hbase hfile` tool. Type the following to see usage:"},"14403":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4603","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"For example, to view the content of the file *hdfs\\://10.81.47.41:9000/hbase/default/TEST/1418428042/DSMP/4759508618286845475*, type the following:"},"14404":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4604","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"If you leave off the option -v to see just a summary on the HFile. See usage for other things to do with the `hfile` tool."},"14405":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4605","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-hfile-tool","content":"In the output of this tool, you might see 'seqid=0' for certain keys in places such as\n'Mid-key'/'firstKey'/'lastKey'. These are 'KeyOnlyKeyValue' type instances - meaning their seqid\nis irrelevant & we just need the keys of these Key-Value instances."},"14406":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4606","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storefile-directory-structure-on-hdfs","content":"For more information of what StoreFiles look like on HDFS with respect to the directory structure, see Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects."},"14407":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4607","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#blocks","content":"StoreFiles are composed of blocks. The blocksize is configured on a per-ColumnFamily basis."},"14408":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4608","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#blocks","content":"Compression happens at the block level within StoreFiles. For more information on compression, see Compression and Data Block Encoding In HBase."},"14409":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4609","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#blocks","content":"For more information on blocks, see the HFileBlock source code."},"14410":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4610","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"The KeyValue class is the heart of data storage in HBase. KeyValue wraps a byte array and takes offsets and lengths into the passed array which specify where to start interpreting the content as KeyValue."},"14411":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4611","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"The KeyValue format inside a byte array is:"},"14412":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4612","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"keylength"},"14413":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4613","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"valuelength"},"14414":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4614","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"key"},"14415":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4615","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"value"},"14416":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4616","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"The Key is further decomposed as:"},"14417":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4617","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"rowlength"},"14418":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4618","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"row (i.e., the rowkey)"},"14419":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4619","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"columnfamilylength"},"14420":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4620","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"columnfamily"},"14421":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4621","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"columnqualifier"},"14422":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4622","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"timestamp"},"14423":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4623","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"keytype (e.g., Put, Delete, DeleteColumn, DeleteFamily)"},"14424":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4624","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#keyvalue","content":"KeyValue instances are *not* split across blocks. For example, if there is an 8 MB KeyValue, even if the block-size is 64kb this KeyValue will be read in as a coherent block. For more information, see the KeyValue source code."},"14425":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4625","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"To emphasize the points above, examine what happens with two Puts for two different columns for the same row:"},"14426":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4626","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Put #1: `rowkey=row1, cf:attr1=value1`"},"14427":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4627","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Put #2: `rowkey=row1, cf:attr2=value2`"},"14428":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4628","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Even though these are for the same row, a KeyValue is created for each column:"},"14429":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4629","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Key portion for Put #1:"},"14430":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4630","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`rowlength -----------→ 4`"},"14431":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4631","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`row -----------------→ row1`"},"14432":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4632","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamilylength --→ 2`"},"14433":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4633","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamily --------→ cf`"},"14434":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4634","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnqualifier -----→ attr1`"},"14435":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4635","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`timestamp -----------→ server time of Put`"},"14436":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4636","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`keytype -------------→ Put`"},"14437":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4637","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"Key portion for Put #2:"},"14438":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4638","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`rowlength -----------→ 4`"},"14439":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4639","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`row -----------------→ row1`"},"14440":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4640","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamilylength --→ 2`"},"14441":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4641","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnfamily --------→ cf`"},"14442":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4642","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`columnqualifier -----→ attr2`"},"14443":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4643","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`timestamp -----------→ server time of Put`"},"14444":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4644","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"`keytype -------------→ Put`"},"14445":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4645","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-regions-store-keyvalue-example","content":"It is critical to understand that the rowkey, ColumnFamily, and column (aka columnqualifier) are embedded within the KeyValue instance. The longer these identifiers are, the bigger the KeyValue is."},"14446":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4646","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"**Ambiguous Terminology:**"},"14447":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4647","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"A *StoreFile* is a facade of HFile. In terms of compaction, use of StoreFile seems to have prevailed in the past."},"14448":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4648","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"A *Store* is the same thing as a ColumnFamily. StoreFiles are related to a Store, or ColumnFamily."},"14449":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4649","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"If you want to read more about StoreFiles versus HFiles and Stores versus ColumnFamilies, see HBASE-11316."},"14450":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4650","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"When the MemStore reaches a given size (`hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size`), it flushes its contents to a StoreFile. The number of StoreFiles in a Store increases over time. *Compaction* is an operation which reduces the number of StoreFiles in a Store, by merging them together, in order to increase performance on read operations. Compactions can be resource-intensive to perform, and can either help or hinder performance depending on many factors."},"14451":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4651","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"Compactions fall into two categories: minor and major. Minor and major compactions differ in the following ways."},"14452":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4652","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"*Minor compactions* usually select a small number of small, adjacent StoreFiles and rewrite them as a single StoreFile. Minor compactions do not drop (filter out) deletes or expired versions, because of potential side effects. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions. The end result of a minor compaction is fewer, larger StoreFiles for a given Store."},"14453":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4653","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction","content":"The end result of a *major compaction* is a single StoreFile per Store. Major compactions also process delete markers and max versions. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions."},"14454":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4654","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-and-deletions","content":"When an explicit deletion occurs in HBase, the data is not actually deleted. Instead, a *tombstone* marker is written. The tombstone marker prevents the data from being returned with queries. During a major compaction, the data is actually deleted, and the tombstone marker is removed from the StoreFile. If the deletion happens because of an expired TTL, no tombstone is created. Instead, the expired data is filtered out and is not written back to the compacted StoreFile."},"14455":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4655","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-and-versions","content":"When you create a Column Family, you can specify the maximum number of versions to keep, by specifying `ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder.setMaxVersions(int versions)`. The default value is `1`. If more versions than the specified maximum exist, the excess versions are filtered out and not written back to the compacted StoreFile."},"14456":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4656","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-and-versions","content":"In some situations, older versions can be inadvertently resurrected if a newer version is\nexplicitly deleted. See Major compactions change query\nresults for a more in-depth explanation.\nThis situation is only possible before the compaction finishes."},"14457":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4657","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-and-versions","content":"In theory, major compactions improve performance. However, on a highly loaded system, major compactions can require an inappropriate number of resources and adversely affect performance. In a default configuration, major compactions are scheduled automatically to run once in a 7-day period. This is sometimes inappropriate for systems in production. You can manage major compactions manually. See Managed Compactions."},"14458":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4658","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-and-versions","content":"Compactions do not perform region merges. See Merge for more information on region merging."},"14459":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4659","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-switch","content":"We can switch on and off the compactions at region servers. Switching off compactions will also interrupt any currently ongoing compactions. It can be done dynamically using the \"compaction\\_switch\" command from hbase shell. If done from the command line, this setting will be lost on restart of the server. To persist the changes across region servers modify the configuration hbase.regionserver .compaction.enabled in hbase-site.xml and restart HBase."},"14460":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4660","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Compacting large StoreFiles, or too many StoreFiles at once, can cause more IO load than your cluster is able to handle without causing performance problems. The method by which HBase selects which StoreFiles to include in a compaction (and whether the compaction is a minor or major compaction) is called the *compaction policy*."},"14461":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4661","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Prior to HBase 0.96.x, there was only one compaction policy. That original compaction policy is still available as `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy`. The new compaction default policy, called `ExploringCompactionPolicy`, was subsequently backported to HBase 0.94 and HBase 0.95, and is the default in HBase 0.96 and newer. It was implemented in HBASE-7842. In short, `ExploringCompactionPolicy` attempts to select the best possible set of StoreFiles to compact with the least amount of work, while the `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` selects the first set that meets the criteria."},"14462":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4662","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-policy---hbase-096x-and-newer","content":"Regardless of the compaction policy used, file selection is controlled by several configurable parameters and happens in a multi-step approach. These parameters will be explained in context, and then will be given in a table which shows their descriptions, defaults, and implications of changing them."},"14463":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4663","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#being-stuck","content":"When the MemStore gets too large, it needs to flush its contents to a StoreFile. However, Stores are configured with a bound on the number StoreFiles, `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles`, and if in excess, the MemStore flush must wait until the StoreFile count is reduced by one or more compactions. If the MemStore is too large and the number of StoreFiles is also too high, the algorithm is said to be \"stuck\". By default we'll wait on compactions up to `hbase.hstore.blockingWaitTime` milliseconds. If this period expires, we'll flush anyways even though we are in excess of the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` count."},"14464":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4664","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#being-stuck","content":"Upping the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` count will allow flushes to happen but a Store with many StoreFiles in will likely have higher read latencies. Try to figure why Compactions are not keeping up. Is it a write spurt that is bringing about this situation or is a regular occurance and the cluster is under-provisioned for the volume of writes?"},"14465":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4665","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The ExploringCompactionPolicy algorithm considers each possible set of adjacent StoreFiles before choosing the set where compaction will have the most benefit."},"14466":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4666","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"One situation where the ExploringCompactionPolicy works especially well is when you are bulk-loading data and the bulk loads create larger StoreFiles than the StoreFiles which are holding data older than the bulk-loaded data. This can \"trick\" HBase into choosing to perform a major compaction each time a compaction is needed, and cause a lot of extra overhead. With the ExploringCompactionPolicy, major compactions happen much less frequently because minor compactions are more efficient."},"14467":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4667","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"In general, ExploringCompactionPolicy is the right choice for most situations, and thus is the default compaction policy. You can also use ExploringCompactionPolicy along with Experimental: Stripe Compactions."},"14468":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4668","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The logic of this policy can be examined in hbase-server/src/main/java/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/regionserver/compactions/ExploringCompactionPolicy.java. The following is a walk-through of the logic of the ExploringCompactionPolicy."},"14469":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4669","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Make a list of all existing StoreFiles in the Store. The rest of the algorithm filters this list to come up with the subset of HFiles which will be chosen for compaction."},"14470":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4670","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If this was a user-requested compaction, attempt to perform the requested compaction type, regardless of what would normally be chosen. Note that even if the user requests a major compaction, it may not be possible to perform a major compaction. This may be because not all StoreFiles in the Column Family are available to compact or because there are too many Stores in the Column Family."},"14471":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4671","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Some StoreFiles are automatically excluded from consideration. These include:"},"14472":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4672","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that are larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`"},"14473":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4673","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that were created by a bulk-load operation which explicitly excluded compaction. You may decide to exclude StoreFiles resulting from bulk loads, from compaction. To do this, specify the `hbase.mapreduce.hfileoutputformat.compaction.exclude` parameter during the bulk load operation."},"14474":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4674","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Iterate through the list from step 1, and make a list of all potential sets of StoreFiles to compact together. A potential set is a grouping of `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` contiguous StoreFiles in the list. For each set, perform some sanity-checking and figure out whether this is the best compaction that could be done:"},"14475":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4675","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the number of StoreFiles in this set (not the size of the StoreFiles) is fewer than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` or more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`, take it out of consideration."},"14476":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4676","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Compare the size of this set of StoreFiles with the size of the smallest possible compaction that has been found in the list so far. If the size of this set of StoreFiles represents the smallest compaction that could be done, store it to be used as a fall-back if the algorithm is \"stuck\" and no StoreFiles would otherwise be chosen. See Being Stuck."},"14477":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4677","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Do size-based sanity checks against each StoreFile in this set of StoreFiles."},"14478":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4678","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the size of this StoreFile is larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`, take it out of consideration."},"14479":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4679","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the size is greater than or equal to `hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size`, sanity-check it against the file-based ratio to see whether it is too large to be considered."},"14480":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4680","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The sanity-checking is successful if:"},"14481":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4681","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"There is only one StoreFile in this set, or"},"14482":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4682","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"For each StoreFile, its size multiplied by `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` (or `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak` if off-peak hours are configured and it is during off-peak hours) is less than the sum of the sizes of the other HFiles in the set."},"14483":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4683","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If this set of StoreFiles is still in consideration, compare it to the previously-selected best compaction. If it is better, replace the previously-selected best compaction with this one."},"14484":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4684","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#the-exploringcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"When the entire list of potential compactions has been processed, perform the best compaction that was found. If no StoreFiles were selected for compaction, but there are multiple StoreFiles, assume the algorithm is stuck (see Being Stuck) and if so, perform the smallest compaction that was found in step 3."},"14485":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4685","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The RatioBasedCompactionPolicy was the only compaction policy prior to HBase 0.96, though ExploringCompactionPolicy has now been backported to HBase 0.94 and 0.95. To use the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy rather than the ExploringCompactionPolicy, set `hbase.hstore.defaultengine.compactionpolicy.class` to `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` in the *hbase-site.xml* file. To switch back to the ExploringCompactionPolicy, remove the setting from the *hbase-site.xml*."},"14486":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4686","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The following section walks you through the algorithm used to select StoreFiles for compaction in the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy."},"14487":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4687","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The first phase is to create a list of all candidates for compaction. A list is created of all StoreFiles not already in the compaction queue, and all StoreFiles newer than the newest file that is currently being compacted. This list of StoreFiles is ordered by the sequence ID. The sequence ID is generated when a Put is appended to the write-ahead log (WAL), and is stored in the metadata of the HFile."},"14488":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4688","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Check to see if the algorithm is stuck (see Being Stuck, and if so, a major compaction is forced. This is a key area where The ExploringCompactionPolicy Algorithm is often a better choice than the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy."},"14489":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4689","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the compaction was user-requested, try to perform the type of compaction that was requested. Note that a major compaction may not be possible if all HFiles are not available for compaction or if too many StoreFiles exist (more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`)."},"14490":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4690","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"Some StoreFiles are automatically excluded from consideration. These include:"},"14491":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4691","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that are larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`"},"14492":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4692","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"StoreFiles that were created by a bulk-load operation which explicitly excluded compaction. You may decide to exclude StoreFiles resulting from bulk loads, from compaction. To do this, specify the `hbase.mapreduce.hfileoutputformat.compaction.exclude` parameter during the bulk load operation."},"14493":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4693","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The maximum number of StoreFiles allowed in a major compaction is controlled by the `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` parameter. If the list contains more than this number of StoreFiles, a minor compaction is performed even if a major compaction would otherwise have been done. However, a user-requested major compaction still occurs even if there are more than `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` StoreFiles to compact."},"14494":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4694","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the list contains fewer than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` StoreFiles to compact, a minor compaction is aborted. Note that a major compaction can be performed on a single HFile. Its function is to remove deletes and expired versions, and reset locality on the StoreFile."},"14495":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4695","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"The value of the `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` parameter is multiplied by the sum of StoreFiles smaller than a given file, to determine whether that StoreFile is selected for compaction during a minor compaction. For instance, if hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio is 1.2, FileX is 5MB, FileY is 2MB, and FileZ is 3MB:"},"14496":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4696","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"In this scenario, FileX is eligible for minor compaction. If FileX were 7MB, it would not be eligible for minor compaction. This ratio favors smaller StoreFile. You can configure a different ratio for use in off-peak hours, using the parameter `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak`, if you also configure `hbase.offpeak.start.hour` and `hbase.offpeak.end.hour`."},"14497":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4697","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ratiobasedcompactionpolicy-algorithm","content":"If the last major compaction was too long ago and there is more than one StoreFile to be compacted, a major compaction is run, even if it would otherwise have been minor. By default, the maximum time between major compactions is 7 days, plus or minus a 4.8 hour period, and determined randomly within those parameters. Prior to HBase 0.96, the major compaction period was 24 hours. See `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction` in the table below to tune or disable time-based major compactions."},"14498":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4698","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"This table contains the main configuration parameters for compaction. This list is not exhaustive. To tune these parameters from the defaults, edit the *hbase-default.xml* file. For a full list of all configuration parameters available, see config.files"},"14499":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4699","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min`\\\nThe minimum number of StoreFiles which must be eligible for compaction before compaction can run. The goal of tuning `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` is to avoid ending up with too many tiny StoreFiles to compact. Setting this value to 2 would cause a minor compaction each time you have two StoreFiles in a Store, and this is probably not appropriate. If you set this value too high, all the other values will need to be adjusted accordingly. For most cases, the default value is appropriate. In previous versions of HBase, the parameter `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` was called `hbase.hstore.compactionThreshold`.\\\n**Default**: 3"},"14500":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4700","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max`\nThe maximum number of StoreFiles which will be selected for a single minor compaction, regardless of the number of eligible StoreFiles. Effectively, the value of `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` controls the length of time it takes a single compaction to complete. Setting it larger means that more StoreFiles are included in a compaction. For most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: 10"},"14501":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4701","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size`\\\nA StoreFile smaller than this size will always be eligible for minor compaction. StoreFiles this size or larger are evaluated by `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` to determine if they are eligible. Because this limit represents the \"automatic include\" limit for all StoreFiles smaller than this value, this value may need to be reduced in write-heavy environments where many files in the 1-2 MB range are being flushed, because every StoreFile will be targeted for compaction and the resulting StoreFiles may still be under the minimum size and require further compaction. If this parameter is lowered, the ratio check is triggered more quickly. This addressed some issues seen in earlier versions of HBase but changing this parameter is no longer necessary in most situations.\\\n**Default**:128 MB"},"14502":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4702","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size`\\\nA StoreFile larger than this size will be excluded from compaction. The effect of raising `hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` is fewer, larger StoreFiles that do not get compacted often. If you feel that compaction is happening too often without much benefit, you can try raising this value.\\\n**Default**: `Long.MAX_VALUE`"},"14503":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4703","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`\\\nFor minor compaction, this ratio is used to determine whether a given StoreFile which is larger than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` is eligible for compaction. Its effect is to limit compaction of large StoreFile. The value of `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` is expressed as a floating-point decimal."},"14504":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4704","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"A large ratio, such as 10, will produce a single giant StoreFile. Conversely, a value of .25, will produce behavior similar to the BigTable compaction algorithm, producing four StoreFiles."},"14505":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4705","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"A moderate value of between 1.0 and 1.4 is recommended. When tuning this value, you are balancing write costs with read costs. Raising the value (to something like 1.4) will have more write costs, because you will compact larger StoreFiles. However, during reads, HBase will need to seek through fewer StoreFiles to accomplish the read. Consider this approach if you cannot take advantage of Bloom Filters."},"14506":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4706","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"Alternatively, you can lower this value to something like 1.0 to reduce the background cost of writes, and use to limit the number of StoreFiles touched during reads. For most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: `1.2F`"},"14507":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4707","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio.offpeak`\\\nThe compaction ratio used during off-peak compactions, if off-peak hours are also configured (see below). Expressed as a floating-point decimal. This allows for more aggressive (or less aggressive, if you set it lower than `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`) compaction during a set time period. Ignored if off-peak is disabled (default). This works the same as `hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`.\\\n**Default**: `5.0F`"},"14508":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4708","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.offpeak.start.hour`\\\nThe start of off-peak hours, expressed as an integer between 0 and 23, inclusive. Set to -1 to disable off-peak.\\\n**Default**: `-1` (disabled)"},"14509":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4709","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.offpeak.end.hour`\\\nThe end of off-peak hours, expressed as an integer between 0 and 23, inclusive. Set to -1 to disable off-peak.\\\n**Default**: `-1` (disabled)"},"14510":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4710","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.regionserver.thread.compaction.throttle`\\\nThere are two different thread pools for compactions, one for large compactions and the other for small compactions. This helps to keep compaction of lean tables (such as `hbase:meta`) fast. If a compaction is larger than this threshold, it goes into the large compaction pool. In most cases, the default value is appropriate.\\\n**Default**: `2 x hbase.hstore.compaction.max x hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size` (which defaults to `128`)"},"14511":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4711","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hregion.majorcompaction`\\\nTime between major compactions, expressed in milliseconds. Set to 0 to disable time-based automatic major compactions. User-requested and size-based major compactions will still run. This value is multiplied by `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction.jitter` to cause compaction to start at a somewhat-random time during a given window of time.\\\n**Default**: 7 days (`604800000` milliseconds)"},"14512":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4712","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#parameters-used-by-compaction-algorithm","content":"`hbase.hregion.majorcompaction.jitter`\\\nA multiplier applied to hbase.hregion.majorcompaction to cause compaction to occur a given amount of time either side of `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction`. The smaller the number, the closer the compactions will happen to the `hbase.hregion.majorcompaction` interval. Expressed as a floating-point decimal.\\\n**Default**: `.50F`"},"14513":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4713","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"This section has been preserved for historical reasons and refers to the way compaction worked\nprior to HBase 0.96.x. You can still use this behavior if you enable RatioBasedCompactionPolicy\nAlgorithm. For information on\nthe way that compactions work in HBase 0.96.x and later, see\nCompaction."},"14514":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4714","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"To understand the core algorithm for StoreFile selection, there is some ASCII-art in the Store source code that will serve as useful reference."},"14515":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4715","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"It has been copied below:"},"14516":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4716","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"**Important knobs:**"},"14517":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4717","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` Ratio used in compaction file selection algorithm (default 1.2f)."},"14518":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4718","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` (in HBase v 0.90 this is called `hbase.hstore.compactionThreshold`) (files) Minimum number of StoreFiles per Store to be selected for a compaction to occur (default 2)."},"14519":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4719","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` (files) Maximum number of StoreFiles to compact per minor compaction (default 10)."},"14520":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4720","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` (bytes) Any StoreFile smaller than this setting with automatically be a candidate for compaction. Defaults to `hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size` (128 mb)."},"14521":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4721","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` (.92) (bytes) Any StoreFile larger than this setting with automatically be excluded from compaction (default Long.MAX\\_VALUE)."},"14522":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4722","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compaction-file-selection","content":"The minor compaction StoreFile selection logic is size based, and selects a file for compaction when the `file ⇐ sum(smaller_files) * hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio`."},"14523":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4723","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"14524":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4724","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"14525":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4725","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"14526":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4726","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"14527":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4727","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"14528":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4728","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"14529":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4729","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 100, 50, 23, 12, and 12 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, the files that would be selected for minor compaction are 23, 12, and 12."},"14530":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4730","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"Why?"},"14531":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4731","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"100 → No, because sum(50, 23, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 97."},"14532":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4732","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"50 → No, because sum(23, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 47."},"14533":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4733","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"23 → Yes, because sum(12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 24."},"14534":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4734","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"12 → Yes, because the previous file has been included, and because this does not exceed the max-file limit of 5"},"14535":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4735","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-1-basic-example","content":"12 → Yes, because the previous file had been included, and because this does not exceed the max-file limit of 5."},"14536":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4736","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"14537":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4737","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"14538":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4738","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"14539":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4739","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"14540":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4740","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"14541":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4741","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"14542":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4742","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 100, 25, 12, and 12 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, no compaction will be started."},"14543":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4743","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"Why?"},"14544":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4744","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"100 → No, because sum(25, 12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 47"},"14545":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4745","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"25 → No, because sum(12, 12) \\* 1.0 = 24"},"14546":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4746","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"12 → No. Candidate because sum(12) \\* 1.0 = 12, there are only 2 files to compact and that is less than the threshold of 3"},"14547":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4747","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-2-not-enough-files-tocompact","content":"12 → No. Candidate because the previous StoreFile was, but there are not enough files to compact"},"14548":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4748","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"This example mirrors an example from the unit test `TestCompactSelection`."},"14549":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4749","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.ratio` = 1.0f"},"14550":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4750","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min` = 3 (files)"},"14551":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4751","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max` = 5 (files)"},"14552":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4752","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.min.size` = 10 (bytes)"},"14553":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4753","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.max.size` = 1000 (bytes)"},"14554":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4754","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"The following StoreFiles exist: 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, and 1 bytes apiece (oldest to newest). With the above parameters, the files that would be selected for minor compaction are 7, 6, 5, 4, 3."},"14555":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4755","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"Why?"},"14556":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4756","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"7 → Yes, because sum(6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 21. Also, 7 is less than the min-size"},"14557":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4757","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"6 → Yes, because sum(5, 4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 15. Also, 6 is less than the min-size."},"14558":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4758","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"5 → Yes, because sum(4, 3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 10. Also, 5 is less than the min-size."},"14559":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4759","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"4 → Yes, because sum(3, 2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 6. Also, 4 is less than the min-size."},"14560":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4760","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"3 → Yes, because sum(2, 1) \\* 1.0 = 3. Also, 3 is less than the min-size."},"14561":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4761","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"2 → No. Candidate because previous file was selected and 2 is less than the min-size, but the max-number of files to compact has been reached."},"14562":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4762","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"1 → No. Candidate because previous file was selected and 1 is less than the min-size, but max-number of files to compact has been reached."},"14563":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4763","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#minor-compaction-file-selection---example-3-limiting-files-to-compact","content":"This information is now included in the configuration parameter table in Parameters Used by\nCompaction Algorithm."},"14564":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4764","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#date-tiered-compaction","content":"Date tiered compaction is a date-aware store file compaction strategy that is beneficial for time-range scans for time-series data."},"14565":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4765","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Consider using Date Tiered Compaction for reads for limited time ranges, especially scans of recent data"},"14566":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4766","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Don't use it for"},"14567":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4767","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"random gets without a limited time range"},"14568":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4768","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"frequent deletes and updates"},"14569":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4769","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"Frequent out of order data writes creating long tails, especially writes with future timestamps"},"14570":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4770","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"frequent bulk loads with heavily overlapping time ranges"},"14571":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4771","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-date-tiered-compactions","content":"**Performance Improvements**\\\nPerformance testing has shown that the performance of time-range scans improve greatly for limited time ranges, especially scans of recent data."},"14572":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4772","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You can enable Date Tiered compaction for a table or a column family, by setting its `hbase.hstore.engine.class` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DateTieredStoreEngine`."},"14573":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4773","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You also need to set `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to a high number, such as 60, if using all default settings, rather than the default value of 12). Use 1.5\\~2 x projected file count if changing the parameters, Projected file count = windows per tier x tier count + incoming window min + files older than max age"},"14574":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4774","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"You also need to set `hbase.hstore.compaction.max` to the same value as `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to unblock major compaction."},"14575":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4775","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Enable Date Tiered Compaction**"},"14576":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4776","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Run one of following commands in the HBase shell. Replace the table name `orders_table` with the name of your table."},"14577":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4777","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Configure other options if needed. See Configuring Date Tiered Compaction for more information."},"14578":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4778","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Disable Date Tiered Compaction**"},"14579":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4779","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Set the `hbase.hstore.engine.class` option to either nil or `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DefaultStoreEngine`. Either option has the same effect. Make sure you set the other options you changed to the original settings too."},"14580":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4780","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-date-tiered-compaction","content":"When you change the store engine either way, a major compaction will likely be performed on most regions. This is not necessary on new tables."},"14581":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4781","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Each of the settings for date tiered compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"},"14582":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4782","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Data Tier Parameters**\\\nYou can configure your date tiers by changing the settings for the following parameters:"},"14583":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4783","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Setting"},"14584":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4784","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Notes"},"14585":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4785","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.max.storefile.age.millis`"},"14586":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4786","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Files with max-timestamp smaller than this will no longer be compacted.Default at Long.MAX\\_VALUE."},"14587":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4787","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.base.window.millis`"},"14588":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4788","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Base window size in milliseconds. Default at 6 hours."},"14589":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4789","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.windows.per.tier`"},"14590":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4790","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Number of windows per tier. Default at 4."},"14591":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4791","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.incoming.window.min`"},"14592":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4792","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"Minimal number of files to compact in the incoming window. Set it to expected number of files in the window to avoid wasteful compaction. Default at 6."},"14593":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4793","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"`hbase.hstore.compaction.date.tiered.window.policy.class`"},"14594":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4794","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"The policy to select store files within the same time window. It doesn't apply to the incoming window. Default at exploring compaction. This is to avoid wasteful compaction."},"14595":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4795","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"**Compaction Throttler**\\\nWith tiered compaction all servers in the cluster will promote windows to higher tier at the same time, so using a compaction throttle is recommended: Set `hbase.regionserver.throughput.controller` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.compactions.PressureAwareCompactionThroughputController`."},"14596":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4796","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-date-tiered-compaction","content":"For more information about date tiered compaction, please refer to the design specification at\nhttps\\://docs.google.com/document/d/1\\_AmlNb2N8Us1xICsTeGDLKIqL6T-oHoRLZ323MG\\_uy8"},"14597":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4797","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compactions is an experimental feature added in HBase 0.98 which aims to improve compactions for large regions or non-uniformly distributed row keys. In order to achieve smaller and/or more granular compactions, the StoreFiles within a region are maintained separately for several row-key sub-ranges, or \"stripes\", of the region. The stripes are transparent to the rest of HBase, so other operations on the HFiles or data work without modification."},"14598":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4798","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compactions change the HFile layout, creating sub-regions within regions. These sub-regions are easier to compact, and should result in fewer major compactions. This approach alleviates some of the challenges of larger regions."},"14599":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4799","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#experimental-stripe-compactions","content":"Stripe compaction is fully compatible with Compaction and works in conjunction with either the ExploringCompactionPolicy or RatioBasedCompactionPolicy. It can be enabled for existing tables, and the table will continue to operate normally if it is disabled later."},"14600":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4800","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Consider using stripe compaction if you have either of the following:"},"14601":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4801","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Large regions. You can get the positive effects of smaller regions without additional overhead for MemStore and region management overhead."},"14602":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4802","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"Non-uniform keys, such as time dimension in a key. Only the stripes receiving the new keys will need to compact. Old data will not compact as often, if at all"},"14603":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4803","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-stripe-compactions","content":"**Performance Improvements**\\\nPerformance testing has shown that the performance of reads improves somewhat, and variability of performance of reads and writes is greatly reduced. An overall long-term performance improvement is seen on large non-uniform-row key regions, such as a hash-prefixed timestamp key. These performance gains are the most dramatic on a table which is already large. It is possible that the performance improvement might extend to region splits."},"14604":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4804","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"You can enable stripe compaction for a table or a column family, by setting its `hbase.hstore.engine.class` to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.StripeStoreEngine`. You also need to set the `hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles` to a high number, such as 100 (rather than the default value of 10)."},"14605":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4805","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Enable Stripe Compaction**"},"14606":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4806","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Run one of following commands in the HBase shell. Replace the table name `orders_table` with the name of your table."},"14607":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4807","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Configure other options if needed. See Configuring Stripe Compaction for more information."},"14608":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4808","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Enable the table."},"14609":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4809","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"**Procedure: Disable Stripe Compaction**"},"14610":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4810","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Set the `hbase.hstore.engine.class` option to either nil or `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.DefaultStoreEngine`. Either option has the same effect."},"14611":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4811","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"Enable the table."},"14612":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4812","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-stripe-compaction","content":"When you enable a large table after changing the store engine either way, a major compaction will likely be performed on most regions. This is not necessary on new tables."},"14613":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4813","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Each of the settings for stripe compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"},"14614":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4814","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Region and stripe sizing**\\\nYou can configure your stripe sizing based upon your region sizing. By default, your new regions will start with one stripe. On the next compaction after the stripe has grown too large (16 x MemStore flushes size), it is split into two stripes. Stripe splitting continues as the region grows, until the region is large enough to split."},"14615":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4815","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"You can improve this pattern for your own data. A good rule is to aim for a stripe size of at least 1 GB, and about 8-12 stripes for uniform row keys. For example, if your regions are 30 GB, 12 x 2.5 GB stripes might be a good starting point."},"14616":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4816","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Stripe Sizing Settings**"},"14617":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4817","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Setting"},"14618":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4818","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"Notes"},"14619":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4819","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.initialStripeCount`"},"14620":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4820","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The number of stripes to create when stripe compaction is enabled. You can use it as follows:- For relatively uniform row keys, if you know the approximate target number of stripes from the above, you can avoid some splitting overhead by starting with several stripes (2, 5, 10...). If the early data is not representative of overall row key distribution, this will not be as efficient.- For existing tables with a large amount of data, this setting will effectively pre-split your stripes.- For keys such as hash-prefixed sequential keys, with more than one hash prefix per region, pre-splitting may make sense."},"14621":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4821","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.sizeToSplit`"},"14622":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4822","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The maximum size a stripe grows before splitting. Use this in conjunction with `hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount` to control the target stripe size (`sizeToSplit = splitPartsCount * target stripe size`), according to the above sizing considerations."},"14623":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4823","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"`hbase.store.stripe.splitPartCount`"},"14624":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4824","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"The number of new stripes to create when splitting a stripe. The default is 2, which is appropriate for most cases. For non-uniform row keys, you can experiment with increasing the number to 3 or 4, to isolate the arriving updates into narrower slice of the region without additional splits being required."},"14625":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4825","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**MemStore Size Settings**\\\nBy default, the flush creates several files from one MemStore, according to existing stripe boundaries and row keys to flush. This approach minimizes write amplification, but can be undesirable if the MemStore is small and there are many stripes, because the files will be too small.\\\nIn this type of situation, you can set `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.flushToL0` to `true`. This will cause a MemStore flush to create a single file instead. When at least `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFilesL0` such files (by default, 4) accumulate, they will be compacted into striped files."},"14626":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4826","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-stripe-compaction","content":"**Normal Compaction Configuration and Stripe Compaction**\\\nAll the settings that apply to normal compactions (see Parameters Used by Compaction Algorithm) apply to stripe compactions. The exceptions are the minimum and maximum number of files, which are set to higher values by default because the files in stripes are smaller. To control these for stripe compactions, use `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.minFiles` and `hbase.store.stripe.compaction.maxFiles`, rather than `hbase.hstore.compaction.min` and `hbase.hstore.compaction.max`."},"14627":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4827","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fifo-compaction","content":"FIFO compaction policy selects only files which have all cells expired. The column family **MUST** have non-default TTL. Essentially, FIFO compactor only collects expired store files."},"14628":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4828","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fifo-compaction","content":"Because we don't do any real compaction, we do not use CPU and IO (disk and network) and evict hot data from a block cache. As a result, both RW throughput and latency can be improved."},"14629":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4829","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Consider using FIFO Compaction when your use case is"},"14630":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4830","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Very high volume raw data which has low TTL and which is the source of another data (after additional processing)."},"14631":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4831","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Data which can be kept entirely in a a block cache (RAM/SSD). No need for compaction of a raw data at all."},"14632":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4832","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Do not use FIFO compaction when"},"14633":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4833","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Table/ColumnFamily has MIN\\_VERSION > 0"},"14634":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4834","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#when-to-use-fifo-compaction","content":"Table/ColumnFamily has TTL = FOREVER (HColumnDescriptor.DEFAULT\\_TTL)"},"14635":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4835","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"For Table:"},"14636":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4836","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"For Column Family:"},"14637":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4837","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"From HBase Shell:"},"14638":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4838","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling-fifo-compaction","content":"Although region splitting is still supported, for optimal performance it should be disabled, either by setting explicitly `DisabledRegionSplitPolicy` or by setting `ConstantSizeRegionSplitPolicy` and very large max region size. You will have to increase to a very large number store's blocking file (`hbase.hstore.blockingStoreFiles`) as well. There is a sanity check on table/column family configuration in case of FIFO compaction and minimum value for number of blocking file is 1000."},"14639":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4839","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-overview","content":"HBase includes several methods of loading data into tables. The most straightforward method is to either use the `TableOutputFormat` class from a MapReduce job, or use the normal client APIs; however, these are not always the most efficient methods."},"14640":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4840","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-overview","content":"The bulk load feature uses a MapReduce job to output table data in HBase's internal data format, and then directly load the generated StoreFiles into a running cluster. Using bulk load will use less CPU and network resources than loading via the HBase API."},"14641":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4841","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load-architecture","content":"The HBase bulk load process consists of two main steps."},"14642":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4842","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"The first step of a bulk load is to generate HBase data files (StoreFiles) from a MapReduce job using `HFileOutputFormat2`. This output format writes out data in HBase's internal storage format so that they can be later loaded efficiently into the cluster."},"14643":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4843","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"In order to function efficiently, `HFileOutputFormat2` must be configured such that each output HFile fits within a single region. In order to do this, jobs whose output will be bulk loaded into HBase use Hadoop's `TotalOrderPartitioner` class to partition the map output into disjoint ranges of the key space, corresponding to the key ranges of the regions in the table."},"14644":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4844","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#preparing-data-via-a-mapreduce-job","content":"`HFileOutputFormat2` includes a convenience function, `configureIncrementalLoad()`, which automatically sets up a `TotalOrderPartitioner` based on the current region boundaries of a table."},"14645":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4845","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completing-the-data-load","content":"After a data import has been prepared, either by using the `importtsv` tool with the “importtsv.bulk.output” option or by some other MapReduce job using the `HFileOutputFormat`, the `completebulkload` tool is used to import the data into the running cluster. This command line tool iterates through the prepared data files, and for each one determines the region the file belongs to. It then contacts the appropriate RegionServer which adopts the HFile, moving it into its storage directory and making the data available to clients."},"14646":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4846","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completing-the-data-load","content":"If the region boundaries have changed during the course of bulk load preparation, or between the preparation and completion steps, the `completebulkload` utility will automatically split the data files into pieces corresponding to the new boundaries. This process is not optimally efficient, so users should take care to minimize the delay between preparing a bulk load and importing it into the cluster, especially if other clients are simultaneously loading data through other means."},"14647":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4847","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#completing-the-data-load","content":"The `-c config-file` option can be used to specify a file containing the appropriate hbase parameters (e.g., hbase-site.xml) if not supplied already on the CLASSPATH (In addition, the CLASSPATH must contain the directory that has the zookeeper configuration file if zookeeper is NOT managed by HBase)."},"14648":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4848","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-see-also","content":"For more information about the referenced utilities, see ImportTsv and CompleteBulkLoad."},"14649":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4849","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-see-also","content":"See How-to: Use HBase Bulk Loading, and Why for an old blog post on loading."},"14650":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4850","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#advanced-usage","content":"Although the `importtsv` tool is useful in many cases, advanced users may want to generate data programmatically, or import data from other formats. To get started doing so, dig into `ImportTsv.java` and check the JavaDoc for HFileOutputFormat."},"14651":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4851","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#advanced-usage","content":"The import step of the bulk load can also be done programmatically. See the `LoadIncrementalHFiles` class for more information."},"14652":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4852","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adopting-stray-data","content":"Should an HBase cluster lose account of regions or files during an outage or error, you can use the `completebulkload` tool to add back the dropped data. HBase operator tooling such as HBCK2 or the reporting added to the Master's UI under the `HBCK Report` (Since HBase 2.0.6/2.1.6/2.2.1) can identify such 'orphan' directories."},"14653":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4853","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adopting-stray-data","content":"Before you begin the 'adoption', ensure the `hbase:meta` table is in a healthy state. Run the `CatalogJanitor` by executing the `catalogjanitor_run` command on the HBase shell. When finished, check the `HBCK Report` page on the Master UI. Work on fixing any inconsistencies, holes, or overlaps found before proceeding. The `hbase:meta` table is the authority on where all data is to be found and must be consistent for the `completebulkload` tool to work properly."},"14654":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4854","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adopting-stray-data","content":"The `completebulkload` tool takes a directory and a `tablename`. The directory has subdirectories named for column families of the targeted `tablename`. In these subdirectories are `hfiles` to load. Given this structure, you can pass errant region directories (and the table name to which the region directory belongs) and the tool will bring the data files back into the fold by moving them under the approprate serving directory. If stray files, then you will need to mock up this structure before invoking the `completebulkload` tool; you may have to look at the file content using the HFile Tool to see what the column family to use is. When the tool completes its run, you will notice that the source errant directory has had its storefiles moved/removed. It is now desiccated since its data has been drained, and the pointed-to directory can be safely removed. It may still have `.regioninfo` files and other subdirectories but they are of no relevance now (There may be content still under the *recovered\\_edits* directory; a TODO is tooling to replay the content of *recovered\\_edits* if needed; see Add RecoveredEditsPlayer). If you pass `completebulkload` a directory without store files, it will run and note the directory is storefile-free. Just remove such 'empty' directories."},"14655":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4855","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adopting-stray-data","content":"For example, presuming a directory at the top level in HDFS named `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` has data we need to re-add to the HBase `TestTable`:"},"14656":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4856","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adopting-stray-data","content":"After it successfully completes, any files that were in `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` have been moved under hbase and the `eb3352fb5c9c9a05feeb2caba101e1cc` directory can be deleted (Check content before and after by running `ls -r` on the HDFS directory)."},"14657":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4857","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"HBASE-13153 adds replication support for bulk loaded HFiles, available since HBase 1.3/2.0. This feature is enabled by setting `hbase.replication.bulkload.enabled` to `true` (default is `false`). You also need to copy the source cluster configuration files to the destination cluster."},"14658":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4858","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"Additional configurations are required too:"},"14659":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4859","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.source.fs.conf.provider`\\\nThis defines the class which loads the source cluster file system client configuration in the destination cluster. This should be configured for all the RS in the destination cluster. Default is `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.replication.regionserver.DefaultSourceFSConfigurationProvider`."},"14660":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4860","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.conf.dir`\\\nThis represents the base directory where the file system client configurations of the source cluster are copied to the destination cluster. This should be configured for all the RS in the destination cluster. Default is `$HBASE_CONF_DIR`."},"14661":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4861","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`hbase.replication.cluster.id`\\\nThis configuration is required in the cluster where replication for bulk loaded data is enabled. A source cluster is uniquely identified by the destination cluster using this id. This should be configured for all the RS in the source cluster configuration file for all the RS."},"14662":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4862","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"For example: If source cluster FS client configurations are copied to the destination cluster under directory `/home/user/dc1/`, then `hbase.replication.cluster.id` should be configured as `dc1` and `hbase.replication.conf.dir` as `/home/user`."},"14663":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4863","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-replication","content":"`DefaultSourceFSConfigurationProvider` supports only `xml` type files. It loads source cluster FS\nclient configuration only once, so if source cluster FS client configuration files are updated,\nevery peer(s) cluster RS must be restarted to reload the configuration."},"14664":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4864","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hdfs","content":"As HBase runs on HDFS (and each StoreFile is written as a file on HDFS), it is important to have an understanding of the HDFS Architecture especially in terms of how it stores files, handles failovers, and replicates blocks."},"14665":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4865","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hdfs","content":"See the Hadoop documentation on HDFS Architecture for more information."},"14666":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4866","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hdfs-namenode","content":"The NameNode is responsible for maintaining the filesystem metadata. See the above HDFS Architecture link for more information."},"14667":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4867","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hdfs-datanode","content":"The DataNodes are responsible for storing HDFS blocks. See the above HDFS Architecture link for more information."},"14668":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4868","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#introduction","content":"HBase, architecturally, always had the strong consistency guarantee from the start. All reads and writes are routed through a single region server, which guarantees that all writes happen in an order, and all reads are seeing the most recent committed data."},"14669":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4869","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#introduction","content":"However, because of this single homing of the reads to a single location, if the server becomes unavailable, the regions of the table that were hosted in the region server become unavailable for some time. There are three phases in the region recovery process - detection, assignment, and recovery. Of these, the detection is usually the longest and is presently in the order of 20-30 seconds depending on the ZooKeeper session timeout. During this time and before the recovery is complete, the clients will not be able to read the region data."},"14670":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4870","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#introduction","content":"However, for some use cases, either the data may be read-only, or doing reads against some stale data is acceptable. With timeline-consistent high available reads, HBase can be used for these kind of latency-sensitive use cases where the application can expect to have a time bound on the read completion."},"14671":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4871","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#introduction","content":"For achieving high availability for reads, HBase provides a feature called *region replication*. In this model, for each region of a table, there will be multiple replicas that are opened in different RegionServers. By default, the region replication is set to 1, so only a single region replica is deployed and there will not be any changes from the original model. If region replication is set to 2 or more, then the master will assign replicas of the regions of the table. The Load Balancer ensures that the region replicas are not co-hosted in the same region servers and also in the same rack (if possible)."},"14672":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4872","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#introduction","content":"All of the replicas for a single region will have a unique replica*id, starting from 0. The region replica having replica\\_id==0 is called the primary region, and the others \\_secondary regions* or secondaries. Only the primary can accept writes from the client, and the primary will always contain the latest changes. Since all writes still have to go through the primary region, the writes are not highly-available (meaning they might block for some time if the region becomes unavailable)."},"14673":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4873","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"With this feature, HBase introduces a Consistency definition, which can be provided per read operation (get or scan)."},"14674":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4874","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"`Consistency.STRONG` is the default consistency model provided by HBase. In case the table has region replication = 1, or in a table with region replicas but the reads are done with this consistency, the read is always performed by the primary regions, so that there will not be any change from the previous behaviour, and the client always observes the latest data."},"14675":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4875","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"In case a read is performed with `Consistency.TIMELINE`, then the read RPC will be sent to the primary region server first. After a short interval (`hbase.client.primaryCallTimeout.get`, 10ms by default), parallel RPC for secondary region replicas will also be sent if the primary does not respond back. After this, the result is returned from whichever RPC is finished first. If the response came back from the primary region replica, we can always know that the data is latest. For this Result.isStale() API has been added to inspect the staleness. If the result is from a secondary region, then Result.isStale() will be set to true. The user can then inspect this field to possibly reason about the data."},"14676":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4876","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"In terms of semantics, TIMELINE consistency as implemented by HBase differs from pure eventual consistency in these respects:"},"14677":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4877","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"Single homed and ordered updates: Region replication or not, on the write side, there is still only 1 defined replica (primary) which can accept writes. This replica is responsible for ordering the edits and preventing conflicts. This guarantees that two different writes are not committed at the same time by different replicas and the data diverges. With this, there is no need to do read-repair or last-timestamp-wins kind of conflict resolution."},"14678":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4878","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"The secondaries also apply the edits in the order that the primary committed them. This way the secondaries will contain a snapshot of the primaries data at any point in time. This is similar to RDBMS replications and even HBase's own multi-datacenter replication, however in a single cluster."},"14679":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4879","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"On the read side, the client can detect whether the read is coming from up-to-date data or is stale data. Also, the client can issue reads with different consistency requirements on a per-operation basis to ensure its own semantic guarantees."},"14680":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4880","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"The client can still observe edits out-of-order, and can go back in time, if it observes reads from one secondary replica first, then another secondary replica. There is no stickiness to region replicas or a transaction-id based guarantee. If required, this can be implemented later though."},"14681":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4881","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"**Timeline Consistency**"},"14682":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4882","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"To better understand the TIMELINE semantics, let's look at the above diagram. Let's say that there are two clients, and the first one writes x=1 at first, then x=2 and x=3 later. As above, all writes are handled by the primary region replica. The writes are saved in the write ahead log (WAL), and replicated to the other replicas asynchronously. In the above diagram, notice that replica\\_id=1 received 2 updates, and its data shows that x=2, while the replica\\_id=2 only received a single update, and its data shows that x=1."},"14683":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4883","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistency","content":"If client1 reads with STRONG consistency, it will only talk with the replica\\_id=0, and thus is guaranteed to observe the latest value of x=3. In case of a client issuing TIMELINE consistency reads, the RPC will go to all replicas (after primary timeout) and the result from the first response will be returned back. Thus the client can see either 1, 2 or 3 as the value of x. Let's say that the primary region has failed and log replication cannot continue for some time. If the client does multiple reads with TIMELINE consistency, she can observe x=2 first, then x=1, and so on."},"14684":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4884","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Having secondary regions hosted for read availability comes with some tradeoffs which should be carefully evaluated per use case. Following are advantages and disadvantages."},"14685":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4885","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"**Advantages:**"},"14686":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4886","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"High availability for read-only tables"},"14687":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4887","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"High availability for stale reads"},"14688":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4888","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Ability to do very low latency reads with very high percentile (99.9%+) latencies for stale reads"},"14689":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4889","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"**Disadvantages:**"},"14690":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4890","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Double / Triple MemStore usage (depending on region replication count) for tables with region replication > 1"},"14691":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4891","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Increased block cache usage"},"14692":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4892","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Extra network traffic for log replication"},"14693":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4893","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"Extra backup RPCs for replicas"},"14694":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4894","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tradeoffs","content":"To serve the region data from multiple replicas, HBase opens the regions in secondary mode in the region servers. The regions opened in secondary mode will share the same data files with the primary region replica, however each secondary region replica will have its own MemStore to keep the unflushed data (only primary region can do flushes). Also to serve reads from secondary regions, the blocks of data files may be also cached in the block caches for the secondary regions."},"14695":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4895","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#where-is-the-code","content":"This feature is delivered in two phases, Phase 1 and 2. The first phase is done in time for HBase-1.0.0 release. Meaning that using HBase-1.0.x, you can use all the features that are marked for Phase 1. Phase 2 is committed in HBase-1.1.0, meaning all HBase versions after 1.1.0 should contain Phase 2 items."},"14696":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4896","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#propagating-writes-to-region-replicas","content":"As discussed above writes only go to the primary region replica. For propagating the writes from the primary region replica to the secondaries, there are two different mechanisms. For read-only tables, you do not need to use any of the following methods. Disabling and enabling the table should make the data available in all region replicas. For mutable tables, you have to use **only** one of the following mechanisms: storefile refresher, or async wal replication. The latter is recommended."},"14697":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4897","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storefile-refresher","content":"The first mechanism is store file refresher which is introduced in HBase-1.0+. Store file refresher is a thread per region server, which runs periodically, and does a refresh operation for the store files of the primary region for the secondary region replicas. If enabled, the refresher will ensure that the secondary region replicas see the new flushed, compacted or bulk loaded files from the primary region in a timely manner. However, this means that only flushed data can be read back from the secondary region replicas, and after the refresher is run, making the secondaries lag behind the primary for an a longer time."},"14698":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4898","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#storefile-refresher","content":"For turning this feature on, you should configure `hbase.regionserver.storefile.refresh.period` to a non-zero value. See Configuration section below."},"14699":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4899","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"The second mechanism for propagation of writes to secondaries is done via the “Async WAL Replication” feature. It is only available in HBase-1.1+. This works similarly to HBase's multi-datacenter replication, but instead the data from a region is replicated to the secondary regions. Each secondary replica always receives and observes the writes in the same order that the primary region committed them. In some sense, this design can be thought of as “in-cluster replication”, where instead of replicating to a different datacenter, the data goes to secondary regions to keep secondary region's in-memory state up to date. The data files are shared between the primary region and the other replicas, so that there is no extra storage overhead. However, the secondary regions will have recent non-flushed data in their memstores, which increases the memory overhead. The primary region writes flush, compaction, and bulk load events to its WAL as well, which are also replicated through wal replication to secondaries. When they observe the flush/compaction or bulk load event, the secondary regions replay the event to pick up the new files and drop the old ones."},"14700":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4900","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"Committing writes in the same order as in primary ensures that the secondaries won't diverge from the primary regions data, but since the log replication is asynchronous, the data might still be stale in secondary regions."},"14701":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4901","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"Async WAL Replication is **disabled** by default. You can enable this feature by setting `hbase.region.replica.replication.enabled` to `true`."},"14702":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4902","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"Before 3.0.0, this feature works as a replication endpoint, the performance and latency characteristics is expected to be similar to inter-cluster replication. And once enabled, it will create a replication peer named `region_replica_replication` as a replication peer when you create a table with region replication > 1 for the first time."},"14703":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4903","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"if you want to disable this feature, you need to do two actions in the following order: \\* Set configuration property `hbase.region.replica.replication.enabled` to false in `hbase-site.xml` (see Configuration section below) \\* Disable the replication peer named `region_replica_replication` in the cluster using hbase shell or `Admin` class:"},"14704":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4904","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"In 3.0.0, this feature is re-implemented to decouple with the general replication framework. Now we do not need to create a special replication peer. And during rolling upgrading, we will remove this replication peer automatically if it is present. See HBASE-26233 and the design doc in our git repo for more details."},"14705":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4905","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication","content":"Async WAL Replication and the `hbase:meta` table is a little more involved and gets its own section below; see Region replication for META table's region"},"14706":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4906","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#store-file-ttl","content":"In both of the write propagation approaches mentioned above, store files of the primary will be opened in secondaries independent of the primary region. So for files that the primary compacted away, the secondaries might still be referring to these files for reading. Both features are using HFileLinks to refer to files, but there is no protection (yet) for guaranteeing that the file will not be deleted prematurely. Thus, as a guard, you should set the configuration property `hbase.master.hfilecleaner.ttl` to a larger value, such as 1 hour to guarantee that you will not receive IOExceptions for requests going to replicas."},"14707":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4907","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#region-replication-for-meta-tables-region","content":"The general Async WAL Replication does not work for the META table's WAL. The meta table's secondary replicas refresh themselves from the persistent store files every `hbase.regionserver.meta.storefile.refresh.period`, (a non-zero value). Note how the META replication period is distinct from the user-space `hbase.regionserver.storefile.refresh.period` value."},"14708":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4908","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-240","content":"Async WAL replication for META is added as a new feature in 2.4.0. Set `hbase.region.replica.replication.catalog.enabled` to enable async WAL Replication for META region replicas. It is off by default."},"14709":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4909","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-240","content":"Regarding META replicas count, up to hbase-2.4.0, you would set the special property 'hbase.meta.replica.count'. Now you can alter the META table as you would a user-space table (if `hbase.meta.replica.count` is set, it will take precedent over what is set for replica count in the META table updating META replica count to match)."},"14710":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4910","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#async-wal-replication-for-meta-table-as-of-hbase-300","content":"In HBASE-26233 we re-implemented the region replication framework to not rely on the general replication framework, so it can work together with META table as well. The code described in the above section have been removed mostly, but the config `hbase.region.replica.replication.catalog.enabled` is still kept, you could still use it to control whether to enable async wal replication for META table. And the ability to alter META table is also kept."},"14711":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4911","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"hbase-2.4.0 also adds a new client-side `LoadBalance` mode. When enabled client-side, clients will try to read META replicas first before falling back on the primary. Before this, the replica lookup mode — now named `HedgedRead` in hbase-2.4.0 — had clients read the primary and if no response after a configurable amount of time had elapsed, it would start up reads against the replicas. Starting from hbase-2.4.12(and all higher minor versions), with client-side `LoadBalance` mode, clients load balance META scan requests across all META replica regions, including the primary META region. In case of exceptions such as NotServingRegionException, it will fall back on the primary META region."},"14712":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4912","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"The new 'LoadBalance' mode helps alleviate hotspotting on the META table distributing META read load."},"14713":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4913","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-balancing-meta-table-load","content":"To enable the meta replica locator's load balance mode, please set the following configuration at on the **client-side** (only): set 'hbase.locator.meta.replicas.mode' to \"LoadBalance\". Valid options for this configuration are `None`, `HedgedRead`, and `LoadBalance`. Option parse is case insensitive. The default mode is `None` (which falls through to `HedgedRead`, the current default). Do NOT put this configuration in any hbase server-side's configuration, Master or RegionServer (Master could make decisions based off stale state — to be avoided)."},"14714":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4914","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#memory-accounting","content":"The secondary region replicas refer to the data files of the primary region replica, but they have their own memstores (in HBase-1.1+) and uses block cache as well. However, one distinction is that the secondary region replicas cannot flush the data when there is memory pressure for their memstores. They can only free up memstore memory when the primary region does a flush and this flush is replicated to the secondary. Since in a region server hosting primary replicas for some regions and secondaries for some others, the secondaries might cause extra flushes to the primary regions in the same host. In extreme situations, there can be no memory left for adding new writes coming from the primary via wal replication. For unblocking this situation (and since secondary cannot flush by itself), the secondary is allowed to do a “store file refresh” by doing a file system list operation to pick up new files from primary, and possibly dropping its memstore. This refresh will only be performed if the memstore size of the biggest secondary region replica is at least `hbase.region.replica.storefile.refresh.memstore.multiplier` (default 4) times bigger than the biggest memstore of a primary replica. One caveat is that if this is performed, the secondary can observe partial row updates across column families (since column families are flushed independently). The default should be good to not do this operation frequently. You can set this value to a large number to disable this feature if desired, but be warned that it might cause the replication to block forever."},"14715":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4915","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secondary-replica-failover","content":"When a secondary region replica first comes online, or fails over, it may have served some edits from its memstore. Since the recovery is handled differently for secondary replicas, the secondary has to ensure that it does not go back in time before it starts serving requests after assignment. For doing that, the secondary waits until it observes a full flush cycle (start flush, commit flush) or a “region open event” replicated from the primary. Until this happens, the secondary region replica will reject all read requests by throwing an IOException with message “The region's reads are disabled”. However, the other replicas will probably still be available to read, thus not causing any impact for the rpc with TIMELINE consistency. To facilitate faster recovery, the secondary region will trigger a flush request from the primary when it is opened. The configuration property `hbase.region.replica.wait.for.primary.flush` (enabled by default) can be used to disable this feature if needed."},"14716":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4916","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-properties","content":"To use highly available reads, you should set the following properties in `hbase-site.xml` file. There is no specific configuration to enable or disable region replicas. Instead you can change the number of region replicas per table to increase or decrease at the table creation or with alter table. The following configuration is for using async wal replication and using meta replicas of 3."},"14717":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4917","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server-side-properties","content":"One thing to keep in mind also is that, region replica placement policy is only enforced by the `StochasticLoadBalancer` which is the default balancer. If you are using a custom load balancer property in hbase-site.xml (`hbase.master.loadbalancer.class`) replicas of regions might end up being hosted in the same server."},"14718":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4918","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-properties","content":"Ensure to set the following for all clients (and servers) that will use region replicas."},"14719":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4919","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#client-side-properties","content":"Note HBase-1.0.x users should use `hbase.ipc.client.allowsInterrupt` rather than `hbase.ipc.client.specificThreadForWriting`."},"14720":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4920","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#user-interface","content":"In the masters user interface, the region replicas of a table are also shown together with the primary regions. You can notice that the replicas of a region will share the same start and end keys and the same region name prefix. The only difference would be the appended replica\\_id (which is encoded as hex), and the region encoded name will be different. You can also see the replica ids shown explicitly in the UI."},"14721":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4921","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-table-with-region-replication","content":"Region replication is a per-table property. All tables have `REGION_REPLICATION = 1` by default, which means that there is only one replica per region. You can set and change the number of replicas per region of a table by supplying the `REGION_REPLICATION` property in the table descriptor."},"14722":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4922","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-table-with-region-replication","content":"There is another per-table property `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION`.All tables have `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION = true` by default, which means the new data written to the primary region should be replicated. If you set this to `false`, replicas do not receive memstore updates from the primary RegionServer,they will only receive updates for events like flushes and bulkloads, and will not have access to data which the primary has not yet flushed. Please note that if you set `REGION_MEMSTORE_REPLICATION` to false,`hbase.region.replica.wait.for.primary.flush` will be ignored."},"14723":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4923","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#architecture-timeline-consistent-reads-creating-table-with-region-replication-java","content":"You can also use `setRegionReplication()` and alter table to increase, decrease the region replication for a table."},"14724":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4924","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"You can do reads in shell using a the Consistency.TIMELINE semantics as follows"},"14725":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4925","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"You can simulate a region server pausing or becoming unavailable and do a read from the secondary replica:"},"14726":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4926","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#timeline-consistent-reads-shell-read-api","content":"Using scans is also similar"},"14727":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4927","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java","content":"You can set the consistency for Gets and Scans and do requests as follows."},"14728":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4928","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java","content":"You can also pass multiple gets:"},"14729":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4929","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java","content":"And Scans:"},"14730":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4930","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java","content":"You can inspect whether the results are coming from primary region or not by calling the `Result.isStale()` method:"},"14731":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4931","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob","content":"Data comes in many sizes, and saving all of your data in HBase, including binary\ndata such as images and documents, is ideal. While HBase can technically handle\nbinary objects with cells that are larger than 100 KB in size, HBase's normal\nread and write paths are optimized for values smaller than 100KB in size. When\nHBase deals with large numbers of objects over this threshold, referred to here\nas medium objects, or MOBs, performance is degraded due to write amplification\ncaused by splits and compactions. When using MOBs, ideally your objects will be between\n100KB and 10MB (see the faq). HBase 2 added special internal handling of MOBs\nto maintain performance, consistency, and low operational overhead. MOB support is\nprovided by the work done in HBASE-11339.\nTo take advantage of MOB, you need to use HFile version 3. Optionally,\nconfigure the MOB file reader's cache settings for each RegionServer (see\nConfigure the MOB Cache), then configure specific columns to hold MOB data.\nClient code does not need to change to take advantage of HBase MOB support. The\nfeature is transparent to the client."},"14732":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4932","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-columns-for-mob","content":"You can configure columns to support MOB during table creation or alteration,\neither in HBase Shell or via the Java API. The two relevant properties are the\nboolean `IS_MOB` and the `MOB_THRESHOLD`, which is the number of bytes at which\nan object is considered to be a MOB. Only `IS_MOB` is required. If you do not\nspecify the `MOB_THRESHOLD`, the default threshold value of 100 KB is used."},"14733":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4933","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-mob","content":"The utility `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.IntegrationTestIngestWithMOB` is provided to assist with testing\nthe MOB feature. The utility is run as follows:"},"14734":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4934","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-mob","content":"**`threshold`** is the threshold at which cells are considered to be MOBs.\nThe default is 1 kB, expressed in bytes."},"14735":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4935","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-mob","content":"**`minMobDataSize`** is the minimum value for the size of MOB data.\nThe default is 512 B, expressed in bytes."},"14736":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4936","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-mob","content":"**`maxMobDataSize`** is the maximum value for the size of MOB data.\nThe default is 5 kB, expressed in bytes."},"14737":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4937","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-architecture","content":"This section is derived from information found in\nHBASE-11339, which covered the initial GA\nimplementation of MOB in HBase and\nHBASE-22749, which improved things by\nparallelizing MOB maintenance across the RegionServers. For more information see\nthe last version of the design doc created during the initial work,\n\"HBASE-11339 MOB GA design.pdf\",\nand the design doc for the distributed mob compaction feature,\n\"HBASE-22749 MOB distributed compaction.pdf\"."},"14738":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4938","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"The MOB feature reduces the overall IO load for configured column families by storing values that\nare larger than the configured threshold outside of the normal regions to avoid splits, merges, and\nmost importantly normal compactions."},"14739":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4939","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"When a cell is first written to a region it is stored in the WAL and memstore regardless of value\nsize. When memstores from a column family configured to use MOB are eventually flushed two hfiles\nare written simultaneously. Cells with a value smaller than the threshold size are written to a\nnormal region hfile. Cells with a value larger than the threshold are written into a special MOB\nhfile and also have a MOB reference cell written into the normal region HFile. As the Region Server\nflushes a MOB enabled memstore and closes a given normal region HFile it appends metadata that lists\neach of the special MOB hfiles referenced by the cells within."},"14740":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4940","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"MOB reference cells have the same key as the cell they are based on. The value of the reference cell\nis made up of two pieces of metadata: the size of the actual value and the MOB hfile that contains\nthe original cell. In addition to any tags originally written to HBase, the reference cell prepends\ntwo additional tags. The first is a marker tag that says the cell is a MOB reference. This can be\nused later to scan specifically just for reference cells. The second stores the namespace and table\nat the time the MOB hfile is written out. This tag is used to optimize how the MOB system finds\nthe underlying value in MOB hfiles after a series of HBase snapshot operations (ref HBASE-12332).\nNote that tags are only available within HBase servers and by default are not sent over RPCs."},"14741":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4941","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"All MOB hfiles for a given table are managed within a logical region that does not directly serve\nrequests. When these MOB hfiles are created from a flush or MOB compaction they are placed in a\ndedicated mob data area under the hbase root directory specific to the namespace, table, mob\nlogical region, and column family. In general that means a path structured like:"},"14742":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4942","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"With default configs, an example table named 'some\\_table' in the\ndefault namespace with a MOB enabled column family named 'foo' this HDFS directory would be"},"14743":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4943","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"These MOB hfiles are maintained by special chores in the HBase Master and across the individual\nRegion Servers. Specifically those chores take care of enforcing TTLs and compacting them. Note that\nthis compaction is primarily a matter of controlling the total number of files in HDFS because our\noperational assumptions for MOB data is that it will seldom update or delete."},"14744":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4944","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"When a given MOB hfile is no longer needed as a result of our compaction process then a chore in\nthe Master will take care of moving it to the archive just\nlike any normal hfile. Because the table's mob region is independent of all the normal regions it\ncan coexist with them in the regular archive storage area:"},"14745":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4945","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-mob-overview","content":"The same hfile cleaning chores that take care of eventually deleting unneeded archived files from\nnormal regions thus also will take care of these MOB hfiles. As such, if there is a snapshot of a\nMOB enabled table then the cleaning system will make sure those MOB files stick around in the\narchive area as long as they are needed by a snapshot or a clone of a snapshot."},"14746":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4946","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-compaction","content":"Each time the memstore for a MOB enabled column family performs a flush HBase will write values over\nthe MOB threshold into MOB specific hfiles. When normal region compaction occurs the Region Server\nrewrites the normal data files while maintaining references to these MOB files without rewriting\nthem. Normal client lookups for MOB values transparently will receive the original values because\nthe Region Server internals take care of using the reference data to then pull the value out of a\nspecific MOB file. This indirection means that building up a large number of MOB hfiles doesn't\nimpact the overall time to retrieve any specific MOB cell. Thus, we need not perform compactions of\nthe MOB hfiles nearly as often as normal hfiles. As a result, HBase saves IO by not rewriting MOB\nhfiles as a part of the periodic compactions a Region Server does on its own."},"14747":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4947","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-compaction","content":"However, if deletes and updates of MOB cells are frequent then this indirection will begin to waste\nspace. The only way to stop using the space of a particular MOB hfile is to ensure no cells still\nhold references to it. To do that we need to ensure we have written the current values into a new\nMOB hfile. If our backing filesystem has a limitation on the number of files that can be present, as\nHDFS does, then even if we do not have deletes or updates of MOB cells eventually there will be a\nsufficient number of MOB hfiles that we will need to coalesce them."},"14748":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4948","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-compaction","content":"Periodically a chore in the master coordinates having the region servers\nperform a special major compaction that also handles rewriting new MOB files. Like all compactions\nthe Region Server will create updated hfiles that hold both the cells that are smaller than the MOB\nthreshold and cells that hold references to the newly rewritten MOB file. Because this rewriting has\nthe advantage of looking across all active cells for the region our several small MOB files should\nend up as a single MOB file per region. The chore defaults to running weekly and can be\nconfigured by setting `hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` to the desired period in seconds."},"14749":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4949","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-compaction","content":"By default, the periodic MOB compaction coordination chore will attempt to keep every region\nbusy doing compactions in parallel in order to maximize the amount of work done on the cluster.\nIf you need to tune the amount of IO this compaction generates on the underlying filesystem, you\ncan control how many concurrent region-level compaction requests are allowed by setting\n`hbase.mob.major.compaction.region.batch.size` to an integer number greater than zero. If you set\nthe configuration to 0 then you will get the default behavior of attempting to do all regions in\nparallel."},"14750":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4950","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"Eventually we will have MOB hfiles that are no longer needed. Either clients will overwrite the\nvalue or a MOB-rewriting compaction will store a reference to a newer larger MOB hfile. Because any\ngiven MOB cell could have originally been written either in the current region or in a parent region\nthat existed at some prior point in time, individual Region Servers do not decide when it is time\nto archive MOB hfiles. Instead a periodic chore in the Master evaluates MOB hfiles for archiving."},"14751":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4951","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"A MOB HFile will be subject to archiving under any of the following conditions:"},"14752":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4952","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"Any MOB HFile older than the column family's TTL"},"14753":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4953","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"Any MOB HFile older than a \"too recent\" threshold with no references to it from the regular hfiles\nfor all regions in a column family"},"14754":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4954","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"To determine if a MOB HFile meets the second criteria the chore extracts metadata from the regular\nHFiles for each MOB enabled column family for a given table. That metadata enumerates the complete\nset of MOB HFiles needed to satisfy the references stored in the normal HFile area."},"14755":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4955","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-file-archiving","content":"The period of the cleaner chore can be configured by setting `hbase.master.mob.cleaner.period` to a\npositive integer number of seconds. It defaults to running daily. You should not need to tune it\nunless you have a very aggressive TTL or a very high rate of MOB updates with a correspondingly\nhigh rate of non-MOB compactions."},"14756":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4956","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"If your MOB workload has few to no updates or deletes then you can opt-in to MOB compactions that\noptimize for limiting the amount of write amplification. It achieves this by setting a\nsize threshold to ignore MOB files during the compaction process. When a given region goes\nthrough MOB compaction it will evaluate the size of the MOB file that currently holds the actual\nvalue and skip rewriting the value if that file is over threshold."},"14757":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4957","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"The bound of write amplification in this mode can be approximated as\n\"Write Amplification\" = $\\log_{K}\\!\\left(\\frac{M}{S}\\right)$ where **K** is the number of files in compaction\nselection, **M** is the configurable threshold for MOB files size, and **S** is the minmum size of\nmemstore flushes that create MOB files in the first place. For example given 5 files picked up per\ncompaction, a threshold of 1 GB, and a flush size of 10MB the write amplification will be\n$\\log\\\\_{5}\\!\\left(\\frac{1\\,\\text{GB}}{10\\,\\text{MB}}\\right) = \\log\\\\_{5}(100) \\approx 2.86$."},"14758":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4958","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"If we are using an underlying filesystem with a limitation on the number of files, such as HDFS,\nand we know our expected data set size we can choose our maximum file size in order to approach\nthis limit but stay within it in order to minimize write amplification. For example, if we expect to\nstore a petabyte and we have a conservative limitation of a million files in our HDFS instance, then\n$\\frac{1\\,\\text{PB}}{1\\,\\text{M}} = 1\\,\\text{GB}$ gives us a target limitation of a gigabyte per MOB file."},"14759":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4959","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"To opt-in to this compaction mode you must set `hbase.mob.compaction.type` to `optimized`. The\ndefault MOB size threshold in this mode is set to 1GB. It can be changed by setting\n`hbase.mob.compactions.max.file.size` to a positive integer number of bytes."},"14760":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4960","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"Additionally, when operating in this mode the compaction process will seek to avoid writing MOB\nfiles that are over the max file threshold. As it is writing out a additional MOB values into a MOB\nhfile it will check to see if the additional data causes the hfile to be over the max file size.\nWhen the hfile of MOB values reaches limit, the MOB hfile is committed to the MOB storage area and\na new one is created. The hfile with reference cells will track the complete set of MOB hfiles it\nneeds in its metadata."},"14761":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4961","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#further-limiting-write-amplification","content":"When using the write amplification optimized compaction mode you need to watch for the maximum\ntime to compact a single region. If it nears an hour you should read through the troubleshooting\nsection below Adjusting the MOB cleaner's tolerance for new\nhfiles. Failure\nto make the adjustments discussed there could lead to dataloss."},"14762":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4962","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-the-mob-cache","content":"Because there can be a large number of MOB files at any time, as compared to the number of HFiles,\nMOB files are not always kept open. The MOB file reader cache is a LRU cache which keeps the most\nrecently used MOB files open. To configure the MOB file reader's cache on each RegionServer, add\nthe following properties to the RegionServer's `hbase-site.xml`, customize the configuration to\nsuit your environment, and restart or rolling restart the RegionServer."},"14763":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4963","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manually-compacting-mob-files","content":"To manually compact MOB files, rather than waiting for the\nperiodic chore to trigger compaction, use the\n`major_compact` HBase shell commands. These commands\nrequire the first argument to be the table name, and take a column\nfamily as the second argument. If used with a column family that includes MOB data, then\nthese operator requests will result in the MOB data being compacted."},"14764":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4964","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#manually-compacting-mob-files","content":"This same request can be made via the `Admin.majorCompact` Java API."},"14765":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4965","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"The MOB cleaner chore ignores all MOB hfiles that were created more recently than an hour prior to\nthe start of the chore to ensure we don't miss the reference metadata from the corresponding regular\nhfile. Without this safety check it would be possible for the cleaner chore to see a MOB hfile for\nan in progress flush or compaction and prematurely archive the MOB data. This default buffer should\nbe sufficient for normal use."},"14766":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4966","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"You will need to adjust the tolerance if you use write amplification optimized MOB compaction and\nthe combination of your underlying filesystem performance and data shape is such that it could take\nmore than an hour to complete major compaction of a single region. For example, if your MOB data is\ndistributed such that your largest region adds 80GB of MOB data between compactions that include\nrewriting MOB data and your HDFS cluster is only capable of writing 20MB/s for a single file then\nwhen performing the optimized compaction the Region Server will take about a minute to write the\nfirst 1GB MOB hfile and then another hour and seven minutes to write the remaining seventy-nine 1GB\nMOB hfiles before finally committing the new reference hfile at the end of the compaction. Given\nthis example, you would need a larger tolerance window."},"14767":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4967","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"You will also need to adjust the tolerance if Region Server flush operations take longer than an\nhour for the two HDFS move operations needed to commit both the MOB hfile and the normal hfile that\nreferences it. Such a delay should not happen with a normally configured and healthy HDFS and HBase."},"14768":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4968","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#adjusting-the-mob-cleaners-tolerance-for-new-hfiles","content":"The cleaner's window for \"too recent\" is controlled by setting `hbase.mob.min.age.archive` to a\npositive integer number of milliseconds."},"14769":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4969","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"While working on troubleshooting failures in the MOB system you can retrieve some of the internal\ninformation through the HBase shell by specifying special attributes on a scan."},"14770":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4970","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"The MOB internal information is stored as four bytes for the size of the underlying cell value and\nthen a UTF8 string with the name of the MOB HFile that contains the underlying cell value. Note that\nby default the entirety of this serialized structure will be passed through the HBase shell's binary\nstring converter. That means the bytes that make up the value size will most likely be written as\nescaped non-printable byte values, e.g. '\\x03', unless they happen to correspond to ASCII\ncharacters."},"14771":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4971","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"Let's look at a specific example:"},"14772":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4972","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"In this case the first four bytes are `\\x00\\x02|\\x94` which corresponds to the bytes\n`[0x00, 0x02, 0x7C, 0x94]`. (Note that the third byte was printed as the ASCII character '|'.)\nDecoded as an integer this gives us an underlying value size of 162,964 bytes."},"14773":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4973","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"The remaining bytes give us an HFile name,\n'd41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e19700118ffd9c244fe69488bbc9f2c77d24a3e6a'. This HFile will most\nlikely be stored in the designated MOB storage area for this specific table. However, the file could\nalso be in the archive area if this table is from a restored snapshot. Furthermore, if the table is\nfrom a cloned snapshot of a different table then the file could be in either the active or archive\narea of that source table. As mentioned in the explanation of MOB reference cells above, the Region\nServer will use a server side tag to optimize looking at the mob and archive area of the correct\noriginal table when finding the MOB HFile. Since your scan is client side it can't retrieve that tag\nand you'll either need to already know the lineage of your table or you'll need to search across all\ntables."},"14774":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4974","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#retrieving-mob-metadata-through-the-hbase-shell","content":"Assuming you are authenticated as a user with HBase superuser rights, you can search for it:"},"14775":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4975","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#moving-a-column-family-out-of-mob","content":"If you want to disable MOB on a column family you must ensure you instruct HBase to migrate the data\nout of the MOB system prior to turning the feature off. If you fail to do this HBase will return the\ninternal MOB metadata to applications because it will not know that it needs to resolve the actual\nvalues."},"14776":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4976","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#moving-a-column-family-out-of-mob","content":"The following procedure will safely migrate the underlying data without requiring a cluster outage.\nClients will see a number of retries when configuration settings are applied and regions are\nreloaded."},"14777":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4977","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-stop-mob-maintenance-change-mob-threshold-rewrite-data-via-compaction","content":"Ensure the MOB compaction chore in the Master is off by setting\n`hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` to `0`. Applying this configuration change will require a\nrolling restart of HBase Masters. That will require at least one fail-over of the active master,\nwhich may cause retries for clients doing HBase administrative operations."},"14778":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#procedure-stop-mob-maintenance-change-mob-threshold-rewrite-data-via-compaction","content":"Ensure no MOB compactions are issued for the table via the HBase shell for the duration of this\nmigration."},"14779":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-mob-size-threshold","content":"Use the HBase shell to change the MOB size threshold for the column family you are migrating to a\nvalue that is larger than the largest cell present in the column family. E.g. given a table named\n'some\\_table' and a column family named 'foo' we can pick one gigabyte as an arbitrary \"bigger than\nwhat we store\" value:"},"14780":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#change-the-mob-size-threshold","content":"Note that if you are still ingesting data you must ensure this threshold is larger than any cell value you might write; MAX\\_INT would be a safe choice."},"14781":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#perform-a-major-compaction-on-the-table","content":"Specifically you are performing a \"normal\" compaction and not a MOB compaction."},"14782":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"Since compaction is handled asynchronously you'll need to use the shell to first see the compaction start and then see it end."},"14783":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"HBase should first say that a \"MAJOR\" compaction is happening."},"14784":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"When the compaction has finished the result should print out \"NONE\"."},"14785":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"Run the *mobrefs* utility to ensure there are no MOB cells. Specifically, the tool will launch a\nHadoop MapReduce job that will show a job counter of 0 input records when we've successfully\nrewritten all of the data."},"14786":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"If the data has not successfully been migrated out, this report will show both a non-zero number\nof input records and a count of mob cells."},"14787":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#monitor-for-the-end-of-the-major-compaction","content":"If this happens you should verify that MOB compactions are disabled, verify that you have picked a sufficiently large MOB threshold, and redo the major compaction step."},"14788":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"When the *mobrefs* report shows that no more data is stored in the MOB system then you can safely alter the column family configuration so that the MOB feature is disabled."},"14789":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"The MOB feature will be disabled on a column family only after altering the column family and performing a major compaction. Before performing the major compaction after altering the column family, the MOB cells will still be present in the MOB storage."},"14790":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#disable-the-mob-feature-for-the-column-family","content":"After the column family no longer shows the MOB feature enabled, it is safe to start MOB maintenance chores again. You can allow the default to be used for `hbase.mob.compaction.chore.period` by removing it from your configuration files or restore it to whatever custom value you had prior to starting this process."},"14791":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#clean-up-residual-mob-data","content":"Once the MOB feature is disabled for the column family there will be no internal HBase process\nlooking for data in the MOB storage area specific to this column family. There will still be data\npresent there from prior to the compaction process that rewrote the values into HBase's data area.\nYou can check for this residual data directly in HDFS as an HBase superuser."},"14792":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#clean-up-residual-mob-data","content":"This data is spurious and may be reclaimed. You should sideline it, verify your application's view of the table, and then delete it."},"14793":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4993","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-values-over-than-the-mob-threshold-show-up-stored-in-non-mob-hfiles","content":"Bulk load and WAL split-to-HFile don't consider MOB threshold and write data into normal hfile (under /hbase/data directory)."},"14794":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4994","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-values-over-than-the-mob-threshold-show-up-stored-in-non-mob-hfiles","content":"This won't cause any functional problem, during next compaction such data will be written out to\nthe MOB hfiles."},"14795":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4995","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#mob-upgrade-considerations","content":"Generally, data stored using the MOB feature should transparently continue to work correctly across\nHBase upgrades."},"14796":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4996","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Prior to the work in HBASE-22749, \"Distributed MOB compactions\", HBase had the Master coordinate all\ncompaction maintenance of the MOB hfiles. Centralizing management of the MOB data allowed for space\noptimizations but safely coordinating that management with Region Servers resulted in edge cases that\ncaused data loss (ref HBASE-22075)."},"14797":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4997","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Users of the MOB feature upgrading to a version of HBase that includes HBASE-22749 should be aware\nof the following changes:"},"14798":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4998","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer allows setting \"MOB Compaction Policies\""},"14799":{"id":"/docs/single-page-4999","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer attempts to group MOB values by the date of the original cell's timestamp\naccording to said compaction policies, daily or otherwise"},"14800":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5000","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The MOB system no longer needs to track individual cell deletes through the use of special\nfiles in the MOB storage area with the suffix `_del`. After upgrading you should sideline these\nfiles."},"14801":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5001","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Under default configuration the MOB system should take much less time to perform a compaction of\nMOB stored values. This is a direct consequence of the fact that HBase will place a much larger\nload on the underlying filesystem when doing compactions of MOB stored values; the additional load\nshould be a multiple on the order of magnitude of number of region servers. I.e. for a cluster\nwith three region servers and two masters the default configuration should have HBase put three\ntimes the load on HDFS during major compactions that rewrite MOB data when compared to Master\nhandled MOB compaction; it should also be approximately three times as fast."},"14802":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5002","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"When the MOB system detects that a table has hfiles with references to MOB data but the reference\nhfiles do not yet have the needed file level metadata (i.e. from use of the MOB feature prior to\nHBASE-22749) then it will refuse to archive *any* MOB hfiles from that table. The normal course of\nperiodic compactions done by Region Servers will update existing hfiles with MOB references, but\nuntil a given table has been through the needed compactions operators should expect to see an\nincreased amount of storage used by the MOB feature."},"14803":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5003","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Performing a compaction with type \"MOB\" no longer has special handling to compact specifically the\nMOB hfiles. Instead it will issue a warning and do a compaction of the table. For example using\nthe HBase shell as follows will result in a warning in the Master logs followed by a major\ncompaction of the 'example' table in its entirety or for the 'big' column respectively."},"14804":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5004","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The same is true for directly using the Java API for `admin.majorCompact(TableName.valueOf(\"example\"), CompactType.MOB)`."},"14805":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5005","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"Similarly, manually performing a major compaction on a table or region will also handle compacting\nthe MOB stored values for that table or region respectively."},"14806":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5006","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The following configuration setting has been deprecated and replaced:"},"14807":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5007","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.master.mob.ttl.cleaner.period` has been replaced with `hbase.master.mob.cleaner.period`"},"14808":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5008","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"The following configuration settings are no longer used:"},"14809":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5009","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.mergeable.threshold`"},"14810":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5010","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.delfile.max.count`"},"14811":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5011","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.batch.size`"},"14812":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5012","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compactor.class`"},"14813":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5013","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#upgrading-to-a-version-with-the-distributed-mob-compaction-feature","content":"`hbase.mob.compaction.threads.max`"},"14814":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5014","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#snapshot-scanner","content":"In HBase, a scan of a table costs server-side HBase resources reading, formating, and returning data back to the client.\nLuckily, HBase provides a TableSnapshotScanner and TableSnapshotInputFormat (introduced by HBASE-8369),\nwhich can scan HBase-written HFiles directly in the HDFS filesystem completely by-passing hbase. This access mode\nperforms better than going via HBase and can be used with an offline HBase with in-place or exported\nsnapshot HFiles."},"14815":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5015","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#snapshot-scanner","content":"To read HFiles directly, the user must have sufficient permissions to access snapshots or in-place hbase HFiles."},"14816":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5016","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tablesnapshotscanner","content":"TableSnapshotScanner provides a means for running a single client-side scan over snapshot files.\nWhen using TableSnapshotScanner, we must specify a temporary directory to copy the snapshot files into.\nThe client user should have write permissions to this directory, and the dir should not be a subdirectory of\nthe hbase.rootdir. The scanner deletes the contents of the directory once the scanner is closed."},"14817":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5017","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tablesnapshotinputformat","content":"TableSnapshotInputFormat provides a way to scan over snapshot HFiles in a MapReduce job."},"14818":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5018","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#permission-to-access-snapshot-and-data-files","content":"Generally, only the HBase owner or the HDFS admin have the permission to access HFiles."},"14819":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5019","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#permission-to-access-snapshot-and-data-files","content":"HBASE-18659 uses HDFS ACLs to make HBase granted user have permission to access snapshot files."},"14820":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5020","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hdfs-acls","content":"HDFS ACLs supports an \"access ACL\", which defines the rules to enforce during permission checks, and a \"default ACL\", which defines the ACL entries that new child files or sub-directories receive automatically during creation. Via HDFS ACLs, HBase syncs granted users with read permission to HFiles."},"14821":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5021","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"The HBase files are organized in the following ways:"},"14822":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5022","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/.tmp/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"14823":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5023","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"14824":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5024","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/archive/data/{namespace}/{table}`"},"14825":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5025","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"`{hbase-rootdir}/.hbase-snapshot/{snapshotName}`"},"14826":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5026","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"So the basic idea is to add or remove HDFS ACLs to files of the global/namespace/table directory\nwhen grant or revoke permission to global/namespace/table."},"14827":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5027","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-idea","content":"See the design doc in HBASE-18659 for more details."},"14828":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5028","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Firstly, make sure that HDFS ACLs are enabled and umask is set to 027"},"14829":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5029","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Add master coprocessor, please make sure the SnapshotScannerHDFSAclController is configured after AccessController"},"14830":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5030","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Enable this feature"},"14831":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5031","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-to-use-this-feature","content":"Modify table scheme to enable this feature for a specified table, this config is\nfalse by default for every table, this means the HBase granted ACLs will not be synced to HDFS"},"14832":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5032","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitation","content":"There are some limitations for this feature:"},"14833":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5033","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitation","content":"If we enable this feature, some master operations such as grant, revoke, snapshot... (See the design doc for more details) will be slower as we need to sync HDFS ACLs to related hfiles."},"14834":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5034","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitation","content":"HDFS has a config which limits the max ACL entries num for one directory or file:"},"14835":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5035","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitation","content":"The 32 entries include four fixed users for each directory or file: owner, group, other, and mask. For a directory, the four users contain 8 ACL entries(access and default) and for a file, the four users contain 4 ACL entries(access). This means there are 24 ACL entries left for named users or groups.\\\nBased on this limitation, we can only sync up to 12 HBase granted users' ACLs. This means, if a table enables this feature, then the total users with table, namespace of this table, global READ permission should not be greater than 12."},"14836":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5036","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitation","content":"There are some cases that this coprocessor has not handled or could not handle, so the user HDFS ACLs are not synced normally. It will not make a reference link to another hfile of other tables."},"14837":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5037","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In-memory Compaction (A.K.A Accordion) is a new feature in hbase-2.0.0.\nIt was first introduced on the Apache HBase Blog at\nAccordion: HBase Breathes with In-Memory Compaction.\nQuoting the blog:"},"14838":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5038","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"> Accordion reapplies the LSM principal \\[*Log-Structured-Merge Tree*, the design pattern upon which HBase is based] to MemStore, in order to eliminate redundancies and other overhead while the data is still in RAM. Doing so decreases the frequency of flushes to HDFS, thereby reducing the write amplification and the overall disk footprint. With less flushes, the write operations are stalled less frequently as the MemStore overflows, therefore the write performance is improved. Less data on disk also implies less pressure on the block cache, higher hit rates, and eventually better read response times. Finally, having less disk writes also means having less compaction happening in the background, i.e., less cycles are stolen from productive (read and write) work. All in all, the effect of in-memory compaction can be envisioned as a catalyst that enables the system move faster as a whole."},"14839":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5039","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"A developer view is available at\nAccordion: Developer View of In-Memory Compaction."},"14840":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5040","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In-memory compaction works best when high data churn; overwrites or over-versions\ncan be eliminated while the data is still in memory. If the writes are all uniques,\nit may drag write throughput (In-memory compaction costs CPU). We suggest you test\nand compare before deploying to production."},"14841":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5041","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#inmemory-compaction-overview","content":"In this section we describe how to enable Accordion and the available configurations."},"14842":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5042","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"To enable in-memory compactions, set the *IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION* attribute\non per column family where you want the behavior. The *IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION*\nattribute can have one of four values."},"14843":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5043","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"*NONE*: No in-memory compaction."},"14844":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5044","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"*BASIC*: Basic policy enables flushing and keeps a pipeline of flushes until we trip the pipeline maximum threshold and then we flush to disk. No in-memory compaction but can help throughput as data is moved from the profligate, native ConcurrentSkipListMap data-type to more compact (and efficient) data types."},"14845":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5045","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"*EAGER*: This is *BASIC* policy plus in-memory compaction of flushes (much like the on-disk compactions done to hfiles); on compaction we apply on-disk rules eliminating versions, duplicates, ttl'd cells, etc."},"14846":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5046","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"*ADAPTIVE*: Adaptive compaction adapts to the workload. It applies either index compaction or data compaction based on the ratio of duplicate cells in the data. Experimental."},"14847":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5047","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"To enable *BASIC* on the *info* column family in the table *radish*, add the attribute to the *info* column family:"},"14848":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5048","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"Note how the IN*MEMORY\\_COMPACTION attribute shows as part of the \\_METADATA* map."},"14849":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5049","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"There is also a global configuration, *hbase.hregion.compacting.memstore.type* which you can set in your *hbase-site.xml* file. Use it to set the\ndefault on creation of a new table (On creation of a column family Store, we look first to the column family configuration looking for the\n*IN\\_MEMORY\\_COMPACTION* setting, and if none, we then consult the *hbase.hregion.compacting.memstore.type* value using its content; default is\n*NONE*)."},"14850":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5050","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"By default, new hbase system tables will have *NONE* in-memory compaction set. To specify otherwise,\non new table-creation, set *hbase.systemtables.compacting.memstore.type* to *BASIC* / *EAGER* / *ADAPTIVE* (Note, setting this value\npost-creation of system tables will not have a retroactive effect; you will have to alter your tables\nto set the in-memory attribute)."},"14851":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5051","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"When an in-memory flush happens is calculated by dividing the configured region flush size (Set in the table descriptor\nor read from *hbase.hregion.memstore.flush.size*) by the number of column families and then multiplying by\n*hbase.memstore.inmemoryflush.threshold.factor*. Default is 0.014."},"14852":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5052","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"The number of flushes carried by the pipeline is monitored so as to fit within the bounds of memstore sizing\nbut you can also set a maximum on the number of flushes total by setting\n*hbase.hregion.compacting.pipeline.segments.limit*. Default is 2."},"14853":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5053","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"When a column family Store is created, it says what memstore type is in effect. As of this writing\nthere is the old-school *DefaultMemStore* which fills a *ConcurrentSkipListMap* and then flushes\nto disk or the new *CompactingMemStore* that is the implementation that provides this new\nin-memory compactions facility. Here is a log-line from a RegionServer that shows a column\nfamily Store named *family* configured to use a *CompactingMemStore*:"},"14854":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5054","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enabling","content":"Enable TRACE-level logging on the CompactingMemStore class (*org.apache.hadoop.hbase.regionserver.CompactingMemStore*) to see detail on its operation."},"14855":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5055","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-write-overview","content":"To help reduce P99/P999 RPC latencies, HBase 2.x has made the read and write path use a pool of offheap buffers. Cells are\nallocated in offheap memory outside of the purview of the JVM garbage collector with attendent reduction in GC pressure.\nIn the write path, the request packet received from client will be read in on a pre-allocated offheap buffer and retained\noffheap until those cells are successfully persisted to the WAL and Memstore. The memory data structure in Memstore does\nnot directly store the cell memory, but references the cells encoded in the offheap buffers. Similarly for the read path.\nWe'll try to read the block cache first and if a cache misses, we'll go to the HFile and read the respective block. The\nworkflow from reading blocks to sending cells to client does its best to avoid on-heap memory allocations reducing the\namount of work the GC has to do."},"14856":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5056","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-write-overview","content":"For redress for the single mention of onheap in the read-section of the diagram above see Read block from HDFS to offheap directly."},"14857":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5057","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-path","content":"In HBase-2.0.0, HBASE-11425 changed the HBase read path so it\ncould hold the read-data off-heap avoiding copying of cached data (BlockCache) on to the java heap (for uncached data,\nsee note under the diagram in the section above). This reduces GC pauses given there is less garbage made and so less\nto clear. The off-heap read path can have a performance that is similar or better to that of the on-heap LRU cache.\nThis feature is available since HBase 2.0.0. Refer to below blogs for more details and test results on off heaped read path\nOffheaping the Read Path in Apache HBase: Part 1 of 2\nand Offheap Read-Path in Production - The Alibaba story"},"14858":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5058","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-path","content":"For an end-to-end off-heaped read-path, all you have to do is enable an off-heap backed Off-heap Block Cache(BC).\nTo do this, configure *hbase.bucketcache.ioengine* to be *offheap* in *hbase-site.xml* (See BucketCache Deploy Modes to learn\nmore about *hbase.bucketcache.ioengine* options). Also specify the total capacity of the BC using `hbase.bucketcache.size`.\nPlease remember to adjust value of 'HBASE*OFFHEAPSIZE' in \\_hbase-env.sh* (See BucketCache Example Configuration for help sizing and an example\nenabling). This configuration is for specifying the maximum possible off-heap memory allocation for the RegionServer java\nprocess. This should be bigger than the off-heap BC size to accommodate usage by other features making use of off-heap memory\nsuch as Server RPC buffer pool and short-circuit reads (See discussion in BucketCache Example Configuration)."},"14859":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5059","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-path","content":"Please keep in mind that there is no default for `hbase.bucketcache.ioengine` which means the `BlockCache` is OFF by default\n(See the \"Direct Memory Usage In HBase\" info section in BucketCache Example Configuration)."},"14860":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5060","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-read-path","content":"This is all you need to do to enable off-heap read path. Most buffers in HBase are already off-heap. With BC off-heap,\nthe read pipeline will copy data between HDFS and the server socket — caveat hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max —\nsending results back to the client."},"14861":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5061","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"It is possible to tune the ByteBuffer pool on the RPC server side used to accumulate the cell bytes and create result\ncell blocks to send back to the client side. Use `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled` to turn this pool ON or OFF. By\ndefault this pool is ON and available. HBase will create off-heap ByteBuffers and pool them them by default. Please\nmake sure not to turn this OFF if you want end-to-end off-heaping in read path."},"14862":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5062","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If this pool is turned off, the server will create temp buffers onheap to accumulate the cell bytes and\nmake a result cell block. This can impact the GC on a highly read loaded server."},"14863":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5063","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The config keys which start with prefix `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir` are deprecated in hbase-3.x\n(the internal pool implementation changed). If you are still in hbase-2.2.x or older, then just\nuse the old config keys. Otherwise if in hbase-3.x or hbase-2.3.x+, please use the new config keys\n(See deprecated and new configs in\nHBase3.x)"},"14864":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5064","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"Next thing to tune is the ByteBuffer pool on the RPC server side. The user can tune this pool with respect to how\nmany buffers are in the pool and what should be the size of each ByteBuffer. Use the config\n`hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.buffer.size` to tune each of the buffer sizes. Default is 64KB for hbase-2.2.x\nand less, changed to 65KB by default for hbase-2.3.x+\n(see HBASE-22532)"},"14865":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5065","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"When the result size is larger than one 64KB (Default) ByteBuffer size, the server will try to grab more than one\nByteBuffer and make a result cell block out of a collection of fixed-sized ByteBuffers. When the pool is running\nout of buffers, the server will skip the pool and create temporary on-heap buffers."},"14866":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5066","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The maximum number of ByteBuffers in the pool can be tuned using the config `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max`.\nIts default is a factor of region server handlers count (See the config `hbase.regionserver.handler.count`). The\nmath is such that by default we consider 2 MB as the result cell block size per read result and each handler will be\nhandling a read. For 2 MB size, we need 32 buffers each of size 64 KB (See default buffer size in pool). So per handler\n32 ByteBuffers(BB). We allocate twice this size as the max BBs count such that one handler can be creating the response\nand handing it to the RPC Responder thread and then handling a new request creating a new response cell block (using\npooled buffers). Even if the responder could not send back the first TCP reply immediately, our count should allow that\nwe should still have enough buffers in our pool without having to make temporary buffers on the heap. Again for smaller\nsized random row reads, tune this max count. These are lazily created buffers and the count is the max count to be pooled."},"14867":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5067","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If you still see GC issues even after making end-to-end read path off-heap, look for issues in the appropriate buffer\npool. Check for the below RegionServer log line at INFO level in HBase2.x:"},"14868":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5068","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"Or the following log message in HBase3.x:"},"14869":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5069","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"The setting for *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* in *hbase-env.sh* should consider this off heap buffer pool on the server side also.\nWe need to config this max off heap size for the RegionServer as a bit higher than the sum of this max pool size and\nthe off heap cache size. The TCP layer will also need to create direct bytebuffers for TCP communication. Also the DFS\nclient will need some off-heap to do its workings especially if short-circuit reads are configured. Allocating an extra\n1 - 2 GB for the max direct memory size has worked in tests."},"14870":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5070","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tuning-the-rpc-buffer-pool","content":"If you are using coprocessors and refer to the Cells in the read results, DO NOT store reference to these Cells out of\nthe scope of the CP hook methods. Some times the CPs want to store info about the cell (Like its row key) for considering\nin the next CP hook call etc. For such cases, pls clone the required fields of the entire Cell as per the use cases.\n\\[ See CellUtil#cloneXXX(Cell) APIs ]"},"14871":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5071","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"In HBase-2.x, the RegionServer will read blocks from HDFS to a temporary onheap ByteBuffer and then flush to\nthe BucketCache. Even if the BucketCache is offheap, we will first pull the HDFS read onheap before writing\nit out to the offheap BucketCache. We can observe much GC pressure when cache hit ratio low (e.g. a cacheHitRatio \\~ 60% ).\nHBASE-21879 addresses this issue (Requires hbase-2.3.x/hbase-3.x).\nIt depends on there being a supporting HDFS being in place (hadoop-2.10.x or hadoop-3.3.x) and it may require patching\nHBase itself (as of this writing); see\nHBASE-21879 Read HFile's block to ByteBuffer directly instead of to byte for reducing young gc purpose.\nAppropriately setup, reads from HDFS can be into offheap buffers passed offheap to the offheap BlockCache to cache."},"14872":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5072","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"For more details about the design and performance improvement, please see the\nDesign Doc -Read HFile's block to Offheap."},"14873":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5073","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Here we will share some best practice about the performance tuning but first we introduce new (hbase-3.x/hbase-2.3.x) configuration names\nthat go with the new internal pool implementation (`ByteBuffAllocator` vs the old `ByteBufferPool`), some of which mimic now deprecated\nhbase-2.2.x configurations discussed above in the Tuning the RPC buffer pool. Much of the advice here overlaps that given above\nin the Tuning the RPC buffer pool since the implementations have similar configurations."},"14874":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5074","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.pool.enabled` is for whether the RegionServer will use the pooled offheap ByteBuffer allocator. Default\nvalue is true. In hbase-2.x, the deprecated `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled` did similar and is mapped to this config\nuntil support for the old configuration is removed. This new name will be used in hbase-3.x and hbase-2.3.x+."},"14875":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5075","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.minimal.allocate.size` is the threshold at which we start allocating from the pool. Otherwise the\nrequest will be allocated from onheap directly because it would be wasteful allocating small stuff from our pool of fixed-size\nByteBuffers. The default minimum is `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size/6`."},"14876":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5076","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`: The `ByteBuffAllocator`, the new pool/reservoir implementation, has fixed-size\nByteBuffers. This config is for how many buffers to pool. Its default value is 2MB \\_ 2 \\_ hbase.regionserver.handler.count / 65KB\n(similar to the discussion above in Tuning the RPC buffer pool). If the default `hbase.regionserver.handler.count` is 30, then the default will be 1890."},"14877":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5077","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"`hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`: The byte size of each ByteBuffer. The default value is 66560 (65KB), here we choose 65KB instead of 64KB\nbecause of HBASE-22532."},"14878":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5078","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"The three config keys —`hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.enabled`, `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.buffer.size` and `hbase.ipc.server.reservoir.initial.max` — introduced in hbase-2.x\nhave been renamed and deprecated in hbase-3.x/hbase-2.3.x. Please use the new config keys instead:\n`hbase.server.allocator.pool.enabled`, `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size` and `hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`."},"14879":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5079","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Next, we have some suggestions regards performance."},"14880":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5080","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"**Please make sure that there are enough pooled DirectByteBuffer in your ByteBuffAllocator.**\\\nThe ByteBuffAllocator will allocate ByteBuffer from the DirectByteBuffer pool first. If\nthere's no available ByteBuffer in the pool, then we will allocate the ByteBuffers from onheap.\nBy default, we will pre-allocate 4MB for each RPC handler (The handler count is determined by the config:\n`hbase.regionserver.handler.count`, it has the default value 30) . That's to say, if your `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`\nis 65KB, then your pool will have 2MB \\_ 2 / 65KB \\_ 30 = 945 DirectByteBuffer. If you have a large scan and a big cache,\nyou may have a RPC response whose bytes size is greater than 2MB (another 2MB for receiving rpc request), then it will\nbe better to increase the `hbase.server.allocator.max.buffer.count`."},"14881":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5081","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"The RegionServer web UI has statistics on ByteBuffAllocator:"},"14882":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5082","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"If the following condition is met, you may need to increase your max buffer.count:"},"14883":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5083","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"**Please make sure the buffer size is greater than your block size.**\\\nWe have the default block size of 64KB, so almost all of the data blocks will be 64KB + a small delta, where the delta is\nvery small, depending on the size of the last Cell. If we set `hbase.server.allocator.buffer.size`=64KB,\nthen each block will be allocated as two ByteBuffers: one 64KB DirectByteBuffer and one HeapByteBuffer for the delta bytes.\nIdeally, we should let the data block to be allocated as one ByteBuffer; it has a simpler data structure, faster access speed,\nand less heap usage. Also, if the blocks are a composite of multiple ByteBuffers, to validate the checksum\nwe have to perform a temporary heap copy (see HBASE-21917)\nwhereas if it's a single ByteBuffer we can speed the checksum by calling the hadoop' checksum native lib; it's more faster."},"14884":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5084","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Please also see: HBASE-22483"},"14885":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5085","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#read-block-from-hdfs-to-offheap-directly","content":"Don't forget to up your *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* accordingly."},"14886":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5086","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-write-path","content":"In hbase-2.x, HBASE-15179 made the HBase write path work off-heap. By default, the MemStores in\nHBase have always used MemStore Local Allocation Buffers (MSLABs) to avoid memory fragmentation; an MSLAB creates bigger fixed sized chunks and then the\nMemStores Cell's data gets copied into these MSLAB chunks. These chunks can be pooled also and from hbase-2.x on, the MSLAB pool is by default ON.\nWrite off-heaping makes use of the MSLAB pool. It creates MSLAB chunks as Direct ByteBuffers and pools them."},"14887":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5087","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-write-path","content":"`hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is the configuration key which controls the amount of off-heap data. Its value is the number of megabytes\nof off-heap memory that should be used by MSLAB (e.g. `25` would result in 25MB of off-heap). Be sure to increase *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE* which will set the JVM's\nMaxDirectMemorySize property (see Tuning the RPC buffer pool for more on *HBASE\\_OFFHEAPSIZE*). The default value of\n`hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is 0 which means MSLAB uses onheap, not offheap, chunks by default."},"14888":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5088","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-write-path","content":"`hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.chunksize` controls the size of each off-heap chunk. Default is `2097152` (2MB)."},"14889":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5089","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#offheap-write-path","content":"When a Cell is added to a MemStore, the bytes for that Cell are copied into these off-heap buffers (if `hbase.regionserver.offheap.global.memstore.size` is non-zero)\nand a Cell POJO will refer to this memory area. This can greatly reduce the on-heap occupancy of the MemStores and reduce the total heap utilization for RegionServers\nin a write-heavy workload. On-heap and off-heap memory utiliazation are tracked at multiple levels to implement low level and high level memory management.\nThe decision to flush a MemStore considers both the on-heap and off-heap usage of that MemStore. At the Region level, we sum the on-heap and off-heap usages and\ncompare them against the region flush size (128MB, by default). Globally, on-heap size occupancy of all memstores are tracked as well as off-heap size. When any of\nthese sizes breache the lower mark (`hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size.lower.limit`) or the maximum size `hbase.regionserver.global.memstore.size`), all\nregions are selected for forced flushes."},"14890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5090","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"Backup and restore is a standard operation provided by many databases. An effective backup and restore\nstrategy helps ensure that users can recover data in case of unexpected failures. The HBase backup and restore\nfeature helps ensure that enterprises using HBase as a canonical data repository can recover from catastrophic\nfailures. Another important feature is the ability to restore the database to a particular\npoint-in-time, commonly referred to as a snapshot."},"14891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5091","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"The HBase backup and restore feature provides the ability to create full backups and incremental backups on\ntables in an HBase cluster. The full backup is the foundation on which incremental backups are applied\nto build iterative snapshots. Incremental backups can be run on a schedule to capture changes over time,\nfor example by using a Cron task. Incremental backups are more cost-effective than full backups because they only capture\nthe changes since the last backup and they also enable administrators to restore the database to any prior incremental backup. Furthermore, the\nutilities also enable table-level data backup-and-recovery if you do not want to restore the entire dataset\nof the backup."},"14892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5092","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"The backup and restore feature supplements the HBase Replication feature. While HBase replication is ideal for\ncreating \"hot\" copies of the data (where the replicated data is immediately available for query), the backup and\nrestore feature is ideal for creating \"cold\" copies of data (where a manual step must be taken to restore the system).\nPreviously, users only had the ability to create full backups via the ExportSnapshot functionality. The incremental\nbackup implementation is the novel improvement over the previous \"art\" provided by ExportSnapshot."},"14893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5093","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"The backup and restore feature uses DistCp to transfer files between clusters .\nHADOOP-15850 fixes a bug where CopyCommitter#concatFileChunks\nunconditionally tried to concatenate the files being DistCp'ed to target cluster (though the files are\nindependent) . Without the fix from\nHADOOP-15850 , the transfer would fail.\nSo the backup and restore feature need hadoop version as below"},"14894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5094","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"2.7.x"},"14895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5095","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"2.8.x"},"14896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5096","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"2.9.2+"},"14897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5097","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"2.10.0+"},"14898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5098","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"3.0.4+"},"14899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5099","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"3.1.2+"},"14900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5100","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"3.2.0+"},"14901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5101","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview","content":"3.3.0+"},"14902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5102","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"The backup and restore feature introduces new terminology which can be used to understand how control flows through the\nsystem."},"14903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5103","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"*A backup*: A logical unit of data and metadata which can restore a table to its state at a specific point in time."},"14904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5104","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"*Full backup*: a type of backup which wholly encapsulates the contents of the table at a point in time."},"14905":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5105","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"*Incremental backup*: a type of backup which contains the changes in a table since a full backup."},"14906":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5106","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"*Backup set*: A user-defined name which references one or more tables over which a backup can be executed."},"14907":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5107","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#terminology","content":"*Backup ID*: A unique names which identifies one backup from the rest, e.g. `backupId_1467823988425`"},"14908":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5108","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#planning","content":"There are some common strategies which can be used to implement backup and restore in your environment. The following section\nshows how these strategies are implemented and identifies potential tradeoffs with each."},"14909":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5109","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#planning","content":"This backup and restore tools has not been tested on Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) enabled\nHDFS clusters. This is related to the open issue\nHBASE-16178."},"14910":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5110","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-within-a-cluster","content":"This strategy stores the backups on the same cluster as where the backup was taken. This approach is only appropriate for testing\nas it does not provide any additional safety on top of what the software itself already provides."},"14911":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5111","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-using-a-dedicated-cluster","content":"This strategy provides greater fault tolerance and provides a path towards disaster recovery. In this setting, you will\nstore the backup on a separate HDFS cluster by supplying the backup destination cluster's HDFS URL to the backup utility.\nYou should consider backing up to a different physical location, such as a different data center."},"14912":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5112","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-using-a-dedicated-cluster","content":"Typically, a backup-dedicated HDFS cluster uses a more economical hardware profile to save money."},"14913":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5113","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-to-the-cloud-or-a-storage-vendor-appliance","content":"Another approach to safeguarding HBase incremental backups is to store the data on provisioned, secure servers that belong\nto third-party vendors and that are located off-site. The vendor can be a public cloud provider or a storage vendor who uses\na Hadoop-compatible file system, such as S3 and other HDFS-compatible destinations."},"14914":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5114","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-to-the-cloud-or-a-storage-vendor-appliance","content":"The HBase backup utility does not support backup to multiple destinations. A workaround is to\nmanually create copies of the backup files from HDFS or S3."},"14915":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5115","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#first-time-configuration-steps","content":"This section contains the necessary configuration changes that must be made in order to use the backup and restore feature.\nAs this feature makes significant use of YARN's MapReduce framework to parallelize these I/O heavy operations, configuration\nchanges extend outside of just `hbase-site.xml`."},"14916":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5116","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"The YARN *container-executor.cfg* configuration file must have the following property setting: *allowed.system.users=hbase*. No spaces\nare allowed in entries of this configuration file."},"14917":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5117","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"Skipping this step will result in runtime errors when executing the first backup tasks."},"14918":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5118","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#allow-the-hbase-system-user-in-yarn","content":"**Example of a valid container-executor.cfg file for backup and restore:**"},"14919":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5119","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-specific-changes","content":"Add the following properties to hbase-site.xml and restart HBase if it is already running."},"14920":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5120","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-specific-changes","content":"The \",...\" is an ellipsis meant to imply that this is a comma-separated list of values, not\nliteral text which should be added to hbase-site.xml."},"14921":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5121","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#commands","content":"This covers the command-line utilities that administrators would run to create, restore, and merge backups. Tools to\ninspect details on specific backup sessions is covered in the next section, Administration of Backup Images."},"14922":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5122","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#commands","content":"Run the command `hbase backup help <command>` to access the online help that provides basic information about a command\nand its options. The below information is captured in this help message for each command."},"14923":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5123","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-backup-image","content":"For HBase clusters also using Apache Phoenix: include the SQL system catalog tables in the backup.\nIn the event that you need to restore the HBase backup, access to the system catalog tables enable\nyou to resume Phoenix interoperability with the restored data."},"14924":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5124","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-backup-image","content":"The first step in running the backup and restore utilities is to perform a full backup and to store the data in a separate image\nfrom the source. At a minimum, you must do this to get a baseline before you can rely on incremental backups."},"14925":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5125","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-backup-image","content":"Run the following command as HBase superuser:"},"14926":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5126","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-backup-image","content":"After the command finishes running, the console prints a SUCCESS or FAILURE status message. The SUCCESS message includes a *backup* ID.\nThe backup ID is the Unix time (also known as Epoch time) that the HBase master received the backup request from the client."},"14927":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5127","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#creating-a-backup-image","content":"Record the backup ID that appears at the end of a successful backup. In case the source cluster\nfails and you need to recover the dataset with a restore operation, having the backup ID readily\navailable can save time."},"14928":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5128","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***type***\\\nThe type of backup to execute: *full* or *incremental*. As a reminder, an *incremental* backup requires a *full* backup to\nalready exist."},"14929":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5129","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_path***\\\nThe *backup\\_path* argument specifies the full filesystem URI of where to store the backup image. Valid prefixes are\n&#x2A;hdfs:*, &#x2A;webhdfs:*, &#x2A;s3a:* or other compatible Hadoop File System implementations."},"14930":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5130","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name\\[,table\\_name]>***\\\nA comma-separated list of tables to back up. If no tables are specified, all tables are backed up. No regular-expression or\nwildcard support is present; all table names must be explicitly listed. See Backup Set&#x73; for more\ninformation about peforming operations on collections of tables. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option; one of these\nnamed options are required."},"14931":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5131","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\\nIdentify tables to backup based on a backup set. See Using Backup Set&#x73; for the purpose and usage\nof backup sets. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"14932":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5132","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-w \\<number\\_workers>***\\\n(Optional) Specifies the number of parallel workers to copy data to backup destination. Backups are currently executed by MapReduce jobs\nso this value corresponds to the number of Mappers that will be spawned by the job."},"14933":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5133","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-b \\<bandwidth\\_per\\_worker>***\\\n(Optional) Specifies the bandwidth of each worker in MB per second."},"14934":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5134","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-d***\\\n(Optional) Enables \"DEBUG\" mode which prints additional logging about the backup creation."},"14935":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5135","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-i***\\\n(Optional) Ignore checksum verify between source snapshot and exported snapshot. Especially when the source and target file system types\nare different, we should use -i option to skip checksum-checks."},"14936":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5136","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-q \\<name>***\\\n(Optional) Allows specification of the name of a YARN queue which the MapReduce job to create the backup should be executed in. This option\nis useful to prevent backup tasks from stealing resources away from other MapReduce jobs of high importance."},"14937":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5137","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-creating-a-backup-image-example-usage","content":"This command creates a full backup image of two tables, SALES2 and SALES3, in the HDFS instance who NameNode is host5:9000\nin the path */data/backup&#x2A;. The *-w* option specifies that no more than three parallel works complete the operation."},"14938":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5138","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#restoring-a-backup-image","content":"Run the following command as an HBase superuser. You can only restore a backup on a running HBase cluster because the data must be\nredistributed the RegionServers for the operation to complete successfully."},"14939":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5139","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_path***\\\nThe *backup\\_path* argument specifies the full filesystem URI of where to store the backup image. Valid prefixes are\n&#x2A;hdfs:*, &#x2A;webhdfs:*, &#x2A;s3a:* or other compatible Hadoop File System implementations."},"14940":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5140","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nThe backup ID that uniquely identifies the backup image to be restored."},"14941":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5141","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name\\[,table\\_name]>***\\\nA comma-separated list of tables to restore. See Backup Set&#x73; for more\ninformation about peforming operations on collections of tables. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option; one of these\nnamed options are required."},"14942":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5142","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\\nIdentify tables to backup based on a backup set. See Using Backup Set&#x73; for the purpose and usage\nof backup sets. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"14943":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5143","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-q \\<name>***\\\n(Optional) Allows specification of the name of a YARN queue which the MapReduce job to create the backup should be executed in. This option\nis useful to prevent backup tasks from stealing resources away from other MapReduce jobs of high importance."},"14944":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5144","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-c***\\\n(Optional) Perform a dry-run of the restore. The actions are checked, but not executed."},"14945":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5145","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-m \\<target\\_tables>***\\\n(Optional) A comma-separated list of tables to restore into. If this option is not provided, the original table name is used. When\nthis option is provided, there must be an equal number of entries provided in the `-t` option."},"14946":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5146","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-restoring-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-o***\\\n(Optional) Overwrites the target table for the restore if the table already exists."},"14947":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5147","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-of-usage","content":"This command restores two tables of an incremental backup image. In this example:\n• `/tmp/backup_incremental` is the path to the directory containing the backup image.\n• `backupId_1467823988425` is the backup ID.\n• `mytable1` and `mytable2` are the names of tables in the backup image to be restored."},"14948":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5148","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-of-usage","content":"If the namespace of a table being restored does not exist in the target environment, it will be\nautomatically created during the restore operation.\nHBASE-25707"},"14949":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5149","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#merging-incremental-backup-images","content":"This command can be used to merge two or more incremental backup images into a single incremental\nbackup image. This can be used to consolidate multiple, small incremental backup images into a single\nlarger incremental backup image. This command could be used to merge hourly incremental backups\ninto a daily incremental backup image, or daily incremental backups into a weekly incremental backup."},"14950":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5150","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_ids***\\\nA comma-separated list of incremental backup image IDs that are to be combined into a single image."},"14951":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5151","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-merging-incremental-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"14952":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5152","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"Backup sets can ease the administration of HBase data backups and restores by reducing the amount of repetitive input\nof table names. You can group tables into a named backup set with the `hbase backup set add` command. You can then use\nthe `-set` option to invoke the name of a backup set in the `hbase backup create` or `hbase restore` rather than list\nindividually every table in the group. You can have multiple backup sets."},"14953":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5153","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"Note the differentiation between the `hbase backup set add&#x60; command and the *-set* option. The\n`hbase backup set add` command must be run before using the `-set` option in a different command\nbecause backup sets must be named and defined before using backup sets as a shortcut."},"14954":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5154","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"If you run the `hbase backup set add` command and specify a backup set name that does not yet exist on your system, a new set\nis created. If you run the command with the name of an existing backup set name, then the tables that you specify are added\nto the set."},"14955":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5155","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"In this command, the backup set name is case-sensitive."},"14956":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5156","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"The metadata of backup sets are stored within HBase. If you do not have access to the original\nHBase cluster with the backup set metadata, then you must specify individual table names to\nrestore the data."},"14957":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5157","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-backup-sets","content":"To create a backup set, run the following command as the HBase superuser:"},"14958":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5158","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"The following list details subcommands of the hbase backup set command."},"14959":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5159","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"You must enter one (and no more than one) of the following subcommands after hbase backup set to\ncomplete an operation. Also, the backup set name is case-sensitive in the command-line utility."},"14960":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5160","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***add***\\\nAdds table\\[s] to a backup set. Specify a *backup\\_set\\_name* value after this argument to create a backup set."},"14961":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5161","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***remove***\\\nRemoves tables from the set. Specify the tables to remove in the tables argument."},"14962":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5162","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***list***\\\nLists all backup sets."},"14963":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5163","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***describe***\\\nDisplays a description of a backup set. The information includes whether the set has full\nor incremental backups, start and end times of the backups, and a list of the tables in the set. This subcommand must precede\na valid value for the *backup\\_set\\_name* value."},"14964":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5164","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-set-subcommands","content":"***delete***\\\nDeletes a backup set. Enter the value for the *backup\\_set\\_name* option directly after the `hbase backup set delete` command."},"14965":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5165","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_set\\_name***\\\nUse to assign or invoke a backup set name. The backup set name must contain only printable characters and cannot have any spaces."},"14966":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5166","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***tables***\\\nList of tables (or a single table) to include in the backup set. Enter the table names as a comma-separated list. If no tables\nare specified, all tables are included in the set."},"14967":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5167","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-commands-using-backup-sets-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"Maintain a log or other record of the case-sensitive backup set names and the corresponding tables\nin each set on a separate or remote cluster, backup strategy. This information can help you in\ncase of failure on the primary cluster."},"14968":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5168","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-of-usage-1","content":"Depending on the environment, this command results in *one* of the following actions:"},"14969":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5169","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-of-usage-1","content":"If the `Q1Data` backup set does not exist, a backup set containing tables `TEAM_3` and `TEAM_4` is created."},"14970":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5170","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-of-usage-1","content":"If the `Q1Data` backup set exists already, the tables `TEAM_3` and `TEAM_4` are added to the `Q1Data` backup set."},"14971":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5171","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#administration","content":"The `hbase backup` command has several subcommands that help with administering backup images as they accumulate. Most production\nenvironments require recurring backups, so it is necessary to have utilities to help manage the data of the backup repository.\nSome subcommands enable you to find information that can help identify backups that are relevant in a search for particular data.\nYou can also delete backup images."},"14972":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5172","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#administration","content":"The following list details each `hbase backup subcommand` that can help administer backups. Run the full command-subcommand line as\nthe HBase superuser."},"14973":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5173","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#managing-backup-progress","content":"You can monitor a running backup in another terminal session by running the *hbase backup progress* command and specifying the backup ID as an argument."},"14974":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5174","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#managing-backup-progress","content":"For example, run the following command as hbase superuser to view the progress of a backup"},"14975":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5175","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-progress-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nSpecifies the backup that you want to monitor by seeing the progress information. The backupId is case-sensitive."},"14976":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5176","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-manging-backup-progress-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"14977":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5177","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#managing-backup-history","content":"This command displays a log of backup sessions. The information for each session includes backup ID, type (full or incremental), the tables\nin the backup, status, and start and end time. Specify the number of backup sessions to display with the optional -n argument."},"14978":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5178","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\\\nSpecifies the backup that you want to monitor by seeing the progress information. The backupId is case-sensitive."},"14979":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5179","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-n \\<num\\_records>***\\\n(Optional) The maximum number of backup records (Default: 10)."},"14980":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5180","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-p \\<backup\\_root\\_path>***\\\nThe full filesystem URI of where backup images are stored."},"14981":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5181","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-s \\<backup\\_set\\_name>***\\&#xA;The name of the backup set to obtain history for. Mutually exclusive with the *-t* option."},"14982":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5182","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-managing-backup-history-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-t \\<table\\_name>***\\&#xA;The name of table to obtain history for. Mutually exclusive with the *-s* option."},"14983":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5183","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#describing-a-backup-image","content":"This command can be used to obtain information about a specific backup image."},"14984":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5184","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-command-line-arguments","content":"***backup\\_id***\nThe ID of the backup image to describe."},"14985":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5185","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-describing-a-backup-image-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"14986":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5186","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#deleting-backup-images","content":"The `hbase backup delete` command deletes backup images that are no longer needed."},"14987":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5187","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-l \\<backup\\_id1,backup\\_id2,...>***\\\nComma-separated list of backup IDs to delete."},"14988":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5188","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"***-k \\<days>***\\\nDeletes all backup images completed more than the specified number of days ago."},"14989":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5189","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-named-command-line-arguments","content":"These options are **mutually exclusive**. Only one of `-l` or `-k` may be used at a time."},"14990":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5190","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Delete specific backup images by ID:"},"14991":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5191","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Delete all backup images older than 30 days:"},"14992":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5192","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"Deleting a backup may affect all following incremental backups (in the same backup root) up to\nthe next full backup. For example, if you take a full backup every 2 weeks and\ndaily incremental backups, running `hbase backup delete -k 7` when the full backup is older than\n7 days will effectively remove the data for all subsequent incremental backups.\nThe backup IDs may still be listed, but their data will be gone."},"14993":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5193","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-deleting-backup-images-example-usage","content":"If the most recent backup is an incremental backup and you delete it,\nyou should run a **full backup** next.\nRunning another incremental backup immediately after may result in missing data in the\nbackup image. (See HBASE-28084)"},"14994":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5194","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-repair-command","content":"This command attempts to correct any inconsistencies in persisted backup metadata which exists as\nthe result of software errors or unhandled failure scenarios. While the backup implementation tries\nto correct all errors on its own, this tool may be necessary in the cases where the system cannot\nautomatically recover on its own."},"14995":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5195","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-positional-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"14996":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5196","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#backup-restore-administation-backup-repair-command-named-command-line-arguments","content":"None."},"14997":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5197","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuration-keys","content":"The backup and restore feature includes both required and optional configuration keys."},"14998":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5198","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.enable***: Controls whether or not the feature is enabled (Default: `false`). Set this value to `true`."},"14999":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5199","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.master.logcleaner.plugins***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked when cleaning logs in the HBase Master. Set\nthis value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.master.BackupLogCleaner` or append it to the current value."},"15000":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5200","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.procedure.master.classes***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked with the Procedure framework in the Master. Set\nthis value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.master.LogRollMasterProcedureManager` or append it to the current value."},"15001":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5201","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.procedure.regionserver.classes***: A comma-separated list of classes invoked with the Procedure framework in the RegionServer.\nSet this value to `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.regionserver.LogRollRegionServerProcedureManager` or append it to the current value."},"15002":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5202","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.coprocessor.region.classes***: A comma-separated list of RegionObservers deployed on tables. Set this value to\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupObserver` or append it to the current value."},"15003":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5203","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.coprocessor.master.classes***: A comma-separated list of MasterObservers deployed on tables. Set this value to\n`org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupMasterObserver` or append it to the current value."},"15004":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5204","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#required-properties","content":"***hbase.master.hfilecleaner.plugins***: A comma-separated list of HFileCleaners deployed on the Master. Set this value\nto `org.apache.hadoop.hbase.backup.BackupHFileCleaner` or append it to the current value."},"15005":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5205","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.system.ttl***: The time-to-live in seconds of data in the `hbase:backup` tables (default: forever). This property\nis only relevant prior to the creation of the `hbase:backup` table. Use the `alter` command in the HBase shell to modify the TTL\nwhen this table already exists. See the below section for more details on the impact of this\nconfiguration property."},"15006":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5206","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.attempts.max***: The number of attempts to perform when taking hbase table snapshots (default: 10)."},"15007":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5207","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.attempts.pause.ms***: The amount of time to wait between failed snapshot attempts in milliseconds (default: 10000)."},"15008":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5208","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#optional-properties","content":"***hbase.backup.logroll.timeout.millis***: The amount of time (in milliseconds) to wait for RegionServers to execute a WAL rolling\nin the Master's procedure framework (default: 30000)."},"15009":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5209","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#formulate-a-restore-strategy-and-test-it","content":"Before you rely on a backup and restore strategy for your production environment, identify how backups must be performed,\nand more importantly, how restores must be performed. Test the plan to ensure that it is workable.\nAt a minimum, store backup data from a production cluster on a different cluster or server. To further safeguard the data,\nuse a backup location that is at a different physical location."},"15010":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5210","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#formulate-a-restore-strategy-and-test-it","content":"If you have a unrecoverable loss of data on your primary production cluster as a result of computer system issues, you may\nbe able to restore the data from a different cluster or server at the same site. However, a disaster that destroys the whole\nsite renders locally stored backups useless. Consider storing the backup data and necessary resources (both computing capacity\nand operator expertise) to restore the data at a site sufficiently remote from the production site. In the case of a catastrophe\nat the whole primary site (fire, earthquake, etc.), the remote backup site can be very valuable."},"15011":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5211","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#secure-a-full-backup-image-first","content":"As a baseline, you must complete a full backup of HBase data at least once before you can rely on incremental backups. The full\nbackup should be stored outside of the source cluster. To ensure complete dataset recovery, you must run the restore utility\nwith the option to restore baseline full backup. The full backup is the foundation of your dataset. Incremental backup data\nis applied on top of the full backup during the restore operation to return you to the point in time when backup was last taken."},"15012":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5212","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#define-and-use-backup-sets-for-groups-of-tables-that-are-logical-subsets-of-the-entire-dataset","content":"You can group tables into an object called a backup set. A backup set can save time when you have a particular group of tables\nthat you expect to repeatedly back up or restore."},"15013":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5213","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#define-and-use-backup-sets-for-groups-of-tables-that-are-logical-subsets-of-the-entire-dataset","content":"When you create a backup set, you type table names to include in the group. The backup set includes not only groups of related\ntables, but also retains the HBase backup metadata. Afterwards, you can invoke the backup set name to indicate what tables apply\nto the command execution instead of entering all the table names individually."},"15014":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5214","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#document-the-backup-and-restore-strategy-and-ideally-log-information-about-each-backup","content":"Document the whole process so that the knowledge base can transfer to new administrators after employee turnover. As an extra\nsafety precaution, also log the calendar date, time, and other relevant details about the data of each backup. This metadata\ncan potentially help locate a particular dataset in case of source cluster failure or primary site disaster. Maintain duplicate\ncopies of all documentation: one copy at the production cluster site and another at the backup location or wherever it can be\naccessed by an administrator remotely from the production cluster."},"15015":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5215","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"This scenario describes how a hypothetical retail business uses backups to safeguard application data and then restore the dataset\nafter failure."},"15016":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5216","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The HBase administration team uses backup sets to store data from a group of tables that have interrelated information for an\napplication called green. In this example, one table contains transaction records and the other contains customer details. The\ntwo tables need to be backed up and be recoverable as a group."},"15017":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5217","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin team also wants to ensure daily backups occur automatically."},"15018":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5218","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The following is an outline of the steps and examples of commands that are used to backup the data for the *green* application and\nto recover the data later. All commands are run when logged in as HBase superuser."},"15019":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5219","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"A backup set called *green\\_set* is created as an alias for both the transactions table and the customer table. The backup set can\nbe used for all operations to avoid typing each table name. The backup set name is case-sensitive and should be formed with only\nprintable characters and without spaces."},"15020":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5220","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The first backup of green\\_set data must be a full backup. The following command example shows how credentials are passed to Amazon\nS3 and specifies the file system with the s3a: prefix."},"15021":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5221","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"Incremental backups should be run according to a schedule that ensures essential data recovery in the event of a catastrophe. At\nthis retail company, the HBase admin team decides that automated daily backups secures the data sufficiently. The team decides that\nthey can implement this by modifying an existing Cron job that is defined in `/etc/crontab`. Consequently, IT modifies the Cron job\nby adding the following line:"},"15022":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5222","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"A catastrophic IT incident disables the production cluster that the green application uses. An HBase system administrator of the\nbackup cluster must restore the *green\\_set* dataset to the point in time closest to the recovery objective."},"15023":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5223","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"If the administrator of the backup HBase cluster has the backup ID with relevant details in accessible records, the following\nsearch with the `hdfs dfs -ls` command and manually scanning the backup ID list can be bypassed. Consider continuously maintaining\nand protecting a detailed log of backup IDs outside the production cluster in your environment."},"15024":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5224","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The HBase administrator runs the following command on the directory where backups are stored to print the list of successful backup\nIDs on the console:"},"15025":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5225","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin scans the list to see which backup was created at a date and time closest to the recovery objective. To do this, the\nadmin converts the calendar timestamp of the recovery point in time to Unix time because backup IDs are uniquely identified with\nUnix time. The backup IDs are listed in reverse chronological order, meaning the most recent successful backup appears first."},"15026":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5226","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin notices that the following line in the command output corresponds with the *green\\_set* backup that needs to be restored:"},"15027":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5227","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scenario-safeguarding-application-datasets-on-amazon-s3","content":"The admin restores green\\_set invoking the backup ID and the -overwrite option. The -overwrite option truncates all existing data\nin the destination and populates the tables with data from the backup dataset. Without this flag, the backup data is appended to the\nexisting data in the destination. In this case, the admin decides to overwrite the data because it is corrupted."},"15028":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5228","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-of-backup-data","content":"With this feature which makes copying data to remote locations, it's important to take a moment to clearly state the procedural\nconcerns that exist around data security. Like the HBase replication feature, backup and restore provides the constructs to automatically\ncopy data from within a corporate boundary to some system outside of that boundary. It is imperative when storing sensitive data that with backup and restore, much\nless any feature which extracts data from HBase, the locations to which data is being sent has undergone a security audit to ensure\nthat only authenticated users are allowed to access that data."},"15029":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5229","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#security-of-backup-data","content":"For example, with the above example of backing up data to S3, it is of the utmost importance that the proper permissions are assigned\nto the S3 bucket to ensure that only a minimum set of authorized users are allowed to access this data. Because the data is no longer\nbeing accessed via HBase, and its authentication and authorization controls, we must ensure that the filesystem storing that data is\nproviding a comparable level of security. This is a manual step which users **must** implement on their own."},"15030":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5230","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"HBase incremental backups enable more efficient capture of HBase table images than previous attempts\nat serial backup and restore solutions, such as those that only used HBase Export and Import APIs.\nIncremental backups use Write Ahead Logs (WALs) to capture the data changes since the\nprevious backup was created. A WAL roll (create new WALs) is executed across all RegionServers\nto track the WALs that need to be in the backup.\nIn addition to WALs, incremental backups also track bulk-loaded HFiles for tables under backup."},"15031":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5231","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"Incremental backup gathers all WAL files generated since the last backup from the source cluster,\nconverts them to HFiles in a `.tmp` directory under the `BACKUP_ROOT`, and then moves these\nHFiles to their final location under the backup root directory to form the backup image.\nIt also reads bulk load records from the backup system table, forms the paths for the corresponding\nbulk-loaded HFiles, and copies those files to the backup destination.\nBulk-loaded files are preserved (not deleted by cleaner chores) until they've been included in a\nbackup (for each backup root).\nA process similar to the DistCp (distributed copy) tool is used to move the backup files to the\ntarget file system."},"15032":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5232","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"When a table restore operation starts, a two-step process is initiated.\nFirst, the full backup is restored from the full backup image.\nSecond, all HFiles from incremental backups between the last full backup and the incremental backup\nbeing restored (including bulk-loaded HFiles) are bulk loaded into the table using the\nHBase Bulk Load utility."},"15033":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5233","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#technical-details-of-incremental-backup-and-restore","content":"You can only restore on a live HBase cluster because the data must be redistributed to complete the restore operation successfully."},"15034":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5234","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"As a reminder, incremental backups are implemented via retaining the write-ahead logs which HBase primarily uses for data durability.\nThus, to ensure that all data needing to be included in a backup is still available in the system, the HBase backup and restore feature\nretains all write-ahead logs since the last backup until the next incremental backup is executed."},"15035":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5235","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"Like HBase Snapshots, this can have an expectedly large impact on the HDFS usage of HBase for high volume tables. Take care in enabling\nand using the backup and restore feature, specifically with a mind to removing backup sessions when they are not actively being used."},"15036":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5236","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#a-warning-on-file-system-growth","content":"The only automated, upper-bound on retained write-ahead logs for backup and restore is based on the TTL of the `hbase:backup` system table which,\nas of the time this document is written, is infinite (backup table entries are never automatically deleted). This requires that administrators\nperform backups on a schedule whose frequency is relative to the amount of available space on HDFS (e.g. less available HDFS space requires\nmore aggressive backup merges and deletions). As a reminder, the TTL can be altered on the `hbase:backup` table using the `alter` command\nin the HBase shell. Modifying the configuration property `hbase.backup.system.ttl` in hbase-site.xml after the system table exists has no effect."},"15037":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5237","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#capacity-planning","content":"When designing a distributed system deployment, it is critical that some basic mathmatical rigor is executed to ensure sufficient computational\ncapacity is available given the data and software requirements of the system. For this feature, the availability of network capacity is the largest\nbottleneck when estimating the performance of some implementation of backup and restore. The second most costly function is the speed at which\ndata can be read/written."},"15038":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5238","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"To estimate the duration of a full backup, we have to understand the general actions which are invoked:"},"15039":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5239","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"Write-ahead log roll on each RegionServer: ones to tens of seconds per RegionServer in parallel. Relative to the load on each RegionServer."},"15040":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5240","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"Take an HBase snapshot of the table(s): tens of seconds. Relative to the number of regions and files that comprise the table."},"15041":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5241","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"Export the snapshot to the destination: see below. Relative to the size of the data and the network bandwidth to the destination."},"15042":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5242","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"To approximate how long the final step will take, we have to make some assumptions on hardware. Be aware that these will *not* be accurate for your\nsystem — these are numbers that your or your administrator know for your system. Let's say the speed of reading data from HDFS on a single node is\ncapped at 80MB/s (across all Mappers that run on that host), a modern network interface controller (NIC) supports 10Gb/s, the top-of-rack switch can\nhandle 40Gb/s, and the WAN between your clusters is 10Gb/s. This means that you can only ship data to your remote at a speed of 1.25GB/s — meaning\nthat 16 nodes (`1.25 * 1024 / 80 = 16`) participating in the ExportSnapshot should be able to fully saturate the link between clusters. With more\nnodes in the cluster, we can still saturate the network but at a lesser impact on any one node which helps ensure local SLAs are made. If the size\nof the snapshot is 10TB, this would full backup would take in the ballpark of 2.5 hours (`10 * 1024 / 1.25 / (60 * 60) = 2.23hrs`)"},"15043":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5243","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"As a general statement, it is very likely that the WAN bandwidth between your local cluster and the remote storage is the largest\nbottleneck to the speed of a full backup."},"15044":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5244","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#full-backups","content":"When the concern is restricting the computational impact of backups to a \"production system\", the above formulas can be reused with the optional\ncommand-line arguments to `hbase backup create`: `-b`, `-w`, `-q`. The `-b` option defines the bandwidth at which each worker (Mapper) would\nwrite data. The `-w` argument limits the number of workers that would be spawned in the DistCp job. The `-q` allows the user to specify a YARN\nqueue which can limit the specific nodes where the workers will be spawned — this can quarantine the backup workers performing the copy to\na set of non-critical nodes. Relating the `-b` and `-w` options to our earlier equations: `-b` would be used to restrict each node from reading\ndata at the full 80MB/s and `-w` is used to limit the job from spawning 16 worker tasks."},"15045":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5245","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"Like we did for full backups, we have to understand the incremental backup process to approximate its runtime and cost."},"15046":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5246","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"Identify new write-ahead logs since the last full or incremental backup: negligible. Apriori knowledge from the backup system table(s)."},"15047":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5247","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"Read, filter, and write \"minimized\" HFiles equivalent to the WALs: dominated by the speed of writing data. Relative to write speed of HDFS."},"15048":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5248","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"Read bulk load records from the backup system table, form the paths for bulk-loaded HFiles, and copy them to the backup destination."},"15049":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5249","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"DistCp the HFiles to the destination: see above."},"15050":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5250","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#incremental-backup","content":"For the second step, the dominating cost of this operation would be the re-writing the data (under the assumption that a majority of the\ndata in the WAL is preserved). In this case, we can assume an aggregate write speed of 30MB/s per node. Continuing our 16-node cluster example,\nthis would require approximately 15 minutes to perform this step for 50GB of data (50 \\* 1024 / 60 / 60 = 14.2). The amount of time to start the\nDistCp MapReduce job would likely dominate the actual time taken to copy the data (50 / 1.25 = 40 seconds) and can be ignored."},"15051":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5251","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Serial backup operations**\\\nBackup operations cannot be run concurrently. An operation includes actions like create, delete, restore, and merge. Only one active backup session is supported. HBASE-16391\nwill introduce multiple-backup sessions support."},"15052":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5252","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**No means to cancel backups**\\\nBoth backup and restore operations cannot be canceled. (HBASE-15997, HBASE-15998).\nThe workaround to cancel a backup would be to kill the client-side backup command (`control-C`), ensure all relevant MapReduce jobs have exited, and then\nrun the `hbase backup repair` command to ensure the system backup metadata is consistent."},"15053":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5253","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Backups can only be saved to a single location**\\\nCopying backup information to multiple locations is an exercise left to the user. HBASE-15476 will\nintroduce the ability to specify multiple-backup destinations intrinsically."},"15054":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5254","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**HBase superuser access is required**\\\nOnly an HBase superuser (e.g. hbase) is allowed to perform backup/restore, can pose a problem for shared HBase installations. Current mitigations would require\ncoordination with system administrators to build and deploy a backup and restore strategy (HBASE-14138)."},"15055":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5255","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Backup restoration is an online operation**\\\nTo perform a restore from a backup, it requires that the HBase cluster is online as a caveat of the current implementation (HBASE-16573)."},"15056":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5256","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Some operations may fail and require re-run**\\\nThe HBase backup feature is primarily client driven. While there is the standard HBase retry logic built into the HBase Connection, persistent errors in executing operations\nmay propagate back to the client (e.g. snapshot failure due to region splits). The backup implementation should be moved from client-side into the ProcedureV2 framework\nin the future which would provide additional robustness around transient/retryable failures. The `hbase backup repair` command is meant to correct states which the system\ncannot automatically detect and recover from."},"15057":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5257","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Avoidance of declaration of public API**\\\nWhile the Java API to interact with this feature exists and its implementation is separated from an interface, insufficient rigor has been applied to determine if\nit is exactly what we intend to ship to users. As such, it is marked as for a `Private` audience with the expectation that, as users begin to try the feature, there\nwill be modifications that would necessitate breaking compatibility (HBASE-17517)."},"15058":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5258","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#limitations-of-the-backup-and-restore-utility","content":"**Lack of global metrics for backup and restore**\\\nIndividual backup and restore operations contain metrics about the amount of work the operation included, but there is no centralized location (e.g. the Master UI)\nwhich present information for consumption (HBASE-16565)."},"15059":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5259","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sync-replication-background","content":"The current Cluster Replication in HBase in asynchronous. So if the master cluster crashes, the slave cluster may not have the\nnewest data. If users want strong consistency then they can not switch to the slave cluster."},"15060":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5260","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#design","content":"Please see the design doc on HBASE-19064"},"15061":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5261","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"Add a synchronous peer in both source cluster and peer cluster."},"15062":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5262","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"For source cluster:"},"15063":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5263","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"For peer cluster:"},"15064":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5264","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"For synchronous replication, the current implementation require that we have the same peer id for\nboth source and peer cluster. Another thing that need attention is: the peer does not support\ncluster-level, namespace-level, or cf-level replication, only support table-level replication now."},"15065":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5265","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"Transit the peer cluster to be STANDBY state"},"15066":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5266","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"Transit the source cluster to be ACTIVE state"},"15067":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5267","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case1-setup-two-synchronous-replication-clusters","content":"Now, the synchronous replication has been set up successfully. the HBase client can only request to source cluster, if\nrequest to peer cluster, the peer cluster which is STANDBY state now will reject the read/write requests."},"15068":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5268","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case2-how-to-operate-when-standby-cluster-crashed","content":"If the standby cluster has been crashed, it will fail to write remote WAL for the active cluster. So we need to transit\nthe source cluster to DOWNGRANDE\\_ACTIVE state, which means source cluster won't write any remote WAL any more, but\nthe normal replication (asynchronous Replication) can still work fine, it queue the newly written WALs, but the\nreplication block until the peer cluster come back."},"15069":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5269","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case2-how-to-operate-when-standby-cluster-crashed","content":"Once the peer cluster come back, we can just transit the source cluster to ACTIVE, to ensure that the replication will be\nsynchronous."},"15070":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5270","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case3-how-to-operate-when-active-cluster-crashed","content":"If the active cluster has been crashed (it may be not reachable now), so let's just transit the standby cluster to\nDOWNGRADE\\_ACTIVE state, and after that, we should redirect all the requests from client to the DOWNGRADE\\_ACTIVE cluster."},"15071":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5271","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case3-how-to-operate-when-active-cluster-crashed","content":"If the crashed cluster come back again, we just need to transit it to STANDBY directly. Otherwise if you transit the\ncluster to DOWNGRADE\\_ACTIVE, the original ACTIVE cluster may have redundant data compared to the current ACTIVE\ncluster. Because we designed to write source cluster WALs and remote cluster WALs concurrently, so it's possible that\nthe source cluster WALs has more data than the remote cluster, which result in data inconsistency. The procedure of\ntransiting ACTIVE to STANDBY has no problem, because we'll skip to replay the original WALs."},"15072":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5272","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#case3-how-to-operate-when-active-cluster-crashed","content":"After that, we can promote the DOWNGRADE\\_ACTIVE cluster to ACTIVE now, to ensure that the replication will be synchronous."},"15073":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5273","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-apis","content":"This information is not exhaustive, and provides a quick reference in addition to the User API Reference.\nThe examples here are not comprehensive or complete, and should be used for purposes of illustration only."},"15074":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5274","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-apis","content":"Apache HBase also works with multiple external APIs.\nSee Apache HBase External APIs for more information."},"15075":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5275","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#external-apis","content":"For information on using the native HBase APIs, refer to User API Reference and the HBase APIs chapter."},"15076":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5276","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest","content":"Representational State Transfer (REST) was introduced in 2000 in the doctoral dissertation of Roy Fielding, one of the principal authors of the HTTP specification."},"15077":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5277","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rest","content":"REST itself is out of the scope of this documentation, but in general, REST allows client-server interactions via an API that is tied to the URL itself. This section discusses how to configure and run the REST server included with HBase, which exposes HBase tables, rows, cells, and metadata as URL specified resources. There is also a nice series of blogs on How-to: Use the Apache HBase REST Interface by Jesse Anderson."},"15078":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5278","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#starting-and-stopping-the-rest-server","content":"The included REST server can run as a daemon which starts an embedded Jetty servlet container and deploys the servlet into it. Use one of the following commands to start the REST server in the foreground or background. The port is optional, and defaults to 8080."},"15079":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5279","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#starting-and-stopping-the-rest-server","content":"To stop the REST server, use Ctrl-C if you were running it in the foreground, or the following command if you were running it in the background."},"15080":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5280","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configuring-the-rest-server-and-client","content":"For information about configuring the REST server and client for SSL, as well as `doAs` impersonation for the REST server, see Configure the Thrift Gateway to Authenticate on Behalf of the Client and other portions of the Securing Apache HBase chapter."},"15081":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5281","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-rest-endpoints","content":"The following examples use the placeholder server http\\://example.com:8000, and the following commands can all be run using `curl` or `wget` commands. You can request plain text (the default), XML , or JSON output by adding no header for plain text, or the header \"Accept: text/xml\" for XML, \"Accept: application/json\" for JSON, or \"Accept: application/x-protobuf\" to for protocol buffers."},"15082":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5282","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-rest-endpoints","content":"Unless specified, use `GET` requests for queries, `PUT` or `POST` requests for creation or\nmutation, and `DELETE` for deletion."},"15083":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5283","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Endpoint"},"15084":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5284","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15085":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5285","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Description"},"15086":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5286","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"`/version/cluster`"},"15087":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5287","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"`GET`"},"15088":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5288","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints","content":"Version of HBase running on this cluster"},"15089":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5289","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cluster-wide-endpoints",