erations","content":"Scanning with a filter `PrefixFilter('row1')`. The filter should be specified according to the Thrift Filter Language and then encoded as `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` MIME format string."},"15243":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5443","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`/table/*?filter_b64=b64_encoded_filter`"},"15244":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5444","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`GET`"},"15245":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5445","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"Scanning with a filter `PrefixFilter('row1')`. The filter should be specified according to the Thrift Filter Language and then encoded in URL-safe base64."},"15246":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5446","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"**Examples:**"},"15247":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5447","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"The stateful scanner API expects clients to restart scans if there is a REST server failure in the midst. The stateless does not store any state related to scan operation and all the parameters are specified as query parameters."},"15248":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5448","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"The stateless endpoints are optimized for small results, while the stateful scanner\nAPI can also be used for large\nresults."},"15249":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5449","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"The following are the scan parameters:"},"15250":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5450","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`startrow` - The start row for the scan."},"15251":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5451","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`endrow` - The end row for the scan."},"15252":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5452","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`column` - The comma separated list of columns to scan."},"15253":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5453","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`starttime`, `endtime` - To only retrieve columns within a specific range of version timestamps, both start and end time must be specified."},"15254":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5454","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`maxversions` - To limit the number of versions of each column to be returned."},"15255":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5455","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`batchsize` - To limit the maximum number of values returned for each call to next()."},"15256":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5456","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`limit` - The number of rows to return in the scan operation."},"15257":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5457","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`cacheblocks` - Whether to use the Block Cache in the RegionServer. By default `true`."},"15258":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5458","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`reversed` - When set to `true`, reverse scan will be executed. By default `false`."},"15259":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5459","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`filter` - Allows to specify a filter for the scan as an `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` MIME format string."},"15260":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5460","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`filter_b64` - On versions which include the HBASE-28518 patch, `filter_b64` allows to specify a URL-safe base64 encoded filter for the scan. When both `filter` and `filter_b64` are specified, only `filter_b64` is considered."},"15261":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5461","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`includeStartRow` - Whether start row should be included in the scan. By default `true`."},"15262":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5462","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`includeStopRow` - Whether end row (stop row) should be included in the scan. By default `false`."},"15263":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5463","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"`includeStartRow` and `includeStopRow` are only supported on versions that include HBASE-28627."},"15264":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5464","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"Versions without this patch will either ignore these parameters or will error out if they are set to a non-default value."},"15265":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5465","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"More on start row, end row and limit parameters:"},"15266":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5466","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"If start row, end row and limit not specified, then the whole table will be scanned."},"15267":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5467","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"If start row and limit (say N) is specified, then the scan operation will return N rows from the start row specified."},"15268":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5468","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"If only limit parameter is specified, then the scan operation will return N rows from the start of the table."},"15269":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5469","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"If limit and end row are specified, then the scan operation will return N rows from start of table till the end row. If the end row is reached before N rows ( say M and M \\< N ), then M rows will be returned to the user."},"15270":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5470","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#stateless-endpoints-for-scan-operations","content":"If start row, end row and limit ( say N ) are specified and N \\< number of rows between start row and end row, then N rows from start row will be returned to the user. If N > (number of rows between start row and end row (say M), then M number of rows will be returned to the user."},"15271":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5471","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"Endpoint"},"15272":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5472","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15273":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5473","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"Description"},"15274":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5474","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"`/table/row_key`"},"15275":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5475","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"`PUT`"},"15276":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5476","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"Write a row to a table. The row, column qualifier, and value must each be Base-64 encoded. To encode a string, use the `base64` command-line utility. To decode the string, use `base64 -d`. The payload is in the `--data` argument, and the `/users/fakerow` value is a placeholder. Insert multiple rows by adding them to the `<CellSet>` element. You can also save the data to be inserted to a file and pass it to the `-d` parameter with syntax like `-d @filename.txt`."},"15277":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5477","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-put-operations","content":"**Example:**"},"15278":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5478","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Endpoint"},"15279":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5479","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15280":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5480","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Description"},"15281":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5481","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/?check=put`"},"15282":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5482","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`PUT`"},"15283":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5483","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Conditional Put - Change the current version value of a cell: Compare the current or latest version value (`current-version-value`) of a cell with the `check-value`, and if `current-version-value` == `check-value`, write new data (the `new-value`) into the cell as the current or latest version. The row, column qualifier, and value must each be Base-64 encoded. To encode a string, use the `base64` command-line utility. To decode the string, use `base64 -d`. The payload is in the `--data` or `-d` argument, with `the check cell name (column family:column name) and value` always at the end and right after `the new Put cell name (column family:column name) and value` of the same row key. You can also save the data to be inserted to a file and pass it to the `-d` parameter with syntax like `-d @filename.txt`."},"15284":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5484","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"**Example:**"},"15285":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5485","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Detailed Explanation:"},"15286":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5486","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"In the above json-format example:"},"15287":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5487","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`{\"column\":\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\", \"$\":\"TmV3R3V5\"}` at the end of `-d` option are `the check cell name and check cell value in Base-64` respectively: `\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\" for \"cfa:alias\"`, and `\"TmV3R3V5\" for \"NewGuy\"`"},"15288":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5488","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`{{\"column\":\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\",\"$\":\"T2xkR3V5\"}}` are `the new Put cell name and cell value in Base-64` respectively: `\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\" for \"cfa:alias\"`, and `\"T2xkR3V5\" for \"OldGuy\"`"},"15289":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5489","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`\"cm93MQ==\"` is `the Base-64 for \"row1\"` for the checkAndPut `row key`"},"15290":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5490","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"`\"/?check=put\"` after the `\"row key\" in the request URL` is required for checkAndPut WebHBase operation to work"},"15291":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5491","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"The `\"row key\" in the request URL` should be URL-encoded, e.g., `\"david%20chen\"` and `\"row1\"` are the URL-encoded formats of row keys `\"david chen\"` and `\"row1\"`, respectively"},"15292":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5492","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Note: \"cfa\" is the column family name and \"alias\" are the column (qualifier) name for the\nnon-Base64 encoded cell name."},"15293":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5493","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-put-operations","content":"Basically, the xml-format example is the same as the json-format example, and will not be explained here in detail."},"15294":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5494","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Endpoint"},"15295":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5495","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15296":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5496","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Description"},"15297":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5497","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/?check=delete`"},"15298":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5498","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`DELETE`"},"15299":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5499","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Conditional Deleting a Row: Compare the value of any version of a cell (`any-version-value`) with the `check-value`, and if `any-version-value` == `check-value`, delete the row specified by the `row_key` inside the requesting URL. The row, column qualifier, and value for checking in the payload must each be Base-64 encoded. To encode a string, use the base64 command-line utility. To decode the string, use base64 -d. The payload is in the --data argument. You can also save the data to be checked to a file and pass it to the `-d` parameter with syntax like `-d @filename.txt`."},"15300":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5500","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/column_family/?check=delete`"},"15301":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5501","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`DELETE`"},"15302":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5502","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Conditional Deleting a Column Family of a Row: Compare the value of any version of a cell (`any-version-value`) with the `check-value`, and if `any-version-value` == `check-value`, delete the column family of a row specified by the `row_key/column_family` inside the requesting URL. Anything else is the same as those in `Conditional Deleting a Row`."},"15303":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5503","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/column:qualifier/?check=delete`"},"15304":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5504","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`DELETE`"},"15305":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5505","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Conditional Deleting All Versions of a Column of a Row: Compare the value of any version of a cell (`any-version-value`) with the `check-value`, and if `any-version-value` == `check-value`, delete the column of a row specified by the `row_key/column:qualifier` inside the requesting URL. The `column:qualifier` in the requesting URL is the `column_family:column_name`. Anything else is the same as those in `Conditional Deleting a Row`."},"15306":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5506","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/column:qualifier/version_id/?check=delete`"},"15307":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5507","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`DELETE`"},"15308":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5508","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Conditional Deleting a Single Version of a Column of a Row: Compare the value of any version of a cell (`any-version-value`) with the `check-value`, and if `any-version-value` == `check-value`, delete the version of a column of a row specified by the `row_key/column:qualifier/version_id` inside the requesting URL. The `column:qualifier` in the requesting URL is the `column_family:column_name`. The `version_id` in the requesting URL is a number, which equals to `the timestamp of the targeted version + 1`. Anything else is the same as those in `Conditional Deleting a Row`."},"15309":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5509","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"**Examples:**"},"15310":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5510","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Detailed Explanation:"},"15311":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5511","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"In the above 4 json-format examples:"},"15312":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5512","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`{\"column\":\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\", \"$\":\"TmV3R3V5\"}` at the end of `-d` option are `the check cell name and check cell value in Base-64` respectively: `\"Y2ZhOmFsaWFz\" for \"cfa:alias\"`, and `\"TmV3R3V5\" for \"NewGuy\"`"},"15313":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5513","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`\"cm93MQ==\"` is `the Base-64 for \"row1\"` for the checkAndDelete `row key`"},"15314":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5514","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`\"/?check=delete\"` at the end of `the request URL` is required for checkAndDelete WebHBase operation to work"},"15315":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5515","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"`\"version_id\"` in the `request URL` of the last json-format example should be equivalent to the value of `\"the timestamp number + 1\"`"},"15316":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5516","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"The `\"row key\"`, `\"column family\"`, `\"cell name\" or \"column family:column name\"`, and `\"version_id\"` in `the request URL` of a checkAndDelete WebHBase operation should be URL-encoded, e.g., `\"row1\"`, `\"cfa\"`, `\"cfa:alias\"` and `\"1519423552160\"` in the examples are the URL-encoded `\"row key\"`, `\"column family\"`, `\"column family:column name\"`, and `\"version_id\"`, respectively"},"15317":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5517","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-check-and-delete-operations","content":"Basically, the 4 xml-format examples are the same as the 4 corresponding json-format examples, and will not be explained here in detail."},"15318":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5518","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"Endpoint"},"15319":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5519","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15320":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5520","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"Description"},"15321":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5521","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/?check=append`"},"15322":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5522","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"`PUT`"},"15323":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5523","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"Appends the given new value to the end of the current value of the cell. The row, column qualifier, and value must each be Base-64 encoded."},"15324":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5524","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-append-operations","content":"**Example:**"},"15325":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5525","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"Endpoint"},"15326":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5526","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"HTTP Verb"},"15327":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5527","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"Description"},"15328":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5528","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"`/table/row_key/?check=increment`"},"15329":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5529","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"`PUT`"},"15330":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5530","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"Increments the current value of the cell. The row, column qualifier, and value must each be Base-64 encoded."},"15331":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5531","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoints-for-increment-operations","content":"**Example:**"},"15332":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5532","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#thrift","content":"Documentation about Thrift has moved to Thrift API and Filter Language."},"15333":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5533","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cc-apache-hbase-client","content":"FB's Chip Turner wrote a pure C/C++ client. Check it out."},"15334":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5534","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cc-apache-hbase-client","content":"C++ client implementation. To see HBASE-14850."},"15335":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5535","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"Java Data Objects (JDO) is a standard way to access persistent data in databases, using plain old Java objects (POJO) to represent persistent data."},"15336":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5536","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"**Dependencies**"},"15337":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5537","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"This code example has the following dependencies:"},"15338":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5538","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"HBase 0.90.x or newer"},"15339":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5539","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"commons-beanutils.jar (https\\://commons.apache.org/)"},"15340":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5540","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"commons-pool-1.5.5.jar (https\\://commons.apache.org/)"},"15341":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5541","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"transactional-tableindexed for HBase 0.90 (https\\://github.com/hbase-trx/hbase-transactional-tableindexed)"},"15342":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5542","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"**Download `hbase-jdo`**\\\nDownload the code from http\\://code.google.com/p/hbase-jdo/."},"15343":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5543","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-java-data-objects-jdo-with-hbase","content":"**JDO Example**\\\nThis example uses JDO to create a table and an index, insert a row into a table, get a row, get a column value, perform a query, and do some additional HBase operations."},"15344":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5544","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setting-the-classpath","content":"To use Scala with HBase, your CLASSPATH must include HBase's classpath as well as the Scala JARs required by your code. First, use the following command on a server running the HBase RegionServer process, to get HBase's classpath."},"15345":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5545","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setting-the-classpath","content":"Set the `$CLASSPATH` environment variable to include the path you found in the previous step, plus the path of `scala-library.jar` and each additional Scala-related JAR needed for your project."},"15346":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5546","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scala-sbt-file","content":"Your `build.sbt` file needs the following `resolvers` and `libraryDependencies` to work with HBase."},"15347":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5547","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-scala-code","content":"This example lists HBase tables, creates a new table, adds a row to it, and gets the value of the row."},"15348":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5548","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setting-the-classpath-1","content":"To use Jython with HBase, your CLASSPATH must include HBase's classpath as well as the Jython JARs required by your code."},"15349":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5549","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setting-the-classpath-1","content":"Set the path to directory containing the `jython.jar` and each additional Jython-related JAR needed for your project. Then export HBASE\\_CLASSPATH pointing to the \\$JYTHON\\_HOME env. variable."},"15350":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5550","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#setting-the-classpath-1","content":"Start a Jython shell with HBase and Hadoop JARs in the classpath: \\$ bin/hbase org.python.util.jython"},"15351":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5551","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#jython-code-examples","content":"**Example: Table Creation, Population, Get, and Delete with Jython**\\\nThe following Jython code example checks for table, if it exists, deletes it and then creates it. Then it populates the table with data and fetches the data."},"15352":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5552","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#jython-code-examples","content":"**Example: Table Scan Using Jython**\\\nThis example scans a table and returns the results that match a given family qualifier."},"15353":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5553","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#thrift-filter-language","content":"You can configure Thrift for secure authentication at the server and client side, by following the procedures in Client-side Configuration for Secure Operation - Thrift Gateway and Configure the Thrift Gateway to Authenticate on Behalf of the Client."},"15354":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5554","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#thrift-filter-language","content":"The rest of this chapter discusses the filter language provided by the Thrift API."},"15355":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5555","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#filter-language","content":"Thrift Filter Language was introduced in HBase 0.92.\nIt allows you to perform server-side filtering when accessing HBase over Thrift or in the HBase shell.\nYou can find out more about shell integration by using the `scan help` command in the shell."},"15356":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5556","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#filter-language","content":"You specify a filter as a string, which is parsed on the server to construct the filter."},"15357":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5557","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"A simple filter expression is expressed as a string:"},"15358":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5558","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"Keep the following syntax guidelines in mind."},"15359":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5559","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"Specify the name of the filter followed by the comma-separated argument list in parentheses."},"15360":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5560","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"If the argument represents a string, it should be enclosed in single quotes (`'`)."},"15361":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5561","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"Arguments which represent a boolean, an integer, or a comparison operator (such as `<`, `>`, or `!=`), should not be enclosed in quotes"},"15362":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5562","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"The filter name must be a single word.\nAll ASCII characters are allowed except for whitespace, single quotes and parentheses."},"15363":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5563","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#general-filter-string-syntax","content":"The filter's arguments can contain any ASCII character.\nIf single quotes are present in the argument, they must be escaped by an additional preceding single quote."},"15364":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5564","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-operators","content":"**`AND`**\\\nIf the `AND` operator is used, the key-value must satisfy both filters."},"15365":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5565","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#binary-operators","content":"**`OR`**\\\nIf the `OR` operator is used, the key-value must satisfy at least one of the filters."},"15366":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5566","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unary-operators","content":"**`SKIP`**\\\nFor a particular row, if any of the key-values fail the filter condition, the entire row is skipped."},"15367":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5567","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unary-operators","content":"**`WHILE`**\\\nFor a particular row, key-values will be emitted until a key-value is reached that fails the filter condition."},"15368":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5568","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compound-operators","content":"You can combine multiple operators to create a hierarchy of filters, such as the following example:"},"15369":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5569","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#order-of-evaluation","content":"Parentheses have the highest precedence."},"15370":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5570","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#order-of-evaluation","content":"The unary operators `SKIP` and `WHILE` are next, and have the same precedence."},"15371":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5571","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#order-of-evaluation","content":"The binary operators follow. `AND` has highest precedence, followed by `OR`."},"15372":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5572","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#precedence-example","content":"You can use parentheses to explicitly control the order of evaluation."},"15373":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5573","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"The following compare operators are provided:"},"15374":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5574","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"LESS (`<`)"},"15375":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5575","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"LESS\\_OR\\_EQUAL (`<=`)"},"15376":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5576","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"EQUAL (`=`)"},"15377":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5577","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"NOT\\_EQUAL (`!=`)"},"15378":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5578","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"GREATER\\_OR\\_EQUAL (`>=`)"},"15379":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5579","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"GREATER (`>`)"},"15380":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5580","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"NO\\_OP (no operation)"},"15381":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5581","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compare-operator","content":"The client should use the symbols (`<`, `<=`, `=`, `!=`, `>`, `>=`) to express compare operators."},"15382":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5582","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"A comparator can be any of the following:"},"15383":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5583","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*BinaryComparator* - This lexicographically compares against the specified byte array using Bytes.compareTo(byte\\[], byte\\[])"},"15384":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5584","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*BinaryPrefixComparator* - This lexicographically compares against a specified byte array.\nIt only compares up to the length of this byte array."},"15385":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5585","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*RegexStringComparator* - This compares against the specified byte array using the given regular expression.\nOnly EQUAL and NOT\\_EQUAL comparisons are valid with this comparator"},"15386":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5586","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*SubStringComparator* - This tests if the given substring appears in a specified byte array.\nThe comparison is case insensitive.\nOnly EQUAL and NOT\\_EQUAL comparisons are valid with this comparator"},"15387":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5587","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"The general syntax of a comparator is: `ComparatorType:ComparatorValue`"},"15388":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5588","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"The ComparatorType for the various comparators is as follows:"},"15389":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5589","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*BinaryComparator* - binary"},"15390":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5590","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*BinaryPrefixComparator* - binaryprefix"},"15391":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5591","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*RegexStringComparator* - regexstring"},"15392":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5592","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"*SubStringComparator* - substring"},"15393":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5593","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#comparator","content":"The ComparatorValue can be any value."},"15394":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5594","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-comparatorvalues","content":"`binary:abc` will match everything that is lexicographically greater than \"abc\""},"15395":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5595","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-comparatorvalues","content":"`binaryprefix:abc` will match everything whose first 3 characters are lexicographically equal to \"abc\""},"15396":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5596","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-comparatorvalues","content":"`regexstring:ab*yz` will match everything that doesn't begin with \"ab\" and ends with \"yz\""},"15397":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5597","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-comparatorvalues","content":"`substring:abc123` will match everything that begins with the substring \"abc123\""},"15398":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5598","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"`\"PrefixFilter ('Row') AND PageFilter (1) AND FirstKeyOnlyFilter ()\"` will return all key-value pairs that match the following conditions:"},"15399":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5599","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The row containing the key-value should have prefix *Row*"},"15400":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5600","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The key-value must be located in the first row of the table"},"15401":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5601","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The key-value pair must be the first key-value in the row"},"15402":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5602","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"`\"(RowFilter (=, 'binary:Row 1') AND TimeStampsFilter (74689, 89734)) OR ColumnRangeFilter ('abc', true, 'xyz', false))\"` will return all key-value pairs that match both the following conditions:"},"15403":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5603","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The key-value is in a row having row key *Row 1*"},"15404":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5604","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The key-value must have a timestamp of either 74689 or 89734."},"15405":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5605","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"Or it must match the following condition:"},"15406":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5606","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"The key-value pair must be in a column that is lexicographically >= abc and \\< xyz"},"15407":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5607","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#example-filter-strings","content":"`\"SKIP ValueFilter (0)\"` will skip the entire row if any of the values in the row is not 0"},"15408":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5608","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**KeyOnlyFilter**\\\nThis filter doesn't take any arguments.\nIt returns only the key component of each key-value."},"15409":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5609","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**FirstKeyOnlyFilter**\\\nThis filter doesn't take any arguments.\nIt returns only the first key-value from each row."},"15410":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5610","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**PrefixFilter**\\\nThis filter takes one argument – a prefix of a row key.\nIt returns only those key-values present in a row that starts with the specified row prefix"},"15411":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5611","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**ColumnPrefixFilter**\\\nThis filter takes one argument – a column prefix.\nIt returns only those key-values present in a column that starts with the specified column prefix.\nThe column prefix must be of the form: `\"qualifier\"`."},"15412":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5612","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**MultipleColumnPrefixFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a list of column prefixes.\nIt returns key-values that are present in a column that starts with any of the specified column prefixes.\nEach of the column prefixes must be of the form: `\"qualifier\"`."},"15413":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5613","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**ColumnCountGetFilter**\\\nThis filter takes one argument – a limit.\nIt returns the first limit number of columns in the table."},"15414":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5614","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**PageFilter**\\\nThis filter takes one argument – a page size.\nIt returns page size number of rows from the table."},"15415":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5615","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**ColumnPaginationFilter**\\\nThis filter takes two arguments – a limit and offset.\nIt returns limit number of columns after offset number of columns.\nIt does this for all the rows."},"15416":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5616","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**InclusiveStopFilter**\\\nThis filter takes one argument – a row key on which to stop scanning.\nIt returns all key-values present in rows up to and including the specified row."},"15417":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5617","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**TimeStampsFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a list of timestamps.\nIt returns those key-values whose timestamps matches any of the specified timestamps."},"15418":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5618","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**RowFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a compare operator and a comparator.\nIt compares each row key with the comparator using the compare operator and if the comparison returns true, it returns all the key-values in that row."},"15419":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5619","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**Family Filter**\\\nThis filter takes a compare operator and a comparator.\nIt compares each column family name with the comparator using the compare operator and if the comparison returns true, it returns all the Cells in that column family."},"15420":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5620","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**QualifierFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a compare operator and a comparator.\nIt compares each qualifier name with the comparator using the compare operator and if the comparison returns true, it returns all the key-values in that column."},"15421":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5621","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**ValueFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a compare operator and a comparator.\nIt compares each value with the comparator using the compare operator and if the comparison returns true, it returns that key-value."},"15422":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5622","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**DependentColumnFilter**\\\nThis filter takes two arguments – a family and a qualifier.\nIt tries to locate this column in each row and returns all key-values in that row that have the same timestamp.\nIf the row doesn't contain the specified column – none of the key-values in that row will be returned."},"15423":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5623","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**SingleColumnValueFilter**\\\nThis filter takes a column family, a qualifier, a compare operator and a comparator.\nIf the specified column is not found – all the columns of that row will be emitted.\nIf the column is found and the comparison with the comparator returns true, all the columns of the row will be emitted.\nIf the condition fails, the row will not be emitted."},"15424":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5624","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**SingleColumnValueExcludeFilter**\\\nThis filter takes the same arguments and behaves same as SingleColumnValueFilter – however, if the column is found and the condition passes, all the columns of the row will be emitted except for the tested column value."},"15425":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5625","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#individual-filter-syntax","content":"**ColumnRangeFilter**\\\nThis filter is used for selecting only those keys with columns that are between minColumn and maxColumn.\nIt also takes two boolean variables to indicate whether to include the minColumn and maxColumn or not."},"15426":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5626","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"Spark itself is out of scope of this document, please refer to the Spark site for\nmore information on the Spark project and subprojects. This document will focus\non 4 main interaction points between Spark and HBase. Those interaction points are:"},"15427":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5627","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"**Basic Spark**\\\nThe ability to have an HBase Connection at any point in your Spark DAG."},"15428":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5628","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"**Spark Streaming**\\\nThe ability to have an HBase Connection at any point in your Spark Streaming\napplication."},"15429":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5629","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"**Spark Bulk Load**\\\nThe ability to write directly to HBase HFiles for bulk insertion into HBase"},"15430":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5630","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"**SparkSQL/DataFrames**\\\nThe ability to write SparkSQL that draws on tables that are represented in HBase."},"15431":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5631","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark","content":"The following sections will walk through examples of all these interaction points."},"15432":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5632","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-spark","content":"This section discusses Spark HBase integration at the lowest and simplest levels.\nAll the other interaction points are built upon the concepts that will be described\nhere."},"15433":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5633","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-spark","content":"At the root of all Spark and HBase integration is the HBaseContext. The HBaseContext\ntakes in HBase configurations and pushes them to the Spark executors. This allows\nus to have an HBase Connection per Spark Executor in a static location."},"15434":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5634","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#basic-spark","content":"For reference, Spark Executors can be on the same nodes as the Region Servers or\non different nodes, there is no dependence on co-location. Think of every Spark\nExecutor as a multi-threaded client application. This allows any Spark Tasks\nrunning on the executors to access the shared Connection object."},"15435":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5635","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"This example shows how HBaseContext can be used to do a `foreachPartition` on a RDD\nin Scala:"},"15436":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5636","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"Here is the same example implemented in Java:"},"15437":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5637","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"All functionality between Spark and HBase will be supported both in Scala and in\nJava, with the exception of SparkSQL which will support any language that is\nsupported by Spark. For the remaining of this documentation we will focus on\nScala examples."},"15438":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5638","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"The examples above illustrate how to do a foreachPartition with a connection. A\nnumber of other Spark base functions are supported out of the box:"},"15439":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5639","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"**`bulkPut`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of puts to HBase"},"15440":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5640","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"**`bulkDelete`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of deletes to HBase"},"15441":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5641","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"**`bulkGet`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of gets to HBase to create a new RDD"},"15442":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5642","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"**`mapPartition`**\\\nTo do a Spark Map function with a Connection object to allow full\naccess to HBase"},"15443":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5643","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"**`hbaseRDD`**\\\nTo simplify a distributed scan to create a RDD"},"15444":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5644","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbasecontext-usage-example","content":"For examples of all these functionalities, see the\nhbase-spark integration\nin the hbase-connectors repository\n(the hbase-spark connectors live outside hbase core in a related,\nApache HBase project maintained, associated repo)."},"15445":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5645","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"Spark Streaming is a micro batching stream\nprocessing framework built on top of Spark. HBase and Spark Streaming make great\ncompanions in that HBase can help serve the following benefits alongside Spark\nStreaming."},"15446":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5646","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"A place to grab reference data or profile data on the fly"},"15447":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5647","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"A place to store counts or aggregates in a way that supports Spark Streaming's\npromise of *only once processing*."},"15448":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5648","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"The hbase-spark integration\nwith Spark Streaming is similar to its normal Spark integration points, in that the following\ncommands are possible straight off a Spark Streaming DStream."},"15449":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5649","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"**`bulkPut`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of puts to HBase"},"15450":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5650","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"**`bulkDelete`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of deletes to HBase"},"15451":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5651","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"**`bulkGet`**\\\nFor massively parallel sending of gets to HBase to create a new RDD"},"15452":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5652","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"**`mapPartition`**\\\nTo do a Spark Map function with a Connection object to allow full\naccess to HBase"},"15453":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5653","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#spark-streaming","content":"**`hbaseRDD`**\\\nTo simplify a distributed scan to create a RDD"},"15454":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5654","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulkput-example-with-dstreams","content":"Below is an example of bulkPut with DStreams. It is very close in feel to the RDD\nbulk put."},"15455":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5655","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulkput-example-with-dstreams","content":"There are three inputs to the `hbaseBulkPut` function.\nThe hbaseContext that carries the configuration broadcast information link\nto the HBase Connections in the executor, the table name of the table we are\nputting data into, and a function that will convert a record in the DStream\ninto an HBase Put object."},"15456":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5656","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load","content":"There are two options for bulk loading data into HBase with Spark. There is the\nbasic bulk load functionality that will work for cases where your rows have\nmillions of columns and cases where your columns are not consolidated and\npartitioned before the map side of the Spark bulk load process."},"15457":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5657","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load","content":"There is also a thin record bulk load option with Spark. This second option is\ndesigned for tables that have less then 10k columns per row. The advantage\nof this second option is higher throughput and less over-all load on the Spark\nshuffle operation."},"15458":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5658","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load","content":"Both implementations work more or less like the MapReduce bulk load process in\nthat a partitioner partitions the rowkeys based on region splits and\nthe row keys are sent to the reducers in order, so that HFiles can be written\nout directly from the reduce phase."},"15459":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5659","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load","content":"In Spark terms, the bulk load will be implemented around a Spark\n`repartitionAndSortWithinPartitions` followed by a Spark `foreachPartition`."},"15460":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5660","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-load","content":"First lets look at an example of using the basic bulk load functionality"},"15461":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5661","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"The following example shows bulk loading with Spark."},"15462":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5662","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"The `hbaseBulkLoad` function takes three required parameters:"},"15463":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5663","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"The table name of the table we intend to bulk load too"},"15464":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5664","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"A function that will convert a record in the RDD to a tuple key value par. With\nthe tuple key being a KeyFamilyQualifer object and the value being the cell value.\nThe KeyFamilyQualifer object will hold the RowKey, Column Family, and Column Qualifier.\nThe shuffle will partition on the RowKey but will sort by all three values."},"15465":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5665","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"The temporary path for the HFile to be written out too"},"15466":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5666","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bulk-loading-example","content":"Following the Spark bulk load command, use the HBase's LoadIncrementalHFiles object\nto load the newly created HFiles into HBase."},"15467":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5667","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-parameters-for-bulk-loading-with-spark","content":"You can set the following attributes with additional parameter options on hbaseBulkLoad."},"15468":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5668","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-parameters-for-bulk-loading-with-spark","content":"Max file size of the HFiles"},"15469":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5669","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-parameters-for-bulk-loading-with-spark","content":"A flag to exclude HFiles from compactions"},"15470":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5670","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#additional-parameters-for-bulk-loading-with-spark","content":"Column Family settings for compression, bloomType, blockSize, and dataBlockEncoding"},"15471":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5671","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-additional-parameters","content":"Now lets look at how you would call the thin record bulk load implementation"},"15472":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5672","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-thin-record-bulk-load","content":"Note that the big difference in using bulk load for thin rows is the function\nreturns a tuple with the first value being the row key and the second value\nbeing an object of FamiliesQualifiersValues, which will contain all the\nvalues for this row for all column families."},"15473":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5673","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sparksqldataframes","content":"The hbase-spark integration\nleverages\nDataSource API\n(SPARK-3247)\nintroduced in Spark-1.2.0, which bridges the gap between simple HBase KV store and complex\nrelational SQL queries and enables users to perform complex data analytical work\non top of HBase using Spark. HBase Dataframe is a standard Spark Dataframe, and is able to\ninteract with any other data sources such as Hive, Orc, Parquet, JSON, etc.\nThe hbase-spark integration\napplies critical techniques such as partition pruning, column pruning,\npredicate pushdown and data locality."},"15474":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5674","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sparksqldataframes","content":"To use the\nhbase-spark integration\nconnector, users need to define the Catalog for the schema mapping\nbetween HBase and Spark tables, prepare the data and populate the HBase table,\nthen load the HBase DataFrame. After that, users can do integrated query and access records\nin HBase tables with SQL query. The following illustrates the basic procedure."},"15475":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5675","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#define-catalog","content":"Catalog defines a mapping between HBase and Spark tables. There are two critical parts of this catalog.\nOne is the rowkey definition and the other is the mapping between table column in Spark and\nthe column family and column qualifier in HBase. The above defines a schema for a HBase table\nwith name as table1, row key as key and a number of columns (col1 `-` col8). Note that the rowkey\nalso has to be defined in details as a column (col0), which has a specific cf (rowkey)."},"15476":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5676","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#save-the-dataframe","content":"`data` prepared by the user is a local Scala collection which has 256 HBaseRecord objects.\n`sc.parallelize(data)` function distributes `data` to form an RDD. `toDF` returns a DataFrame.\n`write` function returns a DataFrameWriter used to write the DataFrame to external storage\nsystems (e.g. HBase here). Given a DataFrame with specified schema `catalog`, `save` function\nwill create an HBase table with 5 regions and save the DataFrame inside."},"15477":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5677","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#load-the-dataframe","content":"In 'withCatalog' function, sqlContext is a variable of SQLContext, which is the entry point\nfor working with structured data (rows and columns) in Spark.\n`read` returns a DataFrameReader that can be used to read data in as a DataFrame.\n`option` function adds input options for the underlying data source to the DataFrameReader,\nand `format` function specifies the input data source format for the DataFrameReader.\nThe `load()` function loads input in as a DataFrame. The date frame `df` returned\nby `withCatalog` function could be used to access HBase table, such as 4.4 and 4.5."},"15478":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5678","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#language-integrated-query","content":"DataFrame can do various operations, such as join, sort, select, filter, orderBy and so on.\n`df.filter` above filters rows using the given SQL expression. `select` selects a set of columns:\n`col0`, `col1` and `col4`."},"15479":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5679","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sql-query","content":"`registerTempTable` registers `df` DataFrame as a temporary table using the table name `table1`.\nThe lifetime of this temporary table is tied to the SQLContext that was used to create `df`.\n`sqlContext.sql` function allows the user to execute SQL queries."},"15480":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5680","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-with-different-timestamps","content":"In HBaseSparkConf, four parameters related to timestamp can be set. They are TIMESTAMP,\nMIN\\_TIMESTAMP, MAX\\_TIMESTAMP and MAX\\_VERSIONS respectively. Users can query records with\ndifferent timestamps or time ranges with MIN\\_TIMESTAMP and MAX\\_TIMESTAMP. In the meantime,\nuse concrete value instead of tsSpecified and oldMs in the examples below."},"15481":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5681","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-with-different-timestamps","content":"The example below shows how to load df DataFrame with different timestamps.\ntsSpecified is specified by the user.\nHBaseTableCatalog defines the HBase and Relation relation schema.\nwriteCatalog defines catalog for the schema mapping."},"15482":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5682","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-with-different-timestamps","content":"The example below shows how to load df DataFrame with different time ranges.\noldMs is specified by the user."},"15483":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5683","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#query-with-different-timestamps","content":"After loading df DataFrame, users can query data."},"15484":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5684","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"The hbase-spark integration\nconnector supports different data formats like Avro, JSON, etc. The use case below\nshows how spark supports Avro. Users can persist the Avro record into HBase directly. Internally,\nthe Avro schema is converted to a native Spark Catalyst data type automatically.\nNote that both key-value parts in an HBase table can be defined in Avro format."},"15485":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5685","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"Define catalog for the schema mapping:"},"15486":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5686","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"`catalog` is a schema for a HBase table named `Avrotable`. row key as key and\none column col1. The rowkey also has to be defined in details as a column (col0),\nwhich has a specific cf (rowkey)."},"15487":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5687","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"Prepare the Data:"},"15488":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5688","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"`schemaString` is defined first, then it is parsed to get `avroSchema`. `avroSchema` is used to\ngenerate `AvroHBaseRecord`. `data` prepared by users is a local Scala collection\nwhich has 256 `AvroHBaseRecord` objects."},"15489":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5689","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"Save DataFrame:"},"15490":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5690","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"Given a data frame with specified schema `catalog`, above will create an HBase table with 5\nregions and save the data frame inside."},"15491":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5691","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"Load the DataFrame"},"15492":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5692","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"In `withCatalog` function, `read` returns a DataFrameReader that can be used to read data in as a DataFrame.\nThe `option` function adds input options for the underlying data source to the DataFrameReader.\nThere are two options: one is to set `avroSchema` as `AvroHBaseRecord.schemaString`, and one is to\nset `HBaseTableCatalog.tableCatalog` as `avroCatalog`. The `load()` function loads input in as a DataFrame.\nThe date frame `df` returned by `withCatalog` function could be used to access the HBase table."},"15493":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5693","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"SQL Query"},"15494":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5694","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#native-avro-support","content":"After loading df DataFrame, users can query data. registerTempTable registers df DataFrame\nas a temporary table using the table name avrotable. `sqlContext.sql` function allows the\nuser to execute SQL queries."},"15495":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5695","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"HBase Coprocessors are modeled after Google BigTable's coprocessor implementation\n(http\\://research.google.com/people/jeff/SOCC2010-keynote-slides.pdf pages 41-42.).\nEfforts are ongoing to bridge gaps between HBase's\nimplementation and BigTable's architecture. For more information see\nHBASE-4047."},"15496":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5696","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"The information in this chapter is primarily sourced and heavily reused from the following\nresources:"},"15497":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5697","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"Mingjie Lai's blog post\nCoprocessor Introduction."},"15498":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5698","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"Gaurav Bhardwaj's blog post\nThe How To Of HBase Coprocessors."},"15499":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5699","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"Coprocessors are an advanced feature of HBase and are intended to be used by system\ndevelopers only. Because coprocessor code runs directly on the RegionServer and has\ndirect access to your data, they introduce the risk of data corruption, man-in-the-middle\nattacks, or other malicious data access. Currently, there is no mechanism to prevent\ndata corruption by coprocessors, though work is underway on\nHBASE-4047."},"15500":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5700","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp","content":"In addition, there is no resource isolation, so a well-intentioned but misbehaving\ncoprocessor can severely degrade cluster performance and stability."},"15501":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5701","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-overview","content":"In HBase, you fetch data using a `Get` or `Scan`, whereas in an RDBMS you use a SQL\nquery. In order to fetch only the relevant data, you filter it using a HBase\nFilter\n, whereas in an RDBMS you use a `WHERE` predicate."},"15502":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5702","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-overview","content":"After fetching the data, you perform computations on it. This paradigm works well\nfor \"small data\" with a few thousand rows and several columns. However, when you scale\nto billions of rows and millions of columns, moving large amounts of data across your\nnetwork will create bottlenecks at the network layer, and the client needs to be powerful\nenough and have enough memory to handle the large amounts of data and the computations.\nIn addition, the client code can grow large and complex."},"15503":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5703","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-overview","content":"In this scenario, coprocessors might make sense. You can put the business computation\ncode into a coprocessor which runs on the RegionServer, in the same location as the\ndata, and returns the result to the client."},"15504":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5704","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-overview","content":"This is only one scenario where using coprocessors can provide benefit. Following\nare some analogies which may help to explain some of the benefits of coprocessors."},"15505":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5705","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**Triggers and Stored Procedure**\\\nAn Observer coprocessor is similar to a trigger in a RDBMS in that it executes\nyour code either before or after a specific event (such as a `Get` or `Put`)\noccurs. An endpoint coprocessor is similar to a stored procedure in a RDBMS\nbecause it allows you to perform custom computations on the data on the\nRegionServer itself, rather than on the client."},"15506":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5706","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**MapReduce**\\\nMapReduce operates on the principle of moving the computation to the location of\nthe data. Coprocessors operate on the same principal."},"15507":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5707","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-analogies","content":"**AOP**\\\nIf you are familiar with Aspect Oriented Programming (AOP), you can think of a coprocessor\nas applying advice by intercepting a request and then running some custom code,\nbefore passing the request on to its final destination (or even changing the destination)."},"15508":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5708","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Your class should implement one of the Coprocessor interfaces -\nCoprocessor,\nRegionObserver,\nCoprocessorService - to name a few."},"15509":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5709","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Load the coprocessor, either statically (from the configuration) or dynamically,\nusing HBase Shell. For more details see Loading Coprocessors."},"15510":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5710","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"Call the coprocessor from your client-side code. HBase handles the coprocessor\ntransparently."},"15511":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5711","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-implementation-overview","content":"The framework API is provided in the\ncoprocessor\npackage."},"15512":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5712","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#observer-coprocessors","content":"Observer coprocessors are triggered either before or after a specific event occurs.\nObservers that happen before an event use methods that start with a `pre` prefix,\nsuch as `prePut`. Observers that happen just after an event override methods that start\nwith a `post` prefix, such as `postPut`."},"15513":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5713","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Security**\\\nBefore performing a `Get` or `Put` operation, you can check for permission using\n`preGet` or `prePut` methods."},"15514":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5714","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Referential Integrity**\\\nHBase does not directly support the RDBMS concept of refential integrity, also known\nas foreign keys. You can use a coprocessor to enforce such integrity. For instance,\nif you have a business rule that every insert to the `users` table must be followed\nby a corresponding entry in the `user_daily_attendance` table, you could implement\na coprocessor to use the `prePut` method on `user` to insert a record into `user_daily_attendance`."},"15515":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5715","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#use-cases-for-observer-coprocessors","content":"**Secondary Indexes**\\\nYou can use a coprocessor to maintain secondary indexes. For more information, see\nSecondaryIndexing."},"15516":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5716","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**RegionObserver**\\\nA RegionObserver coprocessor allows you to observe events on a region, such as `Get`\nand `Put` operations. See\nRegionObserver."},"15517":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5717","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**RegionServerObserver**\\\nA RegionServerObserver allows you to observe events related to the RegionServer's\noperation, such as starting, stopping, or performing merges, commits, or rollbacks.\nSee\nRegionServerObserver."},"15518":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5718","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**MasterObserver**\\\nA MasterObserver allows you to observe events related to the HBase Master, such\nas table creation, deletion, or schema modification. See\nMasterObserver."},"15519":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5719","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"**WalObserver**\\\nA WalObserver allows you to observe events related to writes to the Write-Ahead\nLog (WAL). See\nWALObserver."},"15520":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5720","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#types-of-observer-coprocessor","content":"Examples provides working examples of observer coprocessors."},"15521":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5721","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Endpoint processors allow you to perform computation at the location of the data.\nSee Coprocessor Analogy. An example is the need to calculate a running\naverage or summation for an entire table which spans hundreds of regions."},"15522":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5722","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In contrast to observer coprocessors, where your code is run transparently, endpoint\ncoprocessors must be explicitly invoked using the\nCoprocessorService()\nmethod available in\nAsyncTable."},"15523":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5723","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"The coprocessorService method in Table\nhas been deprecated."},"15524":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5724","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In HBASE-21512\nwe reimplement the sync client based on the async client. The coprocessorService\nmethod defined in `Table` interface directly references a method from protobuf's\n`BlockingInterface`, which means we need to use a separate thread pool to execute\nthe method so we avoid blocking the async client(We want to avoid blocking calls in\nour async implementation)."},"15525":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5725","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Since coprocessor is an advanced feature, we believe it is OK for coprocessor users to\ninstead switch over to use `AsyncTable`. There is a lightweight\ntoAsyncConnection\nmethod to get an `AsyncConnection` from `Connection` if needed."},"15526":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5726","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Starting with HBase 0.96, endpoint coprocessors are implemented using Google Protocol\nBuffers (protobuf). For more details on protobuf, see Google's\nProtocol Buffer Guide.\nEndpoints Coprocessor written in version 0.94 are not compatible with version 0.96 or later.\nSee\nHBASE-5448). To upgrade your\nHBase cluster from 0.94 or earlier to 0.96 or later, you need to reimplement your\ncoprocessor."},"15527":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5727","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"In HBase 2.x, we made use of a shaded version of protobuf 3.x, but kept the\nprotobuf for coprocessors on 2.5.0. In HBase 3.0.0, we removed all dependencies on\nnon-shaded protobuf so you need to reimplement your coprocessor to make use of the\nshaded protobuf version provided in hbase-thirdparty. Please see\nthe protobuf section for more details."},"15528":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5728","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Coprocessor Endpoints should make no use of HBase internals and\nonly avail of public APIs; ideally a CPEP should depend on Interfaces\nand data structures only. This is not always possible but beware\nthat doing so makes the Endpoint brittle, liable to breakage as HBase\ninternals evolve. HBase internal APIs annotated as private or evolving\ndo not have to respect semantic versioning rules or general java rules on\ndeprecation before removal. While generated protobuf files are\nabsent the hbase audience annotations — they are created by the\nprotobuf protoc tool which knows nothing of how HBase works —\nthey should be consided `@InterfaceAudience.Private` so are liable to\nchange."},"15529":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5729","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#endpoint-coprocessor","content":"Examples provides working examples of endpoint coprocessors."},"15530":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5730","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#loading-coprocessors","content":"To make your coprocessor available to HBase, it must be *loaded*, either statically\n(through the HBase configuration) or dynamically (using HBase Shell or the Java API)."},"15531":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5731","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"Follow these steps to statically load your coprocessor. Keep in mind that you must\nrestart HBase to unload a coprocessor that has been loaded statically."},"15532":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5732","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"Define the Coprocessor in *hbase-site.xml*, with a `<property>` element with a `<name>`\nand a `<value>` sub-element. The `<name>` should be one of the following:"},"15533":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5733","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.region.classes` for RegionObservers and Endpoints."},"15534":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5734","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.wal.classes` for WALObservers."},"15535":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5735","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"`hbase.coprocessor.master.classes` for MasterObservers."},"15536":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5736","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"`<value>` must contain the fully-qualified class name of your coprocessor's implementation\nclass."},"15537":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5737","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"For example to load a Coprocessor (implemented in class SumEndPoint.java) you have to create\nfollowing entry in RegionServer's 'hbase-site.xml' file (generally located under 'conf' directory):"},"15538":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5738","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"If multiple classes are specified for loading, the class names must be comma-separated.\nThe framework attempts to load all the configured classes using the default class loader.\nTherefore, the jar file must reside on the server-side HBase classpath."},"15539":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5739","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"Coprocessors which are loaded in this way will be active on all regions of all tables.\nThese are also called system Coprocessor.\nThe first listed Coprocessors will be assigned the priority `Coprocessor.Priority.SYSTEM`.\nEach subsequent coprocessor in the list will have its priority value incremented by one (which\nreduces its priority, because priorities have the natural sort order of Integers)."},"15540":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5740","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"These priority values can be manually overriden in hbase-site.xml. This can be useful if you\nwant to guarantee that a coprocessor will execute after another. For example, in the following\nconfiguration `SumEndPoint` would be guaranteed to go last, except in the case of a tie with\nanother coprocessor:"},"15541":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5741","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"When calling out to registered observers, the framework executes their callbacks methods in the\nsorted order of their priority.\nTies are broken arbitrarily."},"15542":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5742","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"Put your code on HBase's classpath. One easy way to do this is to drop the jar\n(containing you code and all the dependencies) into the `lib/` directory in the\nHBase installation."},"15543":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5743","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-loading","content":"Restart HBase."},"15544":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5744","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-unloading","content":"Delete the coprocessor's `<property>` element, including sub-elements, from `hbase-site.xml`."},"15545":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5745","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-unloading","content":"Restart HBase."},"15546":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5746","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#static-unloading","content":"Optionally, remove the coprocessor's JAR file from the classpath or HBase's `lib/`\ndirectory."},"15547":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5747","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"You can also load a coprocessor dynamically, without restarting HBase. This may seem\npreferable to static loading, but dynamically loaded coprocessors are loaded on a\nper-table basis, and are only available to the table for which they were loaded. For\nthis reason, dynamically loaded tables are sometimes called **Table Coprocessor**."},"15548":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5748","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"In addition, dynamically loading a coprocessor acts as a schema change on the table,\nand the table must be taken offline to load the coprocessor."},"15549":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5749","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"There are three ways to dynamically load Coprocessor."},"15550":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5750","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"The below mentioned instructions makes the following assumptions:"},"15551":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5751","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"A JAR called `coprocessor.jar` contains the Coprocessor implementation along with all of its\ndependencies."},"15552":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5752","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#dynamic-loading","content":"The JAR is available in HDFS in some location like\n`hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/coprocessor.jar`."},"15553":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5753","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"Load the Coprocessor, using a command like the following:"},"15554":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5754","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"The Coprocessor framework will try to read the class information from the coprocessor table\nattribute value.\nThe value contains four pieces of information which are separated by the pipe (`|`) character."},"15555":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5755","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"File path: The jar file containing the Coprocessor implementation must be in a location where\nall region servers can read it.\nYou could copy the file onto the local disk on each region server, but it is recommended to store\nit in HDFS.\nHBASE-14548 allows a directory containing the jars\nor some wildcards to be specified, such as: `hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/` or\n`hdfs://NAMENODE:PORT/user/HADOOP_USER/*.jar`. Please note that if a directory is specified,\nall jar files(.jar) in the directory are added. It does not search for files in sub-directories.\nDo not use a wildcard if you would like to specify a directory. This enhancement applies to the\nusage via the JAVA API as well."},"15556":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5756","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"Class name: The full class name of the Coprocessor."},"15557":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5757","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"Priority: An integer. The framework will determine the execution sequence of all configured\nobservers registered at the same hook using priorities. This field can be left blank. In that\ncase the framework will assign a default priority value."},"15558":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5758","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"Arguments (Optional): This field is passed to the Coprocessor implementation. This is optional."},"15559":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5759","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"Verify that the coprocessor loaded:"},"15560":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5760","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell","content":"The coprocessor should be listed in the `TABLE_ATTRIBUTES`."},"15561":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5761","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-the-java-api-all-hbase-versions","content":"The following Java code shows how to use the `setValue()` method of `HTableDescriptor`\nto load a coprocessor on the `users` table."},"15562":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5762","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-the-java-api-hbase-096-only","content":"In HBase 0.96 and newer, the `addCoprocessor()` method of `HTableDescriptor` provides\nan easier way to load a coprocessor dynamically."},"15563":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5763","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-the-java-api-hbase-096-only","content":"There is no guarantee that the framework will load a given Coprocessor successfully. For example,\nthe shell command neither guarantees a jar file exists at a particular location nor verifies\nwhether the given class is actually contained in the jar file."},"15564":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5764","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell-1","content":"Alter the table to remove the coprocessor with `table_att_unset`."},"15565":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5765","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-hbase-shell-1","content":"Alter the table to remove the coprocessor with `table_remove_coprocessor` introduced in\nHBASE-26524 by specifying an explicit\nclassname"},"15566":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5766","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-the-java-api","content":"Reload the table definition without setting the value of the coprocessor either by\nusing `setValue()` or `addCoprocessor()` methods. This will remove any coprocessor\nattached to the table."},"15567":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5767","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#using-the-java-api","content":"In HBase 0.96 and newer, you can instead use the `removeCoprocessor()` method of the\n`HTableDescriptor` class."},"15568":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5768","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"HBase ships examples for Observer Coprocessor."},"15569":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5769","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"A more detailed example is given below."},"15570":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5770","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"These examples assume a table called `users`, which has two column families `personalDet`\nand `salaryDet`, containing personal and salary details. Below is the graphical representation\nof the `users` table."},"15571":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5771","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**Users Table**"},"15572":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5772","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**personalDet**"},"15573":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5773","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**salaryDet**"},"15574":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5774","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**rowkey**"},"15575":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5775","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**name**"},"15576":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5776","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**lastname**"},"15577":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5777","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**dob**"},"15578":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5778","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**gross**"},"15579":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5779","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**net**"},"15580":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5780","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"**allowances**"},"15581":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5781","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"admin"},"15582":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5782","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"Admin"},"15583":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5783","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cp-examples","content":"Admin"},"15584":{"id":"/docs/single-page-5784","page_id":"/do