 the standard hadoop-core jar file from the `target/cached_classpath.txt` file with the version containing the HADOOP-7070 fix.\nYou can use the following script to do this:"},"17655":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7855","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#set-jaas-configuration-programmatically","content":"This would avoid the need for a separate Hadoop jar that fixes HADOOP-7070."},"17656":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7856","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#tls-connection-to-zookeeper","content":"Apache ZooKeeper also supports SSL/TLS client connections to encrypt the data in transmission. This is particularly\nuseful when the ZooKeeper ensemble is running on a host different from HBase and data has to be sent\nover the wire."},"17657":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7857","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-system-properties","content":"The ZooKeeper client supports the following Java system properties to set up TLS connection:"},"17658":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7858","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-system-properties","content":"Setting up KeyStore is optional and only required if ZooKeeper server requests for client certificate."},"17659":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7859","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-system-properties","content":"Find more detailed information in the ZooKeeper SSL User Guide."},"17660":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7860","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-system-properties","content":"These're standard Java properties which should be set in the HBase command line and are effective\nin the entire Java process. All ZooKeeper clients running in the same process will pick them up\nincluding co-processors."},"17661":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7861","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#java-system-properties","content":"Since ZooKeeper version 3.8 the following two properties are useful to store the keystore and\ntruststore passwords in protected text files rather than exposing them in the command line."},"17662":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7862","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-configuration","content":"By adding HBASE-28038, ZooKeeper client TLS\nsettings are also available in *hbase-site.xml* via `hbase.zookeeper.property` prefix. In contrast\nto Java system properties this could be more convenient under some circumstances."},"17663":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7863","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-configuration","content":"These settings are eventually transformed into Java system properties, it's just a convenience\nfeature. So, the same rules that mentioned in the previous point, applies to them as well."},"17664":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7864","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#feature-branches","content":"Feature Branches are easy to make. You do not have to be a committer to make one. Just request the name of your branch be added to JIRA up on the developer's mailing list and a committer will add it for you. Thereafter you can file issues against your feature branch in Apache HBase JIRA. Your code you keep elsewhere — it should be public so it can be observed — and you can update dev mailing list on progress. When the feature is ready for commit, 3 +1s from committers will get your feature merged. See HBase, mail # dev - Thoughts about large feature dev branches"},"17665":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7865","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Here is how we agreed to set versions in JIRA when we resolve an issue. If master is going to be 3.0.0, branch-2 will be 2.4.0, and branch-1 will be 1.7.0 then:"},"17666":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7866","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit only to master (i.e., backward-incompatible new feature): Mark with 3.0.0"},"17667":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7867","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit only to master and branch-2 (i.e., backward-compatible new feature, applicable only to 2.x+): Mark with 3.0.0 and 2.4.0"},"17668":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7868","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit to master, branch-2, and branch-1 (i.e., backward-compatible new feature, applicable everywhere): Mark with 3.0.0, 2.4.0, and 1.7.0"},"17669":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7869","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit to master, branch-2, and branch-2.3, branch-1, branch-1.4 (i.e., bug fix applicable to all active release lines): Mark with 3.0.0, 2.4.0, 2.3.x, 1.7.0, and 1.4.x"},"17670":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7870","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#how-to-set-fix-version-in-jira-on-issue-resolve","content":"Commit a fix to the website: no version"},"17671":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7871","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#policy-on-when-to-set-a-resolved-jira-as-closed","content":"We agreed that for issues that list multiple releases in their *Fix Version/s* field, CLOSE the issue on the release of any of the versions listed; subsequent change to the issue must happen in a new JIRA."},"17672":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7872","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#only-transient-state-in-zookeeper","content":"You should be able to kill the data in zookeeper and hbase should ride over it recreating the zk content as it goes. This is an old adage around these parts. We just made note of it now. We also are currently in violation of this basic tenet — replication at least keeps permanent state in zk — but we are working to undo this breaking of a golden rule."},"17673":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7873","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-managers","content":"Each maintained release branch has a release manager, who volunteers to coordinate new features and bug fixes are backported to that release. The release managers are committers. If you would like your feature or bug fix to be included in a given release, communicate with that release manager. If this list goes out of date or you can't reach the listed person, reach out to someone else on the list."},"17674":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7874","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-managers","content":"**Release Managers:**"},"17675":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7875","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-managers","content":"Release"},"17676":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7876","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-managers","content":"Release Manager"},"17677":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7877","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#release-managers","content":"Latest Release"},"17678":{"id":"/docs/single-page-7878","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"