n HBASE-6370, where a table is created or modified to support a codec that a region server does not support, leading to failures that take a long time to occur and are difficult to debug."},"18702":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8902","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compressor-support-on-the-master","content":"If `hbase.master.check.compression` is enabled, libraries for all desired compressors need to be installed and configured on the Master, even if the Master does not run a region server."},"18703":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8903","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-gz-support-via-native-libraries","content":"HBase uses Java's built-in GZip support unless the native Hadoop libraries are available on the CLASSPATH.\nThe recommended way to add libraries to the CLASSPATH is to set the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the user running HBase.\nIf native libraries are not available and Java's GZIP is used, `Got brand-new compressor` reports will be present in the logs.\nSee brand.new\\.compressor)."},"18704":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8904","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-lzo-support","content":"HBase cannot ship with the Hadoop native LZO codc because of incompatibility between HBase, which uses an Apache Software License (ASL) and LZO, which uses a GPL license.\nSee the Hadoop-LZO at Twitter for information on configuring LZO support for HBase."},"18705":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8905","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-lzo-support","content":"If you depend upon LZO compression, consider using the pure Java and ASL licensed\nAirCompressor LZO codec option instead of the Hadoop native default, or configure your\nRegionServers to fail to start if native LZO support is not available.\nSee hbase.regionserver.codecs."},"18706":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8906","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"LZ4 support is bundled with Hadoop and is the default LZ4 codec implementation.\nIt is not required that you make use of the Hadoop LZ4 codec. Our LZ4 codec implemented\nwith lz4-java offers superior performance, and the AirCompressor LZ4 codec offers a\npure Java option for use where native support is not available."},"18707":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8907","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"That said, if you prefer the Hadoop option, make sure the hadoop shared library\n(libhadoop.so) is accessible when you start HBase.\nAfter configuring your platform (see hadoop.native.lib), you can\nmake a symbolic link from HBase to the native Hadoop libraries. This assumes the two\nsoftware installs are colocated. For example, if my 'platform' is Linux-amd64-64:"},"18708":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8908","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#configure-hadoop-native-lz4-support","content":"Use the compression tool to check that LZ4 is installed on all nodes.\nStart up (or restart) HBase.\nAfterward, you can create and alter tables to enable LZ4 as a compression codec.:"},"18709":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8909","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"Snappy support is bundled with Hadoop and is the default Snappy codec implementation.\nIt is not required that you make use of the Hadoop Snappy codec. Our Snappy codec\nimplemented with Xerial Snappy offers superior performance, and the AirCompressor\nSnappy codec offers a pure Java option for use where native support is not available."},"18710":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8910","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"That said, if you prefer the Hadoop codec option, you can install Snappy binaries (for\ninstance, by using +yum install snappy+ on CentOS) or build Snappy from source.\nAfter installing Snappy, search for the shared library, which will be called *libsnappy.so.X&#x2A; where X is a number.\nIf you built from source, copy the shared library to a known location on your system, such as &#x2A;/opt/snappy/lib/*."},"18711":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8911","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"In addition to the Snappy library, HBase also needs access to the Hadoop shared library, which will be called something like *libhadoop.so.X.Y*, where X and Y are both numbers.\nMake note of the location of the Hadoop library, or copy it to the same location as the Snappy library."},"18712":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8912","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"The Snappy and Hadoop libraries need to be available on each node of your cluster.\nSee compression.test to find out how to test that this is the case."},"18713":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8913","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"See hbase.regionserver.codecs to configure your RegionServers to fail to start if a given compressor is not available."},"18714":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8914","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#install-hadoop-native-snappy-support","content":"Each of these library locations need to be added to the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the operating system user that runs HBase.\nYou need to restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."},"18715":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8915","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#compressiontest","content":"You can use the CompressionTest tool to verify that your compressor is available to HBase:"},"18716":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8916","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enforce-compression-settings-on-a-regionserver","content":"You can configure a RegionServer so that it will fail to restart if compression is configured incorrectly, by adding the option hbase.regionserver.codecs to the *hbase-site.xml*, and setting its value to a comma-separated list of codecs that need to be available.\nFor example, if you set this property to `lzo,gz`, the RegionServer would fail to start if both compressors were not available.\nThis would prevent a new server from being added to the cluster without having codecs configured properly."},"18717":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8917","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-compression-on-a-columnfamily","content":"To enable compression for a ColumnFamily, use an `alter` command.\nYou do not need to re-create the table or copy data.\nIf you are changing codecs, be sure the old codec is still available until all the old StoreFiles have been compacted."},"18718":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8918","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-compression-performance","content":"HBase includes a tool called LoadTestTool which provides mechanisms to test your compression performance.\nYou must specify either `-write` or `-update-read` as your first parameter, and if you do not specify another parameter, usage advice is printed for each option."},"18719":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8919","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#testing-compression-performance","content":"**`LoadTestTool` Usage**"},"18720":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8920","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#enable-data-block-encoding","content":"Codecs are built into HBase so no extra configuration is needed.\nCodecs are enabled on a table by setting the `DATA_BLOCK_ENCODING` property.\nDisable the table before altering its DATA\\_BLOCK\\_ENCODING setting.\nFollowing is an example using HBase Shell:"},"18721":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8921","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sql","content":"Apache Phoenix"},"18722":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8922","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#sql","content":"Trafodion: Transactional SQL-on-HBase"},"18723":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8923","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ycsb","content":"YCSB: The Yahoo! Cloud Serving Benchmark and HBase"},"18724":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8924","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ycsb","content":"TODO: Describe how YCSB is poor for putting up a decent cluster load."},"18725":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8925","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#ycsb","content":"TODO: Describe setup of YCSB for HBase. In particular, presplit your tables before you start a run. See HBASE-4163 Create Split Strategy for YCSB Benchmark for why and a little shell command for how to do it."},"18726":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8926","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-file-format-version-1","content":"As we will be discussing changes to the HFile format, it is useful to give a short overview of the original (HFile version 1) format."},"18727":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8927","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview-of-version-1","content":"An HFile in version 1 format is structured as follows:"},"18728":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8928","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"The block index in version 1 is very straightforward.\nFor each entry, it contains:"},"18729":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8929","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Offset (long)"},"18730":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8930","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Uncompressed size (int)"},"18731":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8931","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key (a serialized byte array written using Bytes.writeByteArray)"},"18732":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8932","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key length as a variable-length integer (VInt)"},"18733":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8933","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"Key bytes"},"18734":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8934","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-format-in-version-1","content":"The number of entries in the block index is stored in the fixed file trailer, and has to be passed in to the method that reads the block index.\nOne of the limitations of the block index in version 1 is that it does not provide the compressed size of a block, which turns out to be necessary for decompression.\nTherefore, the HFile reader has to infer this compressed size from the offset difference between blocks.\nWe fix this limitation in version 2, where we store on-disk block size instead of uncompressed size, and get uncompressed size from the block header."},"18735":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8935","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-file-format-with-inline-blocks-version-2","content":"Note: this feature was introduced in HBase 0.92"},"18736":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8936","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#motivation","content":"We found it necessary to revise the HFile format after encountering high memory usage and slow startup times caused by large Bloom filters and block indexes in the region server.\nBloom filters can get as large as 100 MB per HFile, which adds up to 2 GB when aggregated over 20 regions.\nBlock indexes can grow as large as 6 GB in aggregate size over the same set of regions.\nA region is not considered opened until all of its block index data is loaded.\nLarge Bloom filters produce a different performance problem: the first get request that requires a Bloom filter lookup will incur the latency of loading the entire Bloom filter bit array."},"18737":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8937","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#motivation","content":"To speed up region server startup we break Bloom filters and block indexes into multiple blocks and write those blocks out as they fill up, which also reduces the HFile writer's memory footprint.\nIn the Bloom filter case, \"filling up a block\" means accumulating enough keys to efficiently utilize a fixed-size bit array, and in the block index case we accumulate an \"index block\" of the desired size.\nBloom filter blocks and index blocks (we call these \"inline blocks\") become interspersed with data blocks, and as a side effect we can no longer rely on the difference between block offsets to determine data block length, as it was done in version 1."},"18738":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8938","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#motivation","content":"HFile is a low-level file format by design, and it should not deal with application-specific details such as Bloom filters, which are handled at StoreFile level.\nTherefore, we call Bloom filter blocks in an HFile \"inline\" blocks.\nWe also supply HFile with an interface to write those inline blocks."},"18739":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8939","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#motivation","content":"Another format modification aimed at reducing the region server startup time is to use a contiguous \"load-on-open\" section that has to be loaded in memory at the time an HFile is being opened.\nCurrently, as an HFile opens, there are separate seek operations to read the trailer, data/meta indexes, and file info.\nTo read the Bloom filter, there are two more seek operations for its \"data\" and \"meta\" portions.\nIn version 2, we seek once to read the trailer and seek again to read everything else we need to open the file from a contiguous block."},"18740":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8940","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#overview-of-version-2","content":"The version of HBase introducing the above features reads both version 1 and 2 HFiles, but only writes version 2 HFiles.\nA version 2 HFile is structured as follows:"},"18741":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8941","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"In the version 2 every block in the data section contains the following fields:"},"18742":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8942","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"8 bytes: Block type, a sequence of bytes equivalent to version 1's \"magic records\". Supported block types are:"},"18743":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8943","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"DATA – data blocks"},"18744":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8944","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"LEAF\\_INDEX – leaf-level index blocks in a multi-level-block-index"},"18745":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8945","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"BLOOM\\_CHUNK – Bloom filter chunks"},"18746":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8946","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"META – meta blocks (not used for Bloom filters in version 2 anymore)"},"18747":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8947","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"INTERMEDIATE\\_INDEX – intermediate-level index blocks in a multi-level blockindex"},"18748":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8948","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"ROOT\\_INDEX – root-level index blocks in a multi-level block index"},"18749":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8949","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"FILE\\_INFO – the \"file info\" block, a small key-value map of metadata"},"18750":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8950","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"BLOOM\\_META – a Bloom filter metadata block in the load-on-open section"},"18751":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8951","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"TRAILER – a fixed-size file trailer. As opposed to the above, this is not an HFile v2 block but a fixed-size (for each HFile version) data structure"},"18752":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8952","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"INDEX\\_V1 – this block type is only used for legacy HFile v1 block"},"18753":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8953","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Compressed size of the block's data, not including the header (int).\\\nCan be used for skipping the current data block when scanning HFile data."},"18754":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8954","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Uncompressed size of the block's data, not including the header (int)\\\nThis is equal to the compressed size if the compression algorithm is NONE"},"18755":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8955","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"File offset of the previous block of the same type (long)\\\nCan be used for seeking to the previous data/index block"},"18756":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8956","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"Compressed data (or uncompressed data if the compression algorithm is NONE)."},"18757":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8957","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#unified-version-2-block-format","content":"The above format of blocks is used in the following HFile sections:"},"18758":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8958","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#scanned-block-section","content":"The section is named so because it contains all data blocks that need to be read when an HFile is scanned sequentially.\nAlso contains Leaf index blocks and Bloom chunk blocks."},"18759":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8959","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-scanned-block-section","content":"This section still contains unified-format v2 blocks but it does not have to be read when doing a sequential scan.\nThis section contains \"meta\" blocks and intermediate-level index blocks."},"18760":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8960","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-scanned-block-section","content":"We are supporting \"meta\" blocks in version 2 the same way they were supported in version 1, even though we do not store Bloom filter data in these blocks anymore."},"18761":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8961","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"There are three types of block indexes in HFile version 2, stored in two different formats (root and non-root):"},"18762":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8962","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Data index — version 2 multi-level block index, consisting of:"},"18763":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8963","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Version 2 root index, stored in the data block index section of the file"},"18764":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8964","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Optionally, version 2 intermediate levels, stored in the non-root format in the data index section of the file. Intermediate levels can only be present if leaf level blocks are present"},"18765":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8965","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Optionally, version 2 leaf levels, stored in the non-root format inline with data blocks"},"18766":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8966","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Meta index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the meta index section of the file"},"18767":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8967","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#block-index-in-version-2","content":"Bloom index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the \"load-on-open\" section as part of Bloom filter metadata."},"18768":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8968","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"This format applies to:"},"18769":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8969","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Root level of the version 2 data index"},"18770":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8970","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Entire meta and Bloom indexes in version 2, which are always single-level."},"18771":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8971","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"A version 2 root index block is a sequence of entries of the following format, similar to entries of a version 1 block index, but storing on-disk size instead of uncompressed size."},"18772":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8972","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Offset (long)\\\nThis offset may point to a data block or to a deeper-level index block."},"18773":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8973","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"On-disk size (int)"},"18774":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8974","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key (a serialized byte array stored using Bytes.writeByteArray)"},"18775":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8975","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key (VInt)"},"18776":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8976","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key bytes"},"18777":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8977","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"A single-level version 2 block index consists of just a single root index block.\nTo read a root index block of version 2, one needs to know the number of entries.\nFor the data index and the meta index the number of entries is stored in the trailer, and for the Bloom index it is stored in the compound Bloom filter metadata."},"18778":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8978","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"For a multi-level block index we also store the following fields in the root index block in the load-on-open section of the HFile, in addition to the data structure described above:"},"18779":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8979","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Middle leaf index block offset"},"18780":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8980","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Middle leaf block on-disk size (meaning the leaf index block containing the reference to the \"middle\" data block of the file)"},"18781":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8981","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"The index of the mid-key (defined below) in the middle leaf-level block."},"18782":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8982","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"These additional fields are used to efficiently retrieve the mid-key of the HFile used in HFile splits, which we define as the first key of the block with a zero-based index of (n – 1) / 2, if the total number of blocks in the HFile is n.\nThis definition is consistent with how the mid-key was determined in HFile version 1, and is reasonable in general, because blocks are likely to be the same size on average, but we don't have any estimates on individual key/value pair sizes."},"18783":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8983","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"When writing a version 2 HFile, the total number of data blocks pointed to by every leaf-level index block is kept track of.\nWhen we finish writing and the total number of leaf-level blocks is determined, it is clear which leaf-level block contains the mid-key, and the fields listed above are computed.\nWhen reading the HFile and the mid-key is requested, we retrieve the middle leaf index block (potentially from the block cache) and get the mid-key value from the appropriate position inside that leaf block."},"18784":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8984","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"This format applies to intermediate-level and leaf index blocks of a version 2 multi-level data block index.\nEvery non-root index block is structured as follows."},"18785":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8985","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"numEntries: the number of entries (int)."},"18786":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8986","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"entryOffsets: the \"secondary index\" of offsets of entries in the block, to facilitate\na quick binary search on the key (`numEntries + 1` int values). The last value\nis the total length of all entries in this index block. For example, in a non-root\nindex block with entry sizes 60, 80, 50 the \"secondary index\" will contain the\nfollowing int array: `{0, 60, 140, 190}`."},"18787":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8987","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Entries. Each entry contains:"},"18788":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8988","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Offset of the block referenced by this entry in the file (long)"},"18789":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8989","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"On-disk size of the referenced block (int)"},"18790":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8990","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#non-root-block-index-format-in-version-2","content":"Key. The length can be calculated from entryOffsets."},"18791":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8991","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"In contrast with version 1, in a version 2 HFile Bloom filter metadata is stored in the load-on-open section of the HFile for quick startup."},"18792":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8992","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"A compound Bloom filter."},"18793":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8993","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Bloom filter version = 3 (int). There used to be a DynamicByteBloomFilter class that had the Bloom filter version number 2"},"18794":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8994","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The total byte size of all compound Bloom filter chunks (long)"},"18795":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8995","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Number of hash functions (int)"},"18796":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8996","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Type of hash functions (int)"},"18797":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8997","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The total key count inserted into the Bloom filter (long)"},"18798":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8998","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The maximum total number of keys in the Bloom filter (long)"},"18799":{"id":"/docs/single-page-8999","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"The number of chunks (int)"},"18800":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9000","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Comparator class used for Bloom filter keys, a UTF-8 encoded string stored using Bytes.writeByteArray"},"18801":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9001","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#bloom-filters-in-version-2","content":"Bloom block index in the version 2 root block index format"},"18802":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9002","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The file info block is a serialized map from byte arrays to byte arrays, with the following keys, among others.\nStoreFile-level logic adds more keys to this."},"18803":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9003","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Key"},"18804":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9004","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Description"},"18805":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9005","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.LASTKEY"},"18806":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9006","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The last key of the file (byte array)"},"18807":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9007","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.AVG\\_KEY\\_LEN"},"18808":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9008","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The average key length in the file (int)"},"18809":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9009","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"hfile.AVG\\_VALUE\\_LEN"},"18810":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9010","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"The average value length in the file (int)"},"18811":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9011","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"In version 2, we did not change the file format, but we moved the file info to\nthe final section of the file, which can be loaded as one block when the HFile\nis being opened."},"18812":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9012","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-format-in-versions-1-and-2","content":"Also, we do not store the comparator in the version 2 file info anymore.\nInstead, we store it in the fixed file trailer.\nThis is because we need to know the comparator at the time of parsing the load-on-open section of the HFile."},"18813":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9013","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fixed-file-trailer-format-differences-between-versions-1-and-2","content":"The following table shows common and different fields between fixed file trailers in versions 1 and 2.\nNote that the size of the trailer is different depending on the version, so it is \"fixed\" only within one version.\nHowever, the version is always stored as the last four-byte integer in the file."},"18814":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9014","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version 1"},"18815":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9015","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version 2"},"18816":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9016","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"File info offset (long)"},"18817":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9017","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Data index offset (long)"},"18818":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9018","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"loadOnOpenOffset (long) &#x2A;The offset of the section that we need to load when opening the file.*"},"18819":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9019","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Number of data index entries (int)"},"18820":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9020","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"metaIndexOffset (long) &#x2A;This field is not being used by the version 1 reader, so we removed it from version 2.*"},"18821":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9021","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"uncompressedDataIndexSize (long) &#x2A;The total uncompressed size of the whole data block index, including root-level, intermediate-level, and leaf-level blocks.*"},"18822":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9022","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Number of meta index entries (int)"},"18823":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9023","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Total uncompressed bytes (long)"},"18824":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9024","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"numEntries (int)"},"18825":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9025","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"numEntries (long)"},"18826":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9026","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Compression codec: 0 = LZO, 1 = GZ, 2 = NONE (int)"},"18827":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9027","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Compression codec: 0 = LZO, 1 = GZ, 2 = NONE (int)"},"18828":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9028","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"The number of levels in the data block index (int)"},"18829":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9029","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"firstDataBlockOffset (long) &#x2A;The offset of the first data block. Used when scanning.*"},"18830":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9030","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"lastDataBlockEnd (long) &#x2A;The offset of the first byte after the last key/value data block. We don't need to go beyond this offset when scanning.*"},"18831":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9031","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version: 1 (int)"},"18832":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9032","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#differences-between-hfile-versions-1-and-2","content":"Version: 2 (int)"},"18833":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9033","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"Note: this optimization was introduced in HBase 0.95+"},"18834":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9034","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"HFiles contain many blocks that contain a range of sorted Cells.\nEach cell has a key.\nTo save IO when reading Cells, the HFile also has an index that maps a Cell's start key to the offset of the beginning of a particular block.\nPrior to this optimization, HBase would use the key of the first cell in each data block as the index key."},"18835":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9035","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"In HBASE-7845, we generate a new key that is lexicographically larger than the last key of the previous block and lexicographically equal or smaller than the start key of the current block.\nWhile actual keys can potentially be very long, this \"fake key\" or \"virtual key\" can be much shorter.\nFor example, if the stop key of previous block is \"the quick brown fox\", the start key of current block is \"the who\", we could use \"the r\" as our virtual key in our hfile index."},"18836":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9036","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"There are two benefits to this:"},"18837":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9037","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"having shorter keys reduces the hfile index size, (allowing us to keep more indexes in memory), and"},"18838":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9038","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"using something closer to the end key of the previous block allows us to avoid a potential extra IO when the target key lives in between the \"virtual key\" and the key of the first element in the target block."},"18839":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9039","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#getshortmidpointkey-an-optimization-for-data-index-block","content":"This optimization (implemented by the getShortMidpointKey method) is inspired by LevelDB's ByteWiseComparatorImpl::FindShortestSeparator() and FindShortSuccessor()."},"18840":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9040","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-file-format-with-security-enhancements-version-3","content":"Note: this feature was introduced in HBase 0.98"},"18841":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9041","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#motivation-1","content":"Version 3 of HFile makes changes needed to ease management of encryption at rest and cell-level metadata (which in turn is needed for cell-level ACLs and cell-level visibility labels). For more information see hbase.encryption.server, hbase.tags, hbase.accesscontrol.configuration, and hbase.visibility.labels."},"18842":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9042","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hfile-format-with-inline-blocks-v2-overview","content":"The version of HBase introducing the above features reads HFiles in versions 1, 2, and 3 but only writes version 3 HFiles.\nVersion 3 HFiles are structured the same as version 2 HFiles.\nFor more information see hfilev2.overview."},"18843":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9043","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Version 3 added two additional pieces of information to the reserved keys in the file info block."},"18844":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9044","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Key"},"18845":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9045","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Description"},"18846":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9046","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"hfile.MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"18847":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9047","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"The maximum number of bytes needed to store the serialized tags for any single cell in this hfile (int)"},"18848":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9048","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"hfile.TAGS\\_COMPRESSED"},"18849":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9049","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"Does the block encoder for this hfile compress tags? (boolean). Should only be present if hfile.MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN is also present."},"18850":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9050","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When reading a Version 3 HFile the presence of `MAX_TAGS_LEN` is used to determine how to deserialize the cells within a data block.\nTherefore, consumers must read the file's info block prior to reading any data blocks."},"18851":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9051","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When writing a Version 3 HFile, HBase will always include `MAX_TAGS_LEN` when flushing the memstore to underlying filesystem."},"18852":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9052","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"When compacting extant files, the default writer will omit `MAX_TAGS_LEN` if all of the files selected do not themselves contain any cells with tags."},"18853":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9053","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#file-info-block-in-version-3","content":"See compaction for details on the compaction file selection algorithm."},"18854":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9054","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Within an HFile, HBase cells are stored in data blocks as a sequence of KeyValues (see hfilev1.overview, or Lars George's\nexcellent introduction to HBase Storage). In version 3, these KeyValue optionally will include a set of 0 or more tags:"},"18855":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9055","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Version 1 & 2, Version 3 without MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"18856":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9056","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Version 3 with MAX\\_TAGS\\_LEN"},"18857":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9057","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Key Length (4 bytes)"},"18858":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9058","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18859":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9059","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18860":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9060","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Value Length (4 bytes)"},"18861":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9061","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18862":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9062","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18863":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9063","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Key bytes (variable)"},"18864":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9064","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18865":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9065","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18866":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9066","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Value bytes (variable)"},"18867":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9067","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18868":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9068","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18869":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9069","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Tags Length (2 bytes)"},"18870":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9070","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18871":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9071","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Tags bytes (variable)"},"18872":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9072","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"✓"},"18873":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9073","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"If the info block for a given HFile contains an entry for `MAX_TAGS_LEN` each cell will have the length of that cell's tags included, even if that length is zero.\nThe actual tags are stored as a sequence of tag length (2 bytes), tag type (1 byte), tag bytes (variable). The format an individual tag's bytes depends on the tag type."},"18874":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9074","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#data-blocks-in-version-3","content":"Note that the dependence on the contents of the info block implies that prior to reading any data blocks you must first process a file's info block.\nIt also implies that prior to writing a data block you must know if the file's info block will include `MAX_TAGS_LEN`."},"18875":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9075","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#fixed-file-trailer-in-version-3","content":"The fixed file trailers written with HFile version 3 are always serialized with protocol buffers.\nAdditionally, it adds an optional field to the version 2 protocol buffer named encryption\\_key.\nIf HBase is configured to encrypt HFiles this field will store a data encryption key for this particular HFile, encrypted with the current cluster master key using AES.\nFor more information see hbase.encryption.server."},"18876":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9076","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-videos","content":"Introduction to HBase by Todd Lipcon (Chicago Data Summit 2011)."},"18877":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9077","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-videos","content":"Building Real Time Services at Facebook with HBase by Jonathan Gray (Berlin buzzwords 2011)"},"18878":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9078","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-presentations-slides","content":"Advanced HBase Schema Design by Lars George (Hadoop World 2011)."},"18879":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9079","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-presentations-slides","content":"Introduction to HBase by Todd Lipcon (Chicago Data Summit 2011)."},"18880":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9080","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-presentations-slides","content":"Getting The Most From Your HBase Install by Ryan Rawson, Jonathan Gray (Hadoop World 2009)."},"18881":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9081","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-papers","content":"BigTable by Google (2006)."},"18882":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9082","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-papers","content":"HBase and HDFS Locality by Lars George (2010)."},"18883":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9083","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-papers","content":"No Relation: The Mixed Blessings of Non-Relational Databases by Ian Varley (2009)."},"18884":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9084","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-sites","content":"Cloudera's HBase Blog has a lot of links to useful HBase information."},"18885":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9085","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-sites","content":"CAP Confusion is a relevant entry for background information on distributed storage systems."},"18886":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9086","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-sites","content":"HBase RefCard from DZone."},"18887":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9087","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-books","content":"HBase: The Definitive Guide by Lars George."},"18888":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9088","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hadoop-books","content":"Hadoop: The Definitive Guide by Tom White."},"18889":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9089","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-history","content":"2006: BigTable paper published by Google."},"18890":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9090","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-history","content":"2006 (end of year): HBase development starts."},"18891":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9091","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-history","content":"2008: HBase becomes Hadoop sub-project."},"18892":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9092","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-history","content":"2010: HBase becomes Apache top-level project."},"18893":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9093","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asf-development-process","content":"See the Apache Development Process page for all sorts of information on how the ASF is structured (e.g., PMC, committers, contributors), to tips on contributing and getting involved, and how open-source works at ASF."},"18894":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9094","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#asf-board-reporting","content":"Once a quarter, each project in the ASF portfolio submits a report to the ASF board. This is done by the HBase project lead and the committers. See ASF board reporting for more information."},"18895":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9095","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#orca","content":"Default:"},"18896":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9096","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#orca","content":"For dark theme:"},"18897":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9097","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#orca","content":"An Orca is the Apache HBase mascot. See NOTICES.txt. Our Orca logo we got here: http\\://www\\.vectorfree.com/jumping-orca It is licensed Creative Commons Attribution 3.0. See https\\://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/us/ We changed the logo by stripping the colored background, inverting it and then rotating it some."},"18898":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9098","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#orca","content":"The 'official' HBase color is \"International Orange (Engineering)\", the color of the Golden Gate bridge in San Francisco and for space suits used by NASA."},"18899":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9099","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#orca","content":"Our 'font' is Bitsumishi."},"18900":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9100","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc","content":"In 0.95, all client/server communication is done with protobuf'ed Messages rather than with Hadoop Writables.\nOur RPC wire format therefore changes.\nThis document describes the client/server request/response protocol and our new RPC wire-format."},"18901":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9101","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc","content":"For what RPC is like in 0.94 and previous, see Benoît/Tsuna's Unofficial Hadoop / HBase RPC protocol documentation.\nFor more background on how we arrived at this spec., see HBase RPC: WIP"},"18902":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9102","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#goals","content":"A wire-format we can evolve"},"18903":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9103","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#goals","content":"A format that does not require our rewriting server core or radically changing its current architecture (for later)."},"18904":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9104","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#todo","content":"List of problems with currently specified format and where we would like to go in a version2, etc.\nFor example, what would we have to change if anything to move server async or to support streaming/chunking?"},"18905":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9105","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#todo","content":"Diagram on how it works"},"18906":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9106","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#todo","content":"A grammar that succinctly describes the wire-format.\nCurrently we have these words and the content of the rpc protobuf idl but a grammar for the back and forth would help with groking rpc.\nAlso, a little state machine on client/server interactions would help with understanding (and ensuring correct implementation)."},"18907":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9107","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-overview","content":"The client will send setup information on connection establish.\nThereafter, the client invokes methods against the remote server sending a protobuf Message and receiving a protobuf Message in response.\nCommunication is synchronous.\nAll back and forth is preceded by an int that has the total length of the request/response.\nOptionally, Cells(KeyValues) can be passed outside of protobufs in follow-behind Cell blocks\n(because we can't protobuf megabytes of KeyValues or Cells). These CellBlocks are encoded and optionally compressed."},"18908":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9108","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-overview","content":"For more detail on the protobufs involved, see the\nRPC.proto file in master."},"18909":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9109","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#connection-setup","content":"Client initiates connection."},"18910":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9110","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-setup-client","content":"On connection setup, client sends a preamble followed by a connection header."},"18911":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9111","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-setup-client","content":"`<preamble>`"},"18912":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9112","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-setup-client","content":"We need the auth method spec.\nhere so the connection header is encoded if auth enabled.\\\nE.g.: HBas0x000x50 — 4 bytes of MAGIC — \\`HBas' — plus one-byte of version, 0 in this case, and one byte, 0x50 (SIMPLE). of an auth type."},"18913":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9113","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-connection-setup-client","content":"`<Protobuf ConnectionHeader Message>`\\\nHas user info, and \"protocol\", as well as the encoders and compression the client will use sending CellBlocks.\nCellBlock encoders and compressors are for the life of the connection.\nCellBlock encoders implement org.apache.hadoop.hbase.codec.Codec.\nCellBlocks may then also be compressed.\nCompressors implement org.apache.hadoop.io.compress.CompressionCodec.\nThis protobuf is written using writeDelimited so is prefaced by a pb varint with its serialized length"},"18914":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9114","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#server","content":"After client sends preamble and connection header, server does NOT respond if successful connection setup.\nNo response means server is READY to accept requests and to give out response.\nIf the version or authentication in the preamble is not agreeable or the server has trouble parsing the preamble, it will throw a org.apache.hadoop.hbase.ipc.FatalConnectionException explaining the error and will then disconnect.\nIf the client in the connection header — i.e.\nthe protobuf'd Message that comes after the connection preamble — asks for a Service the server does not support or a codec the server does not have, again we throw a FatalConnectionException with explanation."},"18915":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9115","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request","content":"After a Connection has been set up, client makes requests.\nServer responds."},"18916":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9116","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request","content":"A request is made up of a protobuf RequestHeader followed by a protobuf Message parameter.\nThe header includes the method name and optionally, metadata on the optional CellBlock that may be following.\nThe parameter type suits the method being invoked: i.e.\nif we are doing a getRegionInfo request, the protobuf Message param will be an instance of GetRegionInfoRequest.\nThe response will be a GetRegionInfoResponse.\nThe CellBlock is optionally used ferrying the bulk of the RPC data: i.e. Cells/KeyValues."},"18917":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9117","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request-parts","content":"`<Total Length>`\\\nThe request is prefaced by an int that holds the total length of what follows."},"18918":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9118","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request-parts","content":"`<Protobuf RequestHeader Message>`\\\nWill have call.id, trace.id, and method name, etc.\nincluding optional Metadata on the Cell block IFF one is following.\nData is protobuf'd inline in this pb Message or optionally comes in the following CellBlock"},"18919":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9119","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request-parts","content":"`<Protobuf Param Message>`\\\nIf the method being invoked is getRegionInfo, if you study the Service descriptor for the client to regionserver protocol, you will find that the request sends a GetRegionInfoRequest protobuf Message param in this position."},"18920":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9120","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#request-parts","content":"`<CellBlock>`\\\nAn encoded and optionally compressed Cell block."},"18921":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9121","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#response","content":"Same as Request, it is a protobuf ResponseHeader followed by a protobuf Message response where the Message response type suits the method invoked.\nBulk of the data may come in a following CellBlock."},"18922":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9122","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#response-parts","content":"`<Total Length>`\\\nThe response is prefaced by an int that holds the total length of what follows."},"18923":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9123","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#response-parts","content":"`<Protobuf ResponseHeader Message>`\\\nWill have call.id, etc.\nWill include exception if failed processing.\nOptionally includes metadata on optional, IFF there is a CellBlock following."},"18924":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9124","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#response-parts","content":"`<Protobuf Response Message>`\\\nReturn or may be nothing if exception.\nIf the method being invoked is getRegionInfo, if you study the Service descriptor for the client to regionserver protocol, you will find that the response sends a GetRegionInfoResponse protobuf Message param in this position."},"18925":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9125","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#response-parts","content":"`<CellBlock>`\\\nAn encoded and optionally compressed Cell block."},"18926":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9126","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#exceptions","content":"There are two distinct types.\nThere is the request failed which is encapsulated inside the response header for the response.\nThe connection stays open to receive new requests.\nThe second type, the FatalConnectionException, kills the connection."},"18927":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9127","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#exceptions","content":"Exceptions can carry extra information.\nSee the ExceptionResponse protobuf type.\nIt has a flag to indicate do-no-retry as well as other miscellaneous payload to help improve client responsiveness."},"18928":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9128","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cellblocks","content":"These are not versioned.\nServer can do the codec or it cannot.\nIf new version of a codec with say, tighter encoding, then give it a new class name.\nCodecs will live on the server for all time so old clients can connect."},"18929":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9129","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-constraints","content":"In some part, current wire-format — i.e.\nall requests and responses preceded by a length — has been dictated by current server non-async architecture."},"18930":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9130","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#one-fat-pb-request-or-headerparam","content":"We went with pb header followed by pb param making a request and a pb header followed by pb response for now.\nDoing header+param rather than a single protobuf Message with both header and param content:"},"18931":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9131","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#one-fat-pb-request-or-headerparam","content":"Is closer to what we currently have"},"18932":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9132","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#one-fat-pb-request-or-headerparam","content":"Having a single fat pb requires extra copying putting the already pb'd param into the body of the fat request pb (and same making result)"},"18933":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9133","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#one-fat-pb-request-or-headerparam","content":"We can decide whether to accept the request or not before we read the param; for example, the request might be low priority.\nAs is, we read header+param in one go as server is currently implemented so this is a TODO."},"18934":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9134","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#one-fat-pb-request-or-headerparam","content":"The advantages are minor.\nIf later, fat request has clear advantage, can roll out a v2 later."},"18935":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9135","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cellblock-codecs","content":"To enable a codec other than the default `KeyValueCodec`, set `hbase.client.rpc.codec` to the name of the Codec class to use.\nCodec must implement hbase's `Codec` Interface.\nAfter connection setup, all passed cellblocks will be sent with this codec.\nThe server will return cellblocks using this same codec as long as the codec is on the servers' CLASSPATH (else you will get `UnsupportedCellCodecException`)."},"18936":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9136","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cellblock-codecs","content":"To change the default codec, set `hbase.client.default.rpc.codec`."},"18937":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9137","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#cellblock-codecs","content":"To disable cellblocks completely and to go pure protobuf, set the default to the empty String and do not specify a codec in your Configuration.\nSo, set `hbase.client.default.rpc.codec` to the empty string and do not set `hbase.client.rpc.codec`.\nThis will cause the client to connect to the server with no codec specified.\nIf a server sees no codec, it will return all responses in pure protobuf.\nRunning pure protobuf all the time will be slower than running with cellblocks."},"18938":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9138","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#rpc-notes-rpc-configurations-compression","content":"Uses hadoop's compression codecs.\nTo enable compressing of passed CellBlocks, set `hbase.client.rpc.compressor` to the name of the Compressor to use.\nCompressor must implement Hadoop's CompressionCodec Interface.\nAfter connection setup, all passed cellblocks will be sent compressed.\nThe server will return cellblocks compressed using this same compressor as long as the compressor is on its CLASSPATH (else you will get `UnsupportedCompressionCodecException`)."},"18939":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9139","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#hbase-20-incompatible-changes","content":"This appendix describes incompatible changes from earlier versions of HBase against HBase 2.0.\nThis list is not meant to be wholly encompassing of all possible incompatibilities.\nInstead, this content is intended to give insight into some obvious incompatibilities which most\nusers will face coming from HBase 1.x releases."},"18940":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9140","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#list-of-major-changes-for-hbase-20","content":"HBASE-1912- HBCK is an HBase database checking tool for capturing the inconsistency. As an HBase administrator, you should not use HBase version 1.0 hbck tool to check the HBase 2.0 database. Doing so will break the database and throw an exception error."},"18941":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9141","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#list-of-major-changes-for-hbase-20","content":"HBASE-16189 and HBASE-18945- You cannot open the HBase 2.0 hfiles through HBase 1.0 version. If you are an admin or an HBase user who is using HBase version 1.x, you must first do a rolling upgrade to the latest version of HBase 1.x and then upgrade to HBase 2.0."},"18942":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9142","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#list-of-major-changes-for-hbase-20","content":"HBASE-18240 - Changed the ReplicationEndpoint Interface. It also introduces a new hbase-third party 1.0 that packages all the third party utilities, which are expected to run in the hbase cluster."},"18943":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9143","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-api-changes","content":"HBASE-16769 - Deprecated PB references from MasterObserver and RegionServerObserver."},"18944":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9144","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-api-changes","content":"HBASE-17312 - \\[JDK8] Use default method for Observer Coprocessors. The interface classes of BaseMasterAndRegionObserver, BaseMasterObserver, BaseRegionObserver, BaseRegionServerObserver and BaseWALObserver uses JDK8's 'default' keyword to provide empty and no-op implementations."},"18945":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9145","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#coprocessor-api-changes","content":"Interface HTableInterface\nHBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the methods listed below:"},"18946":{"id":"/docs/single-page-9146","page_id":"/docs/single-page","tags":["single-page"],"type":"text","url":"/docs/single-page#interface-coprocess