\_secs*: the initial refresh interval in seconds, the default value is 1/10 of *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*. The reason why we want to introduce a separated configuration for the delay for first refreshing is that, as end users could configure any nodes in a cluster as the initial bootstrap nodes, it is possible that different end users will configure the same machine which makes the machine over load. So we should have a shorter delay for the initial refresh, to let users quickly switch to the bootstrap nodes we want them to connect to."],[14035,"*hbase.client.bootstrap.initial\\_refresh\\_delay\\_secs*: the initial refresh interval in seconds, the default value is 1/10 of *hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*. The reason why we want to introduce a separated configuration for the delay for first refreshing is that, as end users could configure any nodes in a cluster as the initial bootstrap nodes, it is possible that different end users will configure the same machine which makes the machine over load. So we should have a shorter delay for the initial refresh, to let users quickly switch to the bootstrap nodes we want them to connect to."],[479,"*hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*: the refresh interval in seconds, default 300. A value less than or equal to zero means disable refreshing."],[14034,"*hbase.client.bootstrap.refresh\\_interval\\_secs*: the refresh interval in seconds, default 300. A value less than or equal to zero means disable refreshing."],[3218,"*If* you are managing your own ZooKeeper, start it and confirm it's running, else HBase will start up ZooKeeper for you as part of its start process."],[12281,"*If* you are managing your own ZooKeeper, start it and confirm it's running, else HBase will start up ZooKeeper for you as part of its start process."],[1665,"*Incremental backup*: a type of backup which contains the changes in a table since a full backup."],[14905,"*Incremental backup*: a type of backup which contains the changes in a table since a full backup."],[2209,"*Integration* tests are system level tests.\nSee Integration Tests for more info.\nIf you invoke `$ mvn test` on integration tests, there is no timeout for the test."],[17219,"*Integration* tests are system level tests.\nSee Integration Tests for more info.\nIf you invoke `$ mvn test` on integration tests, there is no timeout for the test."],[7568,"*interrupted*: currently same as *yield*."],[17531,"*interrupted*: currently same as *yield*."],[8603,"*irbrc*"],[10275,"*irbrc*"],[2208,"*Large* test cases are everything else. They are typically large-scale tests, regression tests\nfor specific bugs, timeout tests, or performance tests. No large test suite can take longer than\nthirteen minutes. It will be killed as timed out. Cast your test as an Integration Test if it needs\nto run longer."],[17218,"*Large* test cases are everything else. They are typically large-scale tests, regression tests\nfor specific bugs, timeout tests, or performance tests. No large test suite can take longer than\nthirteen minutes. It will be killed as timed out. Cast your test as an Integration Test if it needs\nto run longer."],[3467,"*log4j2.properties*"],[10134,"*log4j2.properties*"],[647,"*MasterProcWAL is replaced in hbase-2.3.0 by an alternate Procedure Store implementation; see in-master-procedure-store-region. This section pertains to hbase-2.0.0 through hbase-2.2.x*"],[14101,"*MasterProcWAL is replaced in hbase-2.3.0 by an alternate Procedure Store implementation; see in-master-procedure-store-region. This section pertains to hbase-2.0.0 through hbase-2.2.x*"],[2207,"*Medium* test cases are executed in separate JVM and individual test suites or test classes or in\njunit parlance, test fixture, should run in 50 seconds\nor less. These test cases can use a mini cluster. Since we start up a JVM per test fixture (and\noften a cluster too), be sure to make the startup pay by writing test fixtures that do a lot of\ntesting running tens of seconds perhaps combining test rather than spin up a jvm (and cluster)\nper test method; this practice will help w/ overall test times."],[17217,"*Medium* test cases are executed in separate JVM and individual test suites or test classes or in\njunit parlance, test fixture, should run in 50 seconds\nor less. These test cases can use a mini cluster. Since we start up a JVM per test fixture (and\noften a cluster too), be sure to make the startup pay by writing test fixtures that do a lot of\ntesting running tens of seconds perhaps combining test rather than spin up a jvm (and cluster)\nper test method; this practice will help w/ overall test times."],[889,"*Minor compactions* usually select a small number of small, adjacent StoreFiles and rewrite them as a single StoreFile. Minor compactions do not drop (filter out) deletes or expired versions, because of potential side effects. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions. The end result of a minor compaction is fewer, larger StoreFiles for a given Store."],[14452,"*Minor compactions* usually select a small number of small, adjacent StoreFiles and rewrite them as a single StoreFile. Minor compactions do not drop (filter out) deletes or expired versions, because of potential side effects. See Compaction and Deletions and Compaction and Versions for information on how deletes and versions are handled in relation to compactions. The end result of a minor compaction is fewer, larger StoreFiles for a given Store."],[1257,"*multiwal*: This provider is made of multiple instances of *asyncfs* or *filesystem*. See the next section for more on *multiwal*."],[14241,"*multiwal*: This provider is made of multiple instances of *asyncfs* or *filesystem*. See the next section for more on *multiwal*."],[8290,"*Namespace Admins* - a namespace admin with `Create` permissions can create or drop tables within that namespace