ed from the runtime classpath. Downstream client users who do not follow the guidance in Downstream HBase 2.0+ users should use the shaded client will have to examine the set of dependencies Maven pulls in for impact. Downstream users of LimitedPrivate Coprocessor APIs will need to examine the runtime environment for impact."],[8575,"A number of properties exist to configure LDAP authentication for the web server:"],[13624,"A number of properties exist to configure LDAP authentication for the web server:"],[8558,"A number of properties exist to configure SPNEGO authentication for the web server:"],[13607,"A number of properties exist to configure SPNEGO authentication for the web server:"],[9656,"A patch upgrade is a drop-in replacement. Any change that is not Java binary and source compatible would not be allowed.[^2] Downgrading versions within patch releases may not be compatible."],[12734,"A patch upgrade is a drop-in replacement. Any change that is not Java binary and source compatible would not be allowed.[^2] Downgrading versions within patch releases may not be compatible."],[8695,"A place to grab reference data or profile data on the fly"],[15446,"A place to grab reference data or profile data on the fly"],[8696,"A place to store counts or aggregates in a way that supports Spark Streaming's\npromise of *only once processing*."],[15447,"A place to store counts or aggregates in a way that supports Spark Streaming's\npromise of *only once processing*."],[2277,"A policy is a strategy for executing one or more actions. The default policy\nexecutes a random action every minute based on predefined action weights.\nA given policy will be executed until ChaosMonkey is interrupted."],[17287,"A policy is a strategy for executing one or more actions. The default policy\nexecutes a random action every minute based on predefined action weights.\nA given policy will be executed until ChaosMonkey is interrupted."],[1297,"A possible downside to WAL compression is that we lose more data from the last block in the WAL if it is ill-terminated mid-write. If entries in this last block were added with new dictionary entries but we failed persist the amended dictionary because of an abrupt termination, a read of this last block may not be able to resolve last-written entries."],[14281,"A possible downside to WAL compression is that we lose more data from the last block in the WAL if it is ill-terminated mid-write. If entries in this last block were added with new dictionary entries but we failed persist the amended dictionary because of an abrupt termination, a read of this last block may not be able to resolve last-written entries."],[7560,"A Procedure is a transform made on an HBase entity. Examples of HBase entities would be Regions and Tables.\nProcedures are run by a ProcedureExecutor instance. Procedure current state is kept in the ProcedureStore.\nThe ProcedureExecutor has but a primitive view on what goes on inside a Procedure. From its PoV, Procedures are submitted and then the ProcedureExecutor keeps calling &#x2A;#execute(Object)* until the Procedure is done. Execute may be called multiple times in the case of failure or restart, so Procedure code must be idempotent yielding the same result each time it run. Procedure code can also implement *rollback* so steps can be undone if failure. A call to &#x2A;execute()* can result in one of following possibilities:"],[17523,"A Procedure is a transform made on an HBase entity. Examples of HBase entities would be Regions and Tables.\nProcedures are run by a ProcedureExecutor instance. Procedure current state is kept in the ProcedureStore.\nThe ProcedureExecutor has but a primitive view on what goes on inside a Procedure. From its PoV, Procedures are submitted and then the ProcedureExecutor keeps calling &#x2A;#execute(Object)* until the Procedure is done. Execute may be called multiple times in the case of failure or restart, so Procedure code must be idempotent yielding the same result each time it run. Procedure code can also implement *rollback* so steps can be undone if failure. A call to &#x2A;execute()* can result in one of following possibilities:"],[3862,"A pseudo-distributed mode is simply a fully-distributed mode run on a single host. Use this HBase configuration for testing and prototyping purposes only. Do not use this configuration for production or for performance evaluation."],[12260,"A pseudo-distributed mode is simply a fully-distributed mode run on a single host. Use this HBase configuration for testing and prototyping purposes only. Do not use this configuration for production or for performance evaluation."],[1957,"A Pull Request (PR) submission triggers the hbase yetus checks. The checks make\nsure the patch doesn't break the build or introduce test failures. The checks take\naround four hours to run (They are the same set run when you submit a patch via\nHBASE JIRA). When finished, they add a report to the PR as a comment. If a problem\nw/ the patch — failed compile, checkstyle violation, or an added findbugs --\nthe original author makes fixes and pushes a new patch. This re-runs the checks\nto produce a new report."],[17462,"A Pull Request (PR) submission triggers the hbase yetus checks. The checks make\nsure the patch doesn't break the build or introduce test failures. The checks take\naround four hours to run (They are the same set run when you submit a patch via\nHBASE JIRA). When finished, they add a report to the PR as a comment. If a problem\nw/ the patch — failed compile, checkstyle violation, or an added findbugs --\nthe original author makes fixes and pushes a new patch. This re-runs the checks\nto produce a new report."],[3861,"A quickstart has been added to the\nquickstart chapter. See\nquickstart-pseudo. Some of the\ninformation that was originally in this section has been moved there."],[12259,"A quickstart has been added to the\nquickstart chapter. See\nquickstart-pseudo. Some of the\ninformation that was originally in this section has been moved there."],[1268,"A RegionServer serves many regions. All of the regions in a region server share the same active WAL file. Each edit in the WAL file includes information about which region it belongs to. When a region is opened, the edits in the WAL file which belong to that region need to be replayed. Therefore, edits in the WAL file must be grouped by region so that particular sets can be replayed to regenerate the data in a particular region. The process of grouping the WAL edits by region is called *log splitting*. It is a critical process for recovering data if a region server fails."],[14252,"A RegionServer serves many regions. All of the regions in a region server share the same active WAL file. Each edit in the WAL file includes information about which region it belongs to. When a region is opened, the edits in the WAL file which belong to that region need to be replayed. Therefore, edits in the WAL file must be grouped by region so that particular sets can be replayed to regenerate the data in a particular region. The process of grouping the WAL edits by region is called *log splitting*. It is a critical process for recovering data if a region server fails."],[8112,"A request is made up of a protobuf RequestHeader followed by a protobuf Message parameter.\nThe header includes the method name and optionally, metadata on the optional CellBlock that may be following.\nThe parameter type suits the method being invoked: i.e.\nif we are doing a getRegionInfo request, the protobuf Message param will be an instance of GetRegionInfoRequest.\nThe response will be a GetRegionInfoResponse.\nThe CellBlock is optionally used ferrying the bulk of the RPC data: i.e. Cells/KeyValues."],[18916,"A request is made up of a protobuf RequestHeader followed by a protobuf Message parameter.\nThe header includes the method name and optionally, metadata on the optional CellBlock that may be following.\nThe parameter type suits the method being invoked: i.e.\nif we are doing a getRegionInfo request, the protobuf Message param will be an instance of GetRegionInfoRequest.\nThe response will be a GetRegionInfoResponse.\nThe CellBlock is optionally used ferrying the bulk of the RPC data: i.e. Cells/KeyValues."],[9593,"A rolling upgrade is the process by which you update the servers in your cluster a server at a time. You can rolling upgrade across HBase versions if they are binary or wire compatible. See the \"Rolling Upgrade Between Versions that are Binary/Wire Compatible\" section below for more on what this means. Coarsely, a rolling upgrade is a graceful stop each server, update the software, and then restart. You do this for each server in the cluster. Usually you upgrade the Master first and then the RegionServers. See Rolling Restart for tools that can help use the rolling upgrade process."],[12803,"A rolling upgrade is the process by which you update the servers in your cluster a server at a time. You can rolling upgrade across HBase versions if they are binary or wire compatible. See the \"Rolling Upgrade Between Versions that are Binary/Wire Compatible\" section below for more on what this means. Coarsely, a rolling upgrade is a graceful stop each server, update the software, and then restart. You do this for each server in the cluster. Usually you upgrade the Master first and then the RegionServers. See Rolling Restart for tools that can help use the rolling upgrade process."],[4278,"A row in HBase consists of a row key and one or more columns with values associated with them.\nRows are sorted alphabetically by the row key as they are stored.\nFor this reason, the design of the row key is very important.\nThe goal is to store data in such a way that related rows are near each other.\nA common row key pattern is a website domain.\nIf your row keys are domains, you should probably store them in reverse (org.apache.www, org.apache.mail, org.apache.jira). This way, all of the Apache domains are near each other in the table, rather than being spread out based on the first letter of the subdomain."],[13087,"A row in HBase consists of a row key and one or more columns with values associated with them.\nRows are sorted alphabetically by the row key as they are stored.\nFor this reason, the design of the row key is very important.\nThe goal is to store data in such a way that related rows are near each other.\nA common row key pattern is a website domain.\nIf your row keys are domains, you should probably store them in reverse (org.apache.www, org.apache.mail, org.apache.jira). This way, all of the Apache domains are near each other in the table, rather than being spread out based on the first letter of the subdomain."],[6176,"A sample output for above commands:"],[16850,"A sample output for above commands:"],[11,"A scope corresponds to a level of the data model. From broadest to narrowest, the scopes are as follows:"],[18220,"A scope corresponds to a level of the data model. From broadest to narrowest, the scopes are as follows:"],[7860,"A secondary index could be created in another table which is periodically updated via a MapReduce job. The job could be executed intra-day, but depending on load-strategy it could still potentially be out of sync with the main data table."],[13314,"A secondary index could be created in another table which is periodically updated via a MapReduce job. The job could be executed intra-day, but depending on load-strategy it could still potentially be out of sync with the main data table."],[7919,"A series of nested objects for shipping locations and line-items (see Order Object Design for details)"],[13373,"A series of nested objects for shipping locations and line-items (see Order Object Design for details)"],[7940,"a ShippingLocation can have multiple LineItems"],[13394,"a ShippingLocation can have multiple LineItems"],[6835,"A similar example can be seen for an user table with 3 smaller regions and 1 relatively large region. For this example