ilter/SingleColumnValueFilter.html) can be used to test column values for equivalence (`CompareOperaor.EQUAL`), inequality (`CompareOperaor.NOT_EQUAL`), or ranges (e.g., `CompareOperaor.GREATER`). The following is an example of testing equivalence of a column to a String value \"my value\"..."],[14053,"A SingleColumnValueFilter (see: https\\://hbase.apache.org/devapidocs/org/apache/hadoop/hbase/filter/SingleColumnValueFilter.html) can be used to test column values for equivalence (`CompareOperaor.EQUAL`), inequality (`CompareOperaor.NOT_EQUAL`), or ranges (e.g., `CompareOperaor.GREATER`). The following is an example of testing equivalence of a column to a String value \"my value\"..."],[9097,"A slightly longer explanation of why pauses can happen is as follows: Puts are sometimes blocked on the MemStores which are blocked by the flusher thread which is blocked because there are too many files to compact because the compactor is given too many small files to compact and has to compact the same data repeatedly. This situation can occur even with minor compactions. Compounding this situation, Apache HBase doesn't compress data in memory. Thus, the 64MB that lives in the MemStore could become a 6MB file after compression - which results in a smaller StoreFile. The upside is that more data is packed into the same region, but performance is achieved by being able to write larger files - which is why HBase waits until the flushsize before writing a new StoreFile. And smaller StoreFiles become targets for compaction. Without compression the files are much bigger and don't need as much compaction, however this is at the expense of I/O."],[15965,"A slightly longer explanation of why pauses can happen is as follows: Puts are sometimes blocked on the MemStores which are blocked by the flusher thread which is blocked because there are too many files to compact because the compactor is given too many small files to compact and has to compact the same data repeatedly. This situation can occur even with minor compactions. Compounding this situation, Apache HBase doesn't compress data in memory. Thus, the 64MB that lives in the MemStore could become a 6MB file after compression - which results in a smaller StoreFile. The upside is that more data is packed into the same region, but performance is achieved by being able to write larger files - which is why HBase waits until the flushsize before writing a new StoreFile. And smaller StoreFiles become targets for compaction. Without compression the files are much bigger and don't need as much compaction, however this is at the expense of I/O."],[3856,"A sometimes useful variation on standalone hbase has all daemons running inside the one JVM but rather than persist to the local filesystem, instead they persist to an HDFS instance."],[12254,"A sometimes useful variation on standalone hbase has all daemons running inside the one JVM but rather than persist to the local filesystem, instead they persist to an HDFS instance."],[6255,"A source cluster might push changes to a destination cluster, which might also push its own changes back to the original cluster."],[16569,"A source cluster might push changes to a destination cluster, which might also push its own changes back to the original cluster."],[8768,"A special implementation to be used when swapping between Store File Tracking implementations on\npre-existing tables that already contain data, and therefore, files being tracked under an specific\nlogic."],[18068,"A special implementation to be used when swapping between Store File Tracking implementations on\npre-existing tables that already contain data, and therefore, files being tracked under an specific\nlogic."],[825,"A Store hosts a MemStore and 0 or more StoreFiles (HFiles). A Store corresponds to a column family for a table for a given region."],[14388,"A Store hosts a MemStore and 0 or more StoreFiles (HFiles). A Store corresponds to a column family for a table for a given region."],[7798,"A third common trick for preventing hotspotting is to reverse a fixed-width or numeric row key so that the part that changes the most often (the least significant digit) is first. This effectively randomizes row keys, but sacrifices row ordering properties."],[13252,"A third common trick for preventing hotspotting is to reverse a fixed-width or numeric row key so that the part that changes the most often (the least significant digit) is first. This effectively randomizes row keys, but sacrifices row ordering properties."],[9080,"A thread that receives data from HDFS:"],[15948,"A thread that receives data from HDFS:"],[4283,"A timestamp is written alongside each value, and is the identifier for a given version of a value.\nBy default, the timestamp represents the time on the RegionServer when the data was written, but you can specify a different timestamp value when you put data into the cell."],[13092,"A timestamp is written alongside each value, and is the identifier for a given version of a value.\nBy default, the timestamp represents the time on the RegionServer when the data was written, but you can specify a different timestamp value when you put data into the cell."],[7929,"A traditional design approach would have separate tables for CUSTOMER and SALES. Another option is to pack multiple record types into a single table (e.g., CUSTOMER++)."],[13383,"A traditional design approach would have separate tables for CUSTOMER and SALES. Another option is to pack multiple record types into a single table (e.g., CUSTOMER++)."],[8152,"A typical schema has between 1 and 3 column families per table. HBase tables should not be designed to mimic RDBMS tables."],[13233,"A typical schema has between 1 and 3 column families per table. HBase tables should not be designed to mimic RDBMS tables."],[8343,"A user adds visibility expressions to a cell during a Put operation. In the default configuration, the user does not need to have access to a label in order to label cells with it. This behavior is controlled by the configuration option `hbase.security.visibility.mutations.checkauths`. If you set this option to `true`, the labels the user is modifying as part of the mutation must be associated with the user, or the mutation will fail. Whether a user is authorized to read a labelled cell is determined during a Get or Scan, and results which the user is not allowed to read are filtered out. This incurs the same I/O penalty as if the results were returned, but reduces load on the network."],[13847,"A user adds visibility expressions to a cell during a Put operation. In the default configuration, the user does not need to have access to a label in order to label cells with it. This behavior is controlled by the configuration option `hbase.security.visibility.mutations.checkauths`. If you set this option to `true`, the labels the user is modifying as part of the mutation must be associated with the user, or the mutation will fail. Whether a user is authorized to read a labelled cell is determined during a Get or Scan, and results which the user is not allowed to read are filtered out. This incurs the same I/O penalty as if the results were returned, but reduces load on the network."],[8016,"A value between `0` and `1` allocates the number of queues proportionally to the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers."],[13470,"A value between `0` and `1` allocates the number of queues proportionally to the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers."],[7339,"A value between `0` and `1` tunes the number of queues against the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers.\\\nHaving more queues, such as in a situation where you have one queue per handler, reduces contention when adding a task to a queue or selecting it from a queue. The trade-off is that if you have some queues with long-running tasks, a handler may end up waiting to execute from that queue rather than processing another queue which has waiting tasks."],[15704,"A value between `0` and `1` tunes the number of queues against the number of handlers. For instance, a value of `.5` shares one queue between each two handlers.\\\nHaving more queues, such as in a situation where you have one queue per handler, reduces contention when adding a task to a queue or selecting it from a queue. The trade-off is that if you have some queues with long-running tasks, a handler may end up waiting to execute from that queue rather than processing another queue which has waiting tasks."],[7326,"A value of `.3` uses 30% of the queues for reading and 70% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 3 queues would be used for reads and 7 for writes."],[15691,"A value of `.3` uses 30% of the queues for reading and 70% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 3 queues would be used for reads and 7 for writes."],[7332,"A value of `.3` uses 70% of the read queues for Gets and 30% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 7 would be used for Gets and 3 for Scans."],[15697,"A value of `.3` uses 70% of the read queues for Gets and 30% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 7 would be used for Gets and 3 for Scans."],[7333,"A value of `.5` uses half the read queues for Gets and half for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 5 would be used for Gets and 5 for Scans."],[15698,"A value of `.5` uses half the read queues for Gets and half for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 5 would be used for Gets and 5 for Scans."],[7327,"A value of `.5` uses the same number of read queues and write queues. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 5 queues would be used for reads and 5 for writes."],[15692,"A value of `.5` uses the same number of read queues and write queues. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 5 queues would be used for reads and 5 for writes."],[7328,"A value of `.6` uses 60% of the queues for reading and 40% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 6 queues would be used for reads and 4 for writes."],[15693,"A value of `.6` uses 60% of the queues for reading and 40% for writing. Given a value of `10` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue`, 6 queues would be used for reads and 4 for writes."],[7334,"A value of `.7` uses 30% of the read queues for Gets and 70% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 3 would be used for Gets and 7 for Scans."],[15699,"A value of `.7` uses 30% of the read queues for Gets and 70% for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5` for `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.read.ratio`, 10 queues would be used for reads, out of those 10, 3 would be used for Gets and 7 for Scans."],[7331,"A value of `0` does not split the read queue."],[15696,"A value of `0` does not split the read queue."],[7337,"A value of `0` uses a single shared queue between all the handlers."],[15702,"A value of `0` uses a single shared queue between all the handlers."],[7335,"A value of `1.0` uses all but one of the read queues for Scans. Given a value of `20` for `hbase.ipc.server.num.callqueue` and a value of `.5`