eld in memory of that RegionServer will be cleaned up and previous logs are lost. What if we want to persist all these logs forever? What if we want to store them in such a manner that operator can get all historical records with some filters? e.g get me all large/slow RPC logs that are triggered by user1 and are related to region: cluster\\_test,cccccccc,1589635796466.aa45e1571d533f5ed0bb31cdccaaf9cf. ?"],[8443,"All of the data under management is kept under the root directory in the file system (`hbase.rootdir`). Access to the data and WAL files in the filesystem should be restricted so that users cannot bypass the HBase layer, and peek at the underlying data files from the file system. HBase assumes the filesystem used (HDFS or other) enforces permissions hierarchically. If sufficient protection from the file system (both authorization and authentication) is not provided, HBase level authorization control (ACLs, visibility labels, etc) is meaningless since the user can always access the data from the file system."],[13754,"All of the data under management is kept under the root directory in the file system (`hbase.rootdir`). Access to the data and WAL files in the filesystem should be restricted so that users cannot bypass the HBase layer, and peek at the underlying data files from the file system. HBase assumes the filesystem used (HDFS or other) enforces permissions hierarchically. If sufficient protection from the file system (both authorization and authentication) is not provided, HBase level authorization control (ACLs, visibility labels, etc) is meaningless since the user can always access the data from the file system."],[1377,"All of the replicas for a single region will have a unique replica*id, starting from 0. The region replica having replica\\_id==0 is called the primary region, and the others \\_secondary regions* or secondaries. Only the primary can accept writes from the client, and the primary will always contain the latest changes. Since all writes still have to go through the primary region, the writes are not highly-available (meaning they might block for some time if the region becomes unavailable)."],[14672,"All of the replicas for a single region will have a unique replica*id, starting from 0. The region replica having replica\\_id==0 is called the primary region, and the others \\_secondary regions* or secondaries. Only the primary can accept writes from the client, and the primary will always contain the latest changes. Since all writes still have to go through the primary region, the writes are not highly-available (meaning they might block for some time if the region becomes unavailable)."],[2965,"All of the shell scripts that come with HBase rely on the GNU Bash shell."],[12002,"All of the shell scripts that come with HBase rely on the GNU Bash shell."],[9534,"All service rollback"],[10198,"All service rollback"],[6540,"All switch states are read from memory during metrics collection.\nWhen quota support is disabled, `rpcThrottleEnabled` is `0`. With quota support\nenabled, it is omitted while the quota manager is unavailable, including in\nMaster maintenance mode."],[16438,"All switch states are read from memory during metrics collection.\nWhen quota support is disabled, `rpcThrottleEnabled` is `0`. With quota support\nenabled, it is omitted while the quota manager is unavailable, including in\nMaster maintenance mode."],[2239,"All tests must be categorized, if not they could be skipped."],[17249,"All tests must be categorized, if not they could be skipped."],[2236,"All tests must be written to support parallel execution on the same machine, hence they should not use shared resources as fixed ports or fixed file names."],[17246,"All tests must be written to support parallel execution on the same machine, hence they should not use shared resources as fixed ports or fixed file names."],[2240,"All tests should be written to be as fast as possible."],[17250,"All tests should be written to be as fast