ng\nbased on how long the whole Test Fixture/Class/Suite takes and\nthat the variance in how long a test method takes varies wildly\ndependent upon context (loaded Apache Infrastructure versus\ndeveloper machine with nothing else running on it). JUnit 5's `@Timeout` may still be\napplied sparingly to an individual method that genuinely needs its own deadline."],[1085,"Block Cache"],[7256,"Block Cache"],[10576,"Block Cache"],[10974,"Block Cache"],[673,"Block Cache and Bloom Filters: HBase supports a Block Cache and Bloom Filters for high volume query optimization."],[13948,"Block Cache and Bloom Filters: HBase supports a Block Cache and Bloom Filters for high volume query optimization."],[6484,"Block Cache Monitoring"],[11193,"Block Cache Monitoring"],[1151,"Block caching is enabled by default for all the user tables which means that any read operation will load the LRU cache. This might be good for a large number of use cases, but further tunings are usually required in order to achieve better performance. An important concept is the working set size, or WSS, which is: \"the amount of memory needed to compute the answer to a problem\". For a website, this would be the data that's needed to answer the queries over a short amount of time."],[14135,"Block caching is enabled by default for all the user tables which means that any read operation will load the LRU cache. This might be good for a large number of use cases, but further tunings are usually required in order to achieve better performance. An important concept is the working set size, or WSS, which is: \"the amount of memory needed to compute the answer to a problem\". For a website, this would be the data that's needed to answer the queries over a short amount of time."],[2515,"Block Compressors"],[11624,"Block Compressors"],[5769,"Block index format in version 1"],[11656,"Block index format in version 1"],[5776,"Block index in version 2"],[11663,"Block index in version 2"],[5558,"Block Locality"],[17847,"Block Locality"],[707,"Blocks"],[7644,"Blocks"],[10645,"Blocks"],[1193,"Blocks from column families that don't define the age threshold wouldn't be evaluated by the time based priority, and would only be evicted following the LRU eviction logic."],[14177,"Blocks from column families that don't define the age threshold wouldn't be evaluated by the time based priority, and would only be evicted following the LRU eviction logic."],[5865,"Bloom block index in the version 2 root block index format"],[18801,"Bloom block index in the version 2 root block index format"],[7261,"Bloom Filter Configuration"],[10979,"Bloom Filter Configuration"],[5857,"Bloom filter version = 3 (int). There used to be a DynamicByteBloomFilter class that had the Bloom filter version number 2"],[18793,"Bloom filter version = 3 (int). There used to be a DynamicByteBloomFilter class that had the Bloom filter version number 2"],[7231,"Bloom Filters"],[7259,"Bloom Filters"],[10949,"Bloom Filters"],[10977,"Bloom Filters"],[7427,"Bloom filters add an entry to the `StoreFile` general `FileInfo` data structure and then two extra entries to the `StoreFile` metadata section."],[15792,"Bloom filters add an entry to the `StoreFile` general `FileInfo` data structure and then two extra entries to the `StoreFile` metadata section."],[7357,"Bloom filters are enabled on a Column Family. You can do this by using the setBloomFilterType method of HColumnDescriptor or using the HBase API. Valid values are `NONE`, `ROW` (default), or `ROWCOL`. See When To Use Bloom Filters for more information on `ROW` versus `ROWCOL`. See also the API documentation for ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."],[15722,"Bloom filters are enabled on a Column Family. You can do this by using the setBloomFilterType method of HColumnDescriptor or using the HBase API. Valid values are `NONE`, `ROW` (default), or `ROWCOL`. See When To Use Bloom Filters for more information on `ROW` versus `ROWCOL`. See also the API documentation for ColumnFamilyDescriptorBuilder."],[5779,"Bloom filters in version 2"],[11666,"Bloom filters in version 2"],[7356,"Bloom filters need to be rebuilt upon deletion, so may not be appropriate in environments with a large number of deletions."],[15721,"Bloom filters need to be rebuilt upon deletion, so may not be appropriate in environments with a large number of deletions."],[7425,"Bloom filters were developed over in HBase-1200 Add bloomfilters. For description of the development process — why static blooms rather than dynamic — and for an overview of the unique properties that pertain to blooms in HBase, as well as possible future directions, see the *Development Process* section of the document BloomFilters in HBase attached to HBASE-1200. The bloom filters described here are actually version two of blooms in HBase. In versions up to 0.19.x, HBase had a dynamic bloom option based on work done by the European Commission One-Lab Project 034819. The core of the HBase bloom work was later pulled up into Hadoop to implement org.apache.hadoop.io.BloomMapFile. Version 1 of HBase blooms never worked that well. Version 2 is a rewrite from scratch though again it starts with the one-lab work."],[15790,"Bloom filters were developed over in HBase-1200 Add bloomfilters. For description of the development process — why static blooms rather than dynamic — and for an overview of the unique properties that pertain to blooms in HBase, as well as possible future directions, see the *Development Process* section of the document BloomFilters in HBase attached to HBASE-1200. The bloom filters described here are actually version two of blooms in HBase. In versions up to 0.19.x, HBase had a dynamic bloom option based on work done by the European Commission One-Lab Project 034819. The core of the HBase bloom work was later pulled up into Hadoop to implement org.apache.hadoop.io.BloomMapFile. Version 1 of HBase blooms never worked that well. Version 2 is a rewrite from scratch though again it starts with the one-lab work."],[7350,"Bloom filters were introduced in HBASE-1200. Since HBase 0.96, row-based Bloom filters are enabled by default. (HBASE-8450)"],[15715,"Bloom filters were introduced in HBASE-1200. Since HBase 0.96, row-based Bloom filters are enabled by default. (HBASE-8450)"],[5831,"Bloom index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the \"load-on-open\" section as part of Bloom filter metadata."],[18767,"Bloom index — version 2 root index format only, stored in the \"load-on-open\" section as part of Bloom filter metadata."],[7260,"Bloom StoreFile footprint"],[10978,"Bloom StoreFile footprint"],[5809,"BLOOM\\_CHUNK – Bloom filter chunks"],[18745,"BLOOM\\_CHUNK – Bloom filter chunks"],[5814,"BLOOM\\_META – a Bloom filter metadata block in the load-on-open section"],[18750,"BLOOM\\_META – a Bloom filter metadata block in the load-on-open section"],[8707,"Both implementations work more or less like the MapReduce bulk load process in\nthat a partitioner partitions the rowkeys based on region splits and\nthe row keys are sent to the reducers in order, so that HFiles can be written\nout directly from the reduce phase."],[15458,"Both implementations work more or less like the MapReduce bulk load process in\nthat a partitioner partitions the rowkeys based on region splits and\nthe row keys are sent to the reducers in order, so that HFiles can be written\nout directly from the reduce phase."],[822,"Both Master and RegionServer participate in the event of online region merges. Client sends merge RPC to the master, then the master moves the regions together to the RegionServer where the more heavily loaded region resided. Finally the master sends the merge request to this RegionServer which then runs the merge. Similar to process of region splitting, region merges run as a local transaction on the RegionServer. It offlines the regions and then merges two regions on the file system, atomically delete merging regions from `hbase:meta` and adds the merged region to `hbase:meta`, opens the merged region on the RegionServer and reports the merge to the Master."],[14385,"Both Master and RegionServer participate in the event of online region merges. Client sends merge RPC to the master, then the master moves the regions together to the RegionServer where the more heavily loaded region resided. Finally the master sends the merge request to this RegionServer which then runs the merge. Similar to process of region splitting, region merges run as a local transaction on the RegionServer. It offlines the regions and then merges two regions on the file system, atomically delete merging regions from `hbase:meta` and adds the merged region to `hbase:meta`, opens the merged region on the RegionServer and reports the merge to the Master."],[3881,"Both the package and installed size are about 100 MB smaller."],[12279,"Both the package and installed size are about 100 MB smaller."],[7494,"branch-misses"],[15852,"branch-misses"],[7493,"branches"],[15851,"branches"],[1762,"Branches"],[11413,"Branches"],[2708,"Brotli"],[2713,"Brotli"],[2718,"Brotli"],[2723,"Brotli"],[18606,"Brotli"],[18611,"Brotli"],[18616,"Brotli"],[18621,"Brotli"],[2603,"BROTLI"],[2631,"BROTLI"],[18501,"BROTLI"],[18529,"BROTLI"],[774,"Brown: Offline state, a special state that can be transient (after closed before opening), terminal (regions of disabled tables), or initial (regions of newly created tables)"],[14337,"Brown: Offline state, a special state that can be transient (after closed before opening), terminal (regions of disabled tables), or initial (regions of newly created tables)"],[4820,"Browse to the Web UI"],[9853,"Browse to the Web UI"],[8947,"Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects"],[11041,"Browsing HDFS for HBase Objects"],[2507,"Bryan Beaudreault"],[17748,"Bryan Beaudreault"],[1093,"BucketCache Deploy Modes"],[10584,"BucketCache Deploy Modes"],[1094,"BucketCache Example Configuration"],[10585,"BucketCache Example Configuration"],[1987,"Bug: Something is broken in HBase itself