113,"Each log file is split, one at a time"],[10604,"Each log file is split, one at a time"],[2432,"Each maintained release branch has a release manager, who volunteers to coordinate new features and bug fixes are backported to that release. The release managers are committers. If you would like your feature or bug fix to be included in a given release, communicate with that release manager. If this list goes out of date or you can't reach the listed person, reach out to someone else on the list."],[17673,"Each maintained release branch has a release manager, who volunteers to coordinate new features and bug fixes are backported to that release. The release managers are committers. If you would like your feature or bug fix to be included in a given release, communicate with that release manager. If this list goes out of date or you can't reach the listed person, reach out to someone else on the list."],[6326,"Each master cluster region server has its own znode in the replication znodes hierarchy. It contains one znode per peer cluster (if 5 slave clusters, 5 znodes are created), and each of these contain a queue of WALs to process. Each of these queues will track the WALs created by that region server, but they can differ in size. For example, if one slave cluster becomes unavailable for some time, the WALs should not be deleted, so they need to stay in the queue while the others are processed. See rs.failover.details for an example."],[16640,"Each master cluster region server has its own znode in the replication znodes hierarchy. It contains one znode per peer cluster (if 5 slave clusters, 5 znodes are created), and each of these contain a queue of WALs to process. Each of these queues will track the WALs created by that region server, but they can differ in size. For example, if one slave cluster becomes unavailable for some time, the WALs should not be deleted, so they need to stay in the queue while the others are processed. See rs.failover.details for an example."],[4900,"Each node of your cluster needs to have the same configuration information. Copy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to the &#x2A;conf/* directory on `node-b` and `node-c`."],[11860,"Each node of your cluster needs to have the same configuration information. Copy the contents of the &#x2A;conf/* directory to the &#x2A;conf/* directory on `node-b` and `node-c`."],[1018,"Each of the settings for date tiered compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"],[14581,"Each of the settings for date tiered compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"],[1050,"Each of the settings for stripe compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"],[14613,"Each of the settings for stripe compaction should be configured at the table or column family level. If you use HBase shell, the general command pattern is as follows:"],[6040,"Each of the valid program names are bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo run one of the jobs, model your command after the following example."],[13561,"Each of the valid program names are bundled MapReduce jobs.\nTo run one of the jobs, model your command after the following example."],[2816,"Each of these library locations need to be added to the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the operating system user that runs HBase.\nYou need to restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."],[18714,"Each of these library locations need to be added to the environment variable `HBASE_LIBRARY_PATH` for the operating system user that runs HBase.\nYou need to restart the RegionServer for the changes to take effect."],[255,"Each Region Assign or Unassign of a Region is now a Procedure. A Move (Region) Procedure is a compound of Procedures; it is the running of an Unassign Procedure followed by an Assign Procedure. The Move Procedure spawns the Assign and Unassign in series and then waits on their completions."],[17583,"Each Region Assign or Unassign of a Region is now a Procedure. A Move (Region) Procedure is a compound of Procedures; it is the running of an Unassign Procedure followed by an Assign Procedure. The Move Procedure spawns the Assign and Unassign in series and then waits on their completions."],[537,"Each time the memstore for a MOB enabled column family performs a flush HBase will write values over\nthe MOB threshold into MOB specific hfiles. When normal region compaction occurs the Region Server\nrewrites the normal data files while maintaining references to these MOB files without rewriting\nthem. Normal client lookups for MOB values transparently will receive the original values because\nthe Region Server internals take care of using the reference data to then pull the value out of a\nspecific MOB file. This indirection means that building up a large number of MOB hfiles doesn't\nimpact the overall time to retrieve any specific MOB cell. Thus, we need not perform compactions of\nthe MOB hfiles nearly as often as normal hfiles. As a result, HBase saves IO by not rewriting MOB\nhfiles as a part of the periodic compactions a Region Server does on its own."],[14746,"Each time the memstore for a MOB enabled column family performs a flush HBase will write values over\nthe MOB threshold into MOB specific hfiles. When normal region compaction occurs the Region Server\nrewrites the normal data files while maintaining references to these MOB files without rewriting\nthem. Normal client lookups for MOB values transparently will receive the original values because\nthe Region Server internals take care of using the reference data to then pull the value out of a\nspecific MOB file. This indirection means that building up a large number of MOB hfiles doesn't\nimpact the overall time to retrieve any specific MOB cell. Thus, we need not perform compactions of\nthe MOB hfiles nearly as often as normal hfiles. As a result, HBase saves IO by not rewriting MOB\nhfiles as a part of the periodic compactions a Region Server does on its own."],[9764,"Each user who will be an HBase client should also be given a Kerberos principal.\nThis principal should usually have a password assigned to it (as opposed to, as with the HBase servers, a keytab file) which only this user knows.\nThe client's principal's `maxrenewlife` should be set so that it can be renewed enough so that the user can complete their HBase client processes.\nFor example, if a user runs a long-running HBase client process that takes at most 3 days, we might create this user's principal within `kadmin` with: `addprinc -maxrenewlife 3days`.\nThe ZooKeeper client and server libraries manage their own ticket refreshment by running threads that wake up periodically to do the refreshment."],[17629,"Each user who will be an HBase client should also be given a Kerberos principal.\nThis principal should usually have a password assigned to it (as opposed to, as with the HBase servers, a keytab file) which only this user knows.\nThe client's principal's `maxrenewlife` should be set so that it can be renewed enough so that the user can complete their HBase client processes.\nFor example, if a user runs a long-running HBase client process that takes at most 3 days, we might create this user's principal within `kadmin` with: `addprinc -maxrenewlife 3days`.\nThe ZooKeeper client and server libraries manage their own ticket refreshment by running threads that wake up periodically to do the refreshment."],[7558,"Early Pv2 aspects have been shipping in HBase with a good while now but it has continued to evolve as it takes on more involved scenarios. What we have now is powerful but intricate in operation and incomplete, in need of cleanup and hardening. In this doc we have given overview on the system so you can make use of it (and help with its polishing)."],[17521,"Early Pv2 aspects have been shipping in HBase with a good while now but it has continued to evolve as it takes on more involved scenarios. What we have now is powerful but intricate in operation and incomplete, in need of cleanup and hardening. In this doc we have given overview on the system so you can make use of it (and help with its polishing)."],[1260,"EC support has been added to hadoop-3.x, and it is incompatible with WAL as the EC output stream\ndoes not support hflush/hsync. In order to create a non-EC file in an EC directory, we need to use\nthe new builder-based create API for *FileSystem*, but it is only introduced in hadoop-2.9+ and\nfor HBase we still need to support hadoop-2.7.x. So please do not enable EC for the WAL directory\nuntil we find a way to deal with it."],[14244,"EC support has been added to hadoop-3.x, and it is incompatible with WAL as the EC output stream\ndoes not support hflush/hsync. In order to create a non-EC file in an EC directory, we need to use\nthe new builder-based create API for *FileSystem*, but it is only introduced in hadoop-2.9+ and\nfor HBase we still need to support hadoop-2.7.x. So please do not enable EC for the WAL directory\nuntil we find a way to deal with it."],[4773,"EC2 issues are a special case. See Amazon EC2 and Amazon EC2."],[18214,"EC2 issues are a special case. See Amazon EC2 and Amazon EC2."],[2020,"Eclipse"],[11316,"Eclipse"],[2027,"Eclipse - More Information"],[11323,"Eclipse - More Information"],[2022,"Eclipse Git Plugin"],[11318,"Eclipse Git Plugin"],[2026,"Eclipse Known Issues"],[11322,"Eclipse Known Issues"],[2047,"Eclipse will currently complain about *Bytes.java*.\nIt is not possible to turn these errors off."],[17053,"Eclipse will currently complain about *Bytes.java*.\nIt is not possible to turn these errors off."],[4892,"Edit *conf/regionservers* and remove the line which contains `localhost`. Add lines with the hostnames or IP addresses for `node-b` and `node-c`."],[11852,"Edit *conf/reg