dy for commit, 3 +1s from committers will get your feature merged. See HBase, mail # dev - Thoughts about large feature dev branches"],[17664,"Feature Branches are easy to make. You do not have to be a committer to make one. Just request the name of your branch be added to JIRA up on the developer's mailing list and a committer will add it for you. Thereafter you can file issues against your feature branch in Apache HBase JIRA. Your code you keep elsewhere — it should be public so it can be observed — and you can update dev mailing list on progress. When the feature is ready for commit, 3 +1s from committers will get your feature merged. See HBase, mail # dev - Thoughts about large feature dev branches"],[2477,"February 2020"],[17718,"February 2020"],[1170,"Fetching will always be slower when fetching from BucketCache in pre-hbase-2.0.0, as compared to the native on-heap LruBlockCache. However, latencies tend to be less erratic across time, because there is less garbage collection when you use BucketCache since it is managing BlockCache allocations, not the GC. If the BucketCache is deployed in off-heap mode, this memory is not managed by the GC at all. This is why you'd use BucketCache in pre-2.0.0, so your latencies are less erratic, to mitigate GCs and heap fragmentation, and so you can safely use more memory. See Nick Dimiduk's BlockCache 101 for comparisons running on-heap vs off-heap tests. Also see Comparing BlockCache Deploys which finds that if your dataset fits inside your LruBlockCache deploy, use it otherwise if you are experiencing cache churn (or you want your cache to exist beyond the vagaries of java GC), use BucketCache."],[14154,"Fetching will always be slower when fetching from BucketCache in pre-hbase-2.0.0, as compared to the native on-heap LruBlockCache. However, latencies tend to be less erratic across time, because there is less garbage collection when you use BucketCache since it is managing BlockCache allocations, not the GC. If the BucketCache is deployed in off-heap mode, this memory is not managed by the GC at all. This is why you'd use BucketCache in pre-2.0.0, so your latencies are less erratic, to mitigate GCs and heap fragmentation, and so you can safely use more memory. See Nick Dimiduk's BlockCache 101 for comparisons running on-heap vs off-heap tests. Also see Comparing BlockCache Deploys which finds that if your dataset fits inside your LruBlockCache deploy, use it otherwise if you are experiencing cache churn (or you want your cache to exist beyond the vagaries of java GC), use BucketCache."],[1788,"Few general guidelines"],[11439,"Few general guidelines"],[5506,"Field"],[5577,"Field"],[5612,"Field"],[5650,"Field"],[5694,"Field"],[5717,"Field"],[17795,"Field"],[17866,"Field"],[17901,"Field"],[17939,"Field"],[17983,"Field"],[18006,"Field"],[5136,"Field callQueueSize of type org.apache.hadoop.hbase.util.Counter has been removed from this class."],[19090,"Field callQueueSize of type org.apache.hadoop.hbase.util.Counter has been removed from this class."],[5138,"Field connectionList of type java.util.List\\<RpcServer.Connection> has been removed from this class."],[19092,"Field connectionList of type java.util.List\\<RpcServer.Connection> has been removed from this class."],[5360,"Field DEFAULT\\_HBASE\\_CONFIG\\_READ\\_ZOOKEEPER\\_CONFIG of type boolean has been removed from this class."],[19314,"Field DEFAULT\\_HBASE\\_CONFIG\\_READ\\_ZOOKEEPER\\_CONFIG of type boolean has been removed from this class."],[5362,"Field HBASE\\_CONFIG\\_READ\\_ZOOKEEPER\\_CONFIG of type java.lang.String has been removed from this class."],[19316,"Field HBASE\\_CONFIG\\_READ\\_ZOOKEEPER\\_CONFIG of type java.lang.String has been removed from this class."],[5175,"Field LOG of type org.apache.commons.logging.Log has been removed from this class."],[19129,"Field LOG of type org.apache.commons.logging.Log has been removed from this class."],[5179,"Field logSeqNum of type long has been removed from this class."],[191