ities were reported and fixed in 1.7.1. If you are considering an\nupgrade to 1.7.x line, see Upgrading to 1.7.1+."],[4926,"HBase 2.0 Incompatible Changes"],[11709,"HBase 2.0 Incompatible Changes"],[5081,"HBase 2.0 introduces changes to the following classes:"],[19035,"HBase 2.0 introduces changes to the following classes:"],[5011,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the MasterObserver interface."],[18965,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the MasterObserver interface."],[5043,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the RegionObserver interface."],[18997,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the RegionObserver interface."],[5073,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the WALObserver interface."],[19027,"HBase 2.0 introduces following changes to the WALObserver interface."],[7153,"HBase 2.0 removed `PREFIX_TREE` Data Block Encoding from column families. For further information please check *prefix-tree* encoding removed. To verify that none of the column families are using incompatible Data Block Encodings in the cluster run the following command."],[16332,"HBase 2.0 removed `PREFIX_TREE` Data Block Encoding from column families. For further information please check *prefix-tree* encoding removed. To verify that none of the column families are using incompatible Data Block Encodings in the cluster run the following command."],[2912,"HBase 2.0-2.2"],[11949,"HBase 2.0-2.2"],[9313,"HBase 2.0+ can no longer read Sequence File based WAL file. [!toc]"],[10238,"HBase 2.0+ can no longer read Sequence File based WAL file. [!toc]"],[9312,"HBase 2.0+ can no longer write HFile v2 files. [!toc]"],[10237,"HBase 2.0+ can no longer write HFile v2 files. [!toc]"],[9343,"HBase 2.2+ uses a new Procedure form assigning/unassigning/moving Regions. It does not process HBase 2.1 and 2.0's Unassign/Assign Procedure types. Upgrade requires that we first drain the Master Procedure Store of old style Procedures before starting the new 2.2 Master. So you need to make sure that before you kill the old version (2.0 or 2.1) Master, there is no region in transition. And once the new version (2.2+) Master is up, you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one."],[12859,"HBase 2.2+ uses a new Procedure form assigning/unassigning/moving Regions. It does not process HBase 2.1 and 2.0's Unassign/Assign Procedure types. Upgrade requires that we first drain the Master Procedure Store of old style Procedures before starting the new 2.2 Master. So you need to make sure that before you kill the old version (2.0 or 2.1) Master, there is no region in transition. And once the new version (2.2+) Master is up, you can rolling upgrade RegionServers one by one."],[2905,"HBase 2.3"],[11942,"HBase 2.3"],[2898,"HBase 2.4"],[11935,"HBase 2.4"],[2891,"HBase 2.5"],[11928,"HBase 2.5"],[2884,"HBase 2.6"],[11921,"HBase 2.6"],[2877,"HBase 2.7"],[11914,"HBase 2.7"],[9471,"HBase 2.x comes with default limits to the speed at which compactions can execute. This limit is defined per RegionServer. In previous versions of HBase earlier than 1.5, there was no limit to the speed at which a compaction could run by default. Applying a limit to the throughput of a compaction should ensure more stable operations from RegionServers."],[12987,"HBase 2.x comes with default limits to the speed at which compactions can execute. This limit is defined per RegionServer. In previous versions of HBase earlier than 1.5, there was no limit to the speed at which a compaction could run by default. Applying a limit to the throughput of a compaction should ensure more stable operations from RegionServers."],[2870,"HBase 3.0"],[11907,"HBase 3.0"],[1730,"HBase 3.0+"],[17083,"HBase 3.0+"],[2863,"HBase 3.1"],[11900,"HBase 3.1"],[8271,"HBase access levels are granted independently of each other and allow for different types of operations at a given scope."],[13775,"HBase access levels are granted independently of each other and allow for different types of operations at a given scope."],[8449,"HBase adheres to the Apache Software Foundation's policy on reported vulnerabilities, available at http\\://apache.org/security/."],[13594,"HBase adheres to the Apache Software Foundation's policy on reported vulnerabilities, available at http\\://apache.org/security/."],[8560,"HBase allows the adminstrators to be defined via a list of usernames or groups in hbase-site.xml"],[13609,"HBase allows the adminstrators to be defined via a list of usernames or groups in hbase-site.xml"],[3922,"HBase also has a limit on the number of WAL files, designed to ensure there's never too much data that needs to be replayed during recovery. This limit needs to be set according to memstore configuration, so that all the necessary data would fit. It is recommended to allocate enough WAL files to store at least that much data (when all memstores are close to full). For example, with 16Gb RS heap, default memstore settings (0.4), and default WAL file size (\\~60Mb), 16Gb\\*0.4/60, the starting point for WAL file count is \\~109. However, as all memstores are not expected to be full all the time, less WAL files can be allocated."],[12577,"HBase also has a limit on the number of WAL files, designed to ensure there's never too much data that needs to be replayed during recovery. This limit needs to be set according to memstore configuration, so that all the necessary data would fit. It is recommended to allocate enough WAL files to store at least that much data (when all memstores are close to full). For example, with 16Gb RS heap, default memstore settings (0.4), and default WAL file size (\\~60Mb), 16Gb\\*0.4/60, the starting point for WAL file count is \\~109. However, as all memstores are not expected to be full all the time, less WAL files can be allocated."],[3222,"HBase also puts up a UI listing vital attributes. By default it's deployed on the Master host at port 16010 (HBase RegionServers listen on port 16020 by default and put up an informational HTTP server at port 16030). If