ation \"hbase.replication\""],[605,"HDFS"],[7266,"HDFS"],[10681,"HDFS"],[10984,"HDFS"],[1320,"HDFS ACLs"],[10742,"HDFS ACLs"],[1330,"HDFS ACLs supports an \"access ACL\", which defines the rules to enforce during permission checks, and a \"default ACL\", which defines the ACL entries that new child files or sub-directories receive automatically during creation. Via HDFS ACLs, HBase syncs granted users with read permission to HFiles."],[14820,"HDFS ACLs supports an \"access ACL\", which defines the rules to enforce during permission checks, and a \"default ACL\", which defines the ACL entries that new child files or sub-directories receive automatically during creation. Via HDFS ACLs, HBase syncs granted users with read permission to HFiles."],[3890,"HDFS Configurations"],[10152,"HDFS Configurations"],[1344,"HDFS has a config which limits the max ACL entries num for one directory or file:"],[14834,"HDFS has a config which limits the max ACL entries num for one directory or file:"],[680,"HDFS is a distributed file system that is well suited for the storage of large files. Its documentation states that it is not, however, a general purpose file system, and does not provide fast individual record lookups in files. HBase, on the other hand, is built on top of HDFS and provides fast record lookups (and updates) for large tables. This can sometimes be a point of conceptual confusion. HBase internally puts your data in indexed \"StoreFiles\" that exist on HDFS for high-speed lookups. See the Data Model and the rest of this chapter for more information on how HBase achieves its goals."],[13955,"HDFS is a distributed file system that is well suited for the storage of large files. Its documentation states that it is not, however, a general purpose file system, and does not provide fast individual record lookups in files. HBase, on the other hand, is built on top of HDFS and provides fast record lookups (and updates) for large tables. This can sometimes be a point of conceptual confusion. HBase internally puts your data in indexed \"StoreFiles\" that exist on HDFS for high-speed lookups. See the Data Model and the rest of this chapter for more information on how HBase achieves its goals."],[9574,"HDFS super user access"],[12836,"HDFS super user access"],[8946,"HDFS Utilization of Tables and Regions"],[11040,"HDFS Utilization of Tables and Regions"],[9214,"HDFS-6133 is available in HDFS 2.7.0 and higher, but HBase does not support running on HDFS 2.7.0,\nso you must be using HDFS 2.7.1 or higher to use this feature with HBase."],[16082,"HDFS-6133 is available in HDFS 2.7.0 and higher, but HBase does not support running on HDFS 2.7.0,\nso you must be using HDFS 2.7.1 or higher to use this feature with HBase."],[9212,"HDFS-6133 provides the ability to exclude favored-nodes (pinned) blocks from the HDFS load balancer, by setting the `dfs.datanode.block-pinning.enabled` property to `true` in the HDFS service configuration."],[16080,"HDFS-6133 provides the ability to exclude favored-nodes (pinned) blocks from the HDFS load balancer, by setting the `dfs.datanode.block-pinning.enabled` property to `true` in the HDFS service configuration."],[4043,"Headings don't have to be visually unique, but their link IDs must be unique. You can customize the heading ID using Fumadocs syntax:"],[18179,"Headings don't have to be visually unique, but their link IDs must be unique. You can customize the heading ID using Fumadocs syntax:"],[5944,"Heads-up if this is your first foray into the world of distributed computing..."],[9804,"Heads-up if this is your first foray into the world of distributed computing..."],[6925,"Health Checker"],[11123,"Health Checker"],[476,"Hedged read is still supported, the configuration key is now *hbase.client.bootstrap.hedged.fanout*, and its default value is still 2."],[14031,"Hedged read is still supported, the configuration key is now *hbase.client.bootstrap.hedged.fanout*, and its default value is still 2."],[7262,"Hedged Reads"],[10980,"Hedged Reads"],[7434,"Hedged reads are \"...very good at eliminating outlier datanodes, which in turn makes them very good choice for latency sensitive setups. But, if you are looking for maximizing throughput, hedged reads tend to create load amplification as things get slower in general. In short, the thing to watch out for is the non-graceful performance degradation when you are running close a certain throughput threshold.\" (Quote from Ashu Pachauri in HBASE-17083)."],[15799,"Hedged reads are \"...very good at eliminating outlier datanodes, which in turn makes them very good choice for latency sensitive setups. But, if you are looking for maximizing throughput, hedged reads tend to create load amplification as things get slower in general. In short, the thing to watch out for is the non-graceful performance degradation when you are running close a certain throughput threshold.\" (Quote from Ashu Pachauri in HBASE-17083)."],[7433,"Hedged reads are a feature of HDFS, introduced in Hadoop 2.4.0 with HDFS-5776. Normally, a single thread is spawned for each read request. However, if hedged reads are enabled, the client waits some configurable amount of time, and if the read does not return, the client spawns a second read request, against a different block replica of the same data. Whichever read returns first is used, and the other read request is discarded."],[15798,"Hedged reads are a feature of HDFS, introduced in Hadoop 2.4.0 with HDFS-5776. Normally, a single thread is spawned for each read request. However, if hedged reads are enabled, the client waits some configurable amount of time, and if the read does not return, the client spawns a second read request, against a different block replica of the same data. Whichever read returns first is used, and the other read request is discarded."],[7446,"hedgedReadOps - the number of times hedged read threads have been triggered. This could indicate that read requests are often slow, or that hedged reads are triggered too quickly."],[15811,"hedgedReadOps - the number of times hedged read threads have been triggered. This could indicate that read requests are often slow, or that hedged reads are triggered too quickly."],[7448,"hedgedReadOpsInCurThread - the number of times hedged read was rejected from executor and needed to fallback to be executed in current thread. This could indicate that current hedged read thread pool size is not appropriate."],[15813,"hedgedReadOpsInCurThread - the number of times hedged read was rejected from executor and needed to fallback to be executed in current thread. This could indicate that current hedged read thread pool size is not appropriate."],[7447,"hedgeReadOpsWin - the number of times the hedged read thread was faster than the original thread. This could indicate that a given RegionServer is having trouble servicing requests."],[15812,"hedgeReadOpsWin - the number of times the hedged read thread was faster than the original thread. This could indicate that a given RegionServer is having trouble servicing requests."],[1901,"Help attract and retain a diverse community."],[17406,"Help attract and retain a diverse community."],[5461,"Help screen"],[11540,"Help screen"],[6969,"Here are some examples based on the following given case: given two Table objects called test-01 and test-02 each with two column family cf1 and cf2 respectively, deployed on 3 RegionServers. See the following table."],[16148,"Here are some examples based on the following given case: given two Table objects called test-01 and test-02 each with two column family cf1 and cf2 respectively, deployed on 3 RegionServers. See the following table."],[650,"Here are the list of configurations that effect MasterProcWAL operation. You should not have to change your defaults."],[14104,"Here are the list of configurations that effect MasterProcWAL operation. You should not have to change your defaults."],[8491,"Here are the steps needed to upgrade an already running HBase clu