luster earlier than 1.0.0. *Table instances are not thread-safe*. Only one thread can use an instance of Table at any given time. When creating Table instances, it is advisable to use the same HBaseConfiguration instance. This will ensure sharing of ZooKeeper and socket instances to the RegionServers which is usually what you want. For example, this is preferred:"],[13975,"Instances of `HTable` are the way to interact with an HBase cluster earlier than 1.0.0. *Table instances are not thread-safe*. Only one thread can use an instance of Table at any given time. When creating Table instances, it is advisable to use the same HBaseConfiguration instance. This will ensure sharing of ZooKeeper and socket instances to the RegionServers which is usually what you want. For example, this is preferred:"],[7796,"Instead of a random assignment, you could use a one-way **hash** that would cause a given row to always be \"salted\" with the same prefix, in a way that would spread the load across the RegionServers, but allow for predictability during reads. Using a deterministic hash allows the client to reconstruct the complete rowkey and use a Get operation to retrieve that row as normal."],[13250,"Instead of a random assignment, you could use a one-way **hash** that would cause a given row to always be \"salted\" with the same prefix, in a way that would spread the load across the RegionServers, but allow for predictability during reads. Using a deterministic hash allows the client to reconstruct the complete rowkey and use a Get operation to retrieve that row as normal."],[3924,"Instead of allowing HBase to split your regions automatically, you can choose to manage the splitting yourself. Manually managing splits works if you know your keyspace well, otherwise let HBase figure where to split for you. Manual splitting can mitigate region creation and movement under load. It also makes it so region boundaries are known and invariant (if you disable region splitting). If you use manual splits, it is easier doing staggered, time-based major compactions to spread out your network IO load."],[12579,"Instead of allowing HBase to split your regions automatically, you can choose to manage the splitting yourself. Manually managing splits works if you know your keyspace well, otherwise let HBase figure where to split for you. Manual splitting can mitigate region creation and movement under load. It also makes it so region boundaries are known and invariant (if you disable region splitting). If you use manual splits, it is easier doing staggered, time-based major compactions to spread out your network IO load."],[6787,"Instead of moving each server into a group by hand, a non-default rsgroup can be made *regex-governed*: regionservers whose hostname matches a regular expression are placed in the group automatically as they register, and removed again when they go away. Set one property per group, where the suffix is the name of an existing rsgroup:"],[16938,"Instead of moving each server into a group by hand, a non-default rsgroup can be made *regex-governed*: regionservers whose hostname matches a regular expression are placed in the group automatically as they register, and removed again when they go away. Set one property per group, where the suffix is the name of an existing rsgroup:"],[2041,"Instead of using `m2eclipse`, you can generate the Eclipse files from the command line."],[17047,"Instead of using `m2eclipse`, you can generate the Eclipse files from the command line."],[7490,"instructions"],[15848,"instructions"],[9327,"Instructions"],[10252,"Instructions"],[7290,"Insufficient uplink to another rack"],[15655,"Insufficient uplink to another rack"],[7469,"Integrated, on-demand profiling via the Async Profiler project."],[9252,"Integration Testing with an HBase Mini-Cluster"],[11459,"Integration Testing with an HBase Mini-Cluster"],[1790,"Integration Tests"],[2170,"Integration Tests"],[11400,"Integration Tests"],[11441,"Integration Tests"],[9470,"Integration Tests (`IntegrationTests*`) used to rely on the Kerberos credential cache for authentication against secured clusters. This used to lead to tests failing due to authentication failures when the tickets in the credential cache expired. As of hbase-2.0.0 (and hbase-1.3.0+), the integration test clients will make use of the configuration properties `hbase.client.keytab.file` and `hbase.client.kerberos.principal`. They are required. The clients will perform a login from the configured keytab file and automatically refresh the credentials in the background for the process lifetime (See HBASE-16231)."],[12986,"Integration Tests (`IntegrationTests*`) used to rely on the Kerberos credential cache for authentication against secured clusters. This used to lead to tests failing due to authentication failures when the tickets in the credential cache expired. As of hbase-2.0.0 (and hbase-1.3.0+), the integration test clients will make use of the configuration properties `hbase.client.keytab.file` and `hbase.client.kerberos.principal`. They are required. The clients will perform a login from the configured keytab file and automatically refresh the credentials in the background for the process lifetime (See HBASE-16231)."],[9322,"Integration Tests and Kerberos [!toc]"],[10247,"Integration Tests and Kerberos [!toc]"],[2247,"Integration tests can be run in two modes: using a mini cluster, or against an actual distributed cluster.\nMaven failsafe is used to run the tests using the mini cluster.\nIntegrationTestsDriver class is used for executing the tests against a distributed cluster.\nIntegration tests SHOULD NOT assume that they are running against a mini cluster, and SHOULD NOT use private API's to access cluster state.\nTo interact with the distributed or mini cluster uniformly, `IntegrationTestingUtility`, and `HBaseCluster` classes, and public client API's can be used."],[17257,"Integration tests can be run in two modes: using a mini cluster, or against an actual distributed cluster.\nMaven failsafe is used to run the tests using the mini cluster.\nIntegrationTestsDriver class is used for executing the tests against a distributed cluster.\nIntegration tests SHOULD NOT assume that they are running against a mini cluster, and SHOULD NOT use private API's to access cluster state.\nTo interact with the distributed or mini cluster uniformly, `IntegrationTestingUtility`, and `HBaseCluster` classes, and public client API's can be used."],[2246,"Integration tests currently live under the *src/test&#x2A; directory in the hbase-it submodule and will match the regex: **IntegrationTest*.java*.\nAll integration tests are also annotated with `@Tag(IntegrationTests.TAG)`."],[17256,"Integration tests currently live under the *src/test&#x2A; directory in the hbase-it submodule and will match the regex: **IntegrationTest*.java*.\nAll integration tests are also annotated with `@Tag(IntegrationTests.TAG)`."],[2153,"Integration Tests:"],[11383,"Integration Tests:"],[2028,"IntelliJ IDEA"],[11324,"IntelliJ IDEA"],[466,"Inter cluster replication still uses ZooKeeper based connection registry to simplify configuration management."],[14021,"Inter cluster replication still uses ZooKeeper based connection registry to simplify configuration management."],[1902,"Interact with other projects in ways that benefit HBase and those other\nprojects."],[17407,"Interact with other projects in ways that benefit HBase and those other\nprojects."],[30,"Interface"],[624,"Interface"],[1078,"Interface"],[10562,"Interface"],[10569,"Interface"],[18239,"Interface"],[4961,"interface AccessControlConstants [!toc]"],[11744,"interface AccessControlConstants [!toc]"],[4945,"interface Admin [!toc]"],[11728,"interface Admin [!toc]"],[4968,"interface Cell 5 [!toc]"],[11751,"interface Cell 5 [!toc]"],[1765,"Interface Classifications"],[11416,"Interface Classifications"],[4929,"interface CoprocessorEnvironment changes"],[11712,"interface CoprocessorEnvironment changes"],[4991,