CacheBlocks method (set it to false). You can still keep block caching turned on on this table if you need fast random read access. An example would be counting the number of rows in a table that serves live traffic, caching every block of that table would create massive churn and would surely evict data that's currently in use."],[4741,"MapReduce"],[8942,"MapReduce"],[11036,"MapReduce"],[11603,"MapReduce"],[7254,"MapReduce - Input Splits"],[10972,"MapReduce - Input Splits"],[4973,"Mapreduce changes:"],[11756,"Mapreduce changes:"],[7135,"MapReduce mode offers the ability to process each family dir in parallel, as a separate map task.\nGenerally, it would make sense to run in this mode when specifying one or more table dirs as\ntargets for compactions. The caveat, though, is that if number of families to be compacted become\ntoo large, the related mapreduce job may have indirect impacts on **RegionServers** performance .\nSince **NodeManagers** are normally co-located with RegionServers, such large jobs could compete\nfor IO/Bandwidth resources with the **RegionServers**."],[16314,"MapReduce mode offers the ability to process each family dir in parallel, as a separate map task.\nGenerally, it would make sense to run in this mode when specifying one or more table dirs as\ntargets for compactions. The caveat, though, is that if number of families to be compacted become\ntoo large, the related mapreduce job may have indirect impacts on **RegionServers** performance .\nSince **NodeManagers** are normally co-located with RegionServers, such large jobs could compete\nfor IO/Bandwidth resources with the **RegionServers**."],[5991,"MapReduce Scan Caching"],[10410,"MapReduce Scan Caching"],[7055,"Mapreduce-based Export"],[16234,"Mapreduce-based Export"],[670,"MapReduce: HBase supports massively parallelized processing via MapReduce for using HBase as both source and sink."],[13945,"MapReduce: HBase supports massively parallelized processing via MapReduce for using HBase as bo