ng on the localhost at port 8020."],[9265,"Next, add an `@ExtendWith` annotation to your test class, to direct it to use Mockito."],[17484,"Next, add an `@ExtendWith` annotation to your test class, to direct it to use Mockito."],[9259,"Next, add some unit tests to your code.\nTests are annotated with `@Test`.\nHere, the unit tests are in bold."],[17478,"Next, add some unit tests to your code.\nTests are annotated with `@Test`.\nHere, the unit tests are in bold."],[1184,"Next, add the following configuration to the RegionServer's *hbase-site.xml*."],[14168,"Next, add the following configuration to the RegionServer's *hbase-site.xml*."],[2103,"Next, adjust the version in all the POM files appropriately.\nIf you are making a release candidate, you must remove the `-SNAPSHOT` label from all versions\nin all pom.xml files.\nIf you are running this receipe to publish a snapshot, you must keep the `-SNAPSHOT` suffix on the hbase version.\nThe Versions Maven Plugin can be of use here.\nTo set a version in all the many poms of the hbase multi-module project, use a command like the following:"],[17139,"Next, adjust the version in all the POM files appropriately.\nIf you are making a release candidate, you must remove the `-SNAPSHOT` label from all versions\nin all pom.xml files.\nIf you are running this receipe to publish a snapshot, you must keep the `-SNAPSHOT` suffix on the hbase version.\nThe Versions Maven Plugin can be of use here.\nTo set a version in all the many poms of the hbase multi-module project, use a command like the following:"],[2114,"Next, build the binary tarball. Add the `-Prelease` profile when building.\nIt runs the license apache-rat check among other rules that help ensure\nall is wholesome. Do it in two steps."],[17150,"Next, build the binary tarball. Add the `-Prelease` profile when building.\nIt runs the license apache-rat check among other rules that help ensure\nall is wholesome. Do it in two steps."],[2119,"Next, deploy HBase to the Apache Maven repository. Add the\napache-release`profile when running the`mvn deploy\\` command.\nThis profile comes from the Apache parent pom referenced by our pom files.\nIt does signing of your artifacts published to Maven, as long as the\n*settings.xml* is configured correctly, as described in Example \\~/.m2/settings.xml File.\nThis step depends on the local repository having been populate\nby the just-previous bin tarball build."],[17155,"Next, deploy HBase to the Apache Maven repository. Add the\napache-release`profile when running the`mvn deploy\\` command.\nThis profile comes from the Apache parent pom referenced by our pom files.\nIt does signing of your artifacts published to Maven, as long as the\n*settings.xml* is configured correctly, as described in Example \\~/.m2/settings.xml File.\nThis step depends on the local repository having been populate\nby the just-previous bin tarball build."],[8390,"Next, encryption use can be specified in the schema, per column family, by creating or modifying a column descriptor to include two additional attributes: the name of the encryption algorithm to use (currently only \"AES\" is supported), and optionally, a data key wrapped (encrypted) with the cluster master key. If a data key is not explicitly configured for a ColumnFamily, HBase will create a random data key per HFile. This provides an incremental improvement in security over the alternative. Unless you need to supply an explicit data key, such as in a case where you are generating encrypted HFiles for bulk import with a given data key, only specify the encryption algorithm in the ColumnFamily schema metadata and let HBase create data keys on demand. Per Column Family keys facilitate low impact incremental key rotation and reduce the scope of any external leak of key material. The wrapped data key is stored in the ColumnFamily schema metadata, and in each HFile for the Column Family, encrypted with the cluster master key. After the Column Family is configured for encryption, any new HFiles will be written encrypted. To ensure encryption of all HFiles, trigger a major compaction after enabling this feature."],[13894,"Next, encryption use can be specified in the schema, per column family, by creating or modifying a column descriptor to include two additional attributes: the name of the encryption algorithm to use (currently only \"AES\" is supported), and optionally, a data key wrapped (encrypted) with the cluster master key. If a data key is not explicitly configured for a ColumnFamily, HBase will create a random data key per HFile. This provides an incremental improvement in security over the alternative. Unless you need to supply an explicit data key, such as in a case where you are generating encrypted HFiles for bulk import with a given data key, only specify the encryption algorithm in the ColumnFamily schema metadata and let HBase create data keys on demand. Per Column Family keys facilitate low impact incremental key rotation and reduce the scope of any external leak of key material. The wrapped data key is stored in the ColumnFamily schema metadata, and in each HFile for the Column Family, encrypted with the cluster master key. After the Column Family is configured for encryption, any new HFiles will be written encrypted. To ensure encryption of all HFiles, trigger a major compaction after enabling this feature."],[2116,"Next, generate documentation and assemble the tarball. Be warned,\nthis next step can take a good while, a couple of hours generating site\ndocumentation."],[17152,"Next, generate documentation and assemble the tarball. Be warned,\nthis next step can take a good while, a couple of hours generating site\ndocumentation."],[9233,"Next, raise the value. Try doubling, then quadrupling the value. Note that setting the value too low or too high could have detrimental effects on your system. Consult your operating system vendor for specific recommendations."],[16101,"Next, raise the value. Try doubling, then quadru