ons and ownership on this file such that only the HBase service account user can read the file, and securely distribute the key to all HBase servers."],[13903,"The previous command created a file called *hbase.jks* in the HBase &#x2A;conf/* directory. Set the permissions and ownership on this file such that only the HBase service account user can read the file, and securely distribute the key to all HBase servers."],[7823,"The problem is that all the data is going to pile up in the first 2 regions and the last region thus creating a \"lumpy\" (and possibly \"hot\") region problem. To understand why, refer to an ASCII Table. '0' is byte 48, and 'f' is byte 102, but there is a huge gap in byte values (bytes 58 to 96) that will *never appear in this keyspace* because the only values are \\[0-9] and \\[a-f]. Thus, the middle regions will never be used. To make pre-splitting work with this example keyspace, a custom definition of splits (i.e., and not relying on the built-in split method) is required."],[13277,"The problem is that all the data is going to pile up in the first 2 regions and the last region thus creating a \"lumpy\" (and possibly \"hot\") region problem. To understand why, refer to an ASCII Table. '0' is byte 48, and 'f' is byte 102, but there is a huge gap in byte values (bytes 58 to 96) that will *never appear in this keyspace* because the only values are \\[0-9] and \\[a-f]. Thus, the middle regions will never be used. To make pre-splitting work with this example keyspace, a custom definition of splits (i.e., and not relying on the built-in split method) is required."],[1279,"The problematic WAL log will be moved into the *.corrupt* directory under the hbase `rootdir`,"],[14263,"The problematic WAL log will be moved into the *.corrupt* directory under the hbase `rootdir`,"],[7570,"The ProcedureExecutor stamps the frameworks notions of Procedure State into the Procedure itself; e.g. it marks Procedures as INITIALIZING on submit. It moves the state to RUNNABLE when it goes to execute. When done, a Procedure gets marked FAILED or SUCCESS depending. Here is the list of all states as of this writing:"],[17533,"The ProcedureExecutor stamps the frameworks notions of Procedure State into the Procedure itself; e.g. it marks Procedures as INITIALIZING on submit. It moves the state to RUNNABLE when it goes to execute. When done, a Procedure gets marked FAILED or SUCCESS depending. Here is the list of all states as of this writing:"],[2098,"The process below makes use of various tools, mainly *git* and *maven*."],[17134,"The process below makes use of various tools, mainly *git* and *maven*."],[7123,"The program allows you to limit the scope of the run. Provide a row regex or prefix to limit the rows to analyze. Specify a time range to scan the table by using the `--starttime=<starttime>` and `--endtime=<endtime>` flags."],[16302,"The program allows you to limit the scope of the run. Provide a row regex or prefix to limit the rows to analyze. Specify a time range to scan the table by using the `--starttime=<starttime>` and `--endtime=<endtime>` flags."],[7983,"The pros of this approach include a less complex object hierarchy, but one of the cons is that updating gets more complicated in case any of this information changes."],[13437,"The pros of this approach include a less complex object hierarchy, but one of the cons is that updating gets more complicated in case any of this information changes."],[1744,"The protobuf files are located in *hbase-protocol/src/main/protobuf*.\nFor the change to be effective, you will need to regenerate the classes."],[17097,"The protobuf files are located in *hbase-protocol/src/main/protobuf*.\nFor the change to be effective, you will need to regenerate the classes."],[7513,"The query parameter `pid` can be used to specify the process id of a specific process to be profiled. If this parameter is missing the local process in which the infoserver is embedded will be profiled. Profile targets that are not JVMs might work but is not specifically supported. There are security implications. Access to the infoserver should be appropriately restricted."],[15871,"The query parameter `pid` can be used to specify the process id of a specific process to be profiled. If this parameter is missing the local process in which the infoserver is embedded will be profiled. Profile targets that are not JVMs might work but is not specifically supported. There are security implications. Access to the infoserver should be appropriately restricted."],[922,"The RatioBasedCompactionPolicy was the only compaction policy prior to HBase 0.96, though ExploringCompactionPolicy has now been backported to HBase 0.94 and 0.95. To use the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy rather than the ExploringCompactionPolicy, set `hbase.hstore.defaultengine.compactionpolicy.class` to `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` in the *hbase-site.xml* file. To switch back to the ExploringCompactionPolicy, remove the setting from the *hbase-site.xml*."],[14485,"The RatioBasedCompactionPolicy was the only compaction policy prior to HBase 0.96, though ExploringCompactionPolicy has now been backported to HBase 0.94 and 0.95. To use the RatioBasedCompactionPolicy rather than the ExploringCompactionPolicy, set `hbase.hstore.defaultengine.compactionpolicy.class` to `RatioBasedCompactionPolicy` in the *hbase-site.xml* file. To switch back to the ExploringCompactionPolicy, remove the setting from the *hbase-site.xml*."],[3916,"The reason why it is dangerous to keep this setting high is that the aggregate size of all the puts that are currently happening in a region server may impose too much pressure on its memory, or even trigger an OutOfMemoryError. A RegionServer running on low memory will trigger its JVM's garbage collector to run more frequently up to a point where GC pauses become noticeable (the reason being that all the memory used to keep all the requests' payloads cannot be trashed, no matter how hard the garbage collector tries). After some time, the overall cluster throughput is affected since every request that hits that RegionServer will take longer, which exacerbates the problem even more."],[12571,"The reason why it is dangerous to keep this setting high is that the aggregate size of all the puts that are currently happening in a region server may impose too much pressure on its memory, or even trigger an OutOfMemoryError. A RegionServer running on low memory will trigger its JVM's garbage collector to run more frequently up to a point where GC pauses become noticeable (the reason being that all the memory used to keep all the requests' payloads cannot be trashed, no matter how hard the garbage collector tries). After some time, the overall cluster throughput is affected since every request that hits that RegionServer will take longer, which exacerbates the problem even more."],[6014,"The recommended approach is to let HBase add its dependency jars and use `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` or `-libjars`."],[13535,"The recommended approach is to let HBase add its dependency jars and use `HADOOP_CLASSPATH` or `-libjars`."],[5472,"The refresh delay (in seconds); default is 3 seconds"],[17761,"The refresh delay (in seconds); default is 3 seconds"],[6793,"The regex always wins. A manual move that contradicts a regex is rejected, because the next server event or master restart would put the server back where its hostname says: a server matching a regex can only be moved to its regex-governed group, and a server that does not match cannot be moved into one. A regex-governed group cannot be removed while it has servers in the master's in-memory view. Remove the `hbase.rsgroup.regex.<group>` property from the configuration of all masters, including backup masters, and restart all HMasters. The group is then an ordinary admin-managed group with no servers, and its former servers go to `default`. This holds only if you did not reload the configuration first and a group change stored the servers in the meantime; if you did, move the servers out of the group by hand. Then remove the group. Removal also fails while a table or a namespace refers to the group."],[16944,"The regex always wins. A manual move that contradicts a regex is rejected, because the next server event or master restart would put the server back where its hostname says: a server matching a regex can only be moved to its regex-governed group, and a server that does not match cannot be moved into one. A regex-governed group cannot be removed while it has servers in the master's in-memory view. Remove the `hbase.rsgroup.regex.<group>` property from the configuration of all masters, including backup masters, and restart all HMasters. The group is then an ordinary admin-managed group with no servers, and its former servers go to `default`. This holds only if you did not reload the configuration first and a group change stored the servers in the meantime; if you did, move the servers out of the group by hand. Then remove the group. Removal also fails while a table or a namespace refers to the group."],[6788,"The regex is matched against the server's hostname only (not the port), and must match the whole hostname."],[16939,"The regex is matched against the server's hostname only (not the port), and must match the whole hostname."],[754,"The region assignments will be considered invalid and will be re-assigned just like the startup sequence."],[14317,"The region assignments will be considered invalid and will be re-assigned just like the startup sequence."],[6827,"The Region Normalizer is feature available since HBase-1.2. It runs a set of pre-calculated merge/split actions to resize regions that are either too large or too small compared to the average region size for a given table. Region Normalizer when invoked computes a normalization 'plan' for all of the tables in HBase. System tables (such as hbase:meta, hbase:namespace, Phoenix system tables etc) and user tables with normalization disabled are ignored while computing the plan. For normalization enabled tables, normalization plan is carried out in parallel across multiple tables."],[16978,"The Region Normalizer is feature available since HBase-1.2. It runs a set of pre-calculated merge/split actions to resize regions that are either too large or too small compared to the average region size for a given table. Region Normalizer when invoked computes a normalization 'plan' for all of the tables in HBase. System tables (such as hbase:meta, hbase:namespace, Phoenix system tables etc) and user tables with normalization disabled are ignored while computing the plan. For normalization enabled tables, normalization plan is carried out in parallel across multiple tables."],[6824,"The Region Normalizer tries to make Regions all in a table about the same in size. It does this by first calculating total table size and average size per region. It splits any region that is larger than twice this size. Any region that is much smaller is merged into an adjacent region. The Normalizer runs on a regular schedule, which is configurable. It can also be disabled entirely via a runtime \"switch\". It can be run manually via the shell or Admin API call. Even if normally disabled, it is good to run manually after the cluster has been running a while or say after a burst of activity such as a large delete."],[16975,"The Region Normalizer tries to make Regions all in a table about the same in size. It does this by first calculating total table size and average size per region. It splits any region that is larger than twice this size. Any region that is much smaller is merged into an adjacent region. The Normalizer runs on a regular schedule, which is configurable. It can also be disabled entirely via a runtime \"switch\". It can be run manually via the shell or Admin API call. Even if normally disabled, it is good to run manually after the cluster has been running a while or say after a burst of activity such as a large delete."],[6303,"The region server reads the edits sequentially and separates them into buffers, one buffer per table. After all edits are read, each buffer is flushed using Table, HBase's normal client. The master's UUID and the UUIDs of slaves which have already consumed the data are preserved in the edits they are applied, in order to prevent replication loops."],[16617,"The region server reads the edits sequentially and separates them into buffers, one buffer per table. After all edits are read, each buffer is flushed using Table, HBase's normal client. The master's UUID and the UUIDs of slaves which have already consumed the data are preserved in the edits they are applied, in order to prevent replication loops."],[6299,"The region server writes the request to the WAL in a way allows it to be replayed if it is not written successfully."],[16613,"The region server writes the request to the WAL in a way allows it to be replayed if it is not written successfully."],[752,"The regions immediately become unavailable because the RegionServer is down."],[14315,"The regions immediately become unavailable because the RegionServer is down."],[1245,"The RegionServer adds the daughters A and B to `.META.`, together with information that it hosts the regions. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT REGIONS (DAUGHTERS WITH REFERENCES TO PARENT) ARE NOW ONLINE.** After this point, clients can discover the new regions and issue requests to them. Clients cache the `.META.` entries locally, but when they make requests to the RegionServer or `.META.`, their caches will be invalidated, and they will learn about the new regions from `.META.`."],[14229,"The RegionServer adds the daughters A and B to `.META.`, together with information that it hosts the regions. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT REGIONS (DAUGHTERS WITH REFERENCES TO PARENT) ARE NOW ONLINE.** After this point, clients can discover the new regions and issue requests to them. Clients cache the `.META.` entries locally, but when they make requests to the RegionServer or `.META.`, their caches will be invalidated, and they will learn about the new regions from `.META.`."],[1240,"The RegionServer closes the parent region and marks the region as offline in its local data structures. &#x2A;*THE SPLITTING REGION IS NOW OFFLINE.** At this point, client requests coming to the parent region will throw `NotServingRegionException`. The client will retry with some backoff. The closing region is flushed."],[14224,"The RegionServer closes the parent region and marks the region as offline in its local data structures. &#x2A;*THE SPLITTING REGION IS NOW OFFLINE.** At this point, client requests coming to the parent region will throw `NotServingRegionException`. The client will retry with some backoff. The closing region is flushed."],[1239,"The RegionServer creates a sub-directory named `.splits` under the parent's `region` directory in HDFS."],[14223,"The RegionServer creates a sub-directory named `.splits` under the parent's `region` directory in HDFS."],[1241,"The RegionServer creates region directories under the `.splits` directory, for daughter regions A and B, and creates necessary data structures. Then it splits the store files, in the sense that it creates two Reference files per store file in the parent region. Those reference files will point to the parent region's files."],[14225,"The RegionServer creates region directories under the `.splits` directory, for daughter regions A and B, and creates necessary data structures. Then it splits the store files, in the sense that it creates two Reference files per store file in the parent region. Those reference files will point to the parent region's files."],[1242,"The RegionServer creates the actual region directory in HDFS, and moves the reference files for each daughter."],[14226,"The RegionServer creates the actual region directory in HDFS, and moves the reference files for each daughter."],[1237,"The RegionServer decides locally to split the region, and prepares the split. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS STARTED.** As a first step, the RegionServer acquires a shared read lock on the table to prevent schema modifications during the splitting process. Then it creates a znode in zookeeper under `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name`, and sets the znode's state to `SPLITTING`."],[14221,"The RegionServer decides locally to split the region, and prepares the split. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS STARTED.** As a first step, the RegionServer acquires a shared read lock on the table to prevent schema modifications during the splitting process. Then it creates a znode in zookeeper under `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name`, and sets the znode's state to `SPLITTING`."],[9336,"The RegionServer Grouping feature has been reimplemented. See section Migrating From Old Implementation in Apache HBase Operational Management for more details."],[12852,"The RegionServer Grouping feature has been reimplemented. See section Migrating From Old Implementation in Apache HBase Operational Management for more details."],[1244,"The RegionServer opens daughters A and B in parallel."],[14228,"The RegionServer opens daughters A and B in parallel."],[1133,"The RegionServer runs a variety of background threads:"],[14117,"The RegionServer runs a variety of background threads:"],[1243,"The RegionServer sends a `Put` request to the `.META.` table, to set the parent as offline in the `.META.` table and add information about daughter regions. At this point, there won't be individual entries in `.META.` for the daughters. Clients will see that the parent region is split if they scan `.META.`, but won't know about the daughters until they appear in `.META.`. Also, if this `Put` to `.META`. succeeds, the parent will be effectively split. If the RegionServer fails before this RPC succeeds, Master and the next Region Server opening the region will clean dirty state about the region split. After the `.META.` update, though, the region split will be rolled-forward by Master."],[14227,"The RegionServer sends a `Put` request to the `.META.` table, to set the parent as offline in the `.META.` table and add information about daughter regions. At this point, there won't be individual entries in `.META.` for the daughters. Clients will see that the parent region is split if they scan `.META.`, but won't know about the daughters until they appear in `.META.`. Also, if this `Put` to `.META`. succeeds, the parent will be effectively split. If the RegionServer fails before this RPC succeeds, Master and the next Region Server opening the region will clean dirty state about the region split. After the `.META.` update, though, the region split will be rolled-forward by Master."],[1246,"The RegionServer updates znode `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name` in ZooKeeper to state `SPLIT`, so that the master can learn about it. The balancer can freely re-assign the daughter regions to other region servers if necessary. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS NOW FINISHED.**"],[14230,"The RegionServer updates znode `/hbase/region-in-transition/region-name` in ZooKeeper to state `SPLIT`, so that the master can learn about it. The balancer can freely re-assign the daughter regions to other region servers if necessary. &#x2A;*THE SPLIT TRANSACTION IS NOW FINISHED.**"],[6105,"The RegionServer web UI has statistics on ByteBuffAllocator:"],[14881,"The RegionServer web UI has statistics on ByteBuffAllocator:"],[9051,"The RegionServer web UI lists online regions and their start/end keys, as well as point-in-time RegionServer metrics (requests, regions, storeFileIndexSize, compactionQueueSize, etc.)."],[15919,"The RegionServer web UI lists online regions and their start/end keys, as well as point-in-time RegionServer metrics (requests, regions, storeFileIndexSize, compactionQueueSize, etc.)."],[6666,"The RegionServer will first close all regions and then shut itself down. On shutdown, the RegionServer's ephemeral node in ZooKeeper will expire. The master will notice the RegionServer