pedOp"],[6417,"TimeStampOfNextToReplicate"],[16731,"TimeStampOfNextToReplicate"],[6429,"TimeStampsOfLastAppliedOp"],[16743,"TimeStampsOfLastAppliedOp"],[6423,"TimeStampStarted"],[16737,"TimeStampStarted"],[9744,"TLS connection to ZooKeeper"],[11510,"TLS connection to ZooKeeper"],[6504,"To access the HBase metrics, navigate to the appropriate sub-bean: .\\* Master: .\\* RegionServer:"],[16402,"To access the HBase metrics, navigate to the appropriate sub-bean: .\\* Master: .\\* RegionServer:"],[4036,"To add a new section to the HBase documentation:"],[18172,"To add a new section to the HBase documentation:"],[4031,"To add or modify configuration parameters, update the source XML file. To regenerate the documentation from the updated configuration, run:"],[18167,"To add or modify configuration parameters, update the source XML file. To regenerate the documentation from the updated configuration, run:"],[6795,"To add, change or remove a regex, change the configuration of all masters and restart the HMasters. Do not reload the configuration; reload is not recommended for these properties. A restart recomputes the membership of every regex-governed group at once. A reload recomputes nothing: servers move only at the next server event, and the in-memory membership of a group stays as it was until then. Group operations do not recompute it either: `add_rsgroup` and a group rename do not apply a regex, so a regex for a group that does not exist yet is ignored until a later server event or restart."],[16946,"To add, change or remove a regex, change the configuration of all masters and restart the HMasters. Do not reload the configuration; reload is not recommended for these properties. A restart recomputes the membership of every regex-governed group at once. A reload recomputes nothing: servers move only at the next server event, and the in-memory membership of a group stays as it was until then. Group operations do not recompute it either: `add_rsgroup` and a group rename do not apply a regex, so a regex for a group that does not exist yet is ignored until a later server event or restart."],[274,"To address the above, we added a lastUpdate for in-memory Master state. Let a region state have some vintage before we act on it (one second currently)."],[17602,"To address the above, we added a lastUpdate for in-memory Master state. Let a region state have some vintage before we act on it (one second currently)."],[7301,"To address the first, start the CMS earlier than default by adding `-XX:CMSInitiatingOccupancyFraction` and setting it down from defaults. Start at 60 or 70 percent (The lower you bring down the threshold, the more GCing is done, the more CPU used). To address the second fragmentation issue, Todd added an experimental facility, (MSLAB), that must be explicitly enabled in Apache HBase 0.90.x (It's defaulted to be *on* in Apache 0.92.x HBase). Set `hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.enabled` to true in your `Configuration`. See the cited slides for background and detail. The latest JVMs do better regards fragmentation so make sure you are running a recent release. Read down in the message, Identifying concurrent mode failures caused by fragmentation. Be aware that when enabled, each MemStore instance will occupy at least an MSLAB instance of memory. If you have thousands of regions or lots of regions each with many column families, this allocation of MSLAB may be responsible for a good portion of your heap allocation and in an extreme case cause you to OOME. Disable MSLAB in this case, or lower the amount of memory it uses or float less regions per server."],[15666,"To address the first, start the CMS earlier than default by adding `-XX:CMSInitiatingOccupancyFraction` and setting it down from defaults. Start at 60 or 70 percent (The lower you bring down the threshold, the more GCing is done, the more CPU used). To address the second fragmentation issue, Todd added an experimental facility, (MSLAB), that must be explicitly enabled in Apache HBase 0.90.x (It's defaulted to be *on* in Apache 0.92.x HBase). Set `hbase.hregion.memstore.mslab.enabled` to true in your `Configuration`. See the cited slides for background and detail. The latest JVMs do better regards fragmentation so make sure you are running a recent release. Read down in the message, Identifying concurrent mode failures caused by fragmentation. Be aware that when enabled, each MemStore instance will occupy at least an MSLAB instance of memory. If you have thousands of regions or lots of regions each with many column families, this allocation of MSLAB may be responsible for a good portion of your heap allocation and in an extreme case cause you to OOME. Disable MSLAB in this case, or lower the amount of memory it uses or float less regions per server."],[8216,"To allow proxy users, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every HBase server:"],[13684,"To allow proxy users, add the following to the `hbase-site.xml` file for every HBase server:"],[8776,"To apply similar switch at column family level configuration:"],[18076,"To apply similar switch at column family level configuration:"],[1505,"To approximate how long the final step will take, we have to make some assumptions on hardware. Be aware that these will *not* be accurate for your\nsystem — these are numbers that your or your administrator know for your system. Let's say the speed of reading data from HDFS on a single node is\ncapped at 80MB/s (across all Mappers that run on that host), a modern network interface controller (NIC) supports 10Gb/s, the top-of-rack switch can\nhandle 40Gb/s, and the WAN between your clusters is 10Gb/s. This means that you can only ship data to your remote at a speed of 1.25GB/s — meaning\nthat 16 nodes (`1.25 * 1024 / 80 = 16`) participating in the ExportSnapshot should be able to fully saturate the link between clusters. With more\nnodes in the cluster, we can still saturate the network but at a lesser impact on any one node which helps ensure local SLAs are made. If the size\nof the snapshot is 10TB, this would full backup would take in the ballpark of 2.5 hours (`10 * 1024 / 1.25 / (60 * 60) = 2.23hrs`)"],[15042,"To approximate how long the final step will take, we have to make some assumptions on hardware. Be aware that these will *not* be accurate for your\nsystem — these are numbers that your or your administrator know for your system. Let's say the speed of reading data from HDFS on a single node is\ncapped at 80MB/s (across all Mappers that run on that host), a modern network interface controller (NIC) supports 10Gb/s, the top-of-rack switch can\nhandle 40Gb/s, and the WAN between your clusters is 10Gb/s. This means that you can only ship data to your remote at a speed of 1.25GB/s — meaning\nthat 16 nodes (`1.25 * 1024 / 80 = 16`) participating in the ExportSnapshot should be able to fully saturate the link between clusters. With more\nnodes in the cluster, we can still saturate the network but at a lesser impact on any one node which helps ensure local SLAs are made. If the size\nof the snapshot is 10TB, this would full backup would take in the ballpark of 2.5 hours (`10 * 1024 / 1.25 / (60 * 60) = 2.23hrs`)"],[6885,"To avoid contention and separate different kinds of requests, configure the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor` property, which allows you to increase the number of queues and control how many handlers can share the same queue., allows admins to increase the number of queues and decide how many handlers share the same queue."],[16797,"To avoid contention and separate different kinds of requests, configure the `hbase.ipc.server.callqueue.handler.factor` property, which allows you to increase the number of queues and control how many handlers can share the same queue., allows admins to increase the number of queues and decide how many handlers share the same queue."],[1387,"To better understand the TIMELINE semantics, let's look at the above diagram. Let's say that there