tMemorySize`. By default, the `MaxDirectMemorySize` is equal to your `-Xmx` max heapsize setting (if `-Xmx` is set). Try setting it to something smaller (for example, one user had success setting it to `1g` when they had a client-side heap of `12g`). If you set it too small, it will bring on `FullGCs` so keep it a bit hefty. You want to make this setting client-side only especially if you are running the new experimental server-side off-heap cache since this feature depends on being able to use big direct buffers (You may have to keep separate client-side and server-side config dirs)."],[7397,"You can achieve a similar effect using the HBase Shell to create tables by specifying split options."],[15762,"You can achieve a similar effect using the HBase Shell to create tables by specifying split options."],[6884,"You can adjust the IPC queues and handlers based on the type of workload, using static tuning options. This approach is an interim first step that will eventually allow you to change the settings at runtime, and to dynamically adjust values based on the load."],[16796,"You can adjust the IPC queues and handlers based on the type of workload, using static tuning options. This approach is an interim first step that will eventually allow you to change the settings at runtime, and to dynamically adjust values based on the load."],[4135,"You can also load a coprocessor dynamically, without restarting HBase. This may seem\npreferable to static loading, but dynamically loaded coprocessors are loaded on a\nper-table basis, and are only available to the table for which they were loaded. For\nthis reason, dynamically loaded tables are sometimes called **Table Coprocessor**."],[15547,"You can also load a coprocessor dynamically, without restarting HBase. This may seem\npreferable to static loading, but dynamically loaded coprocessors are loaded on a\nper-table basis, and are only available to the table for which they were loaded. For\nthis reason, dynamically loaded tabl