.org/docs/stable/hadoop-aliyun/tools/hadoop-aliyun/index.html."],[6198,"Your hosts must be configured to be aware of the Azure blob storage filesystem. See https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r2.7.1/hadoop-azure/index.html."],[16872,"Your hosts must be configured to be aware of the Azure blob storage filesystem. See https\\://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r2.7.1/hadoop-azure/index.html."],[9272,"Your MRUnit test verifies that the output is as expected, the Put that is inserted into HBase has the correct value, and the ColumnFamily and ColumnQualifier have the correct values."],[17491,"Your MRUnit test verifies that the output is as expected, the Put that is inserted into HBase has the correct value, and the ColumnFamily and ColumnQualifier have the correct values."],[8009,"Your second option is \"wide\": you store a bunch of values in one row, using different qualifiers (where the qualifier is the valueid). The simple way to do that would be to just store ALL values for one user in a single row. I'm guessing you jumped to the \"paginated\" version because you're assuming that storing millions of columns in a single row would be bad for performance, which may or may not be true; as long as you're not trying to do too much in a single request, or do things like scanning over and returning all of the cells in the row, it shouldn't be fundamentally worse. The client has methods that allow you to get specific slices of columns."],[13463,"Your second option is \"wide\": you store a bunch of values in one row, using different qualifiers (where the qualifier is the valueid). The simple way to do that would be to just store ALL values for one user in a single row. I'm guessing you jumped to the \"paginated\" version because you're assuming that storing millions of columns in a single row would be bad for performance, which may or may not be true; as long as you're not trying to do too much in a single request, or do things like scanning over and returning all of the cells in the row, it shouldn't be fundamentally worse. The client has methods that allow you to get specific slices of columns."],[8008,"Your two options mirror a common question people have when designing HBase schemas: should I go \"tall\" or \"wide\"? Your first schema is \"tall\": each row represents one value for one user, and so there are many rows in the table for each user; the row key is user + valueid, and there would be (presumably) a single column qualifier that means \"the value\". This is great if you want to scan over rows in sorted order by row key (thus my question above, about whether these ids are sorted correctly). You can start a scan at any user+valueid, read the next 30, and be done. What you're giving up is the ability to have transactional guarantees around all the rows for one user, but it doesn't sound like you need that. Doing it this way is generally recommended (see here)."],[13462,"Your two options mirror a common question people have when designing HBase schemas: should I go \"tall\" or \"wide\"? Your first schema is \"tall\": each row represents one value for one user, and so there are many rows in the table for each user; the row key is user + valueid, and there would be (presumably) a single column qualifier that means \"the value\". This is great if you want to scan over rows in sorted order by row key (thus my question above, about whether these ids are sorted correctly). You can start a scan at any user+valueid, read the next 30, and be done. What you're giving up is the ability to have transactional guarantees around all the rows for one user, but it doesn't sound like you need that. Doing it this way is generally recommended (see here)."],[8948,"Zero size WALs with data in them [!toc]"],[11042,"Zero size WALs with data in them [!toc]"],[8922,"zkcli"],[11016,"zkcli"],[6381,"zookeeper"],[16695,"zookeeper"],[4867,"ZooKeeper"],[7226,"ZooKeeper"],[8980,"ZooKeeper"],[9730,"ZooKeeper"],[10944,"ZooKeeper"],[11074,"ZooKeeper"],[11497,"ZooKeeper"],[11827